<#
.SYNOPSIS
    FirstBase Windows Update loop - native Windows Update Agent (COM) only.
    For Internal Use Only.

.DESCRIPTION
    Runs the multi-pass Windows Update cycle in Audit Mode using the
    documented Microsoft.Update.Session COM API directly. There is NO
    runtime dependency on PSWindowsUpdate.

    Iteration order (per scan, greedy install):
      Bucket 1: Critical / Security / Definition
      Bucket 2: Drivers
      Bucket 3: Cumulative / Update Rollups / Servicing Stack
      Bucket 4: Optional / remaining software updates
      Final  : verification scan, then sysprep handoff

    Each iteration runs ONE unfiltered online scan and applies ALL FOUR
    buckets back-to-back, even when an earlier bucket reports
    RebootRequired. We only hard-stop the iteration when WUA returns
    an HResult in $ExplicitRebootHResults (e.g. ERROR_SUCCESS_REBOOT_-
    REQUIRED) - in that case the install genuinely cannot make further
    progress without a reboot. For every other failure mode (including
    unknown HResults that just need retrying) we continue to the next
    bucket so we install everything we can before consuming a reboot.

    Convergence guarantees (2026-05-11):
      - Retry counter keyed on KB article when available, so cumulative
        updates whose UpdateID changes between scans still accumulate
        toward $MaxUpdateRetry.
      - Wider $BenignHResultMap so common CBS / installer error codes
        get explicit handling (skip / reboot / cautious retry).
      - Absolute escape hatch: any key offered after $MaxKeyBootCount
        reboots gets force-hidden via IUpdate.IsHidden=true.
      - Cycle reboot ceiling: after $MaxRebootIterations reboots issued
        by this loop we sysprep to OOBE and defer the rest to Windows
        Update post-OOBE.
      - $RequiredEmptySweeps = 1: a single clean final-check immediately
        proceeds to completion (no redundant confirmation reboot).

    The script reboots automatically when WUA reports a reboot is
    required and resumes via SYSTEM scheduled tasks (ONSTART + ONLOGON
    backup).

    2026.05.13.1900-two-stage-sysprep-handoff: CRITICAL ARCHITECTURAL
    FIX. Field evidence from the 2026-05-13 0800 test device exposed
    a fundamental flaw in build 1700's OOBE-handoff routing.
    `i:\Panther\setupact.log` showed:

      2026-05-13 08:21:52, Error  SYSPRP Sysprep_Clean_Validate_Opk:
        There are one or more Windows updates that require a reboot.
        To run Sysprep, reboot the computer and restart the
        application.[gle=0x000036b7]
      2026-05-13 08:21:52, Error  SYSPRP ActionPlatform::LaunchModule:
        Failure occurred while executing 'Sysprep_Clean_Validate_Opk'
        from C:\Windows\System32\spopk.dll; dwRet = 0x139f
      2026-05-13 08:21:52, Error  SYSPRP RunPlatformActions:Failed
        while validating Sysprep session actions; dwRet = 0x139f
      2026-05-13 08:21:52, Error  SYSPRP WinMain:Hit failure while
        pre-validate sysprep cleanup internal providers;
        hr = 0x8007139f

    Sysprep VALIDATES the system state before generalize and REFUSES
    to run with 0x8007139f (ERROR_INVALID_STATE) if any pending
    Windows-update reboot operation is set. Build 1700 routed to
    sysprep when InstallsCompleted=true AND Test-FbWuRebootRequired=
    true on the assumption that sysprep would handle the reboot
    itself via Setup's path; it cannot. The loop ALSO used
    `Start-Process sysprep.exe ... -NoNewWindow` (fire-and-forget),
    so it never observed the 0x139f exit code, logged
    "Sysprep launched successfully.", and exited 0. The pre-sysprep
    trigger cleanup wiped all four trigger layers, sysprep stamped
    State.ini=IMAGE_STATE_UNDEPLOYABLE on its partial run, and the
    device was stranded on Audit Mode desktop with no live triggers
    in an undeployable state.

    The correct sequence is TWO-STAGE:
      1. Install updates: latch InstallsCompleted=true AND set
         new flag $state.PendingSysprep=true.
      2. Reboot via shutdown.exe to let CBS settle the pending
         operations. Triggers stay armed; sysprep is NOT launched.
      3. Loop relaunches via RunOnce on Audit Mode boot.
      4. Loop reads state: sees PendingSysprep=true AND
         Test-FbWuRebootRequired=false (CBS has settled). Pre-
         sysprep CBS-settle wait confirms 3 consecutive clean
         probes.
      5. NOW launches sysprep WITH -Wait, captures exit code,
         fails loud if non-zero (panic dump + retry budget).
      6. Sysprep returns 0 -> triggers deleted -> exit 0 ->
         sysprep async-reboots to OOBE.

    Fixes (this build):

      Fix 1. New $state.PendingSysprep field on wu-state.json with
             sentinel-preserve in Save-State ($null = preserve) and
             default-false in Load-State. Persists across reboots so
             a STAGE 1 -> reboot -> STAGE 2 sequence is observable
             on the next boot. Backward-compat synthesis in
             Load-State: if InstallsCompleted=true AND PendingSysprep
             is missing from a legacy state file, infer
             PendingSysprep=true (best-effort recovery for devices
             stranded by build 1700's broken routing).

      Fix 2. Two-stage routing in Invoke-FbReboot's evidence-gate
             branch. STAGE 1: latched + has-evidence + pending-
             reboot -> mark PendingSysprep=true, re-arm RunOnce +
             all four trigger layers, fall through to the existing
             patient-shutdown sequence (which now succeeds because
             we are NOT sysprepping). STAGE 2: latched + has-
             evidence + NO pending-reboot -> Invoke-FbOobeHandoff
             (sysprep will succeed). Top-of-function fast path:
             if PendingSysprep=true AND -not Test-FbWuRebootRequired,
             route directly to STAGE 2 (handles no-op iterations
             that reach Invoke-FbReboot on Boot 2). Main-loop body
             post-scan check: if PendingSysprep=true AND
             -not Test-FbWuRebootRequired, route to STAGE 2
             immediately without finishing the iteration.

      Fix 3. Sync, exit-code-aware sysprep launch in
             Invoke-SysprepToOobe. Changed
               Start-Process sysprep.exe -NoNewWindow
             to
               $proc = Start-Process sysprep.exe -Wait -PassThru
               $exit = $proc.ExitCode
             Sysprep returns 0 BEFORE the async generalize+reboot
             in the success case, so -Wait captures only the
             validation phase result. On non-zero exit we now log
             loudly, write a STUCK-DIAG-SYSPREP-PENDING-REBOOT-*.json
             panic dump (with sysprep Panther setupact.log /
             setuperr.log excerpts when exit=0x139f), bump
             SysprepRetryCount, leave the triggers armed, and route
             to plain shutdown for another reboot attempt. After 3
             consecutive sysprep refusals, the escape valve trips
             with reason 'SYSPREP_REPEATEDLY_REFUSED'.

      Fix 4. Pre-sysprep CBS-settle wait in
             Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared. STAGE 2 calls this BEFORE
             invoking sysprep. Waits for Test-FbWuRebootRequired to
             return false on 3 consecutive 10-second probes (max 5
             minutes). If it never settles, returns $false; STAGE
             2 then routes to plain shutdown for another reboot
             attempt (max 3 such attempts before tripping the
             escape valve).

      Fix 5. Pre-sysprep trigger preservation. The build 1700
             cleanup that wiped all four trigger layers BEFORE
             calling Sysprep.exe is DEFERRED. Triggers are now
             deleted only AFTER Sysprep.exe returns exit code 0
             and Invoke-SysprepToOobe is about to return $true.
             On non-zero exit, triggers remain armed so RunOnce /
             ONSTART / ONLOGON / RunOnceEx / Run can relaunch the
             loop on the next boot for retry.

      Fix 6. Dashboard activity bar surfacing. Set-FbDashboardLatch
             Diag prefix now includes Stage=1of2 | 2of2 | N/A in
             addition to the existing Latch / RealInstalls /
             OSPendingReboot / Routing fields. Stage 1 means
             "installs done, rebooting for CBS settle"; Stage 2
             means "reboot done, launching sysprep"; N/A means
             "neither stage active".

    2026.05.12.1030-no-cascade-final: the prior 8-stage reboot cascade
    has been removed. Invoke-FbReboot now (a) always re-reads
    wu-state.json from disk, (b) if InstallsCompleted=true OR
    Test-FbWuRebootRequired=true, routes straight to OOBE handoff with
    NO cascade, (c) otherwise re-arms RunOnce, attempts ONE
    `shutdown /r /f /t 5`, sleeps 30s and exits 1 to let RunOnce /
    ONSTART / ONLOGON relaunch. The kill-switch `NEVER-CASCADE.flag`
    forces the OOBE handoff path unconditionally.

    2026.05.12.1830-patient-shutdown-and-hresult-coverage: field
    evidence on the build 1700 test device (no logs available; visual
    operator report) showed two new operational issues exposed AFTER
    1700 successfully closed the silent-bypass class of bug:

      1. Dashboard rendered correctly and the latch only fired on real
         install evidence (1700 working as intended), but operator saw
         the activity-bar message "Pre-install shutdown.exe did not take
         the box down within 30s; exiting so RunOnce/ONSTART/ONLOGON
         can relaunch" - meaning shutdown.exe /r /f /t 5 was issued, the
         30-second sleep elapsed, the box stayed up, and the script
         exited 1 expecting a relaunch that the OS never produced.
         Root cause: on a freshly-imaged box mid-WUA work, Trusted-
         Installer / Winlogon / pending CBS operations routinely
         defer shutdown for 60-120 seconds. The 30s budget in build
         1700 was demonstrably too short.
      2. Most updates pinned at Status='Retrying' (one at 'Pending
         restart'). With per-update results coming back correctly
         (1700's Fix A) the HResults were the real ones from WUA, but
         many CBS / SXS / WU transient codes (0x80073712,
         0x80246007, 0x800F081F, 0x80070032, 0x80073701, 0x80070005,
         0x80070BC9, etc.) were not in $BenignHResultMap with the
         correct Action='Reboot' classification, so the loop was
         grinding the full 3-retry budget in-boot on HResults that
         can only be resolved by reboot-then-retry.

    Fixes (this build):

      Fix 1. Invoke-FbReboot's Step 5 'PLAIN REBOOT' branch is
             REPLACED by a patient observable shutdown sequence:
               - Phase A: shutdown.exe /r /f /t 30 (2214a; was 60 through
                 2213z), then wait up to
                 5 minutes (300s, 10s ticks). Each tick refreshes
                 the heartbeat, re-runs the registry/WUA reboot-
                 reason probe (Get-FbRebootReasonLabel), and stamps
                 the reason on the activity bar.
               - Phase B: if still up, shutdown.exe /r /f /t 0,
                 then wait up to 2 minutes (120s, 10s ticks).
               - Exit: 7-minute total budget exhausted -> exit 1
                 with an informative message containing the final
                 reason probe so the operator knows WHY the box
                 refused to reboot.
             NO cascade. NO P/Invoke fallback shutdown methods.
             NO ExitWindowsEx / InitiateSystemShutdownExW /
             NtShutdownSystem. Pure user-mode shutdown.exe only.
      Fix 2. $BenignHResultMap EXPANDED with 9 entries covering the
             CBS / SXS / WU transient codes observed on fresh-image
             devices. 0x80073712 ERROR_SXS_COMPONENT_STORE_CORRUPT
             and 0x80246007 WU_E_NOT_DOWNLOADED re-mapped from
             Skip/Retry to Reboot because field evidence showed
             they install cleanly after a settling reboot.
             Action='Reboot' entries still call Set-FbInstalls-
             Completed with -RealInstallEvidence $true (1700's rail
             is preserved verbatim).
      Fix 3. Per-update retry budget in Invoke-FbBucketApply.
             Get-FbUpdateRetryBudget returns 1 when the HResult is
             in $BenignHResultMap with Action='Reboot', else
             $MaxUpdateRetry. The chosen budget is persisted to
             $state.UpdateRetryBudget so it survives reboots. This
             stops the loop from burning the full 3-retry budget
             in-boot on HResults that can only be resolved by
             reboot-then-retry.
      Fix 4. Retry storm transparency. When more than half of a
             bucket's targeted updates end at Retrying, the
             dashboard activity bar is stamped with the bucket
             name, retrying-count, and a comma-separated HResult
             list ("Bucket Critical: 3/4 retrying [HResults: ...].
             Reboot then retry strategy in effect."). Surfaced via
             Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag and Set-UiPhase.
      Fix 5. Watchdog compatibility documented in-place. The
             stuck-Rebooting watchdog's 30s threshold (Trigger 1)
             is preserved because the patient-reboot loop above
             refreshes wu-status.json's updatedAt every 10
             seconds during the 7-minute wait via Set-UiPhase ->
             Save-UiState. The patient-reboot message strings are
             deliberately chosen to avoid matching Triggers 2/3/4
             (no 'cascade' / 'Reboot attempt' / 'REBOOT FAILED' /
             'CascadeStage=' substrings).

    Schema additions:
      - wu-state.json: new UpdateRetryBudget hashtable keyed via
        Get-FbRetryKey. Save-State uses the sentinel-preserve
        pattern ($null parameter -> preserve disk) to avoid
        threading the field through every call site.

    2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence: actual field evidence from
    WU-20260512-110026.log on the test device explained the silent
    bypass conclusively:

      [11:02:36] applying 4 update(s) (HaltOnFailure=True)
      [11:03:04] InstallsCompleted LATCHED to true (bucket Critical
                 reached Installing UI status (4 update(s) past Download))
      [11:03:04] [Critical] 'KB5007651' failed (ResultCode=-1, HResult=0)
      [11:03:04] [Critical] 'KB2267602' failed (ResultCode=-1, HResult=0)
      [11:03:04] [Critical] 'KB5087051' failed (ResultCode=-1, HResult=0)
      [11:03:04] [Critical] 'KB5089549' failed (ResultCode=-1, HResult=0)
      [11:03:04] Invoke-FbReboot probe: InstallsCompleted=True,
                 Test-FbWuRebootRequired=True
      [11:03:04] routing to OOBE handoff
      [11:03:05] OOBE handoff: relabeled 4 pending-restart row(s) to
                 Completed with deferred-settle note.
      [11:03:05] Sysprep launched.

    Two independent bugs, both critical:

      1. Invoke-FbWuApply's async polling returned ResultCode=-1
         (orcNotStarted) for every update, every time. The async
         BeginDownload/BeginInstall job was returning IsCompleted
         signals via GetProgress() that the polling loop treated as
         job completion, but the per-update IInstallationResult was
         being read BEFORE EndInstall returned and before the
         underlying installer had actually reported a real result
         code. The 28-second wall-clock from "applying 4 update(s)"
         to all 4 "failed (ResultCode=-1)" is impossible for a real
         cumulative install - the engine was bailing out of the poll
         before WUA actually started any work.
      2. Invoke-FbBucketApply latched InstallsCompleted on a UI
         status string heuristic ("bucket reached Installing UI
         status"). The latch fired on the cosmetic Set-UiUpdateStatus
         call inside the apply, NOT on a real orcSucceeded /
         orcSucceededWithErrors result. The 1100 build added this
         rail specifically to "fire even if WUA blows up before
         producing per-update results" - which converted every COM
         polling-bug regression into a silent bypass to OOBE.

    Fix policy (this build): the only thing that counts as evidence
    of a real install is a per-update IInstallationResult with a
    ResultCode of 2 (orcSucceeded) or 3 (orcSucceededWithErrors)
    AS RETURNED BY $installer.EndInstall($installJob).GetUpdateResult($i).
    Nothing else. Not "reached Installing UI". Not "past Download
    count". Not "COM apply threw". Not "Test-FbWuRebootRequired=true".
    Not "watchdog declared recovery". Real install evidence or
    nothing.

    Fixes (this build):

      Fix A. FirstBaseWuEngine.ps1 Invoke-FbWuApply REWRITTEN. The
             async download/install polling now:
               - Polls IDownloadJob.IsCompleted / IInstallationJob.
                 IsCompleted ONLY. Does not exit on any other signal.
               - Calls $downloader.EndDownload / $installer.EndInstall
                 exactly once when IsCompleted=$true to obtain the
                 real IDownloadResult / IInstallationResult.
               - Per-update results come from
                 $installationResult.GetUpdateResult($i), never from
                 $installationJob.GetProgress().GetUpdateResult.
               - Hard timeouts (default 30min download / 45min install
                 per bucket). On timeout: RequestAbort() then treat
                 every update as orcFailed (ResultCode=4).
               - Adds Installed count to the public return shape so
                 callers can ask "did anything actually succeed?"
                 without parsing the per-update array.
      Fix B. Invoke-FbBucketApply rewritten so Set-FbInstallsCompleted
             fires ONLY on:
               a) applyResult.Installed > 0 (at least one real
                  orcSucceeded/orcSucceededWithErrors from the engine), OR
               b) A BenignHResultMap Action='Reboot' entry whose
                  HResult was returned by a real orcFailed per-update
                  result (i.e. WUA actually ran the installer and got
                  a known reboot-recoverable failure), OR
               c) Test-FbWuRebootRequired=$true after at least one
                  real install succeeded in THIS boot
                  ($script:RealInstallsThisBoot - in-memory only, does
                  NOT survive reboot so a leftover pending-reboot
                  signal on a fresh image cannot trip the latch).
             "Reached Installing UI" / "past Download" / "COM apply
             threw" rails REMOVED.
      Fix C. Invoke-FbReboot OOBE-handoff routing now requires:
               - InstallsCompleted=$true on disk (which now means
                 a real install happened at some point), AND
               - $script:RealInstallsThisBoot > 0 OR state.RealInstallsTotal
                 > 0 (lifetime counter of real installs across reboots).
             Otherwise: single shutdown /r /f /t 5, 30s sleep, exit 1
             so RunOnce/ONSTART/ONLOGON can relaunch. No sysprep.
      Fix D. Invoke-FbOobeHandoff's Set-FbAllUpdatesDeferred only
             relabels rows that originated from a real install
             attempt with at least one orcSucceeded peer in the
             same bucket. Rows from a polling-bug run (all
             ResultCode=-1) are left in their actual state so the
             dashboard never lies again.
      Fix E. First-iteration hard rail. The startup-gate bypass and
             the main-loop top guard now both refuse to short-circuit
             when (a) BootCount==1, (b) iteration counter==0, and
             (c) $script:RealInstallsThisBoot==0 AND no prior
             RealInstallsTotal on disk. The loop MUST run at least
             one scan + one Invoke-FbBucketApply on a freshly-imaged
             device before any handoff decision is considered.
      Fix F. Dashboard activity bar prefix now stamps a full evidence
             triplet: "Latch={T|F} | RealInstalls=N |
             OSPendingReboot={Y|N} | Routing={Continue|ShutdownR|
             OOBEHandoff}" every time Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag runs,
             so the field can confirm from the splash alone whether
             a silent bypass is being attempted.
      Fix G. ResultCode=-1 from a completed job is now a HARD ERROR.
             If a per-update result still comes back as -1 after the
             Fix A rewrite, a STUCK-DIAG-RESULTCODE-MINUS-ONE-*.txt
             panic dump is written and the row is treated as
             orcFailed (NOT as latchable success).

    Legacy build notes follow:

    2026.05.12.1100-latch-on-pending: field evidence from the 1030
    build showed the pre-install reboot branch in Invoke-FbReboot
    firing on devices that clearly had installed updates - the
    dashboard had 39 rows pinned at 'Pending restart' yet both
    gates (InstallsCompleted=false AND Test-FbWuRebootRequired=false)
    came back false. Two root causes:
      1. Set-FbInstallsCompleted only fired on a per-update
         ResultCode in $ValidInstallCodes OR a post-bucket
         RebootRequired probe. ALL of the OTHER paths in
         Invoke-FbBucketApply that label a row 'Pending restart'
         (COM apply exception, retry-exhausted unknown HResult,
         BenignHResultMap Action='Reboot', BenignHResultMap
         Action='Skip', the post-bucket relabel loop) FORGOT to
         set the latch. So a row could go to 'Pending restart'
         without the latch ever flipping.
      2. Test-FbWuRebootRequired's third registry probe used
         Test-Path against a path that is a registry VALUE, not
         a registry KEY (Session Manager\PendingFileRenameOperations).
         Test-Path on a value name always returns false on this
         provider, so the probe could never see PFRO blockers.
    Fixes:
      * Every Pending-restart-producing path in Invoke-FbBucketApply
        now calls Set-FbInstallsCompleted BEFORE labelling the row.
      * Bucket apply now latches the moment a row transitions to
        'Installing' (post-Download, pre-result-eval): from that
        moment on, an install attempt has been made and the latch
        must fire even if WUA blows up before producing per-update
        results.
      * Test-FbWuRebootRequired now value-probes
        PendingFileRenameOperations correctly (Get-ItemProperty
        with non-empty multi-string).
      * Watchdog-recovery rail now persists InstallsCompleted=true
        to disk via Save-State BEFORE the startup gate runs, so a
        user power-cycle out of a stuck phase forces the latch.
      * Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag wraps every Save-State / Set-UiPhase
        with a stage-tagged "[InstallsCompleted=<true|false> at
        <stage>]" prefix on the activity bar so the latch state is
        visible from the splash alone (stages: startup, bucket-apply,
        reboot-call, watchdog-recovery, pending-restart-detected).

    Completion markers:
      - transient:  C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\FirstBase-Updates-Done.flag
      - persistent: C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\FirstBase-Updates-Complete.flag

    Logs: C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\Logs\WU-<timestamp>.log

    2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve: graceful escape hatch when the WUA
    engine itself is broken on the device (e.g. build 1830 engine
    returns ResultCode=-1 for every update on certain hardware/Windows-
    build combinations). Build 1700's evidence gating remains the
    default - silent bypass on a healthy box is still forbidden - but
    a NEW safety net trips after multiple consecutive "futile" boots
    so the device does not loop forever:

      Fix 1. New $state.FutileBootCount counter on wu-state.json.
             Incremented by Test-FbEscapeValve at every Invoke-FbReboot
             entry when (a) the loop ran (heartbeat alive), (b) the
             scan returned > 0 updates, (c) Invoke-FbBucketApply was
             invoked for at least one bucket, AND (d) RealInstallsTotal
             did NOT advance from boot-start ($script:RealInstallsAt-
             BootStart) to boot-end. A boot that installed something
             RESETS the counter to 0. A boot that found no updates
             does NOT count as futile (different failure mode). A
             boot that crashed before scan does NOT count.
      Fix 2. Escape-valve trigger. When FutileBootCount reaches the
             threshold (default $FutileBootThreshold=3) AND
             RealInstallsTotal is still 0, Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff
             is called from inside Invoke-FbReboot. The handoff
             persists EscapeValveTripped=$true on disk, writes a
             panic record under Logs\ESCAPE-VALVE-TRIPPED-<stamp>.json
             (with timestamp, ScriptVersion, full state snapshot,
             every update ID/title/final status from wu-status.json,
             last 50 lines of the most recent WU-*.log), relabels
             every non-installed UI row to "Failed - tech review
             required", and calls Invoke-FbOobeHandoff directly -
             BYPASSING the build 1700 evidence gate so the device
             arrives at OOBE in an unmistakably "incomplete - tech
             review" state instead of being silently shipped.
      Fix 3. Operator-override flag files under FB_ROOT\State\:
               - FORCE-OOBE-HANDOFF.flag: empty file. Detected at
                 the top of every main-loop iteration AND at the
                 top of Invoke-FbReboot. Trips the escape valve
                 immediately with reason 'OPERATOR_OVERRIDE'.
                 Deleted after handoff so a relaunch does not
                 silently retrigger.
               - EXTEND-BUDGET.flag: text file containing a single
                 positive integer N. Multiplies the threshold by N
                 (e.g. "5" raises threshold to 15). Read by
                 Get-FbFutileBootThreshold at every evaluation;
                 dropping a higher number live mid-cycle works
                 without re-arming anything.
      Fix 4. The post-handoff RunOnce is the warning popup when
             EscapeValveTripped, not the success popup. A short
             inline PowerShell command pops a
             System.Windows.Forms.MessageBox with Warning icon
             reading "Updates Incomplete - <reason> - Real installs
             completed: N - Updates remaining: M - Tech review
             required before delivery". Show-UpdatesCompleteMessage.
             ps1 is left untouched (byte-locked for this release).
             A secondary SYSTEM-scoped scheduled task ("FirstBase-
             EscapeValveRunOnceRewrite") re-writes the RunOnce
             value 60s after handoff to defend against the sysprep
             popup-script overwrite.
      Fix 5. Dashboard activity bar carries FutileBootCount=N/M in
             the footer whenever N > 0, and "*** ESCAPE VALVE
             TRIPPED ***" (plain text per user emoji policy) as a
             prefix when the latch is set. Wired through
             Save-UiState (footer) and Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag
             (prefix), both fed by an in-memory mirror
             ($script:UiFutileBootCount / $script:UiEscapeValveTripped)
             refreshed by Update-UiEscapeValveContext so the 10s
             patient-reboot ticks do not force a disk read each
             paint.
      Fix 6. Panic record at Logs\ESCAPE-VALVE-TRIPPED-<stamp>.json
             gives the receiving tech a single self-contained
             triage artifact. Written by Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff.
      Schema: wu-state.json carries three new fields with the
              standard sentinel-preserve pattern in Save-State so
              the ~15 existing call sites do not need to thread
              them through:
                FutileBootCount    (int,    sentinel -1 = preserve)
                EscapeValveTripped (bool,   sentinel $null = preserve)
                EscapeValveReason  (string, sentinel $null = preserve)
              Load-State treats missing fields as 0 / $false / '' for
              forward compatibility with legacy state files.
#>

[CmdletBinding()]
param(
    [switch]$FinalizeToOobe
)

$ErrorActionPreference = 'Continue'
$ProgressPreference    = 'SilentlyContinue'

$FbRoot           = 'C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase'
$LogDir           = Join-Path $FbRoot 'Logs'
$WaitMarker       = 'C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\FirstBase-Updates-Done.flag'
$CompleteMarker   = 'C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\FirstBase-Updates-Complete.flag'
$StateFile        = Join-Path $FbRoot 'wu-state.json'
$StatusFile       = Join-Path $FbRoot 'wu-status.json'
$HeartbeatFile    = Join-Path $FbRoot 'wu-loop-heartbeat.txt'
$EngineScriptPath = Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseWuEngine.ps1'
$TaskName         = 'FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoop'
$LogonTaskName    = 'FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoopOnLogon'
$Boot2TaskName    = 'FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoopBoot2'
$AnyLogonTaskName = 'FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoopAnyLogon'
$SplashWatcherBoot2Name = 'FirstBase\SplashWatcherBoot2'
$SplashTaskName   = 'FirstBase\UpdateSplash'
# 2026.05.20.2213v: one-shot Windows Activation settings (ms-settings:activation).
# RunOnce value name MUST NOT match Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub STEP 3 regex
# (?i)^!?FirstBase or the scrub deletes it. Command uses only cmd.exe/start
# (no FirstBase .ps1/.cmd — survives 2213t STEP 4 payload deletion).
$FbActivationRunOnceMarkerPath = 'C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\.activation-settings-runonce-armed'
$FbActivationRunOnceName       = 'OpenWindowsActivationSettingsOnce'
$FbActivationRunOnceCommand    = 'cmd.exe /c start "" "ms-settings:activation"'
# 2026.05.21.2216 (2217 supersedes deferred Sound for OOBE-overlay): legacy HKLM RunOnce
# OpenWindowsSoundSettingsOnce + marker paths — retained only so Clear-FbSoundRunOnce2216Artifacts
# can strip stale values; Invoke-SysprepToOobe no longer registers 2216 (immediate OOBE path instead).
$FbSoundRunOnceMarkerPath = 'C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\.sound-settings-runonce-armed'
$FbSoundRunOnceName       = 'OpenWindowsSoundSettingsOnce'
# Mirror paths on $script: for functions that reference $script: scope explicitly.
$script:FbActivationRunOnceMarkerPath = $FbActivationRunOnceMarkerPath
$script:FbActivationRunOnceName       = $FbActivationRunOnceName
$script:FbActivationRunOnceCommand    = $FbActivationRunOnceCommand
$script:FbSoundRunOnceMarkerPath = $FbSoundRunOnceMarkerPath
