# 2249: in-memory flag set by Test-FbDebugForceOobeHandoff when the developer's
# Ctrl+Shift+End hotkey bypass is triggered. Communicates the bypass intent from
# the main-loop call site to the STAGE 1 convergence gate inside
# Invoke-FbOobeHandoff (the flag file is consumed / removed at the main-loop site,
# so the gate reads this script-level variable instead of re-probing disk).
$script:FbDebugBypassActive = $false
# Script / product / payload revisions: Payload\FirstBaseVersion.ps1 (loaded after log init).
# 2026-05-13.1700-no-interactive-prompts:
#   Companion fix to SetupComplete.cmd. Field evidence from the test
#   device on 2026-05-12 showed a cmd window stuck at the pre-OOBE
#   "Getting ready" screen prompting for "TaskName:". Empirical
#   reproduction on the developer machine identified the root cause:
#     - SetupComplete.cmd was saved with LF-only line endings, which
#       caused cmd.exe to fall through past the script's 'exit /b 0'
#       at :DONE and execute every :SUB subroutine body below as
#       inline main-flow code. One of those subroutines is
#       :APPLY_ROBUST_TASK_SETTINGS, which when reached without a
#       'call' has %~1 = '' and passes an empty task name to its
#       inline PowerShell.
#     - The inline PowerShell ran
#         Get-ScheduledTask -TaskName '' | Set-ScheduledTask ...
#       Set-ScheduledTask is downstream in the pipeline with a
#       Mandatory -TaskName parameter. With an empty pipeline,
#       PowerShell prompts STDIN with "TaskName:". The redirection
#       'PowerShell ... >> $log 2>&1' does NOT silence prompts; they
#       go through the host's input-collection API. The console is
#       attached during specialize-pass via SetupComplete, so the
#       user saw the prompt directly.
#   Fixes in SetupComplete.cmd (build 1700):
#     A. File converted to CRLF line endings (prevents fall-through).
#     B. APPLY_ROBUST_TASK_SETTINGS inline PowerShell rewritten to
#        validate input, separate Get and Set into two statements,
#        null-check between them. Cannot prompt even with empty input.
#     C. powershell.exe runs with -NonInteractive (mandatory-param
#        prompts fail fast with parameter-binding exception).
#     D. Every cmd-side invocation that could prompt has '<nul'
#        appended (STDIN -> EOF).
#     E. schtasks /TR points at launcher .cmd shims dropped on disk
#        beforehand (eliminates the \"...\" quote-escape surface).
#     F. SELF-TEST mode (flag file C:\Setup\FirstBase\State\
#        .setupcomplete-selftest.flag) routes schtasks /Create to
#        /Query and dry-runs the PowerShell helper. Lets a tech run
#        SetupComplete.cmd on a dev box without touching task scheduler.
#   Fixes in this PowerShell script (build 1700):
#     G. Set-FbScheduledTaskRobustSettings rewritten with the SAME
#        defensive pattern as SetupComplete.cmd's APPLY_ROBUST_TASK_
#        SETTINGS. Validates input, separates Get/Set, null-checks
#        between them. Cannot prompt even if Test-FbAndRepairTriggers
#        runs in an attached-console session (e.g. during early-boot
#        debugging or under a tech-initiated 'powershell -File ...'
#        invocation).
# 2026.05.12.1030-no-cascade-final: the 8-stage reboot cascade
# (introduced 2026.05.11.0820 and patched through 2026.05.12.0830) has
# been REMOVED. Field evidence across 11 patches showed it cannot reboot
# this hardware - TrustedInstaller's shutdown veto survives every native
# reboot API, the logoff fallback, AND the SYSTEM-scoped scheduled-task
# escalation. The only reliable path is sysprep handoff (which uses
# Setup's own reboot mechanism, distinct from ours). Invoke-FbReboot
# now ALWAYS routes to OOBE handoff when installs are complete or the
# OS reports a reboot is owed; only a single shutdown /r /f /t 5 is
# attempted otherwise. The cascade infrastructure (Wait-FbReadyForReboot,
# Invoke-FbStageWithTimeout, 8 stages, logoff fallback, P/Invoke surface)
# is gone. See `NEVER-CASCADE.flag` kill-switch below.
$NeverCascadeFlag = 'C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\NEVER-CASCADE.flag'
$MaxFailureRetry  = 8
$MaxUpdateRetry   = 3
# Per-key reboot ceiling used by the absolute escape hatch. When the same
# logical update (keyed on KB when available, falling back to UpdateID)
# has caused this many reboots while remaining offered by WUA, we force-
# hide it via IUpdate.IsHidden=$true and mark it Completed so the loop
# can move on. This is a last-resort backstop; the normal retry ceiling
# usually catches it first.
$MaxKeyBootCount  = 5
$MaxStatusItems   = 250
$ScanTimeoutSec   = 1800
$ApplyTimeoutSec  = 7200
$RescanTimeoutSec = 900
# Lowered from 2 to 1 (2026-05-11). The prior confirmation-sweep
# reboot was always a no-op: by the time we reach it we already
# verified Test-FbWuRebootRequired is false AND the final scan
# returned zero updates. Forcing an extra reboot just to "be safe"
# burned ~5 minutes per device with no functional benefit. A real
# settling reboot (SSU+LCU) is still caught by the existing
# Test-FbWuRebootRequired branch below.
$RequiredEmptySweeps = 1
# Hard cap on the number of reboots that the loop will issue within
# a single update cycle. Beyond this we sysprep to OOBE and let
# normal Windows Update pick up whatever did not converge inside
# Audit Mode. Prevents pathological flapping (e.g. WUA re-offering
# the same superseded LCU every boot) from holding the device in
# Audit Mode forever.
$MaxRebootIterations = 3
$ValidInstallCodes = @(2, 3)
# WUA OperationResultCode orcAborted. Must be initialized: Driver apply
# compared $code -eq $script:FbWuResultAborted and threw when unset
# (PF12VZ2W / 5CD930865J / PF2X3W8J 12:18 dumps), zeroing installs.
$script:FbWuResultAborted = 5
$script:FbRebootWallStart = $null
$script:FbRebootWallMaxSeconds = 720

# Allowlist of HResults that must hard-stop the Critical bucket
# (skip remaining buckets within this iteration and reboot now).
# Everything NOT on this list - including unknown HResults - still
# allows greedy fall-through to Driver / Cumulative / Optional so
# we install as much as possible before consuming a reboot. These
# values match the Action='Reboot' entries in $BenignHResultMap.
$ExplicitRebootHResults = @(
    [int]0x00240008,  # WU_S_REBOOT_REQUIRED
    [int]0x80242014,  # WU_E_UH_POSTREBOOTSTILLPENDING
    [int]0x80242015,  # WU_E_UH_POSTREBOOTRESULTUNKNOWN
    [int]0x80070BC2,  # ERROR_SUCCESS_REBOOT_REQUIRED
    [int]0x80070BC9,  # ERROR_FAIL_REBOOT_REQUIRED
    [int]0x800F0922,  # CBS_E_INSTALLERS_FAILED
    [int]0x80248007   # WU_E_DS_NODATA (data store needs reboot to refresh)
)

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Benign WUA HResult classifier
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# When Invoke-FbWuApply hands back a per-update result whose ResultCode is
# not in $ValidInstallCodes, the previous build unconditionally treated it
# as a retryable failure. In the wild this leaves drivers and "already
# installed" rollups stuck at "Retrying" forever:
#
#   * WUA reports the update with a benign HResult (e.g. WU_E_NOT_APPLICABLE
#     for a driver that doesn't match this hardware revision).
#   * The next online scan stops offering that UpdateID, so the per-update
#     retry counter never ticks past 1 and never crosses $MaxUpdateRetry.
#   * The dashboard row is pinned at Status='Retrying', Retries=1 forever.
#
# To make termination deterministic, classify each non-success HResult and
# either treat it as success-equivalent (Skipped), force a reboot, or allow
# one cautious retry. Each entry below carries the wuerror.h symbol plus
# the precise reason we are not retrying it.
#
# WUA marshals HResult through COM as a signed Int32 (0x80240017 arrives
# as -2145124329). PowerShell hashtable lookup matches by value, and the
# numeric literal 0x80240017 inside [int] is the same negative number, so
# direct ContainsKey() lookup works without manual sign juggling.
$BenignHResultMap = @{
    # WU_E_DM_ALREADY_DOWNLOADED / "already installed" semantic. Treat as
    # success: WUA is telling us the package is already in the desired state.
    ([int]0x00240006) = @{ Action = 'Skip';   Reason = 'already installed' }
    # WU_S_ALREADY_INITIALIZED variant returned by some servicing-stack updates.
    ([int]0x00240007) = @{ Action = 'Skip';   Reason = 'already uninstalled / no-op' }
    # WU_S_REBOOT_REQUIRED surfaced as an HResult on the per-update record.
    # Force pending restart so the iteration epilogue reboots and re-scans.
    ([int]0x00240008) = @{ Action = 'Reboot'; Reason = 'WUA flagged reboot required to settle install' }
    # WU_S_UH_INSTALLSTILLPENDING - install actually completed in a prior pass
    # but the UH didn't get to update its books. Same handling as already-installed.
    ([int]0x0024001D) = @{ Action = 'Skip';   Reason = 'install pending from prior pass' }

    # WU_E_NOT_APPLICABLE - by far the most common driver case. The WHQL
    # driver was offered but ApplicabilityRules said no on this hardware.
    # Retrying it forever is pointless.
    ([int]0x80240017) = @{ Action = 'Skip';   Reason = 'not applicable to this device (driver/hardware mismatch)' }
    # WU_E_NOT_SUPPORTED - same family as NotApplicable; OS SKU mismatch.
    ([int]0x80240037) = @{ Action = 'Skip';   Reason = 'not supported on this OS SKU' }
    # WU_E_SOURCE_ABSENT - driver INF source missing; nothing the loop can fix.
    ([int]0x8024002D) = @{ Action = 'Skip';   Reason = 'install source absent (driver INF missing)' }

    # WU_E_UH_POSTREBOOTSTILLPENDING - install requires a reboot before WUA
    # will let it complete. Force pending restart instead of marking Retry.
    ([int]0x80242014) = @{ Action = 'Reboot'; Reason = 'reboot required to complete install (post-reboot still pending)' }
    # WU_E_UH_POSTREBOOTRESULTUNKNOWN - similar; reboot lets WUA settle.
    ([int]0x80242015) = @{ Action = 'Reboot'; Reason = 'post-reboot result unknown; reboot to settle' }
    # ERROR_SUCCESS_REBOOT_REQUIRED from the underlying installer (MSI/MSU).
    ([int]0x80070BC2) = @{ Action = 'Reboot'; Reason = 'installer reported reboot required' }
    # ERROR_FAIL_REBOOT_REQUIRED.
    ([int]0x80070BC9) = @{ Action = 'Reboot'; Reason = 'installer reported fail-reboot-required' }

    # WU_E_NO_INTERACTIVE_USER - some drivers refuse to install without an
    # interactive desktop session. Audit Mode is auto-logged-in but WUA
    # sometimes still rejects the install. No amount of retrying inside
    # the loop helps; sysprep/OOBE will retry on first user logon anyway.
    ([int]0x80240020) = @{ Action = 'Skip';   Reason = 'no interactive user available for install' }
    # WU_E_INSTALL_NOT_ALLOWED - administratively blocked.
    ([int]0x8024200B) = @{ Action = 'Skip';   Reason = 'install blocked by policy / administrative gate' }

    # 0x80246007 WU_E_DM_NOTDOWNLOADED / WU_E_NOT_DOWNLOADED moved
    # to the 2026.05.12.1830 block below (re-mapped from Retry to
    # Reboot based on field evidence that the cautious in-boot
    # retry never succeeds - download has to be re-staged after
    # a reboot for the install to take).

    # --- Added 2026-05-11 to break the "two Critical updates pinned at
    # Retrying" deadlock when WUA returns CBS or installer-side codes
    # that aren't in wuerror.h but are observed in the wild. ---

    # CBS_E_INSTALLERS_FAILED_TO_RESPOND - one or more component
    # installers failed. Reboot frequently lets CBS re-stage the
    # payload cleanly so the retry after reboot succeeds.
    # 2026.05.12.1830: confirmed Reboot mapping in coverage table.
    ([int]0x800F0922) = @{ Action = 'Reboot'; Reason = 'CBS_E_INSTALLERS_FAILED_TO_RESPOND - usually transient; succeeds on retry after reboot' }
    # CBS_E_STORE_CORRUPTION - servicing store corrupt. Cannot fix
    # from loop; terminal skip with a warning.
    ([int]0x800F0831) = @{ Action = 'Skip';   Reason = 'CBS store corruption; cannot fix from loop' }
    # CBS_E_INVALID_CONFIG - corrupt or unsupported package config.
    ([int]0x800F0905) = @{ Action = 'Skip';   Reason = 'CBS invalid package config; cannot fix from loop' }
    # ERROR_INSTALL_FAILURE - the generic "installer returned non-zero".
    # Very common, often transient. Allow one cautious retry, then skip.
    ([int]0x80070643) = @{ Action = 'Retry';  Reason = 'generic installer failure; one retry then skip' }
    # WU_E_DS_NODATA - data store has no record of the update. Refreshing
    # via reboot + rescan usually clears it.
    ([int]0x80248007) = @{ Action = 'Reboot'; Reason = 'WUA data store has no data; reboot to refresh' }
    # WU_E_SETUP_HANDLER_EXEC_FAILURE - update handler exec failed.
    # Allow one retry; if it persists the generic ceiling will catch it.
    ([int]0x8024D013) = @{ Action = 'Retry';  Reason = 'setup handler exec failure; one retry then skip' }
    # WU_E_PT_HTTP_STATUS_SERVICE_UNAVAIL - transient 503 from the WU
    # endpoint. Pure retry; do not skip even at the ceiling because the
    # normal generic-failure path will then take over.
    ([int]0x80244022) = @{ Action = 'Retry';  Reason = 'WU endpoint returned HTTP 503; transient' }

    # --- 2026.05.12.1830-patient-shutdown-and-hresult-coverage. ---
    # Field evidence from the test device (build 1700) showed the
    # silent-bypass class of bug is fixed but most updates now sit at
    # 'Retrying' instead of progressing. The HResults below cover the
    # documented CBS / SXS / installer transient failure modes seen on
    # freshly-imaged hardware. Each maps to the correct action so the
    # loop labels them (Pending restart -> reboot -> retry) instead of
    # grinding on 3 in-boot retries that cannot possibly succeed without
    # a reboot to settle CBS state.

    # ERROR_SXS_COMPONENT_STORE_CORRUPT - needs reboot to settle CBS,
    # then retries clean. Previously mapped to Skip; field evidence on
    # the 1700 test device showed this HResult coming back transiently
    # from updates that DO install successfully after the next reboot
    # (CBS finishes its mid-transaction cleanup). Re-mapped to Reboot.
    ([int]0x80073712) = @{ Action = 'Reboot'; Reason = 'ERROR_SXS_COMPONENT_STORE_CORRUPT - needs reboot to settle CBS, then retries clean' }
    # WU_E_NOT_DOWNLOADED - race; download apparently complete but
    # installer can't find files. Re-download after reboot. Previously
    # mapped to Retry (one cautious in-boot retry); re-mapped to Reboot
    # so we let the reboot re-stage downloads instead of grinding.
    ([int]0x80246007) = @{ Action = 'Reboot'; Reason = 'WU_E_NOT_DOWNLOADED - re-download after reboot' }
    # CBS_E_SOURCE_MISSING - needs reboot to settle CBS state, then retry.
    ([int]0x800F081F) = @{ Action = 'Reboot'; Reason = 'CBS_E_SOURCE_MISSING - needs reboot to settle' }
    # ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED - sometimes returned when CBS is mid-
    # transaction; transient and clears after reboot.
    ([int]0x80070032) = @{ Action = 'Reboot'; Reason = 'ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED - CBS mid-transaction; transient' }
    # ERROR_SXS_ASSEMBLY_MISSING - dependency on a sibling update;
    # succeeds on retry after sibling installs.
    ([int]0x80073701) = @{ Action = 'Reboot'; Reason = 'ERROR_SXS_ASSEMBLY_MISSING - sibling update will provide on retry after reboot' }
    # E_ACCESSDENIED - usually CBS holding a handle on the target file
    # or registry hive. Reboot releases CBS handles and the retry then
    # installs cleanly.
    ([int]0x80070005) = @{ Action = 'Reboot'; Reason = 'E_ACCESSDENIED - usually CBS holding a handle; reboot releases it' }
    # 0x80240020 (WU_E_NO_INTERACTIVE_USER) - installer needs UI (rare;
    # skip cleanly). Already mapped to Skip above at 0x80240020; the
    # build 1830 coverage table confirms that mapping.
    # 0x80070BC9 (ERROR_FAIL_REBOOT_REQUIRED) - explicit reboot required.
    # Already mapped to Reboot above at 0x80070BC9; build 1830
    # confirms that mapping.
}

function Get-BenignHResultEntry {
    param([int]$HResult)
    if ($HResult -eq 0) { return $null }
    if ($BenignHResultMap.ContainsKey($HResult)) { return $BenignHResultMap[$HResult] }
    return $null
}

function Get-FbUpdateRetryBudget {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Resolve the per-update retry ceiling given its KEY and the last
        observed HResult.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.12.1830-patient-shutdown-and-hresult-coverage. Different
        failure modes deserve different per-boot retry budgets:
          - HResult in $BenignHResultMap with Action='Reboot':
              In-boot retries cannot fix it. The fix is a reboot then a
              fresh attempt. Cap the ceiling at 1 so we don't burn the
              full $MaxUpdateRetry budget in a single boot on something
              that can only succeed AFTER reboot.
          - Anything else (unknown HResult, COM apply exception, etc.):
              Stay at $MaxUpdateRetry. Without a known reboot-recoverable
              signal we don't know whether reboot will help, so the
              cautious 3-retry budget is correct.
        Persistence: caller stores the resolved budget per-key in
        $state.UpdateRetryBudget so a future iteration in the same boot
        (or on a subsequent boot) honors the lowered ceiling.
        Returns an int. Never throws.
    #>
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$Key,
        [int]$HResult = 0,
        [hashtable]$BudgetMap = $null
    )
    # First: a budget value already persisted for this key wins.
    # That covers the case where a prior iteration / prior boot
    # already classified this update as reboot-recoverable.
    if ($null -ne $BudgetMap -and $BudgetMap.ContainsKey($Key)) {
        try { return [int]$BudgetMap[$Key] } catch {}
    }
    # Second: classify by HResult.
    if ($HResult -ne 0 -and $BenignHResultMap.ContainsKey($HResult)) {
        $entry = $BenignHResultMap[$HResult]
        if ($entry -and [string]$entry.Action -eq 'Reboot') {
            return 1
        }
    }
    return $MaxUpdateRetry
}

function Format-Hex32 {
    # Render a signed Int32 HResult as the conventional 0xHHHHHHHH form
    # (e.g. -2145124329 -> 0x80240017). A direct [uint32]$Value cast
    # throws on Windows PowerShell 5.1 for negative ints; reinterpret
    # the bit pattern via BitConverter to get the unsigned form safely.
    param([int]$Value)
    $u = [System.BitConverter]::ToUInt32([System.BitConverter]::GetBytes($Value), 0)
    return ('0x{0:X8}' -f $u)
}

# 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: explicit terminal
# HResults that should mark an update as "Skipped (terminal failure)"
# AFTER the per-update retry ceiling has been crossed, even if the
# HResult is not in $BenignHResultMap. These codes have all been
# documented as "no point retrying further" in MS docs / WUA history:
#   0x80070643 generic install failure (after N attempts only)
#   0x80240020 user-context required - audit mode cannot supply one
#   0x80246001 not applicable to this device (driver mismatch etc.)
$script:FbWuTerminalAfterRetriesHResults = @(
    -2147023293,  # 0x80070643 ERROR_INSTALL_FAILURE
    -2145124320,  # 0x80240020 WU_E_NO_INTERACTIVE_USER
    -2145107967   # 0x80246001 WU_E_DM_INVALIDHASH (often: "not applicable")
)

# 2026.06.12.2276-fix-0x80246017-terminal Fix A: HResults that are IMMEDIATELY
# terminal on the FIRST failure — no retry budget is consumed at all. These are
# authorization / session-context errors where retrying in the same SYSTEM /
# non-interactive session cannot succeed; WU will return the same code every time.
#   0x80246017 WU_E_DM_UNAUTHORIZED / Download Manager unauthorized — feature
#              upgrades require user authorization; WU blocks automated download
#              in SYSTEM/non-interactive sessions. Will fail identically on retry.
$script:FbWuImmediateTerminalHResults = @(
    -2145099753   # 0x80246017 WU_E_DM_UNAUTHORIZED (download blocked in SYSTEM session)
)

function Get-FbWuOutcome {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Classify a single per-update apply result into the 4-class
        outcome model used by the multi-pass full-scope loop.
    .DESCRIPTION
        Location: Payload\Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1 (NOT in
        FirstBaseWuEngine.ps1; the 2210 verifier mistakenly cited
        the engine. Annotation added in 2211 cosmetic scrub.)

        2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress. Replaces the
        ad-hoc decision tree inside Invoke-FbBucketApply with a
        single classifier so the work-queue mutation logic, the
        UI status text, and the dashboard messaging all agree on
        what each per-update result means.

        Returns a hashtable:
          {
            Class       : 'Success'|'Reboot'|'Transient'|'Terminal'|'Unknown'
            Status      : UI status string for wu-status.json row
            Detail      : UI Detail prefix
            BumpReal    : $true if this row counts as a real install
                          (ResultCode in {2,3} ONLY; the off-by-one
                          fix the user explicitly called out)
            BumpRetry   : $true if this row consumes one retry slot
            Persist     : $true if the row stays on the work queue
                          for the next pass (Reboot/Transient/Unknown)
            ForceHidden : $true if the row should be force-hidden
                          via IUpdate.IsHidden (Terminal escape hatch)
            Reason      : human-readable classification reason
          }

        Decision rules:
          - ResultCode in {2,3} (orcSucceeded, orcSucceededWithErrors)
              -> Class=Success, BumpReal=true. Removed from queue.
          - HResult in $BenignHResultMap with Action='Reboot'
              OR HResult in $ExplicitRebootHResults
              -> Class='Reboot' (force reboot then retry next pass).
                 Stays on queue; real-install-evidence is recorded
                 because the installer DID run and produced a known
                 reboot-recoverable HResult.
          - HResult in $BenignHResultMap with Action='Retry'
              OR HResult network-flavored (transient list below)
              -> Class='Transient' (keep in queue, retry on next
                 pass). Stays on queue with retry++.
          - HResult in $BenignHResultMap with Action='Skip'
              -> Class='Terminal' (move to GivenUpUpdateIds, never
                 retry). Removed from queue. The caller is
                 responsible for adding the UpdateId to
                 GivenUpSet so subsequent fresh scans honor the
                 prior decision (Update-FbWuWorkQueueFromBucket-
                 Results does this in 2210+).
          - HResult in $script:FbWuTerminalAfterRetriesHResults
              AND retry count already at the ceiling
              -> Class='Terminal'. Same as Skip case but reason is
                 "exhausted retries on a known-terminal HResult".
          - Anything else
              -> Class='Unknown'. Treated as Transient (stays on
                 queue with retry++) but logged loudly so the
                 next 2200 incident gives us data to expand the
                 BenignHResultMap.

        2026.05.13.2210-fullscope-multipass-progress-fixes Critical
        Fix 3 wording reconciliation: the spec's textual claim
        that Action='Skip' meant "Transient" was a doc bug in the
        spec. The actual BenignHResultMap (defined at the top of
        Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1) uses Action='Retry' for
        transient/network-style HResults like 0x80244010, and
        Action='Skip' for genuinely-terminal "do not retry"
        HResults. This function's classification matches that
        intent: Action='Retry' -> Transient, Action='Skip' ->
        Terminal. No behavior change in 2210; doc clarification
        only.

        Never throws. If $HResult or $ResultCode are bizarre, the
        function falls through to Class='Unknown'.
    #>
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [int]$ResultCode,
        [int]$HResult = 0,
        [int]$RetryCount = 0,
        [int]$RetryCeiling = 3
    )

    $hex = Format-Hex32 -Value $HResult

    if ($ResultCode -eq 2 -or $ResultCode -eq 3) {
        return @{
            Class       = 'Success'
            Status      = 'Completed'
            Detail      = ('Installed (ResultCode={0})' -f $ResultCode)
            BumpReal    = $true
            BumpRetry   = $false
            Persist     = $false
            ForceHidden = $false
            Reason      = 'orcSucceeded / orcSucceededWithErrors'
        }
    }

    $benign = Get-BenignHResultEntry -HResult $HResult
    if ($benign) {
        $action = [string]$benign.Action
        $reason = [string]$benign.Reason
        if ($action -eq 'Reboot') {
            return @{
                Class       = 'Reboot'
                Status      = 'Pending reboot - will reapply next pass'
                Detail      = ('Reboot required: HRESULT {0} {1}' -f $hex, $reason)
                BumpReal    = $true
                BumpRetry   = $false
                Persist     = $true
                ForceHidden = $false
                Reason      = ('BenignHResultMap Action=Reboot ({0})' -f $reason)
            }
        } elseif ($action -eq 'Skip') {
            return @{
                Class       = 'Terminal'
                Status      = 'Skipped (terminal failure)'
                Detail      = ('Skipped: HRESULT {0} {1}' -f $hex, $reason)
                BumpReal    = $false
                BumpRetry   = $false
                Persist     = $false
                ForceHidden = $false
                Reason      = ('BenignHResultMap Action=Skip ({0})' -f $reason)
            }
        } elseif ($action -eq 'Retry') {
            $exhausted = ($RetryCount + 1) -gt $RetryCeiling
            if ($exhausted) {
                return @{
                    Class       = 'Terminal'
                    Status      = 'Skipped (terminal failure)'
                    Detail      = ('Skipped after {0} retries on benign-Retry HRESULT {1}' -f $RetryCeiling, $hex)
                    BumpReal    = $false
                    BumpRetry   = $false
                    Persist     = $false
                    ForceHidden = $false
                    Reason      = ('BenignHResultMap Action=Retry exhausted at {0} retries' -f $RetryCeiling)
                }
            }
            return @{
                Class       = 'Transient'
                Status      = 'Retry next boot'
                Detail      = ('Transient: HRESULT {0} {1} (retry {2}/{3})' -f $hex, $reason, ($RetryCount + 1), $RetryCeiling)
                BumpReal    = $false
                BumpRetry   = $true
                Persist     = $true
                ForceHidden = $false
                Reason      = ('BenignHResultMap Action=Retry ({0})' -f $reason)
            }
        }
    }

    if ($ExplicitRebootHResults -contains $HResult) {
        return @{
            Class       = 'Reboot'
            Status      = 'Pending reboot - will reapply next pass'
            Detail      = ('Reboot required (explicit list): HRESULT {0}' -f $hex)
            BumpReal    = $true
            BumpRetry   = $false
            Persist     = $true
            ForceHidden = $false
            Reason      = 'ExplicitRebootHResults match'
        }
    }

    if ($script:FbWuTerminalAfterRetriesHResults -contains $HResult -and ($RetryCount + 1) -gt $RetryCeiling) {
        return @{
            Class       = 'Terminal'
            Status      = 'Skipped (terminal failure)'
            Detail      = ('Skipped after {0} retries on terminal HRESULT {1}' -f $RetryCeiling, $hex)
            BumpReal    = $false
            BumpRetry   = $false
            Persist     = $false
            ForceHidden = $false
            Reason      = ('Terminal-after-retries HResult exhausted at {0} retries' -f $RetryCeiling)
        }
    }

    if (($RetryCount + 1) -gt $RetryCeiling) {
        return @{
            Class       = 'Unknown'
            Status      = 'Skipped (terminal failure)'
            Detail      = ('Skipped after {0} retries on unknown HRESULT {1}' -f $RetryCeiling, $hex)
            BumpReal    = $false
            BumpRetry   = $false
            Persist     = $false
            ForceHidden = $false
            Reason      = ('Unknown HResult exhausted at {0} retries; treating as Terminal for queue purposes' -f $RetryCeiling)
        }
    }

    return @{
        Class       = 'Unknown'
        Status      = 'Retry next boot'
        Detail      = ('Unknown HRESULT {0} (retry {1}/{2})' -f $hex, ($RetryCount + 1), $RetryCeiling)
        BumpReal    = $false
        BumpRetry   = $true
        Persist     = $true
        ForceHidden = $false
        Reason      = 'Unknown HResult; treating as Transient until retry ceiling'
    }
}

function Write-FbDriverFirstEncounterDiag {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Capture a per-driver result snapshot the FIRST time the
        loop applies any Driver-bucket update on this image.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress. The build
        1915 incident never reached the Driver bucket - the loop
        short-circuited to OOBE handoff after Critical. We have
        ZERO field data on driver HResults / IUpdate2/IUpdate5
        properties for this hardware. This helper writes a single
        INSTALL-DIAG-driver-firstencounter-*.json per driver result
        on the very first Driver bucket apply of this cycle so the
        next 2200 incident gives us data to expand BenignHResultMap
        with driver-specific HResult mappings.

        Only writes if $script:_FbDriverFirstEncounterDoneKeys does
        not already contain this driver's UpdateID. Idempotent
        across iterations within the same boot; resets on reboot
        because $script: vars are not persisted.

        Never throws. Best-effort COM property reads (IUpdate2 /
        IUpdate5 may not be implementable on every driver row).
    #>
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$UpdateId,
        [string]$Title = '',
        [string]$KB = '',
        [int]$ResultCode = -1,
        [int]$HResult = 0,
        $UpdateComObject = $null,
        $UpdateExceptionInfo = $null
    )

    # 2213d Bug E fix: under StrictMode 2.0+ (and on PowerShell hosts
    # with set-strict-mode in profile), the pre-2213d guard
    #   if (-not $script:_FbDriverFirstEncounterDoneKeys) { ... }
    # threw a terminating error - "The variable
    # '$script:_FbDriverFirstEncounterDoneKeys' cannot be retrieved
    # because it has not been set." - on every driver install in
    # the first Driver bucket apply of each boot (76+ times per pass
    # in F:\fb-dump-final-0635 WU log). The Get-Variable -Scope Script
    # -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue idiom probes for existence without
    # triggering the retrieve-error path. Once initialized the
    # original Contains/Add logic is unchanged.
    if (-not (Get-Variable -Name '_FbDriverFirstEncounterDoneKeys' -Scope Script -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)) {
        $script:_FbDriverFirstEncounterDoneKeys = New-Object 'System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]' ([System.StringComparer]::OrdinalIgnoreCase)
    }
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($UpdateId)) { return }
    if ($script:_FbDriverFirstEncounterDoneKeys.Contains($UpdateId)) { return }
    [void]$script:_FbDriverFirstEncounterDoneKeys.Add($UpdateId)

    $stamp = (Get-Date).ToString('yyyyMMdd-HHmmss')
    $diagDir = Join-Path $LogDir 'PanicDumps'
    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $diagDir)) {
            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $diagDir -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
        }
    } catch {}
    $safeId = ($UpdateId -replace '[^a-zA-Z0-9\-]', '_')
    if ($safeId.Length -gt 60) { $safeId = $safeId.Substring(0,60) }
    $diagPath = Join-Path $diagDir ("INSTALL-DIAG-driver-firstencounter-{0}-{1}.json" -f $stamp, $safeId)

    $driverProps = @{
        DriverClass    = ''
        DriverModel    = ''
        DriverProvider = ''
        DriverVerDate  = ''
        DriverHwIds    = @()
    }
    if ($UpdateComObject) {
        try { $driverProps.DriverClass    = [string]$UpdateComObject.DriverClass    } catch {}
        try { $driverProps.DriverModel    = [string]$UpdateComObject.DriverModel    } catch {}
        try { $driverProps.DriverProvider = [string]$UpdateComObject.DriverProvider } catch {}
        try { $driverProps.DriverVerDate  = [string]$UpdateComObject.DriverVerDate  } catch {}
        try {
            $hwids = @()
            foreach ($h in @($UpdateComObject.DriverHardwareID)) {
                if ($h) { $hwids += [string]$h }
            }
            $driverProps.DriverHwIds = $hwids
        } catch {}
    }

    $diag = [pscustomobject]@{
        Timestamp     = (Get-Date).ToString('o')
        ScriptVersion = $ScriptVersion
        Bucket        = 'Driver'
        UpdateId      = $UpdateId
        Title         = $Title
        KB            = $KB
        ResultCode    = $ResultCode
        HResult       = $HResult
        HResultHex    = (Format-Hex32 -Value $HResult)
        DriverProps   = $driverProps
        ExceptionInfo = $UpdateExceptionInfo
    }
    try {
        $diag | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 6 | Set-Content -Path $diagPath -Encoding UTF8 -Force
        Write-FbLog ("Write-FbDriverFirstEncounterDiag: wrote {0}" -f $diagPath) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Write-FbDriverFirstEncounterDiag: write failed: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

function New-FbWuWorkQueueRow {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Build a hashtable row for the persistent WorkQueue.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress. The work
        queue is the system-of-record for "what updates do we
        still owe the device after this pass". Save-State accepts
        an array of these rows. Schema enforced here so callers
        cannot accidentally produce malformed entries.
    #>
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$UpdateId,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$Bucket,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$Status,
        [int]$RetryCount = 0,
        [int]$LastHResult = 0,
        [int]$FirstSeenBoot = 0
    )
    return @{
        UpdateId      = [string]$UpdateId
        Bucket        = [string]$Bucket
        Status        = [string]$Status
        RetryCount    = [int]$RetryCount
        LastHResult   = [int]$LastHResult
        FirstSeenBoot = [int]$FirstSeenBoot
    }
}

function Get-FbRetryKey {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Pick a stable retry-map key for an offered update.
    .DESCRIPTION
        WUA may bump an update's UpdateID/RevisionNumber across rescans
        (especially for cumulative LCUs that get superseded mid-cycle).
        Keying the retry counter on UpdateID then lets the count reset
        to zero every scan, so the retry ceiling never trips and the
        dashboard pins at Retrying forever.

        To make accumulation stick across revisions, key on the KB
        article number when WUA gives us one (cumulative + servicing
        stack + most security updates publish KB). Fall back to the
        UpdateID only when no KB is present (some driver records).

        Always returns a non-null string; never throws.
    #>
    param([Parameter(Mandatory)] $Update)
    try {
        $kb = $null
        if ($Update.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'KB') {
            $kb = [string]$Update.KB
        }
        if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($kb)) {
            return ('KB:' + $kb.ToUpperInvariant())
        }
        if ($Update.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'KBArticleIDs') {
            $list = @($Update.KBArticleIDs | Where-Object { $_ })
            if ($list.Count -gt 0) {
                return ('KB:' + ([string]$list[0]).ToUpperInvariant())
            }
        }
    } catch {}
    $uid = ''
    try { $uid = [string]$Update.UpdateId } catch {}
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($uid)) {
        $uid = [guid]::NewGuid().ToString()
    }
    return ('UID:' + $uid)
}

function Format-FbDetail {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Build a screen-readable Detail string for the dashboard row.
    .DESCRIPTION
        Even with file-level logs locked away behind the splash, the
        dashboard's Detail column is the user's only window into the
        actual failure mode. Pack it with everything we can:

          HRESULT 0x80070643 (-2147023293) install failure | retry 2/3

        Always returns a non-empty string. The HResult portion is
        omitted only when $HResult is 0 (i.e. WUA returned success
        and we just want a status note).
    #>
    param(
        [int]$HResult = 0,
        [int]$Retry = 0,
        [int]$MaxRetry = 0,
        [string]$Reason = '',
        [string]$Prefix = ''
    )
    $sb = New-Object System.Text.StringBuilder
    if ($Prefix) { [void]$sb.Append($Prefix) }
    if ($HResult -ne 0) {
        if ($sb.Length -gt 0) { [void]$sb.Append(' ') }
        $hex = Format-Hex32 -Value $HResult
        [void]$sb.Append(("HRESULT {0} ({1})" -f $hex, $HResult))
        if ($Reason) {
            [void]$sb.Append(' ')
            [void]$sb.Append($Reason)
        }
    } elseif ($Reason) {
        if ($sb.Length -gt 0) { [void]$sb.Append(' ') }
        [void]$sb.Append($Reason)
    }
    if ($MaxRetry -gt 0 -and $Retry -gt 0) {
        if ($sb.Length -gt 0) { [void]$sb.Append(' | ') }
        [void]$sb.Append(("retry {0}/{1}" -f $Retry, $MaxRetry))
    }
    if ($sb.Length -eq 0) { [void]$sb.Append('In progress') }
    return $sb.ToString()
}

function Format-FbTitle {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Append [0xHEX] to a title once an HResult is known.
    .DESCRIPTION
        The Detail column truncates with CharacterEllipsis on narrow
        rows. Stamping the hex HResult onto the Title field guarantees
        the user can read at least the hex code without log access,
        even if Detail is truncated. Idempotent: if Title already ends
        in [0xHHHHHHHH] we leave it alone, so the suffix doesn't keep
        growing across updates.
    #>
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$Title,
        [int]$HResult = 0
    )
    if ($HResult -eq 0) { return $Title }
    $hex = Format-Hex32 -Value $HResult
    $suffix = ('  [' + $hex + ']')
    if ($Title -match '\s\[0x[0-9A-Fa-f]{8}\]$') {
        return ($Title -replace '\s\[0x[0-9A-Fa-f]{8}\]$', $suffix)
    }
    return ($Title + $suffix)
}

if (-not (Test-Path $LogDir)) { New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $LogDir -Force | Out-Null }

$timestamp = Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd-HHmmss'
$LogPath   = Join-Path $LogDir "WU-$timestamp.log"

try { Start-Transcript -Path $LogPath -Append -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null } catch {}

# One ID per loop process for log correlation (WU transcript + host lines).
$script:FbRunId = [guid]::NewGuid().ToString('N')

# --- FirstBase version manifest (see Payload\FirstBaseVersion.ps1; cmd mirror: FirstBaseVersion.cmd) ---
$fbVerPs1 = Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseVersion.ps1'
if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $fbVerPs1)) {
    $fbVerPs1 = Join-Path $PSScriptRoot 'FirstBaseVersion.ps1'
}
if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $fbVerPs1) {
    . $fbVerPs1
} else {
    $FirstBaseProductVersion  = '1.0.0'
    $FirstBasePayloadRevision = 'unknown'
    $FirstBaseScriptVersion   = @{}
}
$script:FirstBaseProductVersion  = $FirstBaseProductVersion
$script:FirstBasePayloadRevision = $FirstBasePayloadRevision
$thisLeaf = 'Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1'
try {
    if ($MyInvocation.MyCommand.Path) { $thisLeaf = Split-Path -Leaf $MyInvocation.MyCommand.Path }
} catch {}
$script:ThisScriptVersion = [string]$FirstBaseScriptVersion[$thisLeaf]
if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($script:ThisScriptVersion)) { $script:ThisScriptVersion = $FirstBaseProductVersion }
$ScriptVersion = $script:ThisScriptVersion

function Write-FbLog {
    param([string]$Message, [string]$Level = 'INFO')
    $rid = $script:FbRunId
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($rid)) { $rid = 'unset' }
    $line = "[{0}] [{1}] [runId={2}] {3}" -f (Get-Date -Format 'HH:mm:ss'), $Level, $rid, $Message
    Write-Host $line
    try { [Console]::Out.Flush() } catch {}
    try { [Console]::Error.Flush() } catch {}
}

function Sync-FbWuTranscriptFlush {
    param([string]$Reason = 'transcript flush')
    try { Write-FbLog $Reason 'WARN' } catch {}
    try { [Console]::Out.Flush() } catch {}
    try { [Console]::Error.Flush() } catch {}
    # Recycle Start-Transcript so the last line is on disk before sysprep/shutdown can kill this process (PF12VZ2W truncated WU log).
    try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
    try {
        if ($LogPath) {
            Start-Transcript -Path $LogPath -Append -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
        }
    } catch {}
    try { [Console]::Out.Flush() } catch {}
}

function Get-FbWuLogTailShared {
    param(
        [string]$Path,
        [int]$Tail = 50
    )
    $out = @()
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($Path) -or -not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $Path)) { return $out }
    $fs = $null
    $sr = $null
    try {
        $fs = [System.IO.File]::Open($Path, [System.IO.FileMode]::Open, [System.IO.FileAccess]::Read, [System.IO.FileShare]::ReadWrite)
        $sr = New-Object System.IO.StreamReader($fs)
        $all = $sr.ReadToEnd()
        $arr = @($all -split "`r?`n")
        if ($arr.Count -gt $Tail) {
            $out = @($arr[($arr.Count - $Tail)..($arr.Count - 1)])
        } else {
            $out = $arr
        }
    } catch {
        try { $out = @(Get-Content -LiteralPath $Path -Tail $Tail -ErrorAction Stop) } catch { $out = @() }
    } finally {
        try { if ($sr) { $sr.Dispose() } } catch {}
        try { if ($fs) { $fs.Dispose() } } catch {}
    }
    return $out
}

function Invoke-FbForceOsRestart {
    param([string]$Reason = 'force-restart')
    Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbForceOsRestart: shutdown.exe /r /t 0 /f ({0}). Never /s." -f $Reason) 'ERROR'
    try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message 'Forcing restart now (reboot ladder time cap).' } catch {}
    try { Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Rebooting' } catch {}
    $exe = Join-Path $env:WINDIR 'System32\shutdown.exe'
    try {
        $p = Start-Process -FilePath $exe -ArgumentList @('/r', '/t', '0', '/f') -PassThru -WindowStyle Hidden -ErrorAction Stop
        if ($p) { $null = $p.WaitForExit(8000) }
    } catch {
        try { & $exe /r /t 0 /f | Out-Null } catch {}
    }
}

function Test-FbRebootWallHit {
    if (-not $script:FbRebootWallStart) { return $false }
    $maxSec = 720
    try { $maxSec = [int]$script:FbRebootWallMaxSeconds } catch { $maxSec = 720 }
    if ($maxSec -lt 60) { $maxSec = 720 }
    $elapsed = 0
    try { $elapsed = ((Get-Date) - [datetime]$script:FbRebootWallStart).TotalSeconds } catch { return $false }
    return ($elapsed -ge $maxSec)
}

# 2026.05.13.2213c-onlogon-primary-and-defender-disable: defined
# early (right after Write-FbLog) so EVERY exit 1 path - including
# the pre-loop engine-missing check, mutex contention failure, and
# any early-startup helper-init crash - can call this for the
# bulletproof Defender restore. The function only depends on
# Write-FbLog (already defined above), $script:DefenderStateFlag
# (a path; references resolve lazily at call time) and the OS
# Set-MpPreference cmdlet (built-in on every Windows 11 build).
function Set-FbDefenderRealtime {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Toggle Microsoft Defender real-time monitoring during the
        install pass. Bulletproof - never throws, never blocks the
        loop, always tries to write the marker.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.2213c-onlogon-primary-and-defender-disable. Field
        evidence: Defender's real-time scanner serializes file IO
        with .cab / .mum / .manifest / TI staging during a multi-KB
        cumulative install, which slows install passes substantially
        on this hardware. The pre-OOBE device is offline / airgapped
        during the audit-mode update window so there is no realistic
        threat surface to protect against. We disable real-time
        monitoring BEFORE the first scan-then-install pass and
        restore it BEFORE the OOBE handoff so the device ships to
        the end user with Defender fully enabled.

        Restore must fire on EVERY exit path: OOBE handoff, escape
        valve trip, Tier 7 panic dump, CBS-stuck give-up, version-
        skew exit, every plain exit 1. The disk marker file
        $script:DefenderStateFlag is the system-of-record: writes
        "disabled" when this function disables, "restored" when it
        restores. On loop startup the marker is read; if it says
        "disabled" but InstallsCompleted=$true on disk, we restore
        immediately as a recovery from a prior crash.

        Parameters:
          -Enabled $false -> sets DisableRealtimeMonitoring=$true
                             (i.e. turns Defender OFF for installs)
          -Enabled $true  -> sets DisableRealtimeMonitoring=$false
                             (i.e. turns Defender ON, restore path)
          -Reason        -> short string for log telemetry only
                             ('pre-install', 'OOBE-handoff',
                             'escape-valve', 'tier7-panic',
                             'cbs-stuck-give-up', 'version-skew',
                             'startup-recovery', etc.)

        Logs INFO on success and WARN on failure via Write-FbLog.
        Always returns - never throws.
    #>
    [CmdletBinding()]
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [bool]$Enabled,
        [string]$Reason = ''
    )

    $disable = (-not $Enabled)
    $markerState = if ($Enabled) { 'restored' } else { 'disabled' }

    try {
        Set-MpPreference -DisableRealtimeMonitoring $disable -ErrorAction Stop
        try {
            Write-FbLog ("Set-FbDefenderRealtime: Defender real-time monitoring set to {0} (DisableRealtimeMonitoring={1}, Reason={2})" -f ($(if ($Enabled) { 'ENABLED' } else { 'DISABLED' })), $disable, $Reason) 'INFO'
        } catch {}
    } catch {
        try {
            Write-FbLog ("Set-FbDefenderRealtime: Set-MpPreference threw (Reason={0}): {1} - continuing without blocking the loop." -f $Reason, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        } catch {}
    }

    # Best-effort marker file write so the loop can detect a stuck
    # "disabled" state on startup after a crash. Independent of the
    # Set-MpPreference call - we write the marker even if the cmdlet
    # threw so a subsequent restore attempt has the right ground
    # truth to recover from.
    try {
        $flagPath = $script:DefenderStateFlag
        if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($flagPath)) {
            $stateDir = Split-Path -Parent -Path $flagPath
            if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $stateDir)) {
                New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $stateDir -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
            }
            $markerBody = ("{0}|{1}|{2}|{3}" -f $markerState, (Get-Date -Format o), $Reason, $PID)
            Set-Content -LiteralPath $flagPath -Value $markerBody -Encoding ASCII -Force -ErrorAction Stop
        }
    } catch {
        try {
            Write-FbLog ("Set-FbDefenderRealtime: marker file write threw (Reason={0}): {1}" -f $Reason, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        } catch {}
    }
}

# [DEBUG] 2249: developer bypass — Ctrl+Shift+End → force OOBE handoff
# Checks for the .debug-force-oobe-handoff flag written by
# FirstBaseDebugHotkeyWatcher.ps1 (FirstBase\DebugHotkeyWatcher schtask).
# When $true is returned the main-loop call site routes directly to
# Invoke-FbOobeHandoff. Also sets $script:FbDebugBypassActive so the STAGE 1
# convergence gate inside Invoke-FbOobeHandoff skips the residual scan check
# (the flag file is removed here; the gate reads the script-level variable).
function Test-FbDebugForceOobeHandoff {
    # [DEBUG] Returns $true if the developer pressed Ctrl+Shift+End to bypass updates
    $flagPath = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase '.debug-force-oobe-handoff'
    if (Test-Path $flagPath) {
        Remove-Item $flagPath -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        $script:FbDebugBypassActive = $true
        Write-FbLog '[DEBUG] 2249: .debug-force-oobe-handoff flag detected — bypassing update loop, forcing OOBE handoff.' 'WARN'
        return $true
    }
    return $false
}

Write-FbLog ("Engine=NativeCOM product={0} script={1} payload={2} FinalizeToOobe={3}" -f $script:FirstBaseProductVersion, $ScriptVersion, $script:FirstBasePayloadRevision, [bool]$FinalizeToOobe)

# ============================================================================
# 2026.05.13.2213k-real-single-instance-and-real-multipass-and-builder-pathfix
# Bug X-2 (CRITICAL): bulletproof single-instance via FileStream exclusive
#   lock. The 2213j fix (named mutex Global\FirstBaseWindowsUpdateLoopMutex
#   + 180s wait) was INEFFECTIVE in the field. Evidence from
#   G:\fb-dump-final-1111\State\reboot-tier.log lines 8-9 (PID 3724 +
#   PID 6632 firing Tier 1 reboots 3 seconds apart for the SAME
#   residual) and from G:\fb-dump-final-1111\Logs\WU-20260518-101429.log
#   lines 20-21 ("Failed to initialize loop mutex: ... Access to the
#   path 'Global\FirstBaseWindowsUpdateLoopMutex' is denied.") proved
#   the root cause: cross-session Global named-mutex ACLs. The Audit-
#   Mode SYSTEM (ONSTART task) PID creates the Global mutex with a
#   restrictive ACL; the Administrator-session PID (RunOnce / ONLOGON
#   task) gets ACCESS_DENIED on the New-Object Mutex call itself. The
#   2213j catch{} fell through and let the Administrator PID run the
#   full pipeline alongside the SYSTEM PID, producing the sibling
#   Tier 1 reboots in the dump.
#
# Fix: open a FileStream with FileShare::None on a SYSTEM+Administrators
#   ACL'd path (under C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\State\, created with
#   default inherited ACL). FileStream open semantics are unambiguous:
#   the FIRST process to call File.Open succeeds; ANY second process
#   gets a deterministic IOException (sharing violation) at open time.
#   We hold the stream open for the process lifetime; when the process
#   exits (normal exit, crash, kill, OS reboot mid-script), the kernel
#   closes the handle, and a fresh sibling can open it. This is
#   robust across cross-session ACLs in a way the named Mutex pattern
#   provably is not.
#
# We keep the existing named-mutex code AS DIAGNOSTIC ONLY (downstream).
#   The file lock is the single source of truth for "am I the live
#   instance?"; the mutex stays for log compatibility with the 2213j
#   diagnostic output. If the file-lock acquire fails, we exit clean
#   AS A DUPLICATE - no fall-through, no catch-swallow-and-proceed.
#
# Position: this MUST be the first thing that touches shared state.
#   It runs BEFORE Update-FbHeartbeat (which writes
#   wu-loop-heartbeat.txt - a shared file), BEFORE the watchdog probe
#   (which reads wu-status.json and may write reboot-tier.log),
#   BEFORE the Mutex try{} block, BEFORE engine load, BEFORE any
#   Save-State. The lock file lives under $FbStateDir so a tech can
#   "del" it manually to recover from any hypothetical handle-leak
#   pathology - the next loop instance will reopen and own it.
# ============================================================================
$script:LoopSingleInstanceLockPath   = $null
$script:LoopSingleInstanceLockStream = $null
$script:LoopSingleInstanceLockOwned  = $false
try {
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $FbStateDir)) {
        New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $FbStateDir -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
    }
    $script:LoopSingleInstanceLockPath = Join-Path $FbStateDir 'wu-loop.lock'
    # FileShare::None means: only the holder of this handle can open
    # the file for ANY access. A second PID calling File.Open with the
    # same path gets IOException ("The process cannot access the file
    # because it is being used by another process.") - regardless of
    # whether the second PID runs as SYSTEM, Administrator, or any
    # other identity. This is the bulletproof single-instance gate
    # the 2213j Global\Mutex pattern failed to provide cross-session.
    $script:LoopSingleInstanceLockStream = [System.IO.File]::Open(
        $script:LoopSingleInstanceLockPath,
        [System.IO.FileMode]::OpenOrCreate,
        [System.IO.FileAccess]::ReadWrite,
        [System.IO.FileShare]::None
    )
    $script:LoopSingleInstanceLockOwned = $true
    # Stamp our PID + start time into the lock file as a diagnostic so
    # a tech can `type wu-loop.lock` and see WHO owns it. This write is
    # best-effort; the lock itself is the synchronization primitive,
    # not the file contents.
    try {
        $stampBytes = [System.Text.Encoding]::ASCII.GetBytes(
            ("pid={0}`nstarted={1}`nversion={2}`n" -f $PID, (Get-Date).ToString('o'), $ScriptVersion)
        )
        $script:LoopSingleInstanceLockStream.SetLength(0)
        $script:LoopSingleInstanceLockStream.Write($stampBytes, 0, $stampBytes.Length)
        $script:LoopSingleInstanceLockStream.Flush()
    } catch {}
    Write-FbLog ("Single-instance file lock ACQUIRED: PID={0} path={1} (bulletproof cross-session lock; sibling triggers will exit clean)." -f $PID, $script:LoopSingleInstanceLockPath) 'WARN'
} catch [System.IO.IOException] {
    # Sharing violation: another PID already owns the loop. The
    # second/third/fourth trigger layers (Layer 1-4) will land here
    # within ms of each other and ALL of them exit cleanly. This is
    # the exact behavior 2213k needs: ONE PID runs the loop per boot,
    # period. Read the owner's stamp if possible so the log captures
    # which sibling won the race.
    $ownerStamp = ''
    try {
        if ($script:LoopSingleInstanceLockPath -and (Test-Path -LiteralPath $script:LoopSingleInstanceLockPath)) {
            $rawOwner = Get-Content -LiteralPath $script:LoopSingleInstanceLockPath -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            if ($null -ne $rawOwner) {
                # Coerce to single string (Get-Content without -Raw can yield Object[]; -f would log 'System.Object[]').
                $ownerStamp = ([string]$rawOwner).Trim() -replace '\r?\n', ' | '
            }
        }
    } catch {}
    Write-FbLog ("Single-instance file lock CONTENDED: another PID already owns the loop (sharing violation on {0}; owner stamp='{1}'); exiting CLEAN as duplicate trigger. This is the 2213k fix for Bug X-2." -f $script:LoopSingleInstanceLockPath, $ownerStamp) 'WARN'
    try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
    exit 0
} catch {
    # Any OTHER exception (permissions, drive missing, ...) is a
    # critical failure - we can't safely run without serialization.
    # Log loudly and exit; the watchdog on the next boot will retry.
    try { Write-FbLog ("Single-instance file lock FAILED to open (non-sharing error): {0}. Exiting to avoid running unsynchronized." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR' } catch {}
    try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
    exit 1
}

# Initial heartbeat. The helper below (Update-FbHeartbeat) refreshes this
# every time we hit a phase change or finish a bucket apply, so a stale
# mutex from a crashed prior instance can be detected by the new instance
# (heartbeat older than 90s + PID dead => previous instance died without
# releasing the mutex).
$script:LoopStartedIso = (Get-Date).ToString('o')
$script:LoopPhase      = 'Startup'

function Update-FbHeartbeat {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Refresh wu-loop-heartbeat.txt so a new instance can tell whether
        the prior loop is actually alive.
    .DESCRIPTION
        Stale-mutex protection (#2 in the 2026-05-11 patch) reads this
        file to recover from a crashed instance: parse `pid=`, check
        Get-Process, and if dead, abandon the named-mutex contention.
        Watchdog (#3) reads it to forcibly Stop-Process the orphan when
        wu-status.json reports `phase=Rebooting` is more than 90s old.

        Stored as plaintext key=value (one per line) so it stays human-
        readable in the rare case an operator can reach the file. Never
        throws; heartbeat write failures are logged at WARN but tolerated.
    #>
    param([string]$Phase = '')
    try {
        if ($Phase) { $script:LoopPhase = $Phase }
        $lines = @(
            ('pid={0}'      -f $PID),
            ('started={0}'  -f $script:LoopStartedIso),
            ('lastBeat={0}' -f (Get-Date).ToString('o')),
            ('version={0}'  -f $ScriptVersion),
            ('phase={0}'    -f $script:LoopPhase)
        )
        Set-Content -Path $HeartbeatFile -Value ($lines -join "`n") -Encoding ASCII -Force
        if ($script:FbWuLoopPaintingActive) { Set-FbWuLoopPaintingMarker }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Heartbeat write failed: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Startup'

function Test-ProcessAlive {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Best-effort check: is this PID currently a running process?
    .DESCRIPTION
        Used by the stale-mutex recovery path. Returns $false on any
        lookup error (process dead, PID 0, transient access denial) so
        the caller defaults to "treat as dead and recover". Important
        not to throw - a probe failure must not crash the new loop.
    #>
    param([int]$Pid)
    if ($Pid -le 0) { return $false }
    try {
        $p = Get-Process -Id $Pid -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        if ($p) { return $true }
    } catch {}
    return $false
}

function Read-HeartbeatPid {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Pull the `pid=` value out of wu-loop-heartbeat.txt if present.
    .DESCRIPTION
        Returns 0 when the file is missing/malformed; the caller treats
        0 as "no prior owner" which is safe (we just proceed with the
        normal mutex contention path).
    #>
    if (-not (Test-Path $HeartbeatFile)) { return 0 }
    try {
        $raw = Get-Content -Path $HeartbeatFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        if (-not $raw) { return 0 }
        $m = [regex]::Match($raw, '(?m)^pid\s*=\s*(\d+)\s*$')
        if ($m.Success) { return [int]$m.Groups[1].Value }
    } catch {}
    return 0
}

function Get-StatusPhaseAndAgeSeconds {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Inspect wu-status.json for the dashboard's last reported phase,
        message, and how stale that report is, in whole seconds.
    .DESCRIPTION
        Used by the stuck-Rebooting watchdog. We DON'T trust the named
        mutex here: a crashed loop's mutex may already be in the kernel-
        side garbage-collection window, in which case the next instance
        would otherwise sail right past the check and start a fresh
        update pass while the device is still half-rebooted. The
        watchdog uses the disk artifact instead.

        Triggers (2026-05-11 1045 - tightened from 90/60s):
          - phase='Rebooting' AND age >= 45s
          - phase='Rebooting' AND message matches "Reboot attempt
            \d+/\d+:" AND age >= 30s
            (catches a cascade that is HUNG inside a single stage
            rather than between stages - e.g. Restart-Computer
            -Force blocking on OS refusal of a TI-blocked reboot)
          - phase='Rebooting' AND message contains
            "CascadeStage=K/8" AND age >= 30s
            (catches the cascade stalled mid-stage with the stage
            counter unchanged - same message text simply not being
            re-stamped because the stage call hasn't returned)

        Returns @{ Phase=$null; Message=''; AgeSeconds=-1 } on any
        parse failure.
    #>
    $result = @{ Phase = $null; Message = ''; AgeSeconds = -1 }
    if (-not (Test-Path $StatusFile)) { return $result }
    try {
        $raw = Get-Content -Path $StatusFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        if (-not $raw) { return $result }
        $obj = $raw | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        if (-not $obj) { return $result }
        $phase = [string]$obj.phase
        $message = ''
        try { $message = [string]$obj.message } catch {}
        $age = -1
        try {
            $updatedAt = [DateTime]::Parse([string]$obj.updatedAt, $null, [System.Globalization.DateTimeStyles]::RoundtripKind)
            $age = [int]([Math]::Max(0, ((Get-Date) - $updatedAt).TotalSeconds))
        } catch {}
        $result.Phase      = $phase
        $result.Message    = $message
        $result.AgeSeconds = $age
    } catch {}
    return $result
}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REMOVED 2026.05.12.1030-no-cascade-final
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Win32 P/Invoke surface (ExitWindowsEx / InitiateSystemShutdownEx /
# NtShutdownSystem / SeShutdownPrivilege enablement) was previously
# compiled here to power the 8-stage reboot cascade and its logoff
# fallback. Field evidence across 11 patches showed every single one of
# those APIs is silently vetoed by TrustedInstaller's shutdown block on
# this hardware class, regardless of stage timeout, registry pre-clear,
# or process hard-kill. The cascade has been removed; Invoke-FbReboot
# now goes directly to OOBE handoff (which uses Sysprep's reboot path)
# or, in the rare pre-install case, a single `shutdown /r /f /t 5`.
# Neither path needs the P/Invoke surface or SeShutdownPrivilege
# enablement, so both have been deleted to shrink the script and
# eliminate dead code that field engineers were mis-interpreting as
# "the cascade has another trick up its sleeve."
$script:FbRebootPInvokeReady = $false

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stuck-Rebooting watchdog (runs BEFORE the mutex contention).
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# If wu-status.json says we're already in phase='Rebooting' and the
# timestamp on that record is stale, the prior loop fired a reboot,
# Set-UiPhase wrote 'Rebooting' to disk, and then... nothing. The
# shutdown.exe call did not actually take the machine down. The new
# instance must NOT exit as a duplicate when this is the state of the
# world - we are the recovery process. Kill the orphan PID if it's
# still alive, skip the mutex check, and let the rest of the script
# (the startup gate + Bridge #4 fail-safe + main loop) decide whether
# to route to OOBE handoff or re-attempt a reboot.
#
# 2026.05.12.1030-no-cascade-final TIGHTENING:
#   * Trigger 1 threshold tightened to 30s (was 45s in 0830, 90s
#     originally). With the cascade gone, there is no legitimate
#     reason for phase='Rebooting' to stay live longer than the 30s
#     post-shutdown.exe wait inside Invoke-FbReboot. Anything older
#     is by definition a dead process.
#   * Trigger 2 expanded to ALSO catch any message containing
#     'cascade' / 'CASCADE' / 'Reboot attempt' / 'REBOOT FAILED' so
#     a legacy banner left over from a prior build is recognized
#     even without the LegacyMessage / Reboot attempt N/M shape.
#   * Trigger 3 (legacy CascadeStage=K/8 footer text) RETAINED as
#     a back-compat recognizer for status.json files written by
#     prior builds; never fires from this build's own output.
#   * Trigger 4 (cascade-exhausted banner, no age threshold) is
#     retained verbatim - it is the explicit signal that the prior
#     process exited via the dead-end cascade path. Reading this
#     banner from disk at startup IS dispositive of "we are the
#     recovery period for a stuck device."
#
# 2026.05.12.1830 PATIENT-REBOOT COMPATIBILITY:
#   * Invoke-FbReboot's "Step 5: PLAIN REBOOT" branch now runs a
#     7-minute observable wait inside the SAME PowerShell process
#     after issuing shutdown.exe. During that wait, the loop calls
#     Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' / Update-FbHeartbeat at least
#     every 10 seconds with a freshly-stamped 'updatedAt'. That
#     keeps wu-status.json.AgeSeconds < 30 throughout the wait, so
#     Trigger 1 (Rebooting + age>=30s) does NOT fire on the same
#     device when a fresh instance is somehow launched mid-wait
#     (e.g. the ONLOGON backup task firing while we're still
#     waiting for shutdown.exe to take the box down).
#   * The patient-reboot status messages are deliberately written
#     to AVOID matching Triggers 2/3/4: they contain "Patient
#     shutdown" / "Waiting for shutdown" / "OS reports" / "Forcing
#     second shutdown" -- never the legacy strings 'cascade',
#     'Reboot attempt N/M', 'REBOOT FAILED', or 'CascadeStage='.
#     A grep for any of those substrings inside the Step 5 branch
#     should turn up nothing.
#   * Trigger 1's 30s threshold is therefore the right boundary:
#     a process that has gone silent for >=30s during a 7-minute
#     wait is by definition crashed (the wait's 10s heartbeat
#     loop is dead), and the new instance still does the right
#     thing by entering recovery mode.
$script:WatchdogRecoveryActive = $false
try {
    $statusInfo = Get-StatusPhaseAndAgeSeconds
    $watchdogReason = $null

    # Trigger 1: classic stale-Rebooting. 30s threshold (lowered from
    # 45s) so the user-visible recovery starts within 30s of the
    # prior process going silent.
    # ApplyingUpdates / ManualRestart are live operator-wait phases
    # (CBS applying, or Tier 7 asking for a power cycle) — do not
    # treat those as a wedged reboot ladder.
    if ($statusInfo.Phase -eq 'Rebooting' -and $statusInfo.AgeSeconds -ge 30) {
        $watchdogReason = ("phase='Rebooting' wedged for {0}s" -f $statusInfo.AgeSeconds)
    }

    # Trigger 2: ANY message containing 'cascade', 'CASCADE', 'Reboot
    # attempt', or 'REBOOT FAILED'. No age threshold - the new build
    # never writes those strings, so any presence in status.json is
    # by definition a legacy / leftover banner that needs recovery.
    if (-not $watchdogReason -and $statusInfo.Phase -eq 'Rebooting' -and
        $statusInfo.Message -match '(?i)cascade|Reboot\s+attempt|REBOOT\s+FAILED') {
        $watchdogReason = ("legacy cascade banner in status ('{0}'); prior build's reboot path is dead" -f $statusInfo.Message)
    }

    # Trigger 3 (LEGACY back-compat): CascadeStage=K/N footer text
    # from a prior build. Same treatment as trigger 2; left as a
    # separate clause so future log forensics can tell which kind
    # of stale state we recovered from.
    if (-not $watchdogReason -and $statusInfo.Phase -eq 'Rebooting' -and
        $statusInfo.Message -match 'CascadeStage=\d+/\d+' -and
        $statusInfo.AgeSeconds -ge 30) {
        $watchdogReason = ("legacy CascadeStage footer in status ('{0}') unchanged for {1}s" -f $statusInfo.Message, $statusInfo.AgeSeconds)
    }

    # Trigger 4: cascade-exhausted / reboot-failed BANNER. No age
    # threshold - the banner alone is dispositive.
    if (-not $watchdogReason -and $statusInfo.Phase -eq 'Rebooting' -and
        $statusInfo.Message -match '(?i)cascade\s+budget\s+exhausted|REBOOT\s+FAILED') {
        $watchdogReason = ("cascade-exhausted banner ('{0}') in status file at startup; prior process is dead" -f $statusInfo.Message)
    }

    if ($watchdogReason) {
        Write-FbLog ("Watchdog: {0}; previous reboot attempt is wedged. Entering recovery mode." -f $watchdogReason) 'WARN'
        $orphanPid = Read-HeartbeatPid
        if ($orphanPid -gt 0 -and $orphanPid -ne $PID -and (Test-ProcessAlive -Pid $orphanPid)) {
            Write-FbLog ("Watchdog: terminating orphaned loop process PID={0} (Recovering from stuck reboot)." -f $orphanPid) 'WARN'
            try { Stop-Process -Id $orphanPid -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } catch {}
            Start-Sleep -Milliseconds 500
        }
        # 2026.05.12.1030 STRENGTHENING: stash the reason so the
        # downstream startup InstallsCompleted probe (which DOES have
        # Save-State / Load-State in scope by the time it runs) can
        # synthesize the latch unconditionally. Save-State and
        # Load-State are defined AFTER this watchdog block at file
        # scope, so we cannot call them here directly - PowerShell's
        # single-pass top-level parsing only makes those functions
        # callable once their definitions have been executed.
        $script:WatchdogRecoveryActive = $true
        $script:WatchdogStuckBannerReason = $watchdogReason

        # 2026.05.13.2213f-reboot-ladder-optimization Bug C2: smarter
        # startup recovery. The plain wedge-recovery above doesn't know
        # WHICH reboot tier the prior loop got to before it disappeared.
        # If reboot-tier.log exists, parse out the LAST tier attempted.
        # If Tier <= 3 (i.e. the prior loop wedged at or before Tier 3),
        # the eventual reboot was Tier 4+ -- so on the NEXT call into
        # Invoke-FbReboot we should skip the known-wedged Tier 1-3
        # dance and go straight to Tier 4 (saves ~3 minutes of no-op
        # service-stop + Restart-Computer wedge per reboot).
        # Tier >= 4: nothing special needed (the OS reboot succeeded
        # via WMI/ExitWindowsEx/bugcheck which is what we'd do again
        # anyway), so the cascade can run normally.
        try {
            if ($script:RebootTierLogPath -and (Test-Path -LiteralPath $script:RebootTierLogPath)) {
                $tierLogLines = @(Get-Content -LiteralPath $script:RebootTierLogPath -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)
                if ($tierLogLines -and $tierLogLines.Count -gt 0) {
                    $lastTierLine = ''
                    for ($i = $tierLogLines.Count - 1; $i -ge 0; $i--) {
                        $candidate = [string]$tierLogLines[$i]
                        if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($candidate)) { $lastTierLine = $candidate; break }
                    }
                    if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($lastTierLine)) {
                        $lastTier = -1
                        $m = [regex]::Match($lastTierLine, 'Tier=(\d+)/7')
                        if ($m.Success) {
                            try { $lastTier = [int]$m.Groups[1].Value } catch { $lastTier = -1 }
                        }
                        if ($lastTier -ge 1 -and $lastTier -le 3) {
                            Write-FbLog ("Startup recovery: prior boot's reboot ladder reached Tier {0}/7 then wedged; if this boot needs another reboot, will START at Tier 4 (skip the known-wedged Tier 1-3 dance). LastTierLine='{1}'" -f $lastTier, $lastTierLine) 'WARN'
                            $script:SkipToTier4OnNextReboot = $true
                        } elseif ($lastTier -ge 4) {
                            Write-FbLog ("Startup recovery: prior boot's reboot ladder reached Tier {0}/7 (>=4) then succeeded; full 7-tier cascade re-armed for next reboot. LastTierLine='{1}'" -f $lastTier, $lastTierLine) 'INFO'
                        } else {
                            Write-FbLog ("Startup recovery: reboot-tier.log present but last line did not parse a Tier=N/7 token. LastTierLine='{0}'" -f $lastTierLine) 'INFO'
                        }
                    }
                }
            } else {
                Write-FbLog 'Startup recovery: reboot-tier.log not present (first watchdog wedge or prior boot pre-2213f); cascade will run from Tier 1 normally.' 'INFO'
            }
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Startup recovery: reboot-tier.log probe threw: {0}; defaulting to full cascade." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
    }
} catch {
    Write-FbLog ("Watchdog probe failed (continuing without recovery flag): {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Loop mutex acquisition (with stale-mutex protection).
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The mutex prevents two concurrent loop instances from racing on
# wu-state.json / WUA COM. But a previous instance that crashed in the
# middle of Invoke-Reboot may have leaked the kernel-side mutex grant
# even though no process holds it anymore. WaitOne(0,...) would then
# falsely return $false and we'd exit as duplicate, leaving the device
# permanently stuck on whatever the splash last rendered.
#
# Defense: (a) if the watchdog above already declared recovery, skip the
# mutex entirely; (b) otherwise, on the first WaitOne miss, read the
# heartbeat's pid and check liveness - if the prior owner is dead, the
# mutex grant is abandoned and we proceed; (c) only if both checks pass
# do we re-attempt WaitOne with a generous 5s window before declaring
# the new instance is a true duplicate.
$script:LoopMutex = $null
$script:HasLoopMutex = $false

# 2026.05.13.2213j-multipass-and-dedup-and-buildmarker Bug X
# 2026.05.13.2213k-real-single-instance-and-real-multipass-and-builder-pathfix Bug X-2
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEMOTED TO DIAGNOSTIC: the FileStream lock above (look for
# "Single-instance file lock ACQUIRED") is now the authoritative
# single-instance gate. By the time control reaches this Mutex
# block, we have ALREADY proven we are the sole owning PID because
# any sibling would have exited at the FileStream sharing violation.
# We KEEP the Mutex code purely for log compatibility with 2213j
# diagnostics - so a tech reading WU-*.log can still see the
# "acquired mutex on first try" / "Failed to initialize loop mutex"
# lines they are familiar with. The Mutex's exit paths below cannot
# fire in 2213k because no sibling reaches them.
#
# WHY THE 2213j Mutex FIX WAS INEFFECTIVE (field evidence):
#   G:\fb-dump-final-1111\Logs\WU-20260518-101429.log lines 20-21:
#     "Failed to initialize loop mutex: Exception calling
#      ".ctor" with "2" argument(s): "Access to the path
#      'Global\FirstBaseWindowsUpdateLoopMutex' is denied.""
#   The Audit-Mode SYSTEM-session ONSTART/Run task created the
#   Global named mutex with a session-restricted ACL. The
#   RunOnce/ONLOGON Administrator-session PID could not even
#   construct the same Global mutex object - it threw ACCESS_DENIED
#   at the Mutex constructor itself, before any WaitOne could run.
#   The 2213j catch{} block then logged WARN and let the
#   Administrator PID PROCEED into the engine load and full main
#   loop. Two parallel pipelines per boot produced the 50+ tier
#   escalations and 1-3s sibling Tier 1 reboots in the dump.
#
# WHY 2213k DOES NOT REPEAT THAT MISTAKE:
#   The FileStream lock above is a SHARING constraint enforced
#   by the kernel on the file-table-entry level, not an ACL on a
#   named-object handle. Cross-session sharing checks do not
#   depend on session integrity levels. The first PID succeeds,
#   any sibling PID gets IOException at File.Open and immediately
#   exits 0 - no fall-through code path exists.
try {
    $script:LoopMutex = New-Object System.Threading.Mutex($false, 'Global\FirstBaseWindowsUpdateLoopMutex')
    $script:HasLoopMutex = $script:LoopMutex.WaitOne(0, $false)

    if ($script:WatchdogRecoveryActive) {
        if ($script:HasLoopMutex) {
            Write-FbLog 'Watchdog recovery active; acquired mutex on first try (sole recovery instance this boot).' 'WARN'
        } else {
            # A sibling trigger from the SAME boot already grabbed the
            # mutex and entered watchdog recovery. Block on it for up
            # to 180 seconds. If we time out, the sibling is doing the
            # work and we exit clean as a duplicate trigger. The orphan-
            # kill in the watchdog branch above already terminated any
            # PRIOR-boot stuck process, so anything holding the mutex
            # now is a legitimate this-boot sibling.
            $waitMs = 180000
            Write-FbLog ("Watchdog recovery active; another instance holds the mutex (likely this-boot sibling trigger). Waiting up to {0}ms for it to finish." -f $waitMs) 'WARN'
            $waited = $false
            try {
                $waited = $script:LoopMutex.WaitOne($waitMs, $false)
            } catch [System.Threading.AbandonedMutexException] {
                Write-FbLog 'Watchdog recovery: sibling mutex was abandoned; assuming ownership.' 'WARN'
                $waited = $true
            }
            $script:HasLoopMutex = $waited
            if (-not $script:HasLoopMutex) {
                Write-FbLog 'Watchdog recovery: sibling did not release mutex within 180s; exiting as duplicate trigger so we do not double up the OOBE-handoff pipeline.' 'WARN'
                try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
                exit 0
            }
            Write-FbLog 'Watchdog recovery: acquired mutex from sibling; proceeding as the live recovery instance.' 'WARN'
        }
    } elseif (-not $script:HasLoopMutex) {
        # Non-watchdog path: same stale-grant defense as 2213i, with
        # an extended retry window (30s instead of 5s).
        $heartbeatPid = Read-HeartbeatPid
        $priorAlive = ($heartbeatPid -gt 0 -and $heartbeatPid -ne $PID -and (Test-ProcessAlive -Pid $heartbeatPid))
        if (-not $priorAlive) {
            Write-FbLog ("Mutex appears held but heartbeat PID={0} is not running; treating as stale grant and recovering." -f $heartbeatPid) 'WARN'
            Start-Sleep -Milliseconds 500
            try {
                $script:HasLoopMutex = $script:LoopMutex.WaitOne(30000, $false)
            } catch [System.Threading.AbandonedMutexException] {
                Write-FbLog 'Acquired previously-abandoned mutex; proceeding as new owner.' 'WARN'
                $script:HasLoopMutex = $true
            }
        } else {
            # 2213j: prior owner IS alive. Instead of immediately
            # exiting as a duplicate trigger, wait up to 180s for
            # the live owner to release - protects against the same
            # this-boot sibling-trigger race that bit the watchdog
            # path. If the owner is genuinely making progress, our
            # wait will time out and we exit clean. If the owner
            # finishes quickly (e.g. it already handed off to OOBE
            # and is exiting), we take the mutex and proceed.
            $waitMs = 180000
            Write-FbLog ("Mutex held by alive heartbeat PID={0}; waiting up to {1}ms for it to release before exiting as duplicate." -f $heartbeatPid, $waitMs) 'WARN'
            try {
                $script:HasLoopMutex = $script:LoopMutex.WaitOne($waitMs, $false)
            } catch [System.Threading.AbandonedMutexException] {
                Write-FbLog 'Sibling mutex was abandoned during wait; assuming ownership.' 'WARN'
                $script:HasLoopMutex = $true
            }
            if (-not $script:HasLoopMutex) {
                Write-FbLog ("Mutex still held by PID={0} after {1}ms; exiting as duplicate trigger." -f $heartbeatPid, $waitMs) 'WARN'
                try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
                exit 0
            }
            Write-FbLog ("Acquired mutex from prior holder PID={0}; proceeding." -f $heartbeatPid) 'WARN'
        }
    }
} catch [System.Threading.AbandonedMutexException] {
    Write-FbLog 'Mutex was abandoned by a crashed prior instance; assuming ownership.' 'WARN'
    $script:HasLoopMutex = $true
} catch {
    Write-FbLog "Failed to initialize loop mutex: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN'
}

if (-not (Test-Path $EngineScriptPath)) {
    Write-FbLog "Native COM engine not found at $EngineScriptPath" 'ERROR'
    # 2026.05.13.2213c-onlogon-primary-and-defender-disable: belt-and-
    # braces Defender restore. This exit fires BEFORE the loop disables
    # Defender so the call is idempotent (Set-FbDefenderRealtime never
    # throws and is safe to call without a prior disable). Covers the
    # case where a crashed prior session left Defender disabled.
    Set-FbDefenderRealtime -Enabled $true -Reason 'engine-missing-exit'
    try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
    exit 1
}

. $EngineScriptPath
Write-FbLog "Loaded native COM engine: $EngineScriptPath"

# 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: engine/loop version-
# skew check. Both files should carry the same version stamp on every
# release. A mismatch means a partial hot-deploy (only one of the two
# .ps1 files was overwritten on the live image), which usually causes
# subtle behavior drift that's painful to debug in the field. Log a
# loud WARN line so post-mortem can spot it; do NOT abort the loop -
# this is a soft-fail diagnostic, not a hard gate.
try {
    $loopVer   = $ScriptVersion
    $engineVer = ''
    try { $engineVer = [string]$script:FbWuEngineVersion } catch { $engineVer = '<unset>' }
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($engineVer)) { $engineVer = '<empty>' }
    if ($loopVer -ne $engineVer) {
        Write-FbLog ("Engine/loop version skew: loop={0} engine={1}. This usually indicates a partial hot-deploy. Behavior may be unexpected." -f $loopVer, $engineVer) 'WARN'
    } else {
        Write-FbLog ("Engine/loop version match: {0}" -f $loopVer) 'INFO'
    }
} catch {
    Write-FbLog ("Engine/loop version-skew check threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
}

# 2026.05.13.2213j-multipass-and-dedup-and-buildmarker: prominent
# on-disk build-version marker. Field evidence from G:\fb-dump-858
# (5/18) proved a recurring operator workflow gap: the device was
# imaged with the prior build's USB stick because the 2213i Payload
# rebuild on the stick was skipped. The dump's disk-sha256.txt
# carried the 2213h hashes, but that file is generated by fb-dump.cmd
# and only inspected during post-mortem - not during the field run.
#
# This marker is written on EVERY loop startup, captures every
# Payload file's SHA + version banner, and lives at a single well-
# known path that fb-dump.cmd captures FIRST. The intent: a quick
# `type C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\State\build-version.txt` (live
# device) or `type <dump>\build-version.txt` (after fb-dump) gives
# the operator the build version + per-file SHAs at a glance,
# without scrolling through 380KB of setupact.log or 11000 lines
# of WU-*.log. Detects USB-stick-not-rebuilt cases instantly.
try {
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $FbStateDir)) {
        New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $FbStateDir -Force | Out-Null
    }
    $buildMarkerPath = Join-Path $FbStateDir 'build-version.txt'
    # 2026.05.19.2213n Bug "build marker LOUD": extract the build tag
    # ("2213n") from the version string so the LOUD top banner can
    # render it in a way the operator cannot miss. Defensive: if the
    # extraction fails for any reason, fall back to the full version
    # string. The build tag is between the date and the first '-'
    # in 2026.05.19.2213n-scan-stability-and-...
    $loopBuildTag = $loopVer
    try {
        $tagRegex = [regex]'\d{4}\.\d{2}\.\d{2}\.([^-]+)'
        $tagMatch = $tagRegex.Match([string]$loopVer)
        if ($tagMatch.Success) { $loopBuildTag = $tagMatch.Groups[1].Value }
    } catch {}
    $buildMarkerLines = @()
    $buildMarkerLines += '========================================================'
    $buildMarkerLines += '========================================================'
    $buildMarkerLines += '====                                                ===='
    $buildMarkerLines += '====        FIRSTBASE BUILD IDENTITY                ===='
    $buildMarkerLines += '====                                                ===='
    $buildMarkerLines += '========================================================'
    $buildMarkerLines += '========================================================'
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += ('LoopVersion: {0}' -f $loopVer)
    $buildMarkerLines += ('EngineVer:   {0}' -f $engineVer)
    $buildMarkerLines += ('ProductVer:  {0}' -f $script:FirstBaseProductVersion)
    $buildMarkerLines += ('PayloadRev:  {0}' -f $script:FirstBasePayloadRevision)
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += ('Build Tag:   {0}' -f $loopBuildTag)
    $buildMarkerLines += ('WrittenAt:   {0}' -f (Get-Date -Format 'yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:sszzz'))
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += ('If above does NOT show {0}, the USB was NOT rebuilt' -f $loopBuildTag)
    $buildMarkerLines += 'with the latest payload. Rebuild USB and reimage.'
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += '========================================================'
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== FirstBase Windows Update Loop build marker ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += ('WrittenAt:   {0}' -f (Get-Date -Format 'yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:sszzz'))
    $buildMarkerLines += ('LoopVersion: {0}' -f $loopVer)
    $buildMarkerLines += ('EngineVer:   {0}' -f $engineVer)
    $buildMarkerLines += ('ProductVer:  {0}' -f $script:FirstBaseProductVersion)
    $buildMarkerLines += ('PayloadRev:  {0}' -f $script:FirstBasePayloadRevision)
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== Payload SHAs (on-disk, this boot) ==='
    # 2026.05.13.2213k-real-single-instance-and-real-multipass-and-builder-pathfix
    # Bug DD: SetupComplete.cmd actually lives at C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\
    # SetupComplete.cmd (per autounattend.xml RunSynchronous in
    # FirstBase Image Creation\Policy\autounattend.xml line 29). The 2213j
    # marker only probed $FbRoot ('C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase') and thus
    # reported SetupComplete.cmd as <MISSING> in every dump, masking the
    # field SHA. Marker now searches a per-payload path list and records
    # the path that actually matched, so a future SetupComplete.cmd
    # location change (or a partial copy that leaves SetupComplete only
    # in one of the two locations) is visible at a glance.
    $payloadProbeList = [ordered]@{
        'Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1'    = @((Join-Path $FbRoot 'Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1'))
        'FirstBaseWuEngine.ps1'           = @((Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseWuEngine.ps1'))
        'Show-UpdateProgress.ps1'         = @((Join-Path $FbRoot 'Show-UpdateProgress.ps1'))
        'FirstBaseSplashTechMenu.ps1'     = @((Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseSplashTechMenu.ps1'))
        # 2239: Show-UpdatesCompleteMessage.ps1 removed from probe list — file deleted (dead since 2213s).
        # 2026.05.18.2213l Bug EE: splash single-instance wrapper. Lives
        # under FbRoot alongside Show-UpdateProgress.ps1; the splash
        # launcher cmd points at it. Marker captures its SHA so a future
        # field dump can prove the wrapper version that ran.
        'FirstBaseSplashSingleInstance.ps1' = @((Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseSplashSingleInstance.ps1'))
        # 2026.05.20.2213q Bug MM: SYSTEM Explorer-presence watcher.
        # Lives under FbRoot, invoked by FirstBase\SplashWatcher OnStart
        # task. Marker captures its SHA so a future field dump can prove
        # the watcher version that ran (or that the file was missing,
        # which would mean Bug MM-2 / TechInstall.cmd staging gap).
        'FirstBaseSplashWatcher.ps1'      = @((Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseSplashWatcher.ps1'))
        # 2255: [DEBUG] 2249 hotkey watcher — must appear in build-version.txt SHAs or <MISSING>
        # proves TechInstall/USB staging gap (see fb-dump-final-1519 SetupComplete.log).
        'FirstBaseDebugHotkeyWatcher.ps1' = @((Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseDebugHotkeyWatcher.ps1'))
        'FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1' = @((Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1'))
        'FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1' = @((Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1'))
        'FirstBaseVersion.ps1'            = @((Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseVersion.ps1'))
        'SetupComplete.cmd'               = @(
            'C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\SetupComplete.cmd'
            (Join-Path $FbRoot 'SetupComplete.cmd')
            (Join-Path $env:SystemRoot 'Setup\Scripts\SetupComplete.cmd')
        )
        'FirstBaseConsoleSplash.cmd'      = @((Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseConsoleSplash.cmd'))
        'FirstBaseLoopLauncher.cmd'       = @((Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseLoopLauncher.cmd'))
        'FirstBaseSplashLauncher.cmd'     = @((Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseSplashLauncher.cmd'))
        'FirstBaseWuLoopRun.cmd'          = @((Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseWuLoopRun.cmd'))
    }
    foreach ($payloadName in $payloadProbeList.Keys) {
        $resolved = $null
        foreach ($candidate in $payloadProbeList[$payloadName]) {
            try {
                if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($candidate) -and (Test-Path -LiteralPath $candidate)) {
                    $resolved = $candidate
                    break
                }
            } catch {}
        }
        if ($resolved) {
            try {
                $h = Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 -LiteralPath $resolved -ErrorAction Stop
                $buildMarkerLines += ('{0}  {1}  [{2}]' -f $h.Hash, $payloadName, $resolved)
            } catch {
                $buildMarkerLines += ('<HASH-FAILED: {0}>  {1}  [{2}]' -f $_.Exception.Message, $payloadName, $resolved)
            }
        } else {
            $searchedDisplay = (@($payloadProbeList[$payloadName]) -join '; ')
            $buildMarkerLines += ('<MISSING>                                                          {0}  [searched: {1}]' -f $payloadName, $searchedDisplay)
        }
    }
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== Boot context ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += ('PID:         {0}' -f $PID)
    try { $buildMarkerLines += ('ComputerName:{0}' -f $env:COMPUTERNAME) } catch {}
    try { $buildMarkerLines += ('LastBoot:    {0}' -f ((Get-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem -ErrorAction Stop).LastBootUpTime)) } catch {}
    # 2026.05.18.2213l: bug-fix markers so a tech can see at a glance
    # WHICH 2213l mitigations are active without grepping source. Plain
    # text, no scripting in the marker output itself.
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2026.05.18.2213l bug markers ==='
    $softGivenUpThresholdMarker = 2
    try { if ($null -ne $script:FbSoftGivenUpThreshold) { $softGivenUpThresholdMarker = [int]$script:FbSoftGivenUpThreshold } } catch {}
    $defenderGuidCount = 0
    try { if ($null -ne $script:FbDefenderPlatformGuids) { $defenderGuidCount = @($script:FbDefenderPlatformGuids).Count } } catch {}
    $buildMarkerLines += ('Bug FF: SOFT auto-GivenUp threshold={0}; Defender fast-path active ({1} platform GUID(s) tracked)' -f $softGivenUpThresholdMarker, $defenderGuidCount)
    $buildMarkerLines += ('Bug GG: wu-status.json write retry enabled (5 attempts, 50-200ms jitter; Write-FbWuStatusSafe helper)')
    $buildMarkerLines += ('Bug EE: splash single-instance lock active (splash-launcher.lock + console-splash.lock)')
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2026.05.19.2213m bug markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug FF reach: Invoke-FbSoftGivenUpEvaluate helper called from main-loop + oobe-handoff-stage1 + inline-retry-pass'
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug EE copy: FirstBaseSplashSingleInstance.ps1 + FirstBaseConsoleSplashInstance.ps1 explicit payload copy list'
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug EE lock: console-splash.lock now true FileShare::None hold (was 30s stamp scheme)'
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2026.05.19.2213n bug markers ==='
    $scanStabilityThresholdMarker = 2
    try { if ($null -ne $script:FbScanStabilityThreshold) { $scanStabilityThresholdMarker = [int]$script:FbScanStabilityThreshold } } catch {}
    $buildMarkerLines += ('Bug II: ScanStability threshold={0} (literal); STAGE 1 OOBE handoff gate only; GivenUp promotion path (does not bypass Bug Q)' -f $scanStabilityThresholdMarker)
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug JJ: post-sysprep cleanup of GivenUpUpdateIds + SoftGivenUpCounters + SuccessLedgerIds + ScanStability before sysprep launch'
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Build marker: 00-BUILD-IDENTITY.txt banner emitted first by fb-dump.cmd; loud version-on-top in build-version.txt'
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2026.05.19.2213o bug markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug KK: TechInstall.cmd + disksetup.cmd CRLF normalization (root cause of 2213m WinPE deploy skip)'
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug KK: Build-UsbStick.ps1 enforces CRLF on copy + preflight check on source'
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug KK: 2213m batch hazards sanitized: REM <MISSING> -> MISSING, (Bug EE) -> [Bug EE]'
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2026.05.19.2213p bug markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug LL: splash launch diagnostic instrumentation (splash-launcher-trace.log + splash-diagnostic-*.log + splash-launch-direct.log + splash-wrapper-entry.log + schtasks-firstbase-state.log)'
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug LL: Layer 5 Invoke-FbSplashDirectSpawn added (4th launch attempt path with PID tracking)'
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug LL: DIAGNOSTIC BUILD - no behavior changes to update loop, sysprep, or convergence'
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2026.05.20.2213q bug markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug MM: Layer 1.5 SYSTEM Explorer-presence watcher (FirstBase\SplashWatcher OnStart -> FirstBaseSplashWatcher.ps1)'
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug MM: FirstBase\UpdateSplash OnLogon trigger REMOVED (was racing explorer.exe startup on Dell hardware per dump 1142)'
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug MM: FirstBase\UpdateSplash task now TRIGGERLESS (/SC ONCE far-future placeholder); only runnable via schtasks /Run'
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug MM: watcher polls every 1s for interactive explorer.exe, waits 3s for stabilization, fires /Run UpdateSplash; 20-min hard timeout; FileShare::None lock on splash-watcher.lock'
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug MM: backup splash layers unchanged (Layer 3 RunOnce + Layer 4 UpdateSplashLayer4 + Layer 5 direct spawn) - watcher is PRIMARY, backups handle watcher-failure'
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2026.05.20.2213r bug markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug NN: pre-OOBE scrub gate (Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub) called from Invoke-FbOobeHandoff STAGE 2, AFTER Bug JJ state cleanup, BEFORE Invoke-SysprepToOobe / Start-Process sysprep.exe'
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug NN: scrub kills all FirstBase launch-layer processes via Win32_Process CommandLine match (WPF splash + console splash + watcher + launchers + loop OTHER than $PID)'
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug NN: scrub disables + deletes all FirstBase\* scheduled tasks discovered via schtasks /Query /FO LIST + explicit known-list union (WindowsUpdateLoop / WindowsUpdateLoopOnLogon / UpdateSplash / UpdateSplashLayer4 / SplashWatcher / UpdateSplashInteractive[Launch])'
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug NN: scrub deletes !FirstBase* / FirstBase* values from HKLM RunOnce + Run + RunOnceEx (incl subkeys) + RunServicesOnce + RunServices'
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug NN: state and logs scrub is OPT-IN ($scrubStateAndLogs flag, default $false) to preserve post-OOBE diagnostics for fb-dump'
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug NN: SCRUB-COMPLETE / SCRUB-INCOMPLETE final line in C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\Logs\pre-oobe-scrub.log; scrub failure logs but does NOT block sysprep (try/catch at call site)'
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug NN: complementary to Bug JJ (JJ = internal wu-state.json fields; NN = external surfaces); both run, neither replaces the other'
    $buildMarkerLines += 'Bug NN: design pivot per user 5/19/2026 7:06 PM: splash-over-OOBE is now treated as incomplete teardown evidence, not as a z-order UX problem'
