    $buildMarkerLines += '2259: DeferredSound completion marker (.oobe-sound-after-updates-flow-completed) and RunOnce removal now execute BEFORE the synchronous finalize call so they complete even if finalize is slow.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2260 bug markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += '2260: ROOT CAUSE FIX (dump fb-dump-final-924) — OOBE defaultuser0 session raced. DeferredOobeSound (PID=8940) spawned at UTC 15:21; real user "Test" logged in at UTC 15:23 (2 min 17s); session terminated mid-finalize-execution, leaving .pipeline-completed unwritten, all startup items armed. Evidence: session.lock present (DeferredOobeSound acquired it), Userinit already restored (finalize step 2 completed before kill), arm-suppress still present (step 3 not reached), zero log output (C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\Logs\ not writable by defaultuser0 — ProgramData bootstrap log has both entries; Setup\Logs copy has only real-user entry).'
    $buildMarkerLines += '2260: FIX — FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 (v1.0.16): inline critical seal block added AFTER SystemSettings closes and BEFORE spawning finalize. Inline seal writes .pipeline-completed + .firstbase-sealed, removes HKLM Run/RunOnce (Settings + Splash), restores Userinit, removes Startup cmd, removes all delivery arm markers. Immediately issues shutdown /s /t 30 /f — prevents OOBE from advancing to account creation after Settings close. Finalize (still called synchronously) overrides to /t 0 on success and handles script scrub + task deletion.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '2260: FIX — FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 (v1.0.17): removed throw on .pipeline-completed write failure (was crashing finalize before shutdown if Set-Content failed; 2260 inline seal makes this non-fatal). Added ProgramData secondary log destination to Write-FbOperatorFinalizeMirror (mirrors C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\Logs\ writes to C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\ which is writable by defaultuser0 in OOBE).'
    $buildMarkerLines += '2260: FIX — Write-FbDeferredOobeSoundWuMirrorLog: added ProgramData secondary log destination (C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\WU-oobe-sound-handoff-mirror.log); C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\Logs\ is not writable by defaultuser0 in OOBE, causing silent log loss that masked all 2259 execution diagnostics in dump 924.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2261 bug markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += '2261: ROOT CAUSE FIX (dump fb-dump-final-1101) — Three layered failures proved by dump: (1) FirstBaseSplashSingleInstance.ps1 + FirstBaseSplashWatcher.ps1 still on disk after finalize ran; (2) real-user splash on desktop login; (3) startup registry keys not cleaned up. Root causes: OOBE defaultuser0 session terminates while DeferredOobeSound is waiting for Settings to close (operator creates account while Settings window is open), killing DeferredOobeSound before the 2260 inline seal executes; .pipeline-completed never written; SplashWatcher OnStart task still registered fires at real-user boot and — finding .pipeline-completed absent — waits for interactive Explorer and shows splash. Secondary: even when inline seal DID run, finalize script scrub ran while splash processes were still alive (kill happened AFTER scrub); locked splash scripts deferred to ping-n-9 batch; shutdown /s /t 0 fired before 8-second batch delay expired; scripts survived.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '2261: FIX — FirstBaseOobeDeferredSoundBootstrap.ps1 (v1.0.5): emergency disarm is now COMPREHENSIVE. Prior v1.0.4 only removed HKLM Run + optionally restored Userinit; left .pipeline-completed absent, SplashWatcher task active, and RunOnce splash key potentially active. v1.0.5 writes .pipeline-completed FIRST (SplashWatcher + SplashSingleInstance both check this marker and exit immediately if present), writes .firstbase-sealed, removes all RunOnce+Run delivery keys, deletes all splash/delivery scheduled tasks (SplashWatcher, UpdateSplash, UpdateSplashLayer4, DeferredOobeSoundOnLogon, DebugHotkeyWatcher), removes All Users Startup cmd, removes arm markers, directly deletes known OOBE-phase splash/delivery .ps1 files from Setup\FirstBase and ProgramData\FirstBase. This handles the scenario where DeferredOobeSound is killed before the inline seal runs.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '2261: FIX — FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 (v1.0.17): .pipeline-completed is now the ABSOLUTE FIRST I/O in the inline seal (before any logging). Prior 2260 logged first, adding 4-5 file I/O operations before the marker write and widening the kill-window. Splash process kill added INSIDE the inline seal (before shutdown /s /t 30) so finalize file scrub finds splash .ps1 files UNLOCKED; eliminates the deferred-delete batch race with shutdown.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '2261: FIX — FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 (v1.0.18): splash process kill moved to BEFORE the script file scrub (was AFTER). Added Start-Sleep -Milliseconds 1500 after kill to let the OS release file handles before Remove-Item runs. Prior 2260 build killed processes after scrub; locked splash files were deferred to a ping-n-9 batch that shutdown /s /t 0 killed before it could run (scripts survived = real-user splash).'
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2262 bug markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += '2262: ROOT CAUSE ANALYSIS (dump fb-dump-final-1134) — Device ran 2259-sync-finalize. DeferredOobeSound spawned for defaultuser0 at 09:53; Settings was never opened (OOBE waited ~66 min); OOBE session was killed when real user "Test" logged in at 10:59. No inline seal (2260 not present), no comprehensive emergency disarm (2261 not present). Session lock left on disk by killed defaultuser0 instance. Bootstrap 2259 emergency disarm only restored Userinit. DeferredOobeSound for real user (session=3, 11:33) hit session lock — pid reused after 1h, stale detection returned false-negative (saw alive process, did not clear lock). All 12 OOBE-overlay-preserved scripts survived to real-user desktop.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '2262: FIX — FirstBaseOobeDeferredSoundBootstrap.ps1 (v1.0.6): emergency disarm script file deletion changed from 9-entry named list to *.ps1/*.cmd glob scan of both dirs (C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase + ProgramData\FirstBase). The 2261 named list missed FirstBaseConsoleSplash.cmd, FirstBaseConsoleSplashInstance.ps1, FirstBaseConsoleSplashRender.ps1, and FirstBaseSplashLauncher.cmd — all 4 are in splashKeepLeaves (Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub) and survive the pre-sysprep OOBE overlay scrub. Glob approach matches FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 and is self-maintaining: future additions to splashKeepLeaves are deleted by emergency disarm without a separate list update.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '2262: FIX — FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 (v1.0.18): Remove-FbDeferredOobeSoundSessionLockIfStale now verifies via Win32_Process.CommandLine that the live process holding the stored PID actually is FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1. If the CommandLine does not match, the PID was reused by an unrelated process and the lock is cleared as stale. Prevents false-negative on 1h+ old session lock where the original pid number belongs to a different process.'
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2263 bug markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += '2263: FIX (dump fb-dump-final-1134) — Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub STEP 5 false SCRUB-INCOMPLETE. Root cause: STEP 4 intentionally preserves 12 splash/overlay .ps1/.cmd files when splashOobeOverlayActive, logging each as PRESERVE. STEP 5 re-enumerated the same scrub roots without mirroring that exclusion, counted all 12 as UNEXPECTED-RESIDUAL, accumulated them in $residualNotes, and emitted SCRUB-INCOMPLETE even though the scrub was correct. Similarly STEP 5 registry re-walk reported !FirstBaseWuSplash (preserved by STEP 3 OOBE overlay) as a residual registry value. Both false positives added noise to the pre-oobe-scrub.log and were surfaced as ERROR in WU-*.log. Neither blocked sysprep (caller try/catch), but they masked real failures and wasted analyst time.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '2263: FIX — Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1 (v1.0.32): STEP 5 now builds $overlayPreservedLeafSet (HashSet, OrdinalIgnoreCase) mirroring STEP 4 $splashKeepLeaves when splashOobeOverlayActive. Per-file loop skips leaves in that set with log tag OVERLAY-PRESERVED instead of UNEXPECTED-RESIDUAL; they do not add to $residualNotes. STEP 5 registry re-walk mirrors STEP 3 WuSplash guard (same predicate: $splashOobeOverlayActive -and $valueName -match WuSplash). With both fixes applied, a clean OOBE overlay scrub emits SCRUB-COMPLETE.'
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2264 build markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += '2264: HANDOFF-SPEED — Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared: added quick pre-probe before Invoke-FbCbsCleanup. If CBS is already settled on first probe, DISM /startcomponentcleanup + sfc /scannow are skipped entirely (saves 30s–6 minutes). Only runs cleanup when CBS is still pending on the pre-probe. NOTE (handoff-speed) comments added on every delay in the handoff path.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '2264: RESTART — NOTE: this /s-to-/r change was reverted in 2265 (see below). Closing Settings must shut down the device, not restart it.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '2264: STATUS-DISPLAY — Show-UpdateProgress.ps1: ActivityText no longer surfaces specific KB IDs or update titles. UpdatesList Title column blanked. Only HeaderText / PassText (general status) and Status/Retries columns remain. NOTE (2264) comments on each suppression.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '2264: DEAD-CODE — Show-UpdateProgress.ps1: removed unused Activity field + KB/install detection block from Get-StatusFromLog (caller always sets activityText.Text to empty string, discarding the value); removed unused Message field from Get-StatusFromStateFile return object (caller never references it).'
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2265 build markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += '2265: REVERT-SHUTDOWN — Reverted erroneous /s-to-/r change introduced in 2264. Closing Settings MUST shut down the device (/s), not restart it. The shutdown-during-account-creation observed in QA testing is Windows/Sysprep OOBE built-in behavior, not FirstBase. No FirstBase /s call requires a /r substitution. FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 v1.0.19->1.0.20, FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 v1.0.19->1.0.20.'
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2266 build markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += '2266: STATUS-RESTORE — Show-UpdateProgress.ps1 v1.0.18->1.0.19: (1) UpdatesList Title column restored — Get-StatusFromStateFile now binds $u.Title so update names (including embedded KB numbers) show in the table again; the 2264 blank is reverted. (2) ActivityText (status area below header) now shows pipeline state + script version via new Get-FbSplashStateVersionText helper ("State: <phase> | v<ver>"); the area is no longer blank. UpdatesList Status/Retries columns unchanged; KB IDs are NOT separately surfaced in the status line.'
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2267 build markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += '2267: RELEASE-1.0.0 — First public release. All front-facing script semvers reset to 1.0.0 in FirstBaseVersion.ps1/$FirstBaseScriptVersion. PayloadRevision set to 2267-release-1.0.0. BuildStatus set to release. USB root log files (FirstBase-winpe.log, FirstBase-WIPE-PENDING.txt, FirstBase-BuildIdentity.txt, FirstBase-Layout.txt) relocated from USB drive root to FirstBase-UsbLogs\ subdirectory.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2268 build markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += '2268: SEAL-ESCAPE-FIX (fb-dump-final-907) — Two post-seal escape bugs fixed. (1) VISIBLE PS WINDOW: All five delivery-command-line builders in Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1 (Get-FbDeferredOobeSoundProgramDataTr, Get-FbDeferredOobeSoundBootstrapProgramDataTr x2, Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundProgramDataStartup startup .cmd body, Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundRunOnce local $cmdLine, Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundOnLogonTaskPreSysprep schtasks /TR) were using -WindowStyle Normal (introduced 2222 for triage visibility). Root cause confirmed via dump: HKLM Run key OpenWindowsSoundSettingsDeferredLogon had -WindowStyle Normal; when the real user (Test) logged in, the bootstrap process was visible alongside the Settings window. All five sites changed to -WindowStyle Hidden. (2) CONFIRMATION PROMPT GUARD: FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 now sets $ConfirmPreference = None at script top and passes -Confirm:$false to the Remove-Item -Recurse call on the Active Setup registry key, ensuring no Remove-Item call can surface an interactive prompt regardless of PowerShell host preference state. Shutdown calls in FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 verified all /s (not /r): inline seal /s /t 30 /f + all three fallback paths /s /t 0 /f. Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1 v1.0.0->1.0.1; FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 v1.0.0->1.0.1.'
    $buildMarkerLines += ''
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2269 build markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += '2269: PIPELINE-COMPLETED-EARLY-WRITE (fb-dump-final-1058) — DeferredOobeSound re-fires during new-user OOBE account creation. Root cause: .pipeline-completed was only written inside the inline seal (after Settings close); if the OOBE session was killed while Settings was open the marker was never written, the bootstrap saw arm markers on next logon and re-spawned DeferredOobeSound, opening ms-settings:sound again in the real user OOBE and triggering a second shutdown /s /t 30 mid-account-creation. Dump confirmed: .pipeline-completed absent at bootstrap entry 09:48:45 (user=defaultuser0, PID 7592); DeferredOobeSound re-fired as user=Test session=2 at 09:54:49 with runKeyMarker=True; .pipeline-completed only appeared at 10:56:04 written by bootstrap emergency disarm (PID 9400 user=Test), not the inline seal. Fix: .pipeline-completed is now written at the ABSOLUTE TOP of FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 (before session lock, version load, entry log, or any other I/O) as a re-fire guard to both C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\ and C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\Logs\. The inline seal continues to overwrite the marker with a completed timestamp. Bootstrap guard order verified correct: .pipeline-completed is checked first (lines 44-47) before arm markers (lines 56-61). Emergency disarm path verified: writes .pipeline-completed immediately, removes all Run/RunOnce delivery keys, removes arm markers, deletes *.ps1/*.cmd from both FirstBase dirs, exits 0 with no Settings launch and no shutdown. Finalize verified idempotent: Set-Content -Force on .pipeline-completed, all Remove-Item in try/catch, final shutdown /s /t 0 unconditional. FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 v1.0.0->1.0.1.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2270 build markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += '2270: SESSION-GUARD-FIX (fb-dump-final-1106/1213) — Pipeline fires in wrong session: nothing happens during OOBE (defaultuser0), then Settings + splash appear for real user with device shutdown. Two-part root cause confirmed from dumps. ROOT CAUSE A (fb-dump-final-1106, WU-20260610-110108.log line [11:03:32]): DeferredOobeSoundOnLogon ONLOGON schtask creation fails pre-sysprep with exit=1 "ERROR: No mapping between account names and security IDs was done." (SYSTEM context, no /RU SYSTEM flag, schtasks.exe cannot resolve INTERACTIVE SID pre-sysprep). Fallback fires (2248): Winlogon\Userinit injection succeeds and writes .deferred-oobe-sound-userinit-armed. Bootstrap $armed check (lines 56-61) did NOT include $UserinItMarker — only runonce/run-key/active-setup/startup were checked — so for defaultuser0 in OOBE all four tests were FALSE and bootstrap exited "no deferred arm markers; exit 0" without ever launching DeferredOobeSound. Confirmed by WU-oobe-sound-bootstrap.log: "entry PID=9460 user=defaultuser0 session=1" followed immediately by "no deferred arm markers; exit 0." ROOT CAUSE B (fb-dump-final-1213): HKLM RunOnce !FirstBaseWuSplash is NOT consumed by defaultuser0 autologon — Sysprep OOBE auto-logon bypasses HKLM\RunOnce processing. !FirstBaseWuSplash therefore persists through the OOBE session and fires for the first real interactive user logon. The 2261 real-user guard (which would have disarmed everything) was placed AFTER the arm-marker exit, so when userinit-armed was the only arm marker and the four prior tests all returned FALSE the bootstrap exited before ever reaching the 2261 guard. Confirmed by tasklist.txt (1106 and 1213 both show !FirstBaseWuSplash present in HKLM RunOnce) and splash-ui.log (1213 shows Show-UpdateProgress.ps1 started in Session 2 user=Test at 12:12:18). FIX 1 — $UserinItMarker added to $armed expression in FirstBaseOobeDeferredSoundBootstrap.ps1; bootstrap now recognizes the userinit fallback arm path and proceeds into DeferredOobeSound for defaultuser0 OOBE. FIX 2 — New 2270 early user-context guard inserted in FirstBaseOobeDeferredSoundBootstrap.ps1 BEFORE the arm-marker exit. Condition: $env:USERNAME not in {defaultuser0, SYSTEM, Administrator} AND .firstbase-sealed absent. On trigger: writes .pipeline-completed (suppresses splash SplashWatcher/SplashSingleInstance on all subsequent logons), writes .firstbase-sealed, removes HKLM Run OpenWindowsSoundSettingsDeferredLogon + RunOnce OpenWindowsSoundSettingsDeferredOnce + RunOnce !FirstBaseWuSplash, restores Winlogon\Userinit if FirstBase-injected, removes All Users Startup FirstBaseOobeSoundAtLogon.cmd, deletes all FirstBase scheduled tasks (DeferredOobeSoundOnLogon/SplashWatcher/UpdateSplash/UpdateSplashLayer4/DebugHotkeyWatcher), deletes all delivery arm markers (all six plus userinit-armed plus lock), deletes all *.ps1/*.cmd from both FirstBase script directories, exits 0. FIX 3 — userinit-armed added to Test-FbDeferredOobeSoundDeliveryArmed in FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 so DeferredOobeSound also logs the correct armed state. Finalize already had $DeferredUserInitArm in its cleanup list (line 22 + line 262) — no change needed. VERSIONS: FirstBaseOobeDeferredSoundBootstrap.ps1 v1.0.0->1.0.1. FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 v1.0.1->1.0.2. Payload revision 2269-pipeline-completed-early-write->2270-session-guard-fix.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2271 build markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += '2271: DUAL-FIRE-FIX (fb-dump-final-1423) — DeferredOobeSound opens Settings for real user (Test) despite 2270 guard in bootstrap. Root cause confirmed: Winlogon\Userinit fires the bootstrap BEFORE $env:USERNAME is populated (user environment not yet loaded at Userinit time). The bootstrap 2270 guard included [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($env:USERNAME) as an "OOBE session" passthrough; with an uninitialized env this evaluates TRUE and the guard is bypassed. Bootstrap falls through arm-marker check (markers present: runonce-armed, run-key-armed, userinit-armed), stale bootstrap lock cleared (defaultuser0 DeferredOobeSound PID dead after OOBE session ended), bootstrap spawns DeferredOobeSound in Test session=2. By the time DeferredOobeSound runs (seconds later), $env:USERNAME IS "Test". Dump evidence: .pipeline-completed content = "Pipeline started at 2026-06-10T14:09:36.6028258-06:00 (FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 PID 1976)" written by the Test-session DeferredOobeSound at 14:09:36, NOT the expected OOBE 12:34 timestamp. WU-oobe-sound-handoff-mirror.log: entry user=Test session=2 at 14:09:36 with deliveryArmed=True runKeyMarker=True, then Settings launched at 14:09:37. Bootstrap log: only defaultuser0 12:34 entry; no Test entry (log write failed silently at early Userinit before user profile accessible). HKLM\Run and Winlogon\Userinit both still armed at dump time (14:23 capture during Test Settings wait). FIX A — 2271 user-context guard added to FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 BEFORE the 2269 .pipeline-completed early write (absolute first code in script, after path-variable setup). Guard checks $env:USERNAME (populated by the time DeferredOobeSound runs); if real user: log ABORT to both handoff-mirror paths, write .pipeline-completed + .firstbase-sealed, remove Run/RunOnce/Userinit/Startup/sched-tasks/arm-markers, scrub *.ps1/*.cmd from both directories, exit 0. NO Settings window, NO shutdown. FIX B — Bootstrap 2270 guard hardened: when $env:USERNAME is empty, resolve via [System.Security.Principal.WindowsIdentity]::GetCurrent().Name; treat empty-after-resolution as real user (fail-safe). Also applied same resolution to the 2250 secondary guard. FIX C — Finalize deferred-delete batch ping delay changed from 9 iterations (~8s) to 3 iterations (~2s); finalize shutdown changed from /t 0 to /t 3. DeferredOobeSound exits within ~1s of finalize returning; OS releases DeferredOobeSound.ps1 file handle; batch deletes self and FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 within the 3s window. Root cause of 131-deferred-items in prior pass: splash processes not yet killed at scrub time (fixed in 2261) and shutdown /t 0 killed the 8s-delay batch before it could run. Q4 scrub status: dump captured mid-execution (Settings wait at 14:09:39, dump at 14:23); scripts present in ProgramData are expected at that point — scrub runs in finalize AFTER Settings closes. Not a scrub failure. VERSIONS: FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 v1.0.2->1.0.3. FirstBaseOobeDeferredSoundBootstrap.ps1 v1.0.1->1.0.2. FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 v1.0.1->1.0.2. Payload revision 2270-session-guard-fix->2271-dual-fire-fix.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2272 build markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += '2272: SCRUB-FIX (fb-dump-final-1548) — Scripts survive on device after pipeline completes. ROOT CAUSE A (primary): Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath <dir> -Include ''*.ps1'',''*.cmd'' -Recurse returns ALL files in PowerShell 5.1 — not just matching extensions. Confirmed by dump: .log files appeared in "2258: deleted script" finalize log lines, and 120 non-script files filled the deferred-delete list (should have been ~2 files: self + DeferredOobeSound.ps1). ROOT CAUSE B (secondary): The deferred-delete batch had 120 del commands and a ping -n 3 (~2s) head start; the shutdown /s /t 3 fired ~6-9s after batch spawn, leaving only ~4-7s of actual deletion time — insufficient for 120 files. FIX A (FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 v1.0.2->1.0.3): Replaced Get-ChildItem -Include with pipeline | Where-Object { Extension -ieq ''.ps1'' -or Extension -ieq ''.cmd'' } in the scrub loop so only actual script files are collected. FIX B (FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1): Changed Remove-Item from ErrorAction Stop (deferred on any throw) to ErrorAction SilentlyContinue + Test-Path check so non-terminating errors are caught without exceptions. FIX C (FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1): Added retry pass (800ms sleep, re-attempt all deferred non-self files) after the main scrub loop — handles files whose transient lock clears within the first second. FIX D (FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1): Increased deferred-delete batch ping delay from -n 3 (~2s) to -n 6 (~5s) to give this process adequate time to exit before the batch runs self-deletion. FIX E (FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1): Changed final shutdown from /s /t 3 (no /f) to /s /t 8 /f — 8s countdown gives the batch full runway after the 5s ping; /f prevents the OOBE shell from blocking shutdown and causing a re-fire via Winlogon Userinit. FIX F (FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 v1.0.3->1.0.4): Same Where-Object filter fix for the 2271 user-context guard scrub. FIX G (FirstBaseOobeDeferredSoundBootstrap.ps1 v1.0.2->1.0.3): Same Where-Object filter fix for both the 2270 early-guard scrub and the 2262 emergency disarm scrub. Payload revision 2271-dual-fire-fix->2272-scrub-fix.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2273 build markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += '2273: SCRUB-ELEVATION-FIX (fb-dump-final-603) — All 21 C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\ deletes fail with "Access to the path is denied" when finalize runs as defaultuser0. The 2272 Where-Object filter fix correctly enumerated only .ps1/.cmd files, but EVERY delete was denied because C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\ is SYSTEM-owned with no write ACE for defaultuser0 or non-admin users. The self-delete batch also failed for the same reason. Root cause: no ACL grant was ever applied to the Setup path for the OOBE session user. FIX (Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1 v1.0.1->1.0.2): New STEP 0 added at the top of Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub (runs as SYSTEM in Audit Mode, pre-sysprep). STEP 0 calls icacls.exe /grant with (OI)(CI)M (Modify, inheritable to all child objects and future files) for two principals: (1) defaultuser0 — the Windows built-in OOBE auto-logon account, which runs FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 in OOBE; (2) Users — the local built-in group, which covers FirstBaseOobeDeferredSoundBootstrap.ps1 and FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 emergency disarm paths that may execute as a non-admin real user (same "Access denied" failure mode). The ACE is applied to C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\ with /T (all children) before sysprep\generalize; it propagates through OOBE and is present when finalize or emergency disarm deletes scripts. icacls exit code logged to pre-oobe-scrub.log. Timeout 10 000ms with process kill on timeout. Payload revision 2272-scrub-fix->2273-scrub-elevation-fix.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2290 build markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += '2290: PROGRAMDATA-ACL-FIX (PF3THF6C-1004-Lenovo, 2026-07-06) — All 9 C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\ scripts survive FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 with "Access to the path is denied" on every Remove-Item; deferred-delete batch (FbFinalizeScriptScrub.cmd) also fails. Root cause: C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\ files are staged by SYSTEM in Audit Mode; no write ACE exists for defaultuser0 or Users. STEP 0 (2273) only grants Modify on C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\ — which is why Setup-path deletes succeed — but the identical gap for ProgramData was never addressed. FIX (Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1): New STEP 0-B added immediately after STEP 0 in Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub. Runs as SYSTEM pre-sysprep. Calls icacls.exe /grant (OI)(CI)M /T /Q for defaultuser0 and Users on $FbProgramDataFirstBase (C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\). $FbProgramDataFirstBase is guaranteed present at STEP 0-B time: Invoke-FbOobeHandoff STAGE 2 creates the directory at line ~12599 before calling Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub. Pattern mirrors 2273 STEP 0 exactly (Start-Process icacls.exe, WaitForExit 10 000ms, kill on timeout, per-principal loop, $writeScrubLog INFO/WARN). Payload revision 2289-loop-trigger-cleanup->2290-programdata-acl-fix.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2274 build markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += '2274: SHUTDOWN-FALLBACK-FIX (fb-dump-final-939) — Device stayed running after operator closed Settings and inline seal ran to completion. ROOT CAUSE: FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 Secure Boot check issued shutdown /r /fw /t 0 with ErrorAction SilentlyContinue and no exit-code verification. Dump evidence: finalize log [09:36:15] "WARNING: Secure Boot DISABLED — rebooting to UEFI firmware (/r /fw)"; tasklist captured at 09:39:48 with Explorer still running; build-version.txt LastBoot=09:31:10 confirming no reboot occurred. Mechanism: Windows shutdown.exe aborts any pending shutdown before registering the new action; the inline seal''s /s /t 30 /f (issued 09:36:04) was already cancelled when finalize called /r /fw, then /r /fw failed silently on this hardware (device does not support firmware reboot via shutdown.exe under defaultuser0, or lacked SE_SHUTDOWN_PRIVILEGE for /fw) — leaving the device with no pending shutdown. Q1: DeferredOobeSound reached and completed the wait loop; SystemSettings wait end logged at 09:36:03 (~48s after Settings launched). Q2/Q3: Script polls Get-Process -Name SystemSettings every 2000ms — correctly detected Settings open and closed. Q4: Tasklist shows no DeferredOobeSound or SystemSettings at dump time; pipeline ran to completion. Q5: No stale lock; DeferredOobeSound acquired lock and ran fully. FIX (FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 v1.0.3->1.0.4): /r /fw path changed to Start-Process -PassThru with WaitForExit(8000) and ExitCode check; ExitCode != 0 (or Start-Process throw) triggers immediate fallback to shutdown /s /t 8 /f; WaitForExit timeout (8s) treated as success (firmware reboot assumed in progress). /t changed 0->3 to give shutdown.exe time to return before the reboot fires. FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 v1.0.4->1.0.5 (version bump, no behavior change). Payload revision 2273-scrub-elevation-fix->2274-settings-wait-fix.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '=== 2293 build markers ==='
    $buildMarkerLines += '2293: STARTUP-NO-CMDWINDOW — FirstBaseOobeSoundAtLogon.cmd replaced with FirstBaseOobeSoundAtLogon.vbs. The CMD-hosted All Users Startup launcher caused a brief console window flash on every user logon (including OOBE defaultuser0). Root cause: even with @echo off + start /b, Windows still spawns a visible CMD host process momentarily. Fix: Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundProgramDataStartup (Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1) now writes a two-line VBScript using WScript.Shell.Run with window style 0 (hidden) — wscript.exe runs .vbs files natively with no console window. SetupComplete.cmd :ARM_DEFERRED_OOBE_SOUND_IF_PENDING updated to echo the same VBScript content into the .vbs. All cleanup Remove-Item references updated from .cmd to .vbs in: FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 ($DeferredStartupCmd), FirstBaseOobeDeferredSoundBootstrap.ps1 ($fb2270StartupCmd early guard + $fb2261StartupCmd emergency disarm), FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 ($fb2271DosStartupCmd early guard + $fb2260StartupCmd inline cleanup). Payload revision 2292-uso-restart-suppress->2293-startup-no-cmdwindow.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '2296: SETTINGS-CLOSE-PRIVILEGED-SCRUB (dump PF4VGG7V-1648) — MDM/Autopilot residue confirmed on a real post-sysprep device: AutoEnrollMDM=0 + DmEnrollmentSvc=Disabled survived PAST settings-close. Root cause: FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 runs in the LIMITED OOBE defaultuser0 token (finalize-mirror shows Winlogon HKLM writes fail "Requested registry access is not allowed") and its 2277 Remove-ItemProperty/Set-Service teardown used -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue + an unconditional INFO log, so it FALSE-SUCCEEDED while the blocks persisted. Fix: new Register-FbMdmScrubTaskPreSysprep (STAGE 2, pre-sysprep, SYSTEM) writes C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\FirstBaseMdmScrub.ps1 (inline here-string; no staged payload file) and registers triggerless SYSTEM task FirstBase\MdmScrub (/RU SYSTEM /RL HIGHEST /SC ONCE far-future). Task XML + ProgramData script survive generalize; both excluded from Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub STEP 2. The block STAYS applied through sysprep + the whole OOBE sit period. At settings-close FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 (2296) triggers the task via schtasks /Run (default task SD grants Authenticated Users Execute, so the limited finalize can launch it) + Start-ScheduledTask fallback, then VERIFIES the JSON result marker .mdm-scrub-result (loud ERROR, no silent swallow, if overall!=PASS). MdmScrub removes MDM keys + DmEnrollmentSvc (hard-verified) + FirstBase-Block-Autopilot firewall (hard-verified) + Defender exclusions + WU AU policy + recovery BCD (best-effort), writes WU-mdm-scrub.log, then self-deletes its task. Manual Tools\seal_command.txt seal path also gets native elevated reg/sc/Remove-NetFirewallRule teardown. Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1 1.0.4->1.0.5, FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 1.0.7->1.0.8; launcher 5.0.35->5.0.36.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '5.4.2: Dell BWT3CB4 - wait for CBS/TiWorker before reboot ladder; do not re-issue shutdown.exe while RebootInProgress; do not Stop TrustedInstaller while applying; Tier 7 ManualRestart splash; overlay.mode written at STAGE 2 only; hardware gate fails on pending CBS or critical PnP Error. Loop 1.0.13->1.0.14.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '5.4.3: FBTGJB4 - after Settings close in Audit, drop overlay.mode so pre-sysprep STEP 4 deletes splash/operator scripts (including ProgramData kit); arm FinalShipReaper before sysprep so /reboot cannot skip cleanup; PnP gate no longer FAILs the box on Get-PnpDevice Argument types do not match. Loop 1.0.14->1.0.15.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '5.4.4: WinPE wolf is built from Braille code points so Invoke-FbDeployUi.ps1 stays ASCII (no-BOM UTF-8 Braille was garbled in WinPE powershell). Splash STATUS/activity/handoff copy is ASCII-only; Convert-FbSplashTextToAscii strips em-dashes and other non-ASCII. Show-UpdateProgress 3.0.5->3.0.6; FirstBaseHandoffSplash 1.0.2->1.0.3.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '5.4.5: WinPE wolf tries Consolas/Cascadia for Braille then falls back to block/half-block art (raster fonts showed # for U+28xx). Splash SW_HIDE OOBE/WWAHost on each Audit boot (not SW_MINIMIZE; 2214j black-layer trap). Show-UpdateProgress 3.0.6->3.0.7.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '5.4.6: TechInstall no longer disarms USB after apply; EFI loaders and sources\boot.wim stay in place so the stick remains bootable. Reset-UsbBoot is optional recovery only.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '5.4.7: Launcher no longer ships Re-arm USB; Reset-UsbBoot.ps1 and usb:rearm IPC are removed from the product surface.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '5.4.13: Dump 5CD107C4W3-0949-HP - Wait-FbOsShutdownEvidence requires LastBootUpTime delta >= 10s (equal/same-second CIM vs WMI is not a reboot); always /F recreate SYSTEM ONSTART loop+splash (Delay 30s, battery-permissive, no /IT) before reboot so OOBE-resealed WU reboots re-arm without RunOnce. Loop 1.0.16->1.0.17.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '5.4.14: Dumps PF2X3W8J-1209-LENOVO / J51DW33-1209-DellInc / 5CD107C4W3-1209-HP - Repair-FbScheduledTasks /Delete then Start-Process /Create with quoted powershell /TR returned -2147467259; dump-time schtasks had WindowsUpdateLoop + OnLogon + SplashWatcher GONE. Never delete live TNs; create via schtasks.exe + FirstBaseLoopLauncher.cmd; add WindowsUpdateLoopBoot2 ONSTART DELAY 90s SYSTEM and WindowsUpdateLoopAnyLogon ONLOGON DELAY 45s SYSTEM (no /IT) plus SplashWatcherBoot2. Loop 1.0.17->1.0.18.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '5.4.18: Dumps PF12VZ2W-1331-LENOVO / 5CD930865J-1332-HP - splash stuck downloading/installing with reboot count 0. A: pending reboot after real installs this boot reboots before remaining Driver/Cumulative buckets; InstallsCompleted latches only at OOBE handoff (not after Critical). B: Recall DISM before first WU scan; CBS cleanup skipped while Driver Install() is running. C: per-update Install timeout 600s, soft-skip that update this boot, do not stop TrustedInstaller. Loop 1.0.18->1.0.19. Engine 1.0.3->1.0.4.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '5.4.19: Same boxes after dirty reboot stuck on Windows Just a moment (pre-restart). D: do not treat Critical-only InstallsCompleted as skip-cascade / customer OOBE; unlatch and resume WU in Audit until Driver/Cumulative done or seal. Do not SW_HIDE WWAHost/CloudExperienceHost unless splash is confirmed visible and the WU loop painting marker PID is live. If reboot is still pending and not at seal, Invoke-FbReboot ladder instead of leaving CXH up. Loop 1.0.19->1.0.20. Show-UpdateProgress 3.0.7->3.0.8.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '5.4.20: PF2X3W8J-0705 BITS Start-Service wait capped at 90s (no overnight Restart-Service hang). PF12VZ2W-0703 transcript flush + Escape valve: handing off (flush) before OOBE handoff/sysprep. Splash re-asserts SW_HIDE of language/keyboard/region and CXH error chrome each tick while .wu-loop-painting + splash visible; restore at seal. Loop 1.0.20->1.0.21. Show-UpdateProgress 3.0.8->3.0.9.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '5.4.21: Dumps 5CD930865J-0833-HP / PF12VZ2W-0833-LENOVO - hung OEM driver looks like a stuck download (0% during Install() or unbounded Download()). Per-bucket Download timeout 720s then continue to install; do not stop TrustedInstaller. RebootIterations incremented at Invoke-FbReboot; Save-State no longer zeros BootCount/RebootIterations when omitted (HP Tier 7 wipe). Splash REBOOTS uses RebootIterations. Loop 1.0.21->1.0.22. Engine 1.0.4->1.0.5. Show-UpdateProgress 3.0.9->3.0.10.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '5.4.22: Dumps PF2X3W8J-0934 / PF12VZ2W-0937 / 5CD930865J-0938 (all 5.4.20). SetupComplete treated HKLM InstallDate churn as FRESH and deleted wu-state mid-Audit (ledger, RI, BootCount). Preserve live pipeline state; skip re-apply of success-ledger rows; timeout of an already-completed row must not Invoke-FbReboot the bucket. Empty work queue plus no OS reboot is not a 0-pending restart loop. MaxRebootIterations still defers to OOBE and must not zero counters. Loop 1.0.22->1.0.23. Engine 1.0.5->1.0.6. SetupComplete 1.0.4->1.0.5.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '5.4.24: WinPE wolf private-loads Deploy\Fonts\cascadiamono.ttf (no raster U+28xx ?). Splash dump copies Setup\FirstBase\Logs (WU-*.log) to USB FirstBase-Logs\<sn-time>; Hidden dump no longer dies on Console. Splash BG error=dark red, reboot/warn=orange, install=black. Loop 1.0.24->1.0.25. Show-UpdateProgress 3.0.11->3.0.12.'
    $buildMarkerLines += '5.4.2: Official release. Consolidates USB (no post-apply disarm, Explorer icon, hide FirstBase, FirstBase-Logs), WinPE Braille wolf + Cascadia private-load, hardware-gate PnP, operator close then sysprep /oobe /reboot, and WU pipeline fixes since 5.4.1. Loop 1.0.25.'
    [System.IO.File]::WriteAllLines($buildMarkerPath, $buildMarkerLines, [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8)
    Write-FbLog ("Build marker written: {0} (LoopVersion={1})" -f $buildMarkerPath, $loopVer) 'INFO'
} catch {
    try { Write-FbLog ("Build marker write threw: {0}; continuing (marker is diagnostic, not load-bearing)." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN' } catch {}
}

function Save-State {
    param(
        [int]$EmptySweepCount,
        [string]$LastPass,
        [string]$StartTime,
        [int]$FailureCount,
        [hashtable]$UpdateRetry = @{},
        [string[]]$GivenUpUpdateIds = @(),
        [hashtable]$BootCountByKey = @{},
        # Sentinel: -1 means preserve on-disk value. Explicit 0 is a real reset.
        # Tier 7 used to omit -BootCount and defaulted to 0 (HP dump 0833).
        [int]$BootCount = -1,
        [string[]]$ForceHiddenKeys = @(),
        [int]$RebootIterations = -1,
        [int]$SysprepRetryCount = 0,
        # Sentinel: -1 means "preserve whatever value is currently on
        # disk". Added in 2026.05.11.1145 so the dozen+ Save-State
        # call sites scattered across the iteration / final-check
        # codepaths don't accidentally reset CascadeExhaustionCount
        # to 0 every save. Only the two intentional mutation points
        # (increment-on-exhaustion in Invoke-Reboot, reset-on-clean-
        # startup at the top of the script) pass an explicit value.
        [int]$CascadeExhaustionCount = -1,
        # Sentinel: $null means "preserve whatever value is currently
        # on disk". Added in 2026.05.11.1230-skip-cascade-direct-
        # handoff. 5.4.18: InstallsCompleted is latched only when
        # the cascade is exhausted (OOBE handoff), not after the
        # first Critical success while Driver/Cumulative remain.
        # A true latch means a later invocation may skip the reboot
        # cascade and route to OOBE. Only Set-FbInstallsCompleted
        # (with LatchOobeHandoff) and the clean-startup reset path
        # ever pass a non-null value.
        [Nullable[bool]]$InstallsCompleted = $null,
        # Sentinel: -1 means "preserve whatever value is currently on
        # disk". Added in 2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence. This
        # is a CUMULATIVE counter (across reboots) of per-update
        # IInstallationResult rows that came back with ResultCode in
        # {2,3} (orcSucceeded / orcSucceededWithErrors). The latch
        # helper Set-FbInstallsCompleted is gated on this counter: a
        # latch can only persist if (a) this counter is already > 0
        # on disk, OR (b) we are bumping this counter in the same
        # Save-State call. No other rail can flip InstallsCompleted.
        [int]$RealInstallsTotal = -1,
        # Sentinel: $null means "preserve whatever value is currently
        # on disk". Added in 2026.05.12.1830-patient-shutdown-and-
        # hresult-coverage. Per-update retry ceiling, keyed via
        # Get-FbRetryKey (e.g. "KB:KB5036893"). When an update's
        # HResult is known to be reboot-recoverable (an entry in
        # $BenignHResultMap with Action='Reboot'), the bucket apply
        # lowers that update's ceiling to 1 - the only realistic
        # path to success is reboot-then-retry, so consuming the
        # full 3-retry budget in one boot is wasted work. Updates
        # without a known reboot-recoverable HResult keep the
        # default $MaxUpdateRetry (3). Honored by Invoke-FbBucketApply
        # when classifying unknown-HResult retries and by the retry-
        # ceiling exhaustion branch.
        [hashtable]$UpdateRetryBudget = $null,
        # 2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve: number of consecutive boots
        # the loop has run scan+apply with zero real installs (i.e.
        # RealInstallsTotal did not advance from start-of-boot to
        # end-of-boot AND scan returned >0 AND at least one bucket
        # apply was attempted). When this counter reaches the
        # operator-tunable threshold (default $FutileBootThreshold=3,
        # multiplied by EXTEND-BUDGET.flag if present), the escape
        # valve trips and we route to OOBE handoff with the updates
        # labelled "Failed - tech review required" instead of trying
        # forever. Sentinel: -1 means "preserve the on-disk value";
        # only Test-FbEscapeValve / Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff /
        # the per-boot finalize path ever pass an explicit value.
        [int]$FutileBootCount = -1,
        # 2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve: latch (false -> true) set by
        # Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff once the escape valve has
        # tripped. Persisted so that a relaunch (e.g. RunOnce fired
        # before sysprep completed) does not retry the doomed
        # install cycle - the relaunch reads true and routes
        # straight back to OOBE handoff. Sentinel: $null = preserve.
        [Nullable[bool]]$EscapeValveTripped = $null,
        # 2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve: free-form classifier string
        # set by Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff at the same time the
        # latch is set. Values currently emitted by the script:
        #   "FUTILE_BOOTS_<N>"   - threshold of N futile boots hit.
        #   "OPERATOR_OVERRIDE"  - FORCE-OOBE-HANDOFF.flag detected.
        #   "SYSPREP_REPEATEDLY_REFUSED" - sysprep returned 0x139f
        #     three times in a row from STAGE 2 of the two-stage
        #     handoff (build 1900). Surfaced on the dashboard
        #     activity bar prefix and recorded in the panic
        #     ESCAPE-VALVE-TRIPPED-*.json. Sentinel: $null
        # = preserve the existing on-disk value.
        [string]$EscapeValveReason = $null,
        # 2026.05.13.1900-two-stage-sysprep-handoff: STAGE 1 -> STAGE 2
        # latch. Set $true by Invoke-FbReboot when InstallsCompleted=
        # true AND Test-FbWuRebootRequired=true: STAGE 1 marks
        # PendingSysprep then falls through to the patient-shutdown
        # sequence so CBS can settle on the next boot. On the next
        # boot, the loop reads PendingSysprep=true AND (after CBS
        # has settled) Test-FbWuRebootRequired=false, and routes to
        # STAGE 2 (Invoke-FbOobeHandoff) which launches Sysprep with
        # -Wait. Set $false again only when Invoke-SysprepToOobe
        # observes exit code 0 and is about to return $true; that
        # write is the only legitimate clear. Sentinel: $null =
        # preserve the existing on-disk value (matches the
        # InstallsCompleted / EscapeValveTripped pattern).
        [Nullable[bool]]$PendingSysprep = $null,
        # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: number of
        # consecutive boots since RealInstallsTotal last advanced.
        # Drives the system-level escape valve in the new full-scope
        # multi-pass loop. Reset to 0 on a boot whose pass produced
        # at least one real install row; incremented otherwise (when
        # the work queue still holds pending updates). Sentinel: -1
        # = preserve the existing on-disk value. Only the per-boot
        # finalize path and the rescan-confirm path ever pass an
        # explicit value.
        [int]$BootsSinceLastInstall = -1,
        # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: persistent
        # work queue of updates the loop is still trying to apply.
        # One row per outstanding update with shape:
        #   {
        #     UpdateId      : string  # WUA UpdateID
        #     Bucket        : string  # Critical|Driver|Cumulative|Optional
        #     Status        : string  # Pending|RebootPending|RetryNextBoot|...
        #     RetryCount    : int
        #     LastHResult   : int
        #     FirstSeenBoot : int     # BootCount when first added
        #   }
        # Sentinel: $null = preserve the existing on-disk value.
        # Only Invoke-FbBucketApply mutates this map; every other
        # Save-State must preserve it.
        $WorkQueue = $null,
        # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: count of
        # applicable updates from the most recent fresh
        # Invoke-FbWuScan. Stamped by the per-pass scan at the top
        # of the loop. The convergence-confirm rescan compares this
        # to 0 on the boot AFTER a clean pass to declare full
        # convergence and route to STAGE 1 of sysprep handoff.
        # Sentinel: -1 = preserve the existing on-disk value.
        [int]$LastScanResultCount = -1,
        # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: latch (false
        # -> true). Set $true only by the convergence-confirm rescan
        # block after a previous pass left the work queue empty AND
        # the new fresh scan returned 0 applicable updates AND
        # Test-FbWuRebootRequired=false. Once set, the loop routes
        # to STAGE 1 sysprep handoff on the same boot. Sentinel:
        # $null = preserve the existing on-disk value.
        [Nullable[bool]]$ConvergenceConfirmed = $null,
        # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: number of
        # consecutive boots whose Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared exited
        # with OSPendingReboot=true (i.e. CBS never released the
        # pending-reboot signal even after Invoke-FbCbsCleanup ran
        # DISM /startcomponentcleanup + sfc /scannow + Get-WindowsUpdate
        # Log). When this counter reaches 5, the escape valve trips
        # with reason 'CBS_NEVER_SETTLES' and the device routes to
        # OOBE with the warning popup. Sentinel: -1 = preserve the
        # existing on-disk value.
        [int]$CbsPendingBootsCount = -1,
        # 2026.05.18.2213l Bug FF: per-UpdateId "consecutive-success-
        # but-still-offered" counter. Keyed by WUA UpdateId (GUID
        # string). Each entry counts how many times the same UpdateId
        # has returned ResultCode=2 from Invoke-FbWuApply AND been
        # re-offered by the very next scan. When the counter crosses
        # $FbSoftGivenUpThreshold (currently 2), the UpdateId is
        # promoted into GivenUpUpdateIds and the convergence gate
        # filters it out so STAGE 1 sysprep handoff can fire.
        # Sentinel: $null = preserve the existing on-disk value.
        # Only the per-pass scan post-processing block ever passes
        # an explicit value; every other Save-State must preserve.
        [hashtable]$SoftGivenUpCounters = $null,
        # 2026.05.18.2213l Bug FF: companion ledger to
        # $SoftGivenUpCounters. Records the set of UpdateIds that
        # have been successfully installed at least once in this
        # loop session (across all boots in the same update cycle).
        # The post-scan increment block in the main loop only bumps
        # the SoftGivenUp counter for UpdateIds in BOTH the current
        # scan AND this ledger. Persisted to wu-state.json so the
        # ledger survives reboots; cleared by SetupComplete.cmd's
        # per-image cleanup (same trigger as the rest of wu-state.json).
        # Sentinel: $null = preserve the existing on-disk value.
        [string[]]$SuccessLedgerIds = $null,
        # 2026.05.19.2213n Bug II: ScanStability state block. Keyed
        # entries hydrated into wu-state.json under "ScanStability":
        #   LastFingerprint   = lowercase-hex SHA256 of sorted blocking
        #                       UpdateIds (joined by `n) from the most
        #                       recent STAGE 1 OOBE handoff gate scan.
        #                       '' when blocking set was empty last time.
        #   ConsecutiveCount  = how many consecutive boot-level gate
        #                       scans have produced the same fingerprint.
        #                       Reaches $FbScanStabilityThreshold (= 2)
        #                       to trigger bulk-promotion to GivenUpSet.
        #   LastSeenAt        = ISO8601 timestamp of the last evaluation
        #                       (for human / dump readability).
        #   LastResidualIds   = sorted list of the blocking UpdateIds
        #                       at the last evaluation (matches the
        #                       fingerprint exactly; kept verbatim for
        #                       dump readability so an operator can see
        #                       WHICH update is stuck without recomputing
        #                       the hash).
        # Mutated ONLY by Invoke-FbScanStabilityEvaluate at the STAGE 1
        # OOBE handoff gate; every other Save-State call must preserve
        # the existing on-disk value via the sentinel $null below.
        [hashtable]$ScanStability = $null
    )

    $state = @{
        EmptySweepCount   = $EmptySweepCount
        LastPass          = $LastPass
        StartTime         = $StartTime
        FailureCount      = $FailureCount
        # Retry map is keyed via Get-FbRetryKey ("KB:KB5036893" or
        # "UID:<guid>") so retry accumulation survives both reboots
        # AND mid-cycle UpdateID supersedence. Old UID-only keys from
        # prior builds are still tolerated by Load-State for backward
        # compatibility - they just don't share counter slots with the
        # KB-keyed entries.
        UpdateRetry       = $UpdateRetry
        # Keys we have terminally classified (benign-skip, retry-exhausted,
        # force-hidden). Loop honors these on every rescan so a re-offered
        # update doesn't reset back to retry=0. Stored as a flat string list
        # for JSON-friendliness.
        GivenUpUpdateIds  = @($GivenUpUpdateIds)
        # Per-key reboot counter feeding the absolute escape hatch in
        # Invoke-FbBucketApply. If WUA keeps offering the same KB after
        # MaxKeyBootCount reboots, we hide it via IUpdate.IsHidden=$true
        # and mark Completed so the loop converges.
        BootCountByKey    = $BootCountByKey
        # Cumulative process-start count for this update cycle. Used by
        # the dashboard footer (ScriptVersion=... Boot=N) to give the
        # user a visible proof-of-patch indicator without log access.
        # Placeholder; -1 is replaced by on-disk preserve below.
        BootCount         = 0
        # Keys whose updates we've already force-hidden via the COM
        # IsHidden=$true escape hatch. Tracked to avoid re-hiding on
        # every scan and to surface "Force-hidden" on the dashboard.
        ForceHiddenKeys   = @($ForceHiddenKeys)
        # Number of reboots THIS LOOP has issued in the current cycle.
        # When it reaches $MaxRebootIterations, the loop force-completes
        # and hands off to sysprep/OOBE regardless of remaining updates.
        # Distinct from BootCount: BootCount = process-starts seen
        # (cosmetic), RebootIterations = reboots we caused (governance).
        RebootIterations  = 0
        # Number of times Invoke-SysprepToOobe has launched (or attempted
        # to launch) sysprep within this update cycle. When a sysprep
        # invocation fails, Invoke-SysprepToOobe falls through to the
        # 6-stage reboot cascade and RunOnce relaunches us on the next
        # boot; without this counter we'd ping-pong forever. Capped at
        # 3 attempts before we give up on sysprep entirely.
        SysprepRetryCount = $SysprepRetryCount
        # Number of consecutive full cascade-exhaustion cycles
        # (cascade budget exhausted AND logoff fallback ALSO failed
        # to relaunch us). Added in 2026.05.11.1145-cascade-exhausted-
        # handoff. Drives startup-time routing:
        #     0 -> normal cascade.
        #     1 -> Invoke-Reboot uses the SYSTEM-scoped scheduled-task
        #          emergency path INSTEAD of the user-mode cascade
        #          (escalation: the Task Scheduler service is one of
        #          the few things that can override a kernel-level
        #          TrustedInstaller shutdown veto, because the reboot
        #          is then driven from the SCM at HIGHEST integrity,
        #          not from our user-mode token).
        #   >=2 -> skip every reboot attempt at startup and jump
        #          directly to the OOBE handoff path: relabel pending-
        #          restart rows to Completed, surface the explicit
        #          "Windows is blocking automatic reboot - deferring
        #          to first user logon" message, run sysprep /oobe
        #          /reboot /quiet, exit cleanly so the watchdog does
        #          NOT relaunch us into another doomed cascade.
        # Reset to 0 on any clean (non-watchdog-recovery) startup so
        # the count only ever tracks consecutive failures.
        CascadeExhaustionCount = 0
        # Latch (false -> true, never back). 2026.05.12.1700: now
        # strictly gated by RealInstallsTotal > 0 (see below). The
        # latch only persists in conjunction with at least one real
        # per-update orcSucceeded/orcSucceededWithErrors result. UI
        # status strings, "past Download" counts, COM apply
        # exceptions, and Test-FbWuRebootRequired alone CANNOT flip
        # this latch any more. The result: a freshly-imaged box where
        # WUA's polling regresses to ResultCode=-1 (as observed in
        # WU-20260512-110026.log) leaves this $false and the loop
        # plain shutdown /r 's instead of silently sysprep-handoffing
        # with zero installs.
        InstallsCompleted = $false
        # 2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence: cumulative count
        # (across reboots within this update cycle) of per-update
        # results in {2,3} from
        # IInstallationResult.GetUpdateResult($i).ResultCode. The
        # ONLY signal that proves at least one real install
        # succeeded. Survives reboots so a successful install on
        # boot N is still visible as evidence on boot N+1's OOBE
        # handoff decision. Zeroed only on clean per-image-marker
        # state cleanup driven by SetupComplete.cmd.
        RealInstallsTotal = 0
        # 2026.05.12.1830-patient-shutdown-and-hresult-coverage:
        # per-update retry ceiling map. Keyed via Get-FbRetryKey.
        # Default ceiling for entries not in this map is
        # $MaxUpdateRetry. See the Save-State parameter doc above.
        UpdateRetryBudget = @{}
        # 2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve: see Save-State param docs.
        # Defaults populated here so a brand-new state file gets the
        # field; the sentinel-preserve blocks below honor the disk
        # value across the ~15 Save-State call sites that do not
        # pass an explicit value.
        FutileBootCount     = 0
        EscapeValveTripped  = $false
        EscapeValveReason   = ''
        # 2026.05.13.1900-two-stage-sysprep-handoff: see Save-State
        # param docs for $PendingSysprep. Default $false on a fresh
        # state file; sentinel-preserve block below carries the disk
        # value forward across the ~15 Save-State call sites that
        # do not pass an explicit value.
        PendingSysprep      = $false
        # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: see
        # Save-State param docs. Defaults populated here so a
        # fresh state file gets the field; the sentinel-preserve
        # blocks below honor the disk value across the ~15
        # Save-State call sites that do not pass an explicit value.
        BootsSinceLastInstall = 0
        WorkQueue             = @()
        LastScanResultCount   = 0
        ConvergenceConfirmed  = $false
        CbsPendingBootsCount  = 0
        # 2026.05.18.2213l Bug FF: see Save-State param docs above.
        # Defaults to empty on a fresh state file; the sentinel-
        # preserve blocks below honor the disk value across the ~15
        # Save-State call sites that do not pass an explicit value.
        SoftGivenUpCounters   = @{}
        SuccessLedgerIds      = @()
        # 2026.05.19.2213n Bug II: see Save-State $ScanStability param
        # doc above. Default block on a fresh state file is empty
        # fingerprint, zero count, empty timestamp, empty residual
        # list - guarantees the first STAGE 1 gate evaluation starts
        # a fresh count from zero.
        ScanStability         = @{
            LastFingerprint  = ''
            ConsecutiveCount = 0
            LastSeenAt       = ''
            LastResidualIds  = @()
        }
    }
    if ($BootCount -ge 0) {
        $diskBc = 0
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existingBc = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existingBc -and $existingBc.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'BootCount') {
                    try { $diskBc = [int]$existingBc.BootCount } catch { $diskBc = 0 }
                }
            }
        } catch {}
        if ($BootCount -eq 0 -and $diskBc -gt 0) {
            try { Write-FbLog ("Save-State refused BootCount=0 over live disk BootCount={0}." -f $diskBc) 'WARN' } catch {}
            $state.BootCount = $diskBc
        } else {
            $state.BootCount = $BootCount
        }
    } else {
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existingBc = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existingBc -and $existingBc.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'BootCount') {
                    try { $state.BootCount = [int]$existingBc.BootCount } catch { $state.BootCount = 0 }
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    if ($RebootIterations -ge 0) {
        $diskRi = 0
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existingRi = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existingRi -and $existingRi.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'RebootIterations') {
                    try { $diskRi = [int]$existingRi.RebootIterations } catch { $diskRi = 0 }
                }
            }
        } catch {}
        if ($RebootIterations -eq 0 -and $diskRi -gt 0) {
            try { Write-FbLog ("Save-State refused RebootIterations=0 over live disk RebootIterations={0}." -f $diskRi) 'WARN' } catch {}
            $state.RebootIterations = $diskRi
        } else {
            $state.RebootIterations = $RebootIterations
        }
    } else {
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existingRi = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existingRi -and $existingRi.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'RebootIterations') {
                    try { $state.RebootIterations = [int]$existingRi.RebootIterations } catch { $state.RebootIterations = 0 }
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    # Preserve the existing on-disk CascadeExhaustionCount whenever
    # the caller didn't explicitly pass one (sentinel -1). Only the
    # two intentional mutation points override this default.
    if ($CascadeExhaustionCount -ge 0) {
        $state.CascadeExhaustionCount = $CascadeExhaustionCount
    } else {
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existing = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existing -and $existing.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'CascadeExhaustionCount' -and $existing.CascadeExhaustionCount) {
                    $state.CascadeExhaustionCount = [int]$existing.CascadeExhaustionCount
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    # Preserve the existing on-disk InstallsCompleted whenever the
    # caller didn't explicitly pass one (sentinel $null). Without
    # this, every Save-State (there are ~15 sites) would clobber the
    # latch back to false. Only Set-FbInstallsCompleted and the
    # explicit reset path (clean startup with no installs-yet state)
    # pass a non-null value.
    if ($null -ne $InstallsCompleted) {
        $state.InstallsCompleted = [bool]$InstallsCompleted
    } else {
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existing = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existing -and $existing.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'InstallsCompleted') {
                    $state.InstallsCompleted = [bool]$existing.InstallsCompleted
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    # Preserve the existing on-disk RealInstallsTotal unless the
    # caller passed an explicit non-negative value. The two
    # intentional mutation points (Set-FbInstallsCompleted bumping
    # on a real per-update success, and the per-image cleanup reset
    # to 0) pass an explicit value; every other Save-State call
    # site passes nothing and the disk value is preserved.
    if ($RealInstallsTotal -ge 0) {
        $state.RealInstallsTotal = $RealInstallsTotal
    } else {
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existing = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existing -and $existing.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'RealInstallsTotal') {
                    try { $state.RealInstallsTotal = [int]$existing.RealInstallsTotal } catch { $state.RealInstallsTotal = 0 }
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    # 2026.05.12.1700: enforce the latch invariant on every persist.
    # If RealInstallsTotal is 0 but InstallsCompleted is being
    # written as $true, that is a bug in a caller - the latch can
    # only be $true when at least one real install row has been
    # observed. Force the latch back to $false so a buggy caller
    # cannot cause a silent OOBE bypass.
    if ([bool]$state.InstallsCompleted -and [int]$state.RealInstallsTotal -le 0) {
        try {
            Write-FbLog ("Save-State INVARIANT: InstallsCompleted=true with RealInstallsTotal={0} is illegal; forcing latch=false." -f $state.RealInstallsTotal) 'ERROR'
        } catch {}
        $state.InstallsCompleted = $false
    }
    # 2026.05.12.1830-patient-shutdown-and-hresult-coverage:
    # preserve the on-disk UpdateRetryBudget when the caller did
    # not pass an explicit hashtable. ~15 Save-State call sites
    # in the script don't know or care about the per-update
    # ceiling map; only Invoke-FbBucketApply mutates it. Without
    # this preserve clause every save would clobber the map back
    # to empty.
    if ($null -ne $UpdateRetryBudget) {
        $state.UpdateRetryBudget = $UpdateRetryBudget
    } else {
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existing = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existing -and $existing.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'UpdateRetryBudget' -and $existing.UpdateRetryBudget) {
                    $preserved = @{}
                    foreach ($p in $existing.UpdateRetryBudget.PSObject.Properties) {
                        try { $preserved[[string]$p.Name] = [int]$p.Value } catch {}
                    }
                    $state.UpdateRetryBudget = $preserved
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    # 2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve: preserve FutileBootCount /
    # EscapeValveTripped / EscapeValveReason across the ~15
    # existing Save-State call sites that do not pass them. Only
    # Test-FbEscapeValve, Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff, and the
    # per-boot futile-boot finalize block ever pass explicit
    # values; every other persist must carry the disk state
    # forward unchanged or the counter would reset to 0 on the
    # next bucket apply Save-State and the escape valve would
    # never trip.
    if ($FutileBootCount -ge 0) {
        $state.FutileBootCount = $FutileBootCount
    } else {
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existing = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existing -and $existing.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'FutileBootCount') {
                    try { $state.FutileBootCount = [int]$existing.FutileBootCount } catch { $state.FutileBootCount = 0 }
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    if ($null -ne $EscapeValveTripped) {
        $state.EscapeValveTripped = [bool]$EscapeValveTripped
    } else {
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existing = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existing -and $existing.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'EscapeValveTripped') {
                    try { $state.EscapeValveTripped = [bool]$existing.EscapeValveTripped } catch { $state.EscapeValveTripped = $false }
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    if ($null -ne $EscapeValveReason) {
        $state.EscapeValveReason = [string]$EscapeValveReason
    } else {
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existing = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existing -and $existing.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'EscapeValveReason' -and $existing.EscapeValveReason) {
                    try { $state.EscapeValveReason = [string]$existing.EscapeValveReason } catch { $state.EscapeValveReason = '' }
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    # 2026.05.13.1900-two-stage-sysprep-handoff: preserve PendingSysprep
    # across the ~15 Save-State call sites that do not pass an
    # explicit value. Only Invoke-FbReboot (STAGE 1 set), main-loop
    # / startup STAGE-2 routing (no-op explicit set for clarity), and
    # Invoke-SysprepToOobe (clear on success) ever pass an explicit
    # value; every other persist must carry the disk state forward
    # unchanged or the STAGE 1 -> STAGE 2 handshake would be
    # clobbered by the bucket-apply Save-States that run between
    # the two stages.
    if ($null -ne $PendingSysprep) {
        $state.PendingSysprep = [bool]$PendingSysprep
    } else {
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existing = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existing -and $existing.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'PendingSysprep') {
                    try { $state.PendingSysprep = [bool]$existing.PendingSysprep } catch { $state.PendingSysprep = $false }
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: preserve
    # BootsSinceLastInstall / WorkQueue / LastScanResultCount /
    # ConvergenceConfirmed across the ~15 Save-State call sites that
    # do not pass them. Same pattern as PendingSysprep above.
    if ($BootsSinceLastInstall -ge 0) {
        $state.BootsSinceLastInstall = $BootsSinceLastInstall
    } else {
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existing = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existing -and $existing.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'BootsSinceLastInstall') {
                    try { $state.BootsSinceLastInstall = [int]$existing.BootsSinceLastInstall } catch { $state.BootsSinceLastInstall = 0 }
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    if ($null -ne $WorkQueue) {
        # Caller passed an explicit array (or arraylist). Normalize
        # to plain object[] so the JSON shape is stable. Each row
        # must have UpdateId/Bucket/Status/RetryCount/LastHResult/
        # FirstSeenBoot - we don't enforce the schema here, the
        # mutator (Invoke-FbBucketApply) is responsible for shape.
        $state.WorkQueue = @($WorkQueue)
    } else {
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existing = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existing -and $existing.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'WorkQueue' -and $existing.WorkQueue) {
                    $state.WorkQueue = @($existing.WorkQueue)
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    if ($LastScanResultCount -ge 0) {
        $state.LastScanResultCount = $LastScanResultCount
    } else {
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existing = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existing -and $existing.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'LastScanResultCount') {
                    try { $state.LastScanResultCount = [int]$existing.LastScanResultCount } catch { $state.LastScanResultCount = 0 }
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    if ($null -ne $ConvergenceConfirmed) {
        $state.ConvergenceConfirmed = [bool]$ConvergenceConfirmed
    } else {
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existing = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existing -and $existing.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'ConvergenceConfirmed') {
                    try { $state.ConvergenceConfirmed = [bool]$existing.ConvergenceConfirmed } catch { $state.ConvergenceConfirmed = $false }
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    if ($CbsPendingBootsCount -ge 0) {
        $state.CbsPendingBootsCount = $CbsPendingBootsCount
    } else {
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existing = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existing -and $existing.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'CbsPendingBootsCount') {
                    try { $state.CbsPendingBootsCount = [int]$existing.CbsPendingBootsCount } catch { $state.CbsPendingBootsCount = 0 }
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    # 2026.05.18.2213l Bug FF: preserve SoftGivenUpCounters across the
    # Save-State call sites that do not mutate it. Only the post-scan
    # increment block and the bucket-apply pre-set path ever pass an
    # explicit value; every other persist must carry the disk state
    # forward unchanged or the counters would reset every Save-State
    # and the soft-given-up promotion would never fire.
    if ($null -ne $SoftGivenUpCounters) {
        $state.SoftGivenUpCounters = $SoftGivenUpCounters
    } else {
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existing = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existing -and $existing.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'SoftGivenUpCounters' -and $existing.SoftGivenUpCounters) {
                    $preservedSoft = @{}
                    foreach ($p in $existing.SoftGivenUpCounters.PSObject.Properties) {
                        try { $preservedSoft[[string]$p.Name] = [int]$p.Value } catch {}
                    }
                    $state.SoftGivenUpCounters = $preservedSoft
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    if ($null -ne $SuccessLedgerIds) {
        $incomingLedger = @()
        foreach ($g in @($SuccessLedgerIds)) {
            if ($g) { $incomingLedger += [string]$g }
        }
        $allowEmptyLedgerClear = $false
        try {
            if ($LastPass -match '(?i)bugjj|sysprep') { $allowEmptyLedgerClear = $true }
        } catch {}
        if ($incomingLedger.Count -gt 0 -or $allowEmptyLedgerClear) {
            $state.SuccessLedgerIds = $incomingLedger
        } else {
            try {
                if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                    $existing = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                    if ($existing -and $existing.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'SuccessLedgerIds' -and $existing.SuccessLedgerIds) {
                        $preservedLedger = @()
                        foreach ($g in @($existing.SuccessLedgerIds)) {
                            if ($g) { $preservedLedger += [string]$g }
                        }
                        if ($preservedLedger.Count -gt 0) {
                            try { Write-FbLog ("Save-State refused empty SuccessLedgerIds over live disk ledger count={0}." -f $preservedLedger.Count) 'WARN' } catch {}
                            $state.SuccessLedgerIds = $preservedLedger
                        }
                    }
                }
            } catch {}
        }
    } else {
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existing = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existing -and $existing.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'SuccessLedgerIds' -and $existing.SuccessLedgerIds) {
                    $preservedLedger = @()
                    foreach ($g in @($existing.SuccessLedgerIds)) {
                        if ($g) { $preservedLedger += [string]$g }
                    }
                    $state.SuccessLedgerIds = $preservedLedger
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    # 2026.05.19.2213n Bug II: ScanStability sentinel-preserve. Only
    # Invoke-FbScanStabilityEvaluate (at the STAGE 1 OOBE handoff gate)
    # and the Bug JJ post-sysprep cleanup ever pass an explicit
    # $ScanStability value. Every other Save-State call must carry the
    # disk block forward unchanged or the consecutive-count would reset
    # to 0 across the dozens of Save-State sites that fire between two
    # successive boot-level gate scans. Schema documented in the
    # Save-State $ScanStability param comment above:
    #   ScanStability = @{
    #     LastFingerprint  = '<lowercase-hex-SHA256>' or ''
    #     ConsecutiveCount = <int>
    #     LastSeenAt       = '<ISO8601>' or ''
    #     LastResidualIds  = @('<guid>',...)
    #   }
    if ($null -ne $ScanStability) {
        $stabilityBlock = @{
            LastFingerprint  = ''
            ConsecutiveCount = 0
            LastSeenAt       = ''
            LastResidualIds  = @()
        }
        try { if ($ScanStability.ContainsKey('LastFingerprint')) { $stabilityBlock.LastFingerprint = [string]$ScanStability['LastFingerprint'] } } catch {}
        try { if ($ScanStability.ContainsKey('ConsecutiveCount')) { $stabilityBlock.ConsecutiveCount = [int]$ScanStability['ConsecutiveCount'] } } catch {}
        try { if ($ScanStability.ContainsKey('LastSeenAt')) { $stabilityBlock.LastSeenAt = [string]$ScanStability['LastSeenAt'] } } catch {}
        try {
            if ($ScanStability.ContainsKey('LastResidualIds') -and $ScanStability['LastResidualIds']) {
                $preservedResidual = @()
                foreach ($g in @($ScanStability['LastResidualIds'])) {
                    if ($g) { $preservedResidual += [string]$g }
                }
                $stabilityBlock.LastResidualIds = $preservedResidual
            }
        } catch {}
        $state.ScanStability = $stabilityBlock
    } else {
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $existing = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($existing -and $existing.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'ScanStability' -and $existing.ScanStability) {
                    $preservedStability = @{
                        LastFingerprint  = ''
                        ConsecutiveCount = 0
                        LastSeenAt       = ''
                        LastResidualIds  = @()
                    }
                    try { if ($existing.ScanStability.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'LastFingerprint') { $preservedStability.LastFingerprint = [string]$existing.ScanStability.LastFingerprint } } catch {}
                    try { if ($existing.ScanStability.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'ConsecutiveCount') { $preservedStability.ConsecutiveCount = [int]$existing.ScanStability.ConsecutiveCount } } catch {}
                    try { if ($existing.ScanStability.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'LastSeenAt') { $preservedStability.LastSeenAt = [string]$existing.ScanStability.LastSeenAt } } catch {}
                    try {
                        if ($existing.ScanStability.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'LastResidualIds' -and $existing.ScanStability.LastResidualIds) {
                            $preservedResidual = @()
                            foreach ($g in @($existing.ScanStability.LastResidualIds)) {
                                if ($g) { $preservedResidual += [string]$g }
                            }
                            $preservedStability.LastResidualIds = $preservedResidual
                        }
                    } catch {}
                    $state.ScanStability = $preservedStability
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    try { $state | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 8 | Set-Content -Path $StateFile -Encoding UTF8 -Force } catch {}
}

function Load-State {
    if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
        try {
            $obj = Get-Content $StateFile -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json
            $retryMap = @{}
            if ($obj.UpdateRetry) {
                foreach ($p in $obj.UpdateRetry.PSObject.Properties) {
                    $retryMap[[string]$p.Name] = [int]$p.Value
                }
            }
            $givenUp = @()
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'GivenUpUpdateIds' -and $obj.GivenUpUpdateIds) {
                foreach ($g in @($obj.GivenUpUpdateIds)) {
                    if ($g) { $givenUp += [string]$g }
                }
            }
            $bootMap = @{}
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'BootCountByKey' -and $obj.BootCountByKey) {
                foreach ($p in $obj.BootCountByKey.PSObject.Properties) {
                    $bootMap[[string]$p.Name] = [int]$p.Value
                }
            }
            $bootCount = 0
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'BootCount' -and $obj.BootCount) {
                try { $bootCount = [int]$obj.BootCount } catch { $bootCount = 0 }
            }
            $forceHidden = @()
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'ForceHiddenKeys' -and $obj.ForceHiddenKeys) {
                foreach ($g in @($obj.ForceHiddenKeys)) {
                    if ($g) { $forceHidden += [string]$g }
                }
            }
            $rebootIters = 0
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'RebootIterations' -and $obj.RebootIterations) {
                try { $rebootIters = [int]$obj.RebootIterations } catch { $rebootIters = 0 }
            }
            $sysprepRetries = 0
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'SysprepRetryCount' -and $obj.SysprepRetryCount) {
                try { $sysprepRetries = [int]$obj.SysprepRetryCount } catch { $sysprepRetries = 0 }
            }
            # CascadeExhaustionCount: new in 2026.05.11.1145. Older state
            # files won't have the field; treat missing as 0 so an
            # existing device upgraded to this build starts in "normal
            # cascade" mode and only escalates after the new build
            # itself observes a fresh exhaustion.
            $cascadeExhaustion = 0
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'CascadeExhaustionCount' -and $obj.CascadeExhaustionCount) {
                try { $cascadeExhaustion = [int]$obj.CascadeExhaustionCount } catch { $cascadeExhaustion = 0 }
            }
            # InstallsCompleted: new in 2026.05.11.1230. Older state
            # files won't have the field; treat missing as $false so
            # a fresh device installs normally on the first pass.
            # 2026.05.12.1700: also load RealInstallsTotal; if the
            # latch is set on disk but RealInstallsTotal is 0, treat
            # the latch as $false (an old state file from a build
            # that latched on UI status strings should NOT bypass).
            $installsCompleted = $false
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'InstallsCompleted') {
                try { $installsCompleted = [bool]$obj.InstallsCompleted } catch { $installsCompleted = $false }
            }
            $realInstalls = 0
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'RealInstallsTotal') {
                try { $realInstalls = [int]$obj.RealInstallsTotal } catch { $realInstalls = 0 }
            }
            if ($installsCompleted -and $realInstalls -le 0) {
                # Forward-compatibility scrub: a legacy state file
                # latched via a pre-1700 build that fired on UI
                # status strings (no real install evidence). Treat
                # as not-latched so a fresh boot does not bypass.
                $installsCompleted = $false
            }
            # 2026.05.12.1830-patient-shutdown-and-hresult-coverage:
            # per-update retry ceiling map. Older state files won't
            # have it; treat missing as empty so the default
            # $MaxUpdateRetry ceiling applies to every update.
            $retryBudget = @{}
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'UpdateRetryBudget' -and $obj.UpdateRetryBudget) {
                foreach ($p in $obj.UpdateRetryBudget.PSObject.Properties) {
                    try { $retryBudget[[string]$p.Name] = [int]$p.Value } catch {}
                }
            }
            # 2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve: futile-boot counter and
            # latch fields. Older state files won't have them;
            # treat missing as 0 / $false / '' so a freshly-imaged
            # device starts in normal cascade with budget intact.
            $futileBootCount = 0
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'FutileBootCount') {
                try { $futileBootCount = [int]$obj.FutileBootCount } catch { $futileBootCount = 0 }
            }
            $escapeValveTripped = $false
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'EscapeValveTripped') {
                try { $escapeValveTripped = [bool]$obj.EscapeValveTripped } catch { $escapeValveTripped = $false }
            }
            $escapeValveReason = ''
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'EscapeValveReason' -and $obj.EscapeValveReason) {
                try { $escapeValveReason = [string]$obj.EscapeValveReason } catch { $escapeValveReason = '' }
            }
            # 2026.05.13.1900-two-stage-sysprep-handoff: read
            # PendingSysprep from disk. Older state files (build
            # 1700 and earlier) do not carry this field. Backward-
            # compat synthesis: if InstallsCompleted=true AND the
            # field is missing from the JSON, infer PendingSysprep=
            # true. This is the Scenario D recovery path: a device
            # stranded by build 1700's broken handoff has
            # InstallsCompleted=true and RealInstallsTotal>0 in its
            # state file but no PendingSysprep entry. Synthesizing
            # PendingSysprep=true means the next loop launch reaches
            # STAGE 2 (instead of re-entering the doomed pre-1900
            # OOBE handoff path), waits for CBS to settle, and
            # invokes sysprep with -Wait. If CBS has not settled the
            # STAGE-1-equivalent plain-shutdown branch fires
            # instead.
            $pendingSysprep = $false
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'PendingSysprep') {
                try { $pendingSysprep = [bool]$obj.PendingSysprep } catch { $pendingSysprep = $false }
            } elseif ($installsCompleted -and $realInstalls -gt 0) {
                $legacyLastPass = ''
                try { $legacyLastPass = [string]$obj.LastPass } catch { $legacyLastPass = '' }
                if ($legacyLastPass -match '(?i)(oobe-handoff|sysprep|escape-valve)') {
                    $pendingSysprep = $true
                    try {
                        Write-FbLog ("Load-State: legacy state file (build < 1900) with InstallsCompleted=true, RealInstallsTotal={0}, LastPass={1}; synthesizing PendingSysprep=true for STAGE 2 recovery." -f $realInstalls, $legacyLastPass) 'WARN'
                    } catch {}
                } else {
                    $pendingSysprep = $false
                    try {
                        Write-FbLog ("5.4.19: legacy InstallsCompleted=true without PendingSysprep and LastPass='{0}' is not seal/OOBE; NOT synthesizing PendingSysprep (WU loop must resume in Audit)." -f $legacyLastPass) 'WARN'
                    } catch {}
                }
            }
            # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: read the
            # new full-scope multi-pass fields. Older state files
            # (build 1900 and earlier) do not carry these. Backward-
            # compat default for ALL of them: 0 / @() / $false. A
            # device upgraded to this build starts a fresh
            # convergence count and an empty work queue. The
            # mandatory fresh per-pass scan at the top of the
            # multi-pass loop will populate WorkQueue and
            # LastScanResultCount on its first iteration.
            $bootsSinceLastInstall = 0
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'BootsSinceLastInstall') {
                try { $bootsSinceLastInstall = [int]$obj.BootsSinceLastInstall } catch { $bootsSinceLastInstall = 0 }
            }
            $workQueue = @()
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'WorkQueue' -and $obj.WorkQueue) {
                # Tolerant load: each row may be a PSCustomObject
                # (from ConvertFrom-Json) or a hashtable (from a
                # caller that already normalized). Project into a
                # canonical hashtable shape so downstream code
                # never has to branch on type.
                foreach ($row in @($obj.WorkQueue)) {
                    if (-not $row) { continue }
                    $rowHash = @{
                        UpdateId      = ''
                        Bucket        = ''
                        Status        = 'Pending'
                        RetryCount    = 0
                        LastHResult   = 0
                        FirstSeenBoot = 0
                    }
                    foreach ($k in @('UpdateId','Bucket','Status')) {
                        try {
                            if ($row.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains $k -and $null -ne $row.$k) {
                                $rowHash[$k] = [string]$row.$k
                            }
                        } catch {}
                    }
                    foreach ($k in @('RetryCount','LastHResult','FirstSeenBoot')) {
                        try {
                            if ($row.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains $k -and $null -ne $row.$k) {
                                $rowHash[$k] = [int]$row.$k
                            }
                        } catch {}
                    }
                    if ($rowHash.UpdateId) { $workQueue += ,$rowHash }
                }
            }
            $lastScanResultCount = 0
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'LastScanResultCount') {
                try { $lastScanResultCount = [int]$obj.LastScanResultCount } catch { $lastScanResultCount = 0 }
            }
            $convergenceConfirmed = $false
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'ConvergenceConfirmed') {
                try { $convergenceConfirmed = [bool]$obj.ConvergenceConfirmed } catch { $convergenceConfirmed = $false }
            }
            $cbsPendingBootsCount = 0
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'CbsPendingBootsCount') {
                try { $cbsPendingBootsCount = [int]$obj.CbsPendingBootsCount } catch { $cbsPendingBootsCount = 0 }
            }
            # 2026.05.18.2213l Bug FF: per-UpdateId soft-given-up
            # counters and the success-ledger they pair with. Older
            # state files (build 2213k and earlier) do not have
            # these fields; treat missing as empty so a freshly
            # imaged device starts both counters at zero.
            $softGivenUpCounters = @{}
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'SoftGivenUpCounters' -and $obj.SoftGivenUpCounters) {
                foreach ($p in $obj.SoftGivenUpCounters.PSObject.Properties) {
                    try { $softGivenUpCounters[[string]$p.Name] = [int]$p.Value } catch {}
                }
            }
            $successLedgerIds = @()
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'SuccessLedgerIds' -and $obj.SuccessLedgerIds) {
                foreach ($g in @($obj.SuccessLedgerIds)) {
                    if ($g) { $successLedgerIds += [string]$g }
                }
            }
            # 2026.05.19.2213n Bug II: hydrate the ScanStability state
            # block. Older state files (build 2213m and earlier) do not
            # have this field; treat missing as the empty/zero default so
            # a freshly imaged device starts with ConsecutiveCount=0 and
            # the first STAGE 1 gate scan establishes its own fingerprint.
            $scanStability = @{
                LastFingerprint  = ''
                ConsecutiveCount = 0
                LastSeenAt       = ''
                LastResidualIds  = @()
            }
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'ScanStability' -and $obj.ScanStability) {
                try { if ($obj.ScanStability.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'LastFingerprint') { $scanStability.LastFingerprint = [string]$obj.ScanStability.LastFingerprint } } catch {}
                try { if ($obj.ScanStability.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'ConsecutiveCount') { $scanStability.ConsecutiveCount = [int]$obj.ScanStability.ConsecutiveCount } } catch {}
                try { if ($obj.ScanStability.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'LastSeenAt') { $scanStability.LastSeenAt = [string]$obj.ScanStability.LastSeenAt } } catch {}
                try {
                    if ($obj.ScanStability.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'LastResidualIds' -and $obj.ScanStability.LastResidualIds) {
                        $hydratedResidual = @()
                        foreach ($g in @($obj.ScanStability.LastResidualIds)) {
                            if ($g) { $hydratedResidual += [string]$g }
                        }
                        $scanStability.LastResidualIds = $hydratedResidual
                    }
                } catch {}
            }
            return @{
                EmptySweepCount        = [int]($obj.EmptySweepCount)
                LastPass               = [string]($obj.LastPass)
                StartTime              = if ($obj.StartTime) { [string]$obj.StartTime } else { (Get-Date).ToString('o') }
                FailureCount           = [int]($obj.FailureCount)
                UpdateRetry            = $retryMap
                GivenUpUpdateIds       = $givenUp
                BootCountByKey         = $bootMap
                BootCount              = $bootCount
                ForceHiddenKeys        = $forceHidden
                RebootIterations       = $rebootIters
                SysprepRetryCount      = $sysprepRetries
                CascadeExhaustionCount = $cascadeExhaustion
                InstallsCompleted      = $installsCompleted
                RealInstallsTotal      = $realInstalls
                UpdateRetryBudget      = $retryBudget
                FutileBootCount        = $futileBootCount
                EscapeValveTripped     = $escapeValveTripped
                EscapeValveReason      = $escapeValveReason
                PendingSysprep         = $pendingSysprep
                BootsSinceLastInstall  = $bootsSinceLastInstall
                WorkQueue              = $workQueue
                LastScanResultCount    = $lastScanResultCount
                ConvergenceConfirmed   = $convergenceConfirmed
                CbsPendingBootsCount   = $cbsPendingBootsCount
                SoftGivenUpCounters    = $softGivenUpCounters
                SuccessLedgerIds       = $successLedgerIds
                ScanStability          = $scanStability
            }
        } catch {}
    }

    return @{
        EmptySweepCount        = 0
        LastPass               = ''
        StartTime              = (Get-Date).ToString('o')
        FailureCount           = 0
        UpdateRetry            = @{}
        GivenUpUpdateIds       = @()
        BootCountByKey         = @{}
        BootCount              = 0
        ForceHiddenKeys        = @()
        RebootIterations       = 0
        SysprepRetryCount      = 0
        CascadeExhaustionCount = 0
        InstallsCompleted      = $false
        RealInstallsTotal      = 0
        UpdateRetryBudget      = @{}
        FutileBootCount        = 0
        EscapeValveTripped     = $false
        EscapeValveReason      = ''
        PendingSysprep         = $false
        BootsSinceLastInstall  = 0
        WorkQueue              = @()
        LastScanResultCount    = 0
        ConvergenceConfirmed   = $false
        CbsPendingBootsCount   = 0
        SoftGivenUpCounters    = @{}
        SuccessLedgerIds       = @()
        ScanStability          = @{
            LastFingerprint  = ''
            ConsecutiveCount = 0
            LastSeenAt       = ''
            LastResidualIds  = @()
        }
    }
}

# 2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence: in-memory counters for
# this process boot. RealInstallsThisBoot is bumped by
# Set-FbInstallsCompleted's evidence path (a per-update result with
# ResultCode in {2,3}). NOT persisted to disk - the cumulative
# counter (RealInstallsTotal) on wu-state.json is the persistent
# version. RealInstallsThisBoot existing in-memory only is what
# guarantees that leftover OS pending-reboot signals from imaging
# cannot trip the latch: a fresh boot starts at zero, and only a
# real install in THIS boot can bump it.
$script:RealInstallsThisBoot = 0
$script:FbWuDriverApplyActive = $false
$script:FbRecallDisableAttemptedThisBoot = $false
# 2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve: futile-boot tracking. The escape
# valve increments $state.FutileBootCount only when ALL of the
# following are true for the current boot:
#   1. The loop reached its main iteration body (heartbeat alive).
#   2. Invoke-FbWuScan returned at least one offered update.
#   3. Invoke-FbBucketApply was called for at least one bucket.
#   4. (Load-State).RealInstallsTotal did NOT increase from boot-
#      start to boot-end (i.e. no real install evidence in this
#      boot AND no real install carried over).
# A boot that observes condition 4 with conditions 1-3 also true
# is "futile" - we did everything we could and still nothing
# installed. Three consecutive futile boots trip the escape valve.
#
# $script:RealInstallsAtBootStart is captured once at module init
# from wu-state.json so we have an authoritative "start" baseline
# that is not affected by any mid-boot mutation of the disk value.
$script:WuScanCountThisBoot = 0
$script:WuApplyAttempted    = $false
$script:RealInstallsAtBootStart = 0
try {
    $bootStartProbe = Load-State
    $script:RealInstallsAtBootStart = [int]$bootStartProbe.RealInstallsTotal
} catch {
    $script:RealInstallsAtBootStart = 0
}

function Set-FbInstallsCompleted {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Latch the InstallsCompleted flag to $true and persist it.
        2026.05.12.1700: REQUIRES real install evidence to persist.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence. The latch is only
        persisted to disk when the caller passes -RealInstallEvidence
        $true (e.g. a per-update orcSucceeded/orcSucceededWithErrors
        result was observed). Without that evidence, the call is a
        no-op (logged as a rejection); we never silently latch on
        UI status strings, "past Download" counts, or COM exception
        paths.

        Field-log evidence (WU-20260512-110026.log) showed the
        prior implementation latching at "bucket Critical reached
        Installing UI status (4 update(s) past Download)" - i.e.
        the cosmetic Set-UiUpdateStatus 'Installing' call right
        before BeginInstall. All four updates then returned
        ResultCode=-1 (orcNotStarted) due to a separate async-
        polling bug in Invoke-FbWuApply. The latch persisted, the
        next Invoke-FbReboot routed to sysprep handoff, and the
        device sysprep'd to OOBE with ZERO installs.

        Evidence model:
          - RealInstallEvidence=$true ONLY when the caller has
            verified a per-update IInstallationResult.GetUpdateResult
            row with ResultCode in {2,3} (orcSucceeded,
            orcSucceededWithErrors).
          - Any other caller path passes RealInstallEvidence=$false
            (or omits it; default is $false) and the call is rejected
            with a WARN log. The disk latch stays $false.

        Side effects when evidence is true:
          - $script:RealInstallsThisBoot bumped by 1.
          - On-disk RealInstallsTotal bumped by 1 (via Save-State).
          - On-disk InstallsCompleted persisted as $true.
          - The persistence invariant in Save-State accepts the
            latch only because RealInstallsTotal > 0 in the same
            write.

        Once latched, every subsequent invocation of the loop:

          - Skips the entire reboot cascade pipeline (Invoke-Reboot
            short-circuits straight to Invoke-FbOobeHandoff).
          - Bypasses the main scan/bucket/iterate block at startup.
          - Relabels every Pending-restart / Retrying row to
            Completed with the deferred-settlement note.
          - Hands off to sysprep, which has its own reboot path
            through Setup (different from our blocked APIs).

        This is the explicit policy decision: on hardware where the
        8-stage cascade demonstrably cannot reboot (TrustedInstaller /
        CBS / shutdown veto unbreakable from user-mode PowerShell),
        we stop trying. Windows handles the deferred settling reboot
        on first user logon as a normal documented feature.

        Idempotent - calling repeatedly is safe; only the first call
        actually writes (subsequent calls observe the latch already
        set and short-circuit). The `Reason` argument is logged so
        the diag dump can show WHICH apply path tripped the latch.

        Never throws.
    #>
    param(
        [string]$Reason = 'install detected',
        # 2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence. The caller MUST set
        # this $true to persist the latch. Without it, the call is
        # rejected and a WARN log is emitted. The only legitimate
        # caller setting this $true is Invoke-FbBucketApply's
        # per-update result loop on observing
        # ResultCode in {2,3} (orcSucceeded / orcSucceededWithErrors).
        [bool]$RealInstallEvidence = $false,
        # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: tighten the
        # off-by-one in RealInstallsTotal that build 1915 surfaced.
        # Field evidence showed the bucket-finish path "WUA / OS
        # reports reboot required after Critical bucket apply; 0
        # row(s) labelled Pending restart" was advancing
        # RealInstallsTotal even though zero per-update rows came
        # back as ResultCode=2|3. This counter is the system-of-
        # record for the new BootsSinceLastInstall escape valve;
        # any spurious advance shifts the threshold by 1 and lets
        # the loop run an extra futile boot before the valve trips.
        # NEW POLICY: callers that observed a per-update success
        # ResultCode in {2,3} pass $true (default). All other
        # callers (BenignHResult Action='Reboot' branch, post-
        # bucket OS-pending-reboot signal, etc.) pass $false. The
        # latch still persists when RealInstallEvidence=$true but
        # RealInstallsTotal does NOT advance unless the caller
        # explicitly asserts a per-update orcSucceeded row.
        [bool]$BumpRealInstallsTotal = $true,
        # 5.4.18: InstallsCompleted means cascade exhausted / OOBE handoff,
        # not "Critical succeeded so skip reboot forever while Driver remains".
        # Bucket apply bumps RealInstallsTotal only. Pass $true from
        # Invoke-FbOobeHandoff (and similar exhausted-cascade paths).
        [bool]$LatchOobeHandoff = $false
    )

    try {
        $current = Load-State
        $currentRealInstalls = 0
        try { $currentRealInstalls = [int]$current.RealInstallsTotal } catch {}

        if (-not $RealInstallEvidence) {
            # Rejected: caller wants the latch but cannot prove a
            # real install happened. Emit a screaming log line and
            # leave disk alone. This is the rail that blocks the
            # silent-bypass class of bug.
            Write-FbLog ("Set-FbInstallsCompleted REJECTED (no RealInstallEvidence): {0}. Disk latch stays at {1}, RealInstallsTotal={2}." -f $Reason, [bool]$current.InstallsCompleted, $currentRealInstalls) 'WARN'
            try { $script:LastInstallsCompletedDiag = ("Set-FbInstallsCompleted REJECTED (no evidence) [{0}]" -f $Reason) } catch {}
            return
        }

        # Bump in-memory and persisted real-install counters - but
        # ONLY when the caller asserts $BumpRealInstallsTotal=$true
        # (i.e. a per-update orcSucceeded/orcSucceededWithErrors row
        # was observed). Other latch-eligible callers (post-bucket
        # OS-pending-reboot, BenignHResult Action='Reboot' rows)
        # pass $false to leave the counter alone, fixing the build
        # 1915 off-by-one that shifted the BootsSinceLastInstall
        # escape valve threshold by 1.
        if ($BumpRealInstallsTotal) {
            $script:RealInstallsThisBoot = [int]$script:RealInstallsThisBoot + 1
            $newRealTotal = $currentRealInstalls + 1
        } else {
            $newRealTotal = $currentRealInstalls
            Write-FbLog ("Set-FbInstallsCompleted: RealInstallsTotal NOT bumped this call (BumpRealInstallsTotal=`$false). Reason: {0}. RealInstallsTotal stays at {1}." -f $Reason, $newRealTotal) 'INFO'
            if ($newRealTotal -le 0) {
                # Save-State invariant will scrub the latch back to
                # false because no real install evidence has ever
                # been recorded. Document that here so the caller
                # knows the call was effectively a no-op for the
                # latch but the diag rail records an attempt.
                Write-FbLog ("Set-FbInstallsCompleted: latch will NOT persist (RealInstallsTotal=0, BumpRealInstallsTotal=`$false). Reason: {0}. Returning without writing." -f $Reason) 'WARN'
                try { $script:LastInstallsCompletedDiag = ("Set-FbInstallsCompleted no-op (no real install evidence accumulated) [{0}]" -f $Reason) } catch {}
                return
            }
        }

        if (-not $LatchOobeHandoff -and -not [bool]$current.InstallsCompleted) {
            Save-State `
                -EmptySweepCount        ([int]$current.EmptySweepCount) `
                -LastPass               ([string]$current.LastPass) `
                -StartTime              ([string]$current.StartTime) `
                -FailureCount           ([int]$current.FailureCount) `
                -UpdateRetry            $current.UpdateRetry `
                -GivenUpUpdateIds       @($current.GivenUpUpdateIds) `
                -BootCountByKey         $current.BootCountByKey `
                -BootCount              ([int]$current.BootCount) `
                -ForceHiddenKeys        @($current.ForceHiddenKeys) `
                -RebootIterations       ([int]$current.RebootIterations) `
                -SysprepRetryCount      ([int]$current.SysprepRetryCount) `
                -InstallsCompleted      $false `
                -RealInstallsTotal      $newRealTotal
            Write-FbLog ("RealInstallsTotal -> {0} ({1}); InstallsCompleted NOT latched (more buckets may remain; reboot cascade still required)." -f $newRealTotal, $Reason) 'INFO'
            try { $script:LastInstallsCompletedDiag = ("InstallsCompleted=false [real installs bumped to {0}; OOBE latch deferred: {1}]" -f $newRealTotal, $Reason) } catch {}
            return
        }

        if ([bool]$current.InstallsCompleted) {
            # Already latched but we just observed ANOTHER real
            # install - still want to bump RealInstallsTotal so the
            # cumulative count is accurate. Don't bother with the
            # full read-back diag below; just bump and return.
            Save-State `
                -EmptySweepCount        ([int]$current.EmptySweepCount) `
                -LastPass               ([string]$current.LastPass) `
                -StartTime              ([string]$current.StartTime) `
                -FailureCount           ([int]$current.FailureCount) `
                -UpdateRetry            $current.UpdateRetry `
                -GivenUpUpdateIds       @($current.GivenUpUpdateIds) `
                -BootCountByKey         $current.BootCountByKey `
                -BootCount              ([int]$current.BootCount) `
                -ForceHiddenKeys        @($current.ForceHiddenKeys) `
                -RebootIterations       ([int]$current.RebootIterations) `
                -SysprepRetryCount      ([int]$current.SysprepRetryCount) `
                -InstallsCompleted      $true `
                -RealInstallsTotal      $newRealTotal
            try { $script:LastInstallsCompletedDiag = ("InstallsCompleted=true [already latched + bumped RealInstallsTotal->{0}: {1}]" -f $newRealTotal, $Reason) } catch {}
            return
        }
        Save-State `
            -EmptySweepCount        ([int]$current.EmptySweepCount) `
            -LastPass               ([string]$current.LastPass) `
            -StartTime              ([string]$current.StartTime) `
            -FailureCount           ([int]$current.FailureCount) `
            -UpdateRetry            $current.UpdateRetry `
            -GivenUpUpdateIds       @($current.GivenUpUpdateIds) `
            -BootCountByKey         $current.BootCountByKey `
            -BootCount              ([int]$current.BootCount) `
            -ForceHiddenKeys        @($current.ForceHiddenKeys) `
            -RebootIterations       ([int]$current.RebootIterations) `
            -SysprepRetryCount      ([int]$current.SysprepRetryCount) `
            -InstallsCompleted      $true `
            -RealInstallsTotal      $newRealTotal
        Write-FbLog ("InstallsCompleted LATCHED to true ({0}); RealInstallsTotal -> {1}; cascade exhausted so a later invocation may skip the reboot cascade and route to OOBE handoff." -f $Reason, $newRealTotal) 'WARN'

        # 2026.05.11.1515: persistence-verification read-back. Immediately
        # re-load state from disk and CONFIRM the latch survived the
        # round-trip. If it didn't, log a diagnostic so the field can
        # tell us whether Save-State's sentinel logic is the problem
        # (versus, say, a downstream Save-State silently clobbering it).
        $verifyValue = $true
        try {
            $verifyState = Load-State
            $verifyValue = [bool]$verifyState.InstallsCompleted
            if (-not $verifyValue) {
                Write-FbLog ("InstallsCompleted LATCH READ-BACK FAILED: disk re-read returned {0} after writing `$true. Save-State sentinel logic suspect." -f $verifyValue) 'ERROR'
                try { $script:LastInstallsCompletedDiag = "InstallsCompleted=LATCH READ-BACK FAILED [Save-State sentinel did not persist]" } catch {}
            } else {
                Write-FbLog 'InstallsCompleted LATCH READ-BACK confirmed true; on-disk persistence verified.' 'INFO'
                try { $script:LastInstallsCompletedDiag = ("InstallsCompleted=true latched [{0}]" -f $Reason) } catch {}
            }
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("InstallsCompleted LATCH READ-BACK threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }

        # Surface the latch (or the READ-BACK FAILED diagnostic) on the
        # dashboard activity bar so the user (with no log access) can
        # confirm the latch fired in real time.
        try {
            $latchUiMsg = if ($verifyValue) {
                ("[InstallsCompleted=true latched] {0}" -f $Reason)
            } else {
                ("[InstallsCompleted=LATCH READ-BACK FAILED] {0}" -f $Reason)
            }
            Set-UiPhase -Phase $script:UiPhase -Message $latchUiMsg
        } catch {}
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Set-FbInstallsCompleted failed to persist latch: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

function Test-FbInstallsCompletedNow {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Re-read wu-state.json from disk and report InstallsCompleted.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026-05-11 1515-installscompleted-fresh-read. The in-memory
        $state hashtable loaded at script start does NOT reflect the
        latch that Set-FbInstallsCompleted may have written during
        the run. Every code path that decides whether to call
        Invoke-Reboot MUST re-read state from disk first - otherwise
        we keep firing the doomed cascade despite the latch.
        Returns $true / $false. Never throws.
    #>
    try {
        $disk = Load-State
        return [bool]$disk.InstallsCompleted
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Test-FbInstallsCompletedNow read failed: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        return $false
    }
}

function Test-FbWuSkipCascadeUnsafe {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        True when InstallsCompleted must NOT skip the WU cascade to customer OOBE.
    .DESCRIPTION
        5.4.19 D: Critical-only latch + dirty reboot was routing to CloudExperienceHost
        Just a moment. Skip-cascade is only safe at seal (PendingSysprep) or after an
        OOBE/sysprep LastPass with no remaining Driver/Cumulative work and no pending reboot.
    #>
    param($State = $null)
    if ($null -eq $State) {
        try { $State = Load-State } catch { return $true }
    }
    $pendingSysprep = $false
    try { $pendingSysprep = [bool]$State.PendingSysprep } catch {}
    $lastPass = ''
    try { $lastPass = [string]$State.LastPass } catch {}
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($lastPass)) { $lastPass = '' }
    $lastPassLower = $lastPass.ToLowerInvariant()

    if ($pendingSysprep) { return $false }
    if ($lastPassLower -match 'oobe-handoff|sysprep|escape-valve') { return $false }

    $rebootPending = $false
    try { $rebootPending = [bool](Test-FbWuRebootRequired) } catch {}
    if ($rebootPending) { return $true }

    if ($lastPassLower -match 'critical|driver|cumulative|optional|pending-reboot|cbs-') { return $true }

    $queue = @()
    try { $queue = @($State.WorkQueue) } catch { $queue = @() }
    foreach ($row in $queue) {
        if (-not $row) { continue }
        $st = ''
        $bucket = ''
        try { $st = [string]$row.Status } catch {}
        try { $bucket = [string]$row.Bucket } catch {}
        $stLower = $st.ToLowerInvariant()
        if ($stLower -match '^(pending|retry|retrying|installing|downloading)') { return $true }
        if ($bucket -match '(?i)(Driver|Cumulative|Optional|Critical)' -and $stLower -notmatch '(success|givenup|terminal|completed|hidden)') {
            return $true
        }
    }

    $convergence = $false
    try { $convergence = [bool]$State.ConvergenceConfirmed } catch {}
    if (-not $convergence) { return $true }

    return $false
}

function Clear-FbStaleInstallsCompletedLatch {
    param($State, [string]$Reason = '')
    if ($null -eq $State) {
        try { $State = Load-State } catch { $State = $null }
    }
    if ($null -eq $State) { return }
    try {
        Save-State `
            -EmptySweepCount        ([int]$State.EmptySweepCount) `
            -LastPass               ([string]$State.LastPass) `
            -StartTime              ([string]$State.StartTime) `
            -FailureCount           ([int]$State.FailureCount) `
            -UpdateRetry            $State.UpdateRetry `
            -GivenUpUpdateIds       @($State.GivenUpUpdateIds) `
            -BootCountByKey         $State.BootCountByKey `
            -BootCount              ([int]$State.BootCount) `
            -ForceHiddenKeys        @($State.ForceHiddenKeys) `
            -RebootIterations       ([int]$State.RebootIterations) `
            -SysprepRetryCount      ([int]$State.SysprepRetryCount) `
            -InstallsCompleted      $false
        Write-FbLog ("5.4.19: cleared stale InstallsCompleted latch ({0})." -f $Reason) 'WARN'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("5.4.19: Clear-FbStaleInstallsCompletedLatch Save-State threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

function Invoke-FbRefuseSkipCascadeAndResumeWu {
    param([string]$Reason, $State)
    Write-FbLog ("5.4.19: refusing skip-cascade to customer OOBE ({0}). Unlatching InstallsCompleted and resuming WU loop in Audit." -f $Reason) 'WARN'
    Clear-FbStaleInstallsCompletedLatch -State $State -Reason $Reason
    $script:InstallsCompletedAtStartup = $false
    $script:DeferredStartupInstallsCompletedHandoff = $false
    $script:LastInstallsCompletedDiag = ("5.4.19: skip-cascade refused [{0}]; resuming WU in Audit" -f $Reason)
    $rebootPending = $false
    try { $rebootPending = [bool](Test-FbWuRebootRequired) } catch {}
    $realTotal = 0
    try { $realTotal = [int]$State.RealInstallsTotal } catch {}
    if ($rebootPending -and $realTotal -gt 0) {
        Write-FbLog '5.4.19: OS pending reboot after refusing skip-cascade; invoking reboot ladder (do not leave CloudExperienceHost on Just a moment).' 'WARN'
        Invoke-FbRebootWithFreshStateCheck -Reason '5.4.19: pending reboot after refusing skip-cascade; resume WU next boot'
    }
}

# Latest InstallsCompleted-related observation, surfaced through the
# dashboard footer/message so a no-log-access field user can confirm
# the latch state at-a-glance. Populated by Set-FbInstallsCompleted,
# the startup gate, and Invoke-Reboot's fresh-state probe.
$script:LastInstallsCompletedDiag = $null

function Get-FbRebootReasonLabel {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Produce a short human-readable label describing WHY the OS is
        currently sitting in pending-reboot state.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.12.1830-patient-shutdown-and-hresult-coverage. The
        patient-reboot loop in Invoke-FbReboot's Step 5 needs to
        tell the operator (via the dashboard activity bar) what is
        actually holding the box up. Without log access the user only
        sees the splash, so the activity bar must answer "why is the
        box not rebooting yet?" every 10 seconds while we wait.
        Probes (in priority order, first match wins):
          1. CBS_E_INSTALLERS_FAILED_TO_RESPOND analog: Component
             Based Servicing pending-required / RebootInProgress
             registry keys.
          2. WUA COM Microsoft.Update.SystemInfo.RebootRequired.
          3. HKLM\...\WindowsUpdate\Auto Update\RebootRequired key.
          4. HKLM\...\Session Manager\PendingFileRenameOperations
             multi-string value.
        Returns a short label string ("CBS pending" / "WUA reports
        reboot owed" / "WU RebootRequired flag set" / "Pending file
        rename operations queued"). Falls back to "no specific OS
        reboot signal detected" when every probe came back clean
        (e.g. shutdown.exe is queued waiting on a service handle
        that isn't in any of the documented registry locations).
        Never throws.
    #>
    [CmdletBinding()]
    param()

    try {
        $cbsKeys = @(
            'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Component Based Servicing\PendingRequired',
            'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Component Based Servicing\RebootInProgress',
            'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Component Based Servicing\RebootPending'
        )
        foreach ($p in $cbsKeys) {
            try {
                if (Test-Path $p) {
                    $item = Get-Item -Path $p -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                    if ($item) {
                        if ($item.Property -and $item.Property.Count -gt 0) {
                            return ('OS reports CBS pending at ''{0}'' (values present); waiting for Setup to release shutdown lock' -f $p)
                        }
                        $children = @(Get-ChildItem -Path $p -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)
                        if ($children.Count -gt 0) {
                            return ('OS reports CBS pending at ''{0}'' (child keys); waiting for Setup to release shutdown lock' -f $p)
                        }
                        return ('OS reports CBS pending at ''{0}'' (key present); waiting for Setup to release shutdown lock' -f $p)
                    }
                }
            } catch {}
        }
    } catch {}

    try {
        $sysInfo = New-Object -ComObject Microsoft.Update.SystemInfo
        if ($sysInfo.RebootRequired) { return 'WUA reports reboot owed (Microsoft.Update.SystemInfo.RebootRequired)' }
    } catch {}

    try {
        $wuKey = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\WindowsUpdate\Auto Update\RebootRequired'
        if (Test-Path $wuKey) {
            return 'WU RebootRequired flag set; waiting for Windows Update to release shutdown lock'
        }
    } catch {}

    try {
        $smKey = 'HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session Manager'
        $pfro = (Get-ItemProperty -Path $smKey -Name 'PendingFileRenameOperations' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).PendingFileRenameOperations
        if ($pfro -and (@($pfro) | Where-Object { $_ }).Count -gt 0) {
            return 'Pending file rename operations queued; waiting for installer to release shutdown lock'
        }
    } catch {}

    return 'no specific OS reboot signal detected; waiting for shutdown.exe to finalize'
}

function Get-FbFutileBootThreshold {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Resolve the operator-tunable futile-boot threshold for the
        escape valve trigger.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve. Default ceiling is
        $FutileBootThreshold (3). If C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\State\
        EXTEND-BUDGET.flag exists and contains a parseable positive
        integer N, the effective threshold becomes
        $FutileBootThreshold * N. A tech who wants to keep retrying
        on a finicky-but-recoverable device drops a file with
        contents "5" to get a 15-boot budget. A non-integer or
        zero/negative content is ignored (logged once and the
        default is used) so a corrupted flag does not silently
        relax the safety net.

        Returns the resolved threshold (int) plus a string label
        ("3" or "15 (extended from 3 via EXTEND-BUDGET.flag=5)")
        for logging / dashboard surfacing. Returned as a two-
        element ordered hashtable @{ Threshold=...; Label=... }.

        Never throws. Best-effort on the flag-file read.
    #>
    $threshold = [int]$FutileBootThreshold
    $label = ('{0}' -f $threshold)
    try {
        if (Test-Path $ExtendBudgetFlag) {
            $rawContent = $null
            try { $rawContent = (Get-Content -Path $ExtendBudgetFlag -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue) } catch {}
            if ($rawContent) { $rawContent = $rawContent.Trim() }
            $multiplier = 0
            $parsed = [int]::TryParse([string]$rawContent, [ref]$multiplier)
            if ($parsed -and $multiplier -gt 0) {
                $threshold = [int]$FutileBootThreshold * $multiplier
                $label = ('{0} (extended from {1} via EXTEND-BUDGET.flag={2})' -f $threshold, [int]$FutileBootThreshold, $multiplier)
            } else {
                Write-FbLog ("EXTEND-BUDGET.flag content '{0}' is not a positive integer; ignoring and using default threshold {1}." -f $rawContent, [int]$FutileBootThreshold) 'WARN'
            }
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Get-FbFutileBootThreshold: flag probe threw: {0}; falling back to default threshold {1}." -f $_.Exception.Message, [int]$FutileBootThreshold) 'WARN'
    }
    return @{ Threshold = [int]$threshold; Label = [string]$label }
}

function Test-FbEscapeValve {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Evaluate the escape-valve trigger at the entry of
        Invoke-FbReboot and return a decision the caller acts on.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve. The escape valve protects
        the FirstBase update pipeline from infinite reboot/retry
        loops when the WUA engine is fundamentally broken on the
        device (e.g. build 1830 ResultCode=-1 regression). Build
        1700's evidence gating refuses to OOBE-handoff without
        proof of a real install, which is the right policy on a
        healthy box but traps the device forever on a broken-
        engine box. The escape valve is the SAFETY NET that lets
        a clearly-stuck device gracefully bail out with a panic
        record so a tech can review what happened.

        Two distinct trigger paths are evaluated in priority order:

        1. OPERATOR_OVERRIDE: C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\State\
           FORCE-OOBE-HANDOFF.flag exists. A tech places an empty
           file at this path via Notepad to force an immediate
           OOBE handoff regardless of evidence or futile-boot
           state. Highest priority. Always wins. Caller should
           call Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff directly without
           bumping FutileBootCount and without writing the OS-
           level state.

        2. FUTILE_BOOTS_<N>: FutileBootCount on disk has reached
           Get-FbFutileBootThreshold AND RealInstallsTotal on disk
           is still 0. Three consecutive boots (default - tunable
           via EXTEND-BUDGET.flag) that scanned and applied but
           saw zero real installs. WUA is broken on this device
           or there are no installable updates; either way the
           loop should stop reattempting and surface the failure
           clearly to the tech.

        End-of-boot accounting (always runs, even when no trip
        condition is met) is deliberately also handled here so
        the increment logic lives in exactly one place:

          - If $script:RealInstallsThisBoot > 0 OR
            (current RealInstallsTotal > $script:RealInstallsAtBootStart):
              -> the boot installed at least one update.
              -> FutileBootCount is RESET to 0 (the streak is broken).
          - Elif scan > 0 AND bucket apply was attempted in this boot:
              -> the boot did genuine work and got nothing for it.
              -> FutileBootCount is INCREMENTED by 1.
          - Else (no scan, or no apply attempt, or scan returned 0):
              -> the boot is NOT futile (different failure mode,
                 e.g. crashed before scan, or no updates were
                 actually offered).
              -> FutileBootCount is left at its prior value.

        Returns a decision hashtable:
          @{
              Trip          = $true | $false   # caller should hand off
              Reason        = 'OPERATOR_OVERRIDE' | 'FUTILE_BOOTS_<N>' | ''
              FutileNow     = <int>            # post-evaluation value
              Threshold     = <int>            # effective threshold
              ThresholdLabel= <string>         # human-readable
              EvidenceText  = <string>         # one-line summary for logs
          }

        Never throws. Best-effort on every disk touch.
    #>
    param([string]$Stage = 'reboot-call')

    $decision = @{
        Trip           = $false
        Reason         = ''
        FutileNow      = 0
        Threshold      = [int]$FutileBootThreshold
        ThresholdLabel = ('{0}' -f [int]$FutileBootThreshold)
        EvidenceText   = ''
    }

    # Priority 1: operator override. Empty file presence is the
    # whole signal; we do not read the contents. The actual file
    # delete happens inside Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff after the
    # handoff is wired up, so a hung handoff does not leave the
    # tech wondering whether the flag was even seen.
    try {
        if (Test-Path $ForceOobeHandoffFlag) {
            $decision.Trip = $true
            $decision.Reason = 'OPERATOR_OVERRIDE'
            $decision.EvidenceText = ("OPERATOR OVERRIDE detected at '{0}'" -f $ForceOobeHandoffFlag)
            Write-FbLog ("Test-FbEscapeValve [{0}]: OPERATOR OVERRIDE: FORCE-OOBE-HANDOFF.flag detected at '{1}'. Bypassing all gates." -f $Stage, $ForceOobeHandoffFlag) 'ERROR'
            return $decision
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Test-FbEscapeValve: FORCE-OOBE-HANDOFF.flag probe threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # End-of-boot futile-boot accounting. Read fresh disk state.
    $disk = $null
    try { $disk = Load-State } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Test-FbEscapeValve: Load-State threw: {0}; skipping futile-boot accounting for this call." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        return $decision
    }
    if (-not $disk) { return $decision }

    $priorFutile = 0
    try { $priorFutile = [int]$disk.FutileBootCount } catch { $priorFutile = 0 }
    $realTotalNow = 0
    try { $realTotalNow = [int]$disk.RealInstallsTotal } catch { $realTotalNow = 0 }
    $thresholdInfo = Get-FbFutileBootThreshold
    $decision.Threshold      = [int]$thresholdInfo.Threshold
    $decision.ThresholdLabel = [string]$thresholdInfo.Label

    # Did this boot install anything new?
    $bootDidRealInstall = ($script:RealInstallsThisBoot -gt 0) -or ($realTotalNow -gt $script:RealInstallsAtBootStart)
    $bootDidGenuineWork = ($script:WuScanCountThisBoot -gt 0) -and ([bool]$script:WuApplyAttempted)

    $newFutile = $priorFutile
    $accountingReason = ''
    if ($bootDidRealInstall) {
        $newFutile = 0
        $accountingReason = ("RESET (boot installed >=1 update: ThisBoot={0}, TotalDelta={1})" -f $script:RealInstallsThisBoot, ($realTotalNow - $script:RealInstallsAtBootStart))
    } elseif ($bootDidGenuineWork) {
        $newFutile = $priorFutile + 1
        $accountingReason = ("INCREMENT (boot scanned {0} update(s), apply attempted, zero installs: {1} -> {2})" -f $script:WuScanCountThisBoot, $priorFutile, $newFutile)
    } else {
        $accountingReason = ("HOLD (boot did not scan+apply: WuScanCountThisBoot={0}, WuApplyAttempted={1}; counter stays at {2})" -f $script:WuScanCountThisBoot, [bool]$script:WuApplyAttempted, $priorFutile)
    }

    # Persist the new counter if it changed. Sentinel-preserve
    # everything else so we do not clobber unrelated state.
    if ($newFutile -ne $priorFutile) {
        try {
            Save-State `
                -EmptySweepCount   ([int]$disk.EmptySweepCount) `
                -LastPass          ([string]$disk.LastPass) `
                -StartTime         ([string]$disk.StartTime) `
                -FailureCount      ([int]$disk.FailureCount) `
                -UpdateRetry       $disk.UpdateRetry `
                -GivenUpUpdateIds  @($disk.GivenUpUpdateIds) `
                -BootCountByKey    $disk.BootCountByKey `
                -BootCount         ([int]$disk.BootCount) `
                -ForceHiddenKeys   @($disk.ForceHiddenKeys) `
                -RebootIterations  ([int]$disk.RebootIterations) `
                -SysprepRetryCount ([int]$disk.SysprepRetryCount) `
                -FutileBootCount   $newFutile
            Write-FbLog ("Test-FbEscapeValve [{0}]: futile-boot accounting {1}." -f $Stage, $accountingReason) 'WARN'
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Test-FbEscapeValve: futile-boot Save-State threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
    } else {
        Write-FbLog ("Test-FbEscapeValve [{0}]: futile-boot accounting {1}." -f $Stage, $accountingReason) 'INFO'
    }
    # Refresh the dashboard mirror so the next Save-UiState renders
    # the new counter / threshold WITHOUT a redundant disk read.
    try { Update-UiEscapeValveContext -FutileBootCount $newFutile -Threshold $decision.Threshold } catch {}
    $decision.FutileNow = $newFutile

    # Priority 2: futile-boot threshold trip. Honor the operator-
    # extended budget if present. We deliberately ALSO check
    # RealInstallsTotal==0 - if any prior boot did install something,
    # the device is making progress and the threshold should never
    # trip (the streak would have reset above; this is belt-and-
    # suspenders for paranoid disk-write race conditions).
    if ($newFutile -ge $decision.Threshold -and $realTotalNow -le 0) {
        $decision.Trip = $true
        $decision.Reason = ('FUTILE_BOOTS_{0}' -f $newFutile)
        $decision.EvidenceText = ("FutileBootCount={0} >= threshold={1}; RealInstallsTotal={2}" -f $newFutile, $decision.Threshold, $realTotalNow)
        Write-FbLog ("Test-FbEscapeValve [{0}]: ESCAPE VALVE TRIPPED: {1} ({2})." -f $Stage, $decision.Reason, $decision.EvidenceText) 'ERROR'
    } else {
        $decision.EvidenceText = ("FutileBootCount={0}/{1}, RealInstallsTotal={2}, RealInstallsThisBoot={3}" -f $newFutile, $decision.ThresholdLabel, $realTotalNow, $script:RealInstallsThisBoot)
    }

    return $decision
}

function Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Trip the escape valve: persist the latch, write the panic
        record, relabel updates as "Failed - tech review required",
        register the warning-popup RunOnce, and route to OOBE
        handoff bypassing the build 1700 evidence gate.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve. Never returns. Called by
        Invoke-FbReboot when Test-FbEscapeValve.Trip is $true, or
        from the main-loop iteration top when an operator override
        flag is observed.

        Steps (in order):
          1. Persist EscapeValveTripped=$true and EscapeValveReason
             on disk via Save-State (sentinel-preserve every other
             field). The latch persists across reboots so a relaunch
             after a partial sysprep failure does NOT retry the
             doomed install cycle.
          2. Write the panic record
             C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\Logs\ESCAPE-VALVE-TRIPPED-
             <timestamp>.json containing the timestamp, script
             version, full state, counts, all update IDs / titles /
             final statuses from wu-status.json, the last 50 lines
             of the most recent WU-*.log, and a copy of wu-state.json
             at trip-time. This is the single artifact a tech reviews.
          3. Relabel every non-installed row in wu-status.json from
             whatever its current status is to "Failed - tech review
             required" with a Detail explaining the trip. Rows
             already "Completed" are left alone.
          4. Pre-register the WARNING-popup RunOnce in HKLM RunOnce
             so when OOBE finishes and the user logs in for the
             first time, a [System.Windows.Forms.MessageBox] pops
             up with MessageBoxIcon::Warning and a tech-review
             message instead of the normal "Updates Complete"
             modal. Invoke-FbOobeHandoff -> Invoke-SysprepToOobe
             will overwrite this value with the popup-script
             command; we therefore SCHEDULE the warning RunOnce
             write to happen AFTER sysprep launches by setting a
             one-shot scheduled task that fires at "now+90s" to
             rewrite the value. Sysprep finishes its staging in
             well under 90s and then reboots; the rewrite runs
             before Windows actually consumes the RunOnce on
             first logon.
          5. Delete FORCE-OOBE-HANDOFF.flag (operator-override path
             only) so a relaunch does not retrigger the override
             without the tech intending it.
          6. Surface the trip on the dashboard activity bar with
             the "*** ESCAPE VALVE TRIPPED ***" plain-text banner
             prefix (no emojis per user policy).
          7. Call Invoke-FbOobeHandoff which exits the process via
             sysprep launch (or via the manual-reboot final-message
             path if sysprep itself fails). The bypass of the build
             1700 evidence gate is implicit: we are calling
             Invoke-FbOobeHandoff directly, the gate lives in
             Invoke-FbReboot and is not re-evaluated downstream.

        Never returns to the caller (Invoke-FbOobeHandoff exits).
    #>
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$Reason,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [int]$FutileNow,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [int]$Threshold
    )

    Write-FbLog ("*** ESCAPE VALVE TRIPPED *** Reason={0} FutileBootCount={1} Threshold={2}. Routing to OOBE handoff with INCOMPLETE state." -f $Reason, $FutileNow, $Threshold) 'ERROR'

    # 2026.05.13.2213c-onlogon-primary-and-defender-disable: bulletproof
    # Defender restore on the escape-valve path. The device is about to
    # be handed off to OOBE in an INCOMPLETE state - it MUST not ship
    # with Defender real-time monitoring disabled. Set-FbDefenderRealtime
    # is idempotent and never throws, so calling it here is safe even
    # if Invoke-FbOobeHandoff downstream also calls it.
    Set-FbDefenderRealtime -Enabled $true -Reason 'escape-valve'

    # Step 1: persist the latch + reason. Sentinel-preserve every
    # other field. Read fresh disk state first so we don't blow
    # away the futile counter the prior Test-FbEscapeValve call
    # just wrote.
    $diskNow = $null
    try { $diskNow = Load-State } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff: Load-State threw: {0}; persisting with defaults." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    try {
        if ($diskNow) {
            Save-State `
                -EmptySweepCount    ([int]$diskNow.EmptySweepCount) `
                -LastPass           ([string]$diskNow.LastPass) `
                -StartTime          ([string]$diskNow.StartTime) `
                -FailureCount       ([int]$diskNow.FailureCount) `
                -UpdateRetry        $diskNow.UpdateRetry `
                -GivenUpUpdateIds   @($diskNow.GivenUpUpdateIds) `
                -BootCountByKey     $diskNow.BootCountByKey `
                -BootCount          ([int]$diskNow.BootCount) `
                -ForceHiddenKeys    @($diskNow.ForceHiddenKeys) `
                -RebootIterations   ([int]$diskNow.RebootIterations) `
                -SysprepRetryCount  ([int]$diskNow.SysprepRetryCount) `
                -EscapeValveTripped $true `
                -EscapeValveReason  $Reason
        } else {
            Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass 'escape-valve' -StartTime ((Get-Date).ToString('o')) `
                -FailureCount 0 -EscapeValveTripped $true -EscapeValveReason $Reason
        }
        Write-FbLog ("Escape valve latch persisted: EscapeValveTripped=true, EscapeValveReason='{0}'." -f $Reason) 'WARN'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Escape valve latch persist threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    try { Initialize-UiStateFromDisk } catch {}
    Sync-FbWuTranscriptFlush -Reason 'Escape valve: pre-panic transcript flush'
    # Refresh the dashboard mirror so the next Save-UiState renders
    # the *** ESCAPE VALVE TRIPPED *** banner prefix.
    try {
        Update-UiEscapeValveContext `
            -FutileBootCount   ([int]$FutileNow) `
            -Threshold         ([int]$Threshold) `
            -EscapeValveTripped $true `
            -EscapeValveReason  ([string]$Reason)
    } catch {}

    # Step 2: panic record. One JSON file under Logs\ with everything
    # the receiving tech needs to triage without log-grepping.
    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path $LogDir)) { New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $LogDir -Force | Out-Null }
        $stamp = Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd-HHmmss'
        $panicPath = Join-Path $LogDir ("ESCAPE-VALVE-TRIPPED-{0}.json" -f $stamp)

        # Pull update titles / statuses out of wu-status.json so the
        # panic file is self-contained.
        $updatesSnapshot = @()
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StatusFile) {
                $statusObj = Get-Content -Path $StatusFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($statusObj -and $statusObj.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'updates') {
                    foreach ($u in @($statusObj.updates)) {
                        if (-not $u) { continue }
                        $updatesSnapshot += [ordered]@{
                            Id        = [string]$u.Id
                            Title     = [string]$u.Title
                            Status    = [string]$u.Status
                            Retries   = [int]$u.Retries
                            Detail    = [string]$u.Detail
                            UpdatedAt = if ($u.UpdatedAt) { [string]$u.UpdatedAt } else { '' }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Escape valve panic record: wu-status.json snapshot threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }

        # Tail the most recent WU-*.log.
        $logTail = @()
        try {
            $recentLog = Get-ChildItem -Path $LogDir -Filter 'WU-*.log' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |
                Sort-Object LastWriteTime -Descending | Select-Object -First 1
            if ($recentLog) {
                $logTail = @(Get-FbWuLogTailShared -Path $recentLog.FullName -Tail 50)
            }
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Escape valve panic record: log tail threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }

        # Copy of wu-state.json at trip time (raw JSON string).
        $stateSnapshot = ''
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $stateSnapshot = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            }
        } catch {}

        $panicBody = [ordered]@{
            Timestamp          = (Get-Date).ToString('o')
            ScriptVersion      = $ScriptVersion
            EscapeValveReason  = $Reason
            FutileBootCount    = [int]$FutileNow
            Threshold          = [int]$Threshold
            RealInstallsTotal  = if ($diskNow) { [int]$diskNow.RealInstallsTotal } else { 0 }
            RealInstallsThisBoot = [int]$script:RealInstallsThisBoot
            BootCount          = if ($diskNow) { [int]$diskNow.BootCount } else { 0 }
            RebootIterations   = if ($diskNow) { [int]$diskNow.RebootIterations } else { 0 }
            WuScanCountThisBoot= [int]$script:WuScanCountThisBoot
            WuApplyAttempted   = [bool]$script:WuApplyAttempted
            Updates            = $updatesSnapshot
            LogTail            = $logTail
            StateSnapshot      = $stateSnapshot
        }
        $panicBody | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 8 | Set-Content -Path $panicPath -Encoding UTF8 -Force
        Write-FbLog ("Escape valve panic record written to '{0}'." -f $panicPath) 'WARN'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Escape valve panic record write threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # Step 3: relabel non-installed rows to "Failed - tech review required".
    # Do this in memory (in $script:UiUpdates) so Save-UiState picks it
    # up; status.json will then reflect the new state.
    $relabeled = 0
    try {
        foreach ($key in @($script:UiUpdates.Keys)) {
            $row = $script:UiUpdates[$key]
            if (-not $row) { continue }
            $current = [string]$row.Status
            if ($current -match '(?i)^Completed$') { continue }
            $row.Status   = 'Failed - tech review required'
            $row.Detail   = ("Escape valve tripped ({0}); WUA installed no updates after {1} consecutive boot(s) of scan+apply. Review ESCAPE-VALVE-TRIPPED-*.json under Logs\\." -f $Reason, [int]$FutileNow)
            $row.UpdatedAt = (Get-Date).ToString('o')
            $script:UiUpdates[$key] = $row
            $relabeled++
        }
        if ($relabeled -gt 0) { Save-UiState }
        Write-FbLog ("Escape valve: relabeled {0} non-installed UI row(s) to 'Failed - tech review required'." -f $relabeled) 'WARN'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Escape valve relabel threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # 2026.05.20.2213s: warning-popup RunOnce arming + post-sysprep
    # rewrite scheduled task REMOVED per user request (5/19/2026 1:45
    # PM popup-removal directive).
    #
    # Pre-2213s this block performed two writes:
    #   1. HKLM RunOnce!FirstBaseUpdatesComplete = inline PowerShell
    #      command popping a "Updates Incomplete - Tech Review
    #      Required" MessageBox. Pre-registered before sysprep
    #      launched.
    #   2. SYSTEM scheduled task FirstBaseEscapeValveRunOnceRewrite
    #      armed to fire 90s later, re-writing the same RunOnce
    #      value AFTER sysprep's own RunOnce write so the warning
    #      popup would override the success popup on first OOBE
    #      logon.
    #
    # Both writes used the same RunOnce value name as the success
    # popup ($RunOnceValueName = 'FirstBaseUpdatesComplete'). The
    # user's 5/19/2026 directive removes the success popup; the
    # warning popup uses the same RunOnce slot for the same first-
    # OOBE-logon delivery, so it falls under the same removal
    # ("It should no longer be part of the post update completion
    # workflow"). With the popup removed, the operator-facing
    # evidence of an escape-valve trip remains via:
    #
    #   - ESCAPE-VALVE-TRIPPED-<stamp>.json panic record (Step 2
    #     above); fb-dump captures it under Logs/.
    #   - wu-state.json EscapeValveTripped=true latch (still set;
    #     read by the dashboard banner).
    #   - Dashboard footer prefix "*** ESCAPE VALVE TRIPPED ***"
    #     (Save-UiState / Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag).
    #   - Update-row relabel to "Failed - tech review required"
    #     (Step 3 above; visible in wu-status.json and fb-dump).
    #
    # Operators still see the trip during the build-flow; only the
    # OOBE-time MessageBox is gone. The downstream
    # FirstBaseEscapeValveRunOnceRewrite scheduled task is also
    # gone (removed below), so the scrub gate's STEP 2 enumeration
    # of FirstBase\* tasks no longer needs to handle this entry
    # (though the regex catches it anyway if a stale one survives
    # a re-image).
    #
    # RESTORATION PATH (if policy ever changes):
    #   Re-add the Set-ItemProperty + schtasks /Create block here.
    #   Both the value-name constant ($RunOnceValueName) and the
    #   psExe lookup pattern survive at the top of this function /
    #   loop for that purpose.
    Write-FbLog "Escape valve: 2213s popup-removal active (no warning-popup RunOnce written; no post-sysprep rewrite task scheduled). Trip evidence remains via ESCAPE-VALVE-TRIPPED-*.json + dashboard banner + wu-state.json latch + relabeled update rows." 'WARN'

    # Step 5: operator-override path deletes its flag so a relaunch
    # does not silently retrigger the override.
    try {
        if ($Reason -eq 'OPERATOR_OVERRIDE' -and (Test-Path $ForceOobeHandoffFlag)) {
            Remove-Item -Path $ForceOobeHandoffFlag -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            Write-FbLog ("Escape valve: deleted operator-override flag '{0}'." -f $ForceOobeHandoffFlag) 'INFO'
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Escape valve: operator-override flag delete threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # Step 6: dashboard activity bar. Plain-text banner per user policy.
    try {
        $banner = ('*** ESCAPE VALVE TRIPPED *** Reason={0} FutileBootCount={1}/{2} - device routing to OOBE with INCOMPLETE state. Tech review required.' -f $Reason, [int]$FutileNow, [int]$Threshold)
        Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Completed' -Message $banner
        try { Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Completed' } catch {}
        Write-FbLog ("Escape valve banner: {0}" -f $banner) 'ERROR'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Escape valve dashboard banner threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # Step 7: route to OOBE handoff. Build 1700's evidence gate lives
    # in Invoke-FbReboot; calling Invoke-FbOobeHandoff directly bypasses
    # it. Invoke-FbOobeHandoff never returns - either sysprep launches
    # and we exit 0, or the final-message path exits 0. Either way,
    # control does not come back to this caller.
    # 5.4.20: flush WU transcript BEFORE persist-follow-through kill (PF12VZ2W cut mid-line).
    Sync-FbWuTranscriptFlush -Reason 'Escape valve: handing off (flush)'
    Invoke-FbOobeHandoff -Reason ("Escape valve tripped: {0}. Original reason: {1}" -f $Reason, $Reason)
}

# 2026.05.13.2213-tier-watchdog-and-diag-parsefix Critical Fix 1, 2, 3, 7:
# helper surface for the new 7-tier reboot escalation. Field evidence on
# the test device (5/13 13:37:15 and 14:59:21 plus implied repeats)
# proved the 2200-2212 5-tier ladder was insufficient: Tier 3
# (Restart-Computer -Force) issued the call, the loop process got
# killed by session teardown ~2-3s later, but the OS NEVER actually
# rebooted - it sat hung in the shutdown-in-progress state for hours.
# Tiers 4-5 (P/Invoke ExitWindowsEx + panic dump) NEVER FIRED because
# the loop process was already dead. The new escalation:
#   - Adds a Pre-Tier-1 sanity diagnostic (so we have forensics on WHY
#     the shutdown is being blocked, even when an upper tier eventually
#     succeeds).
#   - Adds a watchdog after every shutdown call that probes for any
#     OS-level sign of shutdown-in-progress within 60s. If nothing
#     happens, escalate IMMEDIATELY (don't wait the full pre-2213
#     ~90s tier budget).
#   - Adds Tier 4 WMI Win32_OperatingSystem.Win32Shutdown (different
#     code path than shutdown.exe / Restart-Computer; goes through
#     the WMI service to LSA shutdown subsystem).
#   - Adds Tier 6 NtRaiseHardError forced bugcheck (the nuclear
#     option; guaranteed to take the box down via kernel bugcheck
#     even if every user-mode shutdown path is wedged).

function Get-FbCurrentBootUpTime {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Snapshot the current OS LastBootUpTime as a probe baseline
        for the Tier 3+ watchdogs. If the LastBootUpTime CHANGES
        after a tier issues its shutdown call, we know the OS
        actually rebooted (we just happen to still be alive
        in some weird race - typically impossible). If the value
        STAYS the same after a tier's wait budget, the tier
        wedged and we must escalate.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.2213-tier-watchdog-and-diag-parsefix. Returns
        the LastBootUpTime as a [datetime], or $null on failure.
        Never throws.
    #>
    try {
        $os = Get-CimInstance -ClassName Win32_OperatingSystem -ErrorAction Stop
        return [datetime]$os.LastBootUpTime
    } catch {
        return $null
    }
}

function Test-FbOsShutdownStarted {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Single-shot probe: is the OS reporting shutdown-in-progress?
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.2213-tier-watchdog-and-diag-parsefix Critical Fix 1.
        Combines three independent signals - any one true means the
        OS has acknowledged the shutdown request and the loop should
        NOT escalate (just keep waiting for the box to actually go
        down). All three false means the shutdown is ignored and
        the caller should escalate immediately:
          1. [System.Environment]::HasShutdownStarted - the .NET
             shutdown-in-progress flag.
          2. WTSGetActiveConsoleSessionId() returning 0xFFFFFFFF
             (-1) - winlogon has detached the active console session,
             which only happens during shutdown-in-progress.
          3. LogonUI.exe or consent.exe child process appearing in
             Get-Process - winlogon spawns these to display the
             shutdown screen.
    .OUTPUTS
        [pscustomobject] with Started=[bool] and Evidence=[string]
        describing which signal tripped.
    #>
    $evidence = @()
    $started = $false

    try {
        if ([System.Environment]::HasShutdownStarted) {
            $started = $true
            $evidence += 'Environment.HasShutdownStarted=true'
        }
    } catch {}

    try {
        # WTSGetActiveConsoleSessionId is in kernel32; load it once.
        if (-not ([System.Management.Automation.PSTypeName]'FbWts').Type) {
            Add-Type -Namespace 'FbWts' -Name 'Native' -MemberDefinition @"
[System.Runtime.InteropServices.DllImport("kernel32.dll", SetLastError = true)]
public static extern uint WTSGetActiveConsoleSessionId();
"@ -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        }
        if (([System.Management.Automation.PSTypeName]'FbWts.Native').Type) {
            $sid = [FbWts.Native]::WTSGetActiveConsoleSessionId()
            if ($sid -eq 0xFFFFFFFF) {
                $started = $true
                $evidence += 'WTSGetActiveConsoleSessionId=0xFFFFFFFF'
            }
        }
    } catch {}

    try {
        $shutdownProcs = @(Get-Process -Name 'LogonUI','consent','wininit' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)
        if ($shutdownProcs -and $shutdownProcs.Count -gt 0) {
            foreach ($p in $shutdownProcs) {
                # LogonUI is always running when an interactive
                # session exists, so its mere presence is not a
                # shutdown signal. We need the START TIME to be
                # very recent (< 60s) AND it to be a NEW instance
                # vs the baseline. Best-effort: log the count
                # only.
                $evidence += ("Process:{0}(PID={1})" -f $p.Name, $p.Id)
            }
        }
    } catch {}

    return [pscustomobject]@{
        Started  = $started
        Evidence = ($evidence -join '|')
    }
}

function Write-FbRebootTierLog {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Append a single line to $script:RebootTierLogPath BEFORE a
        reboot tier issues its action (Stop-Service / shutdown.exe /
        Restart-Computer / WMI / P/Invoke / NtRaiseHardError).
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.2213f-reboot-ladder-optimization Bug C1. The 7-tier
        reboot ladder in Invoke-FbReboot can have its host process
        killed mid-tier by either the eventual OS reboot or by session
        teardown after a Tier 3 (Restart-Computer) wedge. The WU log
        has a write-buffer and may lose the last few lines on a hard
        kill, so the post-mortem can't reliably tell which tier was
        last attempted. This helper uses Add-Content (synchronous
        flush) to append a single ISO-timestamped line to the small
        on-disk log BEFORE each tier's action. The next boot's
        watchdog-recovery block reads the LAST line, parses out the
        Tier= value, and uses it to decide whether to skip the
        known-wedged Tier 1-3 dance on the new boot.
        Never throws (Add-Content errors are swallowed). Safe to call
        before $FbStateDir is verified to exist (we attempt to create
        the parent on demand).
    #>
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [int]$Tier,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$Action,
        [string]$Reason = ''
    )
    try {
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($script:RebootTierLogPath)) { return }
        $parent = Split-Path -Parent $script:RebootTierLogPath
        if ($parent -and -not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $parent)) {
            try { New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $parent -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null } catch {}
        }
        $reasonStr = if ($null -eq $Reason) { '' } else { [string]$Reason }
        $line = "[{0}] [PID {1}] Tier={2}/7 Action='{3}' Reason='{4}'" -f `
            ([DateTimeOffset]::Now.ToString('o')), $PID, $Tier, $Action, $reasonStr
        Add-Content -Path $script:RebootTierLogPath -Value $line -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
    } catch { }
}

function Wait-FbOsShutdownEvidence {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Tier-watchdog: poll Test-FbOsShutdownStarted every 5s for
        up to $TimeoutSeconds. Behavior depends on whether shutdown
        evidence ever appears:
          - No evidence at deadline: return $false (caller escalates).
          - Evidence appears, OS goes down within $ShutdownGraceSeconds:
            we never get to return (process is killed by session
            teardown - which is the desired outcome).
          - Evidence appears, $ShutdownGraceSeconds elapses while we
            are STILL ALIVE: shutdown-in-progress wedge confirmed.
            Return $false so caller escalates to next tier (which
            will fire a different code path on top of the wedge).
        LastBootUpTime returns $true only when the new value is at
        least 10 seconds later than the pre-reboot snapshot. Equal
        or same-second values (CIM vs WMI Kind/ms truncation) are
        NOT a reboot; stay in the ladder.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.2213-tier-watchdog-and-diag-parsefix Critical
        Fix 1, refined post-verifier. The pre-2213 5-tier ladder
        used flat $PhaseSeconds (60/90/120) wait, which gave the
        OS time to wedge into the shutdown-lock state Tier 3 then
        couldn't break out of. The original 2213 watchdog tried
        to be polite by staying after evidence appeared - but on
        the test device the OS's failure mode is precisely
        "shutdown-in-progress acknowledged then wedges forever",
        so polite waiting just costs us 60s per tier. The grace
        window (default 30s) is enough for a healthy OS to
        actually power-cycle, and short enough that wedge-cases
        escalate to a different code path within ~35s instead of
        the full 60s.
        Updates the dashboard activity bar each iteration so the
        operator can see the watchdog ticking down. Never throws.
    #>
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [int]$TierNumber,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [int]$TimeoutSeconds,
        [datetime]$BaselineBootUpTime,
        [string]$TierLabel = '',
        [int]$ShutdownGraceSeconds = 30
    )
    $waited = 0
    $tickSeconds = 5
    $firstEvidenceAtSeconds = -1
    $firstEvidenceText = ''
    $loggedBootDelta = $false
    while ($waited -lt $TimeoutSeconds) {
        if (Test-FbRebootWallHit) {
            try { Write-FbLog ("Tier {0} watchdog: Rebooting wall cap hit; forcing shutdown.exe /r /t 0 /f." -f $TierNumber) 'ERROR' } catch {}
            Invoke-FbForceOsRestart -Reason ('reboot-wall-tier-{0}' -f $TierNumber)
            Start-Sleep -Seconds 15
            return $true
        }
        $probe = Test-FbOsShutdownStarted
        if ($probe.Started) {
            if ($firstEvidenceAtSeconds -lt 0) {
                $firstEvidenceAtSeconds = $waited
                $firstEvidenceText = $probe.Evidence
                try { Write-FbLog ("Tier {0} watchdog: shutdown-in-progress evidence at {1}s ({2}); starting {3}s grace window for OS to actually power-cycle." -f $TierNumber, $waited, $probe.Evidence, $ShutdownGraceSeconds) 'INFO' } catch {}
                try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message ("Reboot tier={0}/7 - shutdown-in-progress confirmed ({1}); {2}s grace before escalating." -f $TierNumber, $probe.Evidence, $ShutdownGraceSeconds) } catch {}
            } else {
                $sinceFirst = $waited - $firstEvidenceAtSeconds
                if ($sinceFirst -ge $ShutdownGraceSeconds) {
                    try { Write-FbLog ("Tier {0} watchdog: shutdown-in-progress for {1}s ({2}) but OS has NOT actually powered down. Wedge confirmed - escalating to next tier so a different code path can fire on top." -f $TierNumber, $sinceFirst, $firstEvidenceText) 'WARN' } catch {}
                    try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message ("Reboot tier={0}/7 - shutdown wedge confirmed ({1}s grace expired); escalating." -f $TierNumber, $ShutdownGraceSeconds) } catch {}
                    return $false
                }
                try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message ("Reboot tier={0}/7 - in shutdown grace ({1}s/{2}s); waiting for OS to finish." -f $TierNumber, $sinceFirst, $ShutdownGraceSeconds) } catch {}
                try { Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Rebooting' } catch {}
            }
            Start-Sleep -Seconds $tickSeconds
            $waited += $tickSeconds
            continue
        }
        if ($BaselineBootUpTime) {
            $nowBootUp = Get-FbCurrentBootUpTime
            if ($nowBootUp) {
                $deltaSec = 0
                try {
                    $baseDt = [datetime]$BaselineBootUpTime
                    $nowDt = [datetime]$nowBootUp
                    if ($baseDt.Kind -eq [DateTimeKind]::Unspecified) {
                        $baseDt = [datetime]::SpecifyKind($baseDt, [DateTimeKind]::Local)
                    }
                    if ($nowDt.Kind -eq [DateTimeKind]::Unspecified) {
                        $nowDt = [datetime]::SpecifyKind($nowDt, [DateTimeKind]::Local)
                    }
                    $deltaSec = [int][math]::Floor(($nowDt.ToUniversalTime() - $baseDt.ToUniversalTime()).TotalSeconds)
                } catch {
                    $deltaSec = 0
                }
                # 5.4.13: CIM vs WMI Kind/ms truncation can make -gt true while
                # both values print as the same second. Equal or tiny deltas are
                # NOT a reboot; require a real 10s LastBootUpTime advance.
                if ($deltaSec -ge 10) {
                    try { Write-FbLog ("Tier {0} watchdog: LastBootUpTime CHANGED (delta={1}s; {2} -> {3}); OS rebooted but we are still running. Returning success so caller can exit cleanly." -f $TierNumber, $deltaSec, $BaselineBootUpTime, $nowBootUp) 'WARN' } catch {}
                    return $true
                }
                if (-not $loggedBootDelta) {
                    $loggedBootDelta = $true
                    if ($deltaSec -le 0) {
                        try { Write-FbLog ("Tier {0} watchdog: LastBootUpTime unchanged (delta=0s); not treating as reboot." -f $TierNumber) 'INFO' } catch {}
                    } else {
                        try { Write-FbLog ("Tier {0} watchdog: LastBootUpTime delta={1}s (below 10s); not treating as reboot." -f $TierNumber, $deltaSec) 'INFO' } catch {}
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        $msg = ("Tier {0} watchdog [{1}]: NO shutdown evidence at {2}s/{3}s budget; will escalate to next tier if no signal by deadline." -f $TierNumber, $TierLabel, $waited, $TimeoutSeconds)
        try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message ("Reboot tier={0}/7 - watchdog {2}s/{3}s, no shutdown signal yet (will escalate)" -f $TierNumber, $TierLabel, $waited, $TimeoutSeconds) } catch {}
        try { Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Rebooting' } catch {}
        try { Write-FbLog $msg 'INFO' } catch {}
        Start-Sleep -Seconds $tickSeconds
        $waited += $tickSeconds
    }
    try { Write-FbLog ("Tier {0} watchdog [{1}]: no shutdown evidence after {2}s; escalating immediately." -f $TierNumber, $TierLabel, $TimeoutSeconds) 'WARN' } catch {}
    return $false
}

function Get-FbPreShutdownDiagnostics {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Pre-Tier-1 forensics dump: log everything that affects
        whether the OS will accept a shutdown request, BEFORE we
        issue any shutdown call.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.2213-tier-watchdog-and-diag-parsefix Fix 7.
        Even when a higher tier eventually succeeds in rebooting
        the box, the next-boot operator (or the next-build
        diagnostic worker) needs to know WHY the lower tiers
        failed. Captures:
          - Test-FbWuRebootRequired result.
          - PendingFileRenameOperations registry value (truncated
            to 200 chars - it can be enormous on a fresh sysprep).
          - Status of services known to hold shutdown locks.
          - TiWorker.exe CPU% (if &gt; 0, TI is still working
            and any shutdown attempt will block).
          - Current LastBootUpTime (used as Tier 3 baseline).
        Returns a [pscustomobject] with all fields. Logs a
        summary line. Never throws.
    #>
    $diag = [ordered]@{
        Timestamp           = (Get-Date).ToString('o')
        OSPendingReboot     = $null
        PendingFileRename   = ''
        Services            = @()
        TiWorkerCpuSec      = 0.0
        TiWorkerWorkingMB   = 0.0
        LastBootUpTime      = $null
    }
    try { $diag.OSPendingReboot = [bool](Test-FbWuRebootRequired) } catch {}

    try {
        $sm = 'HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session Manager'
        if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $sm) {
            $pfr = (Get-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $sm -Name 'PendingFileRenameOperations' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).PendingFileRenameOperations
            if ($pfr) {
                $pfrStr = ($pfr -join ' | ')
                if ($pfrStr.Length -gt 200) { $pfrStr = $pfrStr.Substring(0, 200) + '... [truncated]' }
                $diag.PendingFileRename = $pfrStr
            }
        }
    } catch {}

    foreach ($svc in @('wuauserv','bits','trustedinstaller','setup','WindowsModulesInstallerWorker','cryptsvc')) {
        try {
            $s = Get-Service -Name $svc -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            if ($s) {
                $diag.Services += [pscustomobject]@{
                    Name = $svc
                    Status = [string]$s.Status
                    StartType = [string]$s.StartType
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }

    try {
        $ti = @(Get-Process -Name 'TiWorker','TrustedInstaller' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)
        foreach ($t in $ti) {
            try { $diag.TiWorkerCpuSec    += [double]$t.CPU } catch {}
            try { $diag.TiWorkerWorkingMB += ([double]$t.WorkingSet64 / 1MB) } catch {}
        }
    } catch {}

    try { $diag.LastBootUpTime = Get-FbCurrentBootUpTime } catch {}

    $svcSummary = ($diag.Services | ForEach-Object { ('{0}={1}' -f $_.Name, $_.Status) }) -join ','
    $logLine = ("Pre-Tier-1 sanity check: OSPendingReboot={0} TiWorkerCpu={1:N1}s TiWorkerWS={2:N0}MB LastBootUp={3} PendingFileRename='{4}' Services=[{5}]" -f $diag.OSPendingReboot, $diag.TiWorkerCpuSec, $diag.TiWorkerWorkingMB, $diag.LastBootUpTime, $diag.PendingFileRename, $svcSummary)
    try { Write-FbLog $logLine 'WARN' } catch {}

    return [pscustomobject]$diag
}

function Test-FbCbsRebootInProgressKey {
    try {
        return [bool](Test-Path -LiteralPath 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Component Based Servicing\RebootInProgress')
    } catch {
        return $false
    }
}

function Get-FbTiWorkerSample {
    $cpu = 0.0
    $wsMb = 0.0
    $n = 0
    try {
        foreach ($t in @(Get-Process -Name 'TiWorker', 'TrustedInstaller' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)) {
            if (-not $t) { continue }
            $n++
            try { $cpu += [double]$t.CPU } catch {}
            try { $wsMb += ([double]$t.WorkingSet64 / 1MB) } catch {}
        }
    } catch {}
    return [pscustomobject]@{ Count = $n; CpuSec = $cpu; WsMb = $wsMb }
}

function Wait-FbCbsServicingIdle {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Wait for TiWorker/CBS to finish applying packages before issuing shutdown.exe.
        If CBS RebootInProgress is already set, do not issue another shutdown (that
        aborts the in-progress restart). Heartbeat stays live so the Rebooting watchdog
        does not treat this wait as a dead process.
    #>
    param(
        [int]$MaxWaitSeconds = 480,
        [int]$ProbeSeconds = 10,
        [int]$IdleProbesRequired = 3
    )
    $waited = 0
    $idleStreak = 0
    $prevCpu = -1.0
    $script:FbSkipStopTrustedInstaller = $false
    while ($waited -lt $MaxWaitSeconds) {
        $rip = Test-FbCbsRebootInProgressKey
        $sample = Get-FbTiWorkerSample
        $cpuDelta = 0.0
        if ($prevCpu -ge 0) { $cpuDelta = [math]::Max(0, $sample.CpuSec - $prevCpu) }
        $prevCpu = $sample.CpuSec
        $busy = $false
        if ($rip) {
            $busy = $true
            $script:FbSkipStopTrustedInstaller = $true
        } elseif ($sample.Count -gt 0 -and $cpuDelta -ge 0.4) {
            $busy = $true
            $script:FbSkipStopTrustedInstaller = $true
        }
        if ($busy) { $idleStreak = 0 } else { $idleStreak++ }

        $msg = if ($rip) {
            ("Windows is finishing a restart (CBS RebootInProgress). Do not issue another shutdown. Waited {0}s / {1}s." -f $waited, $MaxWaitSeconds)
        } elseif ($sample.Count -gt 0) {
            ("Windows is applying installed updates (TiWorker CPU delta {0:N1}s, WS {1:N0} MB). Please wait. {2}s / {3}s." -f $cpuDelta, $sample.WsMb, $waited, $MaxWaitSeconds)
        } else {
            ("Servicing idle ({0} clean probe(s)). {1}s / {2}s." -f $idleStreak, $waited, $MaxWaitSeconds)
        }
        try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'ApplyingUpdates' -Message $msg } catch {}
        try { Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'ApplyingUpdates' } catch {}
        try { Write-FbLog ("Wait-FbCbsServicingIdle: rip={0} tiCount={1} cpuDelta={2:N1} idleStreak={3} waited={4}s" -f $rip, $sample.Count, $cpuDelta, $idleStreak, $waited) 'INFO' } catch {}

        if ($rip) {
            Start-Sleep -Seconds $ProbeSeconds
            $waited += $ProbeSeconds
            $probe = $null
            try { $probe = Test-FbOsShutdownStarted } catch {}
            if ($probe -and $probe.Started) {
                try { Write-FbLog ("Wait-FbCbsServicingIdle: OS shutdown already in progress ({0}); waiting for the box to go down instead of issuing shutdown.exe." -f $probe.Evidence) 'WARN' } catch {}
                return [pscustomobject]@{ State = 'RebootInProgress'; WaitedSec = $waited }
            }
            continue
        }
        if ($idleStreak -ge $IdleProbesRequired) {
            return [pscustomobject]@{ State = 'Idle'; WaitedSec = $waited }
        }
        Start-Sleep -Seconds $ProbeSeconds
        $waited += $ProbeSeconds
    }
    if (Test-FbCbsRebootInProgressKey) {
        return [pscustomobject]@{ State = 'RebootInProgress'; WaitedSec = $waited }
    }
    return [pscustomobject]@{ State = 'TimeoutBusy'; WaitedSec = $waited }
}

function Invoke-FbWmiWin32ShutdownReboot {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Tier 4 reboot escalation: WMI Win32_OperatingSystem.
        Win32Shutdown(Flags=6) - completely different code path
        than shutdown.exe or Restart-Computer.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.2213-tier-watchdog-and-diag-parsefix Critical
        Fix 2. Field evidence: shutdown.exe and Restart-Computer
        BOTH wedge on the same shutdown-lock state on this
        hardware. WMI Win32Shutdown is dispatched via the WMI
        service (winmgmt) to the LSA shutdown subsystem, which
        is a different IPC path; if the shutdown lock that
        wedges shutdown.exe is held in user-mode, WMI may
        bypass it.
        Flags = 2 (EWX_REBOOT) | 4 (EWX_FORCE) = 6 = forced
        reboot. Never throws. Returns $true if WMI accepted
        the call (HRESULT == 0), $false otherwise.
    #>
    param([string]$Reason = 'FirstBase Tier 4 WMI Win32Shutdown reboot')
    try {
        $os = Get-CimInstance -ClassName Win32_OperatingSystem -ErrorAction Stop
        $result = $os | Invoke-CimMethod -MethodName 'Win32Shutdown' -Arguments @{ Flags = [uint32]6 } -ErrorAction Stop
        $rv = if ($result) { [int]$result.ReturnValue } else { -1 }
        try { Write-FbLog ("Tier 4 WMI Win32Shutdown(Flags=6): ReturnValue={0} (0 = success). Reason='{1}'" -f $rv, $Reason) 'WARN' } catch {}
        return ($rv -eq 0)
    } catch {
        try { Write-FbLog ("Tier 4 WMI Win32Shutdown: invocation threw: {0}." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN' } catch {}
        return $false
    }
}

function Invoke-FbNtRaiseHardErrorReboot {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Tier 6 reboot escalation: NtRaiseHardError forced
        bugcheck. The kernel forces a bugcheck (BSOD) which
        triggers a hard reboot. Last resort.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.2213-tier-watchdog-and-diag-parsefix Critical
        Fix 3. NtRaiseHardError with status code 0xC0000420
        and ValidResponseOption=6 (OptionShutdownSystem) tells
        the kernel "this is an unrecoverable error; bugcheck
        the system". Requires SeShutdownPrivilege on the
        process token (we enable it via RtlAdjustPrivilege).
        WARNING: this leaves a memory dump in C:\Windows\
        MEMORY.DMP and the next-boot Event Log shows a
        bugcheck. Acceptable for this audit-mode pipeline
        because:
          - Audit mode is pre-OOBE; nothing of value to lose.
          - The dump is forensic evidence of the wedge.
          - Without this, the box sits hung for hours.
        Never throws (Add-Type errors etc are caught and
        logged). Returns $true if NtRaiseHardError accepted
        the call (NTSTATUS == 0).
    #>
    param([string]$Reason = 'FirstBase Tier 6 NtRaiseHardError forced bugcheck')

    $sig = @'
using System;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;

public static class FbNtRaiseHardError {
    [DllImport("ntdll.dll", SetLastError = true)]
    public static extern uint NtRaiseHardError(
        uint ErrorStatus,
        uint NumberOfParameters,
        uint UnicodeStringParameterMask,
        IntPtr Parameters,
        uint ValidResponseOption,
        out uint Response);

    [DllImport("ntdll.dll", SetLastError = true)]
    public static extern int RtlAdjustPrivilege(
        int Privilege,
        bool Enable,
        bool CurrentThread,
        ref bool OldValue);
}
'@

    try {
        if (-not ([System.Management.Automation.PSTypeName]'FbNtRaiseHardError').Type) {
            Add-Type -TypeDefinition $sig -Language CSharp -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null
            Write-FbLog 'Tier 6 NtRaiseHardError: Add-Type FbNtRaiseHardError loaded.' 'INFO'
        }
    } catch {
        try { Write-FbLog ("Tier 6 NtRaiseHardError: Add-Type failed: {0}. Cannot bugcheck." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR' } catch {}
        return $false
    }

    try {
        $oldPriv = $false
        # Privilege 19 = SeShutdownPrivilege (NTSTATUS values from
        # ntdef.h; matches the 19 used by sysinternals psshutdown).
        [void][FbNtRaiseHardError]::RtlAdjustPrivilege(19, $true, $false, [ref]$oldPriv)
        try { Write-FbLog ("Tier 6 NtRaiseHardError: RtlAdjustPrivilege(SeShutdownPrivilege=19) OldValue={0}." -f $oldPriv) 'INFO' } catch {}
    } catch {
        try { Write-FbLog ("Tier 6 NtRaiseHardError: RtlAdjustPrivilege threw: {0}." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN' } catch {}
    }

    try {
        $response = [uint32]0
        # ErrorStatus = 0xC0000420 (STATUS_ASSERTION_FAILURE - any
        # critical NTSTATUS works; 0xC0000420 is conventional for
        # forced bugcheck in psshutdown-class tools).
        # ValidResponseOption = 6 (OptionShutdownSystem - the kernel
        # interpretation that triggers the bugcheck-and-reboot path).
        $rv = [FbNtRaiseHardError]::NtRaiseHardError(0xC0000420, 0, 0, [IntPtr]::Zero, 6, [ref]$response)
        try { Write-FbLog ("Tier 6 NtRaiseHardError: ErrorStatus=0xC0000420 ValidResponseOption=6 returned NTSTATUS=0x{0:X8}, Response={1}. Reason='{2}'. Box should bugcheck within seconds." -f $rv, $response, $Reason) 'ERROR' } catch {}
        return ($rv -eq 0)
    } catch {
        try { Write-FbLog ("Tier 6 NtRaiseHardError: invocation threw: {0}." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR' } catch {}
        return $false
    }
}

function Invoke-FbReboot {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        OS-level reboot entry point. Performs reboots ONLY; does NOT
        decide whether to enter sysprep handoff (that responsibility
        lives solely in Invoke-FbWuEndOfPassDecision).

    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.2210-fullscope-multipass-progress-fixes (doc
        synchronized to actual 2210 behavior in the 2211 cosmetic
        scrub):

        Invoke-FbReboot is the single end-all reboot point for the
        loop. It READS PendingSysprep from wu-state.json on entry
        and chooses between (a) plain tiered reboot, or (b) STAGE 2
        sysprep handoff via Invoke-FbOobeHandoff. It NEVER WRITES
        PendingSysprep - the only writer in the entire codebase is
        the strict 4-condition convergence gate in
        Invoke-FbWuEndOfPassDecision.

        Flow (in order, all guarded against null state):

          1. RunOnce re-arm + read-only PendingSysprep entry probe.
             RunOnce is rewritten unconditionally so even a crash
             immediately afterwards leaves the next boot able to
             relaunch us. The PendingSysprep probe is logged
             ("Invoke-FbReboot: PendingSysprep=X (read from state,
             not modified)") for pass-by-pass debug.

          2. Kill-switch. If C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\
             NEVER-CASCADE.flag exists, route UNCONDITIONALLY to
             Invoke-FbOobeHandoff. This is the field-engineer
             escape hatch; documented in the script header so a
             tech can drop the flag in via notepad.

          2.5 Escape-valve probe. If Test-FbEscapeValve.Trip is
             $true (operator override flag, or prior FutileBootCount
             threshold hit), route to Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff.

          2.6 STAGE 2 fast-path. If wu-state.json shows
             PendingSysprep=$true AND Test-FbWuRebootRequired
             returns $false (CBS settled), route directly to
             Invoke-FbOobeHandoff which runs Wait-FbPendingReboot-
             Cleared then launches sysprep. This is how the
             convergence gate's STAGE 1 latch eventually becomes
             a sysprep launch on the next boot.

          3. Fresh state re-read. ALWAYS reads from disk; never
             trusts the in-memory $state hashtable.

          4. Read-only diagnostic probe (2210 Fix 2 replacement
             for the deleted legacy evidence-gate block). Logs
             "Invoke-FbReboot: PendingSysprep=X (read from state,
             not modified). Legacy evidence-gate disabled in 2210"
             with the supporting evidence triplet (RealInstalls-
             Total, RealInstallsThisBoot, OSWantsReboot) so a
             reviewer can correlate the boot's evidence with the
             routing choice. NO writes to disk. NO sysprep launch
             from this branch.

          4.5 PRE-TIER-1 SANITY CHECK (2213 Fix 7). Logs a forensic
             snapshot of the OS state - OSPendingReboot,
             PendingFileRenameOperations, services known to hold
             shutdown locks, TiWorker.exe CPU time, and the
             current LastBootUpTime - BEFORE issuing any shutdown.
             The LastBootUpTime is captured as the watchdog
             baseline used by Tiers 2-6.

          5. TIERED REBOOT ESCALATION (Tiers 1-7, 2213 expanded
             from the pre-2213 5-tier ladder). Each tier (except
             Tier 1's patient-shutdown body) is wrapped in a
             Wait-FbOsShutdownEvidence watchdog that escalates
             IMMEDIATELY at the tier deadline if no OS shutdown-
             in-progress signs appear (instead of the pre-2213
             flat wait). The dashboard activity bar carries
             "Reboot tier=N/7 | Reason=<latest>" while
             $script:UiRebootTier > 0.

             Tier 1 - Patient shutdown.exe (the historical "no
                      cascade" path). Phase A: shutdown.exe /r /f
                      /t 30 (30s warning since 2214a; was 60 in 2213z), then up to 2min observ-
                      able wait (120s in 2213; was 300s pre-2213).
                      Phase B (if still up): shutdown.exe /r /f
                      /t 0, then up to 1min wait (60s in 2213;
                      was 120s pre-2213). Total Tier 1 budget
                      ~3 minutes (was ~7 minutes pre-2213).
                      Reduced because field evidence proved the
                      wide budget gave the OS time to wedge into
                      the shutdown-lock state Tier 3 then could
                      not break.

             Tier 2 - Stop services that hold the shutdown lock
                      (wuauserv, bits, cryptsvc, trustedinstaller,
                      setup) then retry shutdown.exe /r /f /t 0.
                      10s watchdog (2258: reduced from 60s).

             Tier 3 - Restart-Computer -Force (PowerShell cmdlet,
                      different code path than shutdown.exe).
                      15s watchdog (2213f reduced from 60s). Field
                      evidence: this tier WEDGED 3 times on test
                      device - issued Restart-Computer, loop
                      process killed by session teardown ~2-3s
                      later, OS NEVER actually rebooted. Watchdog
                      exists so we can confirm via
                      Wait-FbOsShutdownEvidence whether the OS has
                      acknowledged or whether we should escalate.

             Tier 4 - NEW IN 2213: WMI Win32_OperatingSystem.
                      Win32Shutdown(Flags=6). Different code path
                      than shutdown.exe / Restart-Computer; goes
                      through winmgmt -> LSA shutdown subsystem.
                      If a stale shutdown lock in user-mode wedged
                      Tiers 1-3, WMI may bypass it.
                      10s watchdog (2258: reduced from 60s).

             Tier 5 - P/Invoke ExitWindowsEx with EWX_REBOOT |
                      EWX_FORCE | EWX_FORCEIFHUNG after enabling
                      SeShutdownPrivilege on the process token.
                      Was Tier 4 in 2200-2212; renumbered for
                      the new WMI tier.
                      10s watchdog (2258: reduced from 60s).

             Tier 6 - NEW IN 2213: NtRaiseHardError(0xC0000420,
                      ValidResponseOption=6) NUCLEAR OPTION. The
                      kernel forces a bugcheck (BSOD) which
                      triggers a hard reboot via the kernel's
                      emergency-shutdown path. Bypasses ALL user-
                      mode shutdown machinery. Acceptable in
                      audit mode (pre-OOBE; nothing of value to
                      lose; resulting MEMORY.DMP is forensic
                      evidence). 60s watchdog before falling
                      through to Tier 7.

             Tier 7 - Final dump + re-arm + exit (was Tier 5 in
                      2200-2212; renumbered for the new tiers).
                      Writes STUCK-DIAG-SHUTDOWN-LOCKED-
                      <timestamp>.json panic record (now via the
                      2213 parse-fixed Write-FbShutdownLocked-
                      StuckDiag), increments BootsSinceLastInstall
                      so the next boot's escape valve probe trips
                      at >=3, audits and re-arms all four trigger
                      layers, exits 1 with the dashboard surfacing
                      "Press Restart manually to continue updating
                      | Reboot tier=7/7 | Reason=<latest>".

        Never returns to the caller. Either OOBE handoff / sysprep
        exits 0, a tier successfully takes the box down, or Tier 7
        exits 1 (the operator does the manual restart and the next
        boot's RunOnce / ONSTART / ONLOGON / kickstart launcher
        relaunches the loop).

    .NOTES
        2210+ invariant: Invoke-FbReboot is READ-ONLY for
        PendingSysprep. Verify with:
          rg "-PendingSysprep\s+\$true" Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1
        Should return EXACTLY ONE match - the convergence gate
        in Invoke-FbWuEndOfPassDecision. Any other writer is a
        regression of the 1915-class hang and must be removed
        before the build ships.

        Historical context (kept for the audit trail; superseded
        by the current 2210+ description above):
          - Pre-2210 builds had a "PRIMARY GATE" inside Invoke-
            FbReboot at Step 4 that routed to Invoke-FbOobeHandoff
            on (InstallsCompleted=true + RealInstallsTotal>0 +
            Test-FbWuRebootRequired=true) and wrote PendingSysprep=
            $true on disk. This was the exact pathology the
            multi-pass design (2200) and the strict-convergence
            gate (2210) were created to eliminate; the writer was
            removed in 2210 and Invoke-FbReboot became read-only.
          - The 1030-no-cascade-final, 1700-real-install-evidence,
            1830-patient-shutdown-and-hresult-coverage, and 1900-
            two-stage-sysprep-handoff patches are the lineage that
            shaped Tier 1's behavior. The Tier 2-5 escalation was
            introduced in 2200 after the 1915 incident showed Tier
            1 alone could not reliably take a TrustedInstaller-
            blocked box down.
          - 2213 expanded the ladder to 7 tiers: insertion of WMI
            Win32Shutdown as Tier 4 (between Restart-Computer and
            ExitWindowsEx), insertion of NtRaiseHardError forced
            bugcheck as Tier 6 (between ExitWindowsEx and the
            panic-dump exit), and a Wait-FbOsShutdownEvidence
            watchdog around every escalating tier. Field evidence:
            the test device wedged Tier 3 (Restart-Computer)
            three times in 24 hours - issued the call, the loop
            process was killed by session teardown, but the OS
            never actually rebooted; the pre-2213 ~90s flat wait
            then aged out without ever escalating to Tier 4. The
            new watchdog escalates IMMEDIATELY when no OS-level
            shutdown evidence appears within 60s.
    #>
    param([string]$Reason)
    Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot called: {0}" -f $Reason) 'WARN'
    try {
        $riDisk = Load-State
        $priorRi = 0
        try { $priorRi = [int]$riDisk.RebootIterations } catch { $priorRi = 0 }
        $newRi = $priorRi + 1
        $persistBc = 0
        try { $persistBc = [int]$riDisk.BootCount } catch { $persistBc = 0 }
        $persistRetry = @{}
        try { if ($riDisk.UpdateRetry) { $persistRetry = $riDisk.UpdateRetry } } catch {}
        $persistGivenUp = @()
        try { if ($riDisk.GivenUpUpdateIds) { $persistGivenUp = @($riDisk.GivenUpUpdateIds) } } catch {}
        $persistBck = @{}
        try { if ($riDisk.BootCountByKey) { $persistBck = $riDisk.BootCountByKey } } catch {}
        $persistHidden = @()
        try { if ($riDisk.ForceHiddenKeys) { $persistHidden = @($riDisk.ForceHiddenKeys) } } catch {}
        $persistSysprep = 0
        try { $persistSysprep = [int]$riDisk.SysprepRetryCount } catch { $persistSysprep = 0 }
        Save-State -EmptySweepCount ([int]$riDisk.EmptySweepCount) `
            -LastPass ([string]$riDisk.LastPass) `
            -StartTime ([string]$riDisk.StartTime) `
            -FailureCount ([int]$riDisk.FailureCount) `
            -UpdateRetry $persistRetry `
            -GivenUpUpdateIds $persistGivenUp `
            -BootCountByKey $persistBck `
            -BootCount $persistBc `
            -ForceHiddenKeys $persistHidden `
            -RebootIterations $newRi `
            -SysprepRetryCount $persistSysprep
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: RebootIterations {0} -> {1} (splash REBOOTS source of truth)." -f $priorRi, $newRi) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: RebootIterations bump failed: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # 2026.05.13.2213f-reboot-ladder-optimization Bug C1: prune
    # reboot-tier.log so it doesn't grow unbounded across builds.
    # Cap at 200 lines; if exceeded, keep the LAST 100 (just enough
    # to preserve the last few boots' history for post-mortem).
    # Errors are swallowed - the log is forensic-only and never
    # blocks reboot.
    try {
        if ($script:RebootTierLogPath -and (Test-Path -LiteralPath $script:RebootTierLogPath)) {
            $existing = @(Get-Content -LiteralPath $script:RebootTierLogPath -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)
            if ($existing -and $existing.Count -gt 200) {
                $kept = @($existing | Select-Object -Last 100)
                Set-Content -LiteralPath $script:RebootTierLogPath -Value $kept -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: pruned reboot-tier.log from {0} -> {1} lines (200/100 cap)." -f $existing.Count, $kept.Count) 'INFO'
            }
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: reboot-tier.log prune threw: {0}; continuing." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # 2026.05.13.2210-fullscope-multipass-progress-fixes Critical
    # Fix 2: emit a read-only PendingSysprep probe at every reboot
    # entry. Invoke-FbReboot NEVER writes PendingSysprep; the only
    # writer is Invoke-FbWuEndOfPassDecision's strict-convergence
    # gate. This log line gives pass-by-pass evidence in WU-*.log
    # that Invoke-FbReboot is observing (not setting) the latch.
    try {
        $entryProbeDisk = Load-State
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: PendingSysprep={0} (read from state, not modified)." -f ([bool]$entryProbeDisk.PendingSysprep)) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: PendingSysprep=<load-state threw: {0}> (read from state, not modified)." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    $script:FbRebootWallStart = Get-Date
    Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message ("Reboot requested - probing state: {0}" -f $Reason)
    try { Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Rebooting' } catch {}

    # Step 1: RunOnce re-arm. Idempotent; safe to call repeatedly.
    # Moved here from the prior cascade Stage 0 so it ALWAYS fires
    # before any shutdown attempt, no matter which branch we take
    # below. The ONSTART / ONLOGON backup tasks remain registered
    # independently, so even if reg.exe somehow fails the next
    # boot still relaunches us.
    try { Set-RunOnceAutostart -Reason $Reason } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: Set-RunOnceAutostart threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    # 5.4.13 (5CD107C4W3-0949-HP): ONSTART Last Run stayed 11/30/1999
    # through an OOBE-resealed WU reboot. Existence is not health.
    # 5.4.14: do NOT /Delete the running TNs (that left next boot with
    # no loop/splash tasks). Repair adds sibling Boot2 + AnyLogon.
    try {
        $preRebootTasks = Repair-FbScheduledTasks -Context 'pre-reboot'
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: pre-reboot SYSTEM ONSTART recreate ok={0}." -f $preRebootTasks) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: Repair-FbScheduledTasks (pre-reboot) threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # Step 2: Kill-switch. If the operator dropped NEVER-CASCADE.flag
    # in via Task Manager -> Run -> notepad -> save-as, route straight
    # to OOBE handoff. This is the "last resort recovery" for a device
    # that even the simplified flow somehow can't reboot. Sysprep's
    # reboot path is documented to be different from shutdown.exe's,
    # so even on a TI-blocked device sysprep usually wins.
    if (Test-Path $NeverCascadeFlag) {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: NEVER-CASCADE.flag detected at '{0}'; routing UNCONDITIONALLY to OOBE handoff (no shutdown.exe attempt)." -f $NeverCascadeFlag) 'WARN'
        Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Completed' -Message ("[NEVER-CASCADE.flag set] Routing to OOBE handoff. Original reason: {0}" -f $Reason)
        try { Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Completed' } catch {}
        Invoke-FbOobeHandoff -Reason ("NEVER-CASCADE.flag forced OOBE handoff. Original reason: {0}" -f $Reason)
        return
    }

    # Step 2.5: ESCAPE VALVE. 2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve. The
    # evaluator (Test-FbEscapeValve) ALWAYS does end-of-boot futile-
    # boot accounting (reset / increment / hold) on the disk
    # counter, even when no trip condition fires - so the counter
    # advances on every reboot of every boot in the cycle. If the
    # trip flips on (OPERATOR_OVERRIDE flag, or FutileBootCount
    # >= threshold with RealInstallsTotal still 0), we route to
    # Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff which writes the panic record,
    # relabels updates as "Failed - tech review required",
    # registers the warning-popup RunOnce, and calls
    # Invoke-FbOobeHandoff directly - BYPASSING the build 1700
    # evidence gate below. Never returns to this caller in the
    # trip branch.
    $escape = $null
    try {
        $escape = Test-FbEscapeValve -Stage 'reboot-call'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: Test-FbEscapeValve threw: {0}; continuing to evidence gate." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    if ($escape -and [bool]$escape.Trip) {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: escape valve tripped at reboot-call (Reason={0}, FutileNow={1}, Threshold={2}, Evidence='{3}'). Routing to Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff." -f $escape.Reason, $escape.FutileNow, $escape.Threshold, $escape.EvidenceText) 'ERROR'
        Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff -Reason ([string]$escape.Reason) -FutileNow ([int]$escape.FutileNow) -Threshold ([int]$escape.Threshold)
        return
    }

    # Step 2.6: TWO-STAGE FAST PATH. 2026.05.13.1900-two-stage-
    # sysprep-handoff Fix 2. If a prior boot's Invoke-FbReboot ran
    # STAGE 1 (latched PendingSysprep=true and patient-shutdown'd
    # to let CBS settle), and we are now on the post-reboot boot
    # with CBS settled, route directly to STAGE 2 - Invoke-
    # FbOobeHandoff - without re-reading the evidence triplet and
    # without re-running the patient-shutdown sequence. This is
    # also the entry point for the case where the loop body
    # finishes a no-op iteration on Boot 2 and falls through to a
    # final Invoke-FbReboot call that has no other reason to fire
    # (the main-loop body has its own STAGE 2 short-circuit, but
    # this is the safety net for any code path that reaches
    # Invoke-FbReboot first).
    $stage2Pending = $false
    $stage2OsClean = $false
    try {
        $stage2Disk = Load-State
        $stage2Pending = [bool]$stage2Disk.PendingSysprep
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot STAGE 2 fast-path: Load-State threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    if ($stage2Pending) {
        try { $stage2OsClean = (-not (Test-FbWuRebootRequired)) } catch { $stage2OsClean = $false }
        if ($stage2OsClean) {
            Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot STAGE 2 fast-path: PendingSysprep=true on disk AND Test-FbWuRebootRequired=false. Routing directly to Invoke-FbOobeHandoff (skips patient-shutdown path). Original reason: {0}" -f $Reason) 'WARN'
            try {
                Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'reboot-call-stage2-fastpath' -Message ("STAGE 2 fast-path: PendingSysprep=true, OS clean. Routing to sysprep. Original reason: {0}" -f $Reason)
            } catch {}
            Invoke-FbOobeHandoff -Reason ("Invoke-FbReboot STAGE 2 fast-path (PendingSysprep latched, OS clean). Original reason: {0}" -f $Reason)
            return
        } else {
            Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot STAGE 2 fast-path: PendingSysprep=true but Test-FbWuRebootRequired=true; CBS has not settled yet. Falling through to STAGE 1 patient-shutdown path for another reboot attempt." -f $Reason) 'WARN'
        }
    }

    # Step 3: Fresh state re-read. ALWAYS reads from disk.
    $diskState = $null
    $latched   = $false
    $osWantsReboot = $false
    $realInstallsTotal = 0
    try { $diskState = Load-State } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: Load-State threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    if ($diskState) {
        try { $latched = [bool]$diskState.InstallsCompleted } catch { $latched = $false }
        try { $realInstallsTotal = [int]$diskState.RealInstallsTotal } catch { $realInstallsTotal = 0 }
    }
    try { $osWantsReboot = [bool](Test-FbWuRebootRequired) } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: Test-FbWuRebootRequired threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        $osWantsReboot = $false
    }
    $script:LastInstallsCompletedDiag = ("Invoke-FbReboot entry: InstallsCompleted={0}, RealInstallsTotal={1}, RealInstallsThisBoot={2}, Test-FbWuRebootRequired={3}, reason='{4}'" -f $latched, $realInstallsTotal, $script:RealInstallsThisBoot, $osWantsReboot, $Reason)
    Write-FbLog $script:LastInstallsCompletedDiag 'INFO'
    # 2026.05.12.1700: surface full evidence on the dashboard so
    # the silent-bypass class of bug is visible from the splash.
    try {
        Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'reboot-call' -Message ("Invoke-FbReboot probe: Test-FbWuRebootRequired={0}, reason='{1}'" -f $osWantsReboot, $Reason)
    } catch {}

    # Step 4: 2026.05.13.2210-fullscope-multipass-progress-fixes
    # Critical Fix 2. The 2200 (and prior) builds had Invoke-FbReboot
    # decide on its own whether to enter sysprep handoff, based on
    # the legacy "InstallsCompleted=true + RealInstalls > 0 +
    # Test-FbWuRebootRequired=true" rule. That was a backdoor that
    # could (and did) latch PendingSysprep=true on a CBS-pending
    # boot and re-introduce the exact hang the multi-pass design
    # was supposed to eliminate.
    #
    # 2210: Invoke-FbReboot is now READ-ONLY for PendingSysprep.
    # The decision to enter sysprep handoff lives ONLY in
    # Invoke-FbWuEndOfPassDecision and is gated by the strict
    # 4-condition convergence test (workQueueEmpty +
    # noRebootNeeded + rescanClean + priorPassWasClean).
    #
    # If $stage2Pending (read at the top of this function in
    # Step 2.6) is true AND Test-FbWuRebootRequired=false, the
    # fast-path above already routed to Invoke-FbOobeHandoff and
    # returned. If we are here, either PendingSysprep=$false
    # (plain tiered reboot) or PendingSysprep=$true with CBS
    # still pending (fall through to the tiered patient-shutdown
    # below; STAGE 2 will retry on the next boot).
    $hasRealEvidence = ($script:RealInstallsThisBoot -gt 0) -or ($realInstallsTotal -gt 0)
    Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: PendingSysprep={0} (read from state, not modified). Legacy evidence-gate disabled in 2210; routing decisions live in Invoke-FbWuEndOfPassDecision. RealInstallsTotal={1}, RealInstallsThisBoot={2}, OSWantsReboot={3}." -f $stage2Pending, $realInstallsTotal, $script:RealInstallsThisBoot, $osWantsReboot) 'INFO'

    if ($latched -and -not $hasRealEvidence) {
        # The latch claims true but no real install evidence is
        # visible. This SHOULD be impossible after the 1700 fix
        # (Save-State invariant scrubs the latch on persist; the
        # Load-State scrub clears legacy latches on read-back),
        # but be paranoid: refuse to route to OOBE handoff and
        # downgrade to plain shutdown /r. Log loudly.
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: INVARIANT VIOLATION: latch=true with no real install evidence (RealInstallsTotal={0}, ThisBoot={1}). Downgrading to plain shutdown /r." -f $realInstallsTotal, $script:RealInstallsThisBoot) 'ERROR'
    } elseif ($osWantsReboot -and -not $hasRealEvidence) {
        # OS pending-reboot signal without any real install
        # evidence: very likely sysprep/specialize/image-apply
        # leftovers on a fresh image. A plain shutdown /r lets
        # the OS settle its own pending operations on reboot and
        # we come back ready to retry the scan/install.
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: Test-FbWuRebootRequired=true with no real install evidence (RealInstallsTotal={0}, ThisBoot={1}). Plain shutdown /r (NOT OOBE handoff) so we can retry the scan/install on next boot." -f $realInstallsTotal, $script:RealInstallsThisBoot) 'WARN'
    }

    # Step 5: PATIENT REBOOT. No real install evidence (or the
    # invariant was violated). 2026.05.12.1830 patient sequence:
    #
    #   Phase A (warning): shutdown.exe /r /f /t 30 (2214a; was 60 through 2213z) - shorter
    #     user-visible countdown when shutdown is not blocked. Then wait up to
    #     2 minutes (120s) for the OS to actually go down. Heartbeat +
    #     activity-bar refresh every 10 seconds; reason probe
    #     (registry/WUA) re-runs each tick so the operator sees
    #     WHY we are waiting.
    #   Phase B (force):  if still up after Phase A, shutdown.exe
    #     /r /f /t 0 - no warning; services have had their chance.
    #     Then wait up to 2 minutes.
    #   Exit:  if still up after Phase A+B (total wall clock
    #     budget per tier ladder), exit 1. The exit message
    #     embeds the LAST reason probe so the operator can see
    #     what was holding the box up.
    #
    # NO cascade. NO P/Invoke. Only shutdown.exe / Start-Process.
    Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: entering patient reboot path. Reason: {0}" -f $Reason) 'WARN'

    $shutdownExe = Join-Path $env:WINDIR 'System32\shutdown.exe'
    if (-not (Test-Path $shutdownExe)) { $shutdownExe = 'shutdown.exe' }

    # Initial reason probe so the very first dashboard message is
    # already explanatory rather than a generic "Rebooting...".
    $lastReason = ''
    try { $lastReason = [string](Get-FbRebootReasonLabel) } catch { $lastReason = 'reason probe failed' }
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($lastReason)) { $lastReason = 'no specific OS reboot signal detected' }

    # Patient-reboot helper: tick the heartbeat + dashboard every 10s
    # for $PhaseSeconds total, re-running the reason probe so the
    # activity bar always carries the latest "why are we waiting"
    # explanation. Returns the LAST reason string (used for the
    # final exit message) so the operator can see what was holding
    # the box up at the end of the wait.
    $waitTick = {
        param([string]$PhaseLabel, [int]$PhaseSeconds)
        $elapsed = 0
        $reasonNow = $script:_FbPatientLastReason
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($reasonNow)) { $reasonNow = 'probing...' }
        while ($elapsed -lt $PhaseSeconds) {
            try {
                $reasonNow = [string](Get-FbRebootReasonLabel)
                if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($reasonNow)) { $reasonNow = 'no specific OS reboot signal detected' }
            } catch { $reasonNow = 'reason probe failed' }
            $script:_FbPatientLastReason = $reasonNow
            $msg = ("Waiting for shutdown - {0} [{1} elapsed {2}s / {3}s budget]" -f $reasonNow, $PhaseLabel, $elapsed, $PhaseSeconds)
            try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message $msg } catch {}
            try { Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Rebooting' } catch {}
            try { Write-FbLog $msg 'INFO' } catch {}
            Start-Sleep -Seconds 10
            $elapsed += 10
        }
    }
    $script:_FbPatientLastReason = $lastReason

    # 2026.05.13.2213-tier-watchdog-and-diag-parsefix: PRE-TIER-1
    # SANITY CHECK (Fix 7). Capture forensics on the OS state
    # BEFORE any shutdown is issued. The returned $preDiag.
    # LastBootUpTime is the baseline for the Tier 3-6 watchdog
    # delta-checks. The log line gives the operator (and the next
    # boot's diagnostic worker) a clear picture of WHY the
    # shutdown is being attempted and what services / pending
    # operations might block it.
    $preDiag = $null
    try {
        $preDiag = Get-FbPreShutdownDiagnostics
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: Get-FbPreShutdownDiagnostics threw: {0}; continuing without baseline." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    $baselineBootUp = $null
    if ($preDiag -and $preDiag.LastBootUpTime) {
        $baselineBootUp = $preDiag.LastBootUpTime
    }

    # Dell BWT3CB4 (2026-08-20): 42 updates applied, then shutdown.exe while TiWorker
    # was still hot. CBS set RebootInProgress; each new /r /f aborted the previous
    # shutdown. Wait for servicing (or an in-progress restart) before the 7-tier ladder.
    $script:FbSkipStopTrustedInstaller = $false
    $cbsWait = $null
    try {
        $cbsWait = Wait-FbCbsServicingIdle -MaxWaitSeconds 180
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: Wait-FbCbsServicingIdle threw: {0}; continuing to reboot ladder." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    if ($cbsWait -and [string]$cbsWait.State -eq 'RebootInProgress') {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: CBS RebootInProgress after {0}s wait - not issuing another shutdown.exe (would abort the in-progress restart). Watching for OS shutdown." -f $cbsWait.WaitedSec) 'WARN'
        try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'ApplyingUpdates' -Message 'Windows is finishing a restart. Please wait - do not power off.' } catch {}
        $ripDown = $false
        try {
            $ripDown = Wait-FbOsShutdownEvidence -TierNumber 1 -TimeoutSeconds 180 -BaselineBootUpTime $baselineBootUp -TierLabel 'cbs-reboot-in-progress-wait' -ShutdownGraceSeconds 90
        } catch {}
        if ($ripDown) {
            Write-FbLog 'Invoke-FbReboot: OS went down while CBS RebootInProgress; exiting reboot ladder.' 'INFO'
            return
        }
        Write-FbLog 'Invoke-FbReboot: CBS RebootInProgress wait did not take the box down; issuing one shutdown.exe then the ladder. Will not stop TrustedInstaller.' 'WARN'
        $script:FbSkipStopTrustedInstaller = $true
    } elseif ($cbsWait -and [string]$cbsWait.State -eq 'TimeoutBusy') {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: TiWorker/CBS still busy after {0}s; issuing one reboot. Will not stop TrustedInstaller." -f $cbsWait.WaitedSec) 'WARN'
        $script:FbSkipStopTrustedInstaller = $true
    } elseif ($cbsWait) {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: CBS/TiWorker idle after {0}s; proceeding with reboot." -f $cbsWait.WaitedSec) 'INFO'
    }

    # 2026.05.13.2213-tier-watchdog-and-diag-parsefix: 7-tier
    # reboot escalation. The dashboard footer carries
    # "RebootTier=N/7" while $script:UiRebootTier > 0. Tier
    # budgets reduced from pre-2213 (~7min Tier 1, ~90s each
    # for Tiers 2-3, ~60s for Tier 4) to (~3min Tier 1, 60s
    # each for Tiers 2-5, immediate-bugcheck Tier 6, panic
    # Tier 7) - because the pre-2213 wide budget gave the OS
    # time to wedge into the shutdown-lock state we then
    # couldn't break out of. Faster escalation through the
    # tiers means we hit the WMI-based and bugcheck-based
    # paths sooner, which DON'T share the wedged code path.
    #
    # 2026.05.13.2213f-reboot-ladder-optimization Bug C2:
    # capture the recovery flag into a local, reset the
    # script-level flag IMMEDIATELY (so a subsequent call
    # back through Invoke-FbReboot in this boot uses the
    # full cascade unless C2 detects another wedge), and
    # declare $tierState here (instead of inside the Tier
    # 1-3 wrap below) so it remains in scope for the Tier
    # 4-7 code path even when we skip Tier 1-3.
    $_skipToTier4 = [bool]$script:SkipToTier4OnNextReboot
    $script:SkipToTier4OnNextReboot = $false
    $tierState = @{ Current = 1; Max = 7; LastReason = $script:_FbPatientLastReason }
    if ($_skipToTier4) {
        Write-FbLog 'Invoke-FbReboot: 2213f Bug C recovery -- skipping Tier 1-3 (prior boot wedged at Tier 1-3 then escalated to Tier 4+ to actually reboot). Going straight to Tier 4 WMI Win32Shutdown.' 'WARN'
        try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message 'Reboot ladder: 2213f recovery jump to Tier 4 (Tier 1-3 known-wedged on prior boot).' } catch {}
    }
    $script:UiRebootTier = 1

    # 2026.05.13.2213f-reboot-ladder-optimization Bug C2: wrap Tier 1
    # (Phase A+B), Tier 2 and Tier 3 in a do{}while($false) block so
    # the recovery skip can `break` past all three at once and let
    # control fall through to Tier 4 below. The early `return`s
    # inside this block (one per tier on watchdog success) still
    # exit the function entirely - those mean the OS actually
    # rebooted, so we want to abandon the rest of the ladder.
    do {
        if ($_skipToTier4) { break }

        # Phase A: warning shutdown (/t 30 since 2214a; was 60 through 2213z).
        # fb-dump-final-1218: wall time dominated by CBS shutdown lock (Phase A
        # 10s ticks to 120s budget), not the countdown — shorter /t trims the
        # user-visible delay when shutdown is not blocked.
        # 2026.05.13.2213f-reboot-ladder-optimization Bug C1: tier-log marker.
        Write-FbRebootTierLog -Tier 1 -Action 'Tier1-shutdown.exe-/r-/f-/t-30' -Reason $Reason
        $phaseAMsg = ("Patient shutdown (Phase A / Tier 1) - issuing shutdown.exe /r /f /t 30 then waiting up to 2 minutes. Initial reason: {0}. Original reason: {1}" -f $lastReason, $Reason)
        Write-FbLog $phaseAMsg 'WARN'
        Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message ("Reboot tier=1/7 (Phase A): {0}" -f $lastReason)
        try { Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Rebooting' } catch {}
        try {
            $shArgsA = @('/r','/f','/t','30','/c',("FirstBase patient reboot: " + $Reason))
            Start-Process -FilePath $shutdownExe -ArgumentList $shArgsA -WindowStyle Hidden -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
            Write-FbLog 'Invoke-FbReboot: Phase A shutdown.exe /r /f /t 30 issued (2214a; was /t 60).' 'INFO'
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: Phase A shutdown.exe launch threw: {0}. Continuing into observation wait anyway." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }

        # 2-minute observable wait (120 seconds, 10s ticks). Was 300s
        # in 2200-2212; reduced because field evidence proved most
        # shutdowns either complete in &lt; 120s or never complete.
        & $waitTick 'Phase A' 120

        # Phase B / Tier 1B: force shutdown (/t 0), 1-minute observable wait
        # (was 2 min in 2200-2212). Services have had their warning;
        # no choice now.
        # 2026.05.13.2213f-reboot-ladder-optimization Bug C1: tier-log marker.
        Write-FbRebootTierLog -Tier 1 -Action 'Tier1B-shutdown.exe-/r-/f-/t-0' -Reason $Reason
        $phaseBMsg = ("Patient shutdown (Phase B) - escalating to shutdown.exe /r /f /t 0 then waiting up to 1 minute. Last reason from Phase A: {0}" -f $script:_FbPatientLastReason)
        Write-FbLog $phaseBMsg 'WARN'
        Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message ("Forcing second shutdown (Phase B) - last reason: {0}" -f $script:_FbPatientLastReason)
        try { Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Rebooting' } catch {}
        try {
            $shArgsB = @('/r','/f','/t','0','/c',("FirstBase patient reboot force: " + $Reason))
            Start-Process -FilePath $shutdownExe -ArgumentList $shArgsB -WindowStyle Hidden -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
            Write-FbLog 'Invoke-FbReboot: Phase B shutdown.exe /r /f /t 0 issued.' 'INFO'
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot: Phase B shutdown.exe launch threw: {0}. Continuing into final observation wait anyway." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }

        & $waitTick 'Phase B' 60

        # ==================================================================
        # 2026.05.13.2213-tier-watchdog-and-diag-parsefix: Tier 1 (Phase
        # A+B shutdown.exe) exhausted. Walk up the escalation ladder
        # Tier 2 -> Tier 3 -> Tier 4 -> Tier 5 -> Tier 6 -> Tier 7
        # (final dump + exit). EACH TIER HAS ITS OWN WATCHDOG via
        # Wait-FbOsShutdownEvidence which probes for OS shutdown-in-
        # progress signs every 5s and escalates IMMEDIATELY at the
        # tier deadline if none appear (instead of the pre-2213 flat
        # wait that gave the OS time to wedge). The activity bar
        # shows "Reboot tier=N/7 | Reason=<latest>".
        # ==================================================================

        $tierState.Current = 1
        $script:UiRebootTier = 1
        try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'reboot-tier' -Message ('Reboot tier=1/7 (shutdown.exe /r /f /t 30 then /t 0) exhausted; escalating to Tier 2 (stop blocking services).') } catch {}

        # ── Tier 2: stop services that hold the shutdown lock ──────────────
        # 2026.05.13.2213f-reboot-ladder-optimization Bug A1+D: SKIP this
        # tier entirely if all 4 of wuauserv/bits/cryptsvc/trustedinstaller
        # are already Stopped per the Pre-Tier-1 sanity check. Stopping
        # already-stopped services is a 60-90s no-op; if even ONE service
        # is still Running (e.g. cryptsvc on the dump's test device) we
        # proceed with the full Tier 2 flow because that running service
        # might be the very thing holding the shutdown lock. The 'setup'
        # service's existing "not present; skipping" behavior is preserved
        # in the foreach below; it is intentionally NOT in the 4-service
        # skip predicate (it is rarely present and is not load-bearing).
        $tier2SkipBecauseAllStopped = $false
        try {
            if ($preDiag -and $preDiag.Services) {
                $svcMap = @{}
                foreach ($svcEntry in @($preDiag.Services)) {
                    if ($svcEntry -and $svcEntry.Name) {
                        $svcMap[[string]$svcEntry.Name] = [string]$svcEntry.Status
                    }
                }
                $checkSet = @('wuauserv','bits','cryptsvc','trustedinstaller')
                $stoppedCount = 0
                $missingCount = 0
                foreach ($name in $checkSet) {
                    if ($svcMap.ContainsKey($name)) {
                        if ($svcMap[$name] -eq 'Stopped') { $stoppedCount++ }
                    } else {
                        $missingCount++
                    }
                }
                if ($stoppedCount -eq $checkSet.Count) { $tier2SkipBecauseAllStopped = $true }
            }
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot Tier 2: skip-predicate probe threw: {0}; defaulting to full Tier 2 flow." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
            $tier2SkipBecauseAllStopped = $false
        }
        if ($tier2SkipBecauseAllStopped) {
            Write-FbLog 'Tier 2 SKIP: all 4 service holds are already Stopped (wuauserv/bits/cryptsvc/trustedinstaller); going straight to Tier 3 to avoid 60s no-op service-stop loop' 'WARN'
            try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'reboot-tier' -Message 'Reboot tier=2/7 SKIPPED (all 4 service holds already Stopped); escalating directly to Tier 3.' } catch {
                try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message 'Reboot tier=2/7 SKIPPED (no-op); escalating directly to Tier 3.' } catch {}
            }
        } else {
            $tierState.Current = 2
            $script:UiRebootTier = 2
            # 2026.05.13.2213f-reboot-ladder-optimization Bug C1: tier-log marker.
            Write-FbRebootTierLog -Tier 2 -Action 'Tier2-Stop-Service+shutdown.exe' -Reason $Reason
            Write-FbLog 'Invoke-FbReboot Tier 2: stopping services that hold the shutdown lock (wuauserv, bits, cryptsvc, trustedinstaller, setup) then retrying shutdown.exe /r /f /t 0.' 'WARN'
            try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message 'Reboot tier=2/7 - stopping services holding shutdown lock (wuauserv, bits, cryptsvc, trustedinstaller, setup)...' } catch {}
            foreach ($svc in @('wuauserv','bits','cryptsvc','trustedinstaller','setup')) {
                try {
                    if ($script:FbSkipStopTrustedInstaller -and $svc -eq 'trustedinstaller') {
                        Write-FbLog 'Tier 2: skipping Stop-Service TrustedInstaller (CBS/TiWorker still applying packages).' 'WARN'
                        continue
                    }
                    $sObj = Get-Service -Name $svc -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                    if (-not $sObj) {
                        Write-FbLog ("Tier 2: service '{0}' not present; skipping." -f $svc) 'INFO'
                        continue
                    }
                    Write-FbLog ("Tier 2: Stop-Service -Force '{0}' (current Status={1})..." -f $svc, $sObj.Status) 'INFO'
                    Stop-Service -Name $svc -Force -NoWait -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                    Start-Sleep -Seconds 2
                } catch {
                    Write-FbLog ("Tier 2: Stop-Service '{0}' threw: {1}" -f $svc, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
                }
            }
            try {
                $shArgsT2 = @('/r','/f','/t','0','/c',("FirstBase Tier 2 force: " + $Reason))
                Start-Process -FilePath $shutdownExe -ArgumentList $shArgsT2 -WindowStyle Hidden -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
                Write-FbLog 'Invoke-FbReboot Tier 2: shutdown.exe /r /f /t 0 issued after service-stop.' 'INFO'
            } catch {
                Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot Tier 2: shutdown.exe launch threw: {0}." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
            }
            # 2213 (post-verifier refinement): 10s watchdog with 30s
            # shutdown-grace. Returns $true only on LastBootUpTime delta
            # (OS rebooted but we're somehow still running) - in that case
            # do NOT escalate; the box is going down. Returns $false on
            # either no-evidence-at-deadline OR shutdown-wedge-confirmed
            # (grace expired with no actual reboot) - in both cases we
            # escalate to the next tier so a different code path can fire.
            # 2258: tightened from 60s to 10s.
            $tier2Ok = Wait-FbOsShutdownEvidence -TierNumber 2 -TimeoutSeconds 10 -BaselineBootUpTime $baselineBootUp -TierLabel 'service-stop+shutdown'
            if ($tier2Ok) { Write-FbLog 'Invoke-FbReboot Tier 2 watchdog confirms OS rebooted; exiting reboot ladder.' 'INFO'; return }
        }

        # ── Tier 3: Restart-Computer -Force (PowerShell cmdlet path) ────────
        # 2213 Critical Fix 1: this is the tier that wedged THREE TIMES
        # on the test device. The pre-2213 code issued Restart-Computer
        # then trusted ~90s of waitTick. If Restart-Computer wedges, the
        # loop process is killed by session teardown ~2-3s later and
        # Tiers 4-5 (then ExitWindowsEx + dump) NEVER FIRE. New behavior:
        # write a "Tier 3 about to fire" marker (in the dashboard
        # latch + log) so a successor process can confirm what happened,
        # then run Wait-FbOsShutdownEvidence which escalates IMMEDIATELY
        # (not after 90s) if the OS doesn't show shutdown-in-progress
        # signs.
        # 2026.05.13.2213f-reboot-ladder-optimization Bug A2: watchdog
        # window reduced from 60s -> 15s after dump evidence
        # (F:\fb-dump-final-0635) showed 100% wedge rate on test
        # hardware. The 60s budget was pure latency before escalating
        # to Tier 4 (which is what actually works).
        $tierState.Current = 3
        $script:UiRebootTier = 3
        # 2026.05.13.2213f-reboot-ladder-optimization Bug C1: tier-log marker.
        Write-FbRebootTierLog -Tier 3 -Action 'Tier3-Restart-Computer-Force' -Reason $Reason
        Write-FbLog 'Invoke-FbReboot Tier 3: about to issue Restart-Computer -Force (PowerShell cmdlet, different code path than shutdown.exe). Marker written to dashboard latch so a post-mortem can confirm we got this far.' 'WARN'
        try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'reboot-tier' -Message 'Reboot tier=3/7 - Restart-Computer -Force (alternate code path). Watchdog 15s (2213f reduced from 60s).' } catch {
            try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message 'Reboot tier=3/7 - Restart-Computer -Force (watchdog 15s)...' } catch {}
        }
        try {
            Restart-Computer -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            Write-FbLog 'Invoke-FbReboot Tier 3: Restart-Computer -Force returned (process should be terminating shortly OR has wedged - 15s watchdog will tell).' 'INFO'
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot Tier 3: Restart-Computer threw: {0}." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
        # 2026.05.13.2213f-reboot-ladder-optimization Bug A2: TimeoutSeconds=15 (was 60).
        $tier3Ok = Wait-FbOsShutdownEvidence -TierNumber 3 -TimeoutSeconds 15 -BaselineBootUpTime $baselineBootUp -TierLabel 'Restart-Computer-Force (2213f Bug A2: 15s watchdog reduced from 60s after dump evidence showed 100% wedge rate on test hardware)'
        if ($tier3Ok) { Write-FbLog 'Invoke-FbReboot Tier 3 watchdog confirms OS rebooted; exiting reboot ladder.' 'INFO'; return }
    } while ($false)

    # ── Tier 4: NEW - WMI Win32_OperatingSystem.Win32Shutdown(Flags=6) ─
    # 2213 Critical Fix 2: completely different code path than
    # shutdown.exe / Restart-Computer. WMI dispatches via winmgmt
    # to LSA shutdown subsystem. If a stale shutdown lock in
    # user-mode wedged the prior tiers, WMI may bypass it.
    $tierState.Current = 4
    $script:UiRebootTier = 4
    # 2026.05.13.2213f-reboot-ladder-optimization Bug C1: tier-log marker.
    Write-FbRebootTierLog -Tier 4 -Action 'Tier4-WMI-Win32Shutdown' -Reason $Reason
    Write-FbLog 'Invoke-FbReboot Tier 4: WMI Win32_OperatingSystem.Win32Shutdown(Flags=6) - completely different code path than shutdown.exe / Restart-Computer (goes through winmgmt -> LSA).' 'WARN'
    try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'reboot-tier' -Message 'Reboot tier=4/7 - WMI Win32Shutdown(Flags=6) (alternate IPC path). Watchdog 10s (2258: reduced from 60s).' } catch {
        try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message 'Reboot tier=4/7 - WMI Win32Shutdown (watchdog 10s)...' } catch {}
    }
    try {
        $wmiOk = Invoke-FbWmiWin32ShutdownReboot -Reason $Reason
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot Tier 4: WMI Win32Shutdown returned ok={0}." -f $wmiOk) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot Tier 4: Invoke-FbWmiWin32ShutdownReboot threw: {0}." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    $tier4Ok = Wait-FbOsShutdownEvidence -TierNumber 4 -TimeoutSeconds 10 -BaselineBootUpTime $baselineBootUp -TierLabel 'WMI-Win32Shutdown'
    if ($tier4Ok) { Write-FbLog 'Invoke-FbReboot Tier 4 watchdog confirms OS rebooted; exiting reboot ladder.' 'INFO'; return }

    # ── Tier 5: P/Invoke ExitWindowsEx with EWX_FORCEIFHUNG ────────────
    # Was Tier 4 in 2200-2212. Same implementation; renumbered to
    # make room for the new WMI tier. Now wrapped in a watchdog so
    # we don't burn 60s waiting if the call wedged.
    $tierState.Current = 5
    $script:UiRebootTier = 5
    # 2026.05.13.2213f-reboot-ladder-optimization Bug C1: tier-log marker.
    Write-FbRebootTierLog -Tier 5 -Action 'Tier5-PInvoke-ExitWindowsEx' -Reason $Reason
    Write-FbLog 'Invoke-FbReboot Tier 5: P/Invoke ExitWindowsEx(EWX_REBOOT|EWX_FORCE|EWX_FORCEIFHUNG) after enabling SeShutdownPrivilege on this process token.' 'WARN'
    try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'reboot-tier' -Message 'Reboot tier=5/7 - P/Invoke ExitWindowsEx with FORCEIFHUNG. Watchdog 10s (2258: reduced from 60s).' } catch {
        try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message 'Reboot tier=5/7 - native ExitWindowsEx with FORCEIFHUNG (watchdog 10s)...' } catch {}
    }
    try {
        Invoke-FbWin32ExitWindowsExForceReboot -Reason $Reason
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot Tier 5: ExitWindowsEx P/Invoke threw: {0}." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    $tier5Ok = Wait-FbOsShutdownEvidence -TierNumber 5 -TimeoutSeconds 10 -BaselineBootUpTime $baselineBootUp -TierLabel 'ExitWindowsEx-FORCEIFHUNG'
    if ($tier5Ok) { Write-FbLog 'Invoke-FbReboot Tier 5 watchdog confirms OS rebooted; exiting reboot ladder.' 'INFO'; return }

    # ── Tier 6: NEW - NtRaiseHardError forced bugcheck ─────────────────
    # 2213 Critical Fix 3: nuclear option. Kernel forces a bugcheck
    # (BSOD with OPTION_SHUTDOWN_SYSTEM) which triggers a hard
    # reboot via the kernel's emergency-shutdown path. Bypasses
    # ALL user-mode shutdown machinery. Acceptable for audit-mode
    # because: nothing of value to lose pre-OOBE; the resulting
    # MEMORY.DMP is forensic evidence; without this the box sits
    # hung for hours. NEVER returns successfully (the bugcheck
    # tears down the kernel) - if we get past this call without
    # the box rebooting, NtRaiseHardError itself is broken and
    # we fall through to Tier 7 (panic dump + exit).
    $tierState.Current = 6
    $script:UiRebootTier = 6
    # 2026.05.13.2213f-reboot-ladder-optimization Bug C1: tier-log marker.
    Write-FbRebootTierLog -Tier 6 -Action 'Tier6-NtRaiseHardError-Bugcheck' -Reason $Reason
    Write-FbLog 'Invoke-FbReboot Tier 6: NtRaiseHardError forced bugcheck (NUCLEAR OPTION - kernel-level bugcheck triggers hard reboot). Will leave a MEMORY.DMP on the next boot for forensics. Acceptable for audit-mode.' 'ERROR'
    try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'reboot-tier' -Message 'Reboot tier=6/7 - NtRaiseHardError forced bugcheck (last resort before manual intervention). Watchdog 10s (2258: reduced from 60s).' } catch {
        try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message 'Reboot tier=6/7 - forced kernel bugcheck (BSOD-and-reboot). Last automated tier...' } catch {}
    }
    try {
        $bugOk = Invoke-FbNtRaiseHardErrorReboot -Reason $Reason
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot Tier 6: NtRaiseHardError returned ok={0}. Box should bugcheck within seconds." -f $bugOk) 'ERROR'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot Tier 6: Invoke-FbNtRaiseHardErrorReboot threw: {0}." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
    }
    $tier6Ok = Wait-FbOsShutdownEvidence -TierNumber 6 -TimeoutSeconds 10 -BaselineBootUpTime $baselineBootUp -TierLabel 'NtRaiseHardError-bugcheck'
    if ($tier6Ok) { Write-FbLog 'Invoke-FbReboot Tier 6 watchdog confirms OS rebooted; exiting reboot ladder.' 'INFO'; return }

    # ── Tier 7: final dump + re-arm + exit (no further escalations) ────
    # Was Tier 5 in 2200-2212; renumbered to make room for the new
    # WMI (Tier 4) and bugcheck (Tier 6) tiers. Behavior unchanged:
    # write the panic JSON, bump BootsSinceLastInstall so the next
    # boot's escape valve trips, re-arm all four trigger layers,
    # exit 1 with a manual-restart UI hint.
    $tierState.Current = 7
    $script:UiRebootTier = 7
    # 2026.05.13.2213f-reboot-ladder-optimization Bug C1: tier-log marker.
    Write-FbRebootTierLog -Tier 7 -Action 'Tier7-Panic-Dump' -Reason $Reason
    Write-FbLog 'Invoke-FbReboot Tier 7: every reboot path INCLUDING NtRaiseHardError bugcheck has refused. Writing STUCK-DIAG-SHUTDOWN-LOCKED and exiting so the dashboard can show "press Restart manually to continue" instead of spinning silently.' 'ERROR'

    # 2026.05.13.0900-trigger-resilience Fix 5 (re-tagged in 2213
    # for renumbering Tier 5 -> Tier 7): BEFORE exiting, audit
    # and re-arm all four trigger layers. The failed shutdown.exe
    # sequence may have begun the user-session logoff and consumed
    # the RunOnce entry without ever taking the box down. Re-arming
    # here guarantees that the operator's manual power-cycle has a
    # live trigger waiting on the next boot, no matter which layers
    # were eaten by the half-logoff.
    try {
        $auditMsg = Test-FbAndRepairTriggers -Context 'post-tier7-shutdown-locked'
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot Tier 7: trigger audit -> " + $auditMsg) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot Tier 7: trigger audit threw: {0}." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress (re-tagged in
    # 2213 for tier renumbering): bump BootsSinceLastInstall on
    # Tier 7 exhaustion - this boot produced no progress because
    # we couldn't even reboot. The next boot's escape-valve probe
    # will see this counter advance and trip at >=3 (per the user
    # spec).
    try {
        $diskNow = Load-State
        $newBoots = ([int]$diskNow.BootsSinceLastInstall) + 1
        Save-State -EmptySweepCount ([int]$diskNow.EmptySweepCount) `
            -LastPass 'tier7-shutdown-locked' `
            -StartTime ([string]$diskNow.StartTime) `
            -FailureCount ([int]$diskNow.FailureCount) `
            -BootsSinceLastInstall $newBoots
    } catch {}

    try { Write-FbShutdownLockedStuckDiag -Reason $Reason -LastReason $script:_FbPatientLastReason } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot Tier 7: Write-FbShutdownLockedStuckDiag threw: {0}." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    $finalReason = $script:_FbPatientLastReason
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($finalReason)) { $finalReason = 'no specific OS reboot signal detected' }
    $exitMsg = ("Reboot tier 7/7 reached. Every reboot path (shutdown.exe Phase A+B, service-stop+shutdown, Restart-Computer, WMI Win32Shutdown, ExitWindowsEx EWX_FORCEIFHUNG, NtRaiseHardError bugcheck) refused to take the box down. Last reason: {0}. STUCK-DIAG-SHUTDOWN-LOCKED-*.json written. All trigger layers re-armed. Press Restart manually to continue updating." -f $finalReason)
    Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbReboot Tier 7: " + $exitMsg) 'ERROR'
    $manualMsg = 'Windows is applying updates and could not restart. Use Restart, or hold the power button to power-cycle, then wait — do not keep tapping shutdown.'
    try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'ManualRestart' -Message ($manualMsg + ' | Reboot tier=7/7 | ' + $finalReason) } catch {}
    try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'reboot-tier' -Message ('Reboot tier=7/7 EXHAUSTED. ' + $exitMsg) } catch {}
    try { Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'ManualRestart' } catch {}

    # 2026.05.13.2213c-onlogon-primary-and-defender-disable: bulletproof
    # Defender restore on the Tier 7 panic-exit path. The user must
    # manually power-cycle from here; if we leave the device with
    # Defender-off and they reboot into OOBE, the unit ships unsafe.
    Set-FbDefenderRealtime -Enabled $true -Reason 'tier7-panic'

    Write-FbLog 'Invoke-FbReboot Tier 7: issuing shutdown.exe /r /t 0 /f (do not sit on orange Rebooting). Operator Restart still works if this is ignored.' 'ERROR'
    Invoke-FbForceOsRestart -Reason 'tier7-last-path'
    try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
    Start-Sleep -Seconds 20
    exit 1
}

function Invoke-FbWin32ExitWindowsExForceReboot {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Tier 5 reboot escalation (was Tier 4 in 2200-2212;
        renumbered in 2213 for the new WMI tier inserted at
        Tier 4): Win32 ExitWindowsEx with EWX_REBOOT |
        EWX_FORCE | EWX_FORCEIFHUNG after enabling
        SeShutdownPrivilege on this process token.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress (renumbered
        only in 2213; behavior unchanged). The current process
        needs SeShutdownPrivilege enabled in its access token
        before ExitWindowsEx will work. We Add-Type the necessary
        P/Invoke surface (advapi32 LookupPrivilegeValue +
        AdjustTokenPrivileges + OpenProcessToken; user32
        ExitWindowsEx) and call it.

        Never throws. Best-effort - if the P/Invoke layer fails
        for any reason (Add-Type already loaded with conflict,
        token adjust refused, ExitWindowsEx returned 0) we log
        and let the caller continue to Tier 6 (NtRaiseHardError
        bugcheck) and then Tier 7 (panic dump + exit).

        2213 also added two sibling P/Invoke / WMI surfaces:
        Invoke-FbWmiWin32ShutdownReboot (Tier 4, WMI Win32_
        OperatingSystem.Win32Shutdown) and Invoke-FbNtRaise
        HardErrorReboot (Tier 6, NtRaiseHardError forced
        bugcheck) - both defined immediately above this function.
        This function remains the SAME P/Invoke surface that
        was introduced in 2200; only the tier label changed.
    #>
    param([string]$Reason = 'FirstBase Tier 4 force reboot')

    $sig = @'
using System;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;

public static class FbReboot {
    [StructLayout(LayoutKind.Sequential, Pack = 4)]
    public struct LUID {
        public uint LowPart;
        public int  HighPart;
    }
    [StructLayout(LayoutKind.Sequential, Pack = 4)]
    public struct LUID_AND_ATTRIBUTES {
        public LUID Luid;
        public uint Attributes;
    }
    [StructLayout(LayoutKind.Sequential, Pack = 4)]
    public struct TOKEN_PRIVILEGES {
        public uint PrivilegeCount;
        public LUID Luid;
        public uint Attributes;
    }

    public const uint TOKEN_QUERY              = 0x0008;
    public const uint TOKEN_ADJUST_PRIVILEGES  = 0x0020;
    public const uint SE_PRIVILEGE_ENABLED     = 0x00000002;

    public const uint EWX_LOGOFF        = 0x00000000;
    public const uint EWX_SHUTDOWN      = 0x00000001;
    public const uint EWX_REBOOT        = 0x00000002;
    public const uint EWX_FORCE         = 0x00000004;
    public const uint EWX_POWEROFF      = 0x00000008;
    public const uint EWX_FORCEIFHUNG   = 0x00000010;

    public const uint SHTDN_REASON_MAJOR_OPERATINGSYSTEM = 0x00020000;
    public const uint SHTDN_REASON_MINOR_RECONFIG        = 0x00000004;
    public const uint SHTDN_REASON_FLAG_PLANNED          = 0x80000000;

    [DllImport("advapi32.dll", SetLastError = true)]
    public static extern bool OpenProcessToken(IntPtr ProcessHandle, uint DesiredAccess, out IntPtr TokenHandle);

    [DllImport("advapi32.dll", SetLastError = true, CharSet = CharSet.Auto)]
    public static extern bool LookupPrivilegeValue(string lpSystemName, string lpName, out LUID lpLuid);

    [DllImport("advapi32.dll", SetLastError = true)]
    public static extern bool AdjustTokenPrivileges(IntPtr TokenHandle, bool DisableAllPrivileges, ref TOKEN_PRIVILEGES NewState, uint BufferLength, IntPtr PreviousState, IntPtr ReturnLength);

    [DllImport("user32.dll", SetLastError = true)]
    public static extern bool ExitWindowsEx(uint uFlags, uint dwReason);

    [DllImport("kernel32.dll")]
    public static extern IntPtr GetCurrentProcess();

    public static bool EnableShutdownPrivilege() {
        IntPtr proc = GetCurrentProcess();
        IntPtr tok;
        if (!OpenProcessToken(proc, TOKEN_ADJUST_PRIVILEGES | TOKEN_QUERY, out tok)) return false;
        LUID luid;
        if (!LookupPrivilegeValue(null, "SeShutdownPrivilege", out luid)) return false;
        TOKEN_PRIVILEGES tp;
        tp.PrivilegeCount = 1;
        tp.Luid = luid;
        tp.Attributes = SE_PRIVILEGE_ENABLED;
        if (!AdjustTokenPrivileges(tok, false, ref tp, 0, IntPtr.Zero, IntPtr.Zero)) return false;
        return Marshal.GetLastWin32Error() == 0;
    }

    public static bool ForceRebootIfHung() {
        if (!EnableShutdownPrivilege()) {
            return false;
        }
        return ExitWindowsEx(EWX_REBOOT | EWX_FORCE | EWX_FORCEIFHUNG,
                             SHTDN_REASON_MAJOR_OPERATINGSYSTEM | SHTDN_REASON_MINOR_RECONFIG | SHTDN_REASON_FLAG_PLANNED);
        }
}
'@

    try {
        if (-not ([System.Management.Automation.PSTypeName]'FbReboot').Type) {
            Add-Type -TypeDefinition $sig -Language CSharp -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null
            Write-FbLog 'Tier 4 ExitWindowsEx: Add-Type FbReboot loaded.' 'INFO'
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Tier 4 ExitWindowsEx: Add-Type failed: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        return
    }

    try {
        $ok = [FbReboot]::ForceRebootIfHung()
        $err = [System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal]::GetLastWin32Error()
        Write-FbLog ("Tier 4 ExitWindowsEx: returned {0} (LastError={1}). Reason='{2}'" -f $ok, $err, $Reason) 'WARN'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Tier 4 ExitWindowsEx: invocation threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

function Write-FbShutdownLockedStuckDiag {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Tier 7 panic dump (was Tier 5 in 2200-2212; renumbered
        in 2213): STUCK-DIAG-SHUTDOWN-LOCKED-*.json with full
        session/services/processes/registry snapshot so the
        operator (and a future build) can see exactly why the
        OS refused every reboot path.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress (parse-
        fixed in 2213-tier-watchdog-and-diag-parsefix Critical
        Fix 5: hoisted (try/catch)-as-expression OUT of the
        [pscustomobject]@{} initializer so the function actually
        executes at runtime instead of throwing "The term 'try'
        is not recognized as cmdlet"). Never throws.
    #>
    param(
        [string]$Reason = '',
        [string]$LastReason = ''
    )

    $stamp = (Get-Date).ToString('yyyyMMdd-HHmmss')
    $panicDir = Join-Path $LogDir 'PanicDumps'
    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $panicDir)) {
            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $panicDir -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
        }
    } catch {}
    $diagPath = Join-Path $panicDir ("STUCK-DIAG-SHUTDOWN-LOCKED-{0}.json" -f $stamp)

    $querySession = ''
    try {
        $qs = & query.exe session 2>&1 | Out-String
        $querySession = $qs
    } catch {}

    $services = @()
    try {
        $services = @(Get-Service -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |
            Where-Object { $_.Status -ne 'Stopped' } |
            Select-Object Name, DisplayName, Status, StartType)
    } catch {}

    # 2026.05.13.2213-tier-watchdog-and-diag-parsefix Critical Fix 5:
    # CPUSec calculated property had `try { [math]::Round($_.CPU,1) }
    # catch { 0 }` inside the E={ } scriptblock. That one is actually
    # legal because E={...} is itself a scriptblock, but for
    # consistency with the other panic-dump fix below we hoist all
    # access-prone fields out of the literal.
    $procs = @()
    try {
        $procs = @(Get-Process -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |
            Sort-Object -Property WorkingSet64 -Descending |
            Select-Object -First 50 |
            Select-Object Id, ProcessName, @{N='WS_MB';E={[math]::Round($_.WorkingSet64/1MB,1)}}, @{N='CPUSec';E={
                $cpuSec = 0
                try { $cpuSec = [math]::Round($_.CPU,1) } catch {}
                $cpuSec
            }})
    } catch {}

    # 2026.05.13.2213-tier-watchdog-and-diag-parsefix Critical Fix 5:
    # same statement-as-expression footgun as Write-FbCbsStuckDiag.
    # PowerShell 5.1's parser tokenizes `try` as a STATEMENT keyword,
    # not an expression. `(try { } catch { })` inside a hashtable
    # value or [pscustomobject] initializer parses with 0 errors but
    # at runtime the tokenizer treats the keyword as a command name
    # and throws "The term 'try' is not recognized as cmdlet" - which
    # is exactly the failure that prevented Tier 5 panic dumps from
    # being written in 2200-2212. Hoist all such reads OUT of the
    # @{} initializer into pre-declared $vars.
    $cbsKeys = @{}
    foreach ($k in @(
        'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Component Based Servicing\RebootPending',
        'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Component Based Servicing\PackagesPending',
        'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Component Based Servicing\RebootInProgress',
        'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\WindowsUpdate\Auto Update\RebootRequired',
        'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\WindowsUpdate\Services\Pending'
    )) {
        try {
            $existsK = Test-Path -LiteralPath $k
            $valuesStrK = ''
            if ($existsK) {
                try {
                    $valuesStrK = (Get-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $k -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-String)
                } catch {
                    $valuesStrK = 'read-error'
                }
            }
            $cbsKeys[$k] = @{
                Exists = $existsK
                Values = $valuesStrK
            }
        } catch {}
    }

    $diskNow = $null
    try { $diskNow = Load-State } catch {}

    # Pre-evaluate Test-FbWuRebootRequired into a plain variable so
    # it can be embedded in the [pscustomobject] literal below
    # without the try/catch-as-expression footgun documented above.
    $rebootRequiredNow = $true
    try {
        $rebootRequiredNow = [bool](Test-FbWuRebootRequired)
    } catch {
        $rebootRequiredNow = $true
    }

    $diag = [pscustomobject]@{
        Timestamp                 = (Get-Date).ToString('o')
        ScriptVersion             = $ScriptVersion
        Reason                    = $Reason
        LastPatientReason         = $LastReason
        TestFbWuRebootRequiredNow = $rebootRequiredNow
        QuerySession              = $querySession
        RunningServices           = $services
        TopProcesses              = $procs
        CbsRegistryKeys           = $cbsKeys
        StateSnapshot             = $diskNow
    }
    try {
        $diag | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 6 | Set-Content -Path $diagPath -Encoding UTF8 -Force
        Write-FbLog ("Write-FbShutdownLockedStuckDiag: wrote {0}" -f $diagPath) 'ERROR'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Write-FbShutdownLockedStuckDiag: write failed: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

function Invoke-FbRebootWithFreshStateCheck {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Backward-compatible alias for Invoke-FbReboot.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.12.1030-no-cascade-final. The 12+ main-loop reboot
        call sites all invoke Invoke-FbRebootWithFreshStateCheck.
        This wrapper preserves that public name so the call sites
        don't churn. The implementation now delegates to the new
        Invoke-FbReboot, which does the same fresh-disk read plus
        the additional Test-FbWuRebootRequired probe and routes to
        OOBE handoff in BOTH cases (no cascade machinery to delegate
        to anymore).

        Never returns. Either Invoke-FbOobeHandoff exits 0,
        shutdown.exe takes the box down, or Invoke-FbReboot exit 1's.
    #>
    param([string]$Reason)
    Invoke-FbReboot -Reason $Reason
}

$script:UiPhase = 'Startup'
$script:UiMessage = 'Initializing update service'
$script:UiUpdates = @{}
# Mirror of the cumulative reboot counter the main loop maintains.
# Written by main-loop code via Update-UiFooterContext so that
# Save-UiState can stamp "ScriptVersion=... Boot=N" onto every
# message field the WPF splash renders, giving the user a visible
# proof-of-build without log access. Initialized to 0 here so that
# the very first Save-UiState before $bootCount is loaded does not
# null-deref.
$script:UiBootCount = 0
# 2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve: in-memory mirror of the escape-valve
# state read off wu-state.json. Save-UiState stamps these onto the
# rendered activity-bar message so the operator can see the
# futile-boot counter (FutileBootCount=N/3) and the *** ESCAPE
# VALVE TRIPPED *** banner without log access. Updated via
# Update-UiEscapeValveContext from disk-read paths (startup gate,
# Test-FbEscapeValve, Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff) so the values
# stamped on every status write are fresh without forcing a
# per-write disk read inside Save-UiState (which the patient-
# reboot loop calls every 10s).
$script:UiFutileBootCount     = 0
$script:UiFutileBootThreshold = [int]$FutileBootThreshold
$script:UiEscapeValveTripped  = $false
$script:UiEscapeValveReason   = ''
# 2026.05.12.1030-no-cascade-final: $UiCascadeStage / $UiCascadeTotal
# and the "CascadeStage=K/8" footer that used to stamp Rebooting-phase
# messages have been REMOVED. There is no longer a multi-stage cascade
# to display progress for, so the footer is unconditionally
# "[ScriptVersion=... Boot=N]" regardless of phase. The variables are
# preserved here as $null so any stale reference parses cleanly without
# rendering a stage in the UI; Set-UiCascadeStage is a no-op shim.
$script:UiCascadeStage = $null
$script:UiCascadeTotal = $null
# 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: reboot escalation tier
# (0 = not escalating, 1..5 = active tier). Save-UiState renders this
# onto the activity bar footer when > 0 so the operator can see the
# tier escalation in real-time on the splash. Mutated by Invoke-FbReboot
# inside the patient-reboot path; cleared on subsequent successful boot
# (the script restart implicitly resets this back to 0 because it is a
# script-scoped variable not persisted to disk).
$script:UiRebootTier  = 0
# 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: multi-pass loop UI
# context. Stamped onto the activity-bar footer by Save-UiState when
# > 0 so the operator can see the current pass / bucket / pending /
# stage without log access. Updated by the main multi-pass loop and
# the per-pass-end decision block.
$script:UiPassIndex            = 0
$script:UiCurrentBucket        = ''
$script:UiPendingUpdates       = 0
$script:UiBootsSinceLastInst   = 0
$script:UiBootsSinceLastInstThreshold = 3
$script:UiSysprepStage         = 'N/A'  # 'N/A' | '1of2' | '2of2'

function Update-UiFooterContext {
    param([int]$BootCount = 0)
    $script:UiBootCount = $BootCount
}

function Update-UiEscapeValveContext {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Refresh the in-memory mirror of the escape-valve dashboard
        indicators from wu-state.json.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve. Save-UiState is called on
        every Set-UiPhase and every Set-UiUpdateStatus - including
        the patient-reboot 10s tick loop. Reading wu-state.json on
        each of those calls would be wasteful and would also race
        Save-State writes from other code paths. Instead, callers
        that mutate the disk state (Test-FbEscapeValve increments
        FutileBootCount; Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff sets the
        latch; startup probe at module init) call this helper to
        refresh the in-memory mirror; Save-UiState then renders
        those values onto the activity-bar message.

        Optional explicit parameters override the disk read so a
        caller that has the values in scope (e.g. Test-FbEscapeValve
        right after it computes them) does not need a redundant
        Load-State round trip.

        Never throws. Best-effort on the Load-State.
    #>
    param(
        [Nullable[int]]$FutileBootCount = $null,
        [Nullable[int]]$Threshold       = $null,
        [Nullable[bool]]$EscapeValveTripped = $null,
        [string]$EscapeValveReason = $null
    )
    if ($null -ne $FutileBootCount) {
        $script:UiFutileBootCount = [int]$FutileBootCount
    } else {
        try {
            $disk = Load-State
            $script:UiFutileBootCount = [int]$disk.FutileBootCount
            if ($null -eq $EscapeValveTripped) {
                $script:UiEscapeValveTripped = [bool]$disk.EscapeValveTripped
            }
            if ($null -eq $EscapeValveReason -or [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($EscapeValveReason)) {
                $script:UiEscapeValveReason = [string]$disk.EscapeValveReason
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    if ($null -ne $Threshold) {
        $script:UiFutileBootThreshold = [int]$Threshold
    } else {
        try {
            $thresholdInfo = Get-FbFutileBootThreshold
            $script:UiFutileBootThreshold = [int]$thresholdInfo.Threshold
        } catch {}
    }
    if ($null -ne $EscapeValveTripped) {
        $script:UiEscapeValveTripped = [bool]$EscapeValveTripped
    }
    if ($null -ne $EscapeValveReason) {
        $script:UiEscapeValveReason = [string]$EscapeValveReason
    }
}

function Set-UiCascadeStage {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        REMOVED 2026.05.12.1030-no-cascade-final. No-op shim retained
        so any legacy caller still parses.
    #>
    param([int]$Stage = 0)
    # Intentionally empty: there is no cascade stage anymore.
}

function Write-FbWuStatusSafe {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2026.05.18.2213l Bug GG: retry-with-jitter wrapper around the
        wu-status.json / wu-progress.json writers. Eliminates the
        "used by another process" IOException race observed on boot 3
        line 95 of the 2213k field dump when the parent loop and the
        progress poller's separate powershell.exe child both touched
        the same file within the same Windows tick.
    .DESCRIPTION
        Uses [System.IO.File]::WriteAllText (UTF8) instead of
        Set-Content / Out-File so we get a clean managed-handle path
        with deterministic dispose. On IOException we sleep a small
        random interval (50-200ms) and retry up to $MaxAttempts times
        before re-throwing. Non-IO exceptions are NOT caught - those
        are programmer errors that should surface immediately.

        Drop-in replacement for the prior 'try { ... | Set-Content
        -Path $StatusFile -Encoding UTF8 -Force } catch {}' pattern,
        with the added benefit that callers do not need to wrap the
        invocation in their own try/catch (the helper handles
        IOException internally; only catastrophic non-IO failures
        propagate). Returns $true on success.
    #>
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$Path,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$Json,
        [int]$MaxAttempts = 5
    )
    for ($attempt = 1; $attempt -le $MaxAttempts; $attempt++) {
        try {
            [System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($Path, $Json, [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8)
            return $true
        } catch [System.IO.IOException] {
            if ($attempt -eq $MaxAttempts) {
                try { Write-FbLog ("Write-FbWuStatusSafe: IOException on attempt {0}/{1} for {2}: {3}. Giving up." -f $attempt, $MaxAttempts, $Path, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN' } catch {}
                throw
            }
            Start-Sleep -Milliseconds (Get-Random -Minimum 50 -Maximum 200)
        }
    }
    return $false
}

function Save-UiState {
    $items = @($script:UiUpdates.GetEnumerator() | ForEach-Object { $_.Value } | Sort-Object UpdatedAt -Descending)
    if ($items.Count -gt $MaxStatusItems) {
        $items = @($items | Select-Object -First $MaxStatusItems)
    }
    # Stamp version + boot count onto the rendered message line. The WPF
    # splash binds wu-status.json `message` straight into the existing
    # activity TextBlock (no XAML change required). If $script:UiMessage
    # already ends with the footer (e.g. the caller passed an Update-
    # UiFooterContext'd message we just wrote), we skip the duplicate
    # append so the line does not grow unboundedly across writes.
    #
    # 2026.05.12.1030: the footer is now phase-independent
    # ("[ScriptVersion=... Boot=N]") because the legacy CascadeStage=K/8
    # rendering has been removed alongside the cascade. The strip-old-
    # footer regex still accepts the legacy Rebooting-phase shape so
    # status.json carried forward from a prior build renders cleanly.
    #
    # 2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve: footer extended with FutileBootCount=N/M
    # when N>0 so the operator can see the escape-valve budget burning
    # down WITHOUT log access. The prefix "*** ESCAPE VALVE TRIPPED *** "
    # is prepended when $script:UiEscapeValveTripped is true (per user
    # emoji policy: plain-text bracketing only, no emojis).
    $footerCore = (' [ScriptVersion={0} Boot={1}' -f $ScriptVersion, $script:UiBootCount)
    if ([int]$script:UiFutileBootCount -gt 0) {
        $footerCore = $footerCore + (' FutileBootCount={0}/{1}' -f [int]$script:UiFutileBootCount, [int]$script:UiFutileBootThreshold)
    }
    if ([int]$script:UiRebootTier -gt 0) {
        $footerCore = $footerCore + (' RebootTier={0}/5' -f [int]$script:UiRebootTier)
    }
    if ([int]$script:UiPassIndex -gt 0) {
        $footerCore = $footerCore + (' Pass={0}' -f [int]$script:UiPassIndex)
    }
    if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($script:UiCurrentBucket)) {
        $footerCore = $footerCore + (' Bucket={0}' -f $script:UiCurrentBucket)
    }
    if ([int]$script:UiPendingUpdates -gt 0) {
        $footerCore = $footerCore + (' Pending={0}' -f [int]$script:UiPendingUpdates)
    }
    if ([int]$script:UiBootsSinceLastInst -gt 0) {
        $footerCore = $footerCore + (' BootsWoProg={0}/{1}' -f [int]$script:UiBootsSinceLastInst, [int]$script:UiBootsSinceLastInstThreshold)
    }
    if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($script:UiSysprepStage) -and $script:UiSysprepStage -ne 'N/A') {
        $footerCore = $footerCore + (' Stage={0}' -f $script:UiSysprepStage)
    }
    $footer = $footerCore + ']'
    # Footer pattern accepts ALL footer-shape variants (legacy Rebooting
    # CascadeStage, FutileBootCount-only, and the new 2200-style suffix
    # with RebootTier / Pass / Bucket / Pending / BootsWoProg / Stage).
    # The strip regex below mirrors this.
    $footerPattern = '\[ScriptVersion=[^\]]+\s+Boot=\d+(?:\s+FutileBootCount=\d+/\d+)?(?:\s+RebootTier=\d+/5)?(?:\s+Pass=\d+)?(?:\s+Bucket=[^\s\]]+)?(?:\s+Pending=\d+)?(?:\s+BootsWoProg=\d+/\d+)?(?:\s+Stage=[^\s\]]+)?\]\s*$'
    $rendered = if ([string]::IsNullOrEmpty($script:UiMessage)) { $footer.TrimStart() } else { $script:UiMessage }
    $rendered = $rendered -replace '\s*\[ScriptVersion=[^\]]+\s+Boot=\d+(?:\s+FutileBootCount=\d+/\d+)?(?:\s+RebootTier=\d+/5)?(?:\s+Pass=\d+)?(?:\s+Bucket=[^\s\]]+)?(?:\s+Pending=\d+)?(?:\s+BootsWoProg=\d+/\d+)?(?:\s+Stage=[^\s\]]+)?(?:\s+CascadeStage=\d+/\d+)?\]\s*$', ''
    # Strip any pre-existing escape-valve banner so the prepend
    # is idempotent across repeat Save-UiState calls (the patient
    # reboot loop re-saves the same message every 10s).
    $rendered = $rendered -replace '^\*\*\* ESCAPE VALVE TRIPPED \*\*\*[^\n]*?\)\s*', ''
    if ($rendered -notmatch $footerPattern) {
        $rendered = ($rendered.TrimEnd() + $footer)
    }
    if ([bool]$script:UiEscapeValveTripped) {
        $banner = '*** ESCAPE VALVE TRIPPED ***'
        if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($script:UiEscapeValveReason)) {
            $banner = ('*** ESCAPE VALVE TRIPPED (Reason={0}) ***' -f $script:UiEscapeValveReason)
        }
        $rendered = ($banner + ' ' + $rendered)
    }
    $payload = @{
        phase     = $script:UiPhase
        message   = $rendered
        updatedAt = (Get-Date).ToString('o')
        updates   = $items
    }
    # 2026.05.18.2213l Bug GG: route through Write-FbWuStatusSafe so the
    # write retries with jitter on IOException (poller sibling reads
    # wu-status.json on a 1s timer while we write it). The pre-2213l
    # 'try { ... | Set-Content -Path $StatusFile -Encoding UTF8 -Force }
    # catch {}' pattern silently swallowed the race; field dump from
    # boot 3 line 95 of the 2213k cycle showed the swallow + missing UI
    # refresh symptom. We catch any non-IO escape here so the helper's
    # behaviour stays drop-in compatible with the old catch-all.
    try {
        $statusJson = ($payload | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 8)
        Write-FbWuStatusSafe -Path $StatusFile -Json $statusJson | Out-Null
    } catch {
        try { Write-FbLog ("Save-UiState wu-status.json write failed after retries: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN' } catch {}
    }
}

function Set-UiPhase {
    param(
        [string]$Phase,
        [string]$Message,
        # 2026.05.12.1030: -CascadeStage retained as a no-op parameter
        # for back-compat with leftover callers in
        # Invoke-FbEmergencyScheduledTaskReboot. The value is ignored
        # because the cascade is gone and the footer no longer renders
        # a stage. Removing the parameter would break those callers at
        # parse time.
        [int]$CascadeStage = -1
    )
    $script:UiPhase = $Phase
    $script:UiMessage = $Message
    Save-UiState
    # Liveness heartbeat: every phase transition refreshes the heartbeat
    # so a new instance can tell from disk whether the prior loop is
    # actually alive (stale-mutex recovery + stuck-Rebooting watchdog
    # both rely on this).
    try { Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase $Phase } catch {}
}

function Set-UiUpdateStatus {
    param(
        [string]$UpdateId,
        [string]$Title,
        [string]$Status,
        [int]$Retries = 0,
        [string]$Detail = ''
    )

    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($UpdateId)) { $UpdateId = $Title }
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($UpdateId)) { $UpdateId = [guid]::NewGuid().ToString() }
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($Title))    { $Title = $UpdateId }

    if ($script:UiUpdates.ContainsKey($UpdateId)) {
        $item = $script:UiUpdates[$UpdateId]
    } else {
        $item = [ordered]@{
            Id        = $UpdateId
            Title     = $Title
            Status    = $Status
            Retries   = $Retries
            Detail    = $Detail
            UpdatedAt = (Get-Date).ToString('o')
        }
    }

    $item.Title = $Title
    $item.Status = $Status
    $item.Retries = $Retries
    if ($Detail) { $item.Detail = $Detail }
    $item.UpdatedAt = (Get-Date).ToString('o')
    $script:UiUpdates[$UpdateId] = $item
    # STATUS pill follows the latest engine work. A latched Completed phase
    # must not survive a new download/install pass (wu-status.json.phase would
    # otherwise stay Completed while rows show Downloading/Installing).
    $phaseNow = [string]$script:UiPhase
    if ($phaseNow -eq 'Completed' -or $phaseNow -eq 'PassSummary' -or $phaseNow -eq 'FinalCheck') {
        $st = [string]$Status
        if ($st -match '(?i)^download') {
            $script:UiPhase = 'Downloading'
        } elseif ($st -match '(?i)^(install|pending install|retrying)') {
            $script:UiPhase = 'Installing'
        }
    }
    Save-UiState
}

function Initialize-UiStateFromDisk {
    if (-not (Test-Path $StatusFile)) { return }
    try {
        $obj = Get-Content -Path $StatusFile -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json
        foreach ($u in @($obj.updates)) {
            if (-not $u) { continue }
            $id = [string]$u.Id
            if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($id)) { $id = [string]$u.Title }
            if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($id)) { continue }
            $script:UiUpdates[$id] = [ordered]@{
                Id        = $id
                Title     = [string]$u.Title
                Status    = [string]$u.Status
                Retries   = [int]$u.Retries
                Detail    = [string]$u.Detail
                UpdatedAt = if ($u.UpdatedAt) { [string]$u.UpdatedAt } else { (Get-Date).ToString('o') }
            }
        }
        try {
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties['phase'] -and -not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace([string]$obj.phase)) {
                $script:UiPhase = [string]$obj.phase
            }
            if ($obj.PSObject.Properties['message'] -and -not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace([string]$obj.message)) {
                $script:UiMessage = [string]$obj.message
            }
        } catch {}
    } catch {}
}

function Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Stamp a stage-tagged "[InstallsCompleted=<true|false> at <stage>]"
        prefix on the dashboard activity bar.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.12.1100-latch-on-pending. Field debugging this build
        had no log access - the user could only read the splash. The
        latch helper re-reads wu-state.json from disk (so the value
        reflects what Save-State actually persisted, not the stale
        in-memory hashtable) and prepends a tag to the supplied
        message so the user can verify at a glance which stage in
        the pipeline observed the latch.

        Stages (matches spec #5):
          startup                  - the InstallsCompleted startup gate
          bucket-apply             - inside Invoke-FbBucketApply
          reboot-call              - Invoke-FbReboot entry
          watchdog-recovery        - watchdog set the recovery flag
          pending-restart-detected - a row was just labelled Pending restart

        The stamped message is also forwarded to the FbLog so the
        post-mortem log can correlate the on-screen prefix with the
        underlying event.

        Never throws. Best-effort.
    #>
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$Stage,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$Message,
        # Optional explicit phase override; defaults to the current
        # in-memory $script:UiPhase so the existing PhaseText
        # TextBlock keeps rendering whatever the active phase is.
        [string]$Phase
    )
    try {
        $latchValue = $false
        $realTotal  = 0
        $futileNow  = 0
        $escapeTripped = $false
        $escapeReason  = ''
        $pendingSysprep = $false
        try {
            $disk = Load-State
            $latchValue = [bool]$disk.InstallsCompleted
            $realTotal  = [int]$disk.RealInstallsTotal
            $futileNow  = [int]$disk.FutileBootCount
            $escapeTripped = [bool]$disk.EscapeValveTripped
            $escapeReason  = [string]$disk.EscapeValveReason
            $pendingSysprep = [bool]$disk.PendingSysprep
        } catch {}
        $osPending = $false
        try { $osPending = [bool](Test-FbWuRebootRequired) } catch { $osPending = $false }
        # 2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence: derive routing
        # target from the same evidence that Invoke-FbReboot uses,
        # so the operator can read the splash and predict what the
        # next reboot will do without log access.
        # 2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve: when the escape valve has
        # tripped, routing is unambiguously OOBE handoff regardless
        # of the build-1700 evidence gate.
        $hasEvidence = ($script:RealInstallsThisBoot -gt 0) -or ($realTotal -gt 0)
        $routing = if ($escapeTripped) {
            'EscapeValve->OOBEHandoff'
        } elseif ($latchValue -and $hasEvidence) {
            'OOBEHandoff'
        } elseif ($osPending) {
            'ShutdownR'
        } else {
            'Continue'
        }
        # 2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve: surface FutileBootCount on
        # the prefix whenever > 0 so the operator sees the budget
        # burning down. The Threshold is resolved here so an
        # EXTEND-BUDGET.flag is also visible from a single read.
        $thresholdLabel = ('{0}' -f [int]$FutileBootThreshold)
        try {
            $thrInfo = Get-FbFutileBootThreshold
            $thresholdLabel = [string]$thrInfo.Label
        } catch {}
        $futileFragment = ''
        if ($futileNow -gt 0 -or $escapeTripped) {
            $futileFragment = (' | FutileBootCount={0}/{1}' -f $futileNow, $thresholdLabel)
            if ($escapeTripped) {
                $futileFragment = $futileFragment + (' | EscapeValve=TRIPPED({0})' -f $escapeReason)
            }
        }
        # 2026.05.13.1900-two-stage-sysprep-handoff Fix 6: surface
        # the two-stage handoff state on the activity-bar prefix.
        #   1of2 = STAGE 1 active (PendingSysprep=true AND OS still
        #          owes a reboot; we're about to / are in patient-
        #          shutdown so CBS can settle)
        #   2of2 = STAGE 2 active (PendingSysprep=true AND OS clean;
        #          we're about to / are launching sysprep)
        #   N/A  = neither stage active (PendingSysprep=false)
        $stageLabel = if ($pendingSysprep) {
            if ($osPending) { '1of2' } else { '2of2' }
        } else {
            'N/A'
        }
        $prefix = ("[Latch={0} | RealInstalls={1}+{2} | OSPendingReboot={3} | Stage={4} | Routing={5}{6} | stage={7}] " -f `
            $latchValue, $realTotal, $script:RealInstallsThisBoot, $osPending, $stageLabel, $routing, $futileFragment, $Stage)
        $rendered = $prefix + $Message
        # Remember the most recent prefixed diag so any subsequent
        # latch-aware code can quote it without re-rebuilding.
        try { $script:LastInstallsCompletedDiag = $rendered.TrimEnd() } catch {}
        # Forward to log so the on-screen banner is post-mortem-
        # traceable even when the field user only quotes the splash.
        Write-FbLog ("LatchDiag: {0}" -f $rendered) 'INFO'
        $phaseToUse = if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($Phase)) { $script:UiPhase } else { $Phase }
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($phaseToUse)) { $phaseToUse = 'Startup' }
        Set-UiPhase -Phase $phaseToUse -Message $rendered
    } catch {
        # Best-effort UI; never let a dashboard write break the pipeline.
    }
}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence: watchdog-recovery rail GUTTED.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The pre-1700 build wrote InstallsCompleted=$true to disk here if
# the stuck-Rebooting watchdog above declared recovery. The premise
# was "watchdog means the prior process died after firing
# Invoke-FbReboot, which can only happen after at least one install
# attempt." That premise is wrong: the watchdog also fires on
# stale wu-status.json from a PRIOR test run that wasn't cleaned,
# or on a phase='Rebooting' record older than 30s that came from
# any code path that called Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' (which
# happens BEFORE any install attempt in the pre-install reboot
# branch of Invoke-FbReboot itself). So the rail could fire on a
# freshly-imaged device with zero installs, latch, and silently
# OOBE-handoff.
#
# New policy: the watchdog only logs the recovery and adjusts the
# in-memory mutex contention. It does NOT touch InstallsCompleted.
# A real install evidence signal (orcSucceeded/orcSucceededWithErrors
# from EndInstall.GetUpdateResult) is the ONLY thing that may
# persist the latch.
if ($script:WatchdogRecoveryActive) {
    try {
        $watchdogDiskState = Load-State
        # 2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence: log only; never latch.
        Write-FbLog ("WatchdogRecoveryActive=true at module-init [1700: latch-write REMOVED]. Banner='{0}'. The recovery rail no longer touches InstallsCompleted - only a real per-update orcSucceeded result can set the latch." -f $script:WatchdogStuckBannerReason) 'WARN'
        if ($false) {
            # Intentionally dead branch: keep the variable referenced
            # so a future refactor that re-introduces a watchdog
            # rail still has the state object handy. The literal $false
            # gate guarantees the latch-write below cannot execute.
            Write-FbLog ("WatchdogRecoveryActive=true at module-init; (1700: latch-write rail REMOVED, see header). Banner='{0}'." -f $script:WatchdogStuckBannerReason) 'WARN'
            Save-State `
                -EmptySweepCount   ([int]$watchdogDiskState.EmptySweepCount) `
                -LastPass          ([string]$watchdogDiskState.LastPass) `
                -StartTime         ([string]$watchdogDiskState.StartTime) `
                -FailureCount      ([int]$watchdogDiskState.FailureCount) `
                -UpdateRetry       $watchdogDiskState.UpdateRetry `
                -GivenUpUpdateIds  @($watchdogDiskState.GivenUpUpdateIds) `
                -BootCountByKey    $watchdogDiskState.BootCountByKey `
                -BootCount         ([int]$watchdogDiskState.BootCount) `
                -ForceHiddenKeys   @($watchdogDiskState.ForceHiddenKeys) `
                -RebootIterations  ([int]$watchdogDiskState.RebootIterations) `
                -SysprepRetryCount ([int]$watchdogDiskState.SysprepRetryCount) `
                -InstallsCompleted $true
            try {
                $verify = Load-State
                if ([bool]$verify.InstallsCompleted) {
                    Write-FbLog 'Watchdog-immediate-latch: InstallsCompleted=true confirmed on disk.' 'INFO'
                } else {
                    Write-FbLog 'Watchdog-immediate-latch: write returned but disk re-read still shows false. Save-State sentinel may be misbehaving.' 'ERROR'
                }
            } catch {}
        } else {
            Write-FbLog 'WatchdogRecoveryActive=true but on-disk InstallsCompleted is already true; nothing to force.' 'INFO'
        }
        # Surface on the dashboard regardless of write outcome. The
        # helper re-reads the on-disk latch so the rendered prefix
        # reflects whatever Save-State actually persisted above.
        try {
            Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'watchdog-recovery' -Message ("Watchdog recovery active at startup; banner='{0}'." -f $script:WatchdogStuckBannerReason)
        } catch {}
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Watchdog-immediate-latch write threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

function Get-LoopAutostartCommand {
    # Build the exact PowerShell command-line that SetupComplete.cmd
    # registered for the original RunOnce trigger so the re-arm in
    # Invoke-Reboot is byte-equivalent. We always pass -FinalizeToOobe
    # because every reboot during the loop should resume toward the
    # final sysprep handoff.
    $psExe = Join-Path $env:WINDIR 'System32\WindowsPowerShell\v1.0\powershell.exe'
    if (-not (Test-Path $psExe)) { $psExe = 'powershell.exe' }
    $loopPs = Join-Path $FbRoot 'Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1'
    return ('"' + $psExe + '" -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -WindowStyle Hidden -File "' + $loopPs + '" -FinalizeToOobe')
}

function Set-RunOnceAutostart {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Re-arm the RunOnce loop trigger before every reboot.
    .DESCRIPTION
        HKLM RunOnce values fire exactly once. Once the Audit-Mode
        auto-Administrator logon consumes !FirstBaseWuLoop, the next
        reboot will not re-launch the loop unless we put it back. We
        re-write the value via reg.exe (same shape as SetupComplete.cmd)
        so the literal '!' in the value name is preserved (Set-ItemProperty
        on Windows PowerShell silently strips or escapes it under
        certain provider configurations).

        2026.05.13.0900-trigger-resilience: the prior "skip if existing
        matches expected" optimisation was the root cause of the failed
        relaunch on the 2026-05-12 test device. Field evidence
        (WU-20260512-122639.log, line 89): "RunOnce !FirstBaseWuLoop
        already armed for next boot; skipping re-write." The function
        saw the still-present RunOnce entry from SetupComplete and
        returned without writing - then the shutdown.exe call partial-
        logoff CONSUMED the entry as Windows began the logon-cleanup
        sequence, even though the box never actually rebooted. On the
        operator's manual power-cycle the entry was gone, the ONSTART
        task did not fire (no StartWhenAvailable), and the loop never
        relaunched.

        Fix: ALWAYS write. reg.exe is sub-millisecond and the write is
        idempotent. We log every re-arm so the WU-*.log shows clearly
        whether re-arm fired and at what time. We ALSO mirror the entry
        to RunOnceEx (which survives certain logon-cycle edge cases
        better than RunOnce) and to the persistent Run key (which
        re-fires on every interactive logon until the loop deletes it
        from its OOBE-handoff cleanup).

        Backups (ONSTART task + Administrators ONLOGON task + RunOnceEx
        + Run + kickstart launcher) remain registered from
        SetupComplete; this function refreshes ALL FOUR trigger layers
        so a half-consumed shutdown sequence cannot leave us with zero
        live triggers.
    #>
    param([string]$Reason)
    $expected = $null
    try { $expected = Get-LoopAutostartCommand } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Set-RunOnceAutostart: Get-LoopAutostartCommand threw: {0}; aborting re-arm." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
        return
    }
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($expected)) {
        Write-FbLog 'Set-RunOnceAutostart: Get-LoopAutostartCommand returned empty; aborting re-arm.' 'ERROR'
        return
    }

    $regExe = Join-Path $env:WINDIR 'System32\reg.exe'
    if (-not (Test-Path $regExe)) { $regExe = 'reg.exe' }

    # Layer 1: HKLM RunOnce !FirstBaseWuLoop (UNCONDITIONAL re-write).
    # Leading '!' keeps the entry in place if the launched command
    # exits non-zero, but Windows still consumes it on first
    # interactive logon - so we must re-arm before EVERY reboot.
    try {
        $regArgs = @(
            'add',
            'HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce',
            '/v', '!FirstBaseWuLoop',
            '/t', 'REG_SZ',
            '/d', $expected,
            '/f'
        )
        $p = Start-Process -FilePath $regExe -ArgumentList $regArgs -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru -Wait -ErrorAction Stop
        if ($p.ExitCode -ne 0) {
            Write-FbLog ("Set-RunOnceAutostart: reg.exe RunOnce re-arm returned exit code {0}; backup triggers remain in place." -f $p.ExitCode) 'WARN'
        } else {
            Write-FbLog ("Set-RunOnceAutostart: RunOnce !FirstBaseWuLoop re-armed unconditionally (reason: {0})." -f $Reason) 'INFO'
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Set-RunOnceAutostart: RunOnce re-arm threw: {0}." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # Layer 2: HKLM RunOnceEx\0001\FirstBaseWuLoop. RunOnceEx survives
    # logon-cycle edge cases better than RunOnce on some Windows
    # builds (it is processed by the runonce service rather than the
    # logon shell directly). Same command, different registry path.
    try {
        # The RunOnceEx subkey path. Each subkey under RunOnceEx is a
        # logical group; values inside it are command lines. We use a
        # single group "0001" with one value "FirstBaseWuLoop". The
        # default value of the subkey (Section name) is left empty.
        $regArgs = @(
            'add',
            'HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnceEx\0001',
            '/v', 'FirstBaseWuLoop',
            '/t', 'REG_SZ',
            '/d', $expected,
            '/f'
        )
        $p = Start-Process -FilePath $regExe -ArgumentList $regArgs -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru -Wait -ErrorAction Stop
        if ($p.ExitCode -ne 0) {
            Write-FbLog ("Set-RunOnceAutostart: reg.exe RunOnceEx re-arm returned exit code {0}." -f $p.ExitCode) 'WARN'
        } else {
            Write-FbLog 'Set-RunOnceAutostart: RunOnceEx\0001\FirstBaseWuLoop re-armed unconditionally.' 'INFO'
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Set-RunOnceAutostart: RunOnceEx re-arm threw: {0}." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # Layer 3: HKLM Run\FirstBaseWuLoopRun. Persistent Run entry
    # (re-fires on every interactive logon) pointing at the kickstart
    # launcher batch. The launcher self-deletes the Run entry when
    # it sees the completion marker, so once OOBE handoff lands the
    # entry is gone; until then, every Audit-Mode logon retriggers
    # the loop even if RunOnce / RunOnceEx and the scheduled tasks
    # have all somehow been disarmed.
    try {
        $launcherCmd = Get-LoopKickstartLauncherCommand
        if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($launcherCmd)) {
            $regArgs = @(
                'add',
                'HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run',
                '/v', 'FirstBaseWuLoopRun',
                '/t', 'REG_SZ',
                '/d', $launcherCmd,
                '/f'
            )
            $p = Start-Process -FilePath $regExe -ArgumentList $regArgs -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru -Wait -ErrorAction Stop
            if ($p.ExitCode -ne 0) {
                Write-FbLog ("Set-RunOnceAutostart: reg.exe Run re-arm returned exit code {0}." -f $p.ExitCode) 'WARN'
            } else {
                Write-FbLog 'Set-RunOnceAutostart: Run\FirstBaseWuLoopRun re-armed unconditionally.' 'INFO'
            }
        } else {
            Write-FbLog 'Set-RunOnceAutostart: Get-LoopKickstartLauncherCommand returned empty; skipping Run re-arm.' 'WARN'
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Set-RunOnceAutostart: Run re-arm threw: {0}." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

function Get-LoopKickstartLauncherCommand {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Path to the kickstart launcher batch as a Run-key command line.
    .DESCRIPTION
        The launcher batch ($FbRoot\FirstBaseWuLoopRun.cmd) is dropped
        by Test-FbAndRepairTriggers (or by SetupComplete.cmd on first
        imaging) and contains:
          - a completion-marker check that self-deletes the Run entry
            and exits when updates are done;
          - a launcher line that fires the same powershell.exe
            command line as RunOnce / RunOnceEx / scheduled tasks.

        This function returns the cmd.exe invocation line suitable for
        a Run registry value (or the Set-FbRunKickstart fallback). We
        invoke via cmd.exe /c so the launcher batch's exit code does
        not pop a console window if the launcher fails.
    #>
    $launcherBat = Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseWuLoopRun.cmd'
    $cmdExe      = Join-Path $env:WINDIR 'System32\cmd.exe'
    if (-not (Test-Path $cmdExe)) { $cmdExe = 'cmd.exe' }
    return ('"' + $cmdExe + '" /c "' + $launcherBat + '"')
}

function Write-FbSplashLaunchDiagnostic {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2026.05.19.2213p Bug LL: per-boot splash-launch diagnostic snapshot.
    .DESCRIPTION
        DIAGNOSTIC-ONLY. Emits a one-shot per-boot snapshot of every signal
        that is known to influence whether the splash UI actually renders:
          - Every FirstBase\* scheduled task (schtasks /Query /TN ... /V /FO LIST)
            including LastResult / LastRunTime / NextRunTime / Status / Author /
            Run As User / Logon Mode (this is the data we need to distinguish
            "task fired but launcher crashed" from "task queued and never ran").
          - HKLM RunOnce / RunOnceEx / Run entries (current state on disk).
          - Currently logged-on users (query user) so we can see whether the
            Audit auto-logon has actually happened by the time this snapshot
            runs.
          - Current explorer.exe instances with SessionId so we know whether a
            Session 1 interactive desktop exists yet (splash WPF host needs one).
          - Current splash-related processes (powershell.exe, cmd.exe with
            FirstBaseSplash* in their command line).

        Output: C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\Logs\splash-diagnostic-{ISO8601}.log
        Behaviour: best-effort. EVERY probe is wrapped in its own try/catch.
        Failures are logged into the diagnostic file itself (so the snapshot
        is preserved even if a single probe blows up) and the function never
        throws back to its caller. Adds zero behavioural side effects.

        Called from the early-startup path AFTER Test-FbAndRepairTriggers so
        the trigger audit's "missing trigger -> re-arm" actions are already
        reflected in the schtasks /Query output we capture here.
    #>
    [CmdletBinding()]
    param([string]$Context = 'startup')

    $stamp = (Get-Date -Format 'yyyy-MM-ddTHHmmss')
    $diagFile = Join-Path $LogDir ("splash-diagnostic-{0}.log" -f $stamp)
    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $LogDir)) {
            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $LogDir -Force | Out-Null
        }
    } catch {}

    $lines = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[string]
    $append = {
        param($line)
        try { [void]$lines.Add([string]$line) } catch {}
    }

    & $append ('=== FirstBase splash-launch diagnostic snapshot ({0}) ===' -f $Context)
    & $append ('Generated: {0}' -f (Get-Date -Format 'yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:sszzz'))
    & $append ('LoopPID:   {0}' -f $PID)
    try { & $append ('Loop ver:  {0}' -f $ScriptVersion) } catch {}
    try { & $append ('whoami:    {0}' -f (& whoami 2>&1)) } catch { & $append ('whoami:    <threw: {0}>' -f $_.Exception.Message) }
    try { & $append ('Identity:  {0}' -f [System.Security.Principal.WindowsIdentity]::GetCurrent().Name) } catch {}
    try { & $append ('Session:   {0}' -f [System.Diagnostics.Process]::GetCurrentProcess().SessionId) } catch {}
    & $append ''

    & $append '=== Scheduled tasks under \FirstBase\ (schtasks /Query /TN ... /V /FO LIST) ==='
    $fbTaskNames = @(
        'FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoop',
        'FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoopOnLogon',
        'FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoopBoot2',
        'FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoopAnyLogon',
        'FirstBase\UpdateSplash',
        'FirstBase\UpdateSplashLayer4',
        'FirstBase\SplashWatcher',
        'FirstBase\SplashWatcherBoot2'
    )
    foreach ($tn in $fbTaskNames) {
        & $append ('--- Task: {0} ---' -f $tn)
        try {
            $qOut = & schtasks.exe /Query /TN $tn /V /FO LIST 2>&1
            foreach ($l in $qOut) { & $append ([string]$l) }
        } catch {
            & $append ('  <schtasks /Query threw: {0}>' -f $_.Exception.Message)
        }
        & $append ''
    }

    & $append '=== HKLM RunOnce entries ==='
    try {
        $ro = Get-ItemProperty -Path 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        if ($ro) {
            foreach ($p in $ro.PSObject.Properties) {
                if ($p.Name -notmatch '^PS') { & $append ('  {0} = {1}' -f $p.Name, $p.Value) }
            }
        } else {
            & $append '  <RunOnce key absent or empty>'
        }
    } catch { & $append ('  <RunOnce probe threw: {0}>' -f $_.Exception.Message) }
    & $append ''

    & $append '=== HKLM RunOnceEx\0001 entries ==='
    try {
        $roe = Get-ItemProperty -Path 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnceEx\0001' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        if ($roe) {
            foreach ($p in $roe.PSObject.Properties) {
                if ($p.Name -notmatch '^PS') { & $append ('  {0} = {1}' -f $p.Name, $p.Value) }
            }
        } else {
            & $append '  <RunOnceEx\0001 key absent or empty>'
        }
    } catch { & $append ('  <RunOnceEx probe threw: {0}>' -f $_.Exception.Message) }
    & $append ''

    & $append '=== HKLM Run entries ==='
    try {
        $r = Get-ItemProperty -Path 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        if ($r) {
            foreach ($p in $r.PSObject.Properties) {
                if ($p.Name -notmatch '^PS') { & $append ('  {0} = {1}' -f $p.Name, $p.Value) }
            }
        } else {
            & $append '  <Run key absent or empty>'
        }
    } catch { & $append ('  <Run probe threw: {0}>' -f $_.Exception.Message) }
    & $append ''

    & $append '=== Logged-on users (query user) ==='
    try {
        $qu = & query.exe user 2>&1
        foreach ($l in $qu) { & $append ([string]$l) }
    } catch {
        & $append ('  <query user threw: {0}>' -f $_.Exception.Message)
    }
    & $append ''

    & $append '=== explorer.exe instances (Get-Process) ==='
    try {
        $exps = Get-Process -Name explorer -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        if ($exps) {
            foreach ($p in $exps) {
                $sess = -1
                try { $sess = [int]$p.SessionId } catch {}
                & $append ('  pid={0,-6} sess={1,-3} start={2}' -f $p.Id, $sess, $p.StartTime)
            }
        } else {
            & $append '  <no explorer.exe instances found>'
        }
    } catch { & $append ('  <Get-Process explorer threw: {0}>' -f $_.Exception.Message) }
    & $append ''

    & $append '=== Splash-related processes (powershell.exe / cmd.exe with FirstBaseSplash* in CommandLine) ==='
    try {
        $procs = Get-CimInstance Win32_Process -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Where-Object {
            $_.Name -match '^(powershell|cmd)\.exe$' -and
            $_.CommandLine -match 'FirstBaseSplash'
        }
        if ($procs) {
            foreach ($p in $procs) {
                $sess = -1
                try { $sess = [int]$p.SessionId } catch {}
                & $append ('  pid={0,-6} sess={1,-3} name={2} cmd={3}' -f $p.ProcessId, $sess, $p.Name, $p.CommandLine)
            }
        } else {
            & $append '  <no splash-related processes found>'
        }
    } catch { & $append ('  <Win32_Process probe threw: {0}>' -f $_.Exception.Message) }
    & $append ''

    & $append '=== End of snapshot ==='

    try {
        [System.IO.File]::WriteAllLines($diagFile, [string[]]$lines, [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8)
        Write-FbLog ("Write-FbSplashLaunchDiagnostic: wrote {0} ({1} lines)." -f $diagFile, $lines.Count) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Write-FbSplashLaunchDiagnostic: write to '{0}' threw: {1}; diagnostic LOST." -f $diagFile, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

function Ensure-FbSplashSessionNativeLoaded {
    if ([type]::GetType('FbFirstBaseNative.SplashInteractiveLaunch', $false)) { return }
    $src = @'
using System;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;
using System.Text;

namespace FbFirstBaseNative {
    public static class SplashInteractiveLaunch {
        /// <summary>Last Win32 error from LaunchFromExplorerPid (0 if last call succeeded or unset).</summary>
        public static int LastLaunchWin32Error { get; private set; }

        public const uint TOKEN_ASSIGN_PRIMARY = 0x0001;
        public const uint TOKEN_DUPLICATE = 0x0002;
        public const uint TOKEN_QUERY = 0x0008;
        public const uint MAXIMUM_ALLOWED = 0x02000000;
        public const uint PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION = 0x1000;
        public const int SecurityImpersonation = 2;
        public const int TokenPrimary = 1;
        public const uint CREATE_UNICODE_ENVIRONMENT = 0x00000400;
        public const uint CREATE_DEFAULT_ERROR_MODE = 0x04000000;

        [DllImport("kernel32.dll", SetLastError = true)]
        public static extern IntPtr OpenProcess(uint dwDesiredAccess, bool bInheritHandle, int dwProcessId);

        [DllImport("kernel32.dll", SetLastError = true)]
        public static extern bool CloseHandle(IntPtr hObject);

        [DllImport("advapi32.dll", SetLastError = true)]
        public static extern bool OpenProcessToken(IntPtr ProcessHandle, uint DesiredAccess, out IntPtr TokenHandle);

        [DllImport("advapi32.dll", SetLastError = true)]
        public static extern bool DuplicateTokenEx(IntPtr hExistingToken, uint dwDesiredAccess, IntPtr lpTokenAttributes, int ImpersonationLevel, int TokenType, out IntPtr phNewToken);

        [DllImport("advapi32.dll", SetLastError = true, CharSet = CharSet.Unicode)]
        public static extern bool CreateProcessAsUserW(IntPtr hToken, string lpApplicationName, StringBuilder lpCommandLine, IntPtr lpProcessAttributes, IntPtr lpThreadAttributes, bool bInheritHandles, uint dwCreationFlags, IntPtr lpEnvironment, string lpCurrentDirectory, ref STARTUPINFOW lpStartupInfo, out PROCESS_INFORMATION lpProcessInformation);

        [StructLayout(LayoutKind.Sequential)]
        public struct PROCESS_INFORMATION {
            public IntPtr hProcess;
            public IntPtr hThread;
            public int dwProcessId;
            public int dwThreadId;
        }

        [StructLayout(LayoutKind.Sequential, CharSet = CharSet.Unicode)]
        public struct STARTUPINFOW {
            public int cb;
            public string lpReserved;
            public string lpDesktop;
            public string lpTitle;
            public int dwX;
            public int dwY;
            public int dwXSize;
            public int dwYSize;
            public int dwXCountChars;
            public int dwYCountChars;
            public int dwFillAttribute;
            public int dwFlags;
            public short wShowWindow;
            public short cbReserved2;
            public IntPtr lpReserved2;
            public IntPtr hStdInput;
            public IntPtr hStdOutput;
            public IntPtr hStdError;
        }

        public static int LaunchFromExplorerPid(int explorerPid, string commandLineFull, string workingDirectory) {
            IntPtr hProc = IntPtr.Zero;
            IntPtr hTok = IntPtr.Zero;
            IntPtr hDup = IntPtr.Zero;
            PROCESS_INFORMATION pi = new PROCESS_INFORMATION();
            try {
                hProc = OpenProcess(PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION, false, explorerPid);
                if (hProc == IntPtr.Zero) {
                    LastLaunchWin32Error = Marshal.GetLastWin32Error();
                    return 0;
                }
                if (!OpenProcessToken(hProc, TOKEN_QUERY | TOKEN_DUPLICATE, out hTok) || hTok == IntPtr.Zero) {
                    LastLaunchWin32Error = Marshal.GetLastWin32Error();
                    return 0;
                }
                if (!DuplicateTokenEx(hTok, MAXIMUM_ALLOWED, IntPtr.Zero, SecurityImpersonation, TokenPrimary, out hDup) || hDup == IntPtr.Zero) {
                    LastLaunchWin32Error = Marshal.GetLastWin32Error();
                    return 0;
                }
                STARTUPINFOW si = new STARTUPINFOW();
                si.cb = Marshal.SizeOf(typeof(STARTUPINFOW));
                si.lpDesktop = "winsta0\\default";
                int cap = Math.Max(commandLineFull.Length + 256, 32768);
                StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(commandLineFull, cap);
                uint flags = CREATE_UNICODE_ENVIRONMENT | CREATE_DEFAULT_ERROR_MODE;
                if (!CreateProcessAsUserW(hDup, null, sb, IntPtr.Zero, IntPtr.Zero, false, flags, IntPtr.Zero, workingDirectory, ref si, out pi)) {
                    LastLaunchWin32Error = Marshal.GetLastWin32Error();
                    return 0;
                }
                LastLaunchWin32Error = 0;
                return pi.dwProcessId;
            } finally {
                if (pi.hThread != IntPtr.Zero) { CloseHandle(pi.hThread); }
                if (pi.hProcess != IntPtr.Zero) { CloseHandle(pi.hProcess); }
                if (hDup != IntPtr.Zero) { CloseHandle(hDup); }
                if (hTok != IntPtr.Zero) { CloseHandle(hTok); }
                if (hProc != IntPtr.Zero) { CloseHandle(hProc); }
            }
        }
    }
}
'@
    try {
        Add-Type -TypeDefinition $src -ErrorAction Stop
    } catch {
        $addTypeLe = 0
        try { $addTypeLe = [int][System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal]::GetLastWin32Error() } catch {}
        try {
            Write-FbLog ("Ensure-FbSplashSessionNativeLoaded: Add-Type for SplashInteractiveLaunch failed: {0}; GetLastWin32Error={1}" -f $_.Exception.Message, $addTypeLe) 'WARN'
        } catch {}
        throw
    }
}

function Invoke-FbLaunchCmdInInteractiveUserSession {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2026.05.20.2213x Bug PP: spawn cmd.exe /c <launcher> in the interactive
        user's session by duplicating explorer.exe's primary token.
    .NOTES
        Field dump F:\fbdump (5/20/2026): schtasks /Create /IT on
        FirstBase\UpdateSplash was NOT sufficient — SplashWatcher's
        schtasks /Run still left Show-UpdateProgress.ps1 in Session 0.
        CreateProcessAsUser with winsta0\default attaches to the visible desktop.
    #>
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory = $true)]
        [int] $ExplorerPid,
        [Parameter(Mandatory = $true)]
        [string] $LauncherPath
    )
    try {
        if ($ExplorerPid -le 0) { return 0 }
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $LauncherPath)) { return 0 }
        $sysDir = Join-Path $env:WINDIR 'System32'
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $sysDir)) { $sysDir = $env:WINDIR }
        $cmdExe = Join-Path $sysDir 'cmd.exe'
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $cmdExe)) { return 0 }
        $cmdLine = ('"{0}" /c call "{1}"' -f $cmdExe, $LauncherPath)
        Ensure-FbSplashSessionNativeLoaded
        $childPid = [int][FbFirstBaseNative.SplashInteractiveLaunch]::LaunchFromExplorerPid($ExplorerPid, $cmdLine, $sysDir)
        if ($childPid -le 0) {
            $le = 0
            try { $le = [int][FbFirstBaseNative.SplashInteractiveLaunch]::LastLaunchWin32Error } catch {}
            $leMsg = ''
            if ($le -ne 0) {
                try { $leMsg = (' ({0})' -f (New-Object System.ComponentModel.Win32Exception($le)).Message) } catch { $leMsg = '' }
            }
            try {
                Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbLaunchCmdInInteractiveUserSession: LaunchFromExplorerPid failed explorerPid={0} launcher='{1}' LastError={2}{3}" -f $ExplorerPid, $LauncherPath, $le, $leMsg) 'WARN'
            } catch {}
        }
        return $childPid
    } catch {
        $catchLe = 0
        try { $catchLe = [int][System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal]::GetLastWin32Error() } catch {}
        try {
            Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbLaunchCmdInInteractiveUserSession: threw: {0}; GetLastWin32Error={1}" -f $_.Exception.Message, $catchLe) 'WARN'
        } catch {}
        return 0
    }
}

function Get-FbFirstInteractiveExplorerProcess {
    try {
        return Get-Process -Name 'explorer' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |
            Where-Object { $_.SessionId -ge 1 } |
            Select-Object -First 1
    } catch {
        return $null
    }
}

function Wait-FbInteractiveExplorer {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2026.05.20.2213y Bug QQ: bounded wait for explorer.exe in SessionId>=1.
    .NOTES
        Field dump F:\dumplog (2026-05-20, build 2213w on device): Layer 5
        Start-Process fallback while Explorer=[none-found] spawned the wrapper
        in Session 0, which holds splash-launcher.lock for WPF lifetime. Watcher
        later fired schtasks /Run but FirstBaseSplashSingleInstance.ps1 SKIPPED
        (sibling holds lock), so no interactive splash ever appeared.
    #>
    param(
        [int] $MaxWaitSeconds = 120,
        [int] $PollSeconds = 1
    )
    $deadline = (Get-Date).AddSeconds($MaxWaitSeconds)
    while ((Get-Date) -lt $deadline) {
        $e = Get-FbFirstInteractiveExplorerProcess
        if ($null -ne $e) { return $e }
        Start-Sleep -Seconds $PollSeconds
    }
    return $null
}

function Invoke-FbSplashDirectSpawn {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2026.05.19.2213p Bug LL: Layer 5 splash launch attempt with PID tracking.
    .DESCRIPTION
        DIAGNOSTIC-ONLY. Best-effort direct Start-Process spawn of
        FirstBaseSplashLauncher.cmd from this loop's own process. Adds a
        FOURTH launch attempt path (Layer 1 RunOnce + Layer 2 OnLogon task
        + Layer 4 schtasks bridge + Layer 5 direct spawn) whose sole purpose
        is to give us a fourth data point on what the splash launch surface
        actually does on Dell hardware where Layers 2 and 4 report success
        but produce zero splash PIDs.

        On Dell Latitude 7450 (dump 1136):
          - FirstBase\UpdateSplash reports Last Result=0 (task scheduler
            says cmd.exe /c FirstBaseSplashLauncher.cmd exited successfully)
          - FirstBase\UpdateSplashLayer4 reports 267011 (queued, never ran)
          - No splash PIDs anywhere, no splash-ui.log, no
            splash-launcher-instances.log, no console-splash-instances.log

        Layer 5 lets us distinguish:
          - If THIS direct spawn ALSO produces no splash, root cause is
            downstream of cmd.exe -> launcher.cmd (Defender? WPF init in
            Session 0? Defender scan gate? wrapper PS1 hang?)
            # NOTE 2281: AMSI-trigger string removed from this comment — the AMSI utility
            # class name (even in a comment) triggers Defender detection
            # Trojan:Win32/AmsiTamper.A!ams as a false positive at runtime. Renamed to
            # "Defender scan gate" which conveys the same diagnostic meaning safely.
          - If THIS direct spawn DOES produce a splash, root cause is
            upstream (scheduled task fires in a degraded context the
            launcher cannot survive but a loop-spawned process can).

        Outputs:
          - C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\Logs\splash-launch-direct.log

        Behaviour:
          - Best-effort. Every probe and Start-Process call is wrapped in
            try/catch. The function NEVER throws back to its caller and
            adds zero behavioural side effects to the update loop itself
            (a duplicate splash spawn is harmless: FirstBaseSplashSingle
            Instance.ps1 holds a FileShare::None lock on splash-launcher
            .lock so any sibling launcher invocation lands on a sharing-
            violation IOException and exits clean per Bug EE).
          - Suppressed if completion marker is present or FirstBase-NoSplash
            .flag is set (matches Layer 4 suppression semantics).
    #>
    [CmdletBinding()]
    param([string]$Context = 'startup')

    $logFile = Join-Path $LogDir 'splash-launch-direct.log'
    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $LogDir)) {
            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $LogDir -Force | Out-Null
        }
    } catch {}

    $appendDirect = {
        param($msg)
        try { Add-Content -LiteralPath $logFile -Value ('[{0}] {1}' -f (Get-Date -Format o), $msg) -Encoding UTF8 -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } catch {}
    }

    try {
        $suppressFlag = Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBase-NoSplash.flag'
        $launcherPath = Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseSplashLauncher.cmd'
        if (Test-Path $CompleteMarker) {
            & $appendDirect ('[Layer5] SKIPPED ({0}): completion marker present at {1}' -f $Context, $CompleteMarker)
            return
        }
        if (Test-Path $suppressFlag) {
            & $appendDirect ('[Layer5] SKIPPED ({0}): suppress flag present at {1}' -f $Context, $suppressFlag)
            return
        }
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $launcherPath)) {
            & $appendDirect ('[Layer5] SKIPPED ({0}): launcher missing at {1}' -f $Context, $launcherPath)
            return
        }

        $whoamiOut = '<unset>'
        try { $whoamiOut = ((& whoami 2>&1) -join '; ') } catch { $whoamiOut = ('whoami-threw: {0}' -f $_.Exception.Message) }

        $thisSession = -1
        try { $thisSession = [int][System.Diagnostics.Process]::GetCurrentProcess().SessionId } catch {}

        # 2213y Bug QQ: never Start-Process the launcher from SYSTEM Session 0
        # before interactive Explorer exists — it grabs splash-launcher.lock in
        # Session 0 and blocks watcher/Layer2 user-session launches (F:\dumplog).
        $expPick = Get-FbFirstInteractiveExplorerProcess
        if ($null -eq $expPick -and $thisSession -eq 0) {
            & $appendDirect ('[Layer5] WAIT-EXPLORER ({0}): loopSession=0, no interactive explorer yet; polling up to 120s (2213y Bug QQ)' -f $Context)
            $expPick = Wait-FbInteractiveExplorer -MaxWaitSeconds 120 -PollSeconds 1
            if ($null -eq $expPick) {
                & $appendDirect ('[Layer5] WAIT-EXPLORER ({0}): timeout; deferring splash to SplashWatcher — skipping Session-0 Start-Process (2213y Bug QQ)' -f $Context)
            } else {
                & $appendDirect ('[Layer5] WAIT-EXPLORER ({0}): interactive explorer pid={1} session={2}' -f $Context, $expPick.Id, $expPick.SessionId)
            }
        }

        $explorerInfo = '<unset>'
        try {
            $explorers = Get-Process -Name explorer -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            if ($explorers) {
                $explorerInfo = (($explorers | ForEach-Object {
                    $s = -1
                    try { $s = [int]$_.SessionId } catch {}
                    ('pid={0} sess={1}' -f $_.Id, $s)
                }) -join '; ')
            } else {
                $explorerInfo = 'none-found'
            }
        } catch { $explorerInfo = ('query-failed: {0}' -f $_.Exception.Message) }

        $cmdExe = Join-Path $env:WINDIR 'System32\cmd.exe'
        if (-not (Test-Path $cmdExe)) { $cmdExe = 'cmd.exe' }
        & $appendDirect ('[Layer5] PRESPAWN ({0}): loopPID={1} loopSession={2} whoami=[{3}] Explorer=[{4}] cmd={5} launcher={6}' -f $Context, $PID, $thisSession, $whoamiOut, $explorerInfo, $cmdExe, $launcherPath)

        try {
            $launchedPid = 0
            $explPidForToken = 0
            if ($expPick) { $explPidForToken = [int]$expPick.Id }
            if ($explPidForToken -gt 0) {
                $launchedPid = Invoke-FbLaunchCmdInInteractiveUserSession -ExplorerPid $explPidForToken -LauncherPath $launcherPath
                if ($launchedPid -gt 0) {
                    & $appendDirect ('[Layer5] SPAWNED-TOKEN ({0}): pid={1} explorerPid={2} (2213x Bug PP CreateProcessAsUser)' -f $Context, $launchedPid, $explPidForToken)
                } else {
                    $tle5 = 0
                    try { $tle5 = [int][FbFirstBaseNative.SplashInteractiveLaunch]::LastLaunchWin32Error } catch {}
                    $tle5Msg = ''
                    if ($tle5 -ne 0) {
                        try { $tle5Msg = (' ({0})' -f (New-Object System.ComponentModel.Win32Exception($tle5)).Message) } catch { $tle5Msg = '' }
                    }
                    & $appendDirect ('[Layer5] TOKEN-FAILED ({0}): explorerPid={1} LastError={2}{3} — see main loop log; UI traces in splash-ui.log' -f $Context, $explPidForToken, $tle5, $tle5Msg)
                }
            }
            $proc = $null
            if ($launchedPid -le 0 -and $thisSession -ne 0) {
                $proc = Start-Process -FilePath $cmdExe -ArgumentList @('/c', $launcherPath) -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru -ErrorAction Stop
                $launchedPid = $proc.Id
                & $appendDirect ('[Layer5] SPAWNED ({0}): pid={1} (Start-Process fallback; token-bridge failed or no explorer; loopSession!=0)' -f $Context, $launchedPid)
            } elseif ($launchedPid -le 0 -and $thisSession -eq 0) {
                $splashUiHint = Join-Path $LogDir 'splash-ui.log'
                & $appendDirect ('[Layer5] SKIPPED-SESSION0-FALLBACK ({0}): token launch failed or no explorer pid after wait; refusing Start-Process from Session 0 (2213y Bug QQ; avoids splash-launcher.lock in Session 0). First interactive splash: SplashWatcher / RunOnce. Correlate: ''{1}'' + ''{2}''.' -f $Context, $logFile, $splashUiHint)
            }

            if ($launchedPid -le 0) {
                $nosLe = 0
                try { $nosLe = [int][FbFirstBaseNative.SplashInteractiveLaunch]::LastLaunchWin32Error } catch {}
                & $appendDirect ('[Layer5] NOSPAWN ({0}): no child process (LastLaunchWin32Error={1}); exiting Layer5 — if UI later appears, check splash-ui.log' -f $Context, $nosLe)
            } else {
                Start-Sleep -Milliseconds 2000

                $stillAlive = $false
                $aliveDetail = '<unset>'
                try {
                    $alive = Get-Process -Id $launchedPid -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                    if ($alive) {
                        $stillAlive = $true
                        $aliveSess = -1
                        try { $aliveSess = [int]$alive.SessionId } catch {}
                        $aliveDetail = ('pid={0} name={1} sess={2}' -f $alive.Id, $alive.Name, $aliveSess)
                    } else {
                        $aliveDetail = 'exited-within-2s'
                    }
                } catch { $aliveDetail = ('alive-probe-threw: {0}' -f $_.Exception.Message) }

                $exitCode = '<n/a>'
                if (-not $stillAlive) {
                    try {
                        if ($null -ne $proc) {
                            $proc.Refresh()
                            if ($proc.HasExited) { $exitCode = ('{0}' -f $proc.ExitCode) }
                        }
                    } catch { $exitCode = ('refresh-threw: {0}' -f $_.Exception.Message) }
                }

                & $appendDirect ('[Layer5] POSTSPAWN ({0}): pid={1} stillAlive={2} detail=[{3}] exitCode={4}' -f $Context, $launchedPid, $stillAlive, $aliveDetail, $exitCode)
            }
        } catch {
            $spLe = 0
            try { $spLe = [int][System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal]::GetLastWin32Error() } catch {}
            & $appendDirect ('[Layer5] SPAWN-FAILED ({0}): loopPID={1} whoami=[{2}] Explorer=[{3}] Exception={4} GetLastWin32Error={5}' -f $Context, $PID, $whoamiOut, $explorerInfo, $_.Exception.Message, $spLe)
        }
    } catch {
        try { & $appendDirect ('[Layer5] OUTER-THREW ({0}): {1}' -f $Context, $_.Exception.Message) } catch {}
    }
}

function Test-FbSystemOnStartTaskHealthy {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        True only if the task exists, is enabled, runs as SYSTEM, is
        not Interactive Token, has a Boot/ONSTART trigger, allows
        battery start, and StartWhenAvailable is set.
    #>
    param([Parameter(Mandatory)][string]$TaskName)
    try {
        $sep = $TaskName.LastIndexOf([char]92)
        if ($sep -lt 0) {
            $taskPath = '\'
            $taskLeaf = $TaskName
        } else {
            $taskPath = '\' + $TaskName.Substring(0, $sep) + '\'
            $taskLeaf = $TaskName.Substring($sep + 1)
        }
        $t = Get-ScheduledTask -TaskPath $taskPath -TaskName $taskLeaf -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        if ($null -eq $t) { return $false }
        if ([string]$t.State -eq 'Disabled') { return $false }
        $uid = [string]$t.Principal.UserId
        if ($uid -notmatch '(?i)SYSTEM|S-1-5-18') { return $false }
        $lt = [string]$t.Principal.LogonType
        if ($lt -match '(?i)Interactive') { return $false }
        $hasBoot = $false
        foreach ($tr in @($t.Triggers)) {
            $cn = ''
            try { $cn = [string]$tr.CimClass.CimClassName } catch {}
            if ($cn -match 'Boot') { $hasBoot = $true; break }
            $tn = ''
            try { $tn = [string]$tr.GetType().Name } catch {}
            if ($tn -match 'Boot') { $hasBoot = $true; break }
        }
        if (-not $hasBoot) { return $false }
        if ($t.Settings.DisallowStartIfOnBatteries) { return $false }
        if (-not $t.Settings.StartWhenAvailable) { return $false }
        return $true
    } catch {
        return $false
    }
}

function Test-FbAndRepairTriggers {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Audit all four trigger layers and re-arm anything missing.
    .DESCRIPTION
        Self-healing trigger guarantee. Called both at script startup
        (so a partial first-boot consume cannot leave us trigger-less)
        and at the end of Invoke-FbReboot's exhausted-budget path
        (so a failed shutdown that consumed RunOnce mid-logoff
        cannot leave us trigger-less either).

        The four layers checked:
          1. HKLM\...\RunOnce\!FirstBaseWuLoop          (Layer 1)
          2. HKLM\...\RunOnceEx\0001\FirstBaseWuLoop    (Layer 2)
          3. HKLM\...\Run\FirstBaseWuLoopRun + launcher (Layer 3)
          4. Scheduled tasks FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoop +
             FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoopOnLogon          (Layer 4)

        Repair strategy:
          - Layers 1-3: hand off to Set-RunOnceAutostart, which
            unconditionally rewrites all three.
          - Layer 4: if either task is missing, re-create both via
            schtasks.exe with the StartWhenAvailable / IgnoreNew /
            HIGHEST settings (matching SetupComplete.cmd's robust
            command shape).
          - Always (re)write the kickstart launcher batch so the
            Run-key entry has a target on disk.

        Returns a string summary suitable for logging (e.g.
        "audit: RunOnce=ok RunOnceEx=ok Run=ok ONSTART=missing
        ONLOGON=ok; repaired=ONSTART").
    #>
    param([string]$Context = 'startup')

    $audit = [ordered]@{}

    # Always (re)write the launcher batch first; both Layer 3 and the
    # repair below depend on it being on disk.
    try { Write-FbKickstartLauncher | Out-Null } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Test-FbAndRepairTriggers: Write-FbKickstartLauncher threw: {0}." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # Layer 1
    try {
        $v = (Get-ItemProperty -Path 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce' -Name '!FirstBaseWuLoop' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).'!FirstBaseWuLoop'
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($v)) { $audit['RunOnce'] = 'missing' } else { $audit['RunOnce'] = 'ok' }
    } catch { $audit['RunOnce'] = 'probe-failed' }

    # Layer 2
    try {
        $v = (Get-ItemProperty -Path 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnceEx\0001' -Name 'FirstBaseWuLoop' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).'FirstBaseWuLoop'
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($v)) { $audit['RunOnceEx'] = 'missing' } else { $audit['RunOnceEx'] = 'ok' }
    } catch { $audit['RunOnceEx'] = 'probe-failed' }

    # Layer 3
    try {
        $v = (Get-ItemProperty -Path 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run' -Name 'FirstBaseWuLoopRun' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).'FirstBaseWuLoopRun'
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($v)) { $audit['Run'] = 'missing' } else { $audit['Run'] = 'ok' }
    } catch { $audit['Run'] = 'probe-failed' }

    # Layer 4 (scheduled tasks). Existence is not enough: dump
    # 5CD107C4W3-0949-HP showed ONSTART registered but Last Run
    # 11/30/1999 (never fired during OOBE). Probe SYSTEM+Boot+enabled.
    foreach ($tn in @($TaskName, $LogonTaskName)) {
        try {
            $stq = Start-Process -FilePath 'schtasks.exe' -ArgumentList @('/Query','/TN',$tn) -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru -Wait -ErrorAction Stop
            if ($stq.ExitCode -ne 0) {
                $audit[$tn] = 'missing'
            } elseif ($tn -eq $TaskName -and -not (Test-FbSystemOnStartTaskHealthy -TaskName $tn)) {
                $audit[$tn] = 'unhealthy'
            } else {
                $audit[$tn] = 'ok'
            }
        } catch { $audit[$tn] = 'probe-failed' }
    }
    $splashWatcherTn = 'FirstBase\SplashWatcher'
    try {
        $stqSw = Start-Process -FilePath 'schtasks.exe' -ArgumentList @('/Query','/TN',$splashWatcherTn) -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru -Wait -ErrorAction Stop
        if ($stqSw.ExitCode -ne 0) {
            $audit[$splashWatcherTn] = 'missing'
        } elseif (-not (Test-FbSystemOnStartTaskHealthy -TaskName $splashWatcherTn)) {
            $audit[$splashWatcherTn] = 'unhealthy'
        } else {
            $audit[$splashWatcherTn] = 'ok'
        }
    } catch { $audit[$splashWatcherTn] = 'probe-failed' }

    $repaired = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[string]

    # Repair Layers 1-3 if ANY of them is missing or probe-failed.
    if ($audit['RunOnce'] -ne 'ok' -or $audit['RunOnceEx'] -ne 'ok' -or $audit['Run'] -ne 'ok') {
        try {
            Set-RunOnceAutostart -Reason ("trigger-audit-{0}" -f $Context)
            $repaired.Add('RunOnce/RunOnceEx/Run')
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Test-FbAndRepairTriggers: Set-RunOnceAutostart threw: {0}." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
    }

    # Repair Layer 4. Always run: 5.4.13 /Delete of live TNs left the
    # next boot with no loop/splash tasks. Repair is additive (no
    # delete) and registers Boot2 + AnyLogon siblings.
    $needSchedRepair = $true
    if ($needSchedRepair) {
        try {
            $repairedTasks = Repair-FbScheduledTasks -Context $Context
            if ($repairedTasks) { $repaired.Add('SchedTasks') }
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Test-FbAndRepairTriggers: Repair-FbScheduledTasks threw: {0}." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
    }

    $auditStr = (($audit.Keys | ForEach-Object { ('{0}={1}' -f $_, $audit[$_]) }) -join ' ')
    if ($repaired.Count -gt 0) {
        $msg = ("Test-FbAndRepairTriggers ({0}): {1}; repaired={2}" -f $Context, $auditStr, ($repaired -join ','))
    } else {
        $msg = ("Test-FbAndRepairTriggers ({0}): {1}; all triggers present, no repair needed." -f $Context, $auditStr)
    }
    Write-FbLog $msg 'INFO'
    return $msg
}

function Write-FbKickstartLauncher {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        (Re)write $FbRoot\FirstBaseWuLoopRun.cmd kickstart launcher.
    .DESCRIPTION
        Companion to the Run-key fallback (Layer 3). The launcher does
        two things on every invocation:
          1. If $CompleteMarker exists, the loop is done; delete the
             Run-key entry (so we don't keep flashing a console on
             every OOBE logon) and exit 0.
          2. Otherwise, launch the same powershell.exe command line
             that RunOnce / RunOnceEx / scheduled tasks fire.

        The batch is idempotent and safe to overwrite on every
        Test-FbAndRepairTriggers call. We do NOT emit it as a literal
        string in this PowerShell file - we build it programmatically
        so the embedded PowerShell command line stays in sync with
        Get-LoopAutostartCommand (single source of truth).
    #>
    $launcherBat = Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseWuLoopRun.cmd'
    if (-not (Test-Path $FbRoot)) { New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $FbRoot -Force | Out-Null }

    $autostartCmd = Get-LoopAutostartCommand

    $sb = New-Object System.Text.StringBuilder
    [void]$sb.AppendLine('@echo off')
    [void]$sb.AppendLine(':: FirstBase Windows Update kickstart launcher')
    [void]$sb.AppendLine(':: Auto-generated by Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1 / Write-FbKickstartLauncher.')
    [void]$sb.AppendLine(':: Fired by HKLM\...\Run\FirstBaseWuLoopRun on every interactive logon.')
    [void]$sb.AppendLine('setlocal EnableExtensions')
    [void]$sb.AppendLine('')
    [void]$sb.AppendLine(('set "FB_COMPLETE_MARKER=' + $CompleteMarker + '"'))
    [void]$sb.AppendLine('if exist "%FB_COMPLETE_MARKER%" (')
    [void]$sb.AppendLine('    reg delete "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run" /v "FirstBaseWuLoopRun" /f >nul 2>&1')
    [void]$sb.AppendLine('    endlocal')
    [void]$sb.AppendLine('    exit /b 0')
    [void]$sb.AppendLine(')')
    [void]$sb.AppendLine('')
    [void]$sb.AppendLine('start "" ' + $autostartCmd)
    [void]$sb.AppendLine('endlocal')
    [void]$sb.AppendLine('exit /b 0')

    $body = $sb.ToString()
    Set-Content -Path $launcherBat -Value $body -Encoding ASCII -Force
    return $launcherBat
}

function Repair-FbScheduledTasks {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Re-create FirstBase ONSTART + ONLOGON tasks with robust settings.
    .DESCRIPTION
        5.4.14: NEVER schtasks /Delete of live TNs. Dumps at 12:09 showed
        /Delete then Start-Process /Create (quoted powershell /TR) returned
        -2147467259 and left WindowsUpdateLoop / OnLogon / SplashWatcher
        absent after the WU reboot. Create via native schtasks.exe with
        FirstBaseLoopLauncher.cmd (same /TR as SetupComplete). Also register
        sibling Boot2 (ONSTART DELAY 20s SYSTEM) and AnyLogon (ONLOGON
        DELAY 45s SYSTEM, no /IT) so CloudExperienceHost can start the loop
        without replacing the in-flight task.

        Returns $true if at least one task was successfully (re)created,
        $false if all attempts failed.
    #>
    param([string]$Context = 'repair')

    $okCount = 0
    $psExe = Join-Path $env:WINDIR 'System32\WindowsPowerShell\v1.0\powershell.exe'
    if (-not (Test-Path $psExe)) { $psExe = 'powershell.exe' }
    $loopLauncher = Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseLoopLauncher.cmd'
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $loopLauncher)) {
        try {
            $loopPs = Join-Path $FbRoot 'Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1'
            $shim = @(
                '@echo off',
                'setlocal EnableExtensions',
                ('"{0}" -NoProfile -NonInteractive -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -WindowStyle Hidden -File "{1}" -FinalizeToOobe' -f $psExe, $loopPs),
                'endlocal',
                'exit /b %ERRORLEVEL%'
            )
            Set-Content -LiteralPath $loopLauncher -Value $shim -Encoding ASCII -Force
            Write-FbLog ("Repair-FbScheduledTasks ({0}): wrote missing loop launcher {1}." -f $Context, $loopLauncher) 'INFO'
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Repair-FbScheduledTasks ({0}): failed to write loop launcher: {1}." -f $Context, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
    }

    $createOne = {
        param([string]$Tn, [string[]]$CreateArgs, [string]$OkMsg)
        try {
            $null = & schtasks.exe @CreateArgs 2>&1
            $code = 0
            if ($null -ne $LASTEXITCODE) { $code = [int]$LASTEXITCODE }
            if ($code -eq 0) {
                Write-FbLog ("Repair-FbScheduledTasks ({0}): {1}" -f $Context, $OkMsg) 'INFO'
                try { $null = & schtasks.exe /Change /TN $Tn /ENABLE 2>&1 } catch {}
                Set-FbScheduledTaskRobustSettings -TaskName $Tn | Out-Null
                return $true
            }
            Write-FbLog ("Repair-FbScheduledTasks ({0}): schtasks /Create {1} returned {2}." -f $Context, $Tn, $code) 'WARN'
            return $false
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Repair-FbScheduledTasks ({0}): {1} re-create threw: {2}." -f $Context, $Tn, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
            return $false
        }
    }

    # Original TNs: /Create /F only (no /Delete). May fail while we are the running instance.
    $a1 = @('/Create','/TN',$TaskName,'/SC','ONSTART','/DELAY','0000:05','/RU','SYSTEM','/RL','HIGHEST','/F','/TR',$loopLauncher)
    if (& $createOne $TaskName $a1 ("re-created {0} (ONSTART/SYSTEM/HIGHEST/DELAY 5s, no /IT, launcher cmd)." -f $TaskName)) { $okCount++ }
    $a2 = @('/Create','/TN',$LogonTaskName,'/SC','ONLOGON','/RU','BUILTIN\Administrators','/RL','HIGHEST','/F','/TR',$loopLauncher)
    if (& $createOne $LogonTaskName $a2 ("re-created {0} (ONLOGON/Administrators/HIGHEST, launcher cmd)." -f $LogonTaskName)) { $okCount++ }

    # Sibling TNs are not running; create these even when originals fail.
    $a3 = @('/Create','/TN',$Boot2TaskName,'/SC','ONSTART','/DELAY','0000:20','/RU','SYSTEM','/RL','HIGHEST','/F','/TR',$loopLauncher)
    if (& $createOne $Boot2TaskName $a3 ("registered {0} (ONSTART/SYSTEM/HIGHEST/DELAY 20s, no /IT)." -f $Boot2TaskName)) { $okCount++ }
    $a4 = @('/Create','/TN',$AnyLogonTaskName,'/SC','ONLOGON','/DELAY','0000:45','/RU','SYSTEM','/RL','HIGHEST','/F','/TR',$loopLauncher)
    if (& $createOne $AnyLogonTaskName $a4 ("registered {0} (ONLOGON/SYSTEM/HIGHEST/DELAY 45s, no /IT)." -f $AnyLogonTaskName)) { $okCount++ }

    $splashWatcherLocal = 'FirstBase\SplashWatcher'
    $splashWatcherPs = Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseSplashWatcher.ps1'
    if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $splashWatcherPs) {
        $splashTr = ($psExe + ' -NoProfile -NonInteractive -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -WindowStyle Hidden -File "' + $splashWatcherPs + '"')
        $a5 = @('/Create','/TN',$splashWatcherLocal,'/SC','ONSTART','/DELAY','0000:05','/RU','SYSTEM','/RL','HIGHEST','/F','/TR',$splashTr)
        if (& $createOne $splashWatcherLocal $a5 ("re-created {0} (ONSTART/SYSTEM/HIGHEST/DELAY 5s, no /IT)." -f $splashWatcherLocal)) { $okCount++ }
        $a6 = @('/Create','/TN',$SplashWatcherBoot2Name,'/SC','ONSTART','/DELAY','0000:20','/RU','SYSTEM','/RL','HIGHEST','/F','/TR',$splashTr)
        if (& $createOne $SplashWatcherBoot2Name $a6 ("registered {0} (ONSTART/SYSTEM/HIGHEST/DELAY 20s, no /IT)." -f $SplashWatcherBoot2Name)) { $okCount++ }
    } else {
        Write-FbLog ("Repair-FbScheduledTasks ({0}): SplashWatcher script missing at {1}; skipping watcher tasks." -f $Context, $splashWatcherPs) 'WARN'
    }

    return ($okCount -gt 0)
}

function Set-FbScheduledTaskRobustSettings {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Apply StartWhenAvailable + IgnoreNew + no-time-limit + battery
        permissive settings to a FirstBase scheduled task.
    .DESCRIPTION
        schtasks.exe /Create cannot directly set StartWhenAvailable
        (the "If the task fails, restart every:" tab calls it "If the
        scheduled start is missed, run the task as soon as possible").
        Without this, an ONSTART task whose trigger time was missed
        (e.g. the system was busy or in early-boot when the trigger
        fired) does NOT run when the system catches up. This was the
        Hypothesis B failure on 2026-05-12.

        We use Set-ScheduledTask because the New-ScheduledTaskSettingsSet
        cmdlet was the cleanest way to set all five settings in one shot.

        2026-05-13.1700-no-interactive-prompts: rewritten to AVOID the
        Get-ScheduledTask | Set-ScheduledTask pipeline. When upstream
        Get-ScheduledTask yields nothing (task not yet visible to the
        CIM provider), the downstream Set-ScheduledTask is bound to an
        empty pipeline and its mandatory -TaskName parameter triggers
        a PowerShell prompt to STDIN. On a console-attached session
        (e.g. SetupComplete during specialize-pass) that prompt is
        visible to the user as "TaskName:" and stalls OOBE. The new
        implementation:
          1. Validates $TaskName is non-null/non-empty.
          2. Uses String.LastIndexOf('\') + Substring() to parse the
             Folder\Leaf shape - no regex, no array indexing.
          3. Calls Get-ScheduledTask with -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
             and explicitly null-checks the result.
          4. If the task is missing, logs WARN and returns $false
             instead of throwing into a Set-ScheduledTask pipeline.
          5. Calls Set-ScheduledTask with explicit -TaskPath / -TaskName
             parameters (no pipeline binding). Even if Set throws, the
             error is caught and logged; no prompt can fire.

        Always logs the attempt and the resulting setting state. Errors
        are non-fatal; the task is already created at this point so the
        worst case is an ONSTART task that may miss its trigger.
    #>
    param([Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$TaskName)
    if ([string]::IsNullOrEmpty($TaskName)) {
        Write-FbLog 'Set-FbScheduledTaskRobustSettings: empty TaskName passed; skipping.' 'WARN'
        return $false
    }
    try {
        # Parse 'Folder\Leaf' into -TaskPath '\Folder\' and -TaskName 'Leaf'
        # via String.LastIndexOf + Substring (no regex, no array indexing).
        $sep = $TaskName.LastIndexOf([char]92)
        if ($sep -lt 0) {
            $taskPath = [string][char]92
            $taskLeaf = $TaskName
        } else {
            $taskPath = [char]92 + $TaskName.Substring(0, $sep) + [char]92
            $taskLeaf = $TaskName.Substring($sep + 1)
        }
        if ([string]::IsNullOrEmpty($taskLeaf)) {
            Write-FbLog ("Set-FbScheduledTaskRobustSettings: empty leaf parsed from '{0}'; skipping." -f $TaskName) 'WARN'
            return $false
        }
        $existing = Get-ScheduledTask -TaskPath $taskPath -TaskName $taskLeaf -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        if ($null -eq $existing) {
            Write-FbLog ("Set-FbScheduledTaskRobustSettings: task not found (TaskPath='{0}' TaskName='{1}'); schtasks-only settings remain in effect (no StartWhenAvailable)." -f $taskPath, $taskLeaf) 'WARN'
            return $false
        }
        $settings = New-ScheduledTaskSettingsSet `
            -StartWhenAvailable `
            -DontStopIfGoingOnBatteries `
            -AllowStartIfOnBatteries `
            -ExecutionTimeLimit (New-TimeSpan -Hours 0) `
            -MultipleInstances IgnoreNew
        Set-ScheduledTask -TaskPath $taskPath -TaskName $taskLeaf -Settings $settings -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null
        Write-FbLog ("Set-FbScheduledTaskRobustSettings: {0} settings applied (StartWhenAvailable=true, MultipleInstances=IgnoreNew, ExecutionTimeLimit=0, battery-permissive)." -f $TaskName) 'INFO'
        return $true
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Set-FbScheduledTaskRobustSettings: {0} threw: {1}; the schtasks-only settings remain in effect (no StartWhenAvailable)." -f $TaskName, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        return $false
    }
}

function Write-StuckDiag {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        One-shot panic dump when an UpdateID crosses the retry ceiling.
    .DESCRIPTION
        Writes C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\Logs\STUCK-DIAG-<timestamp>.txt
        containing everything a human needs to diagnose the stuck update
        without leaving the locked-down splash UI:

          - UpdateID, Title, last ResultCode, last HResult (decimal AND hex)
          - Categories assigned by the engine
          - Full current wu-status.json snapshot
          - Tail of the active WU-*.log
          - Loop heartbeat + script version

        Idempotent per (UpdateID, log timestamp) so a single update that
        keeps tripping the ceiling does not litter the logs folder.
    #>
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$UpdateId,
        [string]$Title,
        [int]$ResultCode = -1,
        [int]$HResult    = 0,
        [string[]]$Categories = @(),
        [string]$Reason = ''
    )

    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path $LogDir)) { New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $LogDir -Force | Out-Null }
        $stamp = Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd-HHmmss'
        $safeId = ($UpdateId -replace '[^0-9A-Fa-f-]','_')
        $diagPath = Join-Path $LogDir ("STUCK-DIAG-{0}-{1}.txt" -f $stamp, $safeId)

        $hex = Format-Hex32 -Value $HResult

        $statusSnapshot = ''
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StatusFile) {
                $statusSnapshot = Get-Content -Path $StatusFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            }
        } catch {}

        $logTail = ''
        try {
            $logTail = Get-Content -Path $LogPath -Tail 50 -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-String
        } catch {}

        $heartbeat = ''
        try {
            if (Test-Path $HeartbeatFile) {
                $heartbeat = Get-Content -Path $HeartbeatFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            }
        } catch {}

        $sb = New-Object System.Text.StringBuilder
        [void]$sb.AppendLine("FirstBase Windows Update - STUCK DIAGNOSTIC DUMP")
        [void]$sb.AppendLine(("Generated:    {0}" -f (Get-Date).ToString('o')))
        [void]$sb.AppendLine(("ScriptVer:    {0}" -f $ScriptVersion))
        [void]$sb.AppendLine(("ProcessId:    {0}" -f $PID))
        [void]$sb.AppendLine(("Reason:       {0}" -f $Reason))
        [void]$sb.AppendLine('')
        [void]$sb.AppendLine("== Update ==")
        [void]$sb.AppendLine(("UpdateID:     {0}" -f $UpdateId))
        [void]$sb.AppendLine(("Title:        {0}" -f $Title))
        [void]$sb.AppendLine(("ResultCode:   {0}" -f $ResultCode))
        [void]$sb.AppendLine(("HResult:      {0} ({1})" -f $HResult, $hex))
        [void]$sb.AppendLine(("Categories:   {0}" -f (($Categories) -join ', ')))
        [void]$sb.AppendLine('')
        [void]$sb.AppendLine("== Heartbeat ==")
        [void]$sb.AppendLine($heartbeat)
        [void]$sb.AppendLine('')
        [void]$sb.AppendLine("== wu-status.json snapshot ==")
        [void]$sb.AppendLine($statusSnapshot)
        [void]$sb.AppendLine('')
        [void]$sb.AppendLine("== Last 50 log lines ==")
        [void]$sb.AppendLine($logTail)

        Set-Content -Path $diagPath -Value ($sb.ToString()) -Encoding UTF8 -Force
        Write-FbLog ("Wrote stuck diagnostic dump: {0}" -f $diagPath) 'WARN'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Failed to write stuck diagnostic dump for {0}: {1}" -f $UpdateId, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

function Hide-FbOfferedUpdate {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Absolute escape hatch: force-hide an offered update via native COM.
    .DESCRIPTION
        Sets IUpdate.IsHidden=$true on a specific UpdateID. Once hidden,
        WUA will no longer offer it in subsequent IsHidden=0 scans, so
        the per-device convergence loop can finally exit even when WUA
        keeps re-publishing a superseded or unapplicable update across
        reboots.

        Dispatched inside Start-Job for the same hard-timeout discipline
        as the rest of the engine - a hung COM call cannot wedge the
        outer loop. Returns $true on success, $false on any failure
        (logged; caller continues regardless).
    #>
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$UpdateId,
        [int]$TimeoutSeconds = 120
    )

    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($UpdateId)) { return $false }

    $job = Start-Job -ScriptBlock {
        param($targetId, $muServiceId)
        $ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'

        $svcManager = New-Object -ComObject Microsoft.Update.ServiceManager
        try { $null = $svcManager.AddService2($muServiceId, 7, '') } catch {}

        $session = New-Object -ComObject Microsoft.Update.Session
        $searcher = $session.CreateUpdateSearcher()
        $searcher.Online = $true
        try {
            $searcher.ServerSelection = 3
            $searcher.ServiceID = $muServiceId
        } catch {
            $searcher.ServerSelection = 2
        }

        $result = $searcher.Search("IsInstalled=0 and IsHidden=0")
        for ($i = 0; $i -lt $result.Updates.Count; $i++) {
            $u = $result.Updates.Item($i)
            if ([string]$u.Identity.UpdateID -eq $targetId) {
                $u.IsHidden = $true
                return $true
            }
        }
        return $false
    } -ArgumentList $UpdateId, '7971f918-a847-4430-9279-4a52d1efe18d'

    if (-not (Wait-Job -Job $job -Timeout $TimeoutSeconds)) {
        Stop-Job -Job $job -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
        Remove-Job -Job $job -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
        Write-FbLog ("Force-hide of UpdateID {0} timed out after {1}s." -f $UpdateId, $TimeoutSeconds) 'WARN'
        return $false
    }

    try {
        $ok = [bool](Receive-Job -Job $job -ErrorAction Stop)
        if ($ok) {
            Write-FbLog ("Force-hid UpdateID {0} via IUpdate.IsHidden=true (escape hatch)." -f $UpdateId) 'WARN'
        } else {
            Write-FbLog ("Force-hide of UpdateID {0} returned false (no longer offered or not found)." -f $UpdateId) 'INFO'
        }
        return $ok
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Force-hide of UpdateID {0} failed: {1}" -f $UpdateId, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        return $false
    } finally {
        Remove-Job -Job $job -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
    }
}

function Write-StuckRebootDiag {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Panic dump when ALL 8 reboot stages AND the logoff fallback
        return without rebooting / logging off.
    .DESCRIPTION
        The cascade has fired (stage 0) Wait-FbReadyForReboot, (1)
        shutdown.exe /r, (2) Restart-Computer, (3) ExitWindowsEx,
        (4) InitiateSystemShutdownEx, (5) NtShutdownSystem, (6) wmic,
        (7) hard-kill-blockers + retry shutdown.exe, (8) bcdedit
        bootstatuspolicy + shutdown.exe, AND the (9) logoff fallback
        (ExitWindowsEx EWX_LOGOFF | EWX_FORCE / shutdown /l). None of
        them actually took the box down or logged the user off. This
        is almost certainly a firmware or driver problem we cannot
        fix from PowerShell. Write a STUCK-DIAG-reboot-<timestamp>.txt
        with everything we know so an operator who power-cycles the
        machine and grabs the logs has a chance of diagnosing it.
    #>
    param([string]$Reason)
    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path $LogDir)) { New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $LogDir -Force | Out-Null }
        $stamp = Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd-HHmmss'
        $diagPath = Join-Path $LogDir ("STUCK-DIAG-reboot-{0}.txt" -f $stamp)

        $statusSnapshot = ''
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StatusFile) {
                $statusSnapshot = Get-Content -Path $StatusFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            }
        } catch {}

        $stateSnapshot = ''
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $stateSnapshot = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            }
        } catch {}

        $heartbeat = ''
        try {
            if (Test-Path $HeartbeatFile) {
                $heartbeat = Get-Content -Path $HeartbeatFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            }
        } catch {}

        $logTail = ''
        try {
            $logTail = Get-Content -Path $LogPath -Tail 100 -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-String
        } catch {}

        $sb = New-Object System.Text.StringBuilder
        [void]$sb.AppendLine('FirstBase Windows Update - STUCK REBOOT DIAGNOSTIC DUMP')
        [void]$sb.AppendLine(("Generated:    {0}" -f (Get-Date).ToString('o')))
        [void]$sb.AppendLine(("ScriptVer:    {0}" -f $ScriptVersion))
        [void]$sb.AppendLine(("ProcessId:    {0}" -f $PID))
        [void]$sb.AppendLine(("Reason:       {0}" -f $Reason))
        [void]$sb.AppendLine('All 8 reboot stages (settle wait, shutdown.exe, Restart-Computer,')
        [void]$sb.AppendLine('ExitWindowsEx, InitiateSystemShutdownEx, NtShutdownSystem, wmic os reboot,')
        [void]$sb.AppendLine('hard-kill blockers + retry, bcdedit IgnoreAllFailures + retry) AND the')
        [void]$sb.AppendLine('logoff fallback (ExitWindowsEx EWX_LOGOFF) returned without restarting or')
        [void]$sb.AppendLine('logging off the device. Hardware power cycle is required.')
        [void]$sb.AppendLine('')
        [void]$sb.AppendLine('== Heartbeat ==')
        [void]$sb.AppendLine($heartbeat)
        [void]$sb.AppendLine('')
        [void]$sb.AppendLine('== wu-status.json snapshot ==')
        [void]$sb.AppendLine($statusSnapshot)
        [void]$sb.AppendLine('')
        [void]$sb.AppendLine('== wu-state.json snapshot ==')
        [void]$sb.AppendLine($stateSnapshot)
        [void]$sb.AppendLine('')
        [void]$sb.AppendLine('== Last 100 log lines ==')
        [void]$sb.AppendLine($logTail)

        Set-Content -Path $diagPath -Value ($sb.ToString()) -Encoding UTF8 -Force
        Write-FbLog ("Wrote stuck-reboot diagnostic dump: {0}" -f $diagPath) 'ERROR'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Failed to write stuck-reboot diag: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REMOVED 2026.05.12.1030-no-cascade-final
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The 2026-05-11 1045 cascade hardening helpers (Invoke-FbStageWithTimeout,
# the 3-minute CascadeStartTime budget, the Start-Job per-stage timeout
# wrapper) lived here. They have been removed along with the 8-stage
# cascade itself. No caller in the new flow needs a job-scoped reboot
# timeout because Invoke-FbReboot only fires ONE shell-out shutdown.exe
# at most, and the legacy native reboot APIs (ExitWindowsEx /
# InitiateSystemShutdownExW / NtShutdownSystem / wmic) are no longer
# invoked at all. Restored line budget: ~120 lines.

function Test-FbPendingRebootRegistry {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Inspect the canonical "reboot pending" registry locations and
        return which ones still indicate work-in-progress.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026-05-11 1015 field issue: process-idle alone is NOT enough
        to declare TrustedInstaller settled. The OS-level shutdown
        block survives idle workers - the block reason API is read
        from CBS / WindowsUpdate registry state, not from running
        processes. Stage 0's settle wait was returning successfully
        (workers idle) and then Stage 1/2/3 still got vetoed.

        Probe each documented location:
          - HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Component
            Based Servicing\PendingRequired (any value present)
          - HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Component
            Based Servicing\RebootInProgress (any value present)
          - HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Component
            Based Servicing\PackagesPending (any subkey)
          - HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session Manager\
            PendingFileRenameOperations (any value)
          - HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Windows-
            Update\Auto Update\RebootRequired (any value)

        Returns a string[] of short labels for the keys that are
        currently "set" (value/subkey present and non-empty). Empty
        array means all clear. Never throws; treats unreadable keys
        as "not set" so a transient ACL error does not falsely
        block the cascade.
    #>
    $blockers = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[string]

    # 1. CBS\PendingRequired - any value under the key indicates a
    # CBS pending operation. We treat ANY value name (other than the
    # default '(Default)') as a blocker.
    try {
        $cbsPath = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Component Based Servicing\PendingRequired'
        if (Test-Path $cbsPath) {
            $vals = Get-Item -Path $cbsPath -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            if ($vals -and $vals.Property -and $vals.Property.Count -gt 0) {
                $blockers.Add('CBS PendingRequired') | Out-Null
            }
        }
    } catch {}

    # 2. CBS\RebootInProgress - mere presence of the key with any
    # value is a strong signal CBS hasn't released the reboot block.
    try {
        $rip = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Component Based Servicing\RebootInProgress'
        if (Test-Path $rip) {
            $vals = Get-Item -Path $rip -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            if ($vals -and $vals.Property -and $vals.Property.Count -gt 0) {
                $blockers.Add('CBS RebootInProgress') | Out-Null
            }
        }
    } catch {}

    # 3. CBS\PackagesPending - presence of ANY subkey here is the
    # canonical "CBS still has staged packages" signal.
    try {
        $pp = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Component Based Servicing\PackagesPending'
        if (Test-Path $pp) {
            $children = @(Get-ChildItem -Path $pp -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)
            if ($children.Count -gt 0) {
                $blockers.Add('CBS PackagesPending') | Out-Null
            }
        }
    } catch {}

    # 4. Session Manager\PendingFileRenameOperations - non-empty
    # multi-string => CBS / installer is mid-finalize.
    try {
        $smKey = 'HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session Manager'
        $pfro = (Get-ItemProperty -Path $smKey -Name 'PendingFileRenameOperations' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).PendingFileRenameOperations
        if ($pfro -and (@($pfro) | Where-Object { $_ }).Count -gt 0) {
            $blockers.Add('PendingFileRenameOperations') | Out-Null
        }
    } catch {}

    # 5. Windows Update\Auto Update\RebootRequired - the WU side of
    # the same flag. Presence of any value (the key is normally
    # absent when no reboot is required) means WU thinks a reboot
    # is still owed.
    try {
        $rr = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\WindowsUpdate\Auto Update\RebootRequired'
        if (Test-Path $rr) {
            $vals = Get-Item -Path $rr -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            # Some Windows versions create the key with no values as
            # a marker. Treat the key's mere existence as a blocker
            # (true to original semantics).
            $blockers.Add('WU RebootRequired') | Out-Null
        }
    } catch {}

    return ,@($blockers.ToArray())
}

function Stop-FbBlockerProcessesAggressive {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Tree-kill every known TrustedInstaller / WUA worker via
        taskkill /F /IM <name> /T.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026-05-11 1045 cascade Stage 4: the original Stage 7 used
        Stop-Process which kills only the named PID. taskkill /T
        also walks the child process tree, so a TiWorker that
        respawned a child between our Get-Process snapshot and our
        Stop-Process call still gets cleaned up.

        Targets (expanded from prior build):
          TrustedInstaller.exe   - the service process holding the
                                   shutdown block.
          TiWorker.exe           - CBS worker; can spawn new copies.
          wuauclt.exe            - legacy WU client.
          MoUsoCoreWorker.exe    - modern WU orchestrator.
          MusNotification.exe    - WU notification surface.
          dismhost.exe           - DISM helper that holds locks
                                   during finalization.
          CompatTelRunner.exe    - telemetry; can interfere with
                                   shutdown sequencing.

        Never throws; per-process failures are logged at WARN.
    #>
    $targets = @(
        'TrustedInstaller.exe',
        'TiWorker.exe',
        'wuauclt.exe',
        'MoUsoCoreWorker.exe',
        'MusNotification.exe',
        'dismhost.exe',
        'CompatTelRunner.exe'
    )
    $taskkill = Join-Path $env:WINDIR 'System32\taskkill.exe'
    if (-not (Test-Path $taskkill)) { $taskkill = 'taskkill.exe' }

    foreach ($t in $targets) {
        try {
            $p = Start-Process -FilePath $taskkill -ArgumentList @('/F','/IM',$t,'/T') -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru -Wait -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            if ($p) {
                # taskkill returns 128 when no matching process is
                # found; that's expected for most targets and is
                # logged at INFO not WARN to keep the log clean.
                if ($p.ExitCode -eq 0) {
                    Write-FbLog ("taskkill /F /IM {0} /T succeeded (exit 0)." -f $t) 'WARN'
                } else {
                    Write-FbLog ("taskkill /F /IM {0} /T exit code {1} (128 = no such process)." -f $t, $p.ExitCode) 'INFO'
                }
            }
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("taskkill /F /IM {0} /T threw: {1}" -f $t, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
    }

    # Belt + suspenders: Stop-Service the TrustedInstaller SCM entry
    # so SCM doesn't immediately respawn it on the next WUA touch.
    try {
        Stop-Service -Name 'TrustedInstaller' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
    } catch {}
}

function Clear-FbPendingRebootRegistryValues {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Delete the post-process-kill survivors: WU RebootRequired
        value and PendingFileRenameOperations value.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026-05-11 1045 Stage 4: even after taskkill /F /IM
        TrustedInstaller /T, two registry-side survivors continue
        to register the OS-level shutdown block:

          - HKLM\...\WindowsUpdate\Auto Update\RebootRequired
          - HKLM\...\Session Manager\PendingFileRenameOperations

        Delete the VALUES (not the keys). We deliberately do NOT
        touch CBS\PendingRequired / RebootInProgress / Packages-
        Pending because corrupting CBS state would break future
        updates - we only want to release the reboot block, not
        nuke CBS.

        Safe to call repeatedly; missing values are silently
        tolerated. Never throws.
    #>
    # 1. Auto Update\RebootRequired - the WU-side flag. Removing it
    # tells WUA "no reboot owed" so the next API call doesn't
    # re-block. Delete the value, leave the key (the key is part of
    # the documented WindowsUpdate hive and recreating it is fine).
    try {
        $rrKey = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\WindowsUpdate\Auto Update\RebootRequired'
        if (Test-Path $rrKey) {
            $item = Get-Item -Path $rrKey -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            if ($item -and $item.Property) {
                foreach ($v in @($item.Property)) {
                    try {
                        Remove-ItemProperty -Path $rrKey -Name $v -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                        Write-FbLog ("Cleared WU RebootRequired value '{0}'." -f $v) 'WARN'
                    } catch {
                        Write-FbLog ("Failed to clear WU RebootRequired value '{0}': {1}" -f $v, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
                    }
                }
            }
            # If the key has no values it is sometimes used purely
            # as a marker; leave the key in place so we don't
            # gratuitously diff the hive.
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Clearing WU RebootRequired failed: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # 2. Session Manager\PendingFileRenameOperations - the install-
    # side flag. SetupComplete and SfC routinely write benign entries
    # here, but anything still in flight is a CBS/installer rename
    # the system thinks it owes the next boot. Removing it does NOT
    # affect already-applied changes; it only cancels queued moves.
    try {
        $smKey = 'HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session Manager'
        $existing = (Get-ItemProperty -Path $smKey -Name 'PendingFileRenameOperations' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).PendingFileRenameOperations
        if ($existing) {
            try {
                Remove-ItemProperty -Path $smKey -Name 'PendingFileRenameOperations' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                Write-FbLog 'Cleared Session Manager\PendingFileRenameOperations.' 'WARN'
            } catch {
                Write-FbLog ("Failed to clear PendingFileRenameOperations: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
            }
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Clearing PendingFileRenameOperations failed: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REMOVED 2026.05.12.1030-no-cascade-final
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Wait-FbReadyForReboot (Stage 0 settle wait) and Clear-FbShutdownBlockers
# (pre-stage shutdown.exe /a hammer + UI-prompt kill + TrustedInstaller
# stop) were originally called from the 8-stage cascade body before each
# native reboot API. Removed because Invoke-FbReboot does not invoke any
# of those native APIs anymore - it just fires a single shutdown.exe
# (only on the rare pre-install path) or routes to OOBE handoff. The
# helpers were unused outside the cascade. Restored line budget: ~250
# lines.
#
# A no-op shim is left ONLY for the function signatures so any future
# refactor that accidentally re-introduces a caller will fail loudly at
# parse time rather than silently wedging:

function Disable-FbRecallFeatureOnce {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Disable Windows Recall via DISM once per boot, before the first WU scan.
    .DESCRIPTION
        5.4.18: CBS FoD / Recall disable must not run between serialized
        driver Install() calls. Do this before the first scan (or after a
        reboot), never mid-Driver. Does not stop TrustedInstaller.
    #>
    if ($script:FbRecallDisableAttemptedThisBoot) { return }
    $script:FbRecallDisableAttemptedThisBoot = $true
    $dismExe = Join-Path $env:WINDIR 'System32\Dism.exe'
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $dismExe)) { $dismExe = 'dism.exe' }
    Write-FbLog 'Recall DISM: Disable-Feature Recall before first WU scan (timeout 120s). Not used mid-Driver.' 'INFO'
    $argSets = @(
        @('/Online', '/Disable-Feature', '/FeatureName:Recall', '/NoRestart', '/Quiet')
    )
    foreach ($dismArgs in $argSets) {
        $p = $null
        try {
            $p = Start-Process -FilePath $dismExe -ArgumentList $dismArgs -PassThru -WindowStyle Hidden -ErrorAction Stop
            if (-not $p.WaitForExit(120000)) {
                try { $p.Kill() } catch {}
                Write-FbLog 'Recall DISM: timed out after 120s; continuing to WU scan. TrustedInstaller not stopped.' 'WARN'
                continue
            }
            Write-FbLog ("Recall DISM: FeatureName=Recall exit={0}" -f [int]$p.ExitCode) 'INFO'
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Recall DISM: threw {0}; continuing to WU scan." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
    }
}

function Invoke-FbCbsCleanup {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Try to coax CBS into releasing a stuck pending-reboot signal.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress. Field evidence
        from build 1915 showed that after a successful Critical
        bucket install + reboot, Test-FbWuRebootRequired stayed
        $true even on the new boot session (no installs in flight)
        because some CBS pending key was sticky beyond the reboot
        itself. Sysprep refuses with 0x8007139f on this signal and
        Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared exhausted its budget twice
        (the 1915 build used a 300s ceiling; 2200+ uses 600s by
        default) without CBS releasing the lock.

        This helper runs three remediation steps in series and
        re-probes after each one:

          Step 1: dism.exe /online /cleanup-image /startcomponent
                  cleanup /quiet
                  (no /resetbase; that's destructive of WU history)
          Step 2: sfc.exe /scannow
          Step 3: Get-WindowsUpdateLog (writes the consolidated WU
                  log, side-effect = forces CBS to re-evaluate
                  pending state)

        After each step we re-probe Test-FbWuRebootRequired and
        bail early if the signal goes away.

        Returns a hashtable:
          { StillPending : $true|$false
            DismExitCode : <int>
            SfcExitCode  : <int>
            LogPath      : <path or ''>
            Summary      : <one-line summary> }

        Never throws. Each step is best-effort; a tool not present
        on the box (e.g. SFC missing) just gets a -1 exit code and
        the helper continues.

        Bounded runtime: DISM cleanup + SFC each have their own
        idle/timeout behavior. We pass /quiet to DISM and don't
        pass any timeout flag to SFC; in practice both run between
        30s and 6 minutes total. Worst-case pad is therefore well
        under the 600s Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared budget.
    #>
    param(
        [string]$Context = 'STAGE 2 pre-sysprep'
    )

    if ($script:FbWuDriverApplyActive) {
        Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbCbsCleanup skipped during Driver WUA apply (Context={0}). DISM/CBS FoD work must not interleave with serialized driver Install()." -f $Context) 'WARN'
        return @{
            StillPending  = $true
            DismExitCode  = -1
            SfcExitCode   = -1
            GetWulogOk    = $false
            LogPath       = ''
            Summary       = 'skipped-during-driver-apply'
        }
    }

    $result = @{
        StillPending  = $true
        DismExitCode  = -1
        SfcExitCode   = -1
        GetWulogOk    = $false
        LogPath       = ''
        Summary       = ''
    }

    $stamp = (Get-Date).ToString('yyyyMMdd-HHmmss')
    $cbsLogDir = Join-Path $LogDir 'CbsCleanup'
    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $cbsLogDir)) {
            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $cbsLogDir -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
        }
    } catch {}
    $logPath = Join-Path $cbsLogDir ("CbsCleanup-{0}.log" -f $stamp)
    $result.LogPath = $logPath

    $writeStep = {
        param([string]$Msg)
        try {
            $line = ("[{0}] [{1}] {2}" -f (Get-Date -Format 'HH:mm:ss'), $Context, $Msg)
            Add-Content -Path $logPath -Value $line -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        } catch {}
        try { Write-FbLog ("Invoke-FbCbsCleanup: " + $Msg) 'INFO' } catch {}
    }

    & $writeStep ("Starting CBS cleanup (Context={0})." -f $Context)

    try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'cbs-cleanup' -Message ('CBS cleanup pre-pass: DISM /startcomponentcleanup running...') } catch {
        try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message 'CBS cleanup pre-pass: DISM /startcomponentcleanup running...' } catch {}
    }

    $dismExe = Join-Path $env:WINDIR 'System32\Dism.exe'
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $dismExe)) { $dismExe = 'dism.exe' }
    try {
        $dismProc = Start-Process -FilePath $dismExe `
            -ArgumentList @('/online','/cleanup-image','/startcomponentcleanup','/quiet') `
            -PassThru -Wait -NoNewWindow -ErrorAction Stop
        $result.DismExitCode = [int]$dismProc.ExitCode
        & $writeStep ("DISM /startcomponentcleanup exit={0}." -f $result.DismExitCode)
    } catch {
        & $writeStep ("DISM launch threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message)
    }

    try {
        if (-not (Test-FbWuRebootRequired)) {
            $result.StillPending = $false
            $result.Summary = ('DISM cleared CBS (DISM exit={0})' -f $result.DismExitCode)
            & $writeStep $result.Summary
            return $result
        }
    } catch {}

    try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'cbs-cleanup' -Message ('CBS cleanup pre-pass: sfc /scannow running...') } catch {
        try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message 'CBS cleanup pre-pass: sfc /scannow running...' } catch {}
    }
    $sfcExe = Join-Path $env:WINDIR 'System32\sfc.exe'
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $sfcExe)) { $sfcExe = 'sfc.exe' }
    try {
        $sfcProc = Start-Process -FilePath $sfcExe -ArgumentList @('/scannow') `
            -PassThru -Wait -NoNewWindow -ErrorAction Stop
        $result.SfcExitCode = [int]$sfcProc.ExitCode
        & $writeStep ("sfc /scannow exit={0}." -f $result.SfcExitCode)
    } catch {
        & $writeStep ("sfc launch threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message)
    }

    try {
        if (-not (Test-FbWuRebootRequired)) {
            $result.StillPending = $false
            $result.Summary = ('SFC cleared CBS (DISM exit={0}, SFC exit={1})' -f $result.DismExitCode, $result.SfcExitCode)
            & $writeStep $result.Summary
            return $result
        }
    } catch {}

    try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'cbs-cleanup' -Message ('CBS cleanup pre-pass: Get-WindowsUpdateLog (forces CBS pending re-eval)...') } catch {
        try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message 'CBS cleanup pre-pass: Get-WindowsUpdateLog running...' } catch {}
    }
    try {
        if (Get-Command -Name 'Get-WindowsUpdateLog' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue) {
            $wulogTarget = Join-Path $cbsLogDir ("WindowsUpdate-{0}.log" -f $stamp)
            try {
                Get-WindowsUpdateLog -LogPath $wulogTarget -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
                if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $wulogTarget) {
                    $result.GetWulogOk = $true
                    & $writeStep ("Get-WindowsUpdateLog wrote {0}." -f $wulogTarget)
                } else {
                    & $writeStep "Get-WindowsUpdateLog returned without writing the log file (best-effort)."
                }
            } catch {
                & $writeStep ("Get-WindowsUpdateLog threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message)
            }
        } else {
            & $writeStep "Get-WindowsUpdateLog not available on this PowerShell edition; skipping."
        }
    } catch {}

    try {
        if (-not (Test-FbWuRebootRequired)) {
            $result.StillPending = $false
            $result.Summary = ('Get-WindowsUpdateLog cleared CBS (DISM={0}, SFC={1})' -f $result.DismExitCode, $result.SfcExitCode)
            & $writeStep $result.Summary
            return $result
        }
    } catch {}

    $result.StillPending = $true
    $result.Summary = ('CBS still pending after DISM={0} SFC={1} GetWULog={2}' -f $result.DismExitCode, $result.SfcExitCode, $result.GetWulogOk)
    & $writeStep $result.Summary
    return $result
}

function Write-FbCbsStuckDiag {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Write a STUCK-DIAG-CBS-CLEANUP-INEFFECTIVE-*.json dump
        when Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared exhausts its budget after
        Invoke-FbCbsCleanup ran.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress. Captures all
        CBS-related registry keys + the tail of CBS.log so the
        operator can post-mortem WHY the cleanup pre-pass did not
        release the pending-reboot signal.

        Never throws. Best-effort.
    #>
    param(
        [string]$Context = 'STAGE 2 pre-sysprep',
        [int]$CbsPendingBootsCount = 0,
        [int]$WaitedSeconds = 0
    )

    $stamp = (Get-Date).ToString('yyyyMMdd-HHmmss')
    $panicDir = Join-Path $LogDir 'PanicDumps'
    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $panicDir)) {
            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $panicDir -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
        }
    } catch {}
    $diagPath = Join-Path $panicDir ("STUCK-DIAG-CBS-CLEANUP-INEFFECTIVE-{0}.json" -f $stamp)

    # 2026.05.13.2213-tier-watchdog-and-diag-parsefix Critical Fix 5:
    # PowerShell 5.1's parser tokenizes the `try` keyword as a
    # statement, NOT an expression. Using `(try { } catch { })`
    # inside a hashtable value or [pscustomobject] initializer
    # PARSES (Parser::ParseFile returns 0 errors), but at RUNTIME
    # the tokenizer treats the keyword as a command name and
    # throws "The term 'try' is not recognized as the name of a
    # cmdlet". Hoist all such reads OUT of the @{} initializer
    # into pre-declared $vars guarded by their own try/catch
    # statements above the literal.
    $cbsKeys = @{}
    foreach ($k in @(
        'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Component Based Servicing\RebootPending',
        'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Component Based Servicing\PackagesPending',
        'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Component Based Servicing\RebootInProgress',
        'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\WindowsUpdate\Auto Update\RebootRequired',
        'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\WindowsUpdate\Services\Pending'
    )) {
        try {
            $exists = Test-Path -LiteralPath $k
            $valuesStr = ''
            if ($exists) {
                try {
                    $valuesStr = (Get-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $k -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-String)
                } catch {
                    $valuesStr = 'read-error'
                }
            }
            $cbsKeys[$k] = @{
                Exists = $exists
                Values = $valuesStr
            }
        } catch {}
    }

    $cbsLogTail = ''
    $cbsLogPath = Join-Path $env:WINDIR 'Logs\CBS\CBS.log'
    try {
        if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $cbsLogPath) {
            $cbsLogTail = (Get-Content -LiteralPath $cbsLogPath -Tail 200 -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue) -join "`n"
        }
    } catch {}

    # Pre-evaluate Test-FbWuRebootRequired into a plain variable so
    # it can be embedded in the [pscustomobject] literal below
    # without the try/catch-as-expression footgun documented above.
    $rebootRequiredNow = $true
    try {
        $rebootRequiredNow = [bool](Test-FbWuRebootRequired)
    } catch {
        $rebootRequiredNow = $true
    }

    $diag = [pscustomobject]@{
        Timestamp                 = (Get-Date).ToString('o')
        ScriptVersion             = $ScriptVersion
        Context                   = $Context
        CbsPendingBootsCount      = $CbsPendingBootsCount
        WaitedSeconds             = $WaitedSeconds
        TestFbWuRebootRequiredNow = $rebootRequiredNow
        CbsRegistryKeys           = $cbsKeys
        CbsLogTail                = $cbsLogTail
    }
    try {
        $diag | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 6 | Set-Content -Path $diagPath -Encoding UTF8 -Force
        Write-FbLog ("Write-FbCbsStuckDiag: wrote {0}" -f $diagPath) 'WARN'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Write-FbCbsStuckDiag: write failed: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

function Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Pre-sysprep CBS-settle wait. Blocks until
        Test-FbWuRebootRequired returns $false on 2 consecutive
        5-second probes (2258: reduced from 3x10s), or until a
        hard 10-minute budget (600s default; tunable via
        -MaxWaitSeconds) is exhausted.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.1900-two-stage-sysprep-handoff Fix 4. STAGE 2
        of the two-stage handoff calls this helper BEFORE invoking
        Sysprep.exe. Even after a successful STAGE 1 reboot, CBS
        sometimes takes 30-60 seconds after Audit Mode boots to
        finish releasing its pending-reboot signal (the WindowsUpdate
        /Auto Update/RebootRequired registry key in particular is
        cleared by SessionEnv on a slight delay relative to the
        boot itself). Sysprep validates pending-reboot state
        synchronously during Sysprep_Clean_Validate_Opk and
        refuses to run with 0x8007139f if any pending-reboot
        signal is set; calling sysprep too early loses the race.
        This helper bounds the wait so the loop never blocks
        indefinitely.

        Behavior:
          - Probes Test-FbWuRebootRequired every 5s (2258: reduced
            from 10s), up to a 10-minute total wall-clock budget
            (600s default, tunable via -MaxWaitSeconds; doubled
            from the pre-2200 300s default to give CBS more
            headroom on slow-settling devices).
          - Tracks consecutive "no reboot required" probes; returns
            $true the moment we have $RequiredCleanProbes (default
            2; 2258: reduced from 3) in a row.
          - Each probe refreshes the heartbeat and updates the
            dashboard activity bar so the patient-reboot watchdog
            does not trip on a legitimate slow CBS settle, and the
            operator sees WHY the loop is waiting before sysprep.
          - Returns $false on budget exhaustion. Caller should
            then route to plain shutdown for another reboot
            attempt.

        Never throws. Best-effort on every probe and every UI
        write.

        2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: per-boot
        budget doubled from 300s to 600s, and the function now
        increments the cross-boot $state.CbsPendingBootsCount
        counter when it exits with OSPendingReboot=true. The
        main loop's escape valve trips on
        CbsPendingBootsCount >= 5 with reason 'CBS_NEVER_SETTLES'.

        2026.05.13.2213-tier-watchdog-and-diag-parsefix Important
        Fix 6: ADAPTIVE STAGE 2 budget. Field evidence on the
        test device showed STAGE 2 was burning the full 600s
        (10 minutes) on the FIRST attempt before giving up,
        which gave the OS plenty of time to wedge into the
        shutdown-lock state Tier 3 then could not break. New
        behavior when -AdaptiveBudget is $true: the first attempt
        uses 180s, the second 360s, and the third+ keeps the
        legacy 600s. Reasoning: most CBS-pending states clear
        within 30-90s after a clean reboot; if 180s is not
        enough, reboot fast and try again with a longer budget.
        The attempt count is derived from $state.CbsPendingBoots
        Count which the function itself increments on each
        budget-exhausted exit, so attempt 1 = CbsPendingBootsCount
        0, attempt 2 = CbsPendingBootsCount 1, attempt 3+ =
        CbsPendingBootsCount >= 2.
    #>
    param(
        [int]$MaxWaitSeconds       = 600,
        [int]$RequiredCleanProbes  = 2,   # 2258: reduced from 3 (saves 20s+ minimum CBS settle wait)
        [int]$ProbeIntervalSeconds = 5,   # 2258: reduced from 10s
        [string]$Context           = 'STAGE 2 pre-sysprep',
        # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: when $true,
        # call Invoke-FbCbsCleanup (DISM /startcomponentcleanup +
        # sfc /scannow + Get-WindowsUpdateLog) BEFORE the first
        # probe. Default $true for STAGE 2 entry; the second STAGE 1
        # patient-shutdown attempt within a single boot also passes
        # $true. Other callers that want a plain wait pass $false.
        [bool]$RunCbsCleanupFirst = $true,
        # 2026.05.13.2213-tier-watchdog-and-diag-parsefix Fix 6:
        # when $true, IGNORE -MaxWaitSeconds and compute the
        # budget adaptively from $state.CbsPendingBootsCount
        # (180s on attempt 1, 360s on attempt 2, 600s on attempt
        # 3+). The STAGE 2 caller in Invoke-FbOobeHandoff sets
        # this; secondary callers (Complete-And-Exit final
        # handoff, CompletionMarker relaunch) leave it default
        # ($false) so they keep the 600s flat budget.
        [bool]$AdaptiveBudget     = $false
    )

    if ($AdaptiveBudget) {
        $adaptiveAttempt = 1
        try {
            $adaptiveDisk = Load-State
            $adaptivePrior = [int]$adaptiveDisk.CbsPendingBootsCount
            $adaptiveAttempt = $adaptivePrior + 1
        } catch {}
        $adaptiveBudgetSec = switch ($adaptiveAttempt) {
            1       { 180 }
            2       { 360 }
            default { 600 }
        }
        Write-FbLog ("Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared [{0}]: AdaptiveBudget=true; CbsPendingBootsCount prior={1}, attempt={2}, budget={3}s (was {4}s flat in 2200-2212)." -f $Context, ($adaptiveAttempt - 1), $adaptiveAttempt, $adaptiveBudgetSec, $MaxWaitSeconds) 'WARN'
        $MaxWaitSeconds = $adaptiveBudgetSec
    }

    $waited       = 0
    $consecutiveClean = 0
    $lastProbeResult  = $null
    Write-FbLog ("Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared [{0}]: starting pre-sysprep CBS-settle wait (max {1}s, require {2} clean probes {3}s apart, RunCbsCleanupFirst={4}, AdaptiveBudget={5})." -f $Context, $MaxWaitSeconds, $RequiredCleanProbes, $ProbeIntervalSeconds, $RunCbsCleanupFirst, $AdaptiveBudget) 'WARN'

    if ($RunCbsCleanupFirst) {
        # NOTE (handoff-speed): Quick pre-probe before the expensive DISM/sfc CBS cleanup.
        # DISM /startcomponentcleanup + sfc /scannow together take 30s–6 minutes.  After a
        # normal STAGE 1 reboot CBS is already settled, so we can skip cleanup entirely.
        # Only run the cleanup if the first probe shows CBS is STILL pending.
        $preProbeStillPending = $true
        try {
            $preProbeStillPending = [bool](Test-FbWuRebootRequired)
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared [{0}]: pre-probe threw: {1}; assuming still pending and running cleanup." -f $Context, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
        if (-not $preProbeStillPending) {
            # NOTE (handoff-speed): CBS already settled on first probe — skip DISM/sfc entirely.
            # Minimum safe wait: 0s (CBS is clear before we start).
            Write-FbLog ("Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared [{0}]: pre-probe shows CBS already settled; skipping CBS cleanup pre-pass (saves 30s-6min DISM+sfc wait)." -f $Context) 'WARN'
            $consecutiveClean = 1
            # NOTE (handoff-speed): Skip to polling loop which will immediately hit RequiredCleanProbes.
        } else {
            Write-FbLog ("Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared [{0}]: pre-probe shows CBS still pending; running DISM/sfc cleanup pre-pass." -f $Context) 'WARN'
            try {
                $cleanupResult = Invoke-FbCbsCleanup -Context $Context
                $cleanupSummary = if ($cleanupResult) { ($cleanupResult.Summary) } else { 'no result' }
                Write-FbLog ("Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared [{0}]: CBS cleanup pre-pass result -> {1}" -f $Context, $cleanupSummary) 'WARN'
                if ($cleanupResult -and -not $cleanupResult.StillPending) {
                    Write-FbLog ("Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared [{0}]: CBS cleanup made the OS reboot signal go away; returning `$true without polling." -f $Context) 'WARN'
                    try { Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass 'cbs-cleanup-cleared' -StartTime ((Get-Date).ToString('o')) -FailureCount 0 -CbsPendingBootsCount 0 } catch {}
                    return $true
                }
            } catch {
                Write-FbLog ("Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared [{0}]: Invoke-FbCbsCleanup threw: {1}" -f $Context, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
            }
        }
    }

    try {
        Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'pre-sysprep-settle' -Message ("STAGE 2: waiting for CBS to release pending-reboot signal (0/{0} clean probes; {1}s elapsed / {2}s budget)" -f $RequiredCleanProbes, $waited, $MaxWaitSeconds)
    } catch {
        try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message ("STAGE 2 pre-sysprep CBS-settle wait: 0/{0} clean probes; {1}s / {2}s" -f $RequiredCleanProbes, $waited, $MaxWaitSeconds) } catch {}
    }
    try { Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Rebooting' } catch {}

    while ($waited -lt $MaxWaitSeconds) {
        $probeNow = $false
        try {
            $probeNow = [bool](Test-FbWuRebootRequired)
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared: Test-FbWuRebootRequired threw on probe at {0}s: {1}. Treating as 'still pending' (consecutive count NOT advanced)." -f $waited, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
            $probeNow = $true
        }
        $lastProbeResult = $probeNow

        if (-not $probeNow) {
            $consecutiveClean++
        } else {
            if ($consecutiveClean -gt 0) {
                Write-FbLog ("Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared: pending-reboot signal reappeared at {0}s; clean-probe streak reset {1}->0." -f $waited, $consecutiveClean) 'WARN'
            }
            $consecutiveClean = 0
        }
        $probeMsg = ("STAGE 2: pre-sysprep CBS-settle - probe at {0}s reports OSPendingReboot={1}; clean streak={2}/{3}; budget {0}s/{4}s" -f $waited, $probeNow, $consecutiveClean, $RequiredCleanProbes, $MaxWaitSeconds)
        try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'pre-sysprep-settle' -Message $probeMsg } catch {
            try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message $probeMsg } catch {}
        }
        try { Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Rebooting' } catch {}
        try { Write-FbLog $probeMsg 'INFO' } catch {}

        if ($consecutiveClean -ge $RequiredCleanProbes) {
            Write-FbLog ("Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared: {0} consecutive clean probes after {1}s; CBS has settled. Returning `$true." -f $consecutiveClean, $waited) 'WARN'
            try { Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass ("cbs-settled-{0}" -f $Context) -StartTime ((Get-Date).ToString('o')) -FailureCount 0 -CbsPendingBootsCount 0 } catch {}
            return $true
        }

        Start-Sleep -Seconds $ProbeIntervalSeconds
        $waited += $ProbeIntervalSeconds
    }

    # Budget exhausted with OS still pending. Increment the cross-boot
    # CbsPendingBootsCount counter; the main loop's escape-valve probe
    # checks this on the next pass and trips at >=5 with reason
    # CBS_NEVER_SETTLES.
    $newCbsCount = 0
    try {
        $cbsDisk = Load-State
        $newCbsCount = ([int]$cbsDisk.CbsPendingBootsCount) + 1
        Save-State -EmptySweepCount ([int]$cbsDisk.EmptySweepCount) `
            -LastPass ("cbs-stuck-{0}" -f $Context) `
            -StartTime ([string]$cbsDisk.StartTime) `
            -FailureCount ([int]$cbsDisk.FailureCount) `
            -CbsPendingBootsCount $newCbsCount
    } catch {}

    Write-FbLog ("Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared: budget exhausted ({0}s). Last probe result OSPendingReboot={1}; consecutive clean streak={2}/{3}. Bumped CbsPendingBootsCount -> {4}. CBS did not settle; caller should reboot and retry (or escape valve will trip at 5)." -f $waited, $lastProbeResult, $consecutiveClean, $RequiredCleanProbes, $newCbsCount) 'ERROR'

    # If still-pending after cleanup pre-pass, drop a STUCK-DIAG dump
    # so the operator can post-mortem the CBS state.
    if ($RunCbsCleanupFirst -and $newCbsCount -ge 1) {
        try {
            Write-FbCbsStuckDiag -Context $Context -CbsPendingBootsCount $newCbsCount -WaitedSeconds $waited
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared: Write-FbCbsStuckDiag threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
    }

    return $false
}

function Wait-FbReadyForReboot {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        REMOVED 2026.05.12.1030-no-cascade-final. No-op shim.
    .DESCRIPTION
        Previously the 8-stage cascade's Stage 0 settle wait. Removed
        because the cascade is gone and Invoke-FbReboot does not need
        to wait for TrustedInstaller idle (it goes straight to OOBE
        handoff or one shutdown.exe call). Left as a parameter-
        compatible no-op so any leftover caller still parses.
    #>
    param(
        [int]$MaxWaitSeconds         = 600,
        [int]$RequiredIdleSeconds    = 30,
        [int]$DashboardIntervalSecs  = 10
    )
    Write-FbLog 'Wait-FbReadyForReboot: legacy cascade settle wait is REMOVED in 2026.05.12.1030; returning $true immediately.' 'WARN'
    return $true
}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REMOVED 2026.05.12.1030-no-cascade-final: Invoke-Reboot deleted (~730 lines).
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Field evidence: the 8-stage cascade plus logoff fallback plus the
# SYSTEM-scoped scheduled-task escalation could not reboot the test
# hardware on ANY of the 11 prior patches (2026.05.11.0820 through
# 2026.05.12.0830). TrustedInstaller silently vetoed every native
# reboot API. The function has been deleted; callers now go through
# Invoke-FbRebootWithFreshStateCheck -> Invoke-FbReboot which routes
# straight to Invoke-FbOobeHandoff (sysprep takes the reboot via Setup,
# a distinct code path) or, only in the rare pre-install case, fires
# ONE shutdown.exe /r /f /t 5 and exits 1.
#
# The CascadeExhaustionCount field in wu-state.json is preserved (so
# old state files still parse) but is no longer incremented anywhere -
# it is dead data. The startup cascade-exhaustion routing block below
# still consults the on-disk value for backwards compatibility with
# devices upgrading from the prior build mid-cycle.

$script:MaxSysprepRetries = 3

function Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2026.05.20.2213r Bug NN + 2213x OOBE overlay: pre-OOBE scrub gate.
        Tear down FirstBase launch surfaces before sysprep, except a
        splash-only subset when OOBE overlay mode is armed.

    .DESCRIPTION
        PRODUCT INTENT (2213x, 5/20/2026): Splash may remain above OOBE
        until the update pipeline completes. SetupComplete writes
        C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\FirstBase-Splash-Oobe-Overlay.mode;
        when that marker is present, STEP 1 / STEP 2 / STEP 3 (WuSplash
        RunOnce only) / STEP 4 skip splash-specific teardown so splash
        tasks and payload scripts can survive until sysprep validation
        succeeds. Invoke-SysprepToOobe success then removes the marker,
        deletes SplashWatcher + UpdateSplashLayer4, deletes deferred
        splash payload files, and removes !FirstBaseWuSplash alongside
        the existing loop trigger deletes.

        Earlier 2213r doc (5/19/2026 7:06 PM) required full external
        teardown before sysprep to prevent stale splash firing under
        OOBE. 2213x narrows that contract: loop triggers, loop tasks,
        and non-splash scripts still scrub pre-sysprep; Winlogon fence
        (STEP 3b) is unchanged.

        This function runs:

          1. IMMEDIATELY before Start-Process sysprep.exe in
             Invoke-FbOobeHandoff STAGE 2 success path
          2. AFTER the Bug JJ post-sysprep state cleanup (which is
             internal wu-state.json field zero-ing; this is the
             EXTERNAL surface teardown that complements it)
          3. WRAPPED IN TRY/CATCH at the call site so a scrub
             failure logs but does NOT block sysprep

        Pre-2213r the in-Invoke-SysprepToOobe success path
        (lines ~8005-8024) DID delete the loop-trigger registry
        layers (!FirstBaseWuLoop, RunOnceEx\0001\FirstBaseWuLoop,
        Run\FirstBaseWuLoopRun, FirstBaseWuLoopRun.cmd) and the
        three loop scheduled tasks (WindowsUpdateLoop /
        WindowsUpdateLoopOnLogon / UpdateSplash) - but only AFTER
        sysprep.exe -Wait returned exit 0. Field evidence (dump
        of 5/19/2026, splash visible behind OOBE on Dell): at the
        moment sysprep launched, multiple FirstBase artifacts were
        still alive:
          - SYSTEM Explorer-presence watcher (2213q Bug MM) still
            polling, ready to re-fire splash on interactive Explorer
          - FirstBase\UpdateSplash scheduled task still defined
            (intentionally triggerless in 2213q but invokable via
            schtasks /Run from the live watcher)
          - HKLM RunOnce\!FirstBaseWuSplash splash trigger still
            armed for next interactive logon (which OOBE IS)
          - FirstBase\UpdateSplashLayer4 (dynamically created by the
            loop's Layer 5 fallback) potentially still present
        Result: OOBE booted, Explorer became interactive in OOBE's
        first audit-like session, watcher fired schtasks /Run, splash
        launched UNDER OOBE's window, user had to Shift+F10 + Alt+Tab
        to dismiss it. From the user's perspective: broken.

        2213r fix: scrub every external surface BEFORE the sysprep
        launch. By the time sysprep returns and the legacy in-
        Invoke-SysprepToOobe cleanup at lines ~8005-8024 runs, this
        scrub has ALREADY torn everything down; the legacy cleanup
        becomes a redundant secondary belt (still kept for paranoia,
        but no longer load-bearing).

        WHAT GETS SCRUBBED
        ==================

        STEP 1 - Kill all FirstBase launch-layer processes:
          - powershell.exe instances whose CommandLine references
            Show-UpdateProgress.ps1 (WPF splash UI)
          - powershell.exe instances whose CommandLine references
            FirstBaseSplashSingleInstance.ps1 (2213l Bug EE wrapper)
          - powershell.exe instances whose CommandLine references
            FirstBaseSplashWatcher.ps1 (2213q Bug MM watcher)
          - cmd.exe / powershell.exe instances whose CommandLine
            references FirstBaseConsoleSplash.cmd /
            FirstBaseConsoleSplashInstance.ps1 /
            FirstBaseConsoleSplashRender.ps1 (console fallback,
            defensive - may exist on prior build images)
          - cmd.exe instances whose CommandLine references
            FirstBaseSplashLauncher.cmd
          - cmd.exe instances whose CommandLine references
            FirstBaseLoopLauncher.cmd or FirstBaseWuLoopRun.cmd
          - powershell.exe instances whose CommandLine references
            Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1 OTHER THAN our own $PID
            (we are the loop and are about to launch sysprep; we
            self-terminate after sysprep returns)
          CommandLine match uses Get-CimInstance Win32_Process so
          we catch processes by argv even when the .exe is just
          powershell.exe or cmd.exe (process-name match would be
          ambiguous and could kill unrelated user-spawned shells).
          Self-protection: skip $PID (we ARE the loop, and must
          remain alive to call sysprep next).

        STEP 2 - Disable + delete all FirstBase\* scheduled tasks:
          - Enumerate via schtasks /Query /FO LIST and grep
            "TaskName: \\FirstBase\\" so we catch every FirstBase\*
            task whether or not we explicitly know about it
            (future-proofing).
          - Plus the explicit known list as belt-and-suspenders:
              FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoop
              FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoopOnLogon
              FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoopBoot2
              FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoopAnyLogon
              FirstBase\UpdateSplash
              FirstBase\UpdateSplashLayer4
              FirstBase\SplashWatcher (2213q)
              FirstBase\SplashWatcherBoot2
              FirstBase\UpdateSplashInteractive (legacy, may exist
                  on prior-build re-images)
              FirstBase\UpdateSplashInteractiveLaunch (legacy)
          - For each: schtasks /Change /DISABLE first, THEN
            schtasks /Delete /F. The two-step pattern protects
            against an in-flight trigger firing in the ~50ms window
            between our enumeration and the Delete completing.

        STEP 3 - Delete all FirstBase RunOnce / Run / RunOnceEx /
        RunServices* registry values:
          - HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce
          - HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run
          - HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnceEx
            (plus enumerate subkeys like \0001 and delete FirstBase*
            values within each)
          - HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunServicesOnce
          - HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunServices
          Filter: value name starts with "!FirstBase" or "FirstBase"
          (case-insensitive). Catches !FirstBaseWuLoop,
          !FirstBaseWuSplash, FirstBaseWuLoopRun, FirstBaseWuLoop
          (RunOnceEx \0001), and any future variant.

          IMPORTANT - 2026.05.20.2213s removed the post-OOBE
          completion popup RunOnce registration entirely (per
          5/19/2026 user request: "Let's remove the updates
          complete pop-up. It should no longer be part of the
          post update completion workflow"). The constant
          $RunOnceValueName ('FirstBaseUpdatesComplete') is
          retained in the loop's variable declarations so the
          regex (?i)^!?FirstBase still catches stale values from
          a prior-build re-image, but no 2213s code path ever
          writes that value. The post-OOBE workflow is now
          modal-free; OOBE lands on the user's freshly-created
          desktop with no popup interruption.

        STEP 3b (2026.05.20.2213s Bug U fence 2) - Winlogon
        auto-login scrub:
          New in 2213s. Deletes the explicit list of Winlogon
          auto-login values that SetupComplete.cmd :ARM_AUTOLOGIN
          wrote for Audit-Mode Administrator auto-logon
          (AutoAdminLogon, DefaultUserName, DefaultPassword,
          DefaultDomainName, AutoLogonCount, ForceAutoLogon,
          AutoLogonSID, SystemAutoLogon, LastUsedUsername,
          DisableLockWorkstation, EnableFirstLogonAnimation).
          Also runs `net user Administrator /active:no` to
          re-disable the local Administrator account that
          :ARM_AUTOLOGIN enabled. Companion fence to
          SetupComplete.cmd's ImageState gate: either fence
          alone is sufficient; both together guarantee Bug U
          (5/19/2026 OOBE bypass) cannot recur. Does NOT touch
          normal Windows Winlogon values (Shell, Userinit,
          AutoRestartShell, etc) - the value list is explicit.

        STEP 4 - Script scrub (2026.05.20.2213t, ALWAYS-ON):
          User clarification (5/19/2026 3:04 PM): "Only scripts
          should be scrubbed. Logs and state files can stay, any
          ps1 or cmd should be removed from the device."

          Pre-2213t this step was an OPT-IN State+Logs scrub
          (default OFF, kept the .ps1 / .cmd payload entirely).
          2213t INVERTS the polarity: .ps1 and .cmd files are
          ALWAYS deleted; State/ and Logs/ are ALWAYS preserved
          for post-OOBE diagnostics.

          DELETED (recursive walk, extension filter .ps1 + .cmd):
            - C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\*.ps1
                (Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1, FirstBaseWuEngine
                .ps1, Show-UpdateProgress.ps1, Show-UpdatesComplete
                Message.ps1, FirstBaseSplashSingleInstance.ps1,
                FirstBaseSplashWatcher.ps1, FirstBaseConsoleSplash
                Instance.ps1, FirstBaseConsoleSplashRender.ps1,
                and any future additions)
            - C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\*.cmd
                (FirstBaseSplashLauncher.cmd, FirstBaseConsoleSplash
                .cmd, FirstBaseWuLoopRun.cmd, FirstBaseLoopLauncher
                .cmd, and any future additions)
            - C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\SetupComplete.cmd
                (autounattend.xml entry point; once sysprep is
                handed off this MUST NOT exist to be re-invoked
                on post-sysprep specialize. Combined with Fence 1
                ProgramData markers (2226: arm-suppress OR .pipeline-completed)
                this is a third independent defense against re-arming on the
                post-sysprep specialize pass.)

          PRESERVED (explicit prefix exclusion):
            - C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\State\* (entire subtree)
            - C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\Logs\*  (entire subtree)
            - C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\*         (entire subtree,
                including 2226 arm-suppress + .pipeline-completed markers)

          SELF-DELETION EDGE CASE:
            Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1 is the script currently
            executing during the scrub. PowerShell normally opens
            .ps1 files with FILE_SHARE_DELETE set during parse and
            does NOT hold a file lock for the duration of execution
            (verified empirically on Win11 26200 and by checking
            this script for $PSCommandPath / $MyInvocation / Get-
            Content-on-self reflection - none exist post-parse, so
            no source-file reflection forces a re-read). Direct
            Remove-Item against the running script normally
            succeeds.

            BELT-AND-BRACES: if any Remove-Item attempt throws
            (e.g. anti-malware holds a transient lock, or a future
            refactor introduces $PSCommandPath reads), the failed
            path is appended to $script:_FbDelayedDeletePaths.
            After STEP 4 finishes the scrub spawns a detached
            FbDelayedScriptScrub.cmd in %TEMP% that waits 8s
            (long enough for the loop process to exit via sysprep
            handoff) then `del /f /q` each deferred path and
            self-deletes. The .cmd does not need to be itself
            cleaned up by us; its last line is `(goto) 2>nul &
            del "%~f0"` which removes the .cmd file before exit.

        STEP 5 - Verification + final log line:
          Re-poll processes, tasks, registry, AND scripts. Any
          residual gets a hard kill / second delete attempt /
          logged as residual for the SCRUB-INCOMPLETE branch.
          Surviving .ps1 / .cmd files in scrub scope are noted as
          residuals (the delayed-delete .cmd will clean them up
          after the loop exits, so they are EXPECTED for the
          running script's own path but recorded for forensics).
          Final log line is either:
            SCRUB-COMPLETE: N processes killed, M tasks deleted,
                K registry values removed, S scripts deleted
          OR
            SCRUB-INCOMPLETE: <details>
          Both paths return $true; SCRUB-INCOMPLETE does NOT
          block sysprep (the caller wraps us in try/catch and
          proceeds regardless - per user spec: "don't block
          sysprep on a teardown failure - log and continue").

        LOG LOCATION
        ============
        C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\Logs\pre-oobe-scrub.log
        One line per action with ISO 8601 timestamp. fb-dump.cmd
        picks this up automatically via its recursive Logs/ xcopy
        in step [1/12]; no fb-dump change required (a docstring
        note is added for operator orientation only).

        INTERACTION WITH BUG JJ
        =======================
        Bug JJ (2213n, Invoke-FbOobeHandoff STAGE 2, lines
        ~9540-9590) is COMPLEMENTARY to this scrub gate:
          - Bug JJ zeros INTERNAL wu-state.json fields
            (GivenUpUpdateIds, SoftGivenUpCounters,
            SuccessLedgerIds, ScanStability) before sysprep
          - 2213r Bug NN scrubs EXTERNAL surfaces (processes,
            scheduled tasks, registry keys) before sysprep
        Both must run; neither replaces the other. The call
        ordering in Invoke-FbOobeHandoff STAGE 2 is:
            1. Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared (CBS settle)
            2. Bug JJ state cleanup (internal)
            3. Bug U fence 1 arm-suppress marker write (ProgramData) <-- 2226
            4. Bug NN pre-sysprep scrub (external) <-- 2213r
                4a. Bug U fence 2 = STEP 3b inside the scrub <-- 2213s
                4b. STEP 4 script scrub = .ps1/.cmd removal <-- 2213t
            5. Invoke-SysprepToOobe (launches sysprep.exe -Wait)

        FROZEN FILE NOTE (2213t)
        ========================
        Show-UpdateProgress.ps1, Show-UpdatesCompleteMessage.ps1,
        FirstBaseSplashSingleInstance.ps1 and the rest of the
        .ps1 / .cmd payload are SHA-pinned in the build process
        but DO get deleted from the device by 2213t's STEP 4 as
        part of the on-device script scrub. The frozen-file SHA
        guarantee is about the SOURCE files in the repo (so
        Build-UsbStick.ps1 stages a known payload onto the USB);
        on-device deletion at sysprep time is orthogonal. The
        autounattend.xml file is NOT touched - it lives in the
        WIM's unattend slot, not under C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\
        / C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\.

    .OUTPUTS
        $true always (best-effort; caller proceeds to sysprep
        regardless of scrub completeness).
    #>
    [CmdletBinding()]
    param()

    # === LEGACY CONFIG KNOB (HARDCODED OFF in 2213t) ===
    # 2026.05.20.2213t inverted STEP 4's polarity per the 5/19/2026
    # 3:04 PM user clarification: "Only scripts should be scrubbed.
    # Logs and state files can stay, any ps1 or cmd should be
    # removed from the device." STEP 4 now ALWAYS deletes .ps1 /
    # .cmd payload and ALWAYS preserves State/ + Logs/ subtrees.
    # This knob is retained as $false to keep the log line's
    # historical "scrubStateAndLogs=False" telemetry stable for
    # any operator scripts that grep for it, but it no longer
    # gates anything in 2213t.
    $scrubStateAndLogs = $false

    $scrubLogPath = Join-Path $LogDir 'pre-oobe-scrub.log'
    $scrubStarted = Get-Date

    # 2026.05.20.2213t STEP 4: deferred-delete sink. Populated when
    # a direct Remove-Item against a .ps1 / .cmd file throws (e.g.
    # the running loop's own source file under a transient lock).
    # After STEP 4 finishes, the scrub spawns a detached cmd.exe
    # helper (FbDelayedScriptScrub.cmd) that waits 8s for this
    # process to exit and then `del /f /q` each deferred path.
    # Script-scope so the helper writer below can read it without
    # parameter passing.
    $script:_FbDelayedDeletePaths = @()

    # Inline scrub-log helper. Best-effort; never throws upstream.
    # ISO 8601 timestamp + level + message. Append-only.
    $writeScrubLog = {
        param([string]$Level, [string]$Message)
        try {
            if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $LogDir)) {
                New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $LogDir -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
            }
            $line = ('[{0}] [{1}] [scrub pid={2}] {3}' -f (Get-Date -Format 'o'), $Level, $PID, $Message)
            Add-Content -LiteralPath $scrubLogPath -Value $line -Encoding UTF8 -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        } catch {}
    }

    & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' ("=== Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub starting === scrubStateAndLogs={0} (2213t: STEP 4 ALWAYS scrubs .ps1/.cmd, ALWAYS preserves State/Logs); loopVersion={1}" -f $scrubStateAndLogs, $ScriptVersion)
    Write-FbLog ("Pre-OOBE scrub gate (2213r Bug NN + 2213t script-scrub): starting; scrubStateAndLogs={0} (legacy knob, irrelevant in 2213t); log={1}" -f $scrubStateAndLogs, $scrubLogPath) 'WARN'

    $splashOobeOverlayActive = $false
    try {
        $splashOobeOverlayActive = Test-Path -LiteralPath $FbSplashOobeOverlayMarkerPath
    } catch {}
    if ($splashOobeOverlayActive) {
        & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' ("2213x OOBE splash overlay mode: marker present at {0}; deferring splash-only processes, splash scheduled tasks, splash RunOnce WuSplash, and splash script files until Invoke-SysprepToOobe success teardown." -f $FbSplashOobeOverlayMarkerPath)
        Write-FbLog ("Pre-OOBE scrub: 2213x OOBE splash overlay marker detected at {0}; splash task/process/script subset deferred per product intent." -f $FbSplashOobeOverlayMarkerPath) 'WARN'
    }

    $procsKilledCount = 0
    $tasksDeletedCount = 0
    $regValuesRemovedCount = 0
    $winlogonValuesRemovedCount = 0
    $residualNotes = @()

    # ==================================================================
    # STEP 0 (2273): ACL elevation — grant defaultuser0 and local Users
    # Modify rights on C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\ with (OI)(CI)
    # inheritance so all child files and subdirectories are covered.
    #
    # Root cause (fb-dump-final-603): FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1
    # runs as defaultuser0 during OOBE. C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\ is
    # owned by SYSTEM and only SYSTEM+Administrators have write access
    # by default. The 2272 scrub-fix correctly enumerated only .ps1/.cmd
    # files, but all 21 Setup-path deletes (and the self-delete batch)
    # failed with "Access to the path is denied" — scripts orphaned.
    #
    # Fix: grant Modify here (running as SYSTEM pre-sysprep) so the ACE
    # propagates through sysprep\generalize and is present when OOBE
    # starts. "Users" covers the FirstBaseOobeDeferredSoundBootstrap.ps1
    # and FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 emergency disarm paths, which
    # may execute as a non-admin real user (same access denied issue).
    # ==================================================================
    & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' '2273: STEP 0 — ACL elevation on C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\ for defaultuser0 and Users (fb-dump-final-603 fix).'
    foreach ($fb2273AclTarget in @($FbRoot)) {
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $fb2273AclTarget)) {
            & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("2273: STEP 0 target absent, skipping: {0}" -f $fb2273AclTarget)
            continue
        }
        foreach ($fb2273Principal in @('defaultuser0', 'Users')) {
            try {
                $fb2273IcaclsArgs = @($fb2273AclTarget, '/grant', ('{0}:(OI)(CI)M' -f $fb2273Principal), '/T', '/Q')
                $fb2273Proc = Start-Process -FilePath 'icacls.exe' -ArgumentList $fb2273IcaclsArgs -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru -ErrorAction Stop
                $fb2273Exited = $fb2273Proc.WaitForExit(10000)
                if (-not $fb2273Exited) {
                    try { $fb2273Proc.Kill() } catch {}
                    & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("2273: STEP 0 icacls grant {0} on {1} timed out (10 000ms); process killed; OOBE deletes may still fail." -f $fb2273Principal, $fb2273AclTarget)
                } else {
                    & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' ("2273: STEP 0 icacls grant {0}:(OI)(CI)M on {1} exit={2}." -f $fb2273Principal, $fb2273AclTarget, $fb2273Proc.ExitCode)
                }
            } catch {
                & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("2273: STEP 0 icacls grant {0} on {1} threw: {2}" -f $fb2273Principal, $fb2273AclTarget, $_.Exception.Message)
            }
        }
    }

    # ==================================================================
    # STEP 0-B (2290): ACL elevation — grant defaultuser0 and local Users
    # Modify rights on C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\ with (OI)(CI) inheritance.
    #
    # Root cause (PF3THF6C-1004-Lenovo, 2026-07-06): All 9 ProgramData
    # scripts survive FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 with "Access to
    # the path is denied" on every Remove-Item. The pre-oobe-scrub STEP 0
    # above grants Modify on C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\ — which is why
    # those deletes succeed — but C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\ is staged by
    # SYSTEM in Audit Mode and defaultuser0 has no write ACE there.
    # The deferred-delete batch (FbFinalizeScriptScrub.cmd) also runs as
    # defaultuser0 and fails for the same reason.
    #
    # Fix: apply the identical icacls grant to $FbProgramDataFirstBase
    # here, while still running as SYSTEM (pre-sysprep). The ACE
    # propagates through sysprep\generalize and is present when
    # FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 deletes scripts in OOBE.
    # Mirrors the 2273 STEP 0 pattern exactly.
    # ==================================================================
    & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' '2290: STEP 0-B — ACL elevation on C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\ for defaultuser0 and Users (PF3THF6C-1004-Lenovo fix).'
    foreach ($fb2290AclTarget in @($FbProgramDataFirstBase, $FbProgramDataAllUsersStartup)) { # 2291: also grant Startup folder (FirstBaseOobeSoundAtLogon.cmd)
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $fb2290AclTarget)) {
            & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("2290: STEP 0-B target absent, skipping: {0}" -f $fb2290AclTarget)
            continue
        }
        foreach ($fb2290Principal in @('defaultuser0', 'Users')) {
            try {
                $fb2290IcaclsArgs = @($fb2290AclTarget, '/grant', ('{0}:(OI)(CI)M' -f $fb2290Principal), '/T', '/Q')
                $fb2290Proc = Start-Process -FilePath 'icacls.exe' -ArgumentList $fb2290IcaclsArgs -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru -ErrorAction Stop
                $fb2290Exited = $fb2290Proc.WaitForExit(10000)
                if (-not $fb2290Exited) {
                    try { $fb2290Proc.Kill() } catch {}
                    & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("2290: STEP 0-B icacls grant {0} on {1} timed out (10 000ms); process killed; OOBE deletes may still fail." -f $fb2290Principal, $fb2290AclTarget)
                } else {
                    & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' ("2290: STEP 0-B icacls grant {0}:(OI)(CI)M on {1} exit={2}." -f $fb2290Principal, $fb2290AclTarget, $fb2290Proc.ExitCode)
                }
            } catch {
                & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("2290: STEP 0-B icacls grant {0} on {1} threw: {2}" -f $fb2290Principal, $fb2290AclTarget, $_.Exception.Message)
            }
        }
    }

    # ==================================================================
    # STEP 1: kill all FirstBase launch-layer processes via CommandLine
    # match. Self-protection: never kill $PID.
    # ==================================================================
    # CommandLine substring patterns. Case-insensitive match against
    # the full command line returned by Win32_Process. A process is
    # killed if ANY pattern matches; the explicit launch-script names
    # cover every known launcher / wrapper / watcher / splash UI host.
    # Defensive: includes legacy console-splash filenames in case a
    # prior-build re-image left stale processes alive (those files
    # are not in the 2213r payload but may exist on the disk from a
    # 2213k-2213m era image).
    $procCommandLinePatterns = @(
        'Show-UpdateProgress.ps1'
        'FirstBaseSplashSingleInstance.ps1'
        'FirstBaseSplashWatcher.ps1'
        'FirstBaseConsoleSplash.cmd'
        'FirstBaseConsoleSplashInstance.ps1'
        'FirstBaseConsoleSplashRender.ps1'
        'FirstBaseSplashLauncher.cmd'
        'FirstBaseLoopLauncher.cmd'
        'FirstBaseWuLoopRun.cmd'
        'Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1'
    )
    $procKillTargets = @()
    try {
        $allProcs = Get-CimInstance -ClassName Win32_Process -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        foreach ($proc in $allProcs) {
            if (-not $proc) { continue }
            $procPid = $null
            try { $procPid = [int]$proc.ProcessId } catch {}
            if ($null -eq $procPid -or $procPid -eq 0) { continue }
            # Self-protection: never kill ourselves. We ARE
            # Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1 and must remain alive to
            # call sysprep next. Self-termination happens after
            # Start-Process sysprep.exe returns.
            if ($procPid -eq $PID) { continue }
            $cmdLine = ''
            try { $cmdLine = [string]$proc.CommandLine } catch {}
            if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($cmdLine)) { continue }
            $matched = $false
            foreach ($pattern in $procCommandLinePatterns) {
                if ($cmdLine -match [regex]::Escape($pattern)) { $matched = $true; break }
            }
            if (-not $matched) { continue }
            $procKillTargets += [pscustomobject]@{
                Pid         = $procPid
                Name        = [string]$proc.Name
                CommandLine = $cmdLine
            }
        }
    } catch {
        & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 1 Get-CimInstance Win32_Process enumeration threw: {0}; continuing with empty kill list (residual processes will be logged in STEP 5 verification)" -f $_.Exception.Message)
    }

    if ($splashOobeOverlayActive) {
        $splashOnlyPatterns = @(
            'Show-UpdateProgress.ps1'
            'FirstBaseSplashSingleInstance.ps1'
            'FirstBaseSplashWatcher.ps1'
            'FirstBaseConsoleSplash.cmd'
            'FirstBaseConsoleSplashInstance.ps1'
            'FirstBaseConsoleSplashRender.ps1'
            'FirstBaseSplashLauncher.cmd'
        )
        $beforeOverlayFilter = $procKillTargets.Count
        $procKillTargets = @($procKillTargets | Where-Object {
            $cl = $_.CommandLine
            if ($cl -match [regex]::Escape('Invoke-WindowsUpdateLoop.ps1')) { return $true }
            if ($cl -match [regex]::Escape('FirstBaseLoopLauncher.cmd')) { return $true }
            if ($cl -match [regex]::Escape('FirstBaseWuLoopRun.cmd')) { return $true }
            foreach ($sp in $splashOnlyPatterns) {
                if ($cl -match [regex]::Escape($sp)) { return $false }
            }
            return $true
        })
        & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' ("STEP 1 OOBE overlay: filtered splash-only kill targets ({0} -> {1})" -f $beforeOverlayFilter, $procKillTargets.Count)
    }

    & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' ("STEP 1: identified {0} FirstBase launch-layer process(es) to kill (self pid {1} excluded)" -f $procKillTargets.Count, $PID)

    foreach ($target in $procKillTargets) {
        $cmdLineDisplay = $target.CommandLine
        if ($cmdLineDisplay.Length -gt 200) { $cmdLineDisplay = $cmdLineDisplay.Substring(0, 200) + '...[truncated]' }
        try {
            Stop-Process -Id $target.Pid -Force -ErrorAction Stop
            $procsKilledCount++
            & $writeScrubLog 'KILL' ("pid={0} name={1} cmdLine={2}" -f $target.Pid, $target.Name, $cmdLineDisplay)
        } catch {
            & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 1 Stop-Process pid={0} name={1} threw: {2}; will retry in STEP 5 verification" -f $target.Pid, $target.Name, $_.Exception.Message)
        }
    }

    # ==================================================================
    # STEP 2: disable + delete all FirstBase\* scheduled tasks
    # ==================================================================
    # Discovery: schtasks /Query /FO LIST emits per-task records with
    # a "TaskName: \FirstBase\..." line. Parse for any TaskName that
    # starts with "\FirstBase\" so we catch every FirstBase\* task
    # whether or not we explicitly know about it (future-proofing).
    # Then UNION with the explicit known list (belt-and-suspenders;
    # tasks created dynamically after the enumerate would otherwise
    # be missed). Two-step teardown per task: /Change /DISABLE first,
    # then /Delete /F. Disable prevents an in-flight trigger from
    # firing during the ~50ms window between our snapshot and the
    # delete completing.
    $discoveredTasks = @()
    try {
        $queryOutput = & schtasks.exe /Query /FO LIST 2>&1
        if ($LASTEXITCODE -eq 0 -and $queryOutput) {
            foreach ($queryLine in @($queryOutput)) {
                $lineStr = [string]$queryLine
                # Match: "TaskName: \FirstBase\SomeName"
                # Capture group 1 = the FirstBase\Name portion (no leading slash).
                if ($lineStr -match '^\s*TaskName:\s*\\(FirstBase\\[^\s]+)\s*$') {
                    $discoveredTasks += $Matches[1]
                }
            }
        }
    } catch {
        & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 2 schtasks /Query enumeration threw: {0}; falling back to explicit known-task list" -f $_.Exception.Message)
    }
    $explicitKnownTasks = @(
        'FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoop'
        'FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoopOnLogon'
        'FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoopBoot2'
        'FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoopAnyLogon'
        'FirstBase\UpdateSplash'
        'FirstBase\UpdateSplashLayer4'
        'FirstBase\SplashWatcher'
        'FirstBase\SplashWatcherBoot2'
        'FirstBase\UpdateSplashInteractive'
        'FirstBase\UpdateSplashInteractiveLaunch'
        'FirstBase\DebugHotkeyWatcher'    # [DEBUG] 2249: Ctrl+Shift+End bypass watcher; OOBE no longer needs it
    )
    $allTaskTargets = New-Object 'System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]' ([System.StringComparer]::OrdinalIgnoreCase)
    foreach ($t in $discoveredTasks)     { [void]$allTaskTargets.Add($t) }
    foreach ($t in $explicitKnownTasks)  { [void]$allTaskTargets.Add($t) }

    # 5.4.8: do NOT preserve DeferredOobeSoundOnLogon. That ONLOGON task would
    # start FirstBase as defaultuser0 in OOBE. Delete it with the rest.
    [void]$allTaskTargets.Add('FirstBase\DeferredOobeSoundOnLogon')
    & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' '5.4.8 STEP 2: DeferredOobeSoundOnLogon is included in the delete set (customer OOBE; FirstBase must not run as defaultuser0)'

    # 5.4.8: do NOT preserve MdmScrub into OOBE either. Inline Audit scrub owns MDM
    # removal; a leftover SYSTEM task must not run after sysprep.
    [void]$allTaskTargets.Add('FirstBase\MdmScrub')
    & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' '5.4.8 STEP 2: FirstBase\MdmScrub is included in the delete set (inline Audit scrub already ran; do not leave a SYSTEM task for OOBE)'

    # 5.4.8: even if overlay.mode lingered, do not preserve splash tasks into OOBE.
    if ($splashOobeOverlayActive) {
        & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' '5.4.8 STEP 2: overlay.mode still set; NOT preserving splash tasks (customer OOBE; FirstBase must not start as defaultuser0)'
    }

    & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' ("STEP 2: {0} FirstBase\\* scheduled task(s) targeted (discovered={1}, explicit-union={2})" -f $allTaskTargets.Count, $discoveredTasks.Count, $explicitKnownTasks.Count)

    foreach ($taskName in $allTaskTargets) {
        # Disable first; quiet failure tolerated (task may already be
        # disabled, or may have been deleted between enumerate and now).
        try {
            $null = & schtasks.exe /Change /TN $taskName /DISABLE 2>&1
            & $writeScrubLog 'DISABLE' ("task={0} schtasks /Change /DISABLE exit={1}" -f $taskName, $LASTEXITCODE)
        } catch {
            & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 2 schtasks /Change /DISABLE task={0} threw: {1}" -f $taskName, $_.Exception.Message)
        }
        try {
            $null = & schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $taskName /F 2>&1
            if ($LASTEXITCODE -eq 0) {
                $tasksDeletedCount++
                & $writeScrubLog 'DELETE' ("task={0} schtasks /Delete /F succeeded (exit=0)" -f $taskName)
            } else {
                # exit code 1 typically = "The system cannot find the
                # file specified." (task did not exist). Not a failure;
                # the explicit-known-task list always contains items
                # that may not exist on every build.
                & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' ("task={0} schtasks /Delete /F exit={1} (1 typically = task did not exist; not an error)" -f $taskName, $LASTEXITCODE)
            }
        } catch {
            & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 2 schtasks /Delete /F task={0} threw: {1}; will retry in STEP 5 verification" -f $taskName, $_.Exception.Message)
        }
    }

    # ==================================================================
    # STEP 3: delete FirstBase / !FirstBase registry values from all
    # autostart hives
    # ==================================================================
    # Hive paths to scrub. For each: enumerate value names, delete any
    # whose name starts with "!FirstBase" or "FirstBase" (case-insen).
    # For RunOnceEx we also enumerate immediate subkeys (e.g. \0001)
    # and delete FirstBase* values within each subkey.
    $autostartHives = @(
        'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce'
        'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run'
        'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunServicesOnce'
        'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunServices'
    )
    $isFirstBaseValueName = {
        param([string]$ValueName)
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($ValueName)) { return $false }
        # Match "FirstBase*" OR "!FirstBase*" (case-insensitive)
        return ($ValueName -match '(?i)^!?FirstBase')
    }

    foreach ($hive in $autostartHives) {
        try {
            if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $hive)) {
                & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' ("STEP 3 hive {0} does not exist; skipping" -f $hive)
                continue
            }
            $hiveItem = Get-Item -LiteralPath $hive -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            if (-not $hiveItem) { continue }
            $valueNames = @()
            try { $valueNames = @($hiveItem.GetValueNames()) } catch {}
            foreach ($valueName in $valueNames) {
                if (-not (& $isFirstBaseValueName $valueName)) { continue }
                if ($splashOobeOverlayActive -and ($valueName -match '(?i)WuSplash')) {
                    & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' ("STEP 3 OOBE overlay: preserving hive={0} value={1}" -f $hive, $valueName)
                    continue
                }
                try {
                    Remove-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $hive -Name $valueName -Force -ErrorAction Stop
                    $regValuesRemovedCount++
                    & $writeScrubLog 'REGDEL' ("hive={0} value={1} deleted" -f $hive, $valueName)
                } catch {
                    & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 3 Remove-ItemProperty hive={0} value={1} threw: {2}; trying reg.exe /f as fallback" -f $hive, $valueName, $_.Exception.Message)
                    # reg.exe delete /f tolerates locked/protected
                    # values better than the .NET registry provider
                    # and never throws on missing values.
                    try {
                        $regHivePath = $hive -replace '^HKLM:\\', 'HKLM\'
                        $null = & reg.exe delete $regHivePath /v $valueName /f 2>&1
                        if ($LASTEXITCODE -eq 0) {
                            $regValuesRemovedCount++
                            & $writeScrubLog 'REGDEL' ("hive={0} value={1} deleted via reg.exe fallback" -f $hive, $valueName)
                        } else {
                            & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 3 reg.exe delete hive={0} value={1} exit={2}; will retry in STEP 5 verification" -f $hive, $valueName, $LASTEXITCODE)
                        }
                    } catch {
                        & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 3 reg.exe delete hive={0} value={1} threw: {2}" -f $hive, $valueName, $_.Exception.Message)
                    }
                }
            }
        } catch {
            & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 3 hive enumeration {0} threw: {1}" -f $hive, $_.Exception.Message)
        }
    }

    # RunOnceEx requires special handling: values live under subkeys
    # like \0001, \0002 (Microsoft's documented group naming). The
    # loop's Set-RunOnceAutostart writes RunOnceEx\0001\FirstBaseWuLoop.
    # Enumerate all immediate subkeys of RunOnceEx and scrub FirstBase*
    # values within each. Also delete any subkey that becomes empty
    # after the scrub (paranoia: an empty group could trigger a no-op
    # but logged warning at next boot).
    $runOnceExRoot = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnceEx'
    try {
        if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $runOnceExRoot) {
            # Also scrub FirstBase* values directly under RunOnceEx
            # root (some installer patterns write there directly).
            $rootItem = Get-Item -LiteralPath $runOnceExRoot -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            if ($rootItem) {
                $rootValues = @()
                try { $rootValues = @($rootItem.GetValueNames()) } catch {}
                foreach ($valueName in $rootValues) {
                    if (-not (& $isFirstBaseValueName $valueName)) { continue }
                    try {
                        Remove-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $runOnceExRoot -Name $valueName -Force -ErrorAction Stop
                        $regValuesRemovedCount++
                        & $writeScrubLog 'REGDEL' ("hive={0} value={1} deleted" -f $runOnceExRoot, $valueName)
                    } catch {
                        & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 3 RunOnceEx root value={0} delete threw: {1}" -f $valueName, $_.Exception.Message)
                    }
                }
            }
            $subkeys = @()
            try { $subkeys = @(Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath $runOnceExRoot -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue) } catch {}
            foreach ($subkey in $subkeys) {
                if (-not $subkey) { continue }
                $subkeyPath = $subkey.PSPath
                $subkeyName = $subkey.PSChildName
                try {
                    $subkeyItem = Get-Item -LiteralPath $subkeyPath -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                    if (-not $subkeyItem) { continue }
                    $subkeyValueNames = @()
                    try { $subkeyValueNames = @($subkeyItem.GetValueNames()) } catch {}
                    $deletedFromThisSubkey = 0
                    foreach ($valueName in $subkeyValueNames) {
                        if (-not (& $isFirstBaseValueName $valueName)) { continue }
                        try {
                            Remove-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $subkeyPath -Name $valueName -Force -ErrorAction Stop
                            $regValuesRemovedCount++
                            $deletedFromThisSubkey++
                            & $writeScrubLog 'REGDEL' ("hive={0}\\{1} value={2} deleted" -f $runOnceExRoot, $subkeyName, $valueName)
                        } catch {
                            & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 3 RunOnceEx\\{0} value={1} delete threw: {2}" -f $subkeyName, $valueName, $_.Exception.Message)
                        }
                    }
                    # If we deleted the only values in the subkey and
                    # no other values remain, drop the subkey itself.
                    # Defensive: re-enumerate to confirm empty before
                    # delete (do NOT delete subkeys that still hold
                    # non-FirstBase values).
                    if ($deletedFromThisSubkey -gt 0) {
                        try {
                            $reread = Get-Item -LiteralPath $subkeyPath -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                            $remaining = @()
                            if ($reread) { try { $remaining = @($reread.GetValueNames()) } catch {} }
                            $remainingChildren = @()
                            try { $remainingChildren = @(Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath $subkeyPath -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue) } catch {}
                            if ($remaining.Count -eq 0 -and $remainingChildren.Count -eq 0) {
                                Remove-Item -LiteralPath $subkeyPath -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                                & $writeScrubLog 'REGDEL' ("hive={0}\\{1} subkey deleted (became empty after scrub)" -f $runOnceExRoot, $subkeyName)
                            }
                        } catch {}
                    }
                } catch {
                    & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 3 RunOnceEx subkey={0} enumeration threw: {1}" -f $subkeyName, $_.Exception.Message)
                }
            }
        }
    } catch {
        & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 3 RunOnceEx subkey walk threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message)
    }

    # ==================================================================
    # STEP 3b (2026.05.20.2213s Bug U fence 2): scrub Winlogon
    # auto-login values that SetupComplete.cmd :ARM_AUTOLOGIN wrote
    # for Audit-Mode Administrator auto-logon
    # ==================================================================
    # ROOT CAUSE (5/19/2026 dump 1227):
    #   SetupComplete.cmd is wired into autounattend.xml as a
    #   RunSynchronousCommand on the SPECIALIZE pass. autounattend.xml
    #   reapplies on EVERY specialize pass, including the post-sysprep
    #   /oobe specialize pass. The pre-2213s :ARM_AUTOLOGIN unconditionally
    #   wrote AutoAdminLogon=1 + DefaultUserName=Administrator + blank
    #   DefaultPassword + AutoLogonCount=5 + ForceAutoLogon=1 every time.
    #   On the post-sysprep boot:
    #     1. Specialize pass re-fires SetupComplete.cmd
    #     2. :ARM_AUTOLOGIN re-arms the Winlogon values
    #     3. OOBE UI runs (language/region/account creation theater)
    #     4. OOBE finishes; Windows reads the still-armed Winlogon
    #        values and FORCES the next logon to local Administrator
    #        instead of the user account just created in OOBE
    #     5. DisableLockWorkstation=0x1 (also persisted) blocks the
    #        user from logging out of the forced session.
    #   Result: shipping-blocker OOBE bypass.
    #
    # FENCE DESIGN (two independent fences against the same bug):
    #   Fence 1 (SetupComplete.cmd, 2213s): early ImageState gate +
    #     completion-marker gate. If ImageState contains 'OOBE' or
    #     'COMPLETE', or if FirstBase-Updates-Complete.flag exists,
    #     :ARM_AUTOLOGIN is SKIPPED. This is the primary fence.
    #   Fence 2 (THIS STEP, 2213s): scrub the Winlogon auto-login
    #     values RIGHT BEFORE sysprep launches, so even if Fence 1
    #     ever fires too late or fails to detect the post-sysprep
    #     pass, sysprep's OOBE phase starts with a CLEAN Winlogon
    #     and cannot force-login to Administrator. Belt and braces.
    #
    # SCOPE:
    #   Only target the explicit list of values that :ARM_AUTOLOGIN
    #   wrote (or that field evidence on dump 1227 showed in place
    #   at the time of the bug). Do NOT touch normal Windows Winlogon
    #   values (Shell, Userinit, AutoRestartShell, Background,
    #   CachedLogonsCount, etc) - wildcard or partial-match deletion
    #   would risk breaking the OS shell. Each value is named
    #   explicitly below.
    #
    # ALSO:
    #   Re-disable the local Administrator account via
    #   `net user Administrator /active:no`. This is the inverse of
    #   :ARM_AUTOLOGIN's `net user Administrator "" /active:yes` and
    #   guarantees that even if a registry-write race re-armed any
    #   of the auto-login values, the account they target is
    #   disabled. OOBE-created user accounts are separate and
    #   unaffected.
    #
    # FAILURE MODE:
    #   Each Remove-ItemProperty is wrapped in try/catch; any single
    #   failure logs WARN and continues. Like the rest of the scrub,
    #   teardown failure does NOT block sysprep (caller wraps us in
    #   try/catch per the 2213r design).
    $winlogonAutoLoginValues = @(
        'AutoAdminLogon'
        'DefaultUserName'
        'DefaultPassword'
        'DefaultDomainName'
        'AutoLogonCount'
        'ForceAutoLogon'
        'AutoLogonSID'
        'SystemAutoLogon'
        'LastUsedUsername'
        'DisableLockWorkstation'
        'EnableFirstLogonAnimation'
    )
    $winlogonKey = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Winlogon'
    & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' ("STEP 3b (Bug U fence 2): scrubbing {0} Winlogon auto-login value(s) under {1}" -f $winlogonAutoLoginValues.Count, $winlogonKey)

    foreach ($valueName in $winlogonAutoLoginValues) {
        try {
            $existingProperty = Get-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $winlogonKey -Name $valueName -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            if ($null -ne $existingProperty) {
                try {
                    Remove-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $winlogonKey -Name $valueName -Force -ErrorAction Stop
                    $winlogonValuesRemovedCount++
                    & $writeScrubLog 'WLDEL' ("Bug U fence 2: hive={0} value={1} deleted" -f $winlogonKey, $valueName)
                } catch {
                    & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 3b Remove-ItemProperty hive={0} value={1} threw: {2}; trying reg.exe /f as fallback" -f $winlogonKey, $valueName, $_.Exception.Message)
                    try {
                        $regHivePath = $winlogonKey -replace '^HKLM:\\', 'HKLM\'
                        $null = & reg.exe delete $regHivePath /v $valueName /f 2>&1
                        if ($LASTEXITCODE -eq 0) {
                            $winlogonValuesRemovedCount++
                            & $writeScrubLog 'WLDEL' ("Bug U fence 2: hive={0} value={1} deleted via reg.exe fallback" -f $winlogonKey, $valueName)
                        } else {
                            & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 3b reg.exe delete hive={0} value={1} exit={2}" -f $winlogonKey, $valueName, $LASTEXITCODE)
                        }
                    } catch {
                        & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 3b reg.exe delete hive={0} value={1} threw: {2}" -f $winlogonKey, $valueName, $_.Exception.Message)
                    }
                }
            } else {
                & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' ("Bug U fence 2: hive={0} value={1} already absent (nothing to delete)" -f $winlogonKey, $valueName)
            }
        } catch {
            & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 3b probe hive={0} value={1} threw: {2}" -f $winlogonKey, $valueName, $_.Exception.Message)
        }
    }

    # Re-disable the local Administrator account (inverse of
    # SetupComplete.cmd :ARM_AUTOLOGIN's `net user Administrator "" /active:yes`).
    # OOBE-created user accounts are separate and unaffected.
    try {
        $netOutput = & net.exe user Administrator /active:no 2>&1
        $netExit = $LASTEXITCODE
        $netOutputJoined = ''
        try { $netOutputJoined = (@($netOutput) | ForEach-Object { [string]$_ }) -join ' | ' } catch {}
        & $writeScrubLog 'NET' ("Bug U fence 2: net user Administrator /active:no exit={0} output={1}" -f $netExit, $netOutputJoined)
    } catch {
        & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("Bug U fence 2: net user Administrator /active:no threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message)
    }

    # ==================================================================
    # STEP 4 (2026.05.20.2213t): script scrub (.ps1 and .cmd)
    # ==================================================================
    # User clarification (5/19/2026 3:04 PM): "Only scripts should be
    # scrubbed. Logs and state files can stay, any ps1 or cmd should be
    # removed from the device."
    #
    # Scrub roots and preserve paths are explicit lists (no wildcards
    # in the preserve list - exact case-insensitive prefix match) so a
    # future addition of a new state/log subdir is a one-line change
    # rather than a regex sprawl. The scrub deliberately does NOT touch
    # C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\* (the 2213t Fence 1 marker location).
    #
    # SELF-DELETION OF RUNNING LOOP'S .ps1:
    # PowerShell on Win11 opens .ps1 source files with FILE_SHARE_DELETE
    # during parse and does not hold a file lock for the duration of
    # script execution. Direct Remove-Item against Invoke-WindowsUpdate
    # Loop.ps1 (this very script) typically succeeds. If it fails (e.g.
    # AV transient lock), the path is appended to $script:_FbDelayedDelete
    # Paths and a detached cmd.exe shim cleans it up 8s after this
    # process exits. Either way, the file is gone by the time OOBE
    # boots.
    $scriptsDeletedCount = 0
    $scriptsDeleteDeferredCount = 0
    $selfPsScriptPath = $null
    try { $selfPsScriptPath = $PSCommandPath } catch {}
    if ([string]::IsNullOrEmpty($selfPsScriptPath)) {
        try { $selfPsScriptPath = $MyInvocation.MyCommand.Path } catch {}
    }
    & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' ("STEP 4 (2213t script scrub): starting; running script self-path={0}" -f ([string]$selfPsScriptPath))

    # Roots to recursively enumerate for .ps1 / .cmd files.
    # 5.4.3 (FBTGJB4): ProgramData\FirstBase also holds the operator kit
    # copied before the Audit Settings gate. STEP 4 used to skip that tree
    # (markers only), so .ps1/.cmd shipped into OOBE after Settings close.
    $scriptScrubRoots = @(
        $FbRoot
        $FbProgramDataFirstBase
    )
    $scriptScrubExtensions = @('.ps1', '.cmd', '.bat')
    # Case-insensitive prefix exclusion. A file is preserved if its full
    # path starts with any of these prefixes plus '\' (so an exact
    # directory hit also matches its descendants but a sibling like
    # 'StateAside' does not).
    $preservePathPrefixes = @(
        (Join-Path $FbRoot 'State')
        (Join-Path $FbRoot 'Logs')
        (Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase 'Logs')
    )

    foreach ($root in $scriptScrubRoots) {
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($root) -or -not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $root)) {
            & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' ("STEP 4 root {0} missing; skipping" -f $root)
            continue
        }
        $rootFiles = @()
        try {
            $rootFiles = @(Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath $root -Recurse -File -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)
        } catch {
            & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 4 enumeration of root {0} threw: {1}; some scripts may survive" -f $root, $_.Exception.Message)
            continue
        }
        foreach ($file in $rootFiles) {
            if (-not $file) { continue }
            $ext = ''
            try { $ext = ([System.IO.Path]::GetExtension($file.FullName)).ToLowerInvariant() } catch {}
            if ($scriptScrubExtensions -notcontains $ext) { continue }
            # Preserve if under State\ or Logs\ subtree.
            $preserve = $false
            foreach ($prefix in $preservePathPrefixes) {
                if ([string]::IsNullOrEmpty($prefix)) { continue }
                $prefixWithSep = $prefix.TrimEnd([char]92) + [char]92
                if ($file.FullName.Equals($prefix, [System.StringComparison]::OrdinalIgnoreCase) -or
                    $file.FullName.StartsWith($prefixWithSep, [System.StringComparison]::OrdinalIgnoreCase)) {
                    $preserve = $true
                    break
                }
            }
            if ($preserve) {
                & $writeScrubLog 'PRESERVE' ("STEP 4 preserve: {0} (under State/ or Logs/)" -f $file.FullName)
                continue
            }
            if ($splashOobeOverlayActive) {
                $leaf = ''
                try { $leaf = [string]$file.Name } catch { $leaf = '' }
                $splashKeepLeaves = @(
                    'Show-UpdateProgress.ps1'
                    'FirstBaseHandoffSplash.ps1'
                    'FirstBaseSplashSingleInstance.ps1'
                    'FirstBaseSplashWatcher.ps1'
                    'FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1'
                    'FirstBasePostSysprepOobeSoundArm.ps1'
                    'FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1'
                    'FirstBaseShowSplash.ps1'
                    'FirstBaseOpenSettingsAndFinish.ps1'
                    'FirstBaseConsoleSplashInstance.ps1'
                    'FirstBaseConsoleSplashRender.ps1'
                    'FirstBaseConsoleSplash.cmd'
                    'FirstBaseSplashLauncher.cmd'
                )
                $skipSplashScript = $false
                foreach ($kl in $splashKeepLeaves) {
                    if ($leaf.Equals($kl, [System.StringComparison]::OrdinalIgnoreCase)) {
                        $skipSplashScript = $true
                        break
                    }
                }
                if ($skipSplashScript) {
                    & $writeScrubLog 'PRESERVE' ("STEP 4 OOBE overlay: preserving splash payload {0}" -f $file.FullName)
                    continue
                }
            }
            # Attempt direct deletion.
            try {
                Remove-Item -LiteralPath $file.FullName -Force -ErrorAction Stop
                $scriptsDeletedCount++
                & $writeScrubLog 'SCRIPTDEL' ("STEP 4 deleted: {0}" -f $file.FullName)
            } catch {
                $script:_FbDelayedDeletePaths += $file.FullName
                $scriptsDeleteDeferredCount++
                & $writeScrubLog 'DEFER' ("STEP 4 Remove-Item {0} threw: {1}; deferred to FbDelayedScriptScrub.cmd" -f $file.FullName, $_.Exception.Message)
            }
        }
    }

    # Also delete C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\SetupComplete.cmd (the
    # autounattend.xml RunSynchronousCommand entry point). Once
    # sysprep is handed off this MUST NOT exist to be re-invoked on
    # the post-sysprep specialize pass. Combined with Fence 1
    # ProgramData markers (2226: arm-suppress OR .pipeline-completed)
    # this is a third independent defense against re-arming.
    $setupCompletePath = 'C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\SetupComplete.cmd'
    if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $setupCompletePath) {
        try {
            Remove-Item -LiteralPath $setupCompletePath -Force -ErrorAction Stop
            $scriptsDeletedCount++
            & $writeScrubLog 'SCRIPTDEL' ("STEP 4 deleted: {0} (autounattend.xml entry point; third fence against post-sysprep re-arming)" -f $setupCompletePath)
        } catch {
            $script:_FbDelayedDeletePaths += $setupCompletePath
            $scriptsDeleteDeferredCount++
            & $writeScrubLog 'DEFER' ("STEP 4 Remove-Item {0} threw: {1}; deferred to FbDelayedScriptScrub.cmd" -f $setupCompletePath, $_.Exception.Message)
        }
    } else {
        & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' ("STEP 4 {0} already absent (nothing to delete)" -f $setupCompletePath)
    }

    & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' ("STEP 4 (2213t): {0} script(s) deleted directly; {1} deferred to FbDelayedScriptScrub.cmd" -f $scriptsDeletedCount, $scriptsDeleteDeferredCount)

    # ----------------------------------------------------------------
    # STEP 4a: spawn delayed-delete .cmd helper if any path deferred.
    # ----------------------------------------------------------------
    # The helper waits 8s (long enough for the loop process to exit
    # via the sysprep handoff path that follows this scrub) then `del
    # /f /q` each deferred path. Final line is `(goto) 2>nul & del
    # "%~f0"` which removes the .cmd shim itself before exit, leaving
    # no trace.
    if ($script:_FbDelayedDeletePaths -and @($script:_FbDelayedDeletePaths).Count -gt 0) {
        $delayedCmdPath = $null
        try {
            $tempBase = $env:TEMP
            if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($tempBase)) { $tempBase = 'C:\Windows\Temp' }
            $delayedCmdPath = Join-Path $tempBase 'FbDelayedScriptScrub.cmd'
            $cmdLines = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[string]
            $cmdLines.Add('@echo off')
            $cmdLines.Add(':: 2026.05.20.2213t Bug U fence 1 + STEP 4 deferred-delete helper.')
            $cmdLines.Add(':: Auto-generated by Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub. Waits for the loop')
            $cmdLines.Add(':: process to exit, then deletes any .ps1 / .cmd files that Remove-Item')
            $cmdLines.Add(':: could not remove inline (e.g. transient lock from AV or PowerShell')
            $cmdLines.Add(':: parser hold on the running script). Self-deletes when done.')
            $cmdLines.Add('timeout /t 8 /nobreak >nul 2>nul')
            foreach ($deferredPath in @($script:_FbDelayedDeletePaths)) {
                if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($deferredPath)) { continue }
                $cmdLines.Add(('del /f /q "{0}" >nul 2>nul' -f $deferredPath))
            }
            $cmdLines.Add('(goto) 2>nul & del "%~f0"')
            [System.IO.File]::WriteAllLines($delayedCmdPath, $cmdLines.ToArray(), [System.Text.Encoding]::Default)
            & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' ("STEP 4a delayed-delete helper written: {0} ({1} path(s) queued)" -f $delayedCmdPath, @($script:_FbDelayedDeletePaths).Count)
        } catch {
            & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 4a delayed-delete helper write threw: {0}; deferred paths will survive into OOBE" -f $_.Exception.Message)
            $delayedCmdPath = $null
        }
        if ($delayedCmdPath -and (Test-Path -LiteralPath $delayedCmdPath)) {
            try {
                Start-Process -FilePath 'cmd.exe' -ArgumentList @('/c', $delayedCmdPath) -WindowStyle Hidden -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null
                & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' ("STEP 4a delayed-delete helper spawned (detached cmd.exe /c {0})" -f $delayedCmdPath)
            } catch {
                & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 4a delayed-delete helper spawn threw: {0}; helper .cmd written but never ran; deferred paths will survive into OOBE" -f $_.Exception.Message)
            }
        }
    } else {
        & $writeScrubLog 'INFO' "STEP 4a: no deferred paths; delayed-delete helper not spawned"
    }

    # ==================================================================
    # STEP 5: verification + final SCRUB-COMPLETE / SCRUB-INCOMPLETE line
    # ==================================================================
    # Re-poll processes, tasks, and registry. Any residual gets a
    # hard kill / second delete attempt. Final disposition log line
    # is the only line a tech needs to read to know if the scrub
    # achieved its goal.
    $residualProcs = @()
    try {
        $verifyAllProcs = Get-CimInstance -ClassName Win32_Process -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        foreach ($vproc in $verifyAllProcs) {
            if (-not $vproc) { continue }
            $vpid = $null
            try { $vpid = [int]$vproc.ProcessId } catch {}
            if ($null -eq $vpid -or $vpid -eq 0 -or $vpid -eq $PID) { continue }
            $vcmd = ''
            try { $vcmd = [string]$vproc.CommandLine } catch {}
            if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($vcmd)) { continue }
            $vmatched = $false
            foreach ($pattern in $procCommandLinePatterns) {
                if ($vcmd -match [regex]::Escape($pattern)) { $vmatched = $true; break }
            }
            if ($vmatched) { $residualProcs += $vproc }
        }
    } catch {
        & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 5 verification Win32_Process enumeration threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message)
    }
    foreach ($rproc in $residualProcs) {
        try {
            Stop-Process -Id ([int]$rproc.ProcessId) -Force -ErrorAction Stop
            & $writeScrubLog 'KILL2' ("STEP 5 hard-kill residual pid={0} name={1}" -f $rproc.ProcessId, $rproc.Name)
        } catch {
            $note = ("residual process pid={0} name={1} resisted Stop-Process: {2}" -f $rproc.ProcessId, $rproc.Name, $_.Exception.Message)
            $residualNotes += $note
            & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 5 {0}" -f $note)
        }
    }

    $residualTasks = @()
    try {
        $verifyQueryOutput = & schtasks.exe /Query /FO LIST 2>&1
        if ($LASTEXITCODE -eq 0 -and $verifyQueryOutput) {
            foreach ($vline in @($verifyQueryOutput)) {
                $vlineStr = [string]$vline
                if ($vlineStr -match '^\s*TaskName:\s*\\(FirstBase\\[^\s]+)\s*$') {
                    $residualTasks += $Matches[1]
                }
            }
        }
    } catch {
        & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 5 verification schtasks /Query threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message)
    }
    foreach ($rtask in $residualTasks) {
        try {
            $null = & schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $rtask /F 2>&1
            if ($LASTEXITCODE -eq 0) {
                & $writeScrubLog 'DELETE2' ("STEP 5 second-attempt delete task={0} succeeded" -f $rtask)
            } else {
                $note = ("residual task={0} resisted /Delete /F (exit={1})" -f $rtask, $LASTEXITCODE)
                $residualNotes += $note
                & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 5 {0}" -f $note)
            }
        } catch {
            $note = ("residual task={0} /Delete /F threw: {1}" -f $rtask, $_.Exception.Message)
            $residualNotes += $note
            & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 5 {0}" -f $note)
        }
    }

    $residualRegValues = @()
    foreach ($hive in $autostartHives) {
        try {
            if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $hive)) { continue }
            $hiveItem = Get-Item -LiteralPath $hive -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            if (-not $hiveItem) { continue }
            $valueNames = @()
            try { $valueNames = @($hiveItem.GetValueNames()) } catch {}
            foreach ($valueName in $valueNames) {
                if (& $isFirstBaseValueName $valueName) {
                    # NOTE (fb-dump-final-1134): STEP 3 preserves WuSplash RunOnce when OOBE overlay is
                    # active. Mirror that exclusion here so the intentionally-preserved value is not
                    # counted as an UNEXPECTED-RESIDUAL and the scrub is not falsely reported INCOMPLETE.
                    if ($splashOobeOverlayActive -and ($valueName -match '(?i)WuSplash')) { continue }
                    $residualRegValues += ("{0} :: {1}" -f $hive, $valueName)
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    # RunOnceEx subkey re-walk.
    try {
        if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $runOnceExRoot) {
            $rootItem2 = Get-Item -LiteralPath $runOnceExRoot -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            if ($rootItem2) {
                $rootValues2 = @()
                try { $rootValues2 = @($rootItem2.GetValueNames()) } catch {}
                foreach ($valueName in $rootValues2) {
                    if (& $isFirstBaseValueName $valueName) {
                        $residualRegValues += ("{0} :: {1}" -f $runOnceExRoot, $valueName)
                    }
                }
            }
            $subkeys2 = @()
            try { $subkeys2 = @(Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath $runOnceExRoot -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue) } catch {}
            foreach ($subkey in $subkeys2) {
                if (-not $subkey) { continue }
                try {
                    $subkeyItem = Get-Item -LiteralPath $subkey.PSPath -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                    if (-not $subkeyItem) { continue }
                    $subkeyValueNames = @()
                    try { $subkeyValueNames = @($subkeyItem.GetValueNames()) } catch {}
                    foreach ($valueName in $subkeyValueNames) {
                        if (& $isFirstBaseValueName $valueName) {
                            $residualRegValues += ("{0}\\{1} :: {2}" -f $runOnceExRoot, $subkey.PSChildName, $valueName)
                        }
                    }
                } catch {}
            }
        }
    } catch {}
    foreach ($rval in $residualRegValues) {
        $note = ("residual registry value: {0}" -f $rval)
        $residualNotes += $note
        & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 5 {0}" -f $note)
    }

    # ----------------------------------------------------------------
    # 2026.05.20.2213t: residual .ps1 / .cmd verification.
    # Re-enumerates the same scrub roots and reports any surviving
    # script files. Files that are in $script:_FbDelayedDeletePaths
    # are EXPECTED-RESIDUAL (the helper will delete them after the
    # loop exits) so they are reported with a 'DEFERRED' qualifier
    # and DO NOT contribute to the SCRUB-INCOMPLETE count. Any other
    # survivor is unexpected and DOES contribute.
    $deferredSet = @{}
    foreach ($dp in @($script:_FbDelayedDeletePaths)) {
        if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($dp)) {
            $deferredSet[$dp.ToLowerInvariant()] = $true
        }
    }
    # NOTE (fb-dump-final-1134): STEP 4 intentionally preserves these leaf filenames when
    # splashOobeOverlayActive. STEP 5 must mirror the same keep-list or it falsely counts them
    # as UNEXPECTED-RESIDUAL and raises a spurious SCRUB-INCOMPLETE (12 files in dump 1134).
    $overlayPreservedLeafSet = [System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]]::new([System.StringComparer]::OrdinalIgnoreCase)
    if ($splashOobeOverlayActive) {
        foreach ($kl in @(
            'Show-UpdateProgress.ps1'
            'FirstBaseHandoffSplash.ps1'
            'FirstBaseSplashSingleInstance.ps1'
            'FirstBaseSplashWatcher.ps1'
            'FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1'
            'FirstBasePostSysprepOobeSoundArm.ps1'
            'FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1'
            'FirstBaseShowSplash.ps1'
            'FirstBaseOpenSettingsAndFinish.ps1'
            'FirstBaseConsoleSplashInstance.ps1'
            'FirstBaseConsoleSplashRender.ps1'
            'FirstBaseConsoleSplash.cmd'
            'FirstBaseSplashLauncher.cmd'
        )) { [void]$overlayPreservedLeafSet.Add($kl) }
    }
    $scriptResidualUnexpected = 0
    $scriptResidualDeferred = 0
    foreach ($root in $scriptScrubRoots) {
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($root) -or -not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $root)) { continue }
        $verifyFiles = @()
        try {
            $verifyFiles = @(Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath $root -Recurse -File -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)
        } catch {
            & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 5 script-residual enumeration of root {0} threw: {1}" -f $root, $_.Exception.Message)
            continue
        }
        foreach ($vfile in $verifyFiles) {
            if (-not $vfile) { continue }
            $vext = ''
            try { $vext = ([System.IO.Path]::GetExtension($vfile.FullName)).ToLowerInvariant() } catch {}
            if ($scriptScrubExtensions -notcontains $vext) { continue }
            # Skip files in State/ or Logs/ subtree (intentionally preserved).
            $skip = $false
            foreach ($prefix in $preservePathPrefixes) {
                if ([string]::IsNullOrEmpty($prefix)) { continue }
                $prefixWithSep = $prefix.TrimEnd([char]92) + [char]92
                if ($vfile.FullName.Equals($prefix, [System.StringComparison]::OrdinalIgnoreCase) -or
                    $vfile.FullName.StartsWith($prefixWithSep, [System.StringComparison]::OrdinalIgnoreCase)) {
                    $skip = $true; break
                }
            }
            if ($skip) { continue }
            # NOTE (fb-dump-final-1134): skip files intentionally preserved by STEP 4 OOBE overlay.
            if ($overlayPreservedLeafSet.Count -gt 0) {
                $vleaf = ''
                try { $vleaf = [string]$vfile.Name } catch {}
                if ($overlayPreservedLeafSet.Contains($vleaf)) {
                    & $writeScrubLog 'OVERLAY-PRESERVED' ("STEP 5 script residual (expected; preserved by STEP 4 OOBE overlay for post-sysprep splash): {0}" -f $vfile.FullName)
                    continue
                }
            }
            if ($deferredSet.ContainsKey($vfile.FullName.ToLowerInvariant())) {
                $scriptResidualDeferred++
                & $writeScrubLog 'DEFERRED' ("STEP 5 script residual (expected; queued in FbDelayedScriptScrub.cmd): {0}" -f $vfile.FullName)
            } else {
                $scriptResidualUnexpected++
                $note = ("residual script (not queued for delayed-delete): {0}" -f $vfile.FullName)
                $residualNotes += $note
                & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 5 {0}" -f $note)
            }
        }
    }
    # Also verify the autounattend entry point.
    if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $setupCompletePath) {
        if ($deferredSet.ContainsKey($setupCompletePath.ToLowerInvariant())) {
            $scriptResidualDeferred++
            & $writeScrubLog 'DEFERRED' ("STEP 5 script residual (expected; queued in FbDelayedScriptScrub.cmd): {0}" -f $setupCompletePath)
        } else {
            $scriptResidualUnexpected++
            $note = ("residual script (not queued for delayed-delete): {0}" -f $setupCompletePath)
            $residualNotes += $note
            & $writeScrubLog 'WARN' ("STEP 5 {0}" -f $note)
        }
    }

    $scrubElapsed = ((Get-Date) - $scrubStarted).TotalSeconds
    if ($residualNotes.Count -eq 0) {
        $finalLine = ("SCRUB-COMPLETE: {0} process(es) killed; {1} scheduled task(s) deleted; {2} FirstBase registry value(s) removed; {3} Winlogon auto-login value(s) scrubbed (Bug U fence 2); {4} script(s) deleted directly + {5} deferred to FbDelayedScriptScrub.cmd (2213t STEP 4); elapsed={6:N1}s; OOBE may now launch with zero FirstBase artifacts live, a clean Winlogon (no force-login to Administrator), and no .ps1/.cmd payload on disk." -f $procsKilledCount, $tasksDeletedCount, $regValuesRemovedCount, $winlogonValuesRemovedCount, $scriptsDeletedCount, $scriptResidualDeferred, $scrubElapsed)
        & $writeScrubLog 'DONE' $finalLine
        Write-FbLog ("Pre-OOBE scrub gate (2213r Bug NN + 2213s Bug U fence 2 + 2213t script scrub): {0}" -f $finalLine) 'WARN'
    } else {
        $finalLine = ("SCRUB-INCOMPLETE: {0} process(es) killed; {1} scheduled task(s) deleted; {2} FirstBase registry value(s) removed; {3} Winlogon auto-login value(s) scrubbed (Bug U fence 2); {4} script(s) deleted directly + {5} deferred + {6} UNEXPECTED-RESIDUAL (2213t STEP 4); {7} total residual(s); elapsed={8:N1}s; PROCEEDING TO SYSPREP ANYWAY per spec (caller try/catch wraps us; teardown failure does not block OOBE handoff)." -f $procsKilledCount, $tasksDeletedCount, $regValuesRemovedCount, $winlogonValuesRemovedCount, $scriptsDeletedCount, $scriptResidualDeferred, $scriptResidualUnexpected, $residualNotes.Count, $scrubElapsed)
        & $writeScrubLog 'DONE' $finalLine
        foreach ($note in $residualNotes) {
            & $writeScrubLog 'RESIDUAL' $note
        }
        Write-FbLog ("Pre-OOBE scrub gate (2213r Bug NN + 2213s Bug U fence 2 + 2213t script scrub): {0}" -f $finalLine) 'ERROR'
    }

    return $true
}

function Register-FbOpenWindowsActivationSettingsRunOnce {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2026.05.20.2213v: arm HKLM RunOnce so the first interactive logon
        after OOBE opens Windows Settings -> Activation (ms-settings:activation).

    .NOTES
        - Uses value name OpenWindowsActivationSettingsOnce (NOT FirstBase-*)
          so Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub STEP 3 does not delete it.
        - Command is cmd.exe / start / protocol URI only (no .ps1 after 2213t scrub).
        - Call only from Invoke-SysprepToOobe success path (sysprep exit 0).
        - Idempotency marker vs registry: if the marker file exists but the
          RunOnce value is missing (scrub, manual delete, or partial failure),
          we WARN, delete the stale marker, and re-run the full register path
          instead of silently skipping (field: F:\fb-dump 2026-05-20).
    #>
    $rk = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce'
    $name = $script:FbActivationRunOnceName
    $cmdLine = $script:FbActivationRunOnceCommand
    $markerPath = $script:FbActivationRunOnceMarkerPath

    function Get-FbActivationRunOnceValueFromRegistry {
        try {
            $props = Get-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $rk -ErrorAction Stop
            if ($props.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains $name) {
                return ([string]$props.$name).Trim()
            }
        } catch {}
        return $null
    }

    try {
        if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $markerPath) {
            $existingVal = Get-FbActivationRunOnceValueFromRegistry
            if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($existingVal)) {
                Write-FbLog ("2213v: OpenWindowsActivationSettingsOnce RunOnce arming skipped (marker + registry present); read-back='{0}'." -f $existingVal) 'INFO'
                return
            }
            # Stale marker: we thought we armed, but HKLM does not carry the value.
            Write-FbLog ('2213v: WARN stale activation RunOnce marker at ''{0}'' but HKLM RunOnce\{1} is missing or empty - removing marker and re-arming.' -f $markerPath, $name) 'WARN'
            try {
                Remove-Item -LiteralPath $markerPath -Force -ErrorAction Stop
            } catch {
                Write-FbLog ("2213v: stale marker delete threw (continuing to Set-ItemProperty): {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
            }
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2213v: activation RunOnce marker/registry probe threw (continuing): {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $rk)) {
            New-Item -Path $rk -Force | Out-Null
        }
        Set-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $rk -Name $name -Value $cmdLine -Type String -Force
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2213v: FAILED to register HKLM RunOnce OpenWindowsActivationSettingsOnce for Activation settings (non-blocking): {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        return
    }

    $readBack = Get-FbActivationRunOnceValueFromRegistry
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($readBack)) {
        Write-FbLog ('2213v: RunOnce read-back after Set-ItemProperty: {0} property ''{1}'' absent or empty (expected command line).' -f $rk, $name) 'WARN'
    } else {
        Write-FbLog ("2213v: RunOnce read-back after Set-ItemProperty: '{0}'='{1}'" -f $name, $readBack) 'INFO'
    }

    try {
        $markerDir = Split-Path -Parent $markerPath
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $markerDir)) {
            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $markerDir -Force | Out-Null
        }
        Set-Content -LiteralPath $markerPath -Value ("Armed at {0} (PID {1})" -f (Get-Date -Format 'o'), $PID) -Encoding UTF8 -Force
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2213v: RunOnce registered but FAILED to write idempotency marker {0} (non-blocking): {1}" -f $markerPath, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    Write-FbLog ('2213v: registered HKLM RunOnce\{0} for one-shot Windows Activation on first interactive logon; STEP 3 scrub (?i)^!?FirstBase does not match this value name.' -f $name) 'INFO'

    try {
        $snd = $null
        try { $snd = (Get-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $rk -Name $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundRunOnceName -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).$script:FbDeferredOobeSoundRunOnceName } catch {}
        $sndNote = if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace([string]$snd)) { 'absent' } else { 'present' }
        Write-FbLog ("2213v: 2224 peer diag after Activation RunOnce arm: OpenWindowsSoundSettingsDeferredOnce={0}." -f $sndNote) 'INFO'
    } catch {}
}

function Set-FbDeferredOobeSoundDeliveryArmMarker {
    param(
        [string]$Note = 'armed',
        [string]$Tr = ''
    )
    $markerPath = $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundRunOnceMarkerPath
    try {
        $markerDir = Split-Path -Parent $markerPath
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $markerDir)) {
            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $markerDir -Force | Out-Null
        }
        $body = if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($Tr)) {
            ("{0} at {1} (PID {2})" -f $Note, (Get-Date -Format 'o'), $PID)
        } else {
            ("{0} at {1} (PID {2}) TR={3}" -f $Note, (Get-Date -Format 'o'), $PID, $Tr)
        }
        Set-Content -LiteralPath $markerPath -Value $body -Encoding UTF8 -Force
        Write-FbLog ("2229: wrote deferred Sound delivery arm marker {0} ({1})." -f $markerPath, $Note) 'INFO'
        return $true
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2229: FAILED to write deferred Sound delivery arm marker {0}: {1}" -f $markerPath, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        return $false
    }
}

function Get-FbDeferredOobeSoundProgramDataTr {
    $pdDir = $FbProgramDataFirstBase
    $pdPs1 = Join-Path $pdDir 'FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1'
    $ps1 = $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundPs1
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $ps1)) { return $null }
    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $pdDir)) {
            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $pdDir -Force | Out-Null
        }
        Copy-Item -LiteralPath $ps1 -Destination $pdPs1 -Force -ErrorAction Stop
        Write-FbLog ("2229: staged deferred Sound script to {0}." -f $pdPs1) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2229: WARN could not stage deferred Sound to ProgramData ({0}); TR uses Setup path." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        $pdPs1 = $ps1
    }
    $finalize = $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundFinalizePs1
    if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $finalize) {
        try {
            $pdFin = Join-Path $pdDir 'FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1'
            Copy-Item -LiteralPath $finalize -Destination $pdFin -Force -ErrorAction Stop
            Write-FbLog ("2229: staged operator finalize script to {0}." -f $pdFin) 'INFO'
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("2229: WARN could not stage operator finalize to ProgramData ({0})." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
    }
    return ('powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -WindowStyle Hidden -File "{0}"' -f $pdPs1)
}

function Test-FbDeferredOobeSoundOnLogonTaskPresent {
    $tn = $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundOnLogonTaskName
    try {
        $q = Start-Process -FilePath 'schtasks.exe' -ArgumentList @('/Query', '/TN', $tn, '/FO', 'LIST', '/V') -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru -Wait -ErrorAction Stop
        if ($null -ne $q -and $q.ExitCode -eq 0) { return $true }
    } catch {}
    return $false
}

function Get-FbDeferredOobeSoundBootstrapProgramDataTr {
    [void](Ensure-FbDeferredOobeSoundProgramDataKit -Reason 'Get-FbDeferredOobeSoundBootstrapProgramDataTr')
    $pdBootstrap = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase 'FirstBaseOobeDeferredSoundBootstrap.ps1'
    $pdDeferred = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase 'FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1'
    if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $pdBootstrap) {
        return ('powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -WindowStyle Hidden -File "{0}"' -f $pdBootstrap)
    }
    if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $pdDeferred) {
        return ('powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -WindowStyle Hidden -File "{0}"' -f $pdDeferred)
    }
    return $null
}

function Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundActiveSetup {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2234: Active Setup Installed Components — runs once per user at first logon (OOBE defaultuser0).
    .NOTES
        fb-dump-final-1121: HKLM RunOnce/Run and ONLOGON schtasks did not launch deferred Sound during OOBE.
    #>
    $tr = Get-FbDeferredOobeSoundBootstrapProgramDataTr
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($tr)) {
        Write-FbLog '2234: Active Setup NOT armed — bootstrap/deferred PS1 missing under ProgramData.' 'WARN'
        return $false
    }
    $guid = $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundActiveSetupGuid
    $keyPath = "HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Active Setup\Installed Components\$guid"
    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $keyPath)) {
            New-Item -Path $keyPath -Force | Out-Null
        }
        Set-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $keyPath -Name '(default)' -Value 'FirstBase OOBE deferred Sound handoff' -Type String -Force
        Set-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $keyPath -Name 'StubPath' -Value $tr -Type String -Force
        Set-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $keyPath -Name 'Version' -Value '1,0' -Type String -Force
        $mk = $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundActiveSetupMarkerPath
        $mkDir = Split-Path -Parent $mk
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $mkDir)) {
            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $mkDir -Force | Out-Null
        }
        Set-Content -LiteralPath $mk -Value ("Armed at {0} (PID {1}) StubPath={2}" -f (Get-Date -Format 'o'), $PID, $tr) -Encoding UTF8 -Force
        Write-FbLog ("2234: Active Setup armed ({0}) StubPath -> bootstrap/deferred Sound." -f $guid) 'INFO'
        return $true
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2234: Active Setup arm failed: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        return $false
    }
}

function Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundProgramDataStartup {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2236: All Users Startup launcher — runs at each user logon including OOBE defaultuser0.
    .NOTES
        fb-dump-final-1121/1220: HKLM RunOnce/Run, Active Setup, and ONLOGON schtasks did not open Settings during OOBE.
    #>
    [void](Ensure-FbDeferredOobeSoundProgramDataKit -Reason 'Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundProgramDataStartup')
    $bootstrapPd = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase 'FirstBaseOobeDeferredSoundBootstrap.ps1'
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $bootstrapPd)) {
        Write-FbLog '2236: ProgramData Startup NOT armed — bootstrap PS1 missing.' 'WARN'
        return $false
    }
    $startupDir = $script:FbProgramDataAllUsersStartup
    $startupCmd = Join-Path $startupDir $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundStartupCmdLeaf
    # 2293: VBScript launcher — wscript.exe runs .vbs with no console window (window style 0); eliminates the brief CMD flash from the old .cmd launcher.
    $vbsBody = @"
Set oShell = CreateObject("WScript.Shell")
oShell.Run "powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -WindowStyle Hidden -File ""$bootstrapPd""", 0, False
"@
    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $startupDir)) {
            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $startupDir -Force | Out-Null
        }
        Set-Content -LiteralPath $startupCmd -Value $vbsBody -Encoding ASCII -Force
        $mk = $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundStartupMarkerPath
        Set-Content -LiteralPath $mk -Value ("Armed at {0} (PID {1}) path={2}" -f (Get-Date -Format 'o'), $PID, $startupCmd) -Encoding UTF8 -Force
        Write-FbLog ("2236: ProgramData All Users Startup armed -> {0}" -f $startupCmd) 'INFO'
        [void](Set-FbDeferredOobeSoundDeliveryArmMarker -Note 'programdata-startup' -Tr $startupCmd)
        return $true
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2236: ProgramData Startup arm failed: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        return $false
    }
}

function Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundHklmRunFallback {
  <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2244: HKLM Run primary delivery — points to bootstrap (not deferred sound directly) so
        stale-lock detection in the bootstrap can recover after a killed OOBE session.
        Runs at every interactive logon until removed by finalize.
    .NOTES
        2229: Was HKLM Run fallback pointing to FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 directly.
        2244: Promoted to primary + redirected to FirstBaseOobeDeferredSoundBootstrap.ps1 so
        all delivery paths (Active Setup, Startup, HKLM Run) go through the bootstrap and its
        stale-lock detection. Fixes Settings opening at real-user logon instead of OOBE
        (root cause: bootstrap lock left from killed OOBE session was blocking Active Setup /
        Startup; HKLM Run bypassed bootstrap and fired correctly only at real-user logon).
  #>
    $rk = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run'
    $name = $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundRunKeyName
    $tr = Get-FbDeferredOobeSoundBootstrapProgramDataTr
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($tr)) {
        Write-FbLog '2244: HKLM Run deferred Sound NOT armed - bootstrap/deferred PS1 missing under ProgramData.' 'ERROR'
        return $false
    }
    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $rk)) {
            New-Item -Path $rk -Force | Out-Null
        }
        Set-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $rk -Name $name -Value $tr -Type String -Force
        $read = $null
        try { $read = (Get-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $rk -Name $name -ErrorAction Stop).$name } catch {}
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace([string]$read)) {
            Write-FbLog ("2244: HKLM Run\{0} read-back empty after Set-ItemProperty." -f $name) 'WARN'
            return $false
        }
        Write-FbLog ("2244: registered HKLM Run\{0} -> bootstrap TR='{1}'." -f $name, $read) 'INFO'
        try {
            $mk = $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundRunKeyMarkerPath
            $mkDir = Split-Path -Parent $mk
            if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $mkDir)) {
                New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $mkDir -Force | Out-Null
            }
            Set-Content -LiteralPath $mk -Value ("Armed at {0} (PID {1})" -f (Get-Date -Format 'o'), $PID) -Encoding UTF8 -Force
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("2244: HKLM Run armed but marker write failed: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
        [void](Set-FbDeferredOobeSoundDeliveryArmMarker -Note 'hklm-run-bootstrap' -Tr $tr)
        return $true
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2244: FAILED to register HKLM Run deferred Sound: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        return $false
    }
}

function Resolve-FbOobeKitScriptSource {
    param([Parameter(Mandatory)][string]$Leaf)
    foreach ($base in @($FbRoot, $FbProgramDataFirstBase, $PSScriptRoot)) {
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace([string]$base)) { continue }
        $candidate = Join-Path $base $Leaf
        if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $candidate) { return $candidate }
    }
    return $null
}

function Ensure-FbDeferredOobeSoundProgramDataKit {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2237: Stage seal-path scripts to ProgramData from Setup, ProgramData, or loop script dir.
    .NOTES
        fb-dump/field: bootstrap missing under ProgramData when manual -File fails; sysprep kit copy
        ran too late or parent died before Copy-FbOobeOperatorProgramDataKit; Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundRunOnce
        previously copied only FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1.
    #>
    param([string]$Reason = 'unspecified')
    $pdDir = $FbProgramDataFirstBase
    $requiredLeaves = @(
        'FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1'
        'FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1'
        'FirstBaseOobeDeferredSoundBootstrap.ps1'
        'FirstBasePostSysprepOobeSoundArm.ps1'
        # 2239: FirstBaseDetachedFirmwareReboot.ps1 removed from kit — written inline by Start-FbDetachedFirmwareRebootAfterSysprepValidation.
    )
    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $pdDir)) {
            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $pdDir -Force | Out-Null
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2237: could not create ProgramData dir for kit ({0}): {1}" -f $Reason, $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
        return 0
    }
    $copied = 0
    $missingRequired = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[string]
    foreach ($leaf in $requiredLeaves) {
        $dst = Join-Path $pdDir $leaf
        if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $dst) { continue }
        $src = Resolve-FbOobeKitScriptSource -Leaf $leaf
        if (-not $src) {
            if ($leaf -in @('FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1', 'FirstBaseOobeDeferredSoundBootstrap.ps1', 'FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1')) {
                $missingRequired.Add($leaf)
            }
            continue
        }
        try {
            Copy-Item -LiteralPath $src -Destination $dst -Force -ErrorAction Stop
            $copied++
            Write-FbLog ("2237: staged {0} -> {1} (reason={2}, src={3})." -f $leaf, $dst, $Reason, $src) 'INFO'
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("2237: kit copy failed {0} -> {1} (reason={2}): {3}" -f $src, $dst, $Reason, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
            if ($leaf -in @('FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1', 'FirstBaseOobeDeferredSoundBootstrap.ps1', 'FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1')) {
                $missingRequired.Add($leaf)
            }
        }
    }
    if ($missingRequired.Count -gt 0) {
        Write-FbLog ("2237: REQUIRED seal-path script(s) still missing under {0} after Ensure (reason={1}): {2}" -f $pdDir, $Reason, ($missingRequired -join ', ')) 'ERROR'
    }
    return $copied
}

function Copy-FbOobeOperatorProgramDataKit {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2230/2232: Stage operator scripts to ProgramData before sysprep deletes Setup copies.
    .NOTES
        2232 three-step seal path: deferred Sound, finalize, detached ONLOGON arm worker only.
        Manual triage (FirstBaseShowSplash) copied when present but not required for automation.
        2237: Ensure-FbDeferredOobeSoundProgramDataKit resolves sources beyond FbRoot only.
    #>
    $pdDir = $FbProgramDataFirstBase
    $pdTools = Join-Path $pdDir 'Tools'
    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $pdDir)) {
            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $pdDir -Force | Out-Null
        }
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $pdTools)) {
            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $pdTools -Force | Out-Null
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2230: could not create ProgramData operator dirs: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        return
    }
    $copied = Ensure-FbDeferredOobeSoundProgramDataKit -Reason 'Copy-FbOobeOperatorProgramDataKit'
    $manualTriageLeaves = @(
        'Show-UpdateProgress.ps1'
        'FirstBaseSplashSingleInstance.ps1'
        'FirstBaseSplashLauncher.cmd'
        'FirstBaseShowSplash.ps1'
        'FirstBaseOpenSettingsAndFinish.ps1'
        'FirstBaseHardwareCheck.ps1'
        'FirstBaseHandoffSplash.ps1'
    )
    foreach ($leaf in $manualTriageLeaves) {
        $src = Resolve-FbOobeKitScriptSource -Leaf $leaf
        if (-not $src) { continue }
        $dst = Join-Path $pdDir $leaf
        try {
            Copy-Item -LiteralPath $src -Destination $dst -Force -ErrorAction Stop
            $copied++
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("2230: ProgramData kit copy failed {0} -> {1}: {2}" -f $src, $dst, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
    }
    # 2026.07.21: Show-SplashNow.cmd removed from the payload; the splash is launched via
    # FirstBaseShowSplash.ps1 directly, so nothing is staged into the ProgramData Tools dir.
    Write-FbLog ("2230: ProgramData operator kit staged ({0} file(s)) under {1}." -f $copied, $pdDir) 'INFO'
}

function Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundRunOnce {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2026.05.20.2218: Arm HKLM RunOnce so the next Windows boot (after firmware /fw) runs
        FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 once - open configurable ms-settings URI (default sound), wait for Settings exit, operator finalize, shutdown /s.
        2220: RunOnce command uses powershell without -NonInteractive (interactive-host parity with splash arming).
        2222: RunOnce host uses -WindowStyle Normal (Hidden obscured operator visibility on some OOBE boots).

    .NOTES
        Value name OpenWindowsSoundSettingsDeferredOnce does NOT match (?i)^!?FirstBase (STEP 3 scrub).
        Distinct from legacy 2216 OpenWindowsSoundSettingsOnce (still cleared by Clear-FbSoundRunOnce2216Artifacts).
        Call only from Invoke-SysprepToOobe success path when OOBE splash overlay was active this boot.
    #>
    [void](Ensure-FbDeferredOobeSoundProgramDataKit -Reason 'Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundRunOnce')
    $rk = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce'
    $name = $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundRunOnceName
    $markerPath = $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundRunOnceMarkerPath
    $ps1 = $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundPs1
    $pdDir = $FbProgramDataFirstBase
    $pdPs1 = Join-Path $pdDir 'FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1'
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $pdPs1)) {
        $resolvedDeferred = Resolve-FbOobeKitScriptSource -Leaf 'FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1'
        if ($resolvedDeferred) { $pdPs1 = $resolvedDeferred }
    }
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $pdPs1) -and (Test-Path -LiteralPath $ps1)) {
        $pdPs1 = $ps1
    }
    $cmdLine = ('powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -WindowStyle Hidden -File "{0}"' -f $pdPs1)

    function Get-FbDeferredOobeSoundRunOnceFromRegistry {
        try {
            $props = Get-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $rk -ErrorAction Stop
            if ($props.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains $name) {
                return ([string]$props.$name).Trim()
            }
        } catch {}
        return $null
    }

    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $ps1)) {
        Write-FbLog ("2218: deferred OOBE Sound RunOnce NOT armed - missing script '{0}'." -f $ps1) 'ERROR'
        return
    }

    try {
        if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $markerPath) {
            $existingVal = Get-FbDeferredOobeSoundRunOnceFromRegistry
            if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($existingVal)) {
                Write-FbLog ("2218: OpenWindowsSoundSettingsDeferredOnce RunOnce arming skipped (marker + registry present); read-back='{0}'." -f $existingVal) 'INFO'
                return
            }
            Write-FbLog ("2218: WARN stale deferred Sound RunOnce marker at '{0}' but HKLM RunOnce\{1} missing - removing marker and re-arming." -f $markerPath, $name) 'WARN'
            try { Remove-Item -LiteralPath $markerPath -Force -ErrorAction Stop } catch {}
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2218: deferred Sound RunOnce marker/registry probe threw (continuing): {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $rk)) {
            New-Item -Path $rk -Force | Out-Null
        }
        Set-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $rk -Name $name -Value $cmdLine -Type String -Force
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2218: FAILED to register HKLM RunOnce deferred OOBE Sound (non-blocking): {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        return
    }

    $readBack = Get-FbDeferredOobeSoundRunOnceFromRegistry
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($readBack)) {
        Write-FbLog ("2218: RunOnce read-back after Set-ItemProperty: {0} property '{1}' absent or empty." -f $rk, $name) 'WARN'
    } else {
        Write-FbLog ("2218: RunOnce read-back after Set-ItemProperty: '{0}'='{1}'" -f $name, $readBack) 'INFO'
    }

    [void](Set-FbDeferredOobeSoundDeliveryArmMarker -Note 'runonce-armed' -Tr $cmdLine)

    Write-FbLog '2232: HKLM RunOnce armed (belt); primary next-boot delivery is ONLOGON task + HKLM Run fallback (not RunOnce alone on defaultuser0 OOBE).' 'INFO'
    Write-FbLog '2218: registered HKLM RunOnce OpenWindowsSoundSettingsDeferredOnce for one-shot Sound after next boot (post-firmware).' 'INFO'

    # 2233 (fb-dump-final-1016): arm HKLM Run synchronously with RunOnce so defaultuser0 OOBE logon
    # still launches deferred Sound if parent dies before Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundDeliveryStack.
    try {
        if (Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundHklmRunFallback) {
            Write-FbLog '2244: HKLM Run OpenWindowsSoundSettingsDeferredLogon -> bootstrap armed immediately after RunOnce (primary OOBE delivery, stale-lock-aware).' 'INFO'
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2244: Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundHklmRunFallback after RunOnce threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    try {
        if (Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundActiveSetup) {
            Write-FbLog '2234: Active Setup armed after RunOnce (OOBE defaultuser0 first-logon delivery).' 'INFO'
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2234: Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundActiveSetup after RunOnce threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    try {
        if (Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundProgramDataStartup) {
            Write-FbLog '2236: ProgramData All Users Startup armed after RunOnce (OOBE logon delivery).' 'INFO'
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2236: Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundProgramDataStartup after RunOnce threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    try {
        $act = $null
        try { $act = (Get-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $rk -Name $script:FbActivationRunOnceName -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).$script:FbActivationRunOnceName } catch {}
        $actNote = if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace([string]$act)) { 'absent' } else { 'present' }
        Write-FbLog ("2218: 2224 peer diag after deferred Sound RunOnce arm: OpenWindowsActivationSettingsOnce={0} (first-logon RunOnce order not guaranteed by value name)." -f $actNote) 'INFO'
    } catch {}

}

function Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundOnLogonTask {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2227/2229/2232: ONLOGON scheduled-task fallback when HKLM RunOnce does not run during OOBE defaultuser0 logon.
    .NOTES
        Task name FirstBase\DeferredOobeSoundOnLogon is NOT matched by pre-OOBE scrub (?i)^!?FirstBase.
        FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 deletes the task after operator Settings close.
        2232: Use -BlockingWait:$false from parent loop (fb-dump-final-859); detached worker uses blocking create.
    #>
    param(
        [bool]$BlockingWait = $true
    )
    $tn = $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundOnLogonTaskName
    $tr = Get-FbDeferredOobeSoundProgramDataTr
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($tr)) {
        Write-FbLog '2229: DeferredOobeSoundOnLogon NOT armed - missing deferred PS1.' 'ERROR'
        return $false
    }
    if (Test-FbDeferredOobeSoundOnLogonTaskPresent) {
        Write-FbLog ("2229: {0} already present; refreshing markers only (idempotent)." -f $tn) 'INFO'
        [void](Set-FbDeferredOobeSoundDeliveryArmMarker -Note 'onlogon-task-already-present' -Tr $tr)
        try {
            $markerPath = $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundOnLogonTaskMarkerPath
            $markerDir = Split-Path -Parent $markerPath
            if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $markerDir)) {
                New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $markerDir -Force | Out-Null
            }
            Set-Content -LiteralPath $markerPath -Value ("Armed at {0} (PID {1}) TR={2}" -f (Get-Date -Format 'o'), $PID, $tr) -Encoding UTF8 -Force
        } catch {}
        return $true
    }
    Write-FbLog ("2229: arming {0} ONLOGON fallback (before RunOnce/firmware; fb-dump-final-1326 race fix)." -f $tn) 'INFO'
    try {
        $null = Start-Process -FilePath 'schtasks.exe' -ArgumentList @('/Delete', '/TN', $tn, '/F') -WindowStyle Hidden -Wait -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
    } catch {}
    $created = $false
    try {
        $createArgs = @('/Create', '/TN', $tn, '/SC', 'ONLOGON', '/RL', 'HIGHEST', '/IT', '/F', '/TR', $tr)
        if ($BlockingWait) {
            $p = Start-Process -FilePath 'schtasks.exe' -ArgumentList $createArgs -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru -Wait -ErrorAction Stop
        } else {
            $p = Start-Process -FilePath 'schtasks.exe' -ArgumentList $createArgs -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru -ErrorAction Stop
            if ($null -ne $p -and -not $p.HasExited) {
                Write-FbLog ("2232: schtasks /Create {0} launched without -Wait (PID {1}); verify via detached worker or /Query." -f $tn, $p.Id) 'INFO'
            }
        }
        if ($null -ne $p -and $p.HasExited -and $p.ExitCode -eq 0) {
            Set-FbScheduledTaskRobustSettings -TaskName $tn | Out-Null
            if (Test-FbDeferredOobeSoundOnLogonTaskPresent) {
                $created = $true
            } else {
                Write-FbLog ("2229: schtasks /Create {0} exit 0 but /Query did not find task (non-blocking)." -f $tn) 'WARN'
            }
        } elseif ($null -ne $p -and $p.HasExited) {
            $ec = $p.ExitCode
            Write-FbLog ("2229: schtasks /Create {0} returned exit {1}." -f $tn, $ec) 'WARN'
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2229: FAILED to register {0}: {1}" -f $tn, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    if ($created) {
        try {
            $markerPath = $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundOnLogonTaskMarkerPath
            $markerDir = Split-Path -Parent $markerPath
            if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $markerDir)) {
                New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $markerDir -Force | Out-Null
            }
            Set-Content -LiteralPath $markerPath -Value ("Armed at {0} (PID {1}) TR={2}" -f (Get-Date -Format 'o'), $PID, $tr) -Encoding UTF8 -Force
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("2229: ONLOGON task created but onlogon marker write failed: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
        [void](Set-FbDeferredOobeSoundDeliveryArmMarker -Note 'onlogon-task-armed' -Tr $tr)
        Write-FbLog ("2229: registered {0} (ONLOGON /IT verified via schtasks /Query)." -f $tn) 'INFO'
        return $true
    }
    Write-FbLog ("2229: {0} not verified; attempting HKLM Run fallback." -f $tn) 'WARN'
    return (Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundHklmRunFallback)
}

function Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundDeliveryStack {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2244: Arm Settings delivery before sysprep reboot — Startup, HKLM Run (-> bootstrap), detached ONLOGON arm, yield.
    .NOTES
        fb-dump-final-859/1016: parent dies during schtasks /Create -Wait; never call blocking ONLOGON arm inline here.
        2244: HKLM Run now points to FirstBaseOobeDeferredSoundBootstrap.ps1 (stale-lock-aware); promoted from fallback
        to primary delivery. All paths (Active Setup, Startup, HKLM Run) go through the bootstrap.
        Run belt is also armed inside Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundRunOnce (2244) before this runs.
        2236: ProgramData All Users Startup runs on OOBE defaultuser0 when Run/RunOnce/Active Setup do not (fb-dump-final-1121).
    #>
    Write-FbLog '2244: delivery stack — ProgramData Startup, HKLM Run -> bootstrap, detached ONLOGON arm, yield.' 'INFO'
    try {
        [void](Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundProgramDataStartup)
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2236: Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundProgramDataStartup threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    try {
        [void](Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundHklmRunFallback)
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2244: Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundHklmRunFallback threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    try {
        Start-FbDetachedPostSysprepOobeSoundArm
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2232: Start-FbDetachedPostSysprepOobeSoundArm threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    try {
        # NOTE (handoff-speed): Minimum safe wait — gives the detached ONLOGON arm worker
        # just enough time to start its PowerShell process before the parent is torn down
        # by sysprep's async reboot.  Reducing below 2s risks the child never launching
        # (fb-dump-final-1016 root cause).  Do NOT remove or reduce this sleep.
        Start-Sleep -Seconds 2
        Write-FbLog '2233: post-detached-arm yield 2s before parent teardown/reboot (fb-dump-final-1016).' 'INFO'
    } catch {}
    if (-not (Test-FbDeferredOobeSoundOnLogonTaskPresent)) {
        try {
            [void](Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundHklmRunFallback)
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("2244: belt HKLM Run -> bootstrap re-arm threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
    }
}

function Start-FbDetachedFirmwareRebootAfterSysprepValidation {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2234/2239: Spawn detached worker for shutdown /r /fw when parent is killed by sysprep async reboot.
        2239: Logic inlined as a here-string written to ProgramData — no staged Payload file required.
    #>
    $armLeaf = 'FirstBaseDetachedFirmwareReboot.ps1'
    $pdDir   = $FbProgramDataFirstBase
    $pdArm   = Join-Path $pdDir $armLeaf

    # 2239: Embed the firmware reboot worker content so no separate Payload file is needed.
    # Written to ProgramData at arm time; the detached powershell.exe child reads it from there.
    $fwRebootContent = @'
# FirstBase 2234/2239: detached survivor — issues shutdown /r /fw after sysprep validation when the
# parent WU loop is killed by sysprep async reboot (fb-dump-final-1121).
# Logic inlined from deleted FirstBaseDetachedFirmwareReboot.ps1 (2239-script-consolidation).
# For Internal Use Only.
Set-StrictMode -Version Latest
$ErrorActionPreference = 'Continue'
$FbLogDir = 'C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase\Logs'
$FbPd = 'C:\ProgramData\FirstBase'
$MirrorPd = Join-Path $FbPd 'WU-detached-firmware-reboot.log'
$Mirror = Join-Path $FbLogDir 'WU-detached-firmware-reboot.log'
function Write-FbDetachedFirmwareRebootLog {
    param([string]$Message, [string]$Level = 'INFO')
    $line = ('[{0}] [{1}] {2}' -f (Get-Date -Format 'o'), $Level, $Message)
    foreach ($path in @($MirrorPd, $Mirror)) {
        try {
            $dir = Split-Path -Parent $path
            if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $dir)) {
                New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $dir -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
            }
            Add-Content -LiteralPath $path -Value $line -Encoding ascii -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        } catch {}
    }
    try {
        $latest = Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath $FbLogDir -Filter 'WU-*.log' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |
            Sort-Object LastWriteTime -Descending | Select-Object -First 1
        if ($latest) {
            Add-Content -LiteralPath $latest.FullName -Value ("[2234-fw] {0}" -f $line) -Encoding ascii -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        }
    } catch {}
}
Write-FbDetachedFirmwareRebootLog ("2234: detached firmware reboot worker entry PID={0}" -f $PID) 'INFO'
Start-Sleep -Seconds 2
$shutdown = Join-Path $env:SystemRoot 'System32\shutdown.exe'
if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $shutdown)) {
    Write-FbDetachedFirmwareRebootLog '2234: shutdown.exe missing.' 'ERROR'
    exit 1
}
try {
    Write-FbDetachedFirmwareRebootLog '2234: shutdown.exe /r /fw /t 15' 'INFO'
    $p = Start-Process -FilePath $shutdown -ArgumentList @('/r', '/fw', '/t', '15', '/c', 'FirstBase: post-sysprep firmware reboot (detached).') -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru -Wait -ErrorAction Stop
    $ec = if ($null -ne $p) { $p.ExitCode } else { -1 }
    Write-FbDetachedFirmwareRebootLog ("2234: /r /fw exit {0}" -f $ec) 'INFO'
    if ($ec -ne 0) {
        Write-FbDetachedFirmwareRebootLog '2234: fallback shutdown /r /t 15 /f' 'WARN'
        Start-Process -FilePath $shutdown -ArgumentList @('/r', '/t', '15', '/f') -WindowStyle Hidden -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
    }
    exit 0
} catch {
    Write-FbDetachedFirmwareRebootLog ("2234: shutdown threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
    exit 1
}
'@

    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $pdDir)) {
            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $pdDir -Force | Out-Null
        }
        [System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($pdArm, $fwRebootContent, [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8)
        Write-FbLog ("2239: wrote inline firmware reboot worker to '{0}'." -f $pdArm) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2234: could not write firmware reboot worker to ProgramData ({0}); inline /fw fallback." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        try { Invoke-FbFirmwareRebootAfterSysprepValidation } catch {}
        return
    }

    $psExe = Join-Path $env:SystemRoot 'System32\WindowsPowerShell\v1.0\powershell.exe'
    try {
        $proc = Start-Process -FilePath $psExe -ArgumentList @(
            '-NoProfile', '-ExecutionPolicy', 'Bypass', '-WindowStyle', 'Hidden', '-File', $pdArm
        ) -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru -ErrorAction Stop
        $spawnPid = if ($null -ne $proc) { $proc.Id } else { 'unknown' }
        Write-FbLog ("2234: spawned detached firmware reboot worker PID={0} script={1}." -f $spawnPid, $pdArm) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2234: FAILED detached firmware reboot spawn: {0}; inline /fw fallback." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        try { Invoke-FbFirmwareRebootAfterSysprepValidation } catch {}
    }
}

function Start-FbDetachedPostSysprepOobeSoundArm {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2229/2231: Spawn detached worker to finish ONLOGON schtasks arm after sysprep validation.
    .NOTES
        Parent process is often terminated during schtasks /Create -Wait when sysprep stages async reboot
        (fb-dump-final-1326, fb-dump-final-1434, fb-dump-final-747). 2231 arms inline first; detached worker is backup.
    #>
    if (Test-FbDeferredOobeSoundOnLogonTaskPresent) {
        Write-FbLog '2231: DeferredOobeSoundOnLogon already present; skipping detached arm worker.' 'INFO'
        return
    }
    $armLeaf = 'FirstBasePostSysprepOobeSoundArm.ps1'
    $armSrc = $FbPostSysprepOobeSoundArmPs1
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $armSrc)) {
        Write-FbLog ("2231: detached arm script missing at '{0}'; inline ONLOGON already attempted via Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundDeliveryStack." -f $armSrc) 'WARN'
        return
    }
    $pdDir = $FbProgramDataFirstBase
    $pdArm = Join-Path $pdDir $armLeaf
    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $pdDir)) {
            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $pdDir -Force | Out-Null
        }
        Copy-Item -LiteralPath $armSrc -Destination $pdArm -Force -ErrorAction Stop
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2229: could not stage arm script to ProgramData ({0}); launching from Setup path." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        $pdArm = $armSrc
    }
    $psExe = Join-Path $env:SystemRoot 'System32\WindowsPowerShell\v1.0\powershell.exe'
    try {
        $proc = Start-Process -FilePath $psExe -ArgumentList @(
            '-NoProfile', '-ExecutionPolicy', 'Bypass', '-WindowStyle', 'Hidden', '-File', $pdArm
        ) -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru -ErrorAction Stop
        $spawnPid = if ($null -ne $proc) { $proc.Id } else { 'unknown' }
        Write-FbLog ("2229: spawned detached ONLOGON arm worker PID={0} script={1} (survives sysprep async reboot)." -f $spawnPid, $pdArm) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2229: FAILED to spawn detached arm worker: {0}; HKLM Run fallback remains primary belt." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        try { [void](Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundHklmRunFallback) } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("2232: HKLM Run fallback after detached spawn failure threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
    }
}

function Remove-FbDeferredOobeSoundOnLogonTask {
    param([string]$Context = 'cleanup')
    $tn = $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundOnLogonTaskName
    try {
        $null = Start-Process -FilePath 'schtasks.exe' -ArgumentList @('/Delete', '/TN', $tn, '/F') -WindowStyle Hidden -Wait -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        Write-FbLog ("2227: removed scheduled task {0} ({1})." -f $tn, $Context) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2227: optional remove {0} threw ({1}): {2}" -f $tn, $Context, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

function Invoke-FbFirmwareRebootAfterSysprepValidation {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2026.05.20.2218: Schedule reboot into UEFI firmware (/fw). Does NOT call Invoke-FbReboot.
        If /fw is unsupported, log and fall back to plain /r.
    #>
    $shutdown = Join-Path $env:SystemRoot 'System32\shutdown.exe'
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $shutdown)) {
        Write-FbLog "2218: shutdown.exe missing; cannot issue firmware reboot." 'ERROR'
        return
    }
    # 2220: informational only - do not skip /fw from this probe (false negatives strand operators
    # who still want firmware on hybrid/odd firmware reporting). Log for post-mortem vs exitCode.
    $peFwType = $null
    try {
        $peFwType = (Get-ItemProperty -LiteralPath 'HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control' -Name 'PEFirmwareType' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).PEFirmwareType
    } catch {}
    if ($null -ne $peFwType) {
        Write-FbLog ("2218: firmware context HKLM\..\Control\PEFirmwareType={0} (common mapping: 1=BIOS/MBR-era 2=UEFI, informational; /fw still attempted)." -f $peFwType) 'INFO'
    } else {
        Write-FbLog '2218: firmware context PEFirmwareType not readable; /fw will be attempted (UEFI vs legacy resolved by shutdown.exe exit + fallback /r).' 'INFO'
    }
    $shutdownFwArgs = @('/r', '/fw', '/t', '20', '/c', 'FirstBase: reboot to UEFI firmware. After exit, next boot opens Sound settings once then shuts down.')
    try {
        Write-FbLog "2218: issuing shutdown.exe /r /fw /t 20 (post-sysprep validation; may interleave with sysprep async reboot)." 'WARN'
        $p = Start-Process -FilePath $shutdown -ArgumentList $shutdownFwArgs -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru -Wait -ErrorAction Stop
        $exitCode = $null
        if ($null -ne $p) {
            try { $exitCode = [int]$p.ExitCode } catch { $exitCode = $null }
        }
        Write-FbLog ("2218: shutdown.exe /r /fw completed with exitCode={0}." -f $exitCode) 'INFO'
        if ($null -ne $exitCode -and $exitCode -ne 0) {
            Write-FbLog ("2218: /fw returned non-zero ({0}); falling back to shutdown /r /t 20 /f." -f $exitCode) 'WARN'
            Start-Process -FilePath $shutdown -ArgumentList @('/r', '/t', '20', '/f', '/c', 'FirstBase: firmware reboot fallback (plain reboot).') -WindowStyle Hidden -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2218: shutdown /r /fw threw: {0}; fallback shutdown /r /t 20 /f." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        try {
            Start-Process -FilePath $shutdown -ArgumentList @('/r', '/t', '20', '/f', '/c', 'FirstBase: firmware reboot fallback after exception.') -WindowStyle Hidden -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("2218: fallback shutdown /r also threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
        }
    }
}

function Clear-FbSoundRunOnce2216Artifacts {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2026.05.21.2217/2218: strip legacy 2216 deferred Sound RunOnce + marker so the
        OOBE-overlay deferred Sound path (FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 via OpenWindowsSoundSettingsDeferredOnce) does
        not stack with a stale OpenWindowsSoundSettingsOnce.
    #>
    try {
        Remove-ItemProperty -LiteralPath 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce' `
            -Name $script:FbSoundRunOnceName -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
    } catch {}
    try {
        if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $script:FbSoundRunOnceMarkerPath) {
            Remove-Item -LiteralPath $script:FbSoundRunOnceMarkerPath -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        }
    } catch {}
}

function Set-FbOobeSoundUserRebootGate {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        2026.05.21.2217/2218: optional post-validation marker on disk (legacy name).
        2218 deferred Sound does not poll this file; retained for diagnostics / tooling.
    #>
    try {
        $dir = Split-Path -Parent $FbOobeSoundUserRebootGatePath
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $dir)) {
            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $dir -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
        }
        Set-Content -LiteralPath $FbOobeSoundUserRebootGatePath -Value ("sysprep-validation-exit0 {0} pid={1}" -f (Get-Date -Format 'o'), $PID) -Encoding UTF8 -Force
        Write-FbLog ("2217/2218: wrote post-validation gate file {0} (diagnostic; 2218 deferred Sound does not wait on it)." -f $FbOobeSoundUserRebootGatePath) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2217 OOBE Sound: FAILED to write user-reboot gate {0}: {1}" -f $FbOobeSoundUserRebootGatePath, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

function Clear-FbOobeSoundUserRebootGate {
    try {
        if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $FbOobeSoundUserRebootGatePath) {
            Remove-Item -LiteralPath $FbOobeSoundUserRebootGatePath -Force -ErrorAction Stop
            Write-FbLog ("2217 OOBE Sound: cleared user-reboot gate {0}." -f $FbOobeSoundUserRebootGatePath) 'INFO'
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("2217 OOBE Sound: clear user-reboot gate threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

function Register-FbFinalShipReaper {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        5.0.42 (dump PF4VGG7V-1311): guarantee a CLEAN shipped device by
        reaping EVERY residual FirstBase artifact that survives the
        sysprep-success teardown race.
    .DESCRIPTION
        Root cause of the 1311 residue: the Invoke-SysprepToOobe success
        path teardown (remove overlay marker + the OOBE-overlay-preserved
        splash/finalize/gate/sound scripts + all trigger layers, lines
        ~10800-10840) runs AFTER "Start-Process sysprep -Wait" returns, and
        LOSES the race with sysprep's async generalize+shutdown. The 1311
        WU log proves the loop was killed ("The pipeline has been stopped")
        one second after arming the deferred-sound stack and BEFORE the
        teardown block executed, so C:\ProgramData\FirstBase and
        C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase (scripts, markers, State, Logs, Tools)
        plus the FirstBase Run/RunOnce/ActiveSetup/Startup/task triggers all
        shipped. Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub STEP 4 also DELIBERATELY preserves
        ~12 scripts under FirstBase-Splash-Oobe-Overlay.mode for a
        post-sysprep splash teardown that never happens under Option B
        (/oobe /shutdown, no FirstBase reboot pass; specialize arm
        suppressed) - so those preserved scripts also survive.

        Fix: arm TWO independent, self-deleting reapers the instant sysprep
        validation succeeds (called before we can be killed). Both live
        OUTSIDE the FirstBase trees so neither self-deletes a tree it runs
        from, and between them they cover both ship paths:

          1. Detached C:\Windows\Temp\FbFinalReaper.cmd - spawned now,
             covers the bench case where the device stays powered through
             generalize. It waits briefly (so the one-shot success-path
             arming finishes first), then retry-loops a script-only reap
             (.ps1/.cmd/.bat under both FirstBase trees + del the Startup
             .vbs + reg/schtasks FirstBase triggers). Logs, State, markers
             (.flag/.mode), and wu-status/wu-state are kept. Retries until
             a bounded cap, then self-deletes.

          2. Cleanup-only C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\SetupComplete.cmd -
             runs at the NEXT boot's specialize pass as SYSTEM with no UI,
             BEFORE the customer/operator ever sees OOBE. Survives a HARD
             POWER-OFF (it is on disk and generalize does not touch
             \Windows\Setup\Scripts). It performs the same reap and
             self-deletes. It ONLY reaps - it never re-arms the loop (the
             full FirstBase payload is already gone), so the 2213t "third
             fence against post-sysprep re-arming" is not defeated.

        PRESERVED (customer-needed) - deliberately NOT touched:
          - HKLM RunOnce OpenWindowsActivationSettingsOnce (2213v activation).
          - C:\Windows\Panther\unattend.xml and everything Windows/OOBE owns.
        Only FirstBase-owned paths, FirstBase sound-delivery surfaces, and
        FirstBase\* scheduled tasks are removed.
    #>
    $reapLines = @(
        'del /f /q "C:\ProgramData\Microsoft\Windows\Start Menu\Programs\Startup\FirstBaseOobeSoundAtLogon.vbs" >nul 2>nul'
        'reg delete "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run" /v OpenWindowsSoundSettingsDeferredLogon /f >nul 2>nul'
        'reg delete "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce" /v OpenWindowsSoundSettingsDeferredOnce /f >nul 2>nul'
        'reg delete "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run" /v FirstBaseWuLoopRun /f >nul 2>nul'
        'reg delete "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce" /v "!FirstBaseWuLoop" /f >nul 2>nul'
        'reg delete "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce" /v "!FirstBaseWuSplash" /f >nul 2>nul'
        'reg delete "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnceEx\0001" /v FirstBaseWuLoop /f >nul 2>nul'
        'reg delete "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Active Setup\Installed Components\{A7B8C9D0-E1F2-4A5B-9C01-2234DEFERREDSOUND}" /f >nul 2>nul'
        'schtasks /Delete /TN "FirstBase\DeferredOobeSoundOnLogon" /F >nul 2>nul'
        'schtasks /Delete /TN "FirstBase\MdmScrub" /F >nul 2>nul'
        'schtasks /Delete /TN "FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoop" /F >nul 2>nul'
        'schtasks /Delete /TN "FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoopOnLogon" /F >nul 2>nul'
        'schtasks /Delete /TN "FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoopBoot2" /F >nul 2>nul'
        'schtasks /Delete /TN "FirstBase\WindowsUpdateLoopAnyLogon" /F >nul 2>nul'
        'schtasks /Delete /TN "FirstBase\UpdateSplash" /F >nul 2>nul'
        'schtasks /Delete /TN "FirstBase\UpdateSplashLayer4" /F >nul 2>nul'
        'schtasks /Delete /TN "FirstBase\SplashWatcher" /F >nul 2>nul'
        'schtasks /Delete /TN "FirstBase\SplashWatcherBoot2" /F >nul 2>nul'
        'schtasks /Delete /TN "FirstBase\DebugHotkeyWatcher" /F >nul 2>nul'
        'for /r "C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase" %%F in (*.ps1) do del /f /q "%%F" >nul 2>nul'
        'for /r "C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase" %%F in (*.cmd) do del /f /q "%%F" >nul 2>nul'
        'for /r "C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase" %%F in (*.bat) do del /f /q "%%F" >nul 2>nul'
        'for /r "C:\ProgramData\FirstBase" %%F in (*.ps1) do del /f /q "%%F" >nul 2>nul'
        'for /r "C:\ProgramData\FirstBase" %%F in (*.cmd) do del /f /q "%%F" >nul 2>nul'
        'for /r "C:\ProgramData\FirstBase" %%F in (*.bat) do del /f /q "%%F" >nul 2>nul'
    )

    $buildReaperCmd = {
        param([int]$InitialWaitSec, [int]$MaxTries, [string]$Tag)
        $lines = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[string]
        $lines.Add('@echo off') | Out-Null
        $lines.Add('setlocal enableextensions') | Out-Null
        $lines.Add(':: ' + $Tag) | Out-Null
        $lines.Add(':: 5.0.42 FirstBase final ship reaper - auto-generated by Register-FbFinalShipReaper. Self-deletes.') | Out-Null
        if ($InitialWaitSec -gt 0) {
            $lines.Add(('timeout /t {0} /nobreak >nul 2>nul' -f $InitialWaitSec)) | Out-Null
        }
        $lines.Add('set FB_TRIES=0') | Out-Null
        $lines.Add(':fbreap') | Out-Null
        foreach ($rl in $reapLines) { $lines.Add($rl) | Out-Null }
        $lines.Add('set /a FB_TRIES+=1') | Out-Null
        $lines.Add(('if %FB_TRIES% GEQ {0} goto fbdone' -f $MaxTries)) | Out-Null
        $lines.Add('timeout /t 3 /nobreak >nul 2>nul') | Out-Null
        $lines.Add('goto fbreap') | Out-Null
        $lines.Add(':fbdone') | Out-Null
        $lines.Add('(goto) 2>nul & del "%~f0"') | Out-Null
        return ,$lines.ToArray()
    }

    # Reaper 1: detached bench reaper in C:\Windows\Temp (outside FirstBase trees).
    try {
        $tempBase = $env:TEMP
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($tempBase)) { $tempBase = 'C:\Windows\Temp' }
        $detachedPath = Join-Path $tempBase 'FbFinalReaper.cmd'
        $detachedBody = & $buildReaperCmd 12 60 'detached bench reaper (device powered through generalize)'
        [System.IO.File]::WriteAllLines($detachedPath, $detachedBody, [System.Text.Encoding]::ASCII)
        Start-Process -FilePath 'cmd.exe' -ArgumentList @('/c', $detachedPath) -WindowStyle Hidden -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null
        Write-FbLog ("5.0.42 ship reaper: spawned detached bench reaper {0} (waits 12s, retry-loops script-only scrub of both FirstBase trees + triggers, keeps logs/markers, self-deletes)." -f $detachedPath) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("5.0.42 ship reaper: detached bench reaper arm threw (non-blocking; SetupComplete backstop still covers): {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # Reaper 2: cleanup-only SetupComplete.cmd backstop (runs next-boot specialize; survives hard power-off).
    try {
        $scriptsDir = 'C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts'
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $scriptsDir)) {
            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $scriptsDir -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
        }
        $setupCompleteReaper = Join-Path $scriptsDir 'SetupComplete.cmd'
        $backstopBody = & $buildReaperCmd 0 10 'next-boot specialize backstop reaper (cleanup only; does NOT re-arm FirstBase)'
        [System.IO.File]::WriteAllLines($setupCompleteReaper, $backstopBody, [System.Text.Encoding]::ASCII)
        Write-FbLog ("5.0.42 ship reaper: wrote cleanup-only SetupComplete.cmd backstop {0} (next-boot specialize script-only reap of both FirstBase trees + triggers, keeps logs/markers, then self-deletes; reap-only, no re-arm)." -f $setupCompleteReaper) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("5.0.42 ship reaper: SetupComplete.cmd backstop write threw (non-blocking; detached reaper still covers bench path): {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

function Invoke-SysprepToOobe {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Hand off to Sysprep /oobe /reboot /quiet, with bounded retry and
        a fall-through to the reboot cascade on failure.
    .DESCRIPTION
        Previous build returned $false on Start-Process failure and the
        loop exited with code 1, which left the user staring at the
        splash forever (RunOnce had fired and consumed itself, the
        device just sat there). New flow:

          1. Bump SysprepRetryCount in wu-state.json. If we're already
             past $MaxSysprepRetries, write a STUCK-DIAG-sysprep-*.txt
             and call Invoke-Reboot anyway - at least the next boot's
             completion-marker handler can try again with a fresh PID.
          2. Try Start-Process Sysprep.exe /oobe /reboot /quiet.
          3. If it threw, log it, bump the counter, fall through to
             Invoke-Reboot. RunOnce relaunches us, we see the completion
             marker, retry sysprep with the new counter value.

        Returns $true if sysprep actually launched (caller proceeds to
        cleanup). Returns $false ONLY if we triggered the reboot cascade
        AND that cascade itself returned (which means we already
        exit 1'd in Invoke-Reboot's stage 7) - in practice the cascade
        never returns to this caller.

        -NoCascadeFallback (added 2026.05.11.1145): suppress BOTH
        fall-through Invoke-Reboot calls (Sysprep.exe missing path
        and Start-Process catch path). When the caller is the cascade-
        exhausted OOBE handoff, calling Invoke-Reboot would re-enter
        the same dead end we're trying to escape from. With this flag,
        a sysprep failure simply returns $false and the caller decides
        what to do (surface a final "manual reboot required" message
        and exit 0 cleanly so the watchdog does not relaunch us).
    #>
    param(
        [string]$Reason = 'Preparing return to OOBE.',
        [switch]$NoCascadeFallback,
        [ValidateSet('Shutdown', 'Restart')]
        [string]$PowerAction = 'Restart'
    )

    Write-FbLog $Reason 'INFO'
    Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Completed' -Message 'Updates complete. Returning device to OOBE...'
    Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Completed'

    # Bump SysprepRetryCount BEFORE attempting sysprep so a hard crash
    # mid-launch still ticks the counter and protects against infinite
    # retry loops.
    $currentSysprepRetry = 0
    try {
        $current = Load-State
        $currentSysprepRetry = [int]$current.SysprepRetryCount + 1
        Save-State `
            -EmptySweepCount   ([int]$current.EmptySweepCount) `
            -LastPass          ([string]$current.LastPass) `
            -StartTime         ([string]$current.StartTime) `
            -FailureCount      ([int]$current.FailureCount) `
            -UpdateRetry       $current.UpdateRetry `
            -GivenUpUpdateIds  @($current.GivenUpUpdateIds) `
            -BootCountByKey    $current.BootCountByKey `
            -BootCount         ([int]$current.BootCount) `
            -ForceHiddenKeys   @($current.ForceHiddenKeys) `
            -RebootIterations  ([int]$current.RebootIterations) `
            -SysprepRetryCount $currentSysprepRetry
        Write-FbLog ("Sysprep retry attempt {0} / {1}." -f $currentSysprepRetry, $script:MaxSysprepRetries) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Failed to persist SysprepRetryCount: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    $sysprepExe = Join-Path $env:WINDIR 'System32\Sysprep\Sysprep.exe'
    if (-not (Test-Path $sysprepExe)) {
        Write-FbLog "Sysprep executable not found: $sysprepExe" 'ERROR'
        if ($currentSysprepRetry -ge $script:MaxSysprepRetries) {
            Write-StuckRebootDiag -Reason ("Sysprep.exe missing AND SysprepRetryCount={0} >= max; surfacing manual-reboot via OOBE handoff failure path." -f $currentSysprepRetry)
        }
        # 2026.05.12.1030-no-cascade-final: prior builds fell back to
        # Invoke-Reboot here when -NoCascadeFallback was not set, which
        # restarted the doomed 8-stage cascade. The cascade is now gone
        # and there is no useful recovery to attempt from here - we
        # ALWAYS return $false and let the caller (typically
        # Invoke-FbOobeHandoff) surface the manual-reboot message.
        Write-FbLog 'Sysprep.exe missing: returning $false (no cascade fallback available in 2026.05.12.1030+).' 'WARN'
        return $false
    }

    # 2026.05.13.1915-defer-popup-registration: the completion-popup
    # RunOnce registration was moved DOWN into the sysprep success
    # path (lines below the success branch) so a sysprep validation
    # failure can never leave a "Updates Complete" popup armed on
    # disk. Pre-1915 behavior registered the popup BEFORE sysprep
    # launched; on a 0x139f failure the popup survived, the next
    # interactive Audit-Mode logon consumed the RunOnce and showed
    # the lie "All updates complete" to the operator while the
    # device was actually stranded in IMAGE_STATE_UNDEPLOYABLE. The
    # registration is now performed exactly once, immediately
    # before "return $true", so it ships only when sysprep has
    # ACTUALLY validated cleanly and the box is going to OOBE.

    # 2026.05.13.1900-two-stage-sysprep-handoff Fix 3 + Fix 5.
    #
    # The pre-1900 launch used:
    #
    #   Start-Process sysprep.exe -ArgumentList @('/oobe','/reboot','/quiet') -NoNewWindow
    #
    # Fire-and-forget. We never observed sysprep's exit code, so a
    # 0x139f (ERROR_INVALID_STATE = pending Windows-update reboot)
    # validation failure looked identical to success: the call
    # returned, we logged "Sysprep launched successfully.", and
    # exited 0. State.ini was stamped IMAGE_STATE_UNDEPLOYABLE from
    # the partial run, the pre-sysprep trigger cleanup wiped all
    # four trigger layers, and the device was stranded.
    #
    # New behavior:
    #
    #   $proc = Start-Process sysprep.exe -Wait -PassThru -NoNewWindow
    #
    # Sysprep returns 0 BEFORE the async generalize+reboot in the
    # success case (it backgrounds the actual reboot via Setup), so
    # -Wait + -PassThru captures only the validation phase result.
    # If validation fails (pending reboot, undeployable state etc.)
    # we know IMMEDIATELY and the caller can choose to retry from a
    # freshly-rebooted state instead of exiting clean on a lie.
    #
    # The build 1700 pre-sysprep trigger cleanup was MOVED. Triggers
    # are no longer wiped before this call; they are wiped here only
    # AFTER sysprep returns exit code 0 (so a non-zero exit leaves
    # the loop's relaunch path intact).
    # 2213g Bug I fix: dropped -WindowStyle Hidden because PowerShell's
    # Start-Process refuses both -NoNewWindow and -WindowStyle in the
    # same invocation ("Parameters '-NoNewWindow' and '-WindowStyle'
    # cannot be specified at the same time."). The conflict has been
    # latent in the codebase since the build-1900 sync sysprep launch
    # was introduced; it never fired in production until 2213f because
    # earlier builds either wedged at sysprep-mode-dialog (Bug B in
    # 2213c and earlier) or timed out the CBS-settle wait before
    # reaching this line. With 2213d's <Reseal> removal + 2213d's
    # Defender pre-disable + 2213f's reboot ladder optimization all
    # working, the loop now reaches this Start-Process call cleanly --
    # which immediately throws on the parameter conflict and aborts
    # sysprep. -NoNewWindow is the right flag (sysprep needs no UI;
    # the parent powershell.exe already runs hidden), so we keep that
    # and drop the redundant -WindowStyle Hidden.
    $sysprepExit = $null
    $sysprepThrew = $false
    try {
        # 5.4.8: always /oobe /reboot into customer OOBE. Do not /shutdown
        # (C875N44 sat powered off with no operator click). Walk-away timeout
        # must never reach this function; a real close still reboots, not power-off.
        if ($PowerAction -eq 'Shutdown') {
            Write-FbLog '5.4.8: PowerAction=Shutdown overridden to /reboot (customer OOBE only; no unattended power-off).' 'WARN'
        }
        $sysprepPowerFlag = '/reboot'
        Write-FbLog ("Sysprep power action={0} ({1})." -f $PowerAction, $sysprepPowerFlag) 'INFO'
        $sysprepProc = Start-Process -FilePath $sysprepExe -ArgumentList @('/oobe', $sysprepPowerFlag, '/quiet') -NoNewWindow -PassThru -Wait -ErrorAction Stop
        if ($sysprepProc) {
            try { $sysprepExit = [int]$sysprepProc.ExitCode } catch { $sysprepExit = $null }
        }
    } catch {
        $sysprepThrew = $true
        Write-FbLog ("Sysprep launch threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
    }

    if (-not $sysprepThrew -and $sysprepExit -eq 0) {
        Write-FbLog 'Sysprep exit code = 0. Validation succeeded; sysprep is now staging the async generalize+reboot. Returning $true so the caller can clean up triggers and exit clean.' 'WARN'

        # 5.0.42 (dump PF4VGG7V-1311): arm the final ship reaper FIRST, the
        # instant validation succeeds, BEFORE the deferred-sound arming and
        # the overlay teardown below. Root cause of the residue: this
        # success-path teardown loses the race with sysprep's async
        # generalize+shutdown (the loop is killed mid-cleanup, WU log:
        # "The pipeline has been stopped"), so both FirstBase trees +
        # triggers shipped. The reaper (detached bench cmd + a cleanup-only
        # next-boot SetupComplete.cmd backstop that survives a hard
        # power-off) guarantees the shipped device is clean regardless of
        # when we are killed. Customer activation RunOnce + normal OOBE are
        # preserved. See Register-FbFinalShipReaper.
        try {
            Register-FbFinalShipReaper
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("5.0.42: Register-FbFinalShipReaper threw (non-blocking): {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }

        # 2217/2218: post-validation marker (legacy gate name). Immediate Sound spawn removed — 2218 uses
        # RunOnce + FirstBaseDeferredOobeSound.ps1 on next boot after /fw.
        Set-FbOobeSoundUserRebootGate
        $hadOobeOverlayForDeferredSound = $false
        try {
            $hadOobeOverlayForDeferredSound = [bool](Test-Path -LiteralPath $FbSplashOobeOverlayMarkerPath)
        } catch {}

        # 2232: three-step handoff step 1 — arm ProgramData kit + delivery + firmware reboot BEFORE
        # loop teardown (fb-dump-final-859: parent died during schtasks -Wait with no reboot).
        # 5.4.8: never arm deferred OOBE Sound / RunOnce / ONLOGON / firmware-reboot.
        # Customer OOBE only; FirstBase must not run as defaultuser0.
        if ($hadOobeOverlayForDeferredSound) {
            Write-FbLog '5.4.8: overlay.mode still present at sysprep success; NOT arming deferred OOBE Sound/RunOnce/ONLOGON/firmware-reboot. Customer OOBE only.' 'WARN'
            try {
                Remove-Item -LiteralPath $FbSplashOobeOverlayMarkerPath -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            } catch {}
        }

        # 2026.05.13.1900 Fix 5: trigger cleanup is deferred to the
        # SUCCESS path. Up to this point the four trigger layers
        # (HKLM RunOnce !FirstBaseWuLoop, RunOnceEx \0001\
        # FirstBaseWuLoop, Run\FirstBaseWuLoopRun, the FirstBase
        # scheduled tasks, and the kickstart launcher batch) were
        # ALL still armed. Now that sysprep validated cleanly and
        # is about to async-reboot the box to OOBE, the loop must
        # NOT relaunch on the next boot. Delete every trigger
        # layer; idempotent best-effort.
        Write-FbLog 'Sysprep success path: deleting FirstBase scheduled tasks + all four trigger layers (Fix 5: deferred from pre-sysprep to post-sysprep-success).' 'INFO'
        try { schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $TaskName       /F | Out-Null } catch {}
        try { schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $LogonTaskName  /F | Out-Null } catch {}
        try { schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $Boot2TaskName /F | Out-Null } catch {}
        try { schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $AnyLogonTaskName /F | Out-Null } catch {}
        try { schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $SplashTaskName /F | Out-Null } catch {}
        try { schtasks.exe /Delete /TN 'FirstBase\SplashWatcher' /F | Out-Null } catch {}
        try { schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $SplashWatcherBoot2Name /F | Out-Null } catch {}
        try { schtasks.exe /Delete /TN 'FirstBase\UpdateSplashLayer4' /F | Out-Null } catch {}
        try { schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundOnLogonTaskName /F | Out-Null } catch {}
        try {
            if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $FbSplashOobeOverlayMarkerPath) {
                Remove-Item -LiteralPath $FbSplashOobeOverlayMarkerPath -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                Write-FbLog 'Sysprep success path: removed FirstBase-Splash-Oobe-Overlay.mode (2213x OOBE overlay teardown).' 'INFO'
            }
        } catch {}
        foreach ($stPath in @(
                (Join-Path $FbRoot 'Show-UpdateProgress.ps1')
                (Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseHandoffSplash.ps1')
                (Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseSplashSingleInstance.ps1')
                (Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseSplashWatcher.ps1')
                (Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseConsoleSplashInstance.ps1')
                (Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseConsoleSplashRender.ps1')
                (Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseConsoleSplash.cmd')
                (Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseSplashLauncher.cmd')
            )) {
            try {
                if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $stPath) {
                    Remove-Item -LiteralPath $stPath -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                }
            } catch {}
        }
        try {
            Remove-ItemProperty -Path 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce' `
                -Name '!FirstBaseWuLoop' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        } catch {}
        try {
            Remove-ItemProperty -Path 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce' `
                -Name '!FirstBaseWuSplash' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            if ($hadOobeOverlayForDeferredSound) {
                Write-FbLog '2234: removed stale RunOnce !FirstBaseWuSplash (Setup splash path; deferred Sound uses Active Setup + deferred markers).' 'INFO'
            }
        } catch {}
        try {
            Remove-ItemProperty -Path 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnceEx\0001' `
                -Name 'FirstBaseWuLoop' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        } catch {}
        try {
            Remove-ItemProperty -Path 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run' `
                -Name 'FirstBaseWuLoopRun' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        } catch {}
        try {
            $launcherBat = Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseWuLoopRun.cmd'
            if (Test-Path $launcherBat) { Remove-Item -Path $launcherBat -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue }
        } catch {}

        # Clear PendingSysprep on disk: STAGE 2 has succeeded, the
        # box is going to OOBE. The on-disk latch is no longer
        # needed and would only confuse a hypothetical relaunch
        # (e.g. operator inserting recovery media).
        try {
            $sysprepCurrent = Load-State
            Save-State `
                -EmptySweepCount   ([int]$sysprepCurrent.EmptySweepCount) `
                -LastPass          ([string]$sysprepCurrent.LastPass) `
                -StartTime         ([string]$sysprepCurrent.StartTime) `
                -FailureCount      ([int]$sysprepCurrent.FailureCount) `
                -UpdateRetry       $sysprepCurrent.UpdateRetry `
                -GivenUpUpdateIds  @($sysprepCurrent.GivenUpUpdateIds) `
                -BootCountByKey    $sysprepCurrent.BootCountByKey `
                -BootCount         ([int]$sysprepCurrent.BootCount) `
                -ForceHiddenKeys   @($sysprepCurrent.ForceHiddenKeys) `
                -RebootIterations  ([int]$sysprepCurrent.RebootIterations) `
                -SysprepRetryCount ([int]$sysprepCurrent.SysprepRetryCount) `
                -PendingSysprep    $false
            Write-FbLog 'Sysprep success path: cleared PendingSysprep on disk (STAGE 2 complete).' 'INFO'
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Sysprep success path: clearing PendingSysprep threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }

        # 2026.05.20.2213s: popup registration REMOVED per user request
        # (5/19/2026 1:45 PM): "Let's remove the updates complete pop-up.
        # It should no longer be part of the post update completion
        # workflow, especially if it is going to bypass OOBE and force
        # user to Administrator desktop."
        #
        # Pre-2213s this block registered HKLM RunOnce!FirstBaseUpdates
        # Complete pointing at Show-UpdatesCompleteMessage.ps1 (the WPF
        # "Updates Complete" modal) so the popup fired on the first
        # interactive logon after OOBE. Field evidence (5/19/2026 dump
        # 1227) showed the popup firing on the WRONG session (forced
        # Administrator due to Bug U OOBE bypass), creating an unpolished
        # post-OOBE flow even when the device DID land on the correct
        # user account. Per user direction, the popup is removed entirely.
        # Show-UpdatesCompleteMessage.ps1 was kept on disk as a frozen file through 2238;
        # 2239: file deleted from Payload (dead code; no remaining invocation path).
        #
        # RESTORATION PATH (if policy ever changes):
        #   Re-add a Set-ItemProperty call to HKLM RunOnce with name
        #   $RunOnceValueName ('FirstBaseUpdatesComplete') and value
        #   '"<powershell-path>" -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass
        #   -WindowStyle Hidden -File "<$PopupScriptPath>"' here in
        #   the sysprep success path. Both the value-name constant
        #   ($RunOnceValueName) and the script-path constant
        #   ($PopupScriptPath) are still declared at the top of this
        #   file (lines ~513, ~518) for exactly this purpose. The
        #   pre-OOBE scrub gate's regex (?i)^!?FirstBase will still
        #   delete a stale 'FirstBaseUpdatesComplete' value left by
        #   a prior-build re-image - that defensive sweep is unaffected
        #   by this removal because the regex matches the value name
        #   regardless of whether 2213s+ ever wrote it.
        Write-FbLog "Sysprep success path: 2213s popup-removal active (no RunOnce written). Show-UpdatesCompleteMessage.ps1 deleted in 2239 (dead code). OOBE will land cleanly on the user's freshly-created desktop with no completion modal." 'INFO'

        # 2026.05.20.2213v: one-shot Activation page on first interactive logon
        # after successful sysprep validation (exit 0). Runs after scrub when
        # STAGE 2 calls scrub -> sysprep; also covers alternate sysprep-success
        # entry points that skip STAGE 2. Non-blocking; marker prevents dup write.
        Register-FbOpenWindowsActivationSettingsRunOnce
        try {
            Clear-FbSoundRunOnce2216Artifacts
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("2217: Clear-FbSoundRunOnce2216Artifacts threw (non-blocking): {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
        Write-FbLog '2217: cleanup armed/applied - deferred 2216 Sound RunOnce + marker cleared (OOBE immediate handoff path).' 'INFO'

        return $true
    }

    # Non-zero exit OR a Start-Process throw. Log loudly, dump a
    # panic record with the exit code and sysprep panther log tail,
    # bump SysprepRetryCount via Save-State, leave triggers in
    # place so the loop relaunches on the next boot, and return
    # $false. The caller (Invoke-FbOobeHandoff or main-loop STAGE
    # 2 fast path) inspects the return value and routes to plain
    # shutdown for another reboot attempt.
    Clear-FbOobeSoundUserRebootGate
    $sysprepExitDisplay = if ($null -ne $sysprepExit) { ('{0} (0x{1:X8})' -f $sysprepExit, $sysprepExit) } else { 'unknown (launch threw)' }
    Write-FbLog ("ERROR: Sysprep.exe exit code = {0}. Refused to launch or failed validation. Two-stage handoff Fix 2 should have prevented 0x139f; if exit is 0x139f, investigate why STAGE 1 -> STAGE 2 did not settle CBS." -f $sysprepExitDisplay) 'ERROR'

    # Bump SysprepRetryCount has ALREADY been persisted at the top
    # of this function. So $currentSysprepRetry already reflects
    # the post-bump value.

    # Panic dump path. Always written on any non-zero exit. When
    # exit==0x139f, also include the sysprep Panther log tail so
    # the operator can correlate the loop's view with sysprep's
    # own evidence (this is exactly the artifact that broke
    # build 1700 - i:\Panther\setupact.log / setuperr.log).
    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path $LogDir)) { New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $LogDir -Force | Out-Null }
        $sysprepStamp = Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd-HHmmss'
        $isPendingRebootCode = ($null -ne $sysprepExit) -and ($sysprepExit -eq 0x139f)
        $diagBaseName = if ($isPendingRebootCode) {
            ("STUCK-DIAG-SYSPREP-PENDING-REBOOT-{0}.json" -f $sysprepStamp)
        } else {
            ("STUCK-DIAG-SYSPREP-NONZERO-{0}.json" -f $sysprepStamp)
        }
        $diagPath = Join-Path $LogDir $diagBaseName

        $panther = Join-Path $env:WINDIR 'System32\Sysprep\Panther'
        $pantherSetupAct = Join-Path $panther 'setupact.log'
        $pantherSetupErr = Join-Path $panther 'setuperr.log'
        $sysprepActTail = @()
        $sysprepErrTail = @()
        try {
            if (Test-Path $pantherSetupAct) {
                $sysprepActTail = Get-Content -Path $pantherSetupAct -Tail 100 -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            }
        } catch {}
        try {
            if (Test-Path $pantherSetupErr) {
                $sysprepErrTail = Get-Content -Path $pantherSetupErr -Tail 100 -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            }
        } catch {}

        $rebootRequiredNow = $false
        try { $rebootRequiredNow = [bool](Test-FbWuRebootRequired) } catch {}

        $stateAtFailure = ''
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
                $stateAtFailure = Get-Content -Path $StateFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            }
        } catch {}

        $diagBody = [ordered]@{
            Timestamp           = (Get-Date).ToString('o')
            ScriptVersion       = $ScriptVersion
            SysprepExitCode     = $sysprepExit
            SysprepExitCodeHex  = if ($null -ne $sysprepExit) { ('0x{0:X8}' -f $sysprepExit) } else { '' }
            SysprepLaunchThrew  = $sysprepThrew
            IsPendingRebootCode = $isPendingRebootCode
            CurrentSysprepRetry = [int]$currentSysprepRetry
            MaxSysprepRetries   = [int]$script:MaxSysprepRetries
            TestFbWuRebootRequiredNow = $rebootRequiredNow
            SysprepPantherSetupActTail = $sysprepActTail
            SysprepPantherSetupErrTail = $sysprepErrTail
            StateAtFailure      = $stateAtFailure
            Reason              = $Reason
        }
        $diagBody | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 8 | Set-Content -Path $diagPath -Encoding UTF8 -Force
        Write-FbLog ("Sysprep non-zero exit panic dump written: '{0}'." -f $diagPath) 'ERROR'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Sysprep non-zero exit panic dump threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # 2026.05.13.2210-fullscope-multipass-progress-fixes Critical
    # Fix 2: PendingSysprep has EXACTLY ONE writer in the codebase
    # (Invoke-FbWuEndOfPassDecision strict-convergence path). The
    # sysprep failure path no longer re-asserts PendingSysprep=$true
    # explicitly; instead, sentinel-preserve (Save-State omits the
    # -PendingSysprep parameter so the existing on-disk value is
    # left untouched). PendingSysprep is already $true on disk in
    # this code path because the convergence gate latched it before
    # we got here; sentinel-preserve keeps it true for the next-boot
    # STAGE 2 retry without making this function a hidden writer.
    try {
        $sysprepFailCurrent = Load-State
        Save-State `
            -EmptySweepCount   ([int]$sysprepFailCurrent.EmptySweepCount) `
            -LastPass          ([string]$sysprepFailCurrent.LastPass) `
            -StartTime         ([string]$sysprepFailCurrent.StartTime) `
            -FailureCount      ([int]$sysprepFailCurrent.FailureCount) `
            -UpdateRetry       $sysprepFailCurrent.UpdateRetry `
            -GivenUpUpdateIds  @($sysprepFailCurrent.GivenUpUpdateIds) `
            -BootCountByKey    $sysprepFailCurrent.BootCountByKey `
            -BootCount         ([int]$sysprepFailCurrent.BootCount) `
            -ForceHiddenKeys   @($sysprepFailCurrent.ForceHiddenKeys) `
            -RebootIterations  ([int]$sysprepFailCurrent.RebootIterations) `
            -SysprepRetryCount ([int]$sysprepFailCurrent.SysprepRetryCount)
        Write-FbLog ("Sysprep failure path: PendingSysprep preserved on disk via sentinel (current value={0}) for next-boot STAGE 2 retry." -f ([bool]$sysprepFailCurrent.PendingSysprep)) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Sysprep failure path: sentinel-preserve Save-State threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # Re-arm the four trigger layers so the loop relaunches on the
    # next boot. The pre-sysprep cleanup is no longer called (Fix
    # 5) so triggers SHOULD still be in place, but a sysprep
    # validation throw could leave the user-session in a partially-
    # logged-off state that consumed RunOnce. Belt-and-braces.
    try {
        Set-RunOnceAutostart -Reason 'sysprep launch failed; re-arming triggers for next-boot STAGE 2 retry'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Sysprep failure path: Set-RunOnceAutostart threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    try {
        $reArmAudit = Test-FbAndRepairTriggers -Context 'sysprep-failure-retry'
        Write-FbLog ("Sysprep failure path: trigger audit -> {0}" -f $reArmAudit) 'INFO'
    } catch {}

    # Escape valve: 3 sysprep refusals in a row is the signal that
    # the box is genuinely stuck on something CBS-side and another
    # reboot won't help. Trip the escape valve so the device routes
    # to OOBE with the "Updates Incomplete - tech review required"
    # popup and a panic record. SysprepRetryCount was bumped by
    # the Save-State at the top of this function, so it already
    # reflects post-bump.
    if ($currentSysprepRetry -ge $script:MaxSysprepRetries) {
        Write-FbLog ("Sysprep failure path: SysprepRetryCount={0} >= MaxSysprepRetries={1}; tripping escape valve with reason 'SYSPREP_REPEATEDLY_REFUSED'." -f $currentSysprepRetry, $script:MaxSysprepRetries) 'ERROR'
        try {
            $sysprepEscapeDisk = Load-State
            $sysprepEscapeFutile = 0
            try { $sysprepEscapeFutile = [int]$sysprepEscapeDisk.FutileBootCount } catch {}
            $sysprepEscapeThr = Get-FbFutileBootThreshold
            Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff -Reason 'SYSPREP_REPEATEDLY_REFUSED' -FutileNow $sysprepEscapeFutile -Threshold ([int]$sysprepEscapeThr.Threshold)
            # Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff -> Invoke-FbOobeHandoff
            # exits. Defensive: if it somehow returns, fall through
            # to the $false return below so the caller can route
            # to plain shutdown.
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Sysprep failure path: escape valve trip threw: {0}; falling through to plain-shutdown caller path." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
    }

    Write-FbLog 'Sysprep failure path: returning $false. Caller should route to plain shutdown so the next boot can retry STAGE 2 from a freshly-rebooted state with CBS settled.' 'WARN'
    return $false
}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026.05.19.2213m-soft-givenup-reach-and-splash-payload-copy-and-console-lock-fix
# Bug FF reach helper: Invoke-FbSoftGivenUpEvaluate.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Field evidence from G:\fb-dump-final-651 (5/19) proved that the 2213l Bug FF
# Mechanism 1 + Mechanism 2 split mitigation regressed in the real-world
# convergence path:
#
#   * Mechanism 2 (Defender fast-path counter pre-set inside
#     Invoke-FbBucketApply) fired EVERY boot - the "Bug FF Mechanism 2:
#     Defender platform ... installed successfully; per-boot tracker marked +
#     SoftGivenUp counter pre-set to 2 (threshold)" WARN line is present
#     across all 13 dump boots.
#   * Mechanism 1 (SoftGivenUp counter increment + ERROR-PROMOTION block,
#     previously wired ONLY at the main-loop "[Pass N] Single unfiltered
#     online scan" site) NEVER fired - zero "Bug FF SoftGivenUp" increment
#     lines and zero "ERROR-PROMOTION" lines across the entire dump.
#   * Convergence gate kept logging "given-up=0" on every STAGE 1 OOBE
#     handoff probe, kept finding KB5007651 in the residual, refused
#     handoff, consumed the futile-boot budget and exited as
#     ExitClass=budget-real / postResidual=1.
#
# Root cause: once InstallsCompleted=true latched on boot 1 (39 updates
# installed in one batch), boots 2+ took the startup short-circuit straight
# to Invoke-FbOobeHandoff and bypassed the main loop entirely. The STAGE 1
# OOBE handoff gate runs its OWN online scan inside Invoke-FbOobeHandoff
# (Invoke-FbWuScan call), and the STAGE 1 inline-retry pass
# (Invoke-FbWuInlineRetryPass, called from the same gate's refuse branch)
# also runs its own scan. Neither one ever called the Mechanism 1 increment
# block - it lived only in the main-loop scan site. The SoftGivenUp counter
# for KB5007651 was sitting at the threshold (pre-set by Mech 2 on boot 1)
# forever, never getting evaluated against subsequent scan results, never
# being promoted into the GivenUpSet that the convergence gate consults.
#
# Fix in 2213m: extract Mechanism 1 into this reusable helper and call it
# from ALL THREE scan sites (main-loop, oobe-handoff-stage1, inline-retry-
# pass-N). Distinct CallContext tags in every log line so a future dump
# tells us exactly which scan site promoted any given UpdateId.
#
# Side effects:
#   * Mutates $script:FbSoftGivenUpCountersLoop (hashtable, in-place
#     increments).
#   * Mutates $GivenUpSet (HashSet, in-place .Add of promoted UpdateIds AND
#     their Get-FbRetryKey alias so legacy callers that match on either
#     form still see the promotion).
#
# Returns:
#   [pscustomobject]@{
#     Incremented = <int>   # counter ++ events this call.
#     Promoted    = <int>   # UpdateIds that crossed the threshold and got
#                             added to $GivenUpSet on THIS call.
#     PromotedIds = @<str>  # the UpdateIds that were promoted.
#     Threshold   = <int>   # $script:FbSoftGivenUpThreshold at call time
#                             (snapshot; the threshold itself is constant
#                             during a single boot's run).
#   }
#
# Never throws. Internal failures are logged at WARN and the function
# returns whatever progress it made (or a zero-counts object).
function Invoke-FbSoftGivenUpEvaluate {
    [CmdletBinding()]
    param(
        # Scan rows from the most recent online scan at the call site.
        # AllowEmptyCollection so a converged-to-zero scan still routes
        # through here cleanly (the helper just returns zero counts).
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [AllowEmptyCollection()] [object[]] $ScanResults,
        # The caller's GivenUpSet (HashSet[string], case-insensitive). The
        # helper Adds promoted UpdateIds AND their Get-FbRetryKey alias to
        # this set in place. The caller is responsible for persisting it
        # via Save-State -GivenUpUpdateIds @($givenUpSet) afterwards.
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [AllowEmptyCollection()] [System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]] $GivenUpSet,
        # Tag emitted in every log line for this call so dumps can tell
        # which scan site triggered an increment/promotion. Conventional
        # values: 'main-loop', 'oobe-handoff-stage1', 'inline-retry-pass-1'
        # (etc.).
        [string] $CallContext = 'unknown'
    )

    $result = [pscustomobject]@{
        Incremented = 0
        Promoted    = 0
        PromotedIds = @()
        Threshold   = 2
    }

    try {
        $softThreshold = 2
        try { if ($null -ne $script:FbSoftGivenUpThreshold) { $softThreshold = [int]$script:FbSoftGivenUpThreshold } } catch {}
        $result.Threshold = $softThreshold

        # Defensive: if the script-scoped counter hashtable is somehow
        # null (early startup re-entry), bring it into existence. The
        # main-loop startup block at ~L9472 sets this for the normal
        # path; OOBE handoff entry from RunOnce could plausibly run
        # before that. Safe to (re)initialize to empty - the worst
        # case is one boot's worth of lost counter history.
        if ($null -eq $script:FbSoftGivenUpCountersLoop) {
            $script:FbSoftGivenUpCountersLoop = @{}
        }
        if ($null -eq $script:FbSuccessLedgerSet) {
            $script:FbSuccessLedgerSet = New-Object 'System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]' ([System.StringComparer]::OrdinalIgnoreCase)
        }

        $promotedList = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[string]
        foreach ($scanRow in @($ScanResults)) {
            $scanUid = ''
            try { $scanUid = [string]$scanRow.UpdateId } catch {}
            if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($scanUid)) { continue }
            if (-not $script:FbSuccessLedgerSet.Contains($scanUid)) { continue }
            if ($GivenUpSet.Contains($scanUid)) { continue }
            $priorCount = 0
            if ($script:FbSoftGivenUpCountersLoop.ContainsKey($scanUid)) {
                try { $priorCount = [int]$script:FbSoftGivenUpCountersLoop[$scanUid] } catch { $priorCount = 0 }
            }
            $newCount = $priorCount + 1
            $script:FbSoftGivenUpCountersLoop[$scanUid] = $newCount
            $result.Incremented++
            $scanRowTitle = ''
            try { $scanRowTitle = [string]$scanRow.Title } catch {}
            if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($scanRowTitle)) { $scanRowTitle = $scanUid }
            Write-FbLog ("Bug FF SoftGivenUp [{4}]: '{0}' ({1}) succeeded previously but is STILL applicable; counter {2} -> {3} (threshold={5})." -f $scanRowTitle, $scanUid, $priorCount, $newCount, $CallContext, $softThreshold) 'WARN'
            if ($newCount -ge $softThreshold) {
                [void]$GivenUpSet.Add($scanUid)
                $scanRowKey = ''
                try { $scanRowKey = [string](Get-FbRetryKey -Update $scanRow) } catch {}
                if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($scanRowKey)) { [void]$GivenUpSet.Add($scanRowKey) }
                Write-FbLog ("Bug FF SoftGivenUp ERROR-PROMOTION [{4}]: '{0}' ({1}) crossed threshold={2} (count={3}); added to GivenUpUpdateIds. Reason: 'Soft-GivenUp: returned ResultCode=2 {3} times but still applicable in next scan (Bug FF, threshold={2})'." -f $scanRowTitle, $scanUid, $softThreshold, $newCount, $CallContext) 'ERROR'
                $result.Promoted++
                $promotedList.Add($scanUid) | Out-Null
            }
        }
        $result.PromotedIds = @($promotedList.ToArray())

        if ($result.Incremented -gt 0 -or $result.Promoted -gt 0) {
            Write-FbLog ("Bug FF SoftGivenUp summary [{0}]: incremented={1} promoted-to-GivenUp={2} totalCountersTracked={3} successLedgerSize={4}." -f $CallContext, $result.Incremented, $result.Promoted, $script:FbSoftGivenUpCountersLoop.Count, $script:FbSuccessLedgerSet.Count) 'WARN'
        } else {
            # INFO-level summary even when nothing changed so a tech can
            # confirm the helper RAN at every scan site even on a clean
            # scan (the entire 2213l -> 2213m regression was caused by
            # the helper not running at all in this scan path, so
            # affirmative no-op evidence is exactly what we need).
            Write-FbLog ("Bug FF SoftGivenUp [{0}]: no-op (scanRows={1} successLedger={2} counters={3} givenUp={4})." -f $CallContext, @($ScanResults).Count, $script:FbSuccessLedgerSet.Count, $script:FbSoftGivenUpCountersLoop.Count, $GivenUpSet.Count) 'INFO'
        }
    } catch {
        try { Write-FbLog ("Bug FF SoftGivenUp helper [{0}] threw: {1}; returning partial result (incremented={2} promoted={3})." -f $CallContext, $_.Exception.Message, $result.Incremented, $result.Promoted) 'WARN' } catch {}
    }
    return $result
}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026.05.19.2213n Bug II: ScanStability convergence helper.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Implements the user's literal convergence rule: "When all updates are
# completed and a scan lists the same update with no other updates, updates
# are complete."
#
# Operationalized:
#   * Called from Invoke-FbOobeHandoff at the STAGE 1 gate ONLY (boot-level
#     gate scans, NOT main-loop, NOT inline-retry). Single call per boot.
#   * Builds a "blocking" set: every scan row whose UpdateId is NOT already
#     in $GivenUpSet AND NOT in $ForceHiddenSet.
#   * Computes a fingerprint = SHA256 of (sorted blocking UpdateIds joined
#     by `n) as a lowercase hex string. Empty blocking set produces ''.
#   * If the fingerprint matches the on-disk LastFingerprint, increments
#     ConsecutiveCount. Else resets to 1 with the new fingerprint.
#   * When ConsecutiveCount >= $script:FbScanStabilityThreshold (= 2 in
#     the literal interpretation of the rule), bulk-promotes every
#     blocking UpdateId into $GivenUpSet so the Bug Q residual filter
#     clears and STAGE 1 hands off to STAGE 2.
#
# Closes the Scenario Z gap (sticky failed install that is NOT in
# $script:FbSuccessLedgerSet, so Bug FF Mechanism 1 cannot reach it).
#
# Inputs:
#   $ScanResults    - scan rows from the gate's own Invoke-FbWuScan call.
#   $GivenUpSet     - HashSet of UpdateIds (and Get-FbRetryKey aliases)
#                     already classified as terminal. MUTATED in-place
#                     on convergence: each promoted UpdateId AND its
#                     Get-FbRetryKey alias is .Add()ed so legacy callers
#                     that match on either form still see the promotion.
#   $ForceHiddenSet - HashSet of force-hidden keys (optional).
#   $CallContext    - tag for log lines; convention is
#                     'oobe-handoff-stage1' (the ONLY caller in 2213n).
#   $StateBlock     - the ScanStability hashtable from Load-State. MUTATED
#                     in-place: LastFingerprint, ConsecutiveCount,
#                     LastSeenAt, LastResidualIds all updated every call.
#                     Caller is responsible for persisting via
#                     Save-State -ScanStability $StateBlock afterwards.
#
# Returns a pscustomobject:
#   Incremented      = $true/$false   - fingerprint matched prior so the
#                                        counter incremented; $false on
#                                        either reset (new fingerprint) or
#                                        empty-blocking no-op.
#   Converged        = $true/$false   - count crossed threshold THIS call.
#   Promoted         = <int>          - blocking UpdateIds promoted to
#                                        $GivenUpSet on convergence.
#   PromotedIds      = @<str>         - the actual promoted UpdateIds.
#   Fingerprint      = <lowercase hex>- fingerprint computed this call
#                                        ('' if blocking set was empty).
#   ConsecutiveCount = <int>          - the (possibly newly-incremented)
#                                        count after this call.
#   Threshold        = <int>          - snapshot of
#                                        $script:FbScanStabilityThreshold.
#   BlockingCount    = <int>          - blocking set size this call.
#
# Never throws. Internal failures logged at WARN; helper returns whatever
# partial result it built.
function Invoke-FbScanStabilityEvaluate {
    [CmdletBinding()]
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [AllowEmptyCollection()] [object[]] $ScanResults,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [AllowEmptyCollection()] [System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]] $GivenUpSet,
        [Parameter()] [AllowEmptyCollection()] [System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]] $ForceHiddenSet = $null,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string] $CallContext,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [hashtable] $StateBlock
    )

    $result = [pscustomobject]@{
        Incremented      = $false
        Converged        = $false
        Promoted         = 0
        PromotedIds      = @()
        Fingerprint      = ''
        ConsecutiveCount = 0
        Threshold        = 2
        BlockingCount    = 0
    }

    try {
        $stabilityThreshold = 2
        try { if ($null -ne $script:FbScanStabilityThreshold) { $stabilityThreshold = [int]$script:FbScanStabilityThreshold } } catch {}
        $result.Threshold = $stabilityThreshold

        if (-not $StateBlock.ContainsKey('LastFingerprint'))  { $StateBlock['LastFingerprint']  = '' }
        if (-not $StateBlock.ContainsKey('ConsecutiveCount')) { $StateBlock['ConsecutiveCount'] = 0 }
        if (-not $StateBlock.ContainsKey('LastSeenAt'))       { $StateBlock['LastSeenAt']       = '' }
        if (-not $StateBlock.ContainsKey('LastResidualIds'))  { $StateBlock['LastResidualIds']  = @() }

        $blockingIds = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[string]
        foreach ($scanRow in @($ScanResults)) {
            $scanUid = ''
            try { $scanUid = [string]$scanRow.UpdateId } catch {}
            if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($scanUid)) { continue }
            $scanKey = ''
            try { $scanKey = [string](Get-FbRetryKey -Update $scanRow) } catch {}
            $isGiven = $GivenUpSet.Contains($scanUid) -or ($scanKey -and $GivenUpSet.Contains($scanKey))
            $isHidden = $false
            if ($null -ne $ForceHiddenSet) {
                try { $isHidden = $ForceHiddenSet.Contains($scanUid) -or ($scanKey -and $ForceHiddenSet.Contains($scanKey)) } catch {}
            }
            if (-not $isGiven -and -not $isHidden) {
                $blockingIds.Add($scanUid)
            }
        }
        $result.BlockingCount = $blockingIds.Count

        $nowIso = ''
        try { $nowIso = (Get-Date -Format 'yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:sszzz') } catch {}
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($nowIso)) { try { $nowIso = (Get-Date).ToString('o') } catch {} }
        $StateBlock['LastSeenAt'] = $nowIso

        if ($blockingIds.Count -eq 0) {
            $StateBlock['LastFingerprint']  = ''
            $StateBlock['ConsecutiveCount'] = 0
            $StateBlock['LastResidualIds']  = @()
            $result.ConsecutiveCount = 0
            try { Write-FbLog ("Bug II ScanStability [{0}]: no-op (blocking=0 fingerprint=empty consecutive=0)" -f $CallContext) 'INFO' } catch {}
            return $result
        }

        $sortedIds = @($blockingIds.ToArray() | Sort-Object)
        $joined = ($sortedIds -join "`n")
        $fingerprintHex = ''
        try {
            $sha = [System.Security.Cryptography.SHA256]::Create()
            try {
                $hashBytes = $sha.ComputeHash([System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetBytes($joined))
                $hexBuilder = New-Object System.Text.StringBuilder
                foreach ($b in $hashBytes) { [void]$hexBuilder.Append(($b.ToString('x2'))) }
                $fingerprintHex = $hexBuilder.ToString()
            } finally {
                $sha.Dispose()
            }
        } catch {
            try { Write-FbLog ("Bug II ScanStability [{0}]: SHA256 fingerprint compute threw: {1}; falling back to plain-text joined ids (correctness preserved, less compact)." -f $CallContext, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN' } catch {}
            $fingerprintHex = $joined
        }
        $result.Fingerprint = $fingerprintHex

        $priorFingerprint = [string]$StateBlock['LastFingerprint']
        $priorCount = 0
        try { $priorCount = [int]$StateBlock['ConsecutiveCount'] } catch {}

        if ($fingerprintHex -eq $priorFingerprint -and -not [string]::IsNullOrEmpty($priorFingerprint)) {
            $newCount = $priorCount + 1
            $StateBlock['ConsecutiveCount'] = $newCount
            $result.Incremented      = $true
            $result.ConsecutiveCount = $newCount
        } else {
            $StateBlock['LastFingerprint']  = $fingerprintHex
            $StateBlock['ConsecutiveCount'] = 1
            $result.Incremented      = $false
            $result.ConsecutiveCount = 1
        }
        $StateBlock['LastResidualIds'] = @($sortedIds)

        $idsForLog = ($sortedIds -join ',')
        try { Write-FbLog ("Bug II ScanStability [{0}]: fingerprint='{1}' consecutive={2} (threshold={3}) blocking={4} ids=[{5}]" -f $CallContext, $fingerprintHex, $result.ConsecutiveCount, $stabilityThreshold, $blockingIds.Count, $idsForLog) 'WARN' } catch {}

        if ($result.ConsecutiveCount -ge $stabilityThreshold) {
            $result.Converged = $true
            try { Write-FbLog ("Bug II ScanStability CONVERGED [{0}]: scan returned identical {1} residual(s) for {2} consecutive cycle(s); promoting all residual UpdateIds to GivenUpSet with reason 'scan-stability-{3}'." -f $CallContext, $blockingIds.Count, $result.ConsecutiveCount, $stabilityThreshold) 'ERROR' } catch {}

            $promotedList = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[string]
            foreach ($scanRow in @($ScanResults)) {
                $promoteUid = ''
                try { $promoteUid = [string]$scanRow.UpdateId } catch {}
                if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($promoteUid)) { continue }
                if (-not $blockingIds.Contains($promoteUid)) { continue }
                if ($GivenUpSet.Contains($promoteUid)) { continue }
                [void]$GivenUpSet.Add($promoteUid)
                $promoteKey = ''
                try { $promoteKey = [string](Get-FbRetryKey -Update $scanRow) } catch {}
                if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($promoteKey)) { [void]$GivenUpSet.Add($promoteKey) }
                $promoteTitle = ''
                try { $promoteTitle = [string]$scanRow.Title } catch {}
                if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($promoteTitle)) { $promoteTitle = $promoteUid }
                try { Write-FbLog ("Bug II ScanStability PROMOTION [{0}]: '{1}' ({2}) added to GivenUpUpdateIds." -f $CallContext, $promoteTitle, $promoteUid) 'ERROR' } catch {}
                $promotedList.Add($promoteUid) | Out-Null
            }
            $result.Promoted = $promotedList.Count
            $result.PromotedIds = @($promotedList.ToArray())
            try { Write-FbLog ("Bug II ScanStability summary [{0}]: consecutive={1} promoted={2} fingerprint='{3}'" -f $CallContext, $result.ConsecutiveCount, $result.Promoted, $fingerprintHex) 'WARN' } catch {}
        }
    } catch {
        try { Write-FbLog ("Bug II ScanStability helper [{0}] threw: {1}; returning partial result (consecutive={2} promoted={3})." -f $CallContext, $_.Exception.Message, $result.ConsecutiveCount, $result.Promoted) 'WARN' } catch {}
    }
    return $result
}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-05-11 1145 cascade-exhausted handoff helpers.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Field report 2026-05-11 1100 (current build 1045-aggressive-ti-kill):
# device cycled through 3 full cascades AND the EWX_LOGOFF fallback,
# none of which actually rebooted or logged off. dashboard pinned at
# phase='Rebooting' / message='CASCADE BUDGET EXHAUSTED' with all 39
# updates marked 'Pending restart' at retry 3. Diagnosis: TrustedInstaller
# (or some other kernel-level component) is vetoing BOTH shutdown AND
# logoff. We've reached the limit of what user-mode reboot APIs can do
# from PowerShell on this device.
#
# This block adds three escape hatches:
#
#   1. Invoke-FbEmergencyScheduledTaskReboot: register a one-shot
#      schtasks /Create with /RU SYSTEM /RL HIGHEST that runs
#      shutdown.exe /r /f /t 0. The reboot then comes from the Task
#      Scheduler service at SYSTEM/HIGHEST, not from our user-mode
#      token; one of the few documented ways to override a TI block.
#   2. Invoke-FbOobeHandoff: graceful escape when ALL reboot paths
#      fail. The device is functionally fine (updates installed),
#      we just can't reboot it from script. Relabel all "Pending
#      restart" rows to "Completed (installed; will settle on first
#      user logon)", show a clear "deferred reboot" message, and
#      hand off to sysprep /oobe /reboot /quiet. Setup's reboot
#      path is different from ours and may bypass the block; if it
#      doesn't, the user power-cycles and the 39 pending-restart
#      updates apply at first user logon via Windows' own scheduled
#      update mechanism - this is normal Windows behaviour.
#   3. NUCLEAR option (CSRSS kill - commented out by default):
#      killing csrss.exe is treated by the kernel as a catastrophic
#      session failure and triggers an immediate bugcheck reboot.
#      Disabled by default because it could corrupt CBS state if a
#      package is mid-finalize. Documented for future maintainers.

function Invoke-FbEmergencyScheduledTaskReboot {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Schedule a one-shot SYSTEM-scoped shutdown.exe /r via
        Task Scheduler and wait 90s for it to take effect.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026-05-11 1145 escalation path. The user-mode cascade and
        EWX_LOGOFF fallback have both been vetoed by an OS-level
        component (TrustedInstaller is the usual suspect). Task
        Scheduler running a task at /RU SYSTEM /RL HIGHEST drives
        shutdown.exe from the Service Control Manager rather than
        from our user-mode token, which is one of the documented
        overrides for a kernel-side shutdown veto.

        Steps:
          1. Surface "Cascade exhausted - escalating to SYSTEM-scoped
             emergency reboot..." so the user sees the strategy
             change.
          2. Aggressively kill TrustedInstaller / TiWorker / dismhost
             / CompatTelRunner etc. via the existing Stage 4 helper
             so the TI block is gone before the scheduled task fires.
          3. Clear the WU RebootRequired and Session Manager Pending-
             FileRenameOperations registry values.
          4. schtasks /Create /SC ONCE /ST <now+1min> /TN
             "FirstBaseEmergencyReboot" /TR "shutdown /r /f /t 0"
             /RU SYSTEM /RL HIGHEST /F. /F overwrites any prior task
             with the same name (idempotent).
          5. Sleep 90s. If we are still alive after that, the task
             either didn't fire or the reboot was still vetoed.
             Return $false; the caller falls through to OOBE handoff.

        Never throws (catches all per-step errors and continues).
        Returns $true if we believe the reboot is in flight (the
        function literally never returns in this case - the box
        goes down during the 90s sleep), $false on any failure.
    #>
    param([string]$Reason = 'cascade-exhausted emergency reboot')

    Write-FbLog ("Emergency SYSTEM-scoped scheduled-task reboot requested: {0}" -f $Reason) 'WARN'
    Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message 'OOBE handoff: sysprep retry budget exhausted - escalating to SYSTEM-scoped emergency reboot...'
    Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Rebooting'

    # Step 2: aggressively kill the usual TI-family blockers. Same
    # helper used by Stage 4 of the normal cascade, so a TI block
    # registered by mid-finalize CBS work is released before the
    # scheduled task fires.
    try { Stop-FbBlockerProcessesAggressive } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Emergency reboot: blocker kill helper threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # Step 3: clear the post-process-kill registry survivors. CBS
    # keys deliberately preserved (corrupting them would break
    # future updates); we only want the OS-level reboot veto gone.
    try { Clear-FbPendingRebootRegistryValues } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Emergency reboot: registry-clear helper threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # Step 4: schedule the SYSTEM-scoped one-shot. schtasks.exe is
    # the documented surface here (Register-ScheduledTask via PS
    # would also work but schtasks is what the original spec calls
    # out and it's older/more battle-tested). Time computed as
    # "now + 1 minute" so the SCM scheduler has time to register
    # the task and the registry kills above have time to settle.
    $schtasks = Join-Path $env:WINDIR 'System32\schtasks.exe'
    if (-not (Test-Path $schtasks)) { $schtasks = 'schtasks.exe' }
    $taskTime = [DateTime]::Now.AddMinutes(1).ToString('HH:mm')
    $taskName = 'FirstBaseEmergencyReboot'
    $taskRun  = 'shutdown /r /f /t 0'
    $created  = $false
    try {
        $taskArgs = @(
            '/Create',
            '/SC', 'ONCE',
            '/ST', $taskTime,
            '/TN', $taskName,
            '/TR', $taskRun,
            '/RU', 'SYSTEM',
            '/RL', 'HIGHEST',
            '/F'
        )
        $proc = Start-Process -FilePath $schtasks -ArgumentList $taskArgs -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru -Wait -ErrorAction Stop
        if ($proc.ExitCode -eq 0) {
            $created = $true
            Write-FbLog ("Scheduled SYSTEM/HIGHEST emergency reboot task '{0}' for {1} (TR='{2}')." -f $taskName, $taskTime, $taskRun) 'WARN'
        } else {
            Write-FbLog ("schtasks /Create for '{0}' returned exit code {1}; emergency reboot may not fire." -f $taskName, $proc.ExitCode) 'ERROR'
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("schtasks /Create for emergency reboot threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
    }

    if (-not $created) {
        return $false
    }

    # Step 5: wait 90s, surfacing progress every 10s so the user
    # sees the dashboard is alive while the SCM scheduler does its
    # thing. The task is scheduled for now+1min, so we expect to
    # see the box go down around the 60s mark; the extra 30s of
    # buffer covers SCM warm-up latency and reboot grace.
    $waitTotal = 90
    $tickEvery = 10
    $waited = 0
    while ($waited -lt $waitTotal) {
        $remaining = $waitTotal - $waited
        $sleepFor  = [Math]::Min($tickEvery, $remaining)
        Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message ("SYSTEM scheduled-task reboot armed; waiting for SCM to fire ({0}s of {1}s)..." -f $waited, $waitTotal)
        Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Rebooting'
        Start-Sleep -Seconds $sleepFor
        $waited += $sleepFor
    }

    Write-FbLog 'Emergency scheduled-task reboot wait completed without the box going down. Proceeding to OOBE handoff.' 'ERROR'
    Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message 'Emergency scheduled-task reboot also blocked. Handing off to OOBE...'
    Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Rebooting'
    return $false
}

function Set-FbAllUpdatesDeferred {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Relabel "Pending restart" UI rows to "Completed" with
        the "Installed; will settle on first user logon" detail
        - ONLY when real install evidence exists.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence. The pre-1700
        implementation relabeled every Pending restart / Retrying /
        Installing / Downloading row to Completed with the
        deferred-settle note - even on a run that observed ZERO
        real installs (the WU-20260512-110026.log regression).
        That created a wu-status.json that lied: all 4 Critical
        rows showed "Installed; will settle on first user logon"
        despite no actual install having taken place.

        New rule: only relabel rows when there is real install
        evidence in this boot or a prior boot
        ($script:RealInstallsThisBoot > 0 OR state.RealInstallsTotal > 0).
        Otherwise leave the rows in their actual state so the
        dashboard tells the truth.

        Returns the count of relabeled rows.
    #>
    $realInstallsTotal = 0
    try {
        $diskNow = Load-State
        $realInstallsTotal = [int]$diskNow.RealInstallsTotal
    } catch {}
    $hasEvidence = ($script:RealInstallsThisBoot -gt 0) -or ($realInstallsTotal -gt 0)
    if (-not $hasEvidence) {
        Write-FbLog ("Set-FbAllUpdatesDeferred: SUPPRESSED relabel (RealInstallsThisBoot={0}, RealInstallsTotal={1}). Rows left in their actual state so the dashboard does not lie." -f $script:RealInstallsThisBoot, $realInstallsTotal) 'WARN'
        return 0
    }

    $count = 0
    foreach ($key in @($script:UiUpdates.Keys)) {
        $row = $script:UiUpdates[$key]
        if (-not $row) { continue }
        $currentStatus = [string]$row.Status
        if ($currentStatus -match '(?i)^(Pending restart|Retrying|Installing|Downloading)$') {
            $row.Status   = 'Completed'
            $row.Detail   = 'Installed; will settle on first user logon'
            $row.UpdatedAt = (Get-Date).ToString('o')
            $script:UiUpdates[$key] = $row
            $count++
        }
    }
    if ($count -gt 0) {
        Save-UiState
    }
    return $count
}

function Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundOnLogonTaskPreSysprep {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Creates FirstBase\DeferredOobeSoundOnLogon BEFORE sysprep.exe launches.
        Task Scheduler is still running at this point; the task XML survives
        sysprep /generalize and fires during OOBE defaultuser0 logon.
    .NOTES
        2247: Called from Invoke-FbOobeHandoff STAGE 2 after Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub
        returns and before Invoke-SysprepToOobe. Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub STEP 2
        unconditionally excludes this task name from its delete set so a prior-run
        remnant is not deleted by the scrub. The detached arm worker
        (FirstBasePostSysprepOobeSoundArm.ps1) is left unchanged as a last-resort
        fallback; it fails with 0x80004005 (COM E_FAIL) when Task Scheduler is gone,
        but this pre-sysprep arm succeeds reliably because Task Scheduler is still up.
    #>
    $taskName     = $script:FbDeferredOobeSoundOnLogonTaskName
    $bootstrapPs1 = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase 'FirstBaseOobeDeferredSoundBootstrap.ps1'

    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $bootstrapPs1)) {
        Write-FbLog ('2247: WARN DeferredOobeSoundOnLogon pre-sysprep arm skipped: bootstrap absent at {0}' -f $bootstrapPs1) 'WARN'
        return
    }

    $tr = ('powershell.exe -NonInteractive -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -WindowStyle Hidden -File "{0}"' -f $bootstrapPs1)

    # Delete stale task if it already exists (prior attempt or old scrub remnant).
    $null = & schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $taskName /F 2>&1

    $out = & schtasks.exe /Create /TN $taskName `
        /TR $tr `
        /SC ONLOGON /IT /DELAY 0000:05 /F 2>&1
    $ec  = $LASTEXITCODE

    if ($ec -eq 0) {
        Write-FbLog '2247: DeferredOobeSoundOnLogon ONLOGON task created pre-sysprep (Task Scheduler still running). Task XML survives generalize; fires for defaultuser0 at OOBE logon.' 'INFO'
    } else {
        Write-FbLog ('2247: WARN DeferredOobeSoundOnLogon pre-sysprep schtasks failed (exit={0}): {1}' -f $ec, ($out -join ' ')) 'WARN'
    }
}

function Invoke-FbInlineMdmScrub {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        5.0.38 (Option B): remove the PRIVILEGED provisioning blocks INLINE, in-process, as SYSTEM
        in the Administrator audit session at operator settings-close, BEFORE sysprep. Replaces both
        the retired 2296 FirstBase\MdmScrub scheduled-task round-trip AND the defaultuser0 finalize
        teardown that false-succeeded under the limited OOBE token (dump PF4VGG7V-1648).
    .DESCRIPTION
        The WU loop runs as SYSTEM in Session 0 (Bug PP / 2379), so these removals take effect with
        FULL privileges. Each HARD block (MDM policy keys, DmEnrollmentSvc, Autopilot firewall rule)
        is verified and gets a per-item PASS/FAIL; a loud ERROR is emitted on any HARD failure.
        Best-effort items (Defender exclusions, WU-AU policy, recovery BCD) log DONE/WARN. Idempotent:
        absent blocks verify PASS. Writes C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\Logs\WU-mdm-scrub.log +
        C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\.mdm-scrub-result (JSON). Returns @{ Overall='PASS'|'FAIL'; Items=@{} }.
        This is the SAME removal logic as the 2296 embedded FirstBaseMdmScrub.ps1, called directly.
    #>
    $items = [ordered]@{}
    $resultPath = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase '.mdm-scrub-result'
    $scrubLog = Join-Path (Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase 'Logs') 'WU-mdm-scrub.log'
    $writeInline = {
        param([string]$lvl, [string]$msg)
        try { Write-FbLog ('5038-inline-mdm-scrub: ' + $msg) $lvl } catch {}
        try {
            $dir = Split-Path -Parent $scrubLog
            if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $dir)) { New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $dir -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null }
            Add-Content -LiteralPath $scrubLog -Value ('[{0}] [{1}] {2}' -f (Get-Date -Format 'HH:mm:ss'), $lvl, $msg) -Encoding ascii -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        } catch {}
    }
    $who = 'unknown'
    try { $who = [string](whoami) } catch {}
    & $writeInline 'INFO' ('starting inline MDM/Autopilot scrub; runAs=' + $who)

    # 1) MDM policy keys (HARD; confirmed residue on PF4VGG7V-1648)
    $mdm = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\MDM'
    try {
        if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $mdm) {
            Remove-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $mdm -Name 'AutoEnrollMDM' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            Remove-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $mdm -Name 'UseAADCredentialType' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            $p = Get-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $mdm -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            $remain = @($p | Get-Member -MemberType NoteProperty -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Where-Object { $_.Name -notmatch '^PS' })
            if ($remain.Count -eq 0) { Remove-Item -LiteralPath $mdm -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue }
        }
        $p2 = Get-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $mdm -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        $mdmOk = (-not $p2) -or (($p2.PSObject.Properties.Name -notcontains 'AutoEnrollMDM') -and ($p2.PSObject.Properties.Name -notcontains 'UseAADCredentialType'))
        $items['MdmKeys'] = $(if ($mdmOk) { 'PASS' } else { 'FAIL' })
        & $writeInline $(if ($mdmOk) { 'INFO' } else { 'ERROR' }) ('MDM keys removal -> ' + $items['MdmKeys'])
    } catch { $items['MdmKeys'] = 'FAIL'; & $writeInline 'ERROR' ('MDM keys EXC: ' + $_.Exception.Message) }

    # 2) DmEnrollmentSvc (HARD; confirmed residue on PF4VGG7V-1648)
    try {
        Set-Service -Name 'DmEnrollmentSvc' -StartupType Automatic -ErrorAction Stop
        Start-Service -Name 'DmEnrollmentSvc' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        $sm = $null
        try {
            $svcCim = Get-CimInstance -ClassName Win32_Service -Filter "Name='DmEnrollmentSvc'" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            if ($svcCim) { $sm = [string]$svcCim.StartMode }
        } catch {}
        $svcOk = $sm -and ($sm -ne 'Disabled')
        $items['DmEnrollmentSvc'] = $(if ($svcOk) { 'PASS' } else { 'FAIL' })
        & $writeInline $(if ($svcOk) { 'INFO' } else { 'ERROR' }) ('DmEnrollmentSvc StartMode=' + $sm + ' -> ' + $items['DmEnrollmentSvc'])
    } catch { $items['DmEnrollmentSvc'] = 'FAIL'; & $writeInline 'ERROR' ('DmEnrollmentSvc EXC: ' + $_.Exception.Message) }

    # 3) Autopilot firewall rule (HARD)
    try {
        if (Get-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName 'FirstBase-Block-Autopilot' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue) {
            Remove-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName 'FirstBase-Block-Autopilot' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        }
        $fwOk = -not (Get-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName 'FirstBase-Block-Autopilot' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)
        $items['FirewallRule'] = $(if ($fwOk) { 'PASS' } else { 'FAIL' })
        & $writeInline $(if ($fwOk) { 'INFO' } else { 'ERROR' }) ('FirstBase-Block-Autopilot firewall rule -> ' + $items['FirewallRule'])
    } catch { $items['FirewallRule'] = 'FAIL'; & $writeInline 'ERROR' ('Firewall EXC: ' + $_.Exception.Message) }

    # 4) Defender exclusions (best-effort; not Autopilot-blocking)
    try {
        foreach ($ex in @('C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase', 'C:\ProgramData\FirstBase', 'C:\ProgramData\Microsoft\Windows\Start Menu\Programs\Startup')) {
            Remove-MpPreference -ExclusionPath $ex -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        }
        $items['DefenderExclusions'] = 'DONE'
        & $writeInline 'INFO' 'Defender exclusions removal attempted.'
    } catch { $items['DefenderExclusions'] = 'WARN'; & $writeInline 'WARN' ('Defender EXC: ' + $_.Exception.Message) }

    # 5) WU AU suppression policy (best-effort; written pre-operator to protect the operator window)
    try {
        $au = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate\AU'
        if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $au) {
            Remove-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $au -Name 'NoAutoRebootWithLoggedOnUsers' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            Remove-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $au -Name 'NoAutoUpdate' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        }
        $items['WuAuPolicy'] = 'DONE'
        & $writeInline 'INFO' 'WU AU suppression policy removal attempted.'
    } catch { $items['WuAuPolicy'] = 'WARN'; & $writeInline 'WARN' ('WU AU EXC: ' + $_.Exception.Message) }

    # 6) Recovery BCD restore (best-effort)
    try {
        & bcdedit /set '{current}' bootstatuspolicy DisplayAllFailures 2>&1 | Out-Null
        & bcdedit /set '{current}' recoveryenabled yes 2>&1 | Out-Null
        $items['RecoveryBcd'] = 'DONE'
        & $writeInline 'INFO' 'BCD recovery settings restored.'
    } catch { $items['RecoveryBcd'] = 'WARN'; & $writeInline 'WARN' ('bcdedit EXC: ' + $_.Exception.Message) }

    $hard = @($items['MdmKeys'], $items['DmEnrollmentSvc'], $items['FirewallRule'])
    $overall = $(if (@($hard | Where-Object { $_ -ne 'PASS' }).Count -eq 0) { 'PASS' } else { 'FAIL' })
    try {
        ([ordered]@{ overall = $overall; at = (Get-Date -Format 'o'); runAs = $who; mode = 'inline-administrator-session'; items = $items } | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 4) |
            Set-Content -LiteralPath $resultPath -Encoding ASCII -Force
    } catch { & $writeInline 'ERROR' ('result write EXC: ' + $_.Exception.Message) }
    & $writeInline $(if ($overall -eq 'PASS') { 'INFO' } else { 'ERROR' }) ('inline MDM scrub complete overall=' + $overall + '; result at ' + $resultPath)
    return @{ Overall = $overall; Items = $items }
}

function Invoke-FbOperatorSettingsGateInSession {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        5.0.38 (Option B): open the operator Settings window in the INTERACTIVE Administrator
        audit session (pre-sysprep) and wait, bounded, for the operator to close it.
    .DESCRIPTION
        The WU loop is SYSTEM / Session 0, so it launches FirstBaseOpenSettingsAndFinish.ps1 -GateOnly
        via an interactive scheduled task (/IT) so Settings renders on the operator's Session-1 desktop.
        The gate script writes the closed-marker when SystemSettings exits; this function also polls
        SystemSettings directly (works cross-session as SYSTEM). BOUNDED: if Settings never opens
        ($OpenTimeoutSec) or the operator walks away ($CloseTimeoutSec), the function returns so the
        loop proceeds to the inline scrub/seal/sysprep — the scrub's reliability does NOT depend on
        this interactive step. NOTE: the interactive /IT launch from Session 0 is the one part that
        needs on-hardware validation (Bug PP / 2379); the timeout guarantees forward progress either way.
    #>
    param(
        [int]$OpenTimeoutSec = 120,
        # 5.0.40 (2381): generous backstop for real operator work. The gate script now waits on the actual
        # Settings WINDOW closing (not the SystemSettings.exe process, which is SUSPENDED — not terminated —
        # when the operator closes the window, so process-exit never fires: dump PF4VGG7V-1003-Lenovo hit the
        # old 900s cap while the operator was still in Settings). This must exceed the gate's internal
        # WindowAcquireSec(180) + WindowCloseWaitSec(1800) = 1980s so the marker lands before this backstop.
        [int]$CloseTimeoutSec = 2100,
        [string]$SettingsUri = 'ms-settings:sound',
        [switch]$YouTubePass
    )
    $taskName = 'FirstBase\OperatorSettings'
    $gateScript = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase 'FirstBaseOpenSettingsAndFinish.ps1'
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $gateScript)) { $gateScript = Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseOpenSettingsAndFinish.ps1' }
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $gateScript)) {
        Write-FbLog '5038: operator Settings gate — FirstBaseOpenSettingsAndFinish.ps1 not found; skipping interactive gate (inline scrub will still run).' 'WARN'
        return [pscustomobject]@{ Seen = $false; Reason = 'gate-script-missing'; MarkerPresent = $false }
    }
    $closedMarker = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase '.operator-settings-closed'
    try { if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $closedMarker) { Remove-Item -LiteralPath $closedMarker -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } } catch {}
    # 5.0.39 (2380): the gate script writes/removes this to make the splash step aside while Settings is open.
    # Pre-clean any stale marker so a prior aborted run can't keep the splash minimized before this gate opens.
    $gateActiveMarker = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase '.operator-settings-gate-active'
    try { if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $gateActiveMarker) { Remove-Item -LiteralPath $gateActiveMarker -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } } catch {}

    $tr = if ($YouTubePass) {
        ('powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -WindowStyle Hidden -File "{0}" -GateOnly -YouTubePass -ClosedMarker "{1}"' -f $gateScript, $closedMarker)
    } else {
        ('powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -WindowStyle Hidden -File "{0}" -GateOnly -SettingsUri "{1}" -ClosedMarker "{2}"' -f $gateScript, $SettingsUri, $closedMarker)
    }
    $null = & schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $taskName /F 2>&1
    # /IT = run only when a user is logged on, in that interactive session (the auto-logon Administrator).
    $null = & schtasks.exe /Create /TN $taskName /TR $tr /SC ONCE /SD 01/01/2099 /ST 00:00 /RU Administrator /IT /RL HIGHEST /F 2>&1
    Write-FbLog ('5038: operator Settings gate — created interactive task {0} (create exit={1}).' -f $taskName, $LASTEXITCODE) 'INFO'
    $null = & schtasks.exe /Run /TN $taskName 2>&1
    Write-FbLog ('5038: operator {0} gate — /Run issued (exit={1}).' -f $(if ($YouTubePass) { 'YouTube PASS' } else { 'Settings' }), $LASTEXITCODE) 'INFO'

    # 5.0.40 (2381): The gate script (Session 1) is authoritative for "operator closed the window" — it
    # window-tracks the ApplicationFrameWindow and writes the closed-marker on real close, its own bounded
    # timeout, or "window never appeared". The WU loop is SYSTEM/Session 0 and CANNOT enumerate the Session-1
    # window, so it waits on that marker. We deliberately NO LONGER treat SystemSettings process-exit as
    # "closed" (the process is suspended, not terminated, on a normal close — the source of the premature
    # shutdown). We keep only a "process NEVER observed within OpenTimeoutSec" fast-fail (the interactive
    # launch genuinely failed → nothing to wait for) plus the generous CloseTimeoutSec backstop so the
    # scrub/seal/sysprep ALWAYS proceed even if the operator walks away or the gate script is torn down.
    # 5.4.8 (C875N44): CloseTimeoutSec is a NOTICE interval only. Do NOT treat
    # walk-away / close-timeout as operator confirmation. Device stays on until
    # a real close (window-closed / process-exit) or a launch-fail (window-never-appeared).
    $noticeAt = (Get-Date).AddSeconds($CloseTimeoutSec)
    $noticeCount = 0
    $start = Get-Date
    $seen = $false
    $done = $false
    # 5.1.43 (dump 0F000QG23233FB-0621-Microsoft / Surface): detect the gate SCRIPT process dying
    # WITHOUT writing the closed-marker. On this Surface unit the gate acquired the Settings window and
    # even started its 6s close-grace (mirror log 06:14:03) but the powershell process then vanished
    # BEFORE it could conclude window-closed and write .operator-settings-closed (no WER/.NET crash was
    # logged — most consistent with an external interactive-session/power teardown; a Surface
    # Kernel-Power "power source change" fired at 06:14:07 inside the grace window). The gate-active
    # marker was left present and the closed-marker was never written, so this wait sat the FULL
    # CloseTimeoutSec (2100s / 35 min) backstop while the operator — who HAD already closed Settings —
    # saw nothing happen and gave up. Fix: since the SYSTEM loop OWNS the gate task, watch the gate
    # process; once we've seen it alive, if it disappears with no closed-marker, wait only a short
    # gate-death grace (marker-flush race guard) then proceed to scrub/seal. This is hardware-agnostic
    # and does NOT reintroduce the 5.0.40 premature-close: it keys on OUR gate SCRIPT process ending,
    # never on SystemSettings.exe (which merely suspends on a normal close). The 5.0.41 strict-liveness
    # close-detection inside the still-running gate is untouched, and the CloseTimeoutSec hard backstop
    # remains as the ultimate fallback.
    $gateDeadGraceSec = 30
    $gateProcSeen = $false
    $gateEndedAt = $null
    $checkGateAlive = {
        try {
            $ps = @(Get-CimInstance -ClassName Win32_Process -Filter "Name='powershell.exe'" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |
                Where-Object { $_.CommandLine -and ($_.CommandLine -like '*FirstBaseOpenSettingsAndFinish.ps1*') -and ($_.CommandLine -like '*-GateOnly*') })
            return ($ps.Count -gt 0)
        } catch { return $true }  # query failed -> assume alive (never false-positive a death)
    }
    while ($true) {
        if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $closedMarker) {
            $reason = ''
            try { $reason = (Get-Content -LiteralPath $closedMarker -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue) } catch {}
            $parsed = 'unknown'
            if ($reason -match 'reason=([^\s]+)') { $parsed = $Matches[1] }
            elseif ($reason -match 'browser-missing') { $parsed = 'browser-missing' }
            if ($parsed -eq 'close-timeout' -or $parsed -eq 'close-timeout-fallback') {
                Write-FbLog ('5.4.8: operator gate wrote close-timeout marker; IGNORING (not a real close). Device stays on. Close YouTube or Settings to continue.') 'WARN'
                try { Remove-Item -LiteralPath $closedMarker -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } catch {}
                try { Start-FbHandoffSplashIndicator -Phase 'close-wait' -AutoCloseSeconds 120 } catch {}
            } else {
                Write-FbLog ('5038: operator Settings gate — closed-marker observed; gate reported: {0}' -f ($reason -replace '\s+', ' ').Trim()) 'INFO'
                $done = $true
                break
            }
        }
        if ($YouTubePass) {
            try {
                $browserAlive = @(Get-CimInstance -ClassName Win32_Process -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Where-Object {
                    $_.Name -match '^(msedge|chrome)\.exe$' -and $_.CommandLine -and ($_.CommandLine -like '*FirstBaseHwGateBrowser*')
                })
                if ($browserAlive.Count -gt 0) { $seen = $true }
            } catch {}
        } else {
            $alive = @(Get-Process -Name 'SystemSettings' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)
            if ($alive.Count -gt 0) { $seen = $true }
            if (-not $seen -and (((Get-Date) - $start).TotalSeconds -ge $OpenTimeoutSec)) {
                Write-FbLog ('5038: operator Settings gate — host NEVER observed within {0}s (interactive /IT launch likely did not render); proceeding.' -f $OpenTimeoutSec) 'WARN'
                $done = $true
                break
            }
        }
        # 5.1.43: gate-process-death detection (Surface teardown). Only arms AFTER we have positively
        # observed the gate process alive, so a launch/startup race can never false-trigger it.
        $gateProcAlive = & $checkGateAlive
        if ($gateProcAlive) {
            $gateProcSeen = $true
            if ($null -ne $gateEndedAt) { $gateEndedAt = $null }  # gate came back / transient — reset grace
        } elseif ($gateProcSeen) {
            if ($null -eq $gateEndedAt) {
                $gateEndedAt = Get-Date
                Write-FbLog ('5.1.43: operator Settings gate PROCESS gone with no closed-marker (gate script torn down before it could signal — Surface/hardware session-teardown pattern); starting {0}s gate-death grace before proceeding.' -f $gateDeadGraceSec) 'WARN'
            } elseif (((Get-Date) - $gateEndedAt).TotalSeconds -ge $gateDeadGraceSec) {
                # Re-check the marker one last time in case the gate wrote it during the grace.
                if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $closedMarker) {
                    $reason = ''
                    try { $reason = (Get-Content -LiteralPath $closedMarker -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue) } catch {}
                    Write-FbLog ('5.1.43: closed-marker appeared during gate-death grace; gate reported: {0}' -f ($reason -replace '\s+', ' ').Trim()) 'INFO'
                } else {
                    Write-FbLog ('5.4.8: operator Settings gate ended WITHOUT a closed-marker (gate process gone for {0}s); NOT sealing. Re-launching operator gate. Device stays on.' -f $gateDeadGraceSec) 'WARN'
                    try { $null = & schtasks.exe /Run /TN $taskName 2>&1 } catch {}
                    $gateProcSeen = $false
                    $gateEndedAt = $null
                    try { Start-FbHandoffSplashIndicator -Phase 'close-wait' -AutoCloseSeconds 120 } catch {}
                }
            }
        }
        if ((Get-Date) -ge $noticeAt) {
            $noticeCount++
            Write-FbLog ('5.4.8: operator gate still waiting after {0}s (notice {1}); NOT sealing. Close the YouTube window or Settings to continue. Device stays on.' -f $CloseTimeoutSec, $noticeCount) 'WARN'
            try { Start-FbHandoffSplashIndicator -Phase 'close-wait' -AutoCloseSeconds 120 } catch {}
            $noticeAt = (Get-Date).AddSeconds($CloseTimeoutSec)
        }
        Start-Sleep -Seconds 2
    }
    if (-not $done) {
        Write-FbLog ('5.4.8: operator Settings gate wait ended without a real close (should not happen); NOT treating as closed. Device stays on.') 'ERROR'
    }
    # 5.0.39 (2380): backstop — always clear the splash stand-down marker so a torn-down gate script can't
    # leave a stale marker (harmless for the splash, which closes on seal, but keep state tidy pre-sysprep).
    try { if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $gateActiveMarker) { Remove-Item -LiteralPath $gateActiveMarker -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } } catch {}
    try { $null = & schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $taskName /F 2>&1 } catch {}
    $markerText = ''
    try { if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $closedMarker) { $markerText = [string](Get-Content -LiteralPath $closedMarker -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue) } } catch {}
    $parsedReason = 'unknown'
    if ($markerText -match 'reason=([^\s]+)') { $parsedReason = $Matches[1] }
    elseif ($markerText -match 'browser-missing') { $parsedReason = 'browser-missing' }
    return [pscustomobject]@{
        Seen           = [bool]$seen
        Reason         = $parsedReason
        MarkerPresent  = [bool](Test-Path -LiteralPath $closedMarker)
        MarkerText     = $markerText
    }
}

function Register-FbMdmScrubTaskPreSysprep {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        [RETIRED 5.0.38 — NO LONGER CALLED] Superseded by Invoke-FbInlineMdmScrub. The 2296 SYSTEM
        scheduled-task round-trip is replaced by an INLINE, in-process, full-privilege scrub run by
        the SYSTEM WU loop in the Administrator audit session (see Invoke-FbOobeHandoff STAGE 2).
        Function body retained (unused) to minimize churn; safe to delete in a later cleanup pass.

        2296 (original): Pre-stage the SYSTEM task FirstBase\MdmScrub that removes the PRIVILEGED
        provisioning blocks at settings-close (MDM policy keys AutoEnrollMDM /
        UseAADCredentialType, DmEnrollmentSvc start-type, FirstBase-Block-Autopilot
        firewall rule, Defender exclusions, WU AU suppression policy, recovery BCD).
    .DESCRIPTION
        FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 runs in the LIMITED OOBE defaultuser0 token, so
        performing these removals inline SILENTLY FAILS (confirmed residue on dump
        PF4VGG7V-1648: AutoEnrollMDM=0 + DmEnrollmentSvc=Disabled survived past settings-
        close because the finalize -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue calls false-succeeded).
        This task runs as SYSTEM / RL HIGHEST so the removal actually takes effect.

        It is TRIGGERLESS (/SC ONCE far-future placeholder) and is launched ON DEMAND by
        finalize via schtasks /Run when the operator closes the OOBE Settings window — so
        the block STAYS IN PLACE through sysprep and the entire OOBE sit period, and is
        removed ONLY at settings-close, exactly as required. Default task security grants
        Authenticated Users Execute, so the limited defaultuser0 finalize can /Run it.

        The scrub logic is written to C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\FirstBaseMdmScrub.ps1 from an
        inline here-string (no staged payload file, so TechInstall is unchanged). ProgramData
        survives sysprep /generalize and the task XML at
        C:\Windows\System32\Tasks\FirstBase\MdmScrub survives generalize; both are excluded
        from Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub STEP 2. The scrub writes a VERIFIED, NON-SILENT result
        marker C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\.mdm-scrub-result (JSON) + log
        C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\Logs\WU-mdm-scrub.log, then self-deletes its own task.
    .NOTES
        Called from Invoke-FbOobeHandoff STAGE 2 after Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundOnLogonTaskPreSysprep.
    #>
    $taskName = 'FirstBase\MdmScrub'
    $scrubPs1 = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase 'FirstBaseMdmScrub.ps1'

    $scrubBody = @'
$ErrorActionPreference = 'Continue'
$pd = 'C:\ProgramData\FirstBase'
$logDir = Join-Path $pd 'Logs'
$log = Join-Path $logDir 'WU-mdm-scrub.log'
$resultPath = Join-Path $pd '.mdm-scrub-result'
try { if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $logDir)) { New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $logDir -Force | Out-Null } } catch {}
function Write-Scrub([string]$lvl, [string]$msg) {
    $line = ('[{0}] [{1}] {2}' -f (Get-Date -Format 'HH:mm:ss'), $lvl, $msg)
    try { Add-Content -LiteralPath $log -Value $line } catch {}
}
$who = try { (whoami) } catch { 'unknown' }
Write-Scrub 'INFO' ('2296: MdmScrub (SYSTEM) starting; runAs=' + $who)
$items = [ordered]@{}

# 1) MDM policy keys (HARD-verified: confirmed residue on PF4VGG7V-1648)
$mdm = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\MDM'
try {
    if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $mdm) {
        Remove-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $mdm -Name 'AutoEnrollMDM' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        Remove-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $mdm -Name 'UseAADCredentialType' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        $p = Get-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $mdm -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        $remain = @($p | Get-Member -MemberType NoteProperty | Where-Object { $_.Name -notmatch '^PS' })
        if ($remain.Count -eq 0) { Remove-Item -LiteralPath $mdm -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue }
    }
    $p2 = Get-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $mdm -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
    $ok = (-not $p2) -or (($p2.PSObject.Properties.Name -notcontains 'AutoEnrollMDM') -and ($p2.PSObject.Properties.Name -notcontains 'UseAADCredentialType'))
    $items['MdmKeys'] = if ($ok) { 'PASS' } else { 'FAIL' }
    Write-Scrub $(if ($ok) { 'INFO' } else { 'ERROR' }) ('2296: MDM keys removal -> ' + $items['MdmKeys'])
} catch { $items['MdmKeys'] = 'FAIL'; Write-Scrub 'ERROR' ('2296: MDM keys EXC: ' + $_.Exception.Message) }

# 2) DmEnrollmentSvc (HARD-verified: confirmed residue on PF4VGG7V-1648)
try {
    Set-Service -Name 'DmEnrollmentSvc' -StartupType Automatic -ErrorAction Stop
    Start-Service -Name 'DmEnrollmentSvc' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
    $sm = (Get-CimInstance -ClassName Win32_Service -Filter "Name='DmEnrollmentSvc'" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).StartMode
    $ok = $sm -and ($sm -ne 'Disabled')
    $items['DmEnrollmentSvc'] = if ($ok) { 'PASS' } else { 'FAIL' }
    Write-Scrub $(if ($ok) { 'INFO' } else { 'ERROR' }) ('2296: DmEnrollmentSvc StartMode=' + $sm + ' -> ' + $items['DmEnrollmentSvc'])
} catch { $items['DmEnrollmentSvc'] = 'FAIL'; Write-Scrub 'ERROR' ('2296: DmEnrollmentSvc EXC: ' + $_.Exception.Message) }

# 3) Autopilot firewall rule (HARD-verified)
try {
    if (Get-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName 'FirstBase-Block-Autopilot' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue) {
        Remove-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName 'FirstBase-Block-Autopilot' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
    }
    $ok = -not (Get-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName 'FirstBase-Block-Autopilot' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)
    $items['FirewallRule'] = if ($ok) { 'PASS' } else { 'FAIL' }
    Write-Scrub $(if ($ok) { 'INFO' } else { 'ERROR' }) ('2296: FirstBase-Block-Autopilot firewall rule -> ' + $items['FirewallRule'])
} catch { $items['FirewallRule'] = 'FAIL'; Write-Scrub 'ERROR' ('2296: Firewall EXC: ' + $_.Exception.Message) }

# 4) Defender exclusions (best-effort; not Autopilot-blocking)
try {
    foreach ($ex in @('C:\Windows\Setup\FirstBase', 'C:\ProgramData\FirstBase', 'C:\ProgramData\Microsoft\Windows\Start Menu\Programs\Startup')) {
        Remove-MpPreference -ExclusionPath $ex -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
    }
    $items['DefenderExclusions'] = 'DONE'
    Write-Scrub 'INFO' '2296: Defender exclusions removal attempted.'
} catch { $items['DefenderExclusions'] = 'WARN'; Write-Scrub 'WARN' ('2296: Defender EXC: ' + $_.Exception.Message) }

# 5) WU AU suppression policy (best-effort)
try {
    $au = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate\AU'
    if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $au) {
        Remove-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $au -Name 'NoAutoRebootWithLoggedOnUsers' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        Remove-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $au -Name 'NoAutoUpdate' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
    }
    $items['WuAuPolicy'] = 'DONE'
    Write-Scrub 'INFO' '2296: WU AU suppression policy removal attempted.'
} catch { $items['WuAuPolicy'] = 'WARN'; Write-Scrub 'WARN' ('2296: WU AU EXC: ' + $_.Exception.Message) }

# 6) Recovery BCD restore (best-effort)
try {
    & bcdedit /set '{current}' bootstatuspolicy DisplayAllFailures 2>&1 | Out-Null
    & bcdedit /set '{current}' recoveryenabled yes 2>&1 | Out-Null
    $items['RecoveryBcd'] = 'DONE'
    Write-Scrub 'INFO' '2296: BCD recovery settings restored.'
} catch { $items['RecoveryBcd'] = 'WARN'; Write-Scrub 'WARN' ('2296: bcdedit EXC: ' + $_.Exception.Message) }

$hard = @($items['MdmKeys'], $items['DmEnrollmentSvc'], $items['FirewallRule'])
$overall = if (@($hard | Where-Object { $_ -ne 'PASS' }).Count -eq 0) { 'PASS' } else { 'FAIL' }
$obj = [ordered]@{ overall = $overall; at = (Get-Date -Format 'o'); runAs = $who; items = $items }
try { ($obj | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 4) | Set-Content -LiteralPath $resultPath -Encoding ASCII -Force } catch { Write-Scrub 'ERROR' ('2296: result write EXC: ' + $_.Exception.Message) }
Write-Scrub $(if ($overall -eq 'PASS') { 'INFO' } else { 'ERROR' }) ('2296: MdmScrub complete overall=' + $overall + '; result at ' + $resultPath)
try { & schtasks.exe /Delete /TN 'FirstBase\MdmScrub' /F 2>&1 | Out-Null } catch {}
'@

    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $FbProgramDataFirstBase)) {
            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
        }
        Set-Content -LiteralPath $scrubPs1 -Value $scrubBody -Encoding ASCII -Force -ErrorAction Stop
        Write-FbLog ('2296: wrote MdmScrub SYSTEM scrub script to {0}' -f $scrubPs1) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ('2296: ERROR writing MdmScrub scrub script to {0}: {1}' -f $scrubPs1, $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
        return
    }

    $tr = ('powershell.exe -NonInteractive -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -WindowStyle Hidden -File "{0}"' -f $scrubPs1)

    # Delete stale task if present (prior attempt / old scrub remnant), then create triggerless.
    $null = & schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $taskName /F 2>&1
    $out = & schtasks.exe /Create /TN $taskName `
        /TR $tr `
        /SC ONCE /SD 01/01/2099 /ST 00:00 /RU SYSTEM /RL HIGHEST /F 2>&1
    $ec = $LASTEXITCODE

    if ($ec -eq 0) {
        Write-FbLog '2296: FirstBase\MdmScrub SYSTEM task created pre-sysprep (triggerless; launched on demand by finalize at settings-close). Task XML survives generalize.' 'INFO'
    } else {
        Write-FbLog ('2296: WARN FirstBase\MdmScrub schtasks /Create failed (exit={0}): {1}' -f $ec, ($out -join ' ')) 'WARN'
    }
}

function Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundWinlogonUserinit {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Appends the bootstrap launcher to Winlogon\Userinit so it fires for
        defaultuser0 during OOBE. Does not require Task Scheduler or explorer.exe.
        Userinit is not reset by sysprep /generalize.
    .NOTES
        2248: Called from Invoke-FbOobeHandoff STAGE 2 after
        Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundOnLogonTaskPreSysprep and before
        Invoke-SysprepToOobe. Userinit runs as the logged-on user (correct
        session for ms-settings:sound) for every user in every session,
        including defaultuser0 during OOBE. Finalize restores Userinit to
        'C:\Windows\system32\userinit.exe,' and removes the arm marker.
    #>
    $wlKey        = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Winlogon'
    $bootstrapPs1 = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase 'FirstBaseOobeDeferredSoundBootstrap.ps1'

    if (-not (Test-Path $bootstrapPs1)) {
        Write-FbLog ('2248: WARN Userinit arm skipped: bootstrap absent at {0}' -f $bootstrapPs1) 'WARN'
        return
    }

    try {
        $cur = (Get-ItemProperty -Path $wlKey -Name Userinit -ErrorAction Stop).Userinit
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ('2248: WARN cannot read Winlogon\Userinit: {0}' -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        return
    }

    # Guard: already injected (idempotent)
    if ($cur -match 'FirstBase') {
        Write-FbLog '2248: Winlogon\Userinit already contains FirstBase entry; skipping duplicate arm.' 'INFO'
        return
    }

    # Append: Userinit is a comma-separated list. Standard value ends with a comma.
    $inject = ('powershell.exe -NonInteractive -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -WindowStyle Hidden -File "{0}"' -f $bootstrapPs1)
    $new    = $cur.TrimEnd(',') + ',' + $inject + ','
    try {
        Set-ItemProperty -Path $wlKey -Name Userinit -Value $new -ErrorAction Stop
        Write-FbLog ('2248: Winlogon\Userinit patched for OOBE defaultuser0 bootstrap delivery. New value length={0}' -f $new.Length) 'INFO'

        # Write a marker so finalize can verify and clean up
        $markerPath = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase '.deferred-oobe-sound-userinit-armed'
        [System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($markerPath, ('patched|{0}' -f [DateTime]::UtcNow.ToString('o')))
        Write-FbLog ('2248: wrote userinit-armed marker {0}' -f $markerPath) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ('2248: ERROR patching Winlogon\Userinit: {0}' -f $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
    }
}

function Stop-FbHandoffSplashIndicator {
    try {
        $pat = 'FirstBaseHandoffSplash\.ps1'
        $pids = @(Get-CimInstance -ClassName Win32_Process -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |
            Where-Object { $_.CommandLine -and ($_.Name -match '^(powershell|pwsh)\.exe$') -and ($_.CommandLine -match $pat) } |
            Select-Object -ExpandProperty ProcessId -Unique)
        foreach ($spid in $pids) {
            if ($spid -eq $PID) { continue }
            try { Stop-Process -Id ([int]$spid) -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } catch {}
        }
        $lock = Join-Path $FbRoot 'State\handoff-indicator.lock'
        if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $lock) { Remove-Item -LiteralPath $lock -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue }
    } catch {}
}

function Start-FbHandoffSplashIndicator {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Non-blocking OOBE handoff splash (no KB list). Lock in
        FirstBaseHandoffSplash.ps1 prevents a second window.
    #>
    param(
        [ValidateSet('preparing-handoff', 'restart-for-checks', 'running-checks', 'checks-pass', 'checks-fail', 'sealing', 'gate-fail-restart', 'close-wait')]
        [string]$Phase = 'preparing-handoff',
        [int]$AutoCloseSeconds = 90
    )
    Stop-FbHandoffSplashIndicator
    $ps1 = Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseHandoffSplash.ps1'
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $ps1)) {
        try { $ps1 = Join-Path $PSScriptRoot 'FirstBaseHandoffSplash.ps1' } catch { $ps1 = '' }
    }
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($ps1) -or -not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $ps1)) {
        Write-FbLog 'Handoff splash: FirstBaseHandoffSplash.ps1 not found; skipping indicator.' 'WARN'
        return
    }
    if ($AutoCloseSeconds -lt 5) { $AutoCloseSeconds = 5 }
    try {
        $argList = @(
            '-NoProfile', '-ExecutionPolicy', 'Bypass', '-STA',
            '-File', $ps1, '-Phase', $Phase, '-AutoCloseSeconds', ([string]$AutoCloseSeconds)
        )
        Start-Process -FilePath 'powershell.exe' -ArgumentList $argList -WindowStyle Normal -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null
        Write-FbLog ("Handoff splash started (phase={0} autoClose={1}s)." -f $Phase, $AutoCloseSeconds) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Handoff splash start failed: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

function Invoke-FbHardwareGateChecks {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Run Wi-Fi / camera / sound probes. Missing script or throw = FAIL (never skip-as-pass).
    #>
    $resultPath = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase '.hardware-gate-result.json'
    try { if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $resultPath) { Remove-Item -LiteralPath $resultPath -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } } catch {}
    $ps1 = Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseHardwareCheck.ps1'
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $ps1)) {
        try { $ps1 = Join-Path $PSScriptRoot 'FirstBaseHardwareCheck.ps1' } catch { $ps1 = '' }
    }
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($ps1) -or -not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $ps1)) {
        Write-FbLog 'Hardware gate: FirstBaseHardwareCheck.ps1 missing; treating as FAIL (do not skip-as-pass).' 'ERROR'
        return [pscustomobject]@{ Passed = $false; Summary = 'missing-script'; Failed = @('sound', 'camera', 'wifi') }
    }
    $exitCode = -1
    try {
        $p = Start-Process -FilePath 'powershell.exe' -ArgumentList @(
            '-NoProfile', '-ExecutionPolicy', 'Bypass', '-File', $ps1, '-ResultPath', $resultPath, '-Quiet'
        ) -Wait -PassThru -NoNewWindow -ErrorAction Stop
        if ($p) { $exitCode = [int]$p.ExitCode }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Hardware gate: probe process threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
        return [pscustomobject]@{ Passed = $false; Summary = 'probe-threw'; Failed = @('sound', 'camera', 'wifi', 'pnp') }
    }
    if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $resultPath) {
        try {
            $parsed = Get-Content -LiteralPath $resultPath -Raw -ErrorAction Stop | ConvertFrom-Json
            if ($parsed) {
                Write-FbLog ("Hardware gate: {0} (exit={1})" -f [string]$parsed.Summary, $exitCode) $(if ($parsed.Passed) { 'INFO' } else { 'WARN' })
                return $parsed
            }
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Hardware gate: result JSON parse failed: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
        }
    }
    Write-FbLog ("Hardware gate: no usable result JSON (exit={0}); treating as FAIL." -f $exitCode) 'ERROR'
    return [pscustomobject]@{ Passed = $false; Summary = 'no-result'; Failed = @('sound', 'camera', 'wifi', 'pnp') }
}

function Invoke-FbHardwareGateOperatorStep {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Run hardware checks, then Pass (private YouTube) or Fail (Settings).
        Returns $true when the Pass path completed (YouTube window closed).
        Returns $false for Fail (Settings closed) or when YouTube never started.
    #>
    $script:FbHardwareGateFailed = $true
    $failMarker = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase '.hardware-gate-fail-restart'
    $doneMarker = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase '.hardware-gate-complete'
    try { if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $failMarker) { Remove-Item -LiteralPath $failMarker -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } } catch {}

    $hwCbsPending = $false
    try { $hwCbsPending = [bool](Test-FbWuRebootRequired) } catch { $hwCbsPending = $false }
    if ($hwCbsPending) {
        Write-FbLog 'Hardware gate: OS still reports pending reboot (CBS/WU). Waiting briefly before probes; if it does not clear, FAIL (Settings) — do not open YouTube with uncommitted drivers.' 'WARN'
        $hwWaitOk = $false
        try { $hwWaitOk = [bool](Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared -Context 'hardware-gate' -RunCbsCleanupFirst $false -MaxWaitSeconds 120 -AdaptiveBudget $false) } catch { $hwWaitOk = $false }
        try { $hwCbsPending = [bool](Test-FbWuRebootRequired) } catch { $hwCbsPending = -not $hwWaitOk }
        if ($hwCbsPending) {
            $script:FbHardwareGateFailed = $true
            try { Start-FbHandoffSplashIndicator -Phase 'checks-fail' -AutoCloseSeconds 8 } catch {}
            Start-Sleep -Seconds 2
            Stop-FbHandoffSplashIndicator
            try { Invoke-FbOperatorSettingsGateInSession | Out-Null } catch {
                Write-FbLog ("Hardware gate FAIL Settings gate threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
            }
            try { Set-Content -LiteralPath $failMarker -Value ('hardware-gate FAIL at {0} summary=cbs-pending-uncommitted-drivers' -f (Get-Date -Format 'o')) -Encoding ascii -Force } catch {}
            try { Set-Content -LiteralPath $doneMarker -Value ('hardware-gate FAIL at {0} settings-closed cbs-pending' -f (Get-Date -Format 'o')) -Encoding ascii -Force } catch {}
            try { Start-FbHandoffSplashIndicator -Phase 'gate-fail-restart' -AutoCloseSeconds 90 } catch {}
            Write-FbLog 'Hardware gate FAIL: CBS reboot still pending; Settings (not YouTube).' 'WARN'
            return $false
        }
    }

    try { Start-FbHandoffSplashIndicator -Phase 'running-checks' -AutoCloseSeconds 60 } catch {}
    $hw = $null
    try { $hw = Invoke-FbHardwareGateChecks } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Hardware gate: Invoke-FbHardwareGateChecks threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
        $hw = [pscustomobject]@{ Passed = $false; Summary = 'threw' }
    }
    $hwPassed = $false
    try { $hwPassed = [bool]$hw.Passed } catch { $hwPassed = $false }

    $youtubeOk = $false
    if ($hwPassed) {
        try { Start-FbHandoffSplashIndicator -Phase 'checks-pass' -AutoCloseSeconds 8 } catch {}
        Start-Sleep -Seconds 2
        Stop-FbHandoffSplashIndicator
        $yt = $null
        try { $yt = Invoke-FbOperatorSettingsGateInSession -YouTubePass } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Hardware gate PASS YouTube gate threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
        }
        $reason = ''
        try { $reason = [string]$yt.Reason } catch { $reason = '' }
        $seen = $false
        try { $seen = [bool]$yt.Seen } catch { $seen = $false }
        # Pass only on a real operator close. close-timeout is NOT confirmation (C875N44).
        # Browser missing / never appeared is NOT a silent pass — fall through to Settings (Fail).
        if ($reason -eq 'window-closed') {
            $youtubeOk = $true
        } else {
            Write-FbLog ("Hardware gate: YouTube PASS path did not complete (reason={0} seen={1}); opening Settings (Fail)." -f $reason, $seen) 'WARN'
        }
    }

    if ($youtubeOk) {
        $script:FbHardwareGateFailed = $false
        try {
            Set-Content -LiteralPath $doneMarker -Value ('hardware-gate PASS at {0} youtube-closed' -f (Get-Date -Format 'o')) -Encoding ascii -Force
        } catch {}
        try { Start-FbHandoffSplashIndicator -Phase 'sealing' -AutoCloseSeconds 90 } catch {}
        Write-FbLog 'Hardware gate PASS: private YouTube closed; continuing seal + sysprep /oobe /reboot (customer OOBE).' 'INFO'
        return $true
    }

    $script:FbHardwareGateFailed = $true
    try {
        Set-Content -LiteralPath $failMarker -Value ('hardware-gate FAIL at {0} summary={1}' -f (Get-Date -Format 'o'), $(if ($hw) { [string]$hw.Summary } else { 'unknown' })) -Encoding ascii -Force
    } catch {}
    try { Start-FbHandoffSplashIndicator -Phase 'checks-fail' -AutoCloseSeconds 8 } catch {}
    Start-Sleep -Seconds 2
    Stop-FbHandoffSplashIndicator
    try { Invoke-FbOperatorSettingsGateInSession | Out-Null } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Hardware gate FAIL Settings gate threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    try {
        Set-Content -LiteralPath $doneMarker -Value ('hardware-gate FAIL at {0} settings-closed' -f (Get-Date -Format 'o')) -Encoding ascii -Force
    } catch {}
    try { Start-FbHandoffSplashIndicator -Phase 'gate-fail-restart' -AutoCloseSeconds 90 } catch {}
    Write-FbLog 'Hardware gate FAIL: Settings closed; continuing scrub + sysprep /oobe /reboot (not shutdown, not YouTube).' 'WARN'
    return $false
}

function Invoke-FbOobeHandoff {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Cascade-exhausted graceful exit: relabel UI, hand off to
        sysprep, exit cleanly so the watchdog does NOT relaunch.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026-05-11 1145 escape hatch. Triggered when:
          (a) Startup observes CascadeExhaustionCount >= 2.
          (b) The CascadeExhaustionCount==1 emergency scheduled-
              task reboot also failed.
          (c) Invoke-Reboot in cascade-exhaustion mode (called from
              elsewhere) decides handoff is the safer next step.

        Steps:
          1. Show phase='Completed' on the dashboard with the
             explicit "Windows is blocking automatic reboot ...
             handing off to OOBE" message. (We reuse the existing
             'Completed' phase rather than adding 'HandingOff'
             because Show-UpdateProgress.ps1 is byte-locked for
             this release; the dashboard already renders Completed
             with appropriate finalization styling.)
          2. Relabel all Pending-restart rows to Completed with
             "Installed; will settle on first user logon" detail.
          3. Drop the completion markers so a future relaunch
             treats us as done.
          4. Run sysprep with -NoCascadeFallback so a sysprep
             failure does NOT re-enter Invoke-Reboot (which is
             the dead end we're escaping from).
          5. If sysprep launched -> exit 0 (cascade-exhausted
             but recoverable; sysprep will handle reboot).
          6. If sysprep failed -> write a STUCK-DIAG dump, set
             phase='Completed' with the FINAL manual-reboot
             message, and exit 0 (NOT 1 - exit 1 would trigger
             the watchdog ONSTART task and we'd be back to square
             one). The dashboard stays up so the operator can
             read the message.

        Never returns to the caller (always exits the process).
    #>
    param([string]$Reason = 'cascade exhausted; handing off to OOBE')

    Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff invoked: {0}" -f $Reason) 'ERROR'
    try { Clear-FbWuLoopPaintingMarker } catch {}
    $script:FbWuLoopPaintingActive = $false
    try {
        $handoffSkipState = $null
        try { $handoffSkipState = Load-State } catch {}
        $handoffPendingSysprep = $false
        try { $handoffPendingSysprep = [bool]$handoffSkipState.PendingSysprep } catch {}
        $handoffRebootPending = $false
        try { $handoffRebootPending = [bool](Test-FbWuRebootRequired) } catch {}
        if ($handoffRebootPending -and -not $handoffPendingSysprep) {
            Write-FbLog '5.4.19: OOBE handoff refused; OS pending reboot and not at seal. Invoking reboot ladder instead of leaving CloudExperienceHost on Just a moment.' 'ERROR'
            if (Test-FbWuSkipCascadeUnsafe -State $handoffSkipState) {
                Clear-FbStaleInstallsCompletedLatch -State $handoffSkipState -Reason 'OOBE handoff pending reboot; cascade not exhausted'
            }
            Invoke-FbRebootWithFreshStateCheck -Reason '5.4.19: pending reboot before OOBE/CXH Just a moment'
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("5.4.19: OOBE handoff pending-reboot probe threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    try {
        $handoffReal = 0
        try { $handoffReal = [int]$script:RealInstallsThisBoot } catch { $handoffReal = 0 }
        if ($handoffReal -le 0) {
            try { $handoffDisk = Load-State; $handoffReal = [int]$handoffDisk.RealInstallsTotal } catch {}
        }
        if ($handoffReal -gt 0) {
            Set-FbInstallsCompleted -Reason ("OOBE handoff cascade exhausted: {0}" -f $Reason) -RealInstallEvidence $true -BumpRealInstallsTotal $false -LatchOobeHandoff $true
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff: InstallsCompleted latch threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # ==================================================================
    # 2026.05.13.2213h-real-convergence-and-ti-serialization Bug Q
    # STAGE 1: zero-or-givenup convergence gate (fresh online scan)
    # ==================================================================
    # Pre-2213h the OOBE handoff trusted the InstallsCompleted disk
    # latch and the prior pass's convergence-confirmed flag to gate
    # the sysprep transition. Field evidence from F:\fb-dump-final-
    # 1347 proved this gate was permissive:
    #   * 11:54:54 main loop: "End-of-cycle check: 38 remaining
    #     update(s)" with workQueue empty (Bug N had swallowed the
    #     failure rows)
    #   * 12:01:13 Tier 3 reboot wedged, pipeline killed
    #   * 12:01:50 next boot: startup gate observed InstallsCompleted
    #     latched + RealInstallsTotal>0 (2 Critical defender updates
    #     had succeeded) and routed straight to OOBE handoff
    #   * User powered on the device, did Windows Update -> Check for
    #     updates -> 38 updates still on offer.
    # The 2213h gate is the LAST line of defence: a fresh online scan
    # NOW, after every other gate has voted "yes converged", and
    # compare against the persisted GivenUpSet. If the residual
    # (ScanResultCount - GivenUpSet.Count) > 0 we have NOT converged
    # and must reboot for another full update pass. This makes false
    # convergence physically unrepresentable (subject only to the
    # existing escape valve / FutileBootCount / SysprepRetryCount
    # budgets which the user explicitly approved as the failsafe).
    #
    # Note: this gate only RUNS when called from a path that is NOT
    # itself the escape-valve handoff. The escape valve is the
    # operator-acknowledged "we tried everything, ship anyway"
    # signal. Probe state.EscapeValveTripped to detect it.

    $skipConvergenceGate = $false
    $stateAtHandoffEntry = $null
    try { $stateAtHandoffEntry = Load-State } catch {}
    if ($stateAtHandoffEntry) {
        try {
            if ([bool]$stateAtHandoffEntry.EscapeValveTripped) {
                $skipConvergenceGate = $true
                Write-FbLog "OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate): EscapeValveTripped=true on disk; bypassing zero-or-givenup convergence gate per operator-acknowledged escape." 'WARN'
            }
        } catch {}
    }
    # Also bypass if NEVER-CASCADE.flag exists (operator override).
    try {
        if (Test-Path $NeverCascadeFlag) {
            $skipConvergenceGate = $true
            Write-FbLog "OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate): NEVER-CASCADE.flag present; bypassing zero-or-givenup convergence gate (operator override)." 'WARN'
        }
    } catch {}
    # [DEBUG] 2249: developer hotkey bypass — Ctrl+Shift+End.
    # $script:FbDebugBypassActive is set by Test-FbDebugForceOobeHandoff at the main-loop
    # call site (flag file is removed there; this variable carries the intent to the gate).
    # Also probe the flag file directly for paths that reach Invoke-FbOobeHandoff without
    # going through the main-loop check (e.g., startup InstallsCompleted gate).
    if (-not $skipConvergenceGate) {
        $debugFlagForGate = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase '.debug-force-oobe-handoff'
        $debugGateActive  = $script:FbDebugBypassActive
        if (-not $debugGateActive) {
            try { $debugGateActive = (Test-Path $debugFlagForGate) } catch {}
        }
        if ($debugGateActive) {
            try { Remove-Item $debugFlagForGate -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } catch {}
            $script:FbDebugBypassActive = $true
            $skipConvergenceGate = $true
            Write-FbLog 'OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate): [DEBUG] 2249: debug bypass active ($script:FbDebugBypassActive or flag file); skipping zero-or-givenup convergence gate.' 'WARN'
        }
    }

    if (-not $skipConvergenceGate) {
        Write-FbLog "OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate): running fresh online scan to verify zero-or-givenup convergence before sysprep." 'WARN'
        $stage1Scan = @()
        $stage1ScanOk = $false
        try {
            # Pre-scan: services must be up. Same pattern the main
            # loop uses before its passes.
            try { Reset-WindowsUpdateServices } catch {}
            try { Invoke-UsoScanKick } catch {}
            $stage1Scan = @(Invoke-FbWuScan -TimeoutSeconds $RescanTimeoutSec)
            $stage1ScanOk = $true
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate): Invoke-FbWuScan threw: {0}; treating scan as INCONCLUSIVE. Refusing handoff and rebooting for another pass." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
            $stage1ScanOk = $false
        }

        if ($stage1ScanOk) {
            # Filter out updates already in the GivenUpSet (terminally
            # classified, force-hidden, or escape-hatched). We match
            # on BOTH the UpdateId and the Get-FbRetryKey result
            # because GivenUpSet entries from prior builds and from
            # the 2213h+ code both can appear in either form.
            $givenUpIds = New-Object 'System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]' ([System.StringComparer]::OrdinalIgnoreCase)
            $forceHiddenIds = New-Object 'System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]' ([System.StringComparer]::OrdinalIgnoreCase)
            try {
                if ($stateAtHandoffEntry) {
                    if ($stateAtHandoffEntry.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'GivenUpUpdateIds' -and $stateAtHandoffEntry.GivenUpUpdateIds) {
                        foreach ($g in @($stateAtHandoffEntry.GivenUpUpdateIds)) {
                            if ($g) { [void]$givenUpIds.Add([string]$g) }
                        }
                    }
                    if ($stateAtHandoffEntry.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'ForceHiddenKeys' -and $stateAtHandoffEntry.ForceHiddenKeys) {
                        foreach ($f in @($stateAtHandoffEntry.ForceHiddenKeys)) {
                            if ($f) { [void]$forceHiddenIds.Add([string]$f) }
                        }
                    }
                }
            } catch {}

            # ==========================================================
            # 2026.05.19.2213m Bug FF reach: evaluate SoftGivenUp HERE,
            # before the residual filter. Dump 651 proved Mechanism 1
            # never fired on boots where InstallsCompleted=true latched
            # on a prior boot (those boots short-circuit to OOBE handoff
            # and bypass the main-loop scan site that originally hosted
            # the SoftGivenUp evaluation). Running the helper here AFTER
            # the gate's own online scan returns and BEFORE the residual
            # filter means any UpdateId whose Mechanism 2 counter was
            # pre-set to threshold on a prior boot can promote into the
            # GivenUpSet in THIS evaluation - the existing
            # $givenUpIds.Contains($uid) check below then excludes it
            # from $residual on the same pass.
            # ==========================================================
            try {
                $stage1SoftEval = Invoke-FbSoftGivenUpEvaluate -ScanResults @($stage1Scan) -GivenUpSet $givenUpIds -CallContext 'oobe-handoff-stage1'
                if ($stage1SoftEval -and ($stage1SoftEval.Promoted -gt 0)) {
                    # The helper already added the promoted UpdateIds (and
                    # their Get-FbRetryKey aliases) to $givenUpIds. We
                    # persist the freshly-grown GivenUpSet to wu-state.json
                    # IMMEDIATELY so a subsequent panic dump (or a future
                    # OOBE handoff that re-loads state) sees the promotion.
                    # The Save-State call deliberately uses sentinel forms
                    # (-1 / $null) for fields we don't have in this scope
                    # so we don't clobber state values updated elsewhere.
                    try {
                        $persistedGiven = @($givenUpIds)
                        $existingForceHidden = @()
                        $existingBootCountByKey = @{}
                        $existingRetryMap = @{}
                        $existingUpdateRetryBudget = @{}
                        try { $existingForceHidden = @($stateAtHandoffEntry.ForceHiddenKeys) } catch {}
                        try { if ($stateAtHandoffEntry.BootCountByKey) { foreach ($k in $stateAtHandoffEntry.BootCountByKey.PSObject.Properties.Name) { $existingBootCountByKey[$k] = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.BootCountByKey.$k } } } catch {}
                        try { if ($stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetry)   { foreach ($k in $stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetry.PSObject.Properties.Name)   { $existingRetryMap[$k] = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetry.$k } } } catch {}
                        try { if ($stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetryBudget) { foreach ($k in $stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetryBudget.PSObject.Properties.Name) { $existingUpdateRetryBudget[$k] = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetryBudget.$k } } } catch {}
                        $persistStartTime = ''
                        try { $persistStartTime = [string]$stateAtHandoffEntry.StartTime } catch {}
                        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($persistStartTime)) { $persistStartTime = (Get-Date -Format s) }
                        $persistFailureCount = 0
                        try { $persistFailureCount = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.FailureCount } catch {}
                        $persistBootCount = 0
                        try { $persistBootCount = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.BootCount } catch {}
                        $persistRebootIters = 0
                        try { $persistRebootIters = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.RebootIterations } catch {}
                        $persistSysprepRetries = 0
                        try { $persistSysprepRetries = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.SysprepRetryCount } catch {}
                        Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass 'oobe-handoff-stage1-softgivenup-promote' `
                            -StartTime $persistStartTime `
                            -FailureCount $persistFailureCount `
                            -UpdateRetry $existingRetryMap `
                            -GivenUpUpdateIds $persistedGiven `
                            -BootCountByKey $existingBootCountByKey `
                            -BootCount $persistBootCount `
                            -ForceHiddenKeys $existingForceHidden `
                            -RebootIterations $persistRebootIters `
                            -SysprepRetryCount $persistSysprepRetries `
                            -UpdateRetryBudget $existingUpdateRetryBudget `
                            -SoftGivenUpCounters $script:FbSoftGivenUpCountersLoop `
                            -SuccessLedgerIds @($script:FbSuccessLedgerSet)
                        Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate): persisted {0} freshly-promoted GivenUp UpdateId(s) to wu-state.json before residual evaluation." -f $stage1SoftEval.Promoted) 'WARN'
                    } catch {
                        Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate): post-promotion Save-State threw: {0}; promotion is in-memory only this boot (next boot will re-evaluate)." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
                    }
                }
            } catch {
                try { Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate): Invoke-FbSoftGivenUpEvaluate threw: {0}; continuing with residual filter using pre-evaluation GivenUpSet." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN' } catch {}
            }

            # ==========================================================
            # 2026.05.19.2213n Bug II ScanStability: literal "same
            # update across consecutive scans = converged" rule.
            # Evaluated HERE, AFTER the Bug FF SoftGivenUp call above
            # (so a sticky-SUCCESS update is already promoted by Bug FF
            # and excluded from the blocking fingerprint), and BEFORE
            # the residual filter below (so a Bug II convergence
            # promotion is visible to the residual filter on the same
            # pass and STAGE 1 hands off naturally via the existing
            # Bug Q gate).
            #
            # Closes Scenario Z: a sticky-FAILED update that is NOT in
            # $script:FbSuccessLedgerSet (so Bug FF cannot reach it)
            # will, after $script:FbScanStabilityThreshold consecutive
            # boot-level gate scans that produce the same blocking-set
            # fingerprint, be bulk-promoted into the GivenUpSet so the
            # residual filter clears and sysprep handoff fires.
            #
            # STAGE 1 ONLY. The main-loop per-pass scan and the inline
            # retry scan deliberately do NOT call this helper: they
            # run multiple times per boot and would over-promote.
            # ==========================================================
            $stateForStability = $null
            try {
                if ($stateAtHandoffEntry -and $stateAtHandoffEntry.ScanStability) {
                    $stateForStability = @{
                        LastFingerprint  = [string]$stateAtHandoffEntry.ScanStability.LastFingerprint
                        ConsecutiveCount = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.ScanStability.ConsecutiveCount
                        LastSeenAt       = [string]$stateAtHandoffEntry.ScanStability.LastSeenAt
                        LastResidualIds  = @($stateAtHandoffEntry.ScanStability.LastResidualIds)
                    }
                }
            } catch {}
            if ($null -eq $stateForStability) {
                $stateForStability = @{
                    LastFingerprint  = ''
                    ConsecutiveCount = 0
                    LastSeenAt       = ''
                    LastResidualIds  = @()
                }
            }
            $stage1StabilityEval = $null
            try {
                $stage1StabilityEval = Invoke-FbScanStabilityEvaluate `
                    -ScanResults @($stage1Scan) `
                    -GivenUpSet $givenUpIds `
                    -ForceHiddenSet $forceHiddenIds `
                    -CallContext 'oobe-handoff-stage1' `
                    -StateBlock $stateForStability

                # Always persist the (possibly-incremented or reset)
                # ScanStability block back to disk. Even on a no-op
                # the LastSeenAt was updated; even on a fingerprint
                # change the LastFingerprint / ConsecutiveCount / 
                # LastResidualIds were updated. Persist via the
                # sentinel-preserve Save-State so we don't clobber
                # any other state field.
                try {
                    $stabilityPersistDisk = Load-State
                    Save-State `
                        -EmptySweepCount   ([int]$stabilityPersistDisk.EmptySweepCount) `
                        -LastPass          ([string]$stabilityPersistDisk.LastPass) `
                        -StartTime         ([string]$stabilityPersistDisk.StartTime) `
                        -FailureCount      ([int]$stabilityPersistDisk.FailureCount) `
                        -UpdateRetry       $stabilityPersistDisk.UpdateRetry `
                        -GivenUpUpdateIds  @($stabilityPersistDisk.GivenUpUpdateIds) `
                        -BootCountByKey    $stabilityPersistDisk.BootCountByKey `
                        -BootCount         ([int]$stabilityPersistDisk.BootCount) `
                        -ForceHiddenKeys   @($stabilityPersistDisk.ForceHiddenKeys) `
                        -RebootIterations  ([int]$stabilityPersistDisk.RebootIterations) `
                        -SysprepRetryCount ([int]$stabilityPersistDisk.SysprepRetryCount) `
                        -ScanStability     $stateForStability
                } catch {
                    Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate): Bug II ScanStability sentinel-preserve Save-State threw: {0}; ScanStability for this boot is in-memory only (next boot will reload prior on-disk value)." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
                }

                # If converged, $givenUpIds has been mutated in-place
                # by the helper. Persist GivenUpUpdateIds (and the
                # full Bug FF context the existing SoftGivenUp-promote
                # block above persists) so a panic dump or a future
                # OOBE handoff that re-loads state sees the
                # convergence-promoted entries.
                if ($stage1StabilityEval -and $stage1StabilityEval.Converged) {
                    try {
                        $persistedGivenII = @($givenUpIds)
                        $existingForceHiddenII = @()
                        $existingBootCountByKeyII = @{}
                        $existingRetryMapII = @{}
                        $existingUpdateRetryBudgetII = @{}
                        try { $existingForceHiddenII = @($stateAtHandoffEntry.ForceHiddenKeys) } catch {}
                        try { if ($stateAtHandoffEntry.BootCountByKey) { foreach ($k in $stateAtHandoffEntry.BootCountByKey.PSObject.Properties.Name) { $existingBootCountByKeyII[$k] = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.BootCountByKey.$k } } } catch {}
                        try { if ($stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetry)   { foreach ($k in $stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetry.PSObject.Properties.Name)   { $existingRetryMapII[$k] = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetry.$k } } } catch {}
                        try { if ($stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetryBudget) { foreach ($k in $stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetryBudget.PSObject.Properties.Name) { $existingUpdateRetryBudgetII[$k] = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetryBudget.$k } } } catch {}
                        $persistStartTimeII = ''
                        try { $persistStartTimeII = [string]$stateAtHandoffEntry.StartTime } catch {}
                        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($persistStartTimeII)) { $persistStartTimeII = (Get-Date -Format s) }
                        $persistFailureCountII = 0
                        try { $persistFailureCountII = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.FailureCount } catch {}
                        $persistBootCountII = 0
                        try { $persistBootCountII = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.BootCount } catch {}
                        $persistRebootItersII = 0
                        try { $persistRebootItersII = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.RebootIterations } catch {}
                        $persistSysprepRetriesII = 0
                        try { $persistSysprepRetriesII = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.SysprepRetryCount } catch {}
                        Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass 'oobe-handoff-stage1-scanstability-promote' `
                            -StartTime $persistStartTimeII `
                            -FailureCount $persistFailureCountII `
                            -UpdateRetry $existingRetryMapII `
                            -GivenUpUpdateIds $persistedGivenII `
                            -BootCountByKey $existingBootCountByKeyII `
                            -BootCount $persistBootCountII `
                            -ForceHiddenKeys $existingForceHiddenII `
                            -RebootIterations $persistRebootItersII `
                            -SysprepRetryCount $persistSysprepRetriesII `
                            -UpdateRetryBudget $existingUpdateRetryBudgetII `
                            -ScanStability $stateForStability
                        Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate): Bug II ScanStability persisted {0} freshly-promoted GivenUp UpdateId(s) to wu-state.json before residual evaluation." -f $stage1StabilityEval.Promoted) 'WARN'
                    } catch {
                        Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate): Bug II post-promotion Save-State threw: {0}; convergence promotion is in-memory only this boot (next boot will re-evaluate)." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
                    }
                }
            } catch {
                try { Write-FbLog ("Bug II ScanStability eval threw at oobe-handoff-stage1 (non-fatal): {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN' } catch {}
            }

            # ==========================================================
            # 2026.06.12.2276-fix-0x80246017-terminal Fix B: OS version
            # guard for feature-upgrade re-offers. If WU still offers
            # "Windows X, version YH2" but the running OS already reports
            # that DisplayVersion, the update cannot be installed (WU will
            # fail with 0x80246017 DM_UNAUTHORIZED every time). Detect
            # this and add the offending UpdateId to GivenUpIds BEFORE
            # the residual filter so it is excluded on this same pass.
            # ==========================================================
            try {
                $fbOsDisplayVersion = ''
                try {
                    $fbOsDisplayVersion = [string](Get-ItemProperty `
                        'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion' `
                        -Name 'DisplayVersion' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).DisplayVersion
                } catch {}
                if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($fbOsDisplayVersion)) {
                    $fbVersionGuardPromoted = 0
                    foreach ($fbScanU in $stage1Scan) {
                        $fbScanUid   = ''; try { $fbScanUid   = [string]$fbScanU.UpdateId } catch {}
                        $fbScanTitle = ''; try { $fbScanTitle = [string]$fbScanU.Title     } catch {}
                        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($fbScanUid) -or [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($fbScanTitle)) { continue }
                        if ($givenUpIds.Contains($fbScanUid)) { continue }
                        if ($fbScanTitle -match 'Windows\s+\d+,\s+version\s+(\w+)') {
                            $fbOfferedVer = $Matches[1]
                            if ($fbOfferedVer -ieq $fbOsDisplayVersion) {
                                Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate) Fix B: Skipping feature-upgrade re-offer '{0}': OS is already at DisplayVersion={1} which satisfies offered version={2}. Adding to GivenUp." -f $fbScanTitle, $fbOsDisplayVersion, $fbOfferedVer) 'WARN'
                                $fbScanKey = ''; try { $fbScanKey = [string](Get-FbRetryKey -Update $fbScanU) } catch {}
                                [void]$givenUpIds.Add($fbScanUid)
                                if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($fbScanKey)) { [void]$givenUpIds.Add($fbScanKey) }
                                $fbVersionGuardPromoted++
                            }
                        }
                    }
                    if ($fbVersionGuardPromoted -gt 0) {
                        try {
                            $fbPersistGiven  = @($givenUpIds)
                            $fbPersistFH     = @(); try { $fbPersistFH  = @($stateAtHandoffEntry.ForceHiddenKeys) } catch {}
                            $fbPersistBCK    = @{}; try { if ($stateAtHandoffEntry.BootCountByKey)  { foreach ($k in $stateAtHandoffEntry.BootCountByKey.PSObject.Properties.Name)  { $fbPersistBCK[$k]  = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.BootCountByKey.$k  } } } catch {}
                            $fbPersistRM     = @{}; try { if ($stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetry)     { foreach ($k in $stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetry.PSObject.Properties.Name)     { $fbPersistRM[$k]   = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetry.$k     } } } catch {}
                            $fbPersistURB    = @{}; try { if ($stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetryBudget) { foreach ($k in $stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetryBudget.PSObject.Properties.Name) { $fbPersistURB[$k] = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetryBudget.$k } } } catch {}
                            $fbPersistST     = ''; try { $fbPersistST = [string]$stateAtHandoffEntry.StartTime } catch {}
                            if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($fbPersistST)) { $fbPersistST = (Get-Date -Format s) }
                            $fbPersistFC     = 0;  try { $fbPersistFC  = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.FailureCount      } catch {}
                            $fbPersistBC     = 0;  try { $fbPersistBC  = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.BootCount         } catch {}
                            $fbPersistRI     = 0;  try { $fbPersistRI  = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.RebootIterations  } catch {}
                            $fbPersistSR     = 0;  try { $fbPersistSR  = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.SysprepRetryCount } catch {}
                            Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass 'oobe-handoff-stage1-featureupgrade-version-guard' `
                                -StartTime        $fbPersistST  `
                                -FailureCount     $fbPersistFC  `
                                -UpdateRetry      $fbPersistRM  `
                                -GivenUpUpdateIds $fbPersistGiven `
                                -BootCountByKey   $fbPersistBCK `
                                -BootCount        $fbPersistBC  `
                                -ForceHiddenKeys  $fbPersistFH  `
                                -RebootIterations $fbPersistRI  `
                                -SysprepRetryCount $fbPersistSR `
                                -UpdateRetryBudget $fbPersistURB `
                                -SoftGivenUpCounters $script:FbSoftGivenUpCountersLoop `
                                -SuccessLedgerIds @($script:FbSuccessLedgerSet)
                            Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate) Fix B: persisted {0} feature-upgrade version-guard GivenUp UpdateId(s) to wu-state.json before residual evaluation." -f $fbVersionGuardPromoted) 'WARN'
                        } catch {
                            Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate) Fix B: post-version-guard Save-State threw: {0}; promotion is in-memory only this boot." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
                        }
                    }
                }
            } catch {
                try { Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate) Fix B: feature-upgrade version guard threw: {0}; continuing without version-guard promotion." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN' } catch {}
            }

            $residual = @()
            foreach ($u in $stage1Scan) {
                $uid = ''
                try { $uid = [string]$u.UpdateId } catch {}
                if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($uid)) { continue }
                $key = ''
                try { $key = [string](Get-FbRetryKey -Update $u) } catch {}
                $isGiven = $givenUpIds.Contains($uid) -or ($key -and $givenUpIds.Contains($key))
                $isHidden = $forceHiddenIds.Contains($uid) -or ($key -and $forceHiddenIds.Contains($key))
                if (-not $isGiven -and -not $isHidden) {
                    $residual += $u
                }
            }

            $scanCount = ($stage1Scan | Measure-Object).Count
            $residualCount = ($residual | Measure-Object).Count
            Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate): scan returned {0} update(s); after GivenUp/ForceHidden filter, residual={1}." -f $scanCount, $residualCount) 'WARN'

            if ($residualCount -gt 0) {
                # Convergence REFUSED. Log every residual title so the
                # operator can confirm from the post-mortem WU log
                # exactly which updates were still on offer.
                $residualSummaries = $residual | ForEach-Object {
                    $rt = ''; try { $rt = [string]$_.Title } catch {}
                    $ru = ''; try { $ru = [string]$_.UpdateId } catch {}
                    ("  - {0} ({1})" -f $rt, $ru)
                }
                Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate): REFUSING handoff. {0} residual update(s) NOT in GivenUpSet/ForceHidden:`n{1}" -f $residualCount, ($residualSummaries -join "`n")) 'ERROR'

                # Check the budgets that allow override. If we have
                # exhausted them, fall through to the regular handoff
                # path (which is the user-approved failsafe).
                $rebootIters = 0
                $sysprepRetries = 0
                try { $rebootIters = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.RebootIterations } catch {}
                try { $sysprepRetries = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.SysprepRetryCount } catch {}
                $futileBoots = 0
                try { $futileBoots = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.FutileBootCount } catch {}
                $bootsSinceLast = 0
                try { $bootsSinceLast = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.BootsSinceLastInstall } catch {}

                $budgetExhausted = ($rebootIters -ge $MaxRebootIterations) -or
                                   ($sysprepRetries -ge $script:MaxSysprepRetries) -or
                                   ($futileBoots -ge $FutileBootThreshold) -or
                                   ($bootsSinceLast -ge 3)

                if ($budgetExhausted) {
                    Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate): residual={0} BUT budgets exhausted (rebootIters={1}/{2}, sysprepRetries={3}/{4}, futileBoots={5}/{6}, bootsSinceLastInstall={7}/3). Honoring failsafe and proceeding with handoff." -f $residualCount, $rebootIters, $MaxRebootIterations, $sysprepRetries, $script:MaxSysprepRetries, $futileBoots, $FutileBootThreshold, $bootsSinceLast) 'ERROR'
                    try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'oobe-handoff-gate' -Message ("STAGE 1 gate: residual={0} but budgets exhausted; failsafe handoff" -f $residualCount) } catch {}
                    # Fall through to normal handoff below.
                } else {
                    # ==========================================================
                    # 2026.05.13.2213j-multipass-and-dedup-and-buildmarker Bug Y
                    # STAGE 1 inline-retry budget BEFORE consuming a reboot.
                    # ==========================================================
                    # Pre-2213j the refuse branch went DIRECTLY to Invoke-FbReboot
                    # whenever the gate found residual updates. Field evidence from
                    # G:\fb-dump-858\State\reboot-tier.log (5/18) showed this
                    # compressed installs across many short cycles:
                    #   * 7:56:27 reboot for "11 residual"
                    #   * 8:03:03 reboot for "16 residual"
                    #   * 8:05:00 reboot for "16 residual"  (DOUBLE: see Bug X)
                    #   * ... 30+ more reboots through 8:57:02 for "1 residual"
                    # The loop installed batches, found new residuals, refused
                    # handoff, rebooted. But the engine already has a per-update
                    # serialized inline retry pass (Invoke-FbWuInlineRetryPass,
                    # added 2213h as Bug P), it just was never plumbed into the
                    # STAGE 1 refusal path. Adding it here lets a single boot
                    # install through up to N=3 additional inline scan+install
                    # passes before consuming a reboot - compressing the 30+
                    # reboot tail into 3-5 reboots when convergence is achievable.
                    #
                    # Termination conditions (whichever fires first):
                    #   (a) Post-retry residual=0  -> fall through to handoff.
                    #   (b) Test-FbWuRebootRequired=true -> reboot now (engine
                    #       cannot install more without one).
                    #   (c) N attempts consumed     -> reboot for next boot.
                    # The 3-pass budget is a balance: in field testing 1-2 passes
                    # typically converge a small bucket; 3 covers the cases where
                    # Microsoft Update re-offers a superseded package mid-pass.
                    # Larger budgets risk masking real wedges, smaller risks
                    # under-converging.
                    $stage1InlineMaxAttempts = 3
                    $stage1InlineAttempt     = 0
                    $stage1InlineConverged   = $false
                    $stage1InlineOsDemands   = $false
                    $stage1RefusedReason     = ("STAGE 1 OOBE handoff REFUSED: zero-or-givenup gate found {0} residual update(s) still on offer (scan={1}, given-up={2}, force-hidden={3})." -f $residualCount, $scanCount, $givenUpIds.Count, $forceHiddenIds.Count)

                    # Reconstruct the parameter set Invoke-FbWuInlineRetryPass
                    # needs from the disk state. We are still inside the OOBE
                    # handoff function, which is called from many call sites
                    # (escape-valve, main-loop top guard, end-of-pass, ...),
                    # not all of which have RetryMap / GivenUpSet / etc. in
                    # local scope. Pulling from $stateAtHandoffEntry (already
                    # loaded above) is the safe path.
                    $inlineRetryRetryMap          = @{}
                    $inlineRetryGivenUpSet        = New-Object 'System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]' ([System.StringComparer]::OrdinalIgnoreCase)
                    $inlineRetryBootCountByKey   = @{}
                    $inlineRetryForceHiddenSet   = New-Object 'System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]' ([System.StringComparer]::OrdinalIgnoreCase)
                    $inlineRetryBootCount        = 0
                    $inlineRetryFailureCount     = 0
                    $inlineRetryUpdateBudget     = @{}
                    $inlineRetryStartTime        = (Get-Date -Format s)
                    try {
                        if ($stateAtHandoffEntry) {
                            try { if ($stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetry) { foreach ($k in $stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetry.PSObject.Properties.Name) { $inlineRetryRetryMap[$k] = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetry.$k } } } catch {}
                            try { foreach ($g in @($stateAtHandoffEntry.GivenUpUpdateIds)) { if ($g) { [void]$inlineRetryGivenUpSet.Add([string]$g) } } } catch {}
                            try { if ($stateAtHandoffEntry.BootCountByKey) { foreach ($k in $stateAtHandoffEntry.BootCountByKey.PSObject.Properties.Name) { $inlineRetryBootCountByKey[$k] = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.BootCountByKey.$k } } } catch {}
                            try { foreach ($f in @($stateAtHandoffEntry.ForceHiddenKeys)) { if ($f) { [void]$inlineRetryForceHiddenSet.Add([string]$f) } } } catch {}
                            try { $inlineRetryBootCount = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.BootCount } catch {}
                            try { $inlineRetryFailureCount = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.FailureCount } catch {}
                            try { if ($stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetryBudget) { foreach ($k in $stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetryBudget.PSObject.Properties.Name) { $inlineRetryUpdateBudget[$k] = [int]$stateAtHandoffEntry.UpdateRetryBudget.$k } } } catch {}
                            try { if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace([string]$stateAtHandoffEntry.StartTime)) { $inlineRetryStartTime = [string]$stateAtHandoffEntry.StartTime } } catch {}
                        }
                    } catch {
                        Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 inline-retry): state reconstruction threw: {0}; continuing with empty/default budgets (engine will rebuild as it goes)." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
                    }

                    # 2026.05.19.2213m Bug FF reach: merge the in-memory $givenUpIds
                    # (which includes anything just promoted by the STAGE 1 helper
                    # call ~L8237) into $inlineRetryGivenUpSet so the inline retry
                    # pass does not waste a budget attempt re-installing an update
                    # we already decided to give up on this boot. The in-memory
                    # $givenUpIds is a strict superset of $inlineRetryGivenUpSet
                    # (both are seeded from the same on-disk GivenUpUpdateIds list;
                    # $givenUpIds may have additional entries from the
                    # Invoke-FbSoftGivenUpEvaluate call above).
                    try {
                        foreach ($g in @($givenUpIds)) {
                            if ($g) { [void]$inlineRetryGivenUpSet.Add([string]$g) }
                        }
                    } catch {}

                    Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 inline-retry): {0} residual update(s) detected; attempting up to {1} inline scan+install pass(es) BEFORE consuming a reboot. Reason='{2}'." -f $residualCount, $stage1InlineMaxAttempts, $stage1RefusedReason) 'WARN'
                    try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Verifying' -Message ("Verifying convergence: {0} update(s) still on offer; running inline retry pass before reboot..." -f $residualCount) } catch {}
                    try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'oobe-handoff-inline-retry' -Message ("STAGE 1 inline-retry: residual={0}; budget={1}" -f $residualCount, $stage1InlineMaxAttempts) } catch {}

                    # 2026.05.13.2213k-real-single-instance-and-real-multipass-and-builder-pathfix
                    # Bug Y-2: classify the loop exit reason explicitly so a future
                    # dump can distinguish "loop ran 3 times" from "loop bailed on
                    # iteration 1 due to a missing function / exception / OS-demands
                    # reboot." In 2213j every dump line said "(1/3 attempts, ...)"
                    # because the catch{} on iteration 1 broke immediately AND the
                    # fall-through still used the generic "budget exhausted" wording.
                    # 2213k tags each exit with a precise classification.
                    $stage1ExitClass    = 'unknown'   # set to one of:
                                                       #   'converged'       - postResidual=0
                                                       #   'os-demands'      - Test-FbWuRebootRequired flipped true
                                                       #   'inline-threw'    - Invoke-FbWuInlineRetryPass raised an exception
                                                       #   'post-scan-threw' - the verification scan after retry raised an exception
                                                       #   'budget-real'     - loop completed all N attempts without convergence
                    $stage1InlineThrew  = $null
                    $stage1PostScanThrew = $null
                    while ($stage1InlineAttempt -lt $stage1InlineMaxAttempts) {
                        $stage1InlineAttempt++
                        Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 inline-retry): attempt {0}/{1} starting." -f $stage1InlineAttempt, $stage1InlineMaxAttempts) 'WARN'
                        try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Verifying' -Message ("Verifying convergence (attempt {0}/{1}): installing residual updates inline..." -f $stage1InlineAttempt, $stage1InlineMaxAttempts) } catch {}

                        $inlineRes = $null
                        try {
                            $inlineRes = Invoke-FbWuInlineRetryPass `
                                -Reason ("STAGE 1 OOBE handoff convergence-gate refusal: {0} residual; attempt {1}/{2}" -f $residualCount, $stage1InlineAttempt, $stage1InlineMaxAttempts) `
                                -StartTime $inlineRetryStartTime `
                                -RetryMap $inlineRetryRetryMap `
                                -GivenUpSet $inlineRetryGivenUpSet `
                                -BootCountByKey $inlineRetryBootCountByKey `
                                -ForceHiddenSet $inlineRetryForceHiddenSet `
                                -BootCount $inlineRetryBootCount `
                                -RebootIterations $rebootIters `
                                -SysprepRetryCount $sysprepRetries `
                                -FailureCount $inlineRetryFailureCount `
                                -UpdateRetryBudget $inlineRetryUpdateBudget `
                                -AttemptIndex $stage1InlineAttempt
                        } catch {
                            $stage1ExitClass   = 'inline-threw'
                            $stage1InlineThrew = $_.Exception.Message
                            Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 inline-retry attempt {0}/{1}): Invoke-FbWuInlineRetryPass threw: {2}; breaking out to reboot. ExitClass=inline-threw." -f $stage1InlineAttempt, $stage1InlineMaxAttempts, $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
                            break
                        }

                        if ($inlineRes -and $inlineRes.OsDemandsReboot) {
                            $stage1InlineOsDemands = $true
                            $stage1ExitClass       = 'os-demands'
                            Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 inline-retry attempt {0}/{1}): OS now demands a reboot (Test-FbWuRebootRequired=true). Halting inline retry; proceeding to reboot. ExitClass=os-demands." -f $stage1InlineAttempt, $stage1InlineMaxAttempts) 'WARN'
                            break
                        }

                        # Re-run the convergence gate (small scan, same
                        # filter) to see if we have converged. Reuse the
                        # GivenUp / ForceHidden sets from $stateAtHandoffEntry
                        # since the inline retry pass updates them in-place
                        # via the RetryMap / GivenUpSet by-ref parameters.
                        $postScan = @()
                        try {
                            $postScan = @(Invoke-FbWuScan -TimeoutSeconds $RescanTimeoutSec)
                        } catch {
                            $stage1ExitClass     = 'post-scan-threw'
                            $stage1PostScanThrew = $_.Exception.Message
                            Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 inline-retry attempt {0}/{1}): post-retry scan threw: {2}; treating as INCONCLUSIVE and breaking to reboot. ExitClass=post-scan-threw." -f $stage1InlineAttempt, $stage1InlineMaxAttempts, $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
                            break
                        }
                        $postResidual = @()
                        foreach ($u in $postScan) {
                            $uid2 = ''
                            try { $uid2 = [string]$u.UpdateId } catch {}
                            if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($uid2)) { continue }
                            $key2 = ''
                            try { $key2 = [string](Get-FbRetryKey -Update $u) } catch {}
                            $isGiven2 = $inlineRetryGivenUpSet.Contains($uid2) -or ($key2 -and $inlineRetryGivenUpSet.Contains($key2))
                            $isHidden2 = $inlineRetryForceHiddenSet.Contains($uid2) -or ($key2 -and $inlineRetryForceHiddenSet.Contains($key2))
                            if (-not $isGiven2 -and -not $isHidden2) {
                                $postResidual += $u
                            }
                        }
                        $postResidualCount = ($postResidual | Measure-Object).Count
                        Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 inline-retry attempt {0}/{1}): attempted={2} installed={3} stillFailed={4} postResidual={5}." -f $stage1InlineAttempt, $stage1InlineMaxAttempts, ($(if ($inlineRes) { [bool]$inlineRes.Attempted } else { $false })), ($(if ($inlineRes) { [int]$inlineRes.Installed } else { 0 })), ($(if ($inlineRes) { [int]$inlineRes.StillFailed } else { 0 })), $postResidualCount) 'WARN'

                        if ($postResidualCount -eq 0) {
                            $stage1InlineConverged = $true
                            $stage1ExitClass       = 'converged'
                            $residualCount = 0
                            Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 inline-retry): CONVERGED on attempt {0}/{1}. Proceeding with handoff. ExitClass=converged." -f $stage1InlineAttempt, $stage1InlineMaxAttempts) 'WARN'
                            try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'oobe-handoff-inline-retry' -Message ("STAGE 1 inline-retry CONVERGED on attempt {0}/{1}; proceeding with handoff" -f $stage1InlineAttempt, $stage1InlineMaxAttempts) } catch {}
                            break
                        }

                        # Keep the residualCount up to date for the reboot
                        # reason if we ultimately fall through to reboot.
                        $residualCount = $postResidualCount
                    }

                    # If we exited the while via the loop condition (i.e. the
                    # body ran all N times without break), the budget is
                    # genuinely exhausted.
                    if ($stage1ExitClass -eq 'unknown' -and -not $stage1InlineConverged -and -not $stage1InlineOsDemands -and $stage1InlineAttempt -ge $stage1InlineMaxAttempts) {
                        $stage1ExitClass = 'budget-real'
                    }

                    if ($stage1InlineConverged) {
                        # Fall through to the normal handoff path below
                        # (the if/else closes; the rest of Invoke-FbOobe-
                        # Handoff runs as if the gate had passed).
                    } else {
                        # Build a precise exhaustion reason that names the
                        # actual exit class instead of misleadingly calling
                        # every break "budget exhausted". A 2213k dump
                        # showing "ExitClass=inline-threw" tells the
                        # next operator EXACTLY what went wrong, without
                        # having to grep WU-*.log for "threw:".
                        $exhaustionReason = switch ($stage1ExitClass) {
                            'os-demands'      { ('OS demands reboot mid-inline-retry (attempt {0}/{1})' -f $stage1InlineAttempt, $stage1InlineMaxAttempts) }
                            'inline-threw'    { ('inline-retry threw on attempt {0}/{1}: {2}' -f $stage1InlineAttempt, $stage1InlineMaxAttempts, $stage1InlineThrew) }
                            'post-scan-threw' { ('post-retry scan threw on attempt {0}/{1}: {2}' -f $stage1InlineAttempt, $stage1InlineMaxAttempts, $stage1PostScanThrew) }
                            'budget-real'     { ('inline-retry budget exhausted ({0}/{1} attempts, postResidual={2})' -f $stage1InlineAttempt, $stage1InlineMaxAttempts, $residualCount) }
                            default           { ('inline-retry inconclusive ({0}/{1} attempts, postResidual={2}, ExitClass={3})' -f $stage1InlineAttempt, $stage1InlineMaxAttempts, $residualCount, $stage1ExitClass) }
                        }
                        Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 inline-retry): {0}; refusing handoff and consuming one reboot. ExitClass={1}." -f $exhaustionReason, $stage1ExitClass) 'ERROR'

                        # Refuse handoff; reboot for another pass. We must
                        # NOT set PendingSysprep here (the convergence gate
                        # in Invoke-FbWuEndOfPassDecision is the single
                        # writer); we just route through Invoke-FbReboot
                        # with the refusal reason. The next boot will
                        # re-enter the main loop, scan, and run buckets.
                        try {
                            $stateForRefusal = Load-State
                            # Sentinel-preserve everything; only clear
                            # PendingSysprep so the next boot does not
                            # short-circuit into a STAGE 2 retry.
                            Save-State `
                                -EmptySweepCount   ([int]$stateForRefusal.EmptySweepCount) `
                                -LastPass          'oobe-handoff-refused-residual' `
                                -StartTime         ([string]$stateForRefusal.StartTime) `
                                -FailureCount      ([int]$stateForRefusal.FailureCount) `
                                -UpdateRetry       $stateForRefusal.UpdateRetry `
                                -GivenUpUpdateIds  @($stateForRefusal.GivenUpUpdateIds) `
                                -BootCountByKey    $stateForRefusal.BootCountByKey `
                                -BootCount         ([int]$stateForRefusal.BootCount) `
                                -ForceHiddenKeys   @($stateForRefusal.ForceHiddenKeys) `
                                -RebootIterations  ([int]$stateForRefusal.RebootIterations) `
                                -SysprepRetryCount ([int]$stateForRefusal.SysprepRetryCount) `
                                -PendingSysprep    $false `
                                -ConvergenceConfirmed $false `
                                -BootsSinceLastInstall ([int]$stateForRefusal.BootsSinceLastInstall)
                        } catch {
                            Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate): Save-State for refusal sentinel-preserve threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
                        }
                        try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'oobe-handoff-gate' -Message ("STAGE 1 gate REFUSED handoff: {0} residual update(s); rebooting for another update pass" -f $residualCount) } catch {}
                        try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message ("Found {0} update(s) remaining; rebooting to continue installing..." -f $residualCount) } catch {}
                        try { Set-RunOnceAutostart -Reason 'STAGE 1 OOBE handoff refused; re-arming for next-boot update pass' } catch {}
                        try { Test-FbAndRepairTriggers -Context 'stage1-oobe-handoff-refused' | Out-Null } catch {}
                        Invoke-FbReboot -Reason ("STAGE 1 OOBE handoff REFUSED ({0}): zero-or-givenup gate found {1} residual update(s) still on offer (scan={2}, given-up={3}, force-hidden={4}). Rebooting for another update pass." -f $exhaustionReason, $residualCount, $scanCount, $givenUpIds.Count, $forceHiddenIds.Count)
                        # Invoke-FbReboot never returns. Defensive guard.
                        try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
                        exit 1
                    }
                }
            } else {
                Write-FbLog "OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 gate): zero-or-givenup convergence verified (residual=0). Proceeding with handoff." 'WARN'
                try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'oobe-handoff-gate' -Message 'STAGE 1 gate PASSED: zero-or-givenup convergence verified' } catch {}
            }
        }
    }

    # 2026.05.13.2213c-onlogon-primary-and-defender-disable:
    # Restore Defender real-time monitoring BEFORE any handoff work
    # so the device ships to the end user with Defender fully enabled
    # regardless of which downstream path Invoke-FbOobeHandoff takes
    # (sysprep success, sysprep failure into another reboot retry,
    # final manual-reboot message). Idempotent - safe to call even
    # if Defender was never disabled on this run.
    Set-FbDefenderRealtime -Enabled $true -Reason 'OOBE-handoff'

    # Step 1: surface the explicit handoff message on the dashboard.
    # Phase='Completed' is reused so the existing splash code path
    # renders the "Finalizing updates" header (verified by reading
    # Show-UpdateProgress.ps1 - the 'Completed' branch is the right
    # visual treatment for a successful end state).
    $handoffMsg = 'Windows is blocking automatic reboot. Updates are installed (39 pending settle) - handing off to OOBE. The remaining updates will apply on first user logon. This is normal.'
    Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Completed' -Message $handoffMsg
    Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Completed'

    # Step 2: relabel pending-restart rows so the user can see the
    # device IS done updating. Count is logged for the diag dump.
    $relabeled = Set-FbAllUpdatesDeferred
    Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff: relabeled {0} pending-restart row(s) to Completed with deferred-settle note." -f $relabeled) 'WARN'

    # Step 3: also surface the explicit activity bar text per the
    # 2026-05-11 1145 spec (the user's request: the device IS done
    # updating, just couldn't reboot - make that unmissable).
    $activityMsg = 'Updates installed - Windows blocking auto-reboot - sysprep handoff in progress (deferred reboot will complete on user logon)'
    Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Completed' -Message $activityMsg
    Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Completed'

    # Step 4: respect the sysprep retry cap. Invoke-SysprepToOobe
    # increments and persists SysprepRetryCount internally; here
    # we peek BEFORE calling it so a cap-hit short-circuits to the
    # final-message path without burning another attempt.
    $sysprepAtCap = $false
    try {
        $stateNow = Load-State
        if ([int]$stateNow.SysprepRetryCount -ge $script:MaxSysprepRetries) {
            $sysprepAtCap = $true
            Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff: SysprepRetryCount={0} >= cap={1}; skipping sysprep launch and proceeding to final manual-reboot message." -f ([int]$stateNow.SysprepRetryCount), $script:MaxSysprepRetries) 'ERROR'
        }
    } catch {}

    # Drop the persistent completion marker so a future relaunch
    # (if the user power-cycles after seeing the message) sees a
    # completed cycle and doesn't try to rerun the loop.
    try { Set-Content -Path $CompleteMarker -Value ("Completed at {0} (cascade-exhausted handoff)" -f (Get-Date -Format s)) -Encoding ASCII -Force } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff: failed to drop completion marker: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    try { New-Item -ItemType File -Path $WaitMarker -Force | Out-Null } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff: failed to drop wait marker: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
    try { Start-FbHandoffSplashIndicator -Phase 'preparing-handoff' } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff: handoff splash start threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # 2026.05.13.1900-two-stage-sysprep-handoff Fix 5: trigger
    # cleanup is DEFERRED. The build 1700 cleanup that ran HERE
    # (deleting all four trigger layers + the FirstBase scheduled
    # tasks before invoking Sysprep.exe) is now performed only
    # AFTER Sysprep.exe returns exit code 0 - inside
    # Invoke-SysprepToOobe's success path. Rationale: build 1700's
    # field evidence showed sysprep refusing with 0x139f, the loop
    # logging "Sysprep launched successfully" (because it ran
    # fire-and-forget), the pre-sysprep cleanup wiping every
    # trigger layer, and the device being stranded on Audit Mode
    # with no live triggers. Deferring the cleanup means triggers
    # remain armed on any sysprep failure path, so the loop can
    # relaunch on the next boot and retry STAGE 2 from a freshly-
    # rebooted state.
    #
    # 2026.05.13.1900-two-stage-sysprep-handoff Fix 4 (budget
    # doubled in 2200, comment scrubbed in 2210): pre-sysprep
    # CBS-settle wait. Even after a successful STAGE 1 reboot, CBS
    # sometimes takes 30-60 seconds to fully clear its pending-
    # reboot signal after Audit Mode boots. Wait for
    # Test-FbWuRebootRequired to return false on 3 consecutive
    # 10-second probes (max 10-minute wall-clock budget; 600s
    # default) before invoking sysprep. If it never settles, route
    # to plain shutdown for another reboot attempt.
    if (-not $sysprepAtCap) {
        Write-FbLog 'OOBE handoff (STAGE 2): starting pre-sysprep CBS-settle wait before invoking Sysprep.exe.' 'WARN'
        $cbsSettled = $false
        try {
            # 2026.05.13.2213-tier-watchdog-and-diag-parsefix Fix 6:
            # opt into adaptive budget (180s/360s/600s by attempt).
            # See Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared .NOTES for rationale.
            $cbsSettled = Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared -Context 'STAGE 2 Invoke-FbOobeHandoff' -AdaptiveBudget $true
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 2): Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared threw: {0}; treating as 'did not settle' and routing to plain shutdown for another reboot attempt." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
            $cbsSettled = $false
        }

        if (-not $cbsSettled) {
            Write-FbLog 'OOBE handoff (STAGE 2): pre-sysprep CBS-settle wait did NOT clear within budget. NOT invoking sysprep (would refuse with 0x139f). Sentinel-preserving PendingSysprep on disk (already true from the convergence gate) and routing to plain shutdown so the next boot can retry STAGE 2 from a freshly-rebooted state.' 'ERROR'
            # 2026.05.13.2210-fullscope-multipass-progress-fixes
            # Critical Fix 2: sentinel-preserve only. PendingSysprep
            # is the convergence gate's single writer; we must not
            # re-assert it here.
            try {
                $stage2RetryDisk = Load-State
                Save-State `
                    -EmptySweepCount   ([int]$stage2RetryDisk.EmptySweepCount) `
                    -LastPass          ([string]$stage2RetryDisk.LastPass) `
                    -StartTime         ([string]$stage2RetryDisk.StartTime) `
                    -FailureCount      ([int]$stage2RetryDisk.FailureCount) `
                    -UpdateRetry       $stage2RetryDisk.UpdateRetry `
                    -GivenUpUpdateIds  @($stage2RetryDisk.GivenUpUpdateIds) `
                    -BootCountByKey    $stage2RetryDisk.BootCountByKey `
                    -BootCount         ([int]$stage2RetryDisk.BootCount) `
                    -ForceHiddenKeys   @($stage2RetryDisk.ForceHiddenKeys) `
                    -RebootIterations  ([int]$stage2RetryDisk.RebootIterations) `
                    -SysprepRetryCount ([int]$stage2RetryDisk.SysprepRetryCount)
                Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 2 retry): PendingSysprep preserved on disk via sentinel (current value={0})." -f ([bool]$stage2RetryDisk.PendingSysprep)) 'INFO'
            } catch {
                Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 2 retry): sentinel-preserve Save-State threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
            }
            # Re-arm triggers BEFORE shutdown; the relaunch needs
            # to fire on the next boot. Invoke-FbReboot's STAGE 1
            # path will handle the actual patient shutdown. The
            # caller of Invoke-FbOobeHandoff (e.g. main-loop) does
            # NOT loop back to this function; we MUST route to
            # the reboot path explicitly here.
            try { Set-RunOnceAutostart -Reason 'STAGE 2 CBS settle did not clear; re-arming for next-boot STAGE 2 retry' } catch {}
            try { Test-FbAndRepairTriggers -Context 'stage2-cbs-did-not-settle' | Out-Null } catch {}
            # Hand off to Invoke-FbReboot for the patient shutdown
            # sequence. PendingSysprep is now true on disk; on the
            # next boot the top-of-function fast path in
            # Invoke-FbReboot WILL re-enter STAGE 2.
            try { Start-FbHandoffSplashIndicator -Phase 'restart-for-checks' } catch {}
            try {
                Invoke-FbReboot -Reason 'STAGE 2 pre-sysprep CBS-settle wait exhausted budget; rebooting for another retry'
            } catch {
                Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 2 retry): Invoke-FbReboot threw: {0}; exiting clean so RunOnce relaunches us on next manual restart." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
            }
            # Invoke-FbReboot either exits via shutdown.exe taking
            # the box down, or exit 1's after the patient sequence.
            # Either way, we should not fall through to sysprep.
            try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
            exit 1
        }

        Write-FbLog 'OOBE handoff (STAGE 2): CBS settled. Invoking sysprep with -Wait + -PassThru (build 1900 sync launch).' 'WARN'

        try {
            if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $FbProgramDataFirstBase)) {
                New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase -Force | Out-Null
            }
            Set-Content -LiteralPath $FbSplashOobeOverlayMarkerPath -Value ('v1-stage2 {0}' -f (Get-Date -Format 'o')) -Encoding ascii -Force
            Write-FbLog ("STAGE 2: wrote splash OOBE overlay marker {0} (not written at specialize)." -f $FbSplashOobeOverlayMarkerPath) 'INFO'
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("STAGE 2: overlay marker write threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }

        # ==========================================================
        # 2026.05.19.2213n Bug JJ: post-sysprep cleanup of GivenUp +
        # SoftGivenUp + SuccessLedger + ScanStability state BEFORE
        # the sysprep launch that will take the device to OOBE.
        # ==========================================================
        # These four state fields are pre-OOBE orchestration state:
        #   * GivenUpUpdateIds    - Bug Q convergence-gate exclusions
        #   * SoftGivenUpCounters - Bug FF Mechanism 1 counter map
        #   * SuccessLedgerIds    - Bug FF success ledger
        #   * ScanStability       - Bug II convergence fingerprint
        # Post-OOBE they are meaningless: Windows reads its own update
        # state via WUA, not via wu-state.json. Clearing them
        # immediately before sysprep launch keeps wu-state.json clean
        # if anyone inspects it post-OOBE, and prevents leftover
        # pre-OOBE classifications from being misinterpreted by a
        # future re-image cycle that somehow lands on the old state
        # file (per-image cleanup in SetupComplete.cmd is the primary
        # guarantor, this is the secondary belt-and-braces).
        # ==========================================================
        $bugJjGivenUpCount = 0
        $bugJjSoftCount    = 0
        $bugJjLedgerCount  = 0
        try {
            $cleanupDisk = Load-State
            try { $bugJjGivenUpCount = @($cleanupDisk.GivenUpUpdateIds).Count } catch {}
            try { $bugJjSoftCount    = @($cleanupDisk.SoftGivenUpCounters.Keys).Count } catch {}
            try { $bugJjLedgerCount  = @($cleanupDisk.SuccessLedgerIds).Count } catch {}
            $bugJjResetStability = @{
                LastFingerprint  = ''
                ConsecutiveCount = 0
                LastSeenAt       = ''
                LastResidualIds  = @()
            }
            Save-State `
                -EmptySweepCount     ([int]$cleanupDisk.EmptySweepCount) `
                -LastPass            'oobe-handoff-stage2-bugjj-cleanup' `
                -StartTime           ([string]$cleanupDisk.StartTime) `
                -FailureCount        ([int]$cleanupDisk.FailureCount) `
                -UpdateRetry         $cleanupDisk.UpdateRetry `
                -GivenUpUpdateIds    @() `
                -BootCountByKey      $cleanupDisk.BootCountByKey `
                -BootCount           ([int]$cleanupDisk.BootCount) `
                -ForceHiddenKeys     @($cleanupDisk.ForceHiddenKeys) `
                -RebootIterations    ([int]$cleanupDisk.RebootIterations) `
                -SysprepRetryCount   ([int]$cleanupDisk.SysprepRetryCount) `
                -SoftGivenUpCounters @{} `
                -SuccessLedgerIds    @() `
                -ScanStability       $bugJjResetStability
            Write-FbLog ("Bug JJ: post-sysprep cleanup of GivenUpUpdateIds ({0} entries), SoftGivenUpCounters ({1} entries), SuccessLedgerIds ({2} entries), ScanStability state before sysprep launch." -f $bugJjGivenUpCount, $bugJjSoftCount, $bugJjLedgerCount) 'INFO'
        } catch {
            try { Write-FbLog ("Bug JJ: post-sysprep cleanup Save-State threw: {0}; proceeding to sysprep with state un-cleared (cosmetic only, non-blocking)." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN' } catch {}
        }

        # ==========================================================
        # 2026.05.20.2213r Bug NN: pre-OOBE scrub gate.
        # ==========================================================
        # User's redefined intent (5/19/2026, 7:06 PM): "When OOBE is
        # visible, that will be the end of the workflow and at that
        # point, scripts should be non-accessible and all registry
        # keys deleted/scrubbed."
        #
        # Splash-over-OOBE: 2213x defers splash-only scrub when
        # FirstBase-Splash-Oobe-Overlay.mode is present (see
        # Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub). Winlogon fence (STEP 3b) still runs.
        #
        # Complementary to Bug JJ above:
        #   * Bug JJ cleans INTERNAL state (wu-state.json fields)
        #   * Bug NN cleans EXTERNAL surfaces (procs/tasks/registry)
        # Both must run; neither replaces the other.
        #
        # Wrapped in try/catch so a scrub failure does NOT block
        # sysprep launch (per spec: log and continue; SCRUB-INCOMPLETE
        # is preferable to NEVER-SYSPREP). The scrub function itself
        # always returns $true and logs its own residuals; this outer
        # try/catch only catches the unlikely case of a fatal throw
        # during the scrub function's own logging setup.

        # ==========================================================
        # 2026.05.20.2213t Bug U fence 1 + 2226 specialize arm-suppress
        # ==========================================================
        # CRITICAL: must be written BEFORE Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub
        # and BEFORE sysprep.exe launches. After sysprep /generalize
        # the post-sysprep specialize pass re-fires SetupComplete.cmd;
        # SetupComplete.cmd Fence 1 skips arming when it sees EITHER
        # (a) legacy .pipeline-completed OR (b) 2226 arm-suppress marker.
        # Pre-2226: .pipeline-completed was written here (operator-done
        # semantics conflated with specialize-skip). 2226 splits:
        #   - $FbArmSuppressMarkerPath now: skip specialize re-arm only.
        #   - .pipeline-completed: written from FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1
        #     after deferred Settings closes on post-/fw boot.
        #
        # Wrapped in try/catch. Failure to write arm-suppress is ERROR;
        # Fence 2 (Winlogon scrub STEP 3b) remains defense in depth.
        try {
            if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $FbProgramDataFirstBase)) {
                New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase -Force | Out-Null
            }
            $armSuppressLine = ('Specialize arm suppressed at {0} (payload {1}, PID {2})' -f (Get-Date -Format 'o'), $script:FirstBasePayloadRevision, $PID)
            Set-Content -LiteralPath $FbArmSuppressMarkerPath -Value $armSuppressLine -Encoding UTF8 -Force
            Write-FbLog ("Bug U fence 1 (2226): wrote arm-suppress marker to {0}; post-sysprep specialize will skip arming. Operator .pipeline-completed is deferred until Settings close (FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1)." -f $FbArmSuppressMarkerPath) 'INFO'
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Bug U fence 1 (2226): FAILED to write arm-suppress marker to {0}: {1}; Fence 1 may not engage on post-sysprep specialize pass; relying on Fence 2 (Winlogon scrub in Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub STEP 3b) as sole defense against Bug U." -f $FbArmSuppressMarkerPath, $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
        }
        try {
            $stageKitForOobeOverlay = $false
            try { $stageKitForOobeOverlay = [bool](Test-Path -LiteralPath $FbSplashOobeOverlayMarkerPath) } catch {}
            if ($stageKitForOobeOverlay) {
                [void](Ensure-FbDeferredOobeSoundProgramDataKit -Reason 'STAGE2-arm-suppress-before-scrub')
            }
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("2237: Ensure-FbDeferredOobeSoundProgramDataKit before scrub threw (non-blocking): {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }

        # ==========================================================
        # 5.0.38 (Option B): STAGE 2 RE-ORDERED.
        # ==========================================================
        # The defaultuser0 OOBE delivery mechanism is RETIRED. Previously this
        # site ran Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub, then armed three defaultuser0
        # delivery surfaces (DeferredOobeSoundOnLogon ONLOGON task [2247],
        # FirstBase\MdmScrub SYSTEM task [2296], Winlogon Userinit injection
        # [2248]) so that FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 would run + scrub
        # under the LIMITED defaultuser0 token in OOBE — which false-succeeded
        # (dump PF4VGG7V-1648) and left the fragile OOBE delivery as the "exit
        # to Windows login" failure surface.
        #
        # New flow (below, after usosvc/WU-AU): open the operator Settings gate
        # in the Administrator audit session, run the INLINE full-privilege
        # MDM/Autopilot scrub (SYSTEM), seal, then Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub
        # (whose STEP 3b is now the ONLY Administrator-disable, at the right
        # moment), then sysprep /oobe /shutdown. Nothing depends on defaultuser0.
        # (Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundOnLogonTaskPreSysprep / Register-FbMdmScrubTaskPreSysprep /
        #  Register-FbDeferredOobeSoundWinlogonUserinit are no longer called.)

        # 2292 Fix 1: Stop usosvc and clear USO orchestrator reboot key before sysprep.
        # UsoClient StartScan (kicked in STAGE 1) can leave a mandatory restart in
        # Orchestrator\reboot\* that Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared never probes.
        # usosvc carries this into OOBE and enforces "Your PC will restart" while
        # the operator has Settings open, breaking the finalize/seal flow.
        Write-FbLog '2292: stopping usosvc and clearing USO orchestrator reboot key before sysprep.' 'INFO'
        try {
            Stop-Service -Name 'usosvc' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            Start-Sleep -Seconds 3
            $fb2292UsoRebootKey = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\WindowsUpdate\Orchestrator\reboot'
            if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $fb2292UsoRebootKey) {
                Remove-Item -LiteralPath $fb2292UsoRebootKey -Recurse -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                Write-FbLog '2292: USO orchestrator reboot key cleared.' 'INFO'
            } else {
                Write-FbLog '2292: USO orchestrator reboot key not present; nothing to clear.' 'INFO'
            }
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("2292: USO stop/clear threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }

        # 2292 Fix 2: Write WU AU policy to suppress OOBE auto-restart.
        # NoAutoRebootWithLoggedOnUsers=1 prevents USO from forcing a restart while
        # defaultuser0 is the active session. NoAutoUpdate=1 prevents a new WU scan
        # from acquiring a fresh mandatory restart during the operator's Settings window.
        # HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Policies survives sysprep /oobe. Removed by finalize (2292).
        Write-FbLog '2292: writing WU AU policy to suppress OOBE mandatory restart (NoAutoRebootWithLoggedOnUsers + NoAutoUpdate).' 'INFO'
        try {
            $fb2292AuKey = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\WindowsUpdate\AU'
            if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $fb2292AuKey)) {
                New-Item -Path $fb2292AuKey -Force -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null
            }
            Set-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $fb2292AuKey -Name 'NoAutoRebootWithLoggedOnUsers' -Value 1 -Type DWord -Force -ErrorAction Stop
            Set-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $fb2292AuKey -Name 'NoAutoUpdate' -Value 1 -Type DWord -Force -ErrorAction Stop
            Write-FbLog '2292: WU AU policy written (NoAutoRebootWithLoggedOnUsers=1, NoAutoUpdate=1).' 'INFO'
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("2292: WU AU policy write threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }

        # ==========================================================
        # 5.0.38 (Option B): Administrator-session operator finalize +
        # INLINE full-privilege scrub + seal, BEFORE sysprep.
        # ==========================================================
        # STEP A — hardware checks (Wi-Fi, camera, sound), then Pass (private YouTube)
        # or Fail (Settings). Bounded; the inline scrub runs regardless.
        try { Copy-FbOobeOperatorProgramDataKit } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Hardware gate: ProgramData kit copy threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
        $fbHwDone = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase '.hardware-gate-complete'
        $fbHwFail = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase '.hardware-gate-fail-restart'
        $script:FbHardwareGateFailed = $false
        if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $fbHwDone) {
            $script:FbHardwareGateFailed = [bool](Test-Path -LiteralPath $fbHwFail)
            Write-FbLog ("Hardware gate already complete (fail={0}); skipping operator step." -f $script:FbHardwareGateFailed) 'INFO'
        } else {
            try {
                Invoke-FbHardwareGateOperatorStep | Out-Null
            } catch {
                Write-FbLog ('Hardware gate operator step threw: {0}; treating as FAIL and proceeding to scrub/seal.' -f $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
                $script:FbHardwareGateFailed = $true
                try { Set-Content -LiteralPath $fbHwFail -Value ('hardware-gate FAIL threw at {0}' -f (Get-Date -Format 'o')) -Encoding ascii -Force } catch {}
            }
        }

        # STEP B — INLINE, verified, full-privilege MDM/Autopilot scrub + temporary-
        # hardening restore (SYSTEM, in-process). Loud ERROR on any HARD-item failure.
        Write-FbLog '5038: running INLINE MDM/Autopilot scrub + hardening restore (SYSTEM, in-process, verified) at operator settings-close.' 'WARN'
        $fb5038Scrub = $null
        try {
            $fb5038Scrub = Invoke-FbInlineMdmScrub
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ('5038: Invoke-FbInlineMdmScrub threw: {0}; privileged blocks may PERSIST — inspect C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\Logs\WU-mdm-scrub.log.' -f $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
        }
        if ($fb5038Scrub -and ($fb5038Scrub.Overall -eq 'PASS')) {
            Write-FbLog '5038: INLINE MDM scrub VERIFIED overall=PASS — MDM keys + DmEnrollmentSvc + Autopilot firewall confirmed removed before seal.' 'WARN'
        } else {
            $fb5038Ov = $(if ($fb5038Scrub) { [string]$fb5038Scrub.Overall } else { 'UNKNOWN' })
            Write-FbLog ('5038: ERROR INLINE MDM scrub overall={0}; one or more PRIVILEGED blocks may PERSIST into the shipped image. Inspect C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\Logs\WU-mdm-scrub.log and .mdm-scrub-result.' -f $fb5038Ov) 'ERROR'
        }

        # STEP C — record pipeline sealed + disarm the (now-unused) defaultuser0
        # sound-delivery surfaces that finalize used to remove in OOBE. Does NOT
        # touch the WU-loop relaunch triggers (!FirstBaseWuLoop RunOnce / RunOnceEx /
        # FirstBaseWuLoopRun Run) — those stay armed until Invoke-SysprepToOobe's
        # success path so a sysprep-validation failure can still relaunch STAGE 2.
        try {
            if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $FbProgramDataFirstBase)) {
                New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
            }
            Set-Content -LiteralPath (Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase '.pipeline-completed') -Value ('Pipeline completed at {0} (5.0.38 Administrator-session finalize, loop PID {1})' -f (Get-Date -Format 'o'), $PID) -Encoding UTF8 -Force
            Set-Content -LiteralPath (Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase '.firstbase-sealed') -Value ('Sealed at {0} (loop PID {1}); device ready for customer OOBE after sysprep /oobe /reboot.' -f (Get-Date -Format 'o'), $PID) -Encoding UTF8 -Force
            Write-FbLog '5038: wrote .pipeline-completed + .firstbase-sealed (Administrator-session finalize).' 'INFO'
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ('5038: WARN seal-marker write failed: {0}; continuing.' -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
        # Defensive: guarantee no FirstBase Winlogon\Userinit ships (the loop no longer
        # injects one; this covers any stray value regardless of source).
        try {
            Set-ItemProperty -LiteralPath 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Winlogon' -Name 'Userinit' -Value 'C:\Windows\system32\userinit.exe,' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        } catch {}
        # Disarm defaultuser0 sound-delivery surfaces (finalize used to do this in OOBE; finalize no longer runs).
        try {
            $fb5038RunRk    = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run'
            $fb5038RunOnceRk = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce'
            Remove-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $fb5038RunRk -Name 'OpenWindowsSoundSettingsDeferredLogon' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            Remove-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $fb5038RunOnceRk -Name 'OpenWindowsSoundSettingsDeferredOnce' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            Remove-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $fb5038RunOnceRk -Name '!FirstBaseWuSplash' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            $fb5038StartupVbs = Join-Path $env:ProgramData 'Microsoft\Windows\Start Menu\Programs\Startup\FirstBaseOobeSoundAtLogon.vbs'
            if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $fb5038StartupVbs) { Remove-Item -LiteralPath $fb5038StartupVbs -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue }
            $fb5038AsKey = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Active Setup\Installed Components\{A7B8C9D0-E1F2-4A5B-9C01-2234DEFERREDSOUND}'
            if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $fb5038AsKey) { Remove-Item -LiteralPath $fb5038AsKey -Recurse -Force -Confirm:$false -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue }
            Write-FbLog '5038: disarmed defaultuser0 sound-delivery surfaces (HKLM Run/RunOnce keys, Startup .vbs, Active Setup component).' 'INFO'
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ('5038: WARN sound-delivery disarm threw: {0}; continuing.' -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }

        # STEP D — process/task/registry/script scrub. Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub STEP 3b
        # performs the autologon-value scrub + `net user Administrator /active:no`; this
        # is now the ONLY Administrator-disable and it runs HERE (after operator finalize
        # + inline scrub + seal), immediately before sysprep, per the 5.0.38 sequencing.
        # STEP 4 deletes FirstBase .ps1/.cmd (incl. the settings-gate helper, already used).
        #
        # 5.4.3 (FBTGJB4-0943): overlay.mode was still present here, so STEP 4
        # PRESERVED splash/operator scripts and left ProgramData copies on disk.
        # Settings already closed in Audit (5.0.38); there is no later OOBE
        # finalize pass. Drop the overlay marker so this scrub is a full delete.
        try {
            if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $FbSplashOobeOverlayMarkerPath) {
                Remove-Item -LiteralPath $FbSplashOobeOverlayMarkerPath -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                Write-FbLog ("5.4.3: removed overlay marker {0} before pre-sysprep scrub (Settings already closed in Audit; do not preserve splash scripts into OOBE)." -f $FbSplashOobeOverlayMarkerPath) 'INFO'
            }
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("5.4.3: overlay marker remove threw: {0}; scrub may still preserve splash scripts." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
        try {
            $null = Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff (STAGE 2): Invoke-FbPreSysprepScrub threw: {0}; proceeding to sysprep anyway (teardown failure must NOT block OOBE handoff per user's 2213r design)." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
        }

        # STEP E — backstop: ensure SetupComplete.cmd is gone before ship so it cannot
        # re-run :SUPPRESS_MDM_ENROLLMENT on the customer's post-sysprep specialize pass
        # (FirstBaseOobeOperatorFinalize.ps1 used to do this at 2288; it no longer runs).
        try {
            $fb5038Sc = 'C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\SetupComplete.cmd'
            if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $fb5038Sc) {
                Remove-Item -LiteralPath $fb5038Sc -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $fb5038Sc) {
                    Write-FbLog '5038: WARN SetupComplete.cmd still present after backstop delete; pre-sysprep STEP 4 deferred batch should clear it.' 'WARN'
                } else {
                    Write-FbLog '5038: backstop deleted SetupComplete.cmd from Setup\Scripts before sysprep.' 'INFO'
                }
            }
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ('5038: SetupComplete.cmd backstop delete threw: {0}' -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }

        # 5.4.3 (FBTGJB4-0943): arm the ship reaper BEFORE sysprep.exe. The copy
        # inside Invoke-SysprepToOobe runs after -Wait returns and loses the race
        # with /reboot (FAIL path) / generalize shutdown (PASS path). Arming now
        # writes the specialize SetupComplete backstop and starts the detached
        # Temp reaper while this process is still alive.
        try {
            Register-FbFinalShipReaper
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("5.4.3: Register-FbFinalShipReaper before sysprep threw (non-blocking): {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }

        Sync-FbWuTranscriptFlush -Reason 'Escape valve: handing off (flush)'

        $sysprepOk = $false
        $sysprepPower = 'Restart'
        try {
            $sysprepOk = Invoke-SysprepToOobe -Reason ('STAGE 2: CBS settled; hardware-gate fail={0}; sysprep /oobe /reboot (customer OOBE).' -f $script:FbHardwareGateFailed) -NoCascadeFallback -PowerAction $sysprepPower
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff: Invoke-SysprepToOobe threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'ERROR'
            $sysprepOk = $false
        }

        if ($sysprepOk) {
            Write-FbLog ('5038: OOBE handoff: sysprep launched (/oobe /reboot). Device will RESTART into customer OOBE (FirstBase will not run as defaultuser0).') 'WARN'
            Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Completed' -Message 'Sysprep launched. Device will restart into Windows OOBE shortly...'
            Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Completed'

            # 2026.05.20.2213s: post-sysprep warning-popup RunOnce
            # overwrite REMOVED per user request (5/19/2026 1:45 PM).
            #
            # Pre-2213s this block read the EscapeValveTripped latch
            # from wu-state.json and, if set, OVERWROTE
            # RunOnce!FirstBaseUpdatesComplete with the
            # "Updates Incomplete - Tech Review Required" warning
            # popup so the warning fired on first OOBE logon instead
            # of the success popup that Invoke-SysprepToOobe had
            # written. With both the success popup (Invoke-Sysprep
            # ToOobe success path) and the warning popup pre-register
            # + scheduled-task rewrite (Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff
            # Step 4) removed in 2213s, there is nothing for this
            # follow-through to overwrite - the RunOnce slot is
            # simply not written by 2213s code.
            #
            # Operator-facing escape-valve evidence still ships via
            # ESCAPE-VALVE-TRIPPED-*.json + wu-state.json latch +
            # dashboard banner + relabeled update rows (all
            # preserved); only the OOBE-time MessageBox is gone.
            #
            # See restoration notes inside Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff
            # Step 4 if policy ever changes.
            try {
                $oobePostDisk = Load-State
                if ([bool]$oobePostDisk.EscapeValveTripped) {
                    Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff: EscapeValveTripped=true on disk (Reason='{0}'); 2213s popup-removal active so no warning-popup RunOnce is registered. Trip evidence remains via ESCAPE-VALVE-TRIPPED-*.json + dashboard latch + relabeled rows." -f ([string]$oobePostDisk.EscapeValveReason)) 'WARN'
                }
            } catch {
                Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff: post-sysprep escape-valve latch probe threw: {0} (popup-removal already in effect; latch read failure is informational only)." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
            }

            # 2026.05.13.1900 Fix 5: trigger cleanup is now performed
            # by Invoke-SysprepToOobe's success path before it returns
            # $true. The defensive cleanup here is a belt-and-braces
            # second pass in case sysprep rearmed anything during its
            # own work, or in case a stale state path missed something.
            try { schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $TaskName /F | Out-Null } catch {}
            try { schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $LogonTaskName /F | Out-Null } catch {}
            try { schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $Boot2TaskName /F | Out-Null } catch {}
            try { schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $AnyLogonTaskName /F | Out-Null } catch {}
            try { schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $SplashTaskName /F | Out-Null } catch {}
            try { schtasks.exe /Delete /TN 'FirstBase\SplashWatcher' /F | Out-Null } catch {}
            try { schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $SplashWatcherBoot2Name /F | Out-Null } catch {}
            try { schtasks.exe /Delete /TN 'FirstBase\UpdateSplashLayer4' /F | Out-Null } catch {}
            try {
                Remove-ItemProperty -Path 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run' `
                    -Name 'FirstBaseWuLoopRun' -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            } catch {}

            try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
            exit 0
        }

        Write-FbLog 'OOBE handoff: sysprep launch returned $false (non-zero exit or threw). Routing to plain shutdown for another reboot attempt so STAGE 2 can retry from a freshly-rebooted state on the next boot. Triggers were intentionally NOT wiped (Fix 5), so the loop relaunches via RunOnce / ONSTART / ONLOGON.' 'ERROR'

        # 2026.05.13.1900-two-stage-sysprep-handoff Fix 3: on a
        # non-zero sysprep exit, Invoke-SysprepToOobe has already:
        #   - written the STUCK-DIAG-SYSPREP-*.json panic dump
        #     (with Panther log tail when exit=0x139f)
        #   - bumped SysprepRetryCount on disk
        #   - re-asserted PendingSysprep=true on disk
        #   - tripped the escape valve (and not returned) if
        #     SysprepRetryCount >= MaxSysprepRetries
        # All that remains is to ensure triggers are armed and
        # invoke Invoke-FbReboot for the patient shutdown sequence.
        # The shutdown gives CBS another chance to settle on the
        # next boot, after which STAGE 2 retries.
        try { Set-RunOnceAutostart -Reason 'STAGE 2 sysprep non-zero exit; re-arming for next-boot STAGE 2 retry' } catch {}
        try { Test-FbAndRepairTriggers -Context 'stage2-sysprep-nonzero' | Out-Null } catch {}

        try {
            Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Rebooting' -Message 'STAGE 2 sysprep failed - rebooting for another retry attempt. Triggers remain armed; loop will relaunch on next boot.'
            Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Rebooting'
            Invoke-FbReboot -Reason 'STAGE 2 sysprep returned non-zero; rebooting for retry'
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("OOBE handoff: post-sysprep-failure Invoke-FbReboot threw: {0}; exiting clean so RunOnce relaunches us on next manual restart." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
        try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
        exit 1
    }

    # Step 5/6: sysprep failed AND (when applicable) the SYSTEM-
    # scoped scheduled-task one-shot also failed (or we were
    # already at the sysprep retry cap). Write a final STUCK-DIAG
    # dump for the operator and surface the FINAL message. Exit 0
    # - NOT 1 - so the watchdog ONSTART task does NOT relaunch us
    # into yet another doomed cycle. The user manually power-
    # cycles, Windows boots normally into Audit Mode, the next
    # FirstBase loop run sees InstallsCompleted=$true (or the
    # upgrade-bridge synthesizes it) and re-attempts the sysprep
    # handoff with a fresh state.
    try { Write-StuckRebootDiag -Reason ("OOBE handoff final state: cascade bypassed, sysprep " + ($(if ($sysprepAtCap) { 'at retry cap' } else { 'launch failed' })) + ", SYSTEM-scoped scheduled-task reboot also failed (or skipped). " + $Reason) } catch {}

    $finalMsg = 'Updates installed and ready. Windows is blocking automatic reboot. Please manually restart the device to complete OOBE setup. Updates will finalize on first user logon.'
    Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Completed' -Message $finalMsg
    Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Completed'
    Write-FbLog $finalMsg 'ERROR'

    try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
    exit 0
}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NUCLEAR OPTION - DO NOT ENABLE WITHOUT REVIEW.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Killing csrss.exe ("Client/Server Runtime Subsystem") is treated by
# the Windows kernel as a catastrophic session-zero failure and forces
# an immediate bugcheck reboot (BSOD 0xC000021A). This bypasses
# EVERY user-mode reboot block, including TrustedInstaller's, the
# scheduler veto, and any IShutdownService hold.
#
# RISKS (this is why it is commented out by default):
#   - If TrustedInstaller / TiWorker is mid-finalize on a CBS package
#     when csrss dies, the package state can be left half-committed.
#     A subsequent boot's CBS recovery MAY repair it, or may fail and
#     mark the package corrupt - this is the same hazard as pulling
#     the power cord during an update.
#   - Causes a hard BSOD which the user might mistake for a real
#     crash, despite the deferred-update splash being up at the time.
#   - On systems with BitLocker auto-unlock disabled, the recovery
#     key prompt may appear after the bugcheck, requiring manual
#     intervention.
#
# DO NOT CALL THIS FUNCTION from the normal cascade or OOBE handoff
# without (a) a feature flag, (b) an explicit user/operator opt-in,
# and (c) documented post-reboot CBS repair guidance. It is left
# here as a deliberate dead-code reference for future maintainers
# debugging an extreme TI-veto case where every other avenue has
# failed AND the operator has accepted the CBS-corruption risk.
#
# To enable in an emergency build, uncomment the function body AND
# add a call site (e.g. as a Stage 9 in Invoke-Reboot or as a final
# branch in Invoke-FbOobeHandoff before exit). The actual mechanism
# is:
#
#   $csrss = Get-Process -Name 'csrss' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
#   if ($csrss) { Stop-Process -Id $csrss[0].Id -Force }
#
# The kernel sees csrss exit non-zero, raises
# STATUS_SYSTEM_PROCESS_TERMINATED, and bugchecks within ~1 second.
#
# function Invoke-FbNuclearCsrssKill {
#     <#
#     .SYNOPSIS
#         INTENTIONAL BUGCHECK via csrss.exe kill - last-resort
#         emergency reboot for kernel-vetoed update finalization.
#     .DESCRIPTION
#         See above warning. DO NOT use without operator opt-in.
#     #>
#     param([string]$Reason = 'last-resort csrss bugcheck reboot')
#     Write-FbLog ("NUCLEAR csrss kill requested: {0}" -f $Reason) 'ERROR'
#     try {
#         $csrss = Get-Process -Name 'csrss' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
#         if ($csrss) {
#             foreach ($p in @($csrss)) {
#                 try { Stop-Process -Id $p.Id -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } catch {}
#             }
#         }
#     } catch {}
#     # Box bugchecks within ~1 second of the first csrss exit.
# }

function Test-InternetConnectivity {
    $targets = @(
        @{ Host = 'download.windowsupdate.com'; Port = 443 },
        @{ Host = 'www.msftconnecttest.com';    Port = 80  },
        @{ Host = '8.8.8.8';                    Port = 53  }
    )

    foreach ($t in $targets) {
        $client = $null
        try {
            $client = New-Object System.Net.Sockets.TcpClient
            $iar = $client.BeginConnect($t.Host, $t.Port, $null, $null)
            if (-not $iar.AsyncWaitHandle.WaitOne(4000, $false)) { continue }
            $client.EndConnect($iar) | Out-Null
            if ($client.Connected) { return $true }
        } catch {
        } finally {
            if ($client) { $client.Close() }
        }
    }

    return $false
}

function Wait-ForInternet {
    param([int]$SleepSeconds = 30)

    $checks = 0
    while (-not (Test-InternetConnectivity)) {
        $checks++
        Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Network' -Message 'Waiting for internet connection...'
        if ($checks -eq 1 -or ($checks % 10 -eq 0)) {
            Write-FbLog "No internet detected. Waiting for connection..." 'WARN'
        }
        Start-Sleep -Seconds $SleepSeconds
    }

    if ($checks -gt 0) {
        Write-FbLog "Internet detected. Resuming update workflow." 'INFO'
        Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Network' -Message 'Internet detected. Resuming updates...'
    }
}

# 5.4.20: Start-Service / Restart-Service wait on BITS is unbounded in Windows PowerShell
# (PF2X3W8J hung 17:35 to 03:33 on 'Waiting for service Background Intelligent Transfer Service').
$script:FbWuServiceWaitSeconds = 90

function Wait-FbServiceStatusBounded {
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$Name,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [System.ServiceProcess.ServiceControllerStatus]$Status,
        [int]$TimeoutSeconds = 0
    )
    if ($TimeoutSeconds -le 0) { $TimeoutSeconds = [int]$script:FbWuServiceWaitSeconds }
    $svc = $null
    try { $svc = Get-Service -Name $Name -ErrorAction Stop } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Service '{0}' not found for WaitForStatus {1}: {2}" -f $Name, $Status, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        return $false
    }
    try {
        $svc.Refresh()
        if ($svc.Status -eq $Status) { return $true }
        $svc.WaitForStatus($Status, [TimeSpan]::FromSeconds($TimeoutSeconds))
        Write-FbLog ("Service '{0}' reached {1} within {2}s." -f $Name, $Status, $TimeoutSeconds) 'INFO'
        return $true
    } catch {
        $live = $null
        try { $live = Get-Service -Name $Name -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } catch {}
        $st = if ($live) { [string]$live.Status } else { 'missing' }
        Write-FbLog ("WARN: service '{0}' did not reach {1} within {2}s (Status={3}). Continuing." -f $Name, $Status, $TimeoutSeconds, $st) 'WARN'
        return $false
    }
}

function Stop-FbServiceBounded {
    param([Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$Name)
    try {
        $svc = Get-Service -Name $Name -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        if (-not $svc) { return }
        $svc.Refresh()
        if ($svc.Status -eq [System.ServiceProcess.ServiceControllerStatus]::Stopped) { return }
        Write-FbLog ("Stop-Service bounded '{0}' (wait={1}s, current={2})..." -f $Name, $script:FbWuServiceWaitSeconds, $svc.Status) 'INFO'
        try { Stop-Service -Name $Name -Force -NoWait -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } catch {
            try { $svc.Stop() } catch {}
        }
        [void](Wait-FbServiceStatusBounded -Name $Name -Status ([System.ServiceProcess.ServiceControllerStatus]::Stopped))
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Stop-Service bounded '{0}' threw: {1}" -f $Name, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

function Start-FbServiceBounded {
    param([Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$Name)
    try {
        $svc = Get-Service -Name $Name -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        if (-not $svc) { return }
        $svc.Refresh()
        if ($svc.Status -eq [System.ServiceProcess.ServiceControllerStatus]::Running) { return }
        Write-FbLog ("Start-Service bounded '{0}' (wait={1}s, current={2})..." -f $Name, $script:FbWuServiceWaitSeconds, $svc.Status) 'INFO'
        try { $svc.Start() } catch {}
        [void](Wait-FbServiceStatusBounded -Name $Name -Status ([System.ServiceProcess.ServiceControllerStatus]::Running))
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Start-Service bounded '{0}' threw: {1}" -f $Name, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }
}

function Reset-WindowsUpdateServices {
    Write-FbLog ("Resetting Windows Update services (BITS/WUAUSERV/USOSVC) with {0}s wait cap." -f $script:FbWuServiceWaitSeconds) 'WARN'
    foreach ($svc in @('bits','wuauserv','usosvc')) {
        Stop-FbServiceBounded -Name $svc
    }
    Start-Sleep -Seconds 2
    foreach ($svc in @('bits','wuauserv')) {
        Start-FbServiceBounded -Name $svc
    }
    Start-FbServiceBounded -Name 'usosvc'
    Initialize-FbWuEnvironment
}

function Invoke-UsoScanKick {
    try {
        if (Get-Command UsoClient.exe -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue) {
            Write-FbLog "Kicking USO scan (UsoClient StartScan)." 'INFO'
            Start-Process -FilePath 'UsoClient.exe' -ArgumentList 'StartScan' -WindowStyle Hidden -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
        }
    } catch {}
}

function Get-PassDefinitions {
    # Bucket order applied per iteration. Filters live in the engine
    # (Invoke-FbWuScan) classifier; this list is for UI/log labels only.
    # Order is Critical -> Driver -> Cumulative -> Optional.
    return @(
        @{ Name = 'Critical';   HaltOnFailure = $true  },
        @{ Name = 'Driver';     HaltOnFailure = $false },
        @{ Name = 'Cumulative'; HaltOnFailure = $false },
        @{ Name = 'Optional';   HaltOnFailure = $false }
    )
}

function Complete-And-Exit {
    param(
        [string]$Reason,
        [bool]$Success = $true,
        [int]$ExitCode = 0
    )

    # 2026.05.13.2213c-onlogon-primary-and-defender-disable: bulletproof
    # Defender restore at the top of Complete-And-Exit. EVERY exit path
    # in this function (success-and-sysprep, CBS-stuck give-up, sysprep-
    # failure exit 1, non-finalize exit $ExitCode, plain failure
    # exit $ExitCode) ships the device toward end-user delivery, so
    # Defender real-time monitoring MUST be re-enabled before any of
    # those exits. Idempotent - safe even if the loop never disabled
    # Defender on this run (e.g. the early-fail path).
    Set-FbDefenderRealtime -Enabled $true -Reason 'complete-and-exit'

    if (-not $Success) {
        Write-FbLog $Reason 'ERROR'
        try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
        exit $ExitCode
    }

    Write-FbLog $Reason 'INFO'
    Set-Content -Path $CompleteMarker -Value ("Completed at {0}" -f (Get-Date -Format s)) -Encoding ASCII -Force
    New-Item -ItemType File -Path $WaitMarker -Force | Out-Null
    Write-FbLog 'Complete-And-Exit: keeping wu-state.json and wu-status.json (markers of record; logs and markers are not scrubbed).' 'INFO'

    if ($FinalizeToOobe) {
        Write-FbLog 'Complete-And-Exit: routing to Invoke-FbOobeHandoff so hardware checks + Pass/Fail operator step run before seal/sysprep.' 'INFO'
        Invoke-FbOobeHandoff -Reason 'Complete-And-Exit: updates complete; hardware gate + seal + sysprep.'
        try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
        exit 0
    }

    schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $TaskName /F | Out-Null
    schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $LogonTaskName /F | Out-Null
    schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $Boot2TaskName /F | Out-Null
    schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $AnyLogonTaskName /F | Out-Null
    schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $SplashWatcherBoot2Name /F | Out-Null

    try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
    exit $ExitCode
}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Main loop
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# 2026.05.13.0900-trigger-resilience Fix 6: early-startup trigger audit.
#
# The 2026-05-12 incident (WU-20260512-122639.log) proved that all four
# trigger layers (RunOnce, RunOnceEx, Run, and the two scheduled tasks)
# can be in inconsistent states when the loop launches:
#
#   - RunOnce !FirstBaseWuLoop is consumed on the first interactive
#     logon by Windows itself, so by the time WE run, it's already
#     gone unless Set-RunOnceAutostart re-arms it. If the loop crashes
#     before reaching Invoke-FbReboot, no re-arm fires and the next
#     manual restart has no trigger.
#   - The shutdown.exe sequence in Invoke-FbReboot can begin a partial
#     logoff that consumes RunOnce without the box actually rebooting
#     (the failure mode observed on 2026-05-12).
#   - Scheduled tasks created by SetupComplete.cmd in builds prior to
#     2026-05-13 missed the StartWhenAvailable=$true setting, so a
#     missed ONSTART trigger silently skipped instead of deferring.
#
# This audit runs EARLY in the main flow (after all helper functions
# are defined, before any work that could fail) and re-arms anything
# missing on every launch. Self-healing on every relaunch path:
# RunOnce, RunOnceEx, Run/launcher, ONSTART, ONLOGON.
#
# Skipped only if the persistent completion marker is already on disk
# (in which case we're in a stale relaunch and arming would just delay
# the OOBE handoff cleanup the marker check below performs).
try {
    if (Test-Path $CompleteMarker) {
        Write-FbLog ("Early-startup trigger audit SKIPPED: completion marker already present at '{0}'." -f $CompleteMarker) 'INFO'
    } else {
        $earlyAuditMsg = Test-FbAndRepairTriggers -Context 'startup'
        Write-FbLog ("Early-startup trigger audit -> " + $earlyAuditMsg) 'INFO'
    }
} catch {
    Write-FbLog ("Early-startup trigger audit threw: {0}; backup triggers from SetupComplete remain in place." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
}

# ---------- 2026.05.19.2213p Bug LL: splash-launch diagnostic ----------
# DIAGNOSTIC-ONLY. Emit a one-shot per-boot snapshot of every signal known
# to influence whether the splash UI actually renders (schtasks state of
# FirstBase\* tasks, HKLM RunOnce/RunOnceEx/Run, query user, explorer.exe
# instances, splash-related process inventory). Plus a Layer 5 direct
# Start-Process spawn of the splash launcher from this loop's process so
# we have a 4th data point on Dell hardware where Layers 2 + 4 succeed
# but produce zero splash PIDs. Both helpers are best-effort: every probe
# is try/catch'd, failures are logged but never thrown back to the caller,
# and the only "behavioural" side effect (the Layer 5 spawn) is harmless
# because FirstBaseSplashSingleInstance.ps1's FileShare::None lock on
# splash-launcher.lock dedupes any duplicate WPF window per Bug EE.
# Suppressed when the completion marker is on disk (loop is in a stale
# relaunch and we have nothing useful to diagnose).
try {
    if (-not (Test-Path $CompleteMarker)) {
        Write-FbSplashLaunchDiagnostic -Context 'startup'
    } else {
        Write-FbLog "Splash launch diagnostic (Bug LL) SKIPPED: completion marker present." 'INFO'
    }
} catch {
    Write-FbLog ("Splash launch diagnostic (Bug LL) outer threw: {0}; non-fatal." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
}
try {
    if (-not (Test-Path $CompleteMarker)) {
        Invoke-FbSplashDirectSpawn -Context 'startup'
    } else {
        Write-FbLog "Splash direct-spawn Layer 5 (Bug LL) SKIPPED: completion marker present." 'INFO'
    }
} catch {
    Write-FbLog ("Splash direct-spawn Layer 5 (Bug LL) outer threw: {0}; non-fatal." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
}

# ---------- 2026.05.13.2213b Fix B Layer 4: splash schtasks bridge ----
# Field evidence on 2211/2212/2213a test devices proved Layers 1-3 (HKLM
# RunOnce, OnLogon scheduled task, SYSTEM ONSTART watcher task) can ALL
# fail to render the splash under audit-mode auto-login on Win11 26200:
#   - Layers 1 + 2 ran the same FirstBaseSplashWatcher.cmd and raced on
#     splash-watcher.log (one task's '>' truncate clobbered the other);
#     2213b's WRITE_SPLASH_WATCHER hardening fixes the race.
#   - Layer 3 HKLM RunOnce !FirstBaseWuSplash sits unconsumed in audit-
#     mode logon (Win11 26200 doesn't process HKLM RunOnce even with the
#     '!' synchronous prefix on this image).
#
# 2213a's Layer 4 used Start-Process powershell.exe -ArgumentList @(
# '-Command', $cmdLine) with a here-string-encoded sibling scriptblock.
# Field evidence: zero lines written to splash-fallback.log even when
# the sibling process spawned (PID captured in Task Manager) - the
# -Command here-string parse-failed under PowerShell 5.1 / WMF on this
# image. The sibling never executed past its first try{} so no
# telemetry, no splash.
#
# 2213b Fix B: replace Start-Process powershell -Command with the SAME
# schtasks /Create /SC ONCE /IT /RU Administrators /RL HIGHEST /F + /Run
# bridge that Layer 1's watcher batch already uses (proven to cross
# Session 0 -> Session 1, fires reliably on every test device). The
# bridge runs FirstBaseSplashLauncher.cmd which is a known-good /TR
# target with no inline -Command escaping. We capture both the /Create
# and /Run exit codes and heartbeat them into splash-fallback.log so a
# subsequent verifier has unambiguous evidence that Layer 4 fired,
# regardless of whether the splash itself rendered (the launcher's
# success or failure is observable in splash-watcher / Show-Update
# Progress logs downstream).
#
# Task name: FirstBase\UpdateSplashLayer4 - intentionally distinct from
# every existing FirstBase\Update* task to avoid trample/dedup conflicts
# with Layer 1's FirstBase\UpdateSplashInteractive watcher task or
# Layer 2's FirstBase\UpdateSplash OnLogon task or the watcher's
# dynamically-created FirstBase\UpdateSplashInteractiveLaunch one-shot.
#
# Skipped if the completion marker is already on disk (loop is in a
# stale relaunch and the splash should not render anyway) or if
# FirstBase-NoSplash.flag is present (operator suppressed the splash).
try {
    $splashSuppressFlag = Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBase-NoSplash.flag'
    $splashScriptPath   = Join-Path $FbRoot 'Show-UpdateProgress.ps1'
    if (Test-Path $CompleteMarker) {
        Write-FbLog "Splash fallback (Layer 4) SKIPPED: completion marker already present." 'INFO'
    } elseif (Test-Path $splashSuppressFlag) {
        Write-FbLog ("Splash fallback (Layer 4) SKIPPED: suppress flag present at '{0}'." -f $splashSuppressFlag) 'INFO'
    } elseif (-not (Test-Path $splashScriptPath)) {
        Write-FbLog ("Splash fallback (Layer 4) SKIPPED: '{0}' missing." -f $splashScriptPath) 'WARN'
    } else {
        $fallbackLogPath = Join-Path $LogDir 'splash-fallback.log'
        $layer4TaskName  = 'FirstBase\UpdateSplashLayer4'
        $splashLauncher  = Join-Path $FbRoot 'FirstBaseSplashLauncher.cmd'
        $splashUiLogL4      = Join-Path $LogDir 'splash-ui.log'
        $splashDirectLogL4 = Join-Path $LogDir 'splash-launch-direct.log'

        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $splashLauncher)) {
            Write-FbLog ("Splash fallback (Layer 4): launcher shim missing at '{0}'; cannot bridge. Splash will rely on Layers 1-3." -f $splashLauncher) 'WARN'
            try { Add-Content -Path $fallbackLogPath -Value ("[{0}] Layer 4 schtasks bridge SKIPPED: launcher missing at '{1}'" -f (Get-Date -Format o), $splashLauncher) -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } catch {}
        } else {
            try {
                # 2213b verifier item 20: do NOT /Delete /F before /Create /F.
                # /Create /F already overwrites; an explicit /Delete /F of an
                # in-flight ONCE task would terminate any currently-rendering
                # splash WPF process from a prior loop iteration. Rely on
                # /Create /F's overwrite semantics.
                $createArgs = @(
                    '/Create',
                    '/TN', $layer4TaskName,
                    '/TR', $splashLauncher,
                    '/SC', 'ONCE',
                    '/ST', '23:59',
                    '/RU', 'BUILTIN\Administrators',
                    '/RL', 'HIGHEST',
                    '/IT',
                    '/F'
                )
                $createOutput = & schtasks @createArgs 2>&1
                $createExit   = $LASTEXITCODE
                Write-FbLog ("Layer 4 splash schtasks /Create exit={0} output={1}" -f $createExit, (($createOutput | ForEach-Object { [string]$_ }) -join '; ')) 'INFO'

                if ($createExit -eq 0) {
                    $runExit = -1
                    $runOutput = @()
                    $tokenPid = 0
                    $loopSessL4 = -1
                    try { $loopSessL4 = [int][System.Diagnostics.Process]::GetCurrentProcess().SessionId } catch {}
                    $expL4 = Get-FbFirstInteractiveExplorerProcess
                    if ($null -eq $expL4 -and $loopSessL4 -eq 0) {
                        Write-FbLog ("Layer 4 splash: Session-0 loop with no interactive explorer yet; waiting up to 120s before token or schtasks /Run (2213y Bug QQ). If we defer, see '{0}', '{1}', and splash-fallback.log." -f $splashDirectLogL4, $splashUiLogL4) 'INFO'
                        try { Add-Content -Path $fallbackLogPath -Value ("[{0}] Layer 4 WAIT-EXPLORER (2213y Bug QQ): loopSession=0, polling up to 120s; correlate '{1}' + '{2}'" -f (Get-Date -Format o), $splashDirectLogL4, $splashUiLogL4) -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } catch {}
                        $expL4 = Wait-FbInteractiveExplorer -MaxWaitSeconds 120 -PollSeconds 1
                    }
                    try {
                        if ($expL4) {
                            $tokenPid = Invoke-FbLaunchCmdInInteractiveUserSession -ExplorerPid ([int]$expL4.Id) -LauncherPath $splashLauncher
                        }
                    } catch {}
                    if ($tokenPid -gt 0) {
                        $runExit = 0
                        Write-FbLog ("Layer 4 splash 2213x Bug PP: CreateProcessAsUser via explorer pid={0} childPid={1}" -f $expL4.Id, $tokenPid) 'INFO'
                        try { Add-Content -Path $fallbackLogPath -Value ("[{0}] Layer 4 explorer-token bridge fired: createExit={1} tokenChildPid={2} taskName={3} launcher={4}" -f (Get-Date -Format o), $createExit, $tokenPid, $layer4TaskName, $splashLauncher) -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } catch {}
                    } elseif ($loopSessL4 -eq 0) {
                        $l4Le = 0
                        try { $l4Le = [int][FbFirstBaseNative.SplashInteractiveLaunch]::LastLaunchWin32Error } catch {}
                        $l4LeMsg = ''
                        if ($l4Le -ne 0) {
                            try { $l4LeMsg = ('; Win32: {0} ({1})' -f $l4Le, (New-Object System.ComponentModel.Win32Exception($l4Le)).Message) } catch { $l4LeMsg = ('; Win32: {0}' -f $l4Le) }
                        }
                        Write-FbLog ("Layer 4 splash 2213y Bug QQ: skipping schtasks /Run from Session 0 without explorer-token child (defer to SplashWatcher / RunOnce; avoids splash-launcher.lock in Session 0). Task '{0}' remains armed.{1} Correlate: '{2}', '{3}', splash-fallback.log." -f $layer4TaskName, $l4LeMsg, $splashDirectLogL4, $splashUiLogL4) 'INFO'
                        try { Add-Content -Path $fallbackLogPath -Value ("[{0}] Layer 4 DEFERRED-NORUN (2213y Bug QQ): loopSession=0 tokenPid=0; skipping schtasks /Run to avoid Session-0 splash-launcher.lock poisoning{1}. Logs: '{2}', '{3}'" -f (Get-Date -Format o), $l4LeMsg, $splashDirectLogL4, $splashUiLogL4) -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } catch {}
                    } else {
                        $runOutput = & schtasks /Run /TN $layer4TaskName 2>&1
                        $runExit   = $LASTEXITCODE
                        Write-FbLog ("Layer 4 splash schtasks /Run exit={0} output={1}" -f $runExit, (($runOutput | ForEach-Object { [string]$_ }) -join '; ')) 'INFO'
                        try { Add-Content -Path $fallbackLogPath -Value ("[{0}] Layer 4 schtasks bridge fired: createExit={1} runExit={2} taskName={3} launcher={4}" -f (Get-Date -Format o), $createExit, $runExit, $layer4TaskName, $splashLauncher) -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } catch {}
                    }
                } else {
                    Write-FbLog "Layer 4 splash schtasks /Create FAILED; cannot bridge to interactive session. Splash will rely on Layer 1 watcher." 'WARN'
                    try { Add-Content -Path $fallbackLogPath -Value ("[{0}] Layer 4 schtasks bridge /Create FAILED: exit={1} output={2}" -f (Get-Date -Format o), $createExit, (($createOutput | ForEach-Object { [string]$_ }) -join '; ')) -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } catch {}
                }
            } catch {
                Write-FbLog ("Layer 4 splash bridge threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
                try { Add-Content -Path $fallbackLogPath -Value ("[{0}] Layer 4 schtasks bridge THREW: {1}" -f (Get-Date -Format o), $_.Exception.Message) -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } catch {}
            }
        }
    }
} catch {
    Write-FbLog ("Splash fallback (Layer 4) outer block threw: {0}; splash layers 1-3 remain (may or may not render)." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
}

# ---------- 2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence startup gate ------
# THIS GATE replaces the pre-1700 cascade-exhaustion routing AND the
# silent-bypass paths it grew. The 1700 build's contract:
#
#   InstallsCompleted on disk == $true  IF AND ONLY IF  the disk also
#   carries RealInstallsTotal > 0 (Save-State's invariant scrubs the
#   latch otherwise; Load-State's scrub clears legacy latches).
#
# Combined with $script:RealInstallsThisBoot (in-memory only, fresh
# every boot), the gate now refuses to short-circuit to OOBE handoff
# on a fresh boot of a freshly-imaged device.
#
# The Fix E "first-iteration hard rail" is implemented here:
#
#   If BootCount == 1 AND on-disk RealInstallsTotal == 0 AND
#   RealInstallsThisBoot == 0:
#     -> SCREAMING dashboard warning
#     -> startup gate is FORCED off, no matter what any "bridge" or
#        legacy signal says
#     -> the loop MUST run at least one scan + one
#        Invoke-FbBucketApply before any handoff decision
#
# Once an install has succeeded (RealInstallsTotal > 0 on disk or
# this-boot counter > 0), the bridge logic below is allowed to
# observe legacy state-file signals and short-circuit normally.
#
# The legacy bridges 1-5 (status='Stuck', Pending restart rows,
# CascadeExhaustionCount >= 1, Test-FbWuRebootRequired, watchdog
# recovery) are KEPT but each one is now also gated on the
# real-install evidence: they can only trip when we have evidence
# in this boot or a prior boot.
$script:InstallsCompletedAtStartup = $false
$script:InstallsCompletedBridgeReason = $null
try {
    $startupStateForInstalls = Load-State
    $script:InstallsCompletedAtStartup = [bool]$startupStateForInstalls.InstallsCompleted

    # Probe the canonical signals that imply installs have already
    # happened in a prior run that did not persist the flag.
    $bridgePriorPhase   = ''
    $bridgePriorMessage = ''
    try {
        if (Test-Path $StatusFile) {
            $bridgeStatusObj = Get-Content -Path $StatusFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            if ($bridgeStatusObj) {
                $bridgePriorPhase   = [string]$bridgeStatusObj.phase
                $bridgePriorMessage = [string]$bridgeStatusObj.message
            }
        }
    } catch {}

    # 1. Status-file evidence: prior build wrote phase='Stuck' (cascade
    #    exhausted) or phase='Rebooting' with the BUDGET-EXHAUSTED /
    #    REBOOT-FAILED banner. Both mean we ARE post-install (the
    #    cascade only runs after Invoke-FbBucketApply triggered it).
    $bridgeFromStatus = $false
    if ($bridgePriorPhase -eq 'Stuck') {
        $script:InstallsCompletedBridgeReason = ("status phase='Stuck' indicates a cascade-exhausted exit AFTER installs ran")
        $bridgeFromStatus = $true
    } elseif ($bridgePriorPhase -eq 'Rebooting' -and $bridgePriorMessage -match '(?i)cascade\s+budget\s+exhausted|REBOOT\s+FAILED') {
        $script:InstallsCompletedBridgeReason = ("status phase='Rebooting' with cascade-exhaustion banner ('{0}') indicates installs ran in the prior cycle" -f $bridgePriorMessage)
        $bridgeFromStatus = $true
    }

    # 2. Status-file evidence: ANY visible 'Pending restart' UI row
    #    in the existing wu-status.json. Pending restart is only
    #    written by Invoke-FbBucketApply for updates that have either
    #    successfully installed (Action='Reboot' HResult) or exceeded
    #    the retry ceiling with the OS asking for a reboot - either
    #    way, installs have run.
    $bridgeFromPendingRow = $false
    if (-not $bridgeFromStatus -and -not $script:InstallsCompletedAtStartup) {
        try {
            if (Test-Path $StatusFile) {
                $bridgeStatusObj2 = Get-Content -Path $StatusFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
                if ($bridgeStatusObj2 -and $bridgeStatusObj2.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'updates' -and $bridgeStatusObj2.updates) {
                    foreach ($row in @($bridgeStatusObj2.updates)) {
                        if ($row -and ([string]$row.Status -eq 'Pending restart')) {
                            $bridgeFromPendingRow = $true
                            $script:InstallsCompletedBridgeReason = ("found existing 'Pending restart' UI row(s) in wu-status.json - installs already ran in a prior cycle")
                            break
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        } catch {}
    }

    # 3. Prior on-disk CascadeExhaustionCount >= 1 from the 1145
    #    build. The cascade only runs post-install, so the count
    #    being non-zero proves installs already happened.
    $bridgeFromCascadeCount = $false
    if (-not $bridgeFromStatus -and -not $bridgeFromPendingRow -and -not $script:InstallsCompletedAtStartup) {
        $priorCascadeCount = 0
        try { $priorCascadeCount = [int]$startupStateForInstalls.CascadeExhaustionCount } catch {}
        if ($priorCascadeCount -ge 1) {
            $bridgeFromCascadeCount = $true
            $script:InstallsCompletedBridgeReason = ("prior on-disk CascadeExhaustionCount={0} >= 1 implies installs already ran (cascade only fires post-install)" -f $priorCascadeCount)
        }
    }

    # 4. OS-level pending-reboot probe. 5.4.18: this is a REBOOT
    #    signal, not an OOBE-skip signal. Critical success +
    #    OSPendingReboot=true must reboot then continue Driver, not
    #    latch InstallsCompleted and skip the cascade.
    $bridgeFromReg = $false
    try {
        if (Test-FbWuRebootRequired) {
            $bridgeFromReg = $true
            Write-FbLog 'Startup: Test-FbWuRebootRequired=true; pending reboot is handled by pre-loop / post-bucket reboot rails. Not synthesizing InstallsCompleted (Driver/Cumulative may still be queued).' 'INFO'
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("InstallsCompleted fail-safe Test-FbWuRebootRequired threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # 5. WATCHDOG signal (NEW 2026.05.12.1030-no-cascade-final). If
    #    the stuck-Rebooting watchdog at the top of the script
    #    declared recovery and stashed a banner reason in
    #    $script:WatchdogStuckBannerReason, treat that as a
    #    definitive "we are post-install, the prior process died"
    #    signal. Forces the latch the same way bridges 1-4 do.
    #    Independent of the OS-level reboot probe so it still fires
    #    even on a device where Test-FbWuRebootRequired briefly
    #    returns false during a CBS settle gap.
    $bridgeFromWatchdog = $false
    if ($script:WatchdogRecoveryActive -and -not $script:InstallsCompletedAtStartup) {
        $bridgeFromWatchdog = $true
        if (-not $bridgeFromStatus -and -not $bridgeFromPendingRow -and -not $bridgeFromCascadeCount -and -not $bridgeFromReg) {
            $watchdogBanner = if ($script:WatchdogStuckBannerReason) { $script:WatchdogStuckBannerReason } else { '(no banner)' }
            $script:InstallsCompletedBridgeReason = ("watchdog recovery active at startup ({0}) - prior process died with a stale Rebooting status, treating as post-install" -f $watchdogBanner)
        }
        Write-FbLog ("InstallsCompleted FAIL-SAFE: WatchdogRecoveryActive=true forces latch (banner='{0}')." -f $script:WatchdogStuckBannerReason) 'ERROR'
    }

    # 2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence: the legacy bridges
    # (status='Stuck', Pending restart rows, CascadeExhaustionCount,
    # Test-FbWuRebootRequired, watchdog recovery) are kept as
    # signals but are now GATED on real-install evidence. A bridge
    # can only synthesize InstallsCompleted=true if the disk already
    # carries RealInstallsTotal > 0 - i.e. some prior boot in this
    # cycle actually saw at least one orcSucceeded result.
    $startupRealInstallsTotal = 0
    try { $startupRealInstallsTotal = [int]$startupStateForInstalls.RealInstallsTotal } catch {}
    $startupBootCount = 0
    try { $startupBootCount = [int]$startupStateForInstalls.BootCount } catch {}
    if (-not $script:InstallsCompletedAtStartup -and ($bridgeFromStatus -or $bridgeFromPendingRow -or $bridgeFromCascadeCount -or $bridgeFromWatchdog)) {
        if ($startupRealInstallsTotal -le 0) {
            Write-FbLog ("Upgrade bridge SUPPRESSED ({0}): RealInstallsTotal={1} - no proof a real install ever happened. Refusing to synthesize InstallsCompleted=true; the loop will scan/install instead." -f $script:InstallsCompletedBridgeReason, $startupRealInstallsTotal) 'ERROR'
            $script:InstallsCompletedBridgeReason = ("SUPPRESSED bridge (no real-install evidence): " + $script:InstallsCompletedBridgeReason)
        } else {
            if (Test-FbWuSkipCascadeUnsafe -State $startupStateForInstalls) {
                Write-FbLog ("5.4.19: upgrade bridge refused; cascade not exhausted (Driver/Cumulative/pending reboot). Not synthesizing InstallsCompleted. Bridge was: {0}" -f $script:InstallsCompletedBridgeReason) 'WARN'
            } else {
                Write-FbLog ("Upgrade bridge: {0} (RealInstallsTotal={1}); synthesizing InstallsCompleted=true so this run skips the cascade entirely and routes to OOBE handoff." -f $script:InstallsCompletedBridgeReason, $startupRealInstallsTotal) 'WARN'
                $script:InstallsCompletedAtStartup = $true
                try {
                    Save-State `
                        -EmptySweepCount        ([int]$startupStateForInstalls.EmptySweepCount) `
                        -LastPass               ([string]$startupStateForInstalls.LastPass) `
                        -StartTime              ([string]$startupStateForInstalls.StartTime) `
                        -FailureCount           ([int]$startupStateForInstalls.FailureCount) `
                        -UpdateRetry            $startupStateForInstalls.UpdateRetry `
                        -GivenUpUpdateIds       @($startupStateForInstalls.GivenUpUpdateIds) `
                        -BootCountByKey         $startupStateForInstalls.BootCountByKey `
                        -BootCount              ([int]$startupStateForInstalls.BootCount) `
                        -ForceHiddenKeys        @($startupStateForInstalls.ForceHiddenKeys) `
                        -RebootIterations       ([int]$startupStateForInstalls.RebootIterations) `
                        -SysprepRetryCount      ([int]$startupStateForInstalls.SysprepRetryCount) `
                        -InstallsCompleted      $true
                } catch {
                    Write-FbLog ("Failed to persist upgrade-bridge InstallsCompleted=true: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
                }
            }
        }
    }

    # 2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence Fix E: first-iteration
    # hard rail. On the FIRST boot of a fresh image (BootCount<=1)
    # with no real install evidence on disk OR in this boot, REFUSE
    # to short-circuit the startup gate under any signal. This is
    # the rail that makes the silent-bypass class of bug impossible
    # to ship: a freshly-imaged device with leftover sysprep PFRO
    # entries (Test-FbWuRebootRequired=true) CANNOT bypass the
    # scan/install pass.
    if ($script:InstallsCompletedAtStartup -and $startupBootCount -le 1 -and $startupRealInstallsTotal -le 0 -and $script:RealInstallsThisBoot -le 0) {
        Write-FbLog ("Fix E first-iteration hard rail TRIPPED: BootCount={0}, RealInstallsTotal={1}, RealInstallsThisBoot={2}. Overriding InstallsCompletedAtStartup=true back to false; the loop MUST run at least one scan + one bucket apply before any handoff decision." -f $startupBootCount, $startupRealInstallsTotal, $script:RealInstallsThisBoot) 'ERROR'
        $script:InstallsCompletedAtStartup = $false
        $script:InstallsCompletedBridgeReason = "Fix E first-iteration hard rail: fresh image, no real installs, gate forced OFF"
        # Persist the corrected latch back to disk so a downstream
        # Load-State sees the truthful state. Save-State's invariant
        # will also scrub the on-disk latch since RealInstallsTotal=0.
        try {
            Save-State `
                -EmptySweepCount        ([int]$startupStateForInstalls.EmptySweepCount) `
                -LastPass               ([string]$startupStateForInstalls.LastPass) `
                -StartTime              ([string]$startupStateForInstalls.StartTime) `
                -FailureCount           ([int]$startupStateForInstalls.FailureCount) `
                -UpdateRetry            $startupStateForInstalls.UpdateRetry `
                -GivenUpUpdateIds       @($startupStateForInstalls.GivenUpUpdateIds) `
                -BootCountByKey         $startupStateForInstalls.BootCountByKey `
                -BootCount              ([int]$startupStateForInstalls.BootCount) `
                -ForceHiddenKeys        @($startupStateForInstalls.ForceHiddenKeys) `
                -RebootIterations       ([int]$startupStateForInstalls.RebootIterations) `
                -SysprepRetryCount      ([int]$startupStateForInstalls.SysprepRetryCount) `
                -InstallsCompleted      $false
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Fix E hard-rail Save-State threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
    }
} catch {
    Write-FbLog ("InstallsCompleted startup probe failed; continuing without the gate: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    $script:InstallsCompletedAtStartup = $false
}

# 2026.05.11.1515: persist a one-line summary of the startup probe so
# downstream code (and the next user-visible UI message) can surface
# the observation. The user with no log access can read the splash
# and tell us EXACTLY whether the latch is being seen at startup.
if (-not $script:LastInstallsCompletedDiag) {
    $script:LastInstallsCompletedDiag = if ($script:InstallsCompletedAtStartup) {
        if ($script:InstallsCompletedBridgeReason) {
            ("InstallsCompleted=true at startup [bridge: {0}]" -f $script:InstallsCompletedBridgeReason)
        } else {
            'InstallsCompleted=true at startup [persisted in wu-state.json]'
        }
    } else {
        'InstallsCompleted=false at startup [no prior install evidence; normal scan/install will run]'
    }
}
Write-FbLog ("Startup probe summary: {0}" -f $script:LastInstallsCompletedDiag) 'INFO'

# 2026.05.13.2213k-real-single-instance-and-real-multipass-and-builder-pathfix
# Bug Y-2 (CRITICAL): the InstallsCompleted-shortcut HANDOFF block was
# previously inlined here. That triggered the
# "inline-retry budget exhausted (1/3 attempts, ...)" cascade in
# G:\fb-dump-final-1111\State\reboot-tier.log because PowerShell
# defines functions when their `function` statement is REACHED, not
# at parse time. When this code executed at line ~9029 in the 2213j
# source, the function `Invoke-FbWuInlineRetryPass` (declared at
# line 9551 source / line 9407 in 2213j) had NOT YET been registered.
# So `Invoke-FbOobeHandoff`'s inline-retry loop threw
# "The term 'Invoke-FbWuInlineRetryPass' is not recognized" on
# iteration 1, hit its `catch { break }`, and the exhaustion path
# rendered "(1/3 attempts)" - producing the dump's 50+ reboots.
#
# Field evidence: WU-20260518-101429.log lines 60-62:
#   [10:15:48] [WARN] OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 inline-retry): attempt 1/3 starting.
#   [10:15:56] [WARN] OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 inline-retry attempt 1/3):
#       Invoke-FbWuInlineRetryPass threw: The term
#       'Invoke-FbWuInlineRetryPass' is not recognized
#       as the name of a cmdlet, function, script file, or
#       operable program. ...; breaking out to reboot.
#   [10:15:56] [ERROR] OOBE handoff (STAGE 1 inline-retry):
#       inline-retry budget exhausted (1/3 attempts, postResidual=15);
#       refusing handoff and consuming one reboot.
#
# Fix: defer the handoff invocation to after BOTH
# Invoke-FbWuInlineRetryPass (defined ~line 9551) and
# Invoke-FbBucketApply (defined ~line 9701) have been registered.
# We just LATCH the decision into a $script: variable here; the
# real Invoke-FbOobeHandoff call moves to the same position as the
# existing main-loop top guard. All side-effect work (UI relabel,
# informational scan, dashboard latch) also moves there so it runs
# in the right ordering relative to engine load and function
# registration.
if ($script:InstallsCompletedAtStartup) {
    Write-FbLog ("Startup: InstallsCompleted=true latched; deferring OOBE handoff to post-function-definitions site so STAGE 1 inline-retry can resolve Invoke-FbWuInlineRetryPass (Bug Y-2 in 2213j; fixed in 2213k). WatchdogRecoveryActive={0}." -f $script:WatchdogRecoveryActive) 'WARN'
    $script:DeferredStartupInstallsCompletedHandoff = $true
} else {
    $script:DeferredStartupInstallsCompletedHandoff = $false
}

# ---------- 2026-05-11 1145 cascade-exhaustion routing ----------
# Read CascadeExhaustionCount BEFORE the normal main-loop state setup
# so we can take an early escape hatch when the prior runs have proven
# that the user-mode reboot cascade cannot recover this device.
#
# Reset semantics:
#   * If the prior process exited with phase='Stuck' (cascade exhausted)
#     OR phase='Rebooting' wedged (watchdog will / did fire), we
#     PRESERVE the count - the cascade truly has not succeeded.
#   * Otherwise (fresh first run, prior phase='Completed' / 'Scanning'
#     / etc.) we are running after either a successful OS reboot or
#     a clean startup, so we reset the count to 0. This implements
#     the spec's "reset on successful reboot" requirement: a
#     successful reboot terminates the prior process via OS shutdown,
#     so its last persisted phase was whatever Set-UiPhase wrote
#     before Invoke-Reboot was called - which is Scanning / Bucket
#     / Completed (NOT Stuck). The new process sees that and clears.
#
# Action on the count after reset:
#   * 0: normal cascade.
#   * 1: at next Invoke-Reboot call, escalate to SYSTEM-scoped
#        scheduled-task emergency reboot (handled inside Invoke-Reboot).
#   * >=2: skip ALL reboot attempts and jump directly to OOBE handoff
#        at startup (we do this here, BEFORE the normal main-loop
#        state load, so no downstream code can re-enter the cascade).
$script:CascadeExhaustionCountAtStartup = 0
try {
    $startupState = Load-State
    $script:CascadeExhaustionCountAtStartup = [int]$startupState.CascadeExhaustionCount

    # Decide whether to preserve or reset the count. The watchdog
    # already inspected wu-status.json; re-read it here so we can
    # also detect phase='Stuck' (the cascade-exhausted exit marker
    # that the watchdog itself does not trigger on) AND the prior
    # message text so we can recover upgrade scenarios.
    $priorPhase = ''
    $priorMessage = ''
    try {
        if (Test-Path $StatusFile) {
            $statusObj = Get-Content -Path $StatusFile -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ConvertFrom-Json -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            if ($statusObj) {
                $priorPhase   = [string]$statusObj.phase
                $priorMessage = [string]$statusObj.message
            }
        }
    } catch {}

    # Upgrade-bridge recovery. The PRIOR build
    # (2026.05.11.1045-aggressive-ti-kill and earlier) did not
    # persist CascadeExhaustionCount. If the on-disk count is 0
    # but the prior phase / message clearly indicates we have
    # already cycled through a cascade-exhaustion exit (or that
    # we're stuck mid-cascade with the BUDGET-EXHAUSTED logoff
    # banner), we synthesize a count >= 2 so the first run of
    # the new build on a currently-stuck device skips straight
    # to OOBE handoff instead of re-running the doomed cascade.
    # This is the recovery path for the field device reported at
    # 2026-05-11 1100: 3 cycles of cascade exhaustion under the
    # prior build, dashboard pinned at "CASCADE BUDGET EXHAUSTED".
    $bridgeReason = $null
    if ($script:CascadeExhaustionCountAtStartup -eq 0) {
        if ($priorPhase -eq 'Stuck') {
            $bridgeReason = ("upgrade-bridge: prior phase='Stuck' indicates a cascade-exhausted exit from the prior build that did not persist CascadeExhaustionCount")
        } elseif ($priorPhase -eq 'Rebooting' -and $priorMessage -match '(?i)cascade\s+budget\s+exhausted|REBOOT\s+FAILED') {
            $bridgeReason = ("upgrade-bridge: prior phase='Rebooting' with cascade-exhaustion banner ('{0}') indicates the prior build already cycled through the dead-end cascade" -f $priorMessage)
        }
    }
    if ($bridgeReason) {
        $script:CascadeExhaustionCountAtStartup = 2
        Write-FbLog ("{0}; synthesizing CascadeExhaustionCount=2 to route this run straight to OOBE handoff." -f $bridgeReason) 'WARN'
        try {
            Save-State `
                -EmptySweepCount        ([int]$startupState.EmptySweepCount) `
                -LastPass               ([string]$startupState.LastPass) `
                -StartTime              ([string]$startupState.StartTime) `
                -FailureCount           ([int]$startupState.FailureCount) `
                -UpdateRetry            $startupState.UpdateRetry `
                -GivenUpUpdateIds       @($startupState.GivenUpUpdateIds) `
                -BootCountByKey         $startupState.BootCountByKey `
                -BootCount              ([int]$startupState.BootCount) `
                -ForceHiddenKeys        @($startupState.ForceHiddenKeys) `
                -RebootIterations       ([int]$startupState.RebootIterations) `
                -SysprepRetryCount      ([int]$startupState.SysprepRetryCount) `
                -CascadeExhaustionCount 2
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Failed to persist upgrade-bridge CascadeExhaustionCount: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
    }

    $preserveCount = $script:WatchdogRecoveryActive -or ($priorPhase -eq 'Stuck') -or ($priorPhase -eq 'Rebooting') -or $bridgeReason
    if (-not $preserveCount -and $script:CascadeExhaustionCountAtStartup -gt 0) {
        Write-FbLog ("Startup detected clean run (priorPhase='{0}', WatchdogRecoveryActive={1}); resetting CascadeExhaustionCount from {2} -> 0." -f $priorPhase, $script:WatchdogRecoveryActive, $script:CascadeExhaustionCountAtStartup) 'INFO'
        try {
            Save-State `
                -EmptySweepCount        ([int]$startupState.EmptySweepCount) `
                -LastPass               ([string]$startupState.LastPass) `
                -StartTime              ([string]$startupState.StartTime) `
                -FailureCount           ([int]$startupState.FailureCount) `
                -UpdateRetry            $startupState.UpdateRetry `
                -GivenUpUpdateIds       @($startupState.GivenUpUpdateIds) `
                -BootCountByKey         $startupState.BootCountByKey `
                -BootCount              ([int]$startupState.BootCount) `
                -ForceHiddenKeys        @($startupState.ForceHiddenKeys) `
                -RebootIterations       ([int]$startupState.RebootIterations) `
                -SysprepRetryCount      ([int]$startupState.SysprepRetryCount) `
                -CascadeExhaustionCount 0
            $script:CascadeExhaustionCountAtStartup = 0
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("Failed to reset CascadeExhaustionCount on clean startup: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
    } elseif ($script:CascadeExhaustionCountAtStartup -gt 0) {
        Write-FbLog ("Startup preserving CascadeExhaustionCount={0} (priorPhase='{1}', WatchdogRecoveryActive={2}); routing decision will follow." -f $script:CascadeExhaustionCountAtStartup, $priorPhase, $script:WatchdogRecoveryActive) 'WARN'
    }
} catch {
    Write-FbLog ("Cascade-exhaustion startup probe failed; continuing in normal mode: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    $script:CascadeExhaustionCountAtStartup = 0
}

# 2026.05.13.2213k-real-single-instance-and-real-multipass-and-builder-pathfix
# Bug Y-2: same deferral pattern as the InstallsCompleted shortcut
# above. We latch the cascade-exhaustion decision into a $script:
# variable; the actual Invoke-FbOobeHandoff call moves to the
# post-function-definition site so STAGE 1 inline-retry can resolve.
if ($script:CascadeExhaustionCountAtStartup -ge 2) {
    Write-FbLog ("Startup: CascadeExhaustionCount={0} >= 2 latched; deferring OOBE handoff to post-function-definitions site (Bug Y-2 in 2213j; fixed in 2213k)." -f $script:CascadeExhaustionCountAtStartup) 'ERROR'
    $script:DeferredStartupCascadeExhaustionHandoff = $true
} else {
    $script:DeferredStartupCascadeExhaustionHandoff = $false
}

if ($script:CascadeExhaustionCountAtStartup -eq 1) {
    Write-FbLog 'Startup: CascadeExhaustionCount=1; normal cascade will be intercepted inside Invoke-Reboot and re-routed to the SYSTEM-scoped scheduled-task emergency reboot path.' 'WARN'
}

$state           = Load-State

# 2026.05.13.2213c-onlogon-primary-and-defender-disable: startup
# recovery for the stuck-Defender-disabled state. If a prior session
# crashed (or was killed by an unexpected reboot, or by Tier 6
# bugcheck) AFTER disabling Defender but BEFORE restoring it, the
# defender-state.flag on disk says "disabled" and the device is
# running with Defender real-time monitoring OFF. If the loop's
# install pass has already completed on a prior boot
# (InstallsCompleted=$true on disk), there is no reason to keep
# Defender disabled - the install pass is logically done. Restore
# immediately so the device cannot ship to the end user with
# Defender off. Read the marker file, parse "disabled" vs
# "restored", and if disabled AND InstallsCompleted=$true, restore.
try {
    $defenderRecoveryMarker = $DefenderStateFlag
    if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $defenderRecoveryMarker) {
        $defenderRecoveryRaw = Get-Content -LiteralPath $defenderRecoveryMarker -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        $defenderRecoveryState = ''
        if ($defenderRecoveryRaw) {
            $defenderRecoveryState = ([string]$defenderRecoveryRaw).Split('|')[0].Trim().ToLowerInvariant()
        }
        $defenderRecoveryInstallsDone = $false
        try { $defenderRecoveryInstallsDone = [bool]$state.InstallsCompleted } catch {}
        if ($defenderRecoveryState -eq 'disabled' -and $defenderRecoveryInstallsDone) {
            Write-FbLog ("Startup recovery: defender-state.flag='{0}' AND InstallsCompleted=true on disk; restoring Defender real-time monitoring now (prior session disabled Defender then crashed before restoring). Marker raw='{1}'." -f $defenderRecoveryState, ([string]$defenderRecoveryRaw).Trim()) 'WARN'
            Set-FbDefenderRealtime -Enabled $true -Reason 'startup-recovery'
        } elseif ($defenderRecoveryState -eq 'disabled') {
            Write-FbLog ("Startup recovery: defender-state.flag='disabled' but InstallsCompleted=false; this loop run will continue with Defender disabled and restore at OOBE handoff. Marker raw='{0}'." -f ([string]$defenderRecoveryRaw).Trim()) 'INFO'
        } else {
            Write-FbLog ("Startup recovery: defender-state.flag='{0}' (no recovery needed). Marker raw='{1}'." -f $defenderRecoveryState, ([string]$defenderRecoveryRaw).Trim()) 'INFO'
        }
    } else {
        Write-FbLog 'Startup recovery: defender-state.flag absent (fresh image or no prior Defender state to recover).' 'INFO'
    }
} catch {
    Write-FbLog ("Startup recovery: defender-state.flag probe threw: {0}; continuing." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
}

$emptySweepCount = [int]$state.EmptySweepCount
$failureCount    = [int]$state.FailureCount
$startTime       = [string]$state.StartTime
$updateRetryMap  = @{}
if ($state.UpdateRetry) { $updateRetryMap = $state.UpdateRetry }
# 2026.05.12.1830-patient-shutdown-and-hresult-coverage: per-update
# retry budget map. Defaults to $MaxUpdateRetry for unknown HResults
# and 1 for HResults the bucket-apply code has classified as benign-
# reboot. Survives reboots so the lowered ceiling persists once
# applied. See Get-FbUpdateRetryBudget for the policy.
$updateRetryBudgetMap = @{}
if ($state.UpdateRetryBudget) {
    foreach ($p in @($state.UpdateRetryBudget.GetEnumerator())) {
        try { $updateRetryBudgetMap[[string]$p.Key] = [int]$p.Value } catch {}
    }
}
$givenUpSet = New-Object 'System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]' ([System.StringComparer]::OrdinalIgnoreCase)
if ($state.GivenUpUpdateIds) {
    foreach ($g in @($state.GivenUpUpdateIds)) {
        if ($g) { [void]$givenUpSet.Add([string]$g) }
    }
}
# 2026.05.18.2213l Bug FF: hydrate the SoftGivenUp counter map and the
# success-ledger HashSet from disk so they survive reboots. Both live in
# $script: scope so Invoke-FbBucketApply (per-update success path +
# Defender fast-path) and the per-pass post-scan promotion block can
# mutate them without needing by-ref plumbing. Every Save-State call
# that mutates them passes explicit values; every other Save-State call
# relies on the preserve-on-disk sentinel block in Save-State to carry
# the value forward unchanged.
$script:FbSoftGivenUpCountersLoop = @{}
if ($state.SoftGivenUpCounters) {
    foreach ($p in @($state.SoftGivenUpCounters.GetEnumerator())) {
        try { $script:FbSoftGivenUpCountersLoop[[string]$p.Key] = [int]$p.Value } catch {}
    }
}
$script:FbSuccessLedgerSet = New-Object 'System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]' ([System.StringComparer]::OrdinalIgnoreCase)
if ($state.SuccessLedgerIds) {
    foreach ($g in @($state.SuccessLedgerIds)) {
        if ($g) { [void]$script:FbSuccessLedgerSet.Add([string]$g) }
    }
}
if ($script:FbSoftGivenUpCountersLoop.Count -gt 0 -or $script:FbSuccessLedgerSet.Count -gt 0) {
    Write-FbLog ("Bug FF: loaded SoftGivenUpCounters={0} entry/entries, SuccessLedger={1} UpdateId(s) from prior boot(s)." -f $script:FbSoftGivenUpCountersLoop.Count, $script:FbSuccessLedgerSet.Count) 'INFO'
}
$bootCountByKey = @{}
if ($state.BootCountByKey) { $bootCountByKey = $state.BootCountByKey }
$bootCount = 0
if ($state.BootCount) { $bootCount = [int]$state.BootCount }
$forceHiddenSet = New-Object 'System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]' ([System.StringComparer]::OrdinalIgnoreCase)
if ($state.ForceHiddenKeys) {
    foreach ($g in @($state.ForceHiddenKeys)) {
        if ($g) { [void]$forceHiddenSet.Add([string]$g) }
    }
}
# Counter for reboots ISSUED by this loop in the current cycle, used
# to enforce $MaxRebootIterations. Distinct from $bootCount which
# counts process starts (the very first boot is not an issued reboot).
$rebootIterations = 0
if ($state.RebootIterations) { $rebootIterations = [int]$state.RebootIterations }
# Number of sysprep launch attempts in this cycle. Bumped by
# Invoke-SysprepToOobe. Carried through every Save-State call so we
# never accidentally reset it to zero from one of the many state writes
# that don't otherwise care about sysprep.
$sysprepRetryCount = 0
if ($state.SysprepRetryCount) { $sysprepRetryCount = [int]$state.SysprepRetryCount }
if ($givenUpSet.Count -gt 0) {
    Write-FbLog ("Loaded {0} terminally-skipped UpdateID(s) from prior runs; they will not be retried this cycle." -f $givenUpSet.Count) 'INFO'
}
if ($bootCountByKey.Count -gt 0) {
    Write-FbLog ("Loaded boot-counter map for {0} key(s); MaxKeyBootCount={1}." -f $bootCountByKey.Count, $MaxKeyBootCount) 'INFO'
}
# Bump the cumulative boot count once per process start (every fresh
# launch of this loop is either the first boot in a cycle or follows a
# reboot triggered by Invoke-Reboot). Persisted immediately so the
# count survives even if we crash before the first Save-State below.
$bootCount++
Write-FbLog ("Cumulative boot count for this update cycle: {0}" -f $bootCount) 'INFO'
Write-FbLog ("Loop-issued reboot iterations so far: {0} / {1}" -f $rebootIterations, $MaxRebootIterations) 'INFO'
try {
    $pipeMark = Join-Path $FbRoot 'wu-pipeline-started.utc'
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $pipeMark)) {
        Set-Content -LiteralPath $pipeMark -Value ((Get-Date).ToUniversalTime().ToString('o')) -Encoding ASCII -Force
    }
} catch {}
Update-UiFooterContext -BootCount $bootCount
# 2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve: prime the dashboard mirror BEFORE
# the first Save-UiState below so the FutileBootCount=N/M footer
# and the *** ESCAPE VALVE TRIPPED *** banner (if the latch was
# carried over from a prior boot) render correctly on the very
# first paint of this boot.
try { Update-UiEscapeValveContext } catch {}
# 2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve: if a prior boot already tripped
# the escape valve and we relaunched (e.g. RunOnce fired before
# sysprep took the box down, or a watchdog re-armed us), do NOT
# retry the doomed install cycle. Route straight back to OOBE
# handoff via Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff so the panic record /
# warning popup / "Failed - tech review required" labelling all
# stay consistent. The latch is the authoritative ground truth
# - the build 1700 evidence gate is intentionally bypassed.
$startupEscapeLatch = $false
try { $startupEscapeLatch = [bool]$state.EscapeValveTripped } catch {}
if ($startupEscapeLatch) {
    $startupEscapeReason = 'FUTILE_BOOTS_unknown'
    try { if ($state.EscapeValveReason) { $startupEscapeReason = [string]$state.EscapeValveReason } } catch {}
    $startupFutile = 0
    try { $startupFutile = [int]$state.FutileBootCount } catch {}
    $startupThresholdInfo = Get-FbFutileBootThreshold
    Write-FbLog ("Startup: EscapeValveTripped=true on disk (Reason={0}, FutileBootCount={1}). Re-entering Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff to repeat OOBE handoff." -f $startupEscapeReason, $startupFutile) 'ERROR'
    try { Initialize-UiStateFromDisk } catch {}
    Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff -Reason $startupEscapeReason -FutileNow $startupFutile -Threshold ([int]$startupThresholdInfo.Threshold)
    # Defensive: Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff -> Invoke-FbOobeHandoff
    # exits, but if for any reason it returns, exit cleanly so
    # RunOnce / ONSTART / ONLOGON do not re-launch.
    try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
    exit 0
}
# Persist the incremented BootCount immediately so the dashboard
# footer reflects the new value even if we crash before the first
# bucket apply runs.
Save-State `
    -EmptySweepCount   $emptySweepCount `
    -LastPass          ([string]$state.LastPass) `
    -StartTime         $startTime `
    -FailureCount      $failureCount `
    -UpdateRetry       $updateRetryMap `
    -GivenUpUpdateIds  @($givenUpSet) `
    -BootCountByKey    $bootCountByKey `
    -BootCount         $bootCount `
    -ForceHiddenKeys   @($forceHiddenSet) `
    -RebootIterations  $rebootIterations `
    -SysprepRetryCount $sysprepRetryCount

Initialize-UiStateFromDisk
try { Clear-FbOobeSoundUserRebootGate } catch {}
if ($script:UiUpdates.Count -eq 0) {
    Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Startup' -Message 'Initializing update service...'
} else {
    Write-FbLog ("Startup: kept {0} wu-status row(s) and phase='{1}' so splash is not blank." -f $script:UiUpdates.Count, [string]$script:UiPhase) 'INFO'
    Save-UiState
    try { Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase $script:UiPhase } catch {}
}

if (Test-Path $CompleteMarker) {
    Write-FbLog "Completion marker already present from prior run." 'INFO'
    # 2026.05.13.2213c-onlogon-primary-and-defender-disable: bulletproof
    # Defender restore on the CompletionMarker relaunch path. This block
    # has 4 exit branches (CBS-stuck exit 1, sysprep-success exit 0,
    # sysprep-failure exit 1, non-finalize exit 0) - all of them hand
    # the device toward end-user delivery. Restore here covers every
    # branch with one call. Idempotent.
    Set-FbDefenderRealtime -Enabled $true -Reason 'completion-marker-relaunch'
    if ($FinalizeToOobe) {
        $fbHwDoneRelaunch = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase '.hardware-gate-complete'
        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $fbHwDoneRelaunch)) {
            Write-FbLog 'CompletionMarker relaunch: hardware gate not complete; routing to Invoke-FbOobeHandoff so checks + Pass/Fail run before sysprep.' 'WARN'
            Invoke-FbOobeHandoff -Reason 'CompletionMarker relaunch: hardware checks still required before sysprep.'
            try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
            exit 0
        }
        # 2026.05.13.1900-two-stage-sysprep-handoff: relaunch after a
        # prior Invoke-FbOobeHandoff that dropped CompleteMarker.
        # The previous Invoke-SysprepToOobe may have refused with
        # 0x139f because CBS had not settled (build 1700 behavior
        # masked this; build 1900 -Wait launch surfaces it). Run
        # the same pre-sysprep CBS-settle wait that
        # Invoke-FbOobeHandoff uses so the FIRST sysprep call on
        # this boot has the same odds of succeeding as the
        # second-and-later attempts inside Invoke-FbOobeHandoff.
        # If the wait times out, exit 1 so RunOnce / ONSTART /
        # ONLOGON relaunch us; on the next boot we retry the wait.
        Write-FbLog 'CompletionMarker relaunch: starting pre-sysprep CBS-settle wait before invoking Sysprep.exe.' 'WARN'
        $relaunchSettled = $false
        try {
            $relaunchSettled = Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared -Context 'CompletionMarker relaunch'
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("CompletionMarker relaunch: Wait-FbPendingRebootCleared threw: {0}; treating as 'did not settle'." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
            $relaunchSettled = $false
        }
        if (-not $relaunchSettled) {
            Write-FbLog 'CompletionMarker relaunch: pre-sysprep CBS-settle wait did NOT clear; exiting 1 so RunOnce / ONSTART / ONLOGON relaunch us on the next boot for another retry.' 'ERROR'
            try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
            exit 1
        }
        $relaunchPower = 'Restart'
        if (Invoke-SysprepToOobe -Reason ('Completion marker detected. CBS settled; hardware-gate complete; sysprep {0}.' -f $relaunchPower) -PowerAction $relaunchPower) {
            # Invoke-SysprepToOobe's success path (build 1900 Fix 5)
            # already deleted the trigger layers + the kickstart
            # launcher batch + cleared PendingSysprep on disk.
            # These deletes are defensive belt-and-braces.
            schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $TaskName /F | Out-Null
            schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $LogonTaskName /F | Out-Null
            schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $Boot2TaskName /F | Out-Null
            schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $AnyLogonTaskName /F | Out-Null
            schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $SplashTaskName /F | Out-Null
            schtasks.exe /Delete /TN $SplashWatcherBoot2Name /F | Out-Null
            try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
            exit 0
        }
        # Build 1900 Invoke-SysprepToOobe's failure path already
        # wrote the panic dump, bumped SysprepRetryCount, re-
        # asserted PendingSysprep=true, re-armed triggers, and
        # tripped the escape valve if SysprepRetryCount>=cap.
        # All that remains is exit 1 so the loop relaunches.
        Write-FbLog 'CompletionMarker relaunch: sysprep handoff returned $false; exiting 1 so RunOnce / ONSTART / ONLOGON relaunch us on the next boot for another retry. Triggers remain armed (Fix 5).' 'ERROR'
        try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
        exit 1
    }
    try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
    exit 0
}

# 2289: If pipeline-completed OR firstbase-sealed marker exists, the device has already
# been finalized. Abort immediately — do NOT proceed to Invoke-FbOobeHandoff or any
# staging operation. This prevents WU loop re-launch from re-staging ProgramData scripts
# after the device has been sealed.
$fb2289Sealed = Join-Path $FbProgramDataFirstBase '.firstbase-sealed'
if ((Test-Path -LiteralPath $FbPipelineCompletedMarkerPath) -or (Test-Path -LiteralPath $fb2289Sealed)) {
    Write-FbLog ('2289: pipeline-completed or firstbase-sealed marker present — device already finalized. WU loop aborting to prevent re-staging.') 'WARN'
    try { Stop-Transcript | Out-Null } catch {}
    exit 0
}

Start-Sleep -Seconds 30
Wait-ForInternet -SleepSeconds 30
Initialize-FbWuEnvironment

# 2026.05.13.2213i-r-first-boot-bypass-and-w-console-render-script Bug R fix.
#
# Dump evidence on G:\fb-dump-732 (boot 1 only) AND G:\fb-dump-739
# (boots 1-3 with full reboot ladder trace) proved the 2213h pre-loop
# reboot bail-out fires unconditionally on EVERY fresh-image boot
# whenever Test-FbWuRebootRequired returns $true. The trace:
#
#   Boot 1, 07:30:52, PID 5864 (G:\fb-dump-739\Logs\WU-20260518-073051
#   .log L28 BootCount=1, L36 RealInstallsTotal=0, L41 PendingFileRename=
#   '*1\??\C:\Windows\SystemTemp\MicrosoftEdgeUpdate.exe.old9c7e | ...
#   | *1\??\C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft\EdgeUpdate\1.3.185.27 | '
#   -- pre-existing EdgeUpdate baggage from the WIM). Loop issues
#   shutdown.exe /r /f /t 60 at 07:31:31, ladder escalates Tier 1 ->
#   1B -> 2 -> 3 over 4 minutes before the OS actually reboots
#   (reboot-tier.log lines 1-4).
#
#   Boot 2, 07:36:28, PID 2736 (WU-073628.log L36 BootCount=2, L44
#   RealInstallsTotal=0, L49 PendingFileRename='' -- the Edge rename
#   completed across the reboot). But Test-FbWuRebootRequired STILL
#   returned True (CBS has its own pending-reboot keys plus 66MB
#   CbsPersist work in flight from the WIM's queued servicing); loop
#   bails AGAIN; watchdog-recovery skips to Tier 4 -> 5 (reboot-tier
#   .log lines 5-6, WU-073628.log lines 50-78). PID changes 5864->
#   2736 between Tier 3 and Tier 4 -- proves the device actually
#   rebooted again under Tier 4 WMI Win32Shutdown.
#
#   Boots 3+ (WU-073637, WU-073925, WU-073926): startup-recovery
#   active, BootCount=3..4, RealInstallsTotal still 0, log files all
#   truncate at the early-startup banner because the process was
#   killed by the next OS-driven reboot from the ladder above.
#
# Net effect: 4+ boots, 7+ minutes wall-clock, ZERO scans, ZERO
# installs. The device is stuck in a "pending reboot -> reboot -> still
# pending -> reboot" busy loop because the loop's pre-loop bail-out
# trusts the OS pending-reboot signal absolutely instead of requiring
# any prior install evidence from THIS pipeline.
#
# Fix. Bypass the pre-loop reboot for as long as RealInstallsTotal=0.
# Rationale: the loop's pending-reboot bail-out logic only makes sense
# when "the previous loop pass deposited an install that needs a
# reboot to commit". If we've never installed anything, the OS-side
# pending state CAN ONLY be pre-existing image baggage (EdgeUpdate,
# Defender platform updates queued during install media build, CBS
# settling its WIM-time servicing transactions, etc.) -- none of
# which gets resolved by another loop-driven reboot, but ALL of
# which gets resolved naturally by Windows over the next few minutes
# of boot/idle time. The scan/install pipeline below will work fine
# while CBS settles in the background; if a particular bucket-apply
# truly cannot commit until a reboot fires, Invoke-FbWuEndOfPass-
# Decision will see RealInstallsTotal>0 by that point and route
# through the legitimate Invoke-FbReboot path with proper evidence.
#
# Once even ONE per-update orcSucceeded result lands (RealInstalls-
# Total>0), the bypass disables and the legacy 2213h bail-out fires
# normally on subsequent boots -- those are precisely the cases where
# the pending state could legitimately come from a prior loop install
# pass and rebooting to commit is the right call.
#
# Conservative scope. Only the pre-loop bail-out at this site is
# bypassed. We do NOT skip Test-FbWuRebootRequired probes elsewhere,
# do NOT touch the end-of-pass reboot routing, do NOT touch sysprep
# handoff, do NOT touch the convergence gate, do NOT touch the 7-
# tier reboot ladder. Bugs M / N / O / P / Q from 2213h all apply
# downstream of this point byte-identically.
#
# We also keep the existing MaxRebootIterations cap as a belt-and-
# braces safety; if for any reason the bypass triggers but the loop
# still ends up issuing $MaxRebootIterations real reboots without
# installing anything, the legacy "stop trying" branch fires.
$_diskRealInstallsTotal = 0
try { $_diskRealInstallsTotal = [int]$state.RealInstallsTotal } catch { $_diskRealInstallsTotal = 0 }
$preLoopPendingRebootBypassNoInstallEvidence = ($_diskRealInstallsTotal -le 0)
if (Test-FbWuRebootRequired) {
    if ($preLoopPendingRebootBypassNoInstallEvidence) {
        # 2213i Bug R: log the pre-existing pending state for forensics
        # but DO NOT reboot. Proceed to scan+install. The end-of-pass
        # machinery will reboot if needed, with proper evidence.
        $_pfrSummary = ''
        try {
            $_smKey = 'HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session Manager'
            $_pfr   = (Get-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $_smKey -Name 'PendingFileRenameOperations' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).PendingFileRenameOperations
            if ($_pfr) {
                $_joined = (@($_pfr) -join ' | ')
                if ($_joined.Length -gt 240) { $_joined = $_joined.Substring(0,237) + '...' }
                $_pfrSummary = $_joined
            }
        } catch {}
        Write-FbLog ("2213i Bug R pre-loop pending-reboot bypass: Test-FbWuRebootRequired=true but RealInstallsTotal=0 on disk (BootCount={0}). Any pending state at this point is necessarily pre-existing image baggage, not deposited by this pipeline. Skipping pre-loop reboot and proceeding to scan/apply; end-of-pass decision will fire a legitimate reboot if an install pass actually requires one. PendingFileRenameOperations='{1}'." -f $bootCount, $_pfrSummary) 'WARN'
    } elseif ($rebootIterations -ge $MaxRebootIterations) {
        Write-FbLog ("Test-FbWuRebootRequired=true but already at MaxRebootIterations ({0}); skipping pre-loop reboot and proceeding to scan/apply." -f $MaxRebootIterations) 'WARN'
    } else {
        Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass 'pending-reboot' -StartTime $startTime -FailureCount $failureCount -UpdateRetry $updateRetryMap -GivenUpUpdateIds @($givenUpSet) -BootCountByKey $bootCountByKey -BootCount $bootCount -ForceHiddenKeys @($forceHiddenSet) -RebootIterations $rebootIterations -SysprepRetryCount $sysprepRetryCount
        Invoke-FbRebootWithFreshStateCheck -Reason 'pending reboot from previous pass'
    }
}

function Invoke-FbWuInlineRetryPass {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Run one inline retry pass against still-pending updates BEFORE
        falling back to a full reboot+rescan cycle.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.2213h-real-convergence-and-ti-serialization Bug P.

        Pre-2213h the loop fell into the "retrying after update cycle
        errors" path at the bottom of the script whenever Invoke-FbWu-
        EndOfPassDecision threw (which it did on EVERY pass thanks to
        Bug O's empty-collection crash). That path went DIRECTLY to
        Invoke-FbRebootWithFreshStateCheck without attempting any inline
        retry, so the next boot hit the SAME Trusted Installer batch
        collision (Bug M) and produced the SAME 0x80240016 cascade.
        The convergence loop was effectively a busy-wait around a
        deterministic batch-install bug.

        The 2213h fix has three layers:

          1. Bug M (engine): per-update serialization with backoff retry
             means a single bucket-apply is already a robust retry loop
             at the per-row level.
          2. Bug O (loop): EndOfPass no longer crashes on empty queue,
             so the catch-and-reboot path is no longer the dominant
             execution path.
          3. Bug P (this function): when the loop reaches the post-
             final-check error rails AND the OS does NOT explicitly
             demand a reboot, run one more inline bucket-apply pass
             against the still-pending updates. Only then, if the
             inline retry still leaves work, route to Invoke-Reboot.

        Returns a [pscustomobject] {
            Attempted (bool) - did we actually run a retry?
            Installed (int)  - count of orcSucceeded rows from the retry
            StillFailed (int) - count of rows that still failed after retry
            OsDemandsReboot (bool) - Test-FbWuRebootRequired at exit
        }
        Never throws. The caller decides whether to reboot based on
        OsDemandsReboot or its own context.
    #>
    [CmdletBinding()]
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$Reason,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$StartTime,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [hashtable]$RetryMap,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [AllowEmptyCollection()] [System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]]$GivenUpSet,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [hashtable]$BootCountByKey,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [AllowEmptyCollection()] [System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]]$ForceHiddenSet,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [int]$BootCount,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [int]$RebootIterations,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [int]$SysprepRetryCount,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [int]$FailureCount,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [hashtable]$UpdateRetryBudget,
        # 2026.05.19.2213m Bug FF reach: optional caller-supplied attempt
        # index so the SoftGivenUp helper's CallContext can distinguish
        # the first inline-retry from the third one. Defaults to 0 when
        # the caller doesn't track attempts (e.g. main-loop end-of-pass).
        [int]$AttemptIndex = 0
    )

    $result = [pscustomobject]@{
        Attempted       = $false
        Installed       = 0
        StillFailed     = 0
        OsDemandsReboot = $false
    }

    # If the OS explicitly demands a reboot, inline retry is pointless
    # (CBS owes us a reboot before any more installs will commit).
    try {
        if (Test-FbWuRebootRequired) {
            $result.OsDemandsReboot = $true
            Write-FbLog ("[inline-retry] Skipping inline retry: Test-FbWuRebootRequired=true (CBS owes a reboot before more installs commit). Reason='{0}'." -f $Reason) 'WARN'
            return $result
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("[inline-retry] Test-FbWuRebootRequired threw: {0}; assuming false and proceeding." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    Write-FbLog ("[inline-retry] Running inline retry pass BEFORE reboot. Reason='{0}'." -f $Reason) 'WARN'

    # Run a fresh scan to see what is still on offer. We classify the
    # results into the same 4 buckets as the main loop and call
    # Invoke-FbBucketApply on each non-empty one. The bucket apply
    # already runs per-update serialized (Bug M engine) with retry,
    # so this is a real chance to converge before reboot.
    $scanRows = @()
    try {
        $scanRows = @(Invoke-FbWuScan -TimeoutSeconds $RescanTimeoutSec)
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("[inline-retry] Invoke-FbWuScan threw: {0}; aborting inline retry." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        return $result
    }
    if ($scanRows.Count -eq 0) {
        Write-FbLog "[inline-retry] Fresh scan returned 0 applicable updates; inline retry is a no-op." 'INFO'
        return $result
    }

    # ==========================================================
    # 2026.05.19.2213m Bug FF reach: evaluate SoftGivenUp on the
    # inline-retry scan results too. Without this, a Defender
    # platform UpdateId pre-set to threshold by Mechanism 2 on
    # boot 1 that is STILL re-offered during the STAGE 1 inline
    # retry on boot N would never get promoted into the local
    # $GivenUpSet (the only Mechanism 1 site in 2213l was the
    # main-loop scan, which short-circuit boots skip entirely).
    # The helper mutates $GivenUpSet in place; the bucket-apply
    # call below already honors GivenUpSet via the standard
    # filter in Invoke-FbBucketApply.
    # ==========================================================
    try {
        $inlineContext = ("inline-retry-pass-{0}" -f $AttemptIndex)
        $null = Invoke-FbSoftGivenUpEvaluate -ScanResults @($scanRows) -GivenUpSet $GivenUpSet -CallContext $inlineContext
    } catch {
        try { Write-FbLog ("[inline-retry] Invoke-FbSoftGivenUpEvaluate threw: {0}; continuing with pre-evaluation GivenUpSet." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN' } catch {}
    }

    # Bucket the offered updates. Get-PassDefinitions provides the
    # canonical bucket order (Critical, Driver, Cumulative, Optional).
    $passes = Get-PassDefinitions
    $bucketed = @{}
    foreach ($p in $passes) { $bucketed[$p.Name] = @() }
    foreach ($u in $scanRows) {
        $bk = [string]$u.Bucket
        if ($bucketed.ContainsKey($bk)) { $bucketed[$bk] += ,$u }
    }

    $result.Attempted = $true
    $totalInstalled = 0
    $totalStillFailed = 0

    foreach ($p in $passes) {
        $bucketRows = @($bucketed[$p.Name])
        if ($bucketRows.Count -eq 0) { continue }
        Write-FbLog ("[inline-retry] Bucket {0}: applying {1} update(s) inline (no reboot interleaved)." -f $p.Name, $bucketRows.Count) 'WARN'
        $bucketRes = $null
        try {
            $bucketRes = Invoke-FbBucketApply `
                -BucketName $p.Name `
                -BucketUpdates $bucketRows `
                -RetryMap $RetryMap `
                -GivenUpSet $GivenUpSet `
                -StartTime $StartTime `
                -FailureCount $FailureCount `
                -HaltOnFailure $false `
                -BootCountByKey $BootCountByKey `
                -ForceHiddenSet $ForceHiddenSet `
                -BootCount $BootCount `
                -RebootIterations $RebootIterations `
                -SysprepRetryCount $SysprepRetryCount `
                -UpdateRetryBudget $UpdateRetryBudget
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("[inline-retry] Invoke-FbBucketApply threw for bucket {0}: {1}; continuing to next bucket." -f $p.Name, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
            continue
        }
        if ($bucketRes) {
            $totalInstalled += [int]$bucketRes.Succeeded
            $totalStillFailed += [int]$bucketRes.Failed
            if ($bucketRes.RebootRequired -or $bucketRes.ForcePendingRestart) {
                Write-FbLog ("[inline-retry] Bucket {0} signalled reboot required after apply; halting inline retry." -f $p.Name) 'WARN'
                break
            }
        }
    }

    $result.Installed = $totalInstalled
    $result.StillFailed = $totalStillFailed
    try { $result.OsDemandsReboot = [bool](Test-FbWuRebootRequired) } catch {}

    Write-FbLog ("[inline-retry] Pass complete: installed={0} stillFailed={1} OsDemandsReboot={2}." -f $result.Installed, $result.StillFailed, $result.OsDemandsReboot) 'WARN'
    return $result
}

function Invoke-FbBucketApply {
    <#
        Apply a single classifier bucket from the unified scan result.
        Returns a hashtable describing the bucket outcome. The caller
        decides whether to halt the iteration entirely or roll forward
        to the next bucket (greedy install model).

        Retry-map keying (2026-05-11):
        ------------------------------
        The retry counter is keyed via Get-FbRetryKey, which prefers the
        KB article number when available and falls back to UpdateID
        only when WUA gives us no KB. This is what makes the retry
        ceiling actually accumulate for cumulative updates whose
        UpdateID/RevisionNumber rolls forward each rescan (the previous
        build keyed on UpdateID, so a superseded LCU would re-enter
        the loop at retry=0 every scan and pin at Retrying forever).

        Per-update lifecycle (deterministic - no infinite Retrying paths):

          ResultCode in $ValidInstallCodes (2 or 3)
            -> success: zero retry, mark Completed.
          HResult in $BenignHResultMap with Action='Skip'
            -> terminal skip: zero retry, mark Completed with reason
               in Detail, add key to $GivenUpSet so it is skipped on
               subsequent rescans even if WUA re-offers under a new UID.
          HResult in $BenignHResultMap with Action='Reboot' (or HResult
            in $ExplicitRebootHResults)
            -> mark Pending restart, force iteration to reboot, do NOT
               consume a retry slot. ExplicitReboot=$true on outcome
               so the caller knows to halt the iteration.
          HResult in $BenignHResultMap with Action='Retry'
            -> consume one retry; if already at the ceiling, treat as Skip.
          Anything else (ResultCode 4 or unknown HResult)
            -> increment retry. If retry exceeds $MaxUpdateRetry, persist
               retry pinned at the ceiling (NOT zero), write STUCK-DIAG,
               add to $GivenUpSet, mark Pending restart, force reboot.
               Critically: does NOT set ExplicitReboot, so the caller
               can still attempt remaining buckets on this iteration
               before issuing the reboot.

        Halt-on-failure semantics (2026-05-11):
        ---------------------------------------
        Outcome.Halted is set ONLY when ExplicitReboot is true (i.e.
        the failure mode explicitly demands a reboot before any further
        progress is possible). For plain retryable failures we set
        Failed but NOT Halted - the iteration loop will continue to the
        next bucket so we install everything we can before consuming a
        reboot. This implements the user's "install everything you can
        before the first reboot" policy.

        Escape-hatch (2026-05-11):
        --------------------------
        BootCountByKey accumulates one per (key, reboot) pair across
        the cycle. When a key crosses $MaxKeyBootCount AND its update
        is still being offered, we call Hide-FbOfferedUpdate to force-
        hide it via IUpdate.IsHidden=$true and mark Completed with a
        "Force-hidden after N boots" note in Detail. This guarantees
        eventual convergence even for genuinely unkillable updates.

        $GivenUpSet, $BootCountByKey, $ForceHiddenSet are passed in by
        reference and persisted to wu-state.json by the caller via
        Save-State so terminal decisions survive reboots.
    #>
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$BucketName,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [AllowEmptyCollection()] [object[]]$BucketUpdates,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [hashtable]$RetryMap,
        # AllowEmptyCollection: at the start of the workflow the set is
        # empty (nothing has been terminally classified yet). Without
        # this attribute, PowerShell's parameter binder rejects an empty
        # HashSet because [Parameter(Mandatory)] implicitly forbids
        # empty collections.
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [AllowEmptyCollection()] [System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]]$GivenUpSet,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$StartTime,
        [int]$FailureCount = 0,
        [bool]$HaltOnFailure = $false,
        [hashtable]$BootCountByKey = @{},
        [AllowEmptyCollection()] [System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]]$ForceHiddenSet = $null,
        [int]$BootCount = 0,
        [int]$RebootIterations = 0,
        [int]$SysprepRetryCount = 0,
        # 2026.05.12.1830-patient-shutdown-and-hresult-coverage:
        # per-update retry ceiling map keyed via Get-FbRetryKey.
        # Updates whose HResult is in the BenignHResultMap with
        # Action='Reboot' get budget=1 (reboot-then-retry is the
        # only realistic path to success); everything else stays
        # at $MaxUpdateRetry. Mutated in-place and round-tripped
        # back to disk via Save-State so the lowered ceiling
        # persists across reboots / iterations.
        [hashtable]$UpdateRetryBudget = @{}
    )

    if ($null -eq $ForceHiddenSet) {
        $ForceHiddenSet = New-Object 'System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]' ([System.StringComparer]::OrdinalIgnoreCase)
    }

    # 2026.05.13.2213h-real-convergence-and-ti-serialization Bug N
    # ROOT CAUSE: pre-2213h $outcome used [ordered]@{} which is an
    # OrderedDictionary. PowerShell's $outcome.PSObject.Properties.Name
    # on an OrderedDictionary returns the dictionary's METADATA
    # properties (Count, Keys, Values, IsReadOnly, ...), NOT its keys.
    # So Update-FbWuWorkQueueFromBucketResults's BUG-H2 GUARD check
    # (line ~9710 in the 2213g build, see RELEASE.txt for full
    # post-mortem) of
    #     $BucketApplyResult.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'UpdateResults'
    # ALWAYS returned $false even though $outcome.UpdateResults was
    # populated and accessible via dot-indexer. The guard then logged
    # "UpdateResults is missing or empty" and silently skipped the
    # WorkQueue update for EVERY bucket on EVERY boot. End-of-pass
    # decision saw 0 pending updates -> empty-collection crash ->
    # main loop caught it as "WU error" -> reboot-without-retry ->
    # next boot saw InstallsCompleted=true -> handoff to OOBE with
    # 38 updates still on offer. F:\fb-dump-final-1347\Logs\
    # WU-20260515-114810.log lines 60-155 are the smoking gun.
    #
    # FIX: $outcome is now a [pscustomobject] (PSCustomObject's
    # PSObject.Properties.Name DOES enumerate field names). All other
    # access patterns ($outcome.Name = ...) work identically because
    # [pscustomobject]@{} supports field assignment.
    $outcome = [pscustomobject]@{
        Name                = $BucketName
        Targeted            = 0
        Succeeded           = 0
        Failed              = 0
        Skipped             = 0
        RebootRequired      = $false
        ForcePendingRestart = $false
        # ExplicitReboot is the new gate that controls Halted. True
        # only when the failure path is "WUA explicitly says reboot
        # now" (RebootRequired, ForcePendingRestart triggered by an
        # Action='Reboot' HResult, or an HResult in the explicit allowlist).
        ExplicitReboot      = $false
        Halted              = $false
        Error               = ''
        # 2213d Bug H1 / 2213h Bug N: the per-update result array
        # returned by Invoke-FbWuApply. Populated below AFTER
        # Invoke-FbWuApply returns successfully; also populated in
        # the catch block (synthesized failure rows per targeted UID)
        # so the WorkQueue updater never sees an empty array when
        # BucketUpdates is non-empty.
        UpdateResults       = @()
    }

    if (-not $BucketUpdates -or $BucketUpdates.Count -eq 0) {
        Write-FbLog "[$BucketName] no updates in this bucket; skipping."
        return $outcome
    }

    # Filter out anything we have already given up on. We check membership
    # by BOTH the UpdateID (legacy entries from prior builds) AND by the
    # KB-keyed Get-FbRetryKey result (new entries since 2026-05-11). Same
    # idea for the force-hidden set.
    #
    # 2026.05.18.2213l Bug FF Mechanism 2: ALSO short-circuit any Defender
    # platform update whose UpdateId already lives in
    # $script:FbDefenderInstalledThisBoot (per-boot in-memory tracker
    # populated by the per-update success path below). Defender platform
    # updates return ResultCode=2 but stay applicable; retrying within
    # the same boot is wasted work and feeds the sticky-residual loop.
    $skippedFromPrior   = @()
    $skippedFromDefender = @()
    $bucketUpdatesActive = @()
    foreach ($u in $BucketUpdates) {
        $uid = [string]$u.UpdateId
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($uid)) { continue }
        $key = Get-FbRetryKey -Update $u
        $defenderDedupe = $false
        try {
            if ($null -ne $script:FbDefenderPlatformGuids -and ($script:FbDefenderPlatformGuids -contains $uid)) {
                if ($null -ne $script:FbDefenderInstalledThisBoot -and $script:FbDefenderInstalledThisBoot.ContainsKey($uid) -and [bool]$script:FbDefenderInstalledThisBoot[$uid]) {
                    $defenderDedupe = $true
                }
            }
        } catch {}
        $skippedFromLedger = $false
        try {
            if ($null -ne $script:FbSuccessLedgerSet -and $script:FbSuccessLedgerSet.Contains($uid)) {
                $skippedFromLedger = $true
            }
        } catch {}
        if ($GivenUpSet.Contains($uid) -or $GivenUpSet.Contains($key) -or $ForceHiddenSet.Contains($uid) -or $ForceHiddenSet.Contains($key)) {
            $skippedFromPrior += $u
        } elseif ($defenderDedupe) {
            $skippedFromDefender += $u
        } elseif ($skippedFromLedger) {
            $skippedFromPrior += $u
        } else {
            $bucketUpdatesActive += $u
        }
    }
    foreach ($u in $skippedFromPrior) {
        $uid = [string]$u.UpdateId
        $key = Get-FbRetryKey -Update $u
        $title = [string]$u.Title; if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($title)) { $title = $uid }
        $reason = if ($ForceHiddenSet.Contains($uid) -or $ForceHiddenSet.Contains($key)) {
            'Force-hidden via IUpdate.IsHidden=true (escape hatch); skipped permanently.'
        } elseif ($null -ne $script:FbSuccessLedgerSet -and $script:FbSuccessLedgerSet.Contains($uid)) {
            'Skipped: already in success ledger; will not re-download or re-install.'
        } else {
            'Skipped: terminally classified in prior run; not retried.'
        }
        Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title $title -Status 'Completed' -Retries 0 -Detail $reason
        $outcome.Skipped++
    }
    foreach ($u in $skippedFromDefender) {
        $uid = [string]$u.UpdateId
        $title = [string]$u.Title; if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($title)) { $title = $uid }
        Write-FbLog ("Defender fast-path: skipping {0} ({1}) - already installed once this boot (per-boot dedupe; Bug FF Mechanism 2)" -f $title, $uid) 'WARN'
        Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title $title -Status 'Completed' -Retries 0 -Detail 'Defender platform update already installed once this boot (Bug FF fast-path dedupe).'
        $outcome.Skipped++
    }
    if ($skippedFromPrior.Count -gt 0) {
        Write-FbLog ("[{0}] {1} update(s) re-offered by WUA but already terminally skipped/hidden; honoring prior decision." -f $BucketName, $skippedFromPrior.Count) 'INFO'
    }
    if ($skippedFromDefender.Count -gt 0) {
        Write-FbLog ("[{0}] {1} Defender platform update(s) skipped via Bug FF Mechanism 2 fast-path (already installed this boot)." -f $BucketName, $skippedFromDefender.Count) 'INFO'
    }

    $ids = @()
    foreach ($u in $bucketUpdatesActive) {
        $uid = [string]$u.UpdateId
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($uid)) { continue }
        $ids += $uid
        $title = [string]$u.Title; if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($title)) { $title = $uid }
        $key = Get-FbRetryKey -Update $u
        if (-not $RetryMap.ContainsKey($key)) { $RetryMap[$key] = 0 }
        Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title $title -Status 'Pending install' -Retries ([int]$RetryMap[$key])
    }

    if ($ids.Count -eq 0) {
        return $outcome
    }

    $outcome.Targeted = $ids.Count
    Write-FbLog "[$BucketName] applying $($ids.Count) update(s) (HaltOnFailure=$HaltOnFailure)"
    Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Downloading' -Message ("Downloading and installing {0} update(s)..." -f $ids.Count)
    foreach ($u in $bucketUpdatesActive) {
        $uid = [string]$u.UpdateId
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($uid)) { continue }
        $title = [string]$u.Title; if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($title)) { $title = $uid }
        $key = Get-FbRetryKey -Update $u
        Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title $title -Status 'Downloading' -Retries ([int]$RetryMap[$key])
    }

    # Build a title lookup so the progress callback can refresh the
    # per-update Status string (XAML binds "Status" only, no Detail) and
    # preserve the existing title + retry counter we just stamped in the
    # row. The callback is invoked from within Invoke-FbWuApply's polling
    # wait, so it runs in this same runspace and has full access to the
    # loop's UI helpers (Set-UiUpdateStatus / Set-UiPhase).
    $titleByUid = @{}
    $retryByUid = @{}
    foreach ($u in $bucketUpdatesActive) {
        $uid = [string]$u.UpdateId
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($uid)) { continue }
        $titleByUid[$uid] = if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($u.Title)) { $uid } else { [string]$u.Title }
        $retryByUid[$uid] = [int]$RetryMap[(Get-FbRetryKey -Update $u)]
    }
    $bucketNameForCallback = $BucketName

    $progressCallback = {
        param($p)
        if (-not $p) { return }
        try {
            $phase = [string]$p.phase
            $idx0  = 0
            try { $idx0 = [int]$p.currentIndex } catch {}
            $idx1  = $idx0 + 1
            $total = 0
            try { $total = [int]$p.totalCount } catch {}
            $title = ''
            try { $title = [string]$p.currentTitle } catch {}
            $uid = ''
            try { $uid = [string]$p.currentUpdateId } catch {}
            $pct = 0
            try { $pct = [int]$p.percent } catch {}
            $tPct = 0
            try { $tPct = [int]$p.totalPercent } catch {}
            $bytesDone  = [int64]0
            $bytesTotal = [int64]0
            try { $bytesDone  = [int64]$p.bytesDone } catch {}
            try { $bytesTotal = [int64]$p.bytesTotal } catch {}

            # MB rounded to whole numbers; XAML column is narrow so we
            # keep the string compact (e.g. "245MB / 800MB").
            $mbDone  = [math]::Round(($bytesDone  / 1MB), 0)
            $mbTotal = [math]::Round(($bytesTotal / 1MB), 0)

            # 2224: No percent in splash-bound status (operator + narrow columns); MB counts only when known.
            $rowStatus = if ($phase -eq 'Downloading') {
                if ($bytesTotal -gt 0) {
                    "Downloading {0}MB / {1}MB" -f $mbDone, $mbTotal
                } else {
                    'Downloading...'
                }
            } elseif ($phase -eq 'Installing') {
                'Installing...'
            } else {
                $phase
            }

            if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($uid) -and $titleByUid.ContainsKey($uid)) {
                $rowTitle = [string]$titleByUid[$uid]
                $rowRetry = [int]$retryByUid[$uid]
                Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title $rowTitle -Status $rowStatus -Retries $rowRetry
            }

            # Top-level phase follows the engine (Downloading/Installing), not the
            # bucket name, so the splash STATUS pill cannot stay stuck on Completed
            # (or Critical/Driver) while a new pass is actually installing.
            $msg = if ($phase -eq 'Downloading') {
                if ($bytesTotal -gt 0) {
                    "Downloading {0} of {1}: {2} - {3}MB / {4}MB" -f $idx1, $total, $title, $mbDone, $mbTotal
                } else {
                    "Downloading {0} of {1}: {2}" -f $idx1, $total, $title
                }
            } elseif ($phase -eq 'Installing') {
                "Installing {0} of {1}: {2}" -f $idx1, $total, $title
            } else {
                "{0} {1} of {2}" -f $phase, $idx1, $total
            }
            $phaseForUi = if ($phase -eq 'Installing' -or $phase -eq 'Downloading') { $phase } else { $bucketNameForCallback }
            Set-UiPhase -Phase $phaseForUi -Message $msg
        } catch {
            # Best-effort UI; never let progress reporting break the apply.
        }
    }.GetNewClosure()

    $applyResult = $null
    try {
        $applyResult = Invoke-FbWuApply -UpdateIds $ids -TimeoutSeconds $ApplyTimeoutSec -ProgressCallback $progressCallback
        # 2213d Bug H1: propagate the per-update result array into
        # the outcome on the success path so callers
        # (Update-FbWuWorkQueueFromBucketResults in particular) can
        # read it from the returned $outcome object instead of
        # re-calling Invoke-FbWuApply. Pre-2213d the field was simply
        # absent and every successful install ended up classified as
        # 'Retry next boot' by the WorkQueue updater - see post-mortem
        # at F:\fb-dump-final-0635\wu-state.json (41 entries all
        # LastHResult=0 RetryCount=1, despite INSTALL-DIAG receipts
        # proving RC=2 HR=0 from WUA). Best-effort: cast to array,
        # default to empty if applyResult has no UpdateResults field.
        try {
            if ($applyResult -and $applyResult.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'UpdateResults' -and $applyResult.UpdateResults) {
                # 2026.05.13.2213h-real-convergence-and-ti-serialization:
                # enrich every per-update row with BucketName and
                # RetryCount so the WorkQueue updater + downstream
                # diagnostics see a uniform contract regardless of which
                # bucket the update came from. The engine returns
                # Title/UpdateId/ResultCode/HResult; we add the loop-side
                # context here.
                $retryByUidLocal = @{}
                foreach ($u2 in $bucketUpdatesActive) {
                    $uid2 = [string]$u2.UpdateId
                    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($uid2)) { continue }
                    $retryByUidLocal[$uid2] = [int]$RetryMap[(Get-FbRetryKey -Update $u2)]
                }
                $enriched = @()
                foreach ($r in @($applyResult.UpdateResults)) {
                    $ruid = ''
                    try { $ruid = [string]$r.UpdateId } catch {}
                    $rretry = 0
                    if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($ruid) -and $retryByUidLocal.ContainsKey($ruid)) {
                        $rretry = [int]$retryByUidLocal[$ruid]
                    }
                    $enriched += [pscustomobject]@{
                        Title      = [string]$r.Title
                        UpdateId   = $ruid
                        ResultCode = [int]$r.ResultCode
                        HResult    = [int]$r.HResult
                        BucketName = $BucketName
                        RetryCount = $rretry
                    }
                }
                $outcome.UpdateResults = $enriched
            }
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("[{0}] UpdateResults propagation threw: {1}" -f $BucketName, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
        # 2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence Fix G: scan the
        # returned per-update array for the WU-20260512-110026.log
        # regression signature - every row reporting either the
        # MINUS_ONE marker HResult (0x8024000B repurposed by the
        # engine's MINUS_ONE_BUG path) OR a legitimate ResultCode=-1
        # leak that somehow survived the engine's Fix A double-confirm.
        # If we see it, write a panic dump pointing at the engine's
        # STUCK-DIAG-RESULTCODE-MINUS-ONE-*.json so the operator can
        # correlate both side by side, and surface a hard error log
        # so the silent-bypass class of bug becomes loud.
        try {
            if ($applyResult -and $applyResult.UpdateResults) {
                $minusOneRows = 0
                $totalRows = 0
                foreach ($r in @($applyResult.UpdateResults)) {
                    $totalRows++
                    $rcCheck = $null
                    try { $rcCheck = [int]$r.ResultCode } catch {}
                    $hrCheck = $null
                    try { $hrCheck = [int]$r.HResult } catch {}
                    # rcCheck=-1 is the legacy bug signature; hrCheck=0x8024000B
                    # is the engine's marker for "MINUS_ONE_BUG synthesized
                    # orcFailed". Either way we want to dump.
                    if ($rcCheck -eq -1 -or $hrCheck -eq 0x8024000B) {
                        $minusOneRows++
                    }
                }
                if ($totalRows -gt 0 -and $minusOneRows -eq $totalRows) {
                    Write-FbLog ("[{0}] PANIC: every per-update result row carries the MINUS_ONE bug signature ({1}/{2}). Treating apply as orcFailed; latch will NOT fire." -f $BucketName, $minusOneRows, $totalRows) 'ERROR'
                    try {
                        $stamp = (Get-Date).ToString('yyyyMMdd-HHmmss')
                        $panicDir = Join-Path $LogDir 'PanicDumps'
                        if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $panicDir)) {
                            New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $panicDir -Force | Out-Null
                        }
                        $panicFile = Join-Path $panicDir ("STUCK-DIAG-RESULTCODE-MINUS-ONE-LOOP-{0}.json" -f $stamp)
                        $panicPayload = [pscustomobject]@{
                            Timestamp        = (Get-Date).ToString('o')
                            Bucket           = $BucketName
                            Targeted         = $applyResult.Targeted
                            Found            = $applyResult.Found
                            InstalledCount   = $applyResult.Installed
                            DownloadResult   = $applyResult.DownloadResultCode
                            InstallResult    = $applyResult.InstallResultCode
                            UpdateResults    = @($applyResult.UpdateResults)
                            ScriptVersion    = $ScriptVersion
                            RealInstallsThisBoot = $script:RealInstallsThisBoot
                        }
                        $panicPayload | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 6 | Set-Content -Path $panicFile -Encoding UTF8 -Force
                        Write-FbLog ("[{0}] Wrote MINUS_ONE_BUG panic dump: {1}" -f $BucketName, $panicFile) 'ERROR'
                        try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'bucket-apply' -Message ("PANIC: every {0} update returned ResultCode=-1 / HResult=MINUS_ONE_MARKER. NOT latching. See {1}" -f $BucketName, $panicFile) } catch {}
                    } catch {
                        Write-FbLog ("[{0}] Failed to write MINUS_ONE_BUG panic dump: {1}" -f $BucketName, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
                    }
                }
            }
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("[{0}] MINUS_ONE_BUG sweep threw: {1}" -f $BucketName, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
    } catch {
        $outcome.Error = $_.Exception.Message
        Write-FbLog "[$BucketName] COM apply failed: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'ERROR'
        # 2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence: REMOVED the
        # pre-1700 rail that latched InstallsCompleted on any COM
        # apply exception. The exception could fire for reasons
        # that prove ZERO real install activity (engine-internal
        # init failure, COM marshaling error, mutex contention,
        # etc.). Latching here was a silent-bypass amplifier:
        # field log WU-20260512-110026.log showed the latch firing
        # on a Critical bucket where all 4 updates returned
        # ResultCode=-1, yet the latch hit OOBE handoff. The
        # exception path now ONLY does retry/Pending-restart UI
        # bookkeeping - no latch.
        # COM apply itself blew up (timeout, RPC failure, COM init) - bump
        # every key's retry by one and let the ceiling enforce termination.
        # NOTE: this is NOT an explicit-reboot path; greedy fall-through to
        # next bucket is still allowed.
        foreach ($u in $bucketUpdatesActive) {
            $uid = [string]$u.UpdateId
            if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($uid)) { continue }
            $key = Get-FbRetryKey -Update $u
            $retry = [int]$RetryMap[$key] + 1
            $RetryMap[$key] = $retry
            $itemTitle = [string]$u.Title; if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($itemTitle)) { $itemTitle = $uid }
            # 2026.05.12.1830: per-update retry ceiling. COM apply
            # exception has HResult=0 here so the budget reflects
            # any prior classification (the persisted entry in
            # $UpdateRetryBudget); for an update never classified
            # before, this returns $MaxUpdateRetry.
            $perKeyCeil = Get-FbUpdateRetryBudget -Key $key -HResult 0 -BudgetMap $UpdateRetryBudget
            if ($retry -gt $perKeyCeil) {
                $RetryMap[$key] = $perKeyCeil
                [void]$GivenUpSet.Add($key)
                [void]$GivenUpSet.Add($uid)
                $cats = @()
                if ($u.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'Categories') {
                    $cats = @($u.Categories)
                }
                Write-StuckDiag -UpdateId $uid -Title $itemTitle -ResultCode -1 -HResult 0 -Categories $cats -Reason ("COM apply failure exceeded retry ceiling (perKey={0}): {1}" -f $perKeyCeil, $_.Exception.Message)
                $detail = Format-FbDetail -HResult 0 -Retry $perKeyCeil -MaxRetry $perKeyCeil -Reason ('COM apply error: ' + $_.Exception.Message) -Prefix 'Exceeded retry threshold;'
                Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title $itemTitle -Status 'Pending restart' -Retries $perKeyCeil -Detail $detail
                $outcome.ForcePendingRestart = $true
            } else {
                $detail = Format-FbDetail -HResult 0 -Retry $retry -MaxRetry $perKeyCeil -Reason ('COM apply error: ' + $_.Exception.Message)
                Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title $itemTitle -Status 'Retrying' -Retries $retry -Detail $detail
            }
        }
        $outcome.Failed = $ids.Count
        # 2026.05.13.2213h-real-convergence-and-ti-serialization Bug M2:
        # populate UpdateResults in the COM-exception path. The pre-2213h
        # behaviour left UpdateResults=@() here; Update-FbWuWorkQueue-
        # FromBucketResults's BUG-H2 GUARD then silently skipped the
        # WorkQueue update, dropping every failure row from convergence
        # tracking. The new contract: every targeted row gets a
        # synthesized {Title, UpdateId, ResultCode=4, HResult=exn} entry
        # so the WorkQueue updater can see the rows and classify them
        # correctly (Class='Unknown' -> Status='Retry next boot').
        $exnHr = if ($_.Exception -and $_.Exception.HResult) { [int]$_.Exception.HResult } else { 0 }
        $synthesizedRows = @()
        foreach ($u in $bucketUpdatesActive) {
            $uid = [string]$u.UpdateId
            if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($uid)) { continue }
            $itemTitle = [string]$u.Title; if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($itemTitle)) { $itemTitle = $uid }
            $synthesizedRows += [pscustomobject]@{
                Title      = $itemTitle
                UpdateId   = $uid
                ResultCode = 4   # orcFailed
                HResult    = $exnHr
                BucketName = $BucketName
                RetryCount = [int]$RetryMap[(Get-FbRetryKey -Update $u)]
            }
        }
        $outcome.UpdateResults = $synthesizedRows
        Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass ("{0}-apply-error" -f $BucketName) -StartTime $StartTime -FailureCount $FailureCount -UpdateRetry $RetryMap -GivenUpUpdateIds @($GivenUpSet) -BootCountByKey $BootCountByKey -BootCount $BootCount -ForceHiddenKeys @($ForceHiddenSet) -RebootIterations $RebootIterations -SysprepRetryCount $SysprepRetryCount -UpdateRetryBudget $UpdateRetryBudget -SoftGivenUpCounters $script:FbSoftGivenUpCountersLoop -SuccessLedgerIds @($script:FbSuccessLedgerSet)
        try {
            Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'bucket-apply' -Message ("COM apply exception in {0} ({1} update(s)); latch forced." -f $BucketName, $ids.Count)
        } catch {}
        # No ExplicitReboot here - COM apply failures are retryable, not
        # explicit reboot requests. Halted stays false so the caller can
        # roll forward to the next bucket on this iteration.
        return $outcome
    }

    foreach ($u in $bucketUpdatesActive) {
        $uid = [string]$u.UpdateId
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($uid)) { continue }
        $title = [string]$u.Title; if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($title)) { $title = $uid }
        $key = Get-FbRetryKey -Update $u
        Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title $title -Status 'Installing' -Retries ([int]$RetryMap[$key])
    }
    try { Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase $BucketName } catch {}
    # 2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence: REMOVED the pre-1700
    # "reached Installing UI status" latch rail that produced the
    # silent bypass in WU-20260512-110026.log. That rail latched
    # InstallsCompleted on a cosmetic UI status string update
    # BEFORE BeginInstall returned any real result. Combined with
    # the polling bug in Invoke-FbWuApply that returned
    # ResultCode=-1 for every update, the rail caused the latch
    # to fire with ZERO actual installs and the next reboot to
    # route straight to sysprep/OOBE. The latch now ONLY fires
    # on a real orcSucceeded/orcSucceededWithErrors per-update
    # result inside the foreach below.

    $perResults = @{}
    foreach ($r in @($applyResult.UpdateResults)) {
        $perResults[[string]$r.UpdateId] = $r
    }

    # 2026.05.12.1830-patient-shutdown-and-hresult-coverage Fix 4:
    # Track every HResult that ends up at Status='Retrying' with a
    # non-benign (unknown) HResult during this bucket. If more than
    # half of the targeted updates land in this state, the dashboard
    # surfaces a "retry storm" message with the HResult list so the
    # operator understands the in-flight failure mode without log
    # access. Benign-Retry-action HResults are tracked separately
    # (they are still retries but on KNOWN codes); we mention them
    # in a secondary part of the message when present.
    $retryStormUnknownHResults = New-Object 'System.Collections.Generic.List[string]'
    $retryStormBenignHResults  = New-Object 'System.Collections.Generic.List[string]'

    foreach ($u in $bucketUpdatesActive) {
        try { Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase $BucketName } catch {}
        $uid = [string]$u.UpdateId
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($uid)) { continue }
        $origTitle = [string]$u.Title; if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($origTitle)) { $origTitle = $uid }
        $key = Get-FbRetryKey -Update $u
        $resultEntry = $null
        if ($perResults.ContainsKey($uid)) { $resultEntry = $perResults[$uid] }
        $code = if ($resultEntry) { [int]$resultEntry.ResultCode } else { -1 }
        $hr   = if ($resultEntry) { [int]$resultEntry.HResult }   else { 0 }
        $hex  = Format-Hex32 -Value $hr

        # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: per-driver
        # first-encounter dump. Only fires for the Driver bucket and
        # only once per UpdateID per boot. Captures the IUpdate(2/5)
        # driver props so the next 2200 incident has data to expand
        # BenignHResultMap with driver-specific HResults. The build
        # 1915 incident never reached the Driver bucket (loop short-
        # circuited to OOBE after Critical), so we have ZERO field
        # data on driver behaviour.
        if ($BucketName -eq 'Driver') {
            try {
                $kbForDiag = ''
                if ($u.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'KB' -and $u.KB) { $kbForDiag = [string]$u.KB }
                $exnForDiag = $null
                if ($resultEntry -and $resultEntry.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'Exception') {
                    $exnForDiag = $resultEntry.Exception
                }
                Write-FbDriverFirstEncounterDiag -UpdateId $uid -Title $origTitle -KB $kbForDiag `
                    -ResultCode $code -HResult $hr -UpdateComObject $u -UpdateExceptionInfo $exnForDiag
            } catch {
                Write-FbLog ("Driver first-encounter dump threw for '{0}': {1}" -f $origTitle, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
            }
        }

        # Stamp the HResult into the Title once we know it (for the
        # narrow Status/Retries columns to be readable off-screen
        # even when the Detail column truncates).
        $title = Format-FbTitle -Title $origTitle -HResult $hr

        # Branch terminates with `continue` to the enclosing foreach. We
        # use an explicit $handled gate instead of `continue` inside a
        # nested switch because `continue` inside switch only exits the
        # switch case, not the parent loop (PowerShell gotcha that would
        # silently double-process every per-update result).
        $handled = $false

        if ($code -eq 5 -or $code -eq $script:FbWuResultAborted) {
            $ledgerAlready = $false
            try {
                if ($null -ne $script:FbSuccessLedgerSet -and $script:FbSuccessLedgerSet.Contains($uid)) { $ledgerAlready = $true }
            } catch {}
            $uiAlreadyDone = $false
            try {
                if ($script:UiUpdates.ContainsKey($uid) -and [string]$script:UiUpdates[$uid].Status -eq 'Completed') { $uiAlreadyDone = $true }
            } catch {}
            if ($ledgerAlready -or $uiAlreadyDone) {
                Write-FbLog ("[{0}] '{1}' timed out but is already completed / success-ledgered; skip continue, no reboot-retry of this bucket." -f $BucketName, $origTitle) 'WARN'
                $RetryMap[$key] = 0
                Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title $origTitle -Status 'Completed' -Retries 0 -Detail 'Timed out after already installed (ResultCode=2 previously); not rebooting to retry.'
                $outcome.Skipped++
                $handled = $true
            }
        }

        if ((-not $handled) -and ($code -in $ValidInstallCodes)) {
            $RetryMap[$key] = 0
            # Successful installs use the original title (no HResult suffix).
            Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title $origTitle -Status 'Completed' -Retries 0 -Detail (Format-FbDetail -Reason ('Installed (ResultCode={0})' -f $code))
            $outcome.Succeeded++
            # 2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence: this is the
            # ONLY rail that may latch InstallsCompleted. The
            # per-update result came back with ResultCode in
            # {2,3} from IInstallationResult.GetUpdateResult,
            # which is the definitive proof that WUA installed
            # this row. Pass RealInstallEvidence=$true so the
            # latch helper persists the flag and bumps the
            # cumulative RealInstallsTotal counter.
            Set-FbInstallsCompleted -Reason ("ValidInstallCode {0} for '{1}' in bucket {2}" -f $code, $origTitle, $BucketName) -RealInstallEvidence $true
            try {
                Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'bucket-apply' -Message ("'{0}' installed (ResultCode={1}) in {2}" -f $origTitle, $code, $BucketName)
            } catch {}
            # 2026.05.18.2213l Bug FF Mechanism 1: record the
            # successful install in the per-session success ledger
            # so the next fresh scan's post-scan increment block
            # can bump the SoftGivenUp counter for this UpdateId if
            # WUA still offers it (sticky-residual signal).
            try {
                if ($null -ne $script:FbSuccessLedgerSet) {
                    [void]$script:FbSuccessLedgerSet.Add($uid)
                }
            } catch {}
            # 2026.05.18.2213l Bug FF Mechanism 2: surgical
            # Defender platform fast-path. If this UpdateId is in
            # the known Defender platform list, mark the per-boot
            # tracker so the next install attempt this boot is
            # SKIPPED entirely (see Defender-dedupe filter above),
            # AND pre-set the SoftGivenUp counter to the threshold
            # so the very next scan's post-scan block promotes the
            # UpdateId straight into GivenUpSet on the FIRST
            # increment instead of waiting two full boot cycles.
            try {
                if ($null -ne $script:FbDefenderPlatformGuids -and ($script:FbDefenderPlatformGuids -contains $uid)) {
                    if ($null -eq $script:FbDefenderInstalledThisBoot) {
                        $script:FbDefenderInstalledThisBoot = @{}
                    }
                    $script:FbDefenderInstalledThisBoot[$uid] = $true
                    $defenderThreshold = 2
                    try { if ($null -ne $script:FbSoftGivenUpThreshold) { $defenderThreshold = [int]$script:FbSoftGivenUpThreshold } } catch {}
                    if ($null -ne $script:FbSoftGivenUpCountersLoop) {
                        $script:FbSoftGivenUpCountersLoop[$uid] = $defenderThreshold
                    }
                    Write-FbLog ("Bug FF Mechanism 2: Defender platform '{0}' ({1}) installed successfully; per-boot tracker marked + SoftGivenUp counter pre-set to {2} (threshold). Next scan will promote to GivenUpSet on first increment." -f $origTitle, $uid, $defenderThreshold) 'WARN'
                }
            } catch {
                try { Write-FbLog ("Bug FF Mechanism 2: Defender fast-path bookkeeping threw for '{0}' ({1}): {2}; continuing (cosmetic failure - install itself succeeded)." -f $origTitle, $uid, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN' } catch {}
            }
            $handled = $true
        }

        if (-not $handled) {
            # Non-success ResultCode. Inspect the HResult to decide
            # whether this is benign (skip / reboot / cautious-retry)
            # or a real failure.
            $benign = Get-BenignHResultEntry -HResult $hr
            if ($benign) {
                $action = [string]$benign.Action
                $reason = [string]$benign.Reason
                if ($action -eq 'Skip') {
                    Write-FbLog ("[{0}] '{1}' classified as benign-skip ({2}, HResult={3}); marking Completed without retry." -f $BucketName, $origTitle, $reason, $hex) 'INFO'
                    # 2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence: REMOVED
                    # the pre-1700 BenignHResult Action='Skip' latch
                    # rail. The Skip codes mean "WUA decided this
                    # row is not applicable or already installed
                    # without running our installer" - that is NOT
                    # proof a real install happened in this cycle.
                    # The latch must not fire here. We still mark
                    # the row Completed for UI purposes (it really
                    # is done - just not by us).
                    $RetryMap[$key] = 0
                    [void]$GivenUpSet.Add($key)
                    [void]$GivenUpSet.Add($uid)
                    $detail = Format-FbDetail -HResult $hr -Reason $reason -Prefix 'Skipped:'
                    Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title $title -Status 'Completed' -Retries 0 -Detail $detail
                    $outcome.Skipped++
                    $handled = $true
                } elseif ($action -eq 'Reboot') {
                    Write-FbLog ("[{0}] '{1}' requires reboot to settle ({2}, HResult={3}); forcing pending restart." -f $BucketName, $origTitle, $reason, $hex) 'WARN'
                    # 2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence: an HResult
                    # in the Action='Reboot' set (WU_S_REBOOT_REQUIRED,
                    # ERROR_SUCCESS_REBOOT_REQUIRED, CBS_E_INSTALLERS_-
                    # FAILED, etc.) is a per-update IInstallationResult
                    # value, returned by EndInstall - the installer
                    # genuinely ran and returned a known-reboot-
                    # recoverable failure code. This IS real install
                    # evidence; latch with -RealInstallEvidence $true.
                    # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress:
                    # tighten the off-by-one. BenignHResult Action=
                    # 'Reboot' rows are NOT per-update orcSucceeded
                    # rows; the install genuinely needs a reboot to
                    # finish. This counts as latch evidence (the
                    # installer ran and produced a known-recoverable
                    # HResult) but does NOT count as a real install
                    # for the BootsSinceLastInstall escape-valve
                    # threshold. Pass BumpRealInstallsTotal=$false.
                    try { Set-FbInstallsCompleted -Reason ("BenignHResult Action=Reboot for '{0}' in {1} (HResult={2}, reason={3})" -f $origTitle, $BucketName, $hex, $reason) -RealInstallEvidence $true -BumpRealInstallsTotal $false } catch {}
                    # 2026.05.12.1830-patient-shutdown-and-hresult-coverage
                    # Fix 3: lower the per-update retry ceiling to 1
                    # for this key now that we know it produces a
                    # reboot-recoverable HResult. Persists across
                    # iterations / reboots via $UpdateRetryBudget.
                    # If this update returns a different HResult on a
                    # later boot, the budget stays at 1 (we already
                    # classified it as reboot-recoverable once; the
                    # cautious behaviour is to not climb back to 3).
                    try { $UpdateRetryBudget[$key] = 1 } catch {}
                    # Do NOT consume a retry slot - this isn't a retryable
                    # failure; the install is effectively complete pending
                    # reboot. Action='Reboot' is an explicit reboot signal
                    # so we mark ExplicitReboot so the caller halts the
                    # iteration (we must reboot before continuing).
                    $detail = Format-FbDetail -HResult $hr -Reason $reason -Prefix 'Reboot required:'
                    Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title $title -Status 'Pending restart' -Retries ([int]$RetryMap[$key]) -Detail $detail
                    try {
                        Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'pending-restart-detected' -Message ("'{0}' marked Pending restart [BenignHResult Action=Reboot, HResult={1}, perKeyBudget=1]" -f $origTitle, $hex)
                    } catch {}
                    $outcome.ForcePendingRestart = $true
                    $outcome.ExplicitReboot = $true
                    $handled = $true
                } elseif ($action -eq 'Retry') {
                    $retry = [int]$RetryMap[$key] + 1
                    if ($retry -gt $MaxUpdateRetry) {
                        Write-FbLog ("[{0}] '{1}' benign-retry exhausted at {2} (HResult={3}); converting to Skip." -f $BucketName, $origTitle, $retry, $hex) 'WARN'
                        $RetryMap[$key] = 0
                        [void]$GivenUpSet.Add($key)
                        [void]$GivenUpSet.Add($uid)
                        $detail = Format-FbDetail -HResult $hr -Retry $MaxUpdateRetry -MaxRetry $MaxUpdateRetry -Reason $reason -Prefix ('Skipped after {0} retries:' -f $MaxUpdateRetry)
                        Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title $title -Status 'Completed' -Retries 0 -Detail $detail
                        $outcome.Skipped++
                    } else {
                        $RetryMap[$key] = $retry
                        Write-FbLog ("[{0}] '{1}' benign-retry {2}/{3} ({4}, HResult={5})" -f $BucketName, $origTitle, $retry, $MaxUpdateRetry, $reason, $hex) 'INFO'
                        $detail = Format-FbDetail -HResult $hr -Retry $retry -MaxRetry $MaxUpdateRetry -Reason $reason
                        Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title $title -Status 'Retrying' -Retries $retry -Detail $detail
                        $outcome.Failed++
                        try { [void]$retryStormBenignHResults.Add($hex) } catch {}
                    }
                    $handled = $true
                }
            }
        }

        if (-not $handled) {
            # 2026.06.12.2276-fix-0x80246017-terminal Fix A: immediate terminal
            # HResults — add to GivenUp on the FIRST failure; do not consume any
            # retry budget. These are auth/context errors (e.g. DM_UNAUTHORIZED)
            # that will return the same failure code on every subsequent attempt
            # in this SYSTEM/non-interactive session.
            if ($hr -ne 0 -and ($script:FbWuImmediateTerminalHResults -contains $hr) -and $code -eq 4) {
                Write-FbLog ("[{0}] Terminal HResult {1} (DM_UNAUTHORIZED / feature-upgrade not downloadable in SYSTEM context): UpdateID {2} added to GivenUp immediately. Will not retry." -f $BucketName, $hex, $uid) 'WARN'
                $RetryMap[$key] = 0
                [void]$GivenUpSet.Add($key)
                [void]$GivenUpSet.Add($uid)
                $detail = Format-FbDetail -HResult $hr -Reason 'ImmediateTerminal: DM_UNAUTHORIZED - feature-upgrade not downloadable in SYSTEM session' -Prefix 'Terminal (no retry):'
                Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title $title -Status 'Completed' -Retries 0 -Detail $detail
                $outcome.Skipped++
            } else {
                # Genuine retryable failure with unknown HResult. Bump the
                # per-KEY counter and either re-attempt on a later iteration
                # (greedy fall-through to next bucket allowed) or, on crossing
                # the ceiling, force a reboot AND persist retry pinned at
                # the ceiling plus add to GivenUpSet so it survives reboot.
                $retry = [int]$RetryMap[$key] + 1
                $RetryMap[$key] = $retry
                # 2026.05.12.1830-patient-shutdown-and-hresult-coverage
                # Fix 3: choose the per-update retry ceiling. If this
                # HResult is in $BenignHResultMap with Action='Reboot'
                # we'd normally have been handled above; reaching here
                # with such an HResult means the prior branch was skipped
                # for some reason - still apply the lowered ceiling so
                # we don't burn the full $MaxUpdateRetry budget. Updates
                # with HResults not in the map keep the default 3-retry
                # budget.
                $perKeyCeil = Get-FbUpdateRetryBudget -Key $key -HResult $hr -BudgetMap $UpdateRetryBudget
                # Persist the resolved ceiling for this key so subsequent
                # iterations / reboots see the same value.
                try { $UpdateRetryBudget[$key] = $perKeyCeil } catch {}
                Write-FbLog "[$BucketName] '$origTitle' failed (ResultCode=$code, HResult=$hr ($hex), retry=$retry/$perKeyCeil)" 'WARN'
                if ($retry -gt $perKeyCeil) {
                    $RetryMap[$key] = $perKeyCeil
                    [void]$GivenUpSet.Add($key)
                    [void]$GivenUpSet.Add($uid)
                    $cats = @()
                    if ($u -and $u.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'Categories') {
                        $cats = @($u.Categories)
                    }
                    Write-StuckDiag -UpdateId $uid -Title $origTitle -ResultCode $code -HResult $hr -Categories $cats -Reason ('Exceeded per-update retry ceiling (>{0})' -f $perKeyCeil)
                    # 2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence: REMOVED the
                    # pre-1700 retry-ceiling latch rail. Crossing the
                    # retry ceiling means we made N+1 ATTEMPTS, but if
                    # every attempt came back as ResultCode=-1 (the
                    # WU-20260512-110026.log regression) there is no
                    # proof any install actually happened. The latch
                    # must not fire here. The row is still marked
                    # Pending restart for UI purposes and a STUCK-DIAG
                    # dump is written, but the latch stays at whatever
                    # real-install evidence the ValidInstallCode rail
                    # has accumulated.
                    $detail = Format-FbDetail -HResult $hr -Retry $perKeyCeil -MaxRetry $perKeyCeil -Reason 'unknown HResult exceeded retry threshold' -Prefix 'Exceeded retry threshold;'
                    Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title $title -Status 'Pending restart' -Retries $perKeyCeil -Detail $detail
                    try {
                        Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'pending-restart-detected' -Message ("'{0}' marked Pending restart [retry ceiling exceeded, HResult={1}, perKeyBudget={2}]" -f $origTitle, $hex, $perKeyCeil)
                    } catch {}
                    $outcome.ForcePendingRestart = $true
                    # Crossing the retry ceiling on an UNKNOWN HResult still
                    # requires a reboot to retry, but it is NOT an explicit
                    # reboot request from WUA - we set ForcePendingRestart
                    # but NOT ExplicitReboot. That way the caller can attempt
                    # remaining buckets first (greedy install) and only then
                    # consume the reboot.
                } else {
                    $detail = Format-FbDetail -HResult $hr -Retry $retry -MaxRetry $perKeyCeil -Reason 'unknown HResult'
                    Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title $title -Status 'Retrying' -Retries $retry -Detail $detail
                    # Unknown HResult retrying inside per-key budget.
                    try { [void]$retryStormUnknownHResults.Add($hex) } catch {}
                }
                $outcome.Failed++

                # Allowlist check: if the unknown HResult turns out to live
                # in $ExplicitRebootHResults (we hit it but didn't catch it
                # via the BenignHResultMap branch above for some reason),
                # treat it as an explicit reboot too.
                if ($hr -ne 0 -and $ExplicitRebootHResults -contains $hr) {
                    $outcome.ExplicitReboot = $true
                }
            }
        }
    }

    # 2026.05.12.1830-patient-shutdown-and-hresult-coverage Fix 4:
    # If more than half of the targeted updates ended at Retrying,
    # surface a dashboard message naming the HResults. This makes
    # the retry storm visible and explainable to an operator with
    # no log access. The retry-storm message includes both the
    # unknown-HResult list (primary, the spec's "non-benign"
    # category) and any benign-Retry-action HResults observed.
    try {
        if ($outcome.Targeted -gt 0) {
            $retryingTotal = $retryStormUnknownHResults.Count + $retryStormBenignHResults.Count
            $retryThreshold = [int][math]::Floor($outcome.Targeted / 2)
            if ($retryingTotal -gt $retryThreshold) {
                $uniqUnknown = @($retryStormUnknownHResults | Select-Object -Unique)
                $uniqBenign  = @($retryStormBenignHResults  | Select-Object -Unique)
                $listParts = @()
                if ($uniqUnknown.Count -gt 0) {
                    $listParts += ($uniqUnknown -join ', ')
                }
                if ($uniqBenign.Count -gt 0) {
                    $listParts += ('benign-retry: ' + ($uniqBenign -join ', '))
                }
                $hresultList = $listParts -join ' | '
                if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($hresultList)) { $hresultList = '(none)' }
                $stormMsg = ("Bucket {0} Critical: {1}/{2} retrying [HResults: {3}]. Reboot then retry strategy in effect." -f $BucketName, $retryingTotal, $outcome.Targeted, $hresultList)
                Write-FbLog $stormMsg 'WARN'
                try {
                    Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'bucket-apply' -Message $stormMsg
                } catch {}
                try {
                    Set-UiPhase -Phase $BucketName -Message $stormMsg
                } catch {}
            }
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("[{0}] retry-storm dashboard surface threw: {1}" -f $BucketName, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass $BucketName -StartTime $StartTime -FailureCount $FailureCount -UpdateRetry $RetryMap -GivenUpUpdateIds @($GivenUpSet) -BootCountByKey $BootCountByKey -BootCount $BootCount -ForceHiddenKeys @($ForceHiddenSet) -RebootIterations $RebootIterations -SysprepRetryCount $SysprepRetryCount -UpdateRetryBudget $UpdateRetryBudget -SoftGivenUpCounters $script:FbSoftGivenUpCountersLoop -SuccessLedgerIds @($script:FbSuccessLedgerSet)

    # 2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence: derive the post-bucket
    # reboot signal and decide whether to latch.
    #   - applyResult.RebootRequired is the IInstallationResult-level
    #     RebootRequired bit returned by EndInstall. It's authoritative
    #     when accompanied by at least one orcSucceeded per-update row
    #     (which is what $script:RealInstallsThisBoot tracks).
    #   - Test-FbWuRebootRequired is the OS-level registry probe.
    #     On a freshly-imaged box this can be $true purely from sysprep
    #     / specialize / image-apply leftovers, even before any install
    #     - we must NOT latch on it alone.
    # We mark the bucket as needing reboot (so the iteration epilogue
    # reboots us) but ONLY latch InstallsCompleted when we have real
    # install evidence from THIS boot or from the engine's Installed
    # count.
    $engineReportedInstalls = 0
    try { $engineReportedInstalls = [int]$applyResult.Installed } catch { $engineReportedInstalls = 0 }
    $bucketRebootRequired = ([bool]$applyResult.RebootRequired) -or (Test-FbWuRebootRequired)
    if ($bucketRebootRequired) {
        $outcome.RebootRequired = $true
        # WUA-reported RebootRequired is an explicit signal - halt iteration.
        $outcome.ExplicitReboot = $true
        if ($engineReportedInstalls -gt 0 -or $script:RealInstallsThisBoot -gt 0) {
            # Real install evidence in this boot: the OS pending-reboot
            # flag is genuinely ours. Latch.
            # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: this is
            # the bucket-finish post-apply OS-pending-reboot path
            # the user explicitly called out. The per-update result
            # loop above has ALREADY bumped RealInstallsTotal once
            # per orcSucceeded row, so bumping again here is the
            # off-by-one. Pass BumpRealInstallsTotal=$false so this
            # call only persists the latch but does not advance
            # the counter.
            Set-FbInstallsCompleted -Reason ("RebootRequired reported by WUA / OS after {0} bucket apply with Installed={1} ThisBoot={2}" -f $BucketName, $engineReportedInstalls, $script:RealInstallsThisBoot) -RealInstallEvidence $true -BumpRealInstallsTotal $false
        } else {
            Write-FbLog ("[{0}] post-bucket reboot signal observed but NO real install evidence in this boot (Installed={1}, RealInstallsThisBoot={2}). NOT latching InstallsCompleted." -f $BucketName, $engineReportedInstalls, $script:RealInstallsThisBoot) 'WARN'
        }
        $rebootRelabeled = 0
        foreach ($u in $bucketUpdatesActive) {
            $uid = [string]$u.UpdateId
            if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($uid)) { continue }
            $title = [string]$u.Title; if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($title)) { $title = $uid }
            $key = Get-FbRetryKey -Update $u
            if ($script:UiUpdates.ContainsKey($uid) -and $script:UiUpdates[$uid].Status -ne 'Completed') {
                Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title $title -Status 'Pending restart' -Retries ([int]$RetryMap[$key])
                $rebootRelabeled++
            }
        }
        # 2026.05.12.1700: surface the post-bucket evidence on the
        # dashboard. The triplet "Latch=X | RealInstalls=Y | OSPendingReboot=Z"
        # is rendered by Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag.
        try {
            Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'bucket-apply' -Message ("WUA / OS reports reboot required after {0} bucket apply; {1} row(s) labelled Pending restart" -f $BucketName, $rebootRelabeled)
        } catch {}
    }

    # Halted = "stop iterating buckets right now and reboot". Only true
    # when the failure mode is an explicit reboot request. Plain retry-
    # bucket failures (Failed>0 but no ExplicitReboot) let the caller
    # continue to the next bucket so we install everything we can on
    # this iteration before consuming a reboot.
    if ($HaltOnFailure -and $outcome.ExplicitReboot) {
        $outcome.Halted = $true
    }

    return $outcome
}

function Update-FbWuWorkQueueFromBucketResults {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Refresh $WorkQueue from a single Invoke-FbBucketApply outcome.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress. Called from
        the main multi-pass loop after each bucket finishes. Walks
        the per-update results returned by the bucket apply and
        either keeps the row on the queue (Reboot/Transient/Unknown
        outcomes) or removes it (Success/Terminal). The queue is the
        system-of-record the next pass uses to decide whether the
        system has converged.

        Also tracks the per-row Outcome class on the wu-status.json
        row so the dashboard shows the new richer statuses
        ("Pending reboot - will reapply next pass", "Retry next
        boot", "Skipped (terminal failure)").

        The queue array is mutated in place; the caller is expected
        to pass a [ref] to a script-scoped or local variable.

        2026.05.13.2210-fullscope-multipass-progress-fixes Critical
        Fix 3: when an update is classified Class='Terminal',
        the UpdateId AND its retry-key are added to the
        $GivenUpSet (passed in via -GivenUpSetRef) BEFORE the row
        is removed from the work queue. This closes the edge case
        where a Terminal outcome from this path produced a queue
        deletion but no GivenUp entry, which would let the same
        update re-enter the queue on the next fresh scan. Mirrors
        the in-bucket-apply pattern at lines ~7936-7937 and ~8086-
        8087.

        Never throws. Best-effort: if an apply result is malformed
        (missing per-update array, etc.) the row is left on the
        queue with RetryCount++ so the next pass tries again.
    #>
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$BucketName,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [array]$BucketUpdates,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] $BucketApplyResult,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [ref]$WorkQueueRef,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [int]$BootCount,
        [hashtable]$RetryBudgetMap = @{},
        # 2026.05.13.2210-fullscope-multipass-progress-fixes
        # Critical Fix 3. Optional ref to the script-scoped
        # GivenUpSet hashset. When supplied, every Terminal
        # outcome adds (UpdateId, RetryKey) to the set BEFORE
        # the row is removed from the queue. The main-loop
        # caller is expected to pass [ref]$givenUpSet so the
        # set persists for the bucket-end Save-State sweep that
        # writes -GivenUpUpdateIds. If the parameter is omitted,
        # the function silently skips the GivenUp augmentation
        # (preserves backward compatibility for unit tests).
        [ref]$GivenUpSetRef = $null
    )

    if (-not $BucketUpdates -or $BucketUpdates.Count -eq 0) { return }

    # 2026.05.13.2213h-real-convergence-and-ti-serialization Bug N
    # POLICY CHANGE: The pre-2213h BUG-H2 GUARD was a SILENT SWALLOW.
    # When UpdateResults was missing/empty (which happened on EVERY
    # bucket in the 2213g build due to the [ordered]@{} property-
    # introspection bug, see Bug N fix in Invoke-FbBucketApply at
    # line ~8948), the guard logged ERROR and returned without
    # advancing the WorkQueue. That dropped failure evidence from
    # convergence tracking and produced the false convergence shipped
    # in 2213d/f/g.
    #
    # The new policy: a missing/empty UpdateResults with non-empty
    # BucketUpdates is itself evidence that the bucket failed to
    # produce per-update telemetry. Treat every BucketUpdate row as
    # an UNKNOWN failure (ResultCode=4, HResult=0) and INCLUDE it
    # in the WorkQueue so the EndOfPass decision sees actual pending
    # work. Falling safe is "Retry next boot" - the row stays on
    # the queue with RetryCount++. The legacy guard concern (an
    # unnecessary reboot+retry cycle) is now the LEAST BAD outcome
    # because the alternative is the silent false-convergence path.
    #
    # The check uses BOTH the PSObject.Properties path AND a defensive
    # dictionary-key fallback, so an ordered-hashtable regression in
    # the future cannot reintroduce the bug.
    $hasUpdateResultsField = $false
    if ($BucketApplyResult) {
        try { $hasUpdateResultsField = ($BucketApplyResult.PSObject.Properties.Name -contains 'UpdateResults') } catch {}
        if (-not $hasUpdateResultsField) {
            # Dictionary/ordered-dict fallback: check by key existence.
            try {
                if ($BucketApplyResult -is [System.Collections.IDictionary]) {
                    $hasUpdateResultsField = $BucketApplyResult.Contains('UpdateResults')
                }
            } catch {}
        }
    }
    $updateResultsValue = $null
    if ($hasUpdateResultsField) {
        try { $updateResultsValue = $BucketApplyResult.UpdateResults } catch {}
    }
    $updateResultsCount = 0
    if ($updateResultsValue) {
        try { $updateResultsCount = (@($updateResultsValue) | Measure-Object).Count } catch {}
    }

    if (-not $hasUpdateResultsField -or $updateResultsCount -eq 0) {
        $bucketUpdateCount = 0
        try { $bucketUpdateCount = (@($BucketUpdates) | Measure-Object).Count } catch {}
        Write-FbLog ("Update-FbWuWorkQueueFromBucketResults: BUG-N FALLBACK: BucketApplyResult.UpdateResults is missing or empty for bucket '{0}' (BucketUpdates count={1}). Synthesizing per-update Retry rows so the failure evidence is NOT silently dropped (2213h policy: include rows as Retry, do NOT swallow). Pre-2213h would have returned without queue advancement; the 2213d/f/g false convergence is the consequence of that swallow." -f $BucketName, $bucketUpdateCount) 'ERROR'
        # Build synthesized rows for every BucketUpdate so the loop
        # below can mark them as Retry/Unknown and persist them on
        # the WorkQueue. Each row gets ResultCode=4 (orcFailed) and
        # HResult=0 (unknown) so Get-FbWuOutcome classifies them as
        # Class='Unknown' / Status='Retry next boot' / Persist=$true.
        $synthesizedUR = @()
        foreach ($u in $BucketUpdates) {
            $uid = ''
            try { $uid = [string]$u.UpdateId } catch {}
            if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($uid)) { continue }
            $title = ''
            try { $title = [string]$u.Title } catch {}
            if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($title)) { $title = $uid }
            $synthesizedUR += [pscustomobject]@{
                Title      = $title
                UpdateId   = $uid
                ResultCode = 4
                HResult    = 0
                BucketName = $BucketName
                RetryCount = 0
            }
        }
        # Re-shape so the foreach below operates on the synthesized
        # array as the source of truth.
        $updateResultsValue = $synthesizedUR
    }

    $existingByUid = @{}
    foreach ($row in @($WorkQueueRef.Value)) {
        if ($row -and $row.UpdateId) { $existingByUid[[string]$row.UpdateId] = $row }
    }

    # 2026.05.13.2213h Bug N: use $updateResultsValue (already
    # normalized above to be either the real UpdateResults array OR
    # the synthesized fallback) instead of re-reading
    # $BucketApplyResult.UpdateResults via the property-introspection
    # path that broke on [ordered]@{}.
    $perResults = @{}
    if ($updateResultsValue) {
        foreach ($r in @($updateResultsValue)) {
            try { $perResults[[string]$r.UpdateId] = $r } catch {}
        }
    }

    foreach ($u in $BucketUpdates) {
        $uid = [string]$u.UpdateId
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($uid)) { continue }
        $title = [string]$u.Title; if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($title)) { $title = $uid }
        $key = Get-FbRetryKey -Update $u

        # 2026.05.13.2213h-real-convergence-and-ti-serialization Bug N
        # defense-in-depth: if this UID/key is ALREADY in the GivenUpSet,
        # the bucket apply ran skip-status code paths for it (Set-UiUpdate-
        # Status 'Completed', skipped++) and we should NOT add it back to
        # the WorkQueue regardless of what the BUG-N FALLBACK synthesized.
        # This protects the case where Invoke-FbBucketApply returns the
        # empty $outcome.UpdateResults early because all targeted rows
        # were filtered as already-given-up and $ids.Count==0.
        $alreadyGivenUp = $false
        if ($null -ne $GivenUpSetRef -and $null -ne $GivenUpSetRef.Value) {
            try {
                if ($GivenUpSetRef.Value.Contains($uid) -or ($key -and $GivenUpSetRef.Value.Contains($key))) {
                    $alreadyGivenUp = $true
                }
            } catch {}
        }
        if ($alreadyGivenUp) {
            if ($existingByUid.ContainsKey($uid)) {
                $existingByUid.Remove($uid) | Out-Null
                Write-FbLog ("Update-FbWuWorkQueueFromBucketResults: UID={0} already in GivenUpSet; removing from WorkQueue (no re-classification needed)." -f $uid) 'INFO'
            }
            continue
        }

        $resultEntry = $null
        if ($perResults.ContainsKey($uid)) { $resultEntry = $perResults[$uid] }
        $code = if ($resultEntry) { [int]$resultEntry.ResultCode } else { -1 }
        $hr   = if ($resultEntry) { [int]$resultEntry.HResult }   else { 0 }

        $existingRow = $null
        if ($existingByUid.ContainsKey($uid)) { $existingRow = $existingByUid[$uid] }
        $retryCount = if ($existingRow) { [int]$existingRow.RetryCount } else { 0 }
        $firstSeenBoot = if ($existingRow) { [int]$existingRow.FirstSeenBoot } else { $BootCount }

        $perKeyCeiling = Get-FbUpdateRetryBudget -Key $key -HResult $hr -BudgetMap $RetryBudgetMap

        $outcome = Get-FbWuOutcome -ResultCode $code -HResult $hr -RetryCount $retryCount -RetryCeiling $perKeyCeiling
        $newRetry = $retryCount
        if ($outcome.BumpRetry) { $newRetry = $retryCount + 1 }

        try {
            $detail = [string]$outcome.Detail
            Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title (Format-FbTitle -Title $title -HResult $hr) `
                -Status ([string]$outcome.Status) -Retries $newRetry -Detail $detail
        } catch {}

        if ($outcome.Persist) {
            $existingByUid[$uid] = New-FbWuWorkQueueRow `
                -UpdateId $uid `
                -Bucket $BucketName `
                -Status ([string]$outcome.Status) `
                -RetryCount $newRetry `
                -LastHResult $hr `
                -FirstSeenBoot $firstSeenBoot
        } else {
            # 2026.05.13.2210-fullscope-multipass-progress-fixes
            # Critical Fix 3: Terminal outcomes MUST land in the
            # GivenUpSet before the row is removed, otherwise the
            # next fresh scan will re-include the same update and
            # the work queue will repopulate it. Success outcomes
            # (ResultCode in {2,3}) are simply removed; they don't
            # need GivenUp because they're actually installed.
            if ($outcome.Class -eq 'Terminal' -and $null -ne $GivenUpSetRef -and $null -ne $GivenUpSetRef.Value) {
                try {
                    [void]$GivenUpSetRef.Value.Add($uid)
                    if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($key)) { [void]$GivenUpSetRef.Value.Add($key) }
                    Write-FbLog ("Update-FbWuWorkQueueFromBucketResults: Class=Terminal for UpdateId={0} (HRESULT={1}, retry-key={2}); added to GivenUpSet so subsequent fresh scans honor the prior decision. Reason='{3}'." -f $uid, (Format-Hex32 -Value $hr), $key, [string]$outcome.Reason) 'WARN'
                } catch {
                    Write-FbLog ("Update-FbWuWorkQueueFromBucketResults: GivenUpSet.Add for UpdateId={0} threw: {1}" -f $uid, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
                }
            }
            if ($existingByUid.ContainsKey($uid)) { $existingByUid.Remove($uid) | Out-Null }
        }
    }

    $WorkQueueRef.Value = @()
    foreach ($k in $existingByUid.Keys) {
        $WorkQueueRef.Value += ,$existingByUid[$k]
    }
}

function Invoke-FbWuEndOfPassDecision {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        Apply the strict-convergence end-of-pass decision rules for
        the multi-pass full-scope loop.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress. Called by the
        main loop after all 4 buckets in a pass have finished.

        Rules (2026.05.13.2210-fullscope-multipass-progress-fixes
        Critical Fix 1: STRICT 4-condition convergence gate):
          1. STAGE 1 trigger requires ALL FOUR independent gates
             to be open SIMULTANEOUSLY:
               a) workQueueEmpty    = $WorkQueue is null or count 0
               b) noRebootNeeded    = -not Test-FbWuRebootRequired
               c) rescanClean       = $LastScanResultCount -eq 0
                                      (the prior fresh scan
                                      returned zero applicable
                                      updates - the rescan
                                      confirmation)
               d) priorPassWasClean = $state.ConvergenceConfirmed
                                      from disk -eq $true (the
                                      prior pass already had a
                                      clean queue + no reboot)
             ONLY when all four are true do we latch
             PendingSysprep=$true and route to STAGE 1. This is
             the only PendingSysprep=$true writer in the entire
             codebase.
          2. workQueueEmpty + noRebootNeeded + rescanClean but
             priorPassWasClean=$false: First clean pass since
             the last install. Mark ConvergenceConfirmed=$true on
             disk and reboot for the rescan-confirmation boot.
             Next pass's fresh scan + clean apply will fire
             STAGE 1 via gate (1) above.
          3. workQueueEmpty AND Test-FbWuRebootRequired=$true:
             Patient shutdown WITHOUT setting PendingSysprep
             (queue is empty but CBS owes us a reboot first).
          4. WorkQueue non-empty: bump or reset
             BootsSinceLastInstall based on whether
             $realInstallsThisPass > 0. Patient shutdown.

        When STAGE 1 is denied, the function logs which of the
        four gates failed so pass-by-pass debug is possible from
        WU-*.log alone.

        Never returns: each branch ends in Invoke-FbReboot which
        either patient-shuts-down or fast-paths sysprep.

        Never throws. Best-effort fall-through to "patient shutdown,
        no sysprep" on internal errors.
    #>
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [int]$PassIndex,
        # 2026.05.13.2213h-real-convergence-and-ti-serialization Bug O:
        # the pre-2213h signature was [Parameter(Mandatory)] [array]
        # which PowerShell's binder REJECTS when the caller passes an
        # empty array (@()). Field log F:\fb-dump-final-1347\Logs\
        # WU-20260515-114810.log line 158 shows the smoking gun:
        #   TerminatingError(Invoke-FbWuEndOfPassDecision): "Cannot
        #   bind argument to parameter 'WorkQueue' because it is an
        #   empty collection."
        # The main loop then caught this as a generic "WU error",
        # routed to Invoke-FbReboot, and rebooted without retry.
        # The fix: AllowNull + AllowEmptyCollection so an empty
        # WorkQueue is a valid input (it semantically means "no
        # pending work" - the function already has a workQueueEmpty
        # branch that handles it correctly). Below, the function
        # also tolerates $null by coercing to @().
        [Parameter(Mandatory)]
        [AllowNull()]
        [AllowEmptyCollection()]
        [array]$WorkQueue,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [int]$RealInstallsAtPassStart,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [int]$LastScanResultCount,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$StartTime,
        [int]$BootCount = 0
    )

    # 2026.05.13.2213h Bug O: tolerate $null by coercing once at the
    # top so downstream Measure-Object / count / comparisons never
    # NRE.
    if ($null -eq $WorkQueue) { $WorkQueue = @() }

    $rebootRequired = $false
    try { $rebootRequired = [bool](Test-FbWuRebootRequired) } catch {}

    $diskNow = $null
    try { $diskNow = Load-State } catch {}
    $realInstallsTotalNow = 0
    if ($diskNow) { try { $realInstallsTotalNow = [int]$diskNow.RealInstallsTotal } catch {} }
    $realInstallsThisPass = $realInstallsTotalNow - $RealInstallsAtPassStart
    if ($realInstallsThisPass -lt 0) { $realInstallsThisPass = 0 }

    $bootsSinceLastInstallPrior = 0
    if ($diskNow) { try { $bootsSinceLastInstallPrior = [int]$diskNow.BootsSinceLastInstall } catch {} }
    $convergenceConfirmedPrior = $false
    if ($diskNow) { try { $convergenceConfirmedPrior = [bool]$diskNow.ConvergenceConfirmed } catch {} }

    $queueCount = ($WorkQueue | Measure-Object).Count
    $script:UiPendingUpdates = $queueCount

    Write-FbLog ("EndOfPass[{0}]: queue={1} rebootRequired={2} realInstallsThisPass={3} convergenceConfirmedPrior={4} bootsSinceLastInstallPrior={5}" -f $PassIndex, $queueCount, $rebootRequired, $realInstallsThisPass, $convergenceConfirmedPrior, $bootsSinceLastInstallPrior) 'WARN'

    $decision = @{
        Action               = ''
        Reason               = ''
        Queue                = $queueCount
        RebootRequired       = $rebootRequired
        RealInstallsThisPass = $realInstallsThisPass
    }

    # 2026.05.13.2210-fullscope-multipass-progress-fixes Critical
    # Fix 1: STRICT convergence gate. The 2200 build accepted
    # ($convergenceConfirmedPrior -or $LastScanResultCount -eq 0)
    # which let STAGE 1 fire on EITHER signal. Spec requires ALL
    # FOUR signals to be true simultaneously before STAGE 1 latches
    # PendingSysprep=$true. Each gate is computed and logged
    # independently so when STAGE 1 is denied the operator can see
    # exactly which condition failed, pass-by-pass.
    $workQueueEmpty    = ($WorkQueue -eq $null) -or (($WorkQueue | Measure-Object).Count -eq 0)
    $noRebootNeeded    = -not $rebootRequired
    $rescanClean       = ($LastScanResultCount -eq 0)
    $priorPassWasClean = ($convergenceConfirmedPrior -eq $true)
    # 5.4.22: empty work queue + no OS reboot is not "0 still pending; reboot".
    # WUA re-offering success-ledger rows (KB5007651 / Defender) must not restart the pass.
    if ($workQueueEmpty -and $noRebootNeeded) {
        $rescanClean = $true
    }

    if ($workQueueEmpty -and $noRebootNeeded -and $rescanClean -and $priorPassWasClean) {
        # All four gates open. This pass IS the confirmation
        # rescan after the prior clean pass. Latch
        # PendingSysprep=$true and route to STAGE 1 sysprep
        # handoff. THIS IS THE ONLY PendingSysprep=$true writer
        # in the entire codebase.
        $decision.Action = 'STAGE1_SYSPREP_HANDOFF'
        $decision.Reason = 'STRICT convergence: workQueueEmpty=true noRebootNeeded=true rescanClean=true priorPassWasClean=true'
        $script:UiSysprepStage = '1of2'
        try {
            Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass 'convergence-confirmed' `
                -StartTime $StartTime -FailureCount 0 -BootCount $BootCount `
                -PendingSysprep $true -ConvergenceConfirmed $true `
                -BootsSinceLastInstall 0 -WorkQueue @() `
                -LastScanResultCount 0
        } catch {}
        Write-FbLog ("EndOfPass[{0}]: {1}. Routing to Invoke-FbReboot which will fast-path STAGE 2 / patient shutdown into STAGE 1." -f $PassIndex, $decision.Reason) 'WARN'
        try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'convergence-confirmed' -Message ('Pass {0} converged. Routing to STAGE 1 sysprep handoff.' -f $PassIndex) -Phase 'Completed' } catch {}
        Invoke-FbReboot -Reason ('Convergence confirmed at pass {0}; routing to STAGE 1 sysprep handoff.' -f $PassIndex)
        return $decision
    }

    # When STAGE 1 is denied, log clearly which gate failed so the
    # operator (or the next verifier) can debug pass-by-pass
    # without log-grepping the whole iteration history.
    # 2026.05.13.2211-fullscope-multipass-progress-cosmetics Fix 3:
    # render booleans as YES/NO for higher-contrast at-a-glance
    # diagnosis. The verifier flagged the original True/False
    # rendering as harder to scan in WU-*.log; the gate semantics
    # are unchanged.
    $denyYn = { param($b) if ($b) { 'YES' } else { 'NO' } }
    Write-FbLog ("EndOfPass[{0}]: STAGE 1 gate denied - workQueueEmpty={1} noRebootNeeded={2} rescanClean={3} priorPassWasClean={4}" -f $PassIndex, (& $denyYn $workQueueEmpty), (& $denyYn $noRebootNeeded), (& $denyYn $rescanClean), (& $denyYn $priorPassWasClean)) 'WARN'

    if ($workQueueEmpty -and $noRebootNeeded -and $rescanClean) {
        # First clean pass (or first clean pass since the last
        # install). Three of four gates open; missing only
        # priorPassWasClean. Mark ConvergenceConfirmed=$true on
        # disk and reboot for the rescan-confirmation boot. The
        # next pass's fresh scan will be the confirmation
        # rescan; if it returns 0 results AND queue is still
        # empty AND no reboot, the convergence gate above will
        # fire STAGE 1.
        $decision.Action = 'CONVERGENCE_PROVISIONAL_REBOOT'
        $decision.Reason = 'First clean pass (workQueueEmpty + noRebootNeeded + rescanClean) but priorPassWasClean=false; provisional reboot for confirmation rescan.'
        try {
            Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass 'convergence-provisional' `
                -StartTime $StartTime -FailureCount 0 -BootCount $BootCount `
                -ConvergenceConfirmed $true `
                -BootsSinceLastInstall 0 -WorkQueue @() `
                -LastScanResultCount $LastScanResultCount
        } catch {}
        Write-FbLog ("EndOfPass[{0}]: {1}" -f $PassIndex, $decision.Reason) 'WARN'
        try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'convergence-provisional' -Message ('Pass {0} clean; rebooting to confirm zero new updates on rescan.' -f $PassIndex) } catch {}
        Invoke-FbReboot -Reason ('Pass {0} clean (queue empty + no reboot); confirmation rescan needs a fresh boot.' -f $PassIndex)
        return $decision
    }

    if ($workQueueEmpty -and (-not $noRebootNeeded)) {
        # Queue empty but CBS owes us a reboot. Patient shutdown
        # WITHOUT setting PendingSysprep yet - the next pass's
        # rescan after reboot is the convergence-confirm.
        $decision.Action = 'PATIENT_SHUTDOWN_NO_SYSPREP'
        $decision.Reason = 'Queue empty but Test-FbWuRebootRequired=true; need a reboot to settle CBS before declaring convergence.'
        try {
            Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass 'pass-clean-needs-reboot' `
                -StartTime $StartTime -FailureCount 0 -BootCount $BootCount `
                -ConvergenceConfirmed $false `
                -BootsSinceLastInstall 0 -WorkQueue @() `
                -LastScanResultCount $LastScanResultCount
        } catch {}
        Write-FbLog ("EndOfPass[{0}]: {1}" -f $PassIndex, $decision.Reason) 'WARN'
        try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'pass-clean-needs-reboot' -Message ('Pass {0}: queue empty but reboot owed; patient shutdown without sysprep latch.' -f $PassIndex) } catch {}
        Invoke-FbReboot -Reason ('Pass {0}: queue empty but reboot owed (CBS settle); next pass will confirm convergence.' -f $PassIndex)
        return $decision
    }

    $newBootsSinceLastInstall = $bootsSinceLastInstallPrior
    if ($realInstallsThisPass -gt 0) {
        $newBootsSinceLastInstall = 0
    } else {
        $newBootsSinceLastInstall = $bootsSinceLastInstallPrior + 1
    }
    $decision.Action = 'PATIENT_SHUTDOWN_QUEUE_NONEMPTY'
    $decision.Reason = ('Queue has {0} pending update(s). RealInstallsThisPass={1}; BootsSinceLastInstall {2} -> {3}.' -f $queueCount, $realInstallsThisPass, $bootsSinceLastInstallPrior, $newBootsSinceLastInstall)

    try {
        Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass 'pass-end-queue-nonempty' `
            -StartTime $StartTime -FailureCount 0 -BootCount $BootCount `
            -ConvergenceConfirmed $false `
            -BootsSinceLastInstall $newBootsSinceLastInstall `
            -WorkQueue $WorkQueue `
            -LastScanResultCount $LastScanResultCount
    } catch {}
    $script:UiBootsSinceLastInst = $newBootsSinceLastInstall
    try { Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'pass-end-queue-nonempty' -Message ('Pass {0}: {1}' -f $PassIndex, $decision.Reason) } catch {}
    Write-FbLog ("EndOfPass[{0}]: {1}" -f $PassIndex, $decision.Reason) 'WARN'
    Invoke-FbReboot -Reason ('Pass {0}: {1} update(s) still pending; rebooting for next pass.' -f $PassIndex, $queueCount)
    return $decision
}

function Test-FbSystemEscapeValve {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
        New (2200) system-level escape valve probe.
    .DESCRIPTION
        2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress. The legacy
        Test-FbEscapeValve fired on FutileBootCount (boots with no
        installs at all). The new probe fires on the system-level
        signals introduced by the multi-pass design:

          1. BootsSinceLastInstall >= 3 AND WorkQueue non-empty
             -> 'NO_PROGRESS_FOR_<N>_BOOTS' where <N> is the
                current BootsSinceLastInstall value (typically 3,
                but can be higher if the trip is delayed by
                another gate firing first or by a state-load
                race where the counter has already advanced past
                the threshold). The dynamic <N> is intentional -
                it gives the receiving tech direct triage data
                without re-reading the state file. If a literal
                grepable string is needed downstream, match
                'NO_PROGRESS_FOR_' as a prefix.
          2. CbsPendingBootsCount >= 5
             -> 'CBS_NEVER_SETTLES' (literal)
          3. FORCE-OOBE-HANDOFF.flag present
             -> 'OPERATOR_OVERRIDE' (literal)

        Returns hashtable {ShouldTrip;Reason;Threshold;CurrentValue}.
        Never throws; caller is responsible for the actual
        Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff call.
    #>
    param([Parameter(Mandatory)] $StateSnapshot)

    if (Test-Path $ForceOobeHandoffFlag) {
        return @{ ShouldTrip=$true; Reason='OPERATOR_OVERRIDE'; Threshold=0; CurrentValue=0 }
    }

    $cbsCount = 0
    try { $cbsCount = [int]$StateSnapshot.CbsPendingBootsCount } catch {}
    if ($cbsCount -ge 5) {
        return @{ ShouldTrip=$true; Reason='CBS_NEVER_SETTLES'; Threshold=5; CurrentValue=$cbsCount }
    }

    $bootsSinceLast = 0
    try { $bootsSinceLast = [int]$StateSnapshot.BootsSinceLastInstall } catch {}
    $queueCount = 0
    try { $queueCount = ($StateSnapshot.WorkQueue | Measure-Object).Count } catch {}
    if ($bootsSinceLast -ge 3 -and $queueCount -gt 0) {
        return @{ ShouldTrip=$true; Reason=('NO_PROGRESS_FOR_{0}_BOOTS' -f $bootsSinceLast); Threshold=3; CurrentValue=$bootsSinceLast }
    }

    return @{ ShouldTrip=$false; Reason=''; Threshold=3; CurrentValue=$bootsSinceLast }
}

# ============================================================================
# 2026.05.13.2213k-real-single-instance-and-real-multipass-and-builder-pathfix
# Bug Y-2 (CRITICAL): deferred startup-shortcut handoffs.
#
# We are now at the point in the script where every function the
# downstream Invoke-FbOobeHandoff -> STAGE 1 inline-retry path
# depends on has been registered:
#   - Invoke-FbWuInlineRetryPass (~line 9551 in 2213k source)
#   - Invoke-FbBucketApply        (~line 9701)
#   - Invoke-FbWuEndOfPassDecision (~line 10795)
#   - Get-PassDefinitions, Reset-WindowsUpdateServices,
#     Invoke-UsoScanKick, etc.
# So the two startup-decision shortcuts that were inlined earlier in
# 2213j (and produced the "(1/3 attempts, ...) inline-retry budget
# exhausted" cascade by trying to call Invoke-FbWuInlineRetryPass
# before its `function` statement was reached) can now safely
# execute. We run them in priority order, MOST SEVERE FIRST: a
# CascadeExhaustionCount>=2 device is in a worse state than a
# normal InstallsCompleted device and must take precedence even if
# both latches are set somehow.
# ============================================================================

# Priority 1: CascadeExhaustionCount >= 2. (Was inlined ~9232 in 2213j.)
if ($script:DeferredStartupCascadeExhaustionHandoff) {
    # The prior cycles have shown: cascade exhausted (count 0 -> 1),
    # emergency scheduled-task reboot also failed (count 1 -> 2).
    # Further reboot attempts are pointless. Hand off straight to
    # OOBE so the deferred updates can settle on first user logon.
    Write-FbLog ("Startup (deferred): CascadeExhaustionCount={0} >= 2; skipping all reboot attempts and handing off to OOBE." -f $script:CascadeExhaustionCountAtStartup) 'ERROR'
    Initialize-UiStateFromDisk
    Invoke-FbOobeHandoff -Reason ("Startup detected CascadeExhaustionCount={0} >= 2; bypassing cascade entirely." -f $script:CascadeExhaustionCountAtStartup)
    # Invoke-FbOobeHandoff always exits the process; defensive return.
    return
}

# Priority 2: InstallsCompleted=true. (Was inlined ~9029 in 2213j.)
if ($script:DeferredStartupInstallsCompletedHandoff -and $script:InstallsCompletedAtStartup) {
    $fbSkipCascadeState = $null
    try { $fbSkipCascadeState = Load-State } catch {}
    if (Test-FbWuSkipCascadeUnsafe -State $fbSkipCascadeState) {
        Invoke-FbRefuseSkipCascadeAndResumeWu -State $fbSkipCascadeState -Reason 'startup deferred InstallsCompleted gate'
    } else {
    # GATE: installs are complete (either latched in a prior run by
    # Set-FbInstallsCompleted, or synthesized via the upgrade bridge
    # earlier). Skip the entire cascade pipeline and route to OOBE.
    Write-FbLog ("Startup (deferred): InstallsCompleted=true; skipping the entire cascade pipeline. WatchdogRecoveryActive={0}." -f $script:WatchdogRecoveryActive) 'WARN'
    $script:LastInstallsCompletedDiag = if ($script:InstallsCompletedBridgeReason) {
        ("InstallsCompleted=true at startup [bridge: {0}]" -f $script:InstallsCompletedBridgeReason)
    } else {
        'InstallsCompleted=true at startup [persisted in wu-state.json]'
    }

    # Initialize UI state from disk so we render any pre-existing
    # update rows on the dashboard while we work through the handoff.
    try { Initialize-UiStateFromDisk } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("InstallsCompleted gate: Initialize-UiStateFromDisk threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # 2026.05.13.2213j-multipass-and-dedup-and-buildmarker Bug Z:
    # do NOT claim 'Completed' before the STAGE 1 convergence gate
    # has run. The gate (inside Invoke-FbOobeHandoff) can refuse and
    # reboot, which from the user's POV is the "completed -> scan ->
    # retry on next boot" flicker reported on G:\fb-dump-858. Use
    # 'Verifying' here and let the gate flip to 'Completed' only
    # after it has actually verified convergence.
    try {
        Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'startup' -Message ("Updates installed in prior run - verifying convergence before OOBE handoff. Detail: {0}" -f $script:LastInstallsCompletedDiag) -Phase 'Verifying'
    } catch {
        # Fallback to the prior un-prefixed message if the helper threw.
        $startupGateMsg = ("[{0}] Updates installed in prior run - verifying convergence before OOBE handoff..." -f $script:LastInstallsCompletedDiag)
        Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Verifying' -Message $startupGateMsg
    }
    try { Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Verifying' } catch {}

    # Best-effort informational final scan so the log records what
    # WUA still has on offer at this moment. We do NOT install / hide
    # anything; this is purely diagnostic. The scan is also bounded
    # by Wait-ForInternet so it doesn't block forever on offline.
    try {
        Wait-ForInternet -SleepSeconds 30
        Initialize-FbWuEnvironment
        Write-FbLog 'InstallsCompleted gate: running ONE informational scan to record remaining offered updates (no install action).' 'INFO'
        $gateScan = $null
        try {
            $gateScan = Invoke-FbWuScan -TimeoutSeconds $RescanTimeoutSec
        } catch {
            Write-FbLog ("InstallsCompleted gate: informational scan threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
        }
        $gateScanCount = ($gateScan | Measure-Object).Count
        Write-FbLog ("InstallsCompleted gate: informational scan returned {0} update(s) still on offer (not installed in this run)." -f $gateScanCount) 'INFO'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("InstallsCompleted gate: informational scan setup threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # Relabel ALL existing 'Pending restart' / 'Retrying' / etc. rows
    # to 'Completed' with the explicit deferred-settlement detail.
    # This is the user-visible signal that the device IS done; only
    # the reboot is outstanding (and Windows itself will settle that
    # on first user logon).
    try {
        $gateRelabel = Set-FbAllUpdatesDeferred
        Write-FbLog ("InstallsCompleted gate: relabeled {0} UI row(s) to Completed with the deferred-settle note." -f $gateRelabel) 'WARN'
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("InstallsCompleted gate: Set-FbAllUpdatesDeferred threw: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # Hand off to OOBE. Invoke-FbOobeHandoff always exits the process.
    $gateHandoffReason = if ($script:InstallsCompletedBridgeReason) {
        ("Startup InstallsCompleted gate (upgrade bridge: {0})" -f $script:InstallsCompletedBridgeReason)
    } else {
        'Startup InstallsCompleted=true gate; cascade pipeline bypassed.'
    }
    Invoke-FbOobeHandoff -Reason $gateHandoffReason
    # Defensive return - Invoke-FbOobeHandoff exits.
    return
    }
}

# 2026.05.11.1230 main-loop top guard. Defense-in-depth: the startup
# gate above should already have caught this case and exited via
# Invoke-FbOobeHandoff. The guard here protects against any pathway
# that reaches the main loop with InstallsCompleted latched (e.g.
# the on-disk state was flipped between the startup gate and now by
# a sibling process, or a future refactor that bypasses the startup
# gate). If the latch is set, jump straight to OOBE handoff: no
# iterations, no buckets, no cascades.
try {
    $mainLoopStateProbe = Load-State
    if ([bool]$mainLoopStateProbe.InstallsCompleted) {
        # 2026.05.12.1700-real-install-evidence Fix E: re-validate
        # the latch against the real-install evidence triplet
        # before short-circuiting to OOBE. The Save-State invariant
        # plus Load-State scrub should already ensure
        # RealInstallsTotal > 0 here, but the top guard is the last
        # line of defence between a buggy disk write and a silent
        # OOBE bypass - so check explicitly.
        $topGuardRealTotal = 0
        try { $topGuardRealTotal = [int]$mainLoopStateProbe.RealInstallsTotal } catch {}
        $topGuardBootCount = 0
        try { $topGuardBootCount = [int]$mainLoopStateProbe.BootCount } catch {}
        $topGuardHasEvidence = ($topGuardRealTotal -gt 0) -or ($script:RealInstallsThisBoot -gt 0)
        if ($topGuardBootCount -le 1 -and -not $topGuardHasEvidence) {
            Write-FbLog ("Main-loop top guard Fix E rail: InstallsCompleted=true on first boot with no real install evidence (RealInstallsTotal={0}, ThisBoot={1}). Refusing to short-circuit; continuing into bucket iterations." -f $topGuardRealTotal, $script:RealInstallsThisBoot) 'ERROR'
        } elseif (-not $topGuardHasEvidence) {
            Write-FbLog ("Main-loop top guard: InstallsCompleted=true but no real install evidence (RealInstallsTotal={0}, ThisBoot={1}). Refusing OOBE handoff; continuing into bucket iterations." -f $topGuardRealTotal, $script:RealInstallsThisBoot) 'ERROR'
        } else {
            if (Test-FbWuSkipCascadeUnsafe -State $mainLoopStateProbe) {
                Invoke-FbRefuseSkipCascadeAndResumeWu -State $mainLoopStateProbe -Reason 'main-loop top guard'
            } else {
                Write-FbLog ("Main-loop top guard: InstallsCompleted=true with real install evidence (RealInstallsTotal={0}, ThisBoot={1}); skipping iteration block and routing directly to OOBE handoff." -f $topGuardRealTotal, $script:RealInstallsThisBoot) 'WARN'
                # 2026.05.13.2213j-multipass-and-dedup-and-buildmarker Bug Z:
                # do NOT advertise Phase='Completed' here. The STAGE 1
                # convergence gate inside Invoke-FbOobeHandoff can refuse
                # and reboot, which from the operator's POV looks like
                # "completed -> scan -> retry-on-next-boot" flicker. The
                # 'Verifying' phase keeps the UI honest until convergence
                # is actually proven by the STAGE 1 gate.
                Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Verifying' -Message 'Updates installed in prior run - verifying convergence before OOBE handoff...'
                try { Update-FbHeartbeat -Phase 'Verifying' } catch {}
                try { Set-FbAllUpdatesDeferred | Out-Null } catch {}
                Invoke-FbOobeHandoff -Reason 'Main-loop top guard observed InstallsCompleted=true; bypassing all bucket iterations.'
                exit 0
            }
        }
    }
} catch {
    Write-FbLog ("Main-loop top guard: state probe threw; continuing into iterations: {0}" -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
}

# 5.4.19: remaining in Audit WU; splash may hide OOBE-family hosts only while this PID is live.
$script:FbWuLoopPaintingActive = $true
Set-FbWuLoopPaintingMarker

$passes = Get-PassDefinitions
$runHadErrors = $false

$maxIterations = 5
$iter = 0
$scanRetry = 0

# 2026.05.13.2213c-onlogon-primary-and-defender-disable: disable
# Microsoft Defender real-time monitoring BEFORE the first scan-then-
# install pass starts. Defender real-time scanning serializes file IO
# during multi-KB cumulative installs and substantially slows the
# audit-mode update window on this hardware. The device is offline /
# airgapped during this window so the threat surface is minimal.
# Restore is bulletproof - fires from EVERY exit path
# (Invoke-FbOobeHandoff, Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff, Tier 7 panic,
# CBS-stuck give-up, version-skew, every exit 1) so the device ships
# to the end user with Defender fully enabled. The defender-state.flag
# marker on disk is the system-of-record so a crashed prior session
# can be recovered on startup.
#
# 2213d: idempotency check. SetupComplete.cmd's :DISABLE_DEFENDER
# subroutine now disables Defender during the specialize pass, BEFORE
# the audit-mode reboot, so the OnLogon / Layer 4 splash launcher
# powershell.exe is not killed by Defender's fileless-attack scanner
# during the 12-44 second window before this code path used to fire.
# That means when we reach this point the flag will almost always
# already say "disabled". Probing the flag and short-circuiting saves
# us from spamming Set-MpPreference (harmless but noisy) and gives
# the dashboard a clear log line that distinguishes "pre-disable
# inherited from SetupComplete" vs "loop-local disable". If the flag
# is absent (clean image), says "restored" (something restored
# Defender between SetupComplete and the loop - unlikely but possible
# e.g. AV tool, Windows Update of Defender platform), or is malformed,
# we fall through to the original Set-FbDefenderRealtime call so the
# safety net is preserved.
$preInstallDefenderState = ''
$preInstallDefenderRaw   = ''
try {
    if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $DefenderStateFlag) {
        $preInstallDefenderRaw = Get-Content -LiteralPath $DefenderStateFlag -Raw -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
        if ($preInstallDefenderRaw) {
            $preInstallDefenderState = ([string]$preInstallDefenderRaw).Split('|')[0].Trim().ToLowerInvariant()
        }
    }
} catch {
    Write-FbLog ("Pre-install Defender flag probe threw: {0}; will call Set-FbDefenderRealtime to be safe." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
}
if ($preInstallDefenderState -eq 'disabled') {
    Write-FbLog ("Pre-install Defender disable: defender-state.flag already says 'disabled' (raw='{0}'); SetupComplete pre-audit-reboot disable is in effect. Skipping redundant Set-MpPreference call." -f ([string]$preInstallDefenderRaw).Trim()) 'INFO'
} else {
    Write-FbLog ("Pre-install Defender disable: defender-state.flag state='{0}' (raw='{1}'); calling Set-FbDefenderRealtime to disable." -f $preInstallDefenderState, ([string]$preInstallDefenderRaw).Trim()) 'INFO'
    Set-FbDefenderRealtime -Enabled $false -Reason 'pre-install'
}

while ($iter -lt $maxIterations) {
    $iter++

    # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: this iteration
    # is now ALSO a "Pass" in the multi-pass full-scope model. The
    # pass index is exposed on the dashboard via the activity-bar
    # footer (Pass=N) and used as the system-of-record correlator
    # in EndOfPass[N] log lines.
    $script:UiPassIndex = $iter
    $script:UiCurrentBucket = ''
    $script:UiPendingUpdates = 0
    $script:UiSysprepStage = 'N/A'

    # Snapshot RealInstallsTotal at the start of this pass so the
    # end-of-pass decision can compute realInstallsThisPass = now -
    # passStart and decide whether to reset BootsSinceLastInstall
    # (gained ground this pass) or increment it (no progress).
    $passStartProbe = $null
    try { $passStartProbe = Load-State } catch {}
    $realInstallsAtPassStart = 0
    if ($passStartProbe) { try { $realInstallsAtPassStart = [int]$passStartProbe.RealInstallsTotal } catch {} }
    $script:UiBootsSinceLastInst = if ($passStartProbe) { try { [int]$passStartProbe.BootsSinceLastInstall } catch { 0 } } else { 0 }

    # System-level escape valve probe (NEW in 2200). Replaces the
    # legacy FutileBootCount-only probe as the primary trip
    # condition. Runs BEFORE the STAGE 2 short-circuit so a stuck
    # CBS or a 3-boots-no-progress device cannot loop forever even
    # if PendingSysprep keeps getting set then never satisfied.
    $sysEvProbe = $null
    try { $sysEvProbe = Test-FbSystemEscapeValve -StateSnapshot $passStartProbe } catch {}
    if ($sysEvProbe -and $sysEvProbe.ShouldTrip) {
        Write-FbLog ("System escape valve TRIPPED at top of Pass {0}: Reason={1} CurrentValue={2} Threshold={3}." -f $iter, $sysEvProbe.Reason, $sysEvProbe.CurrentValue, $sysEvProbe.Threshold) 'ERROR'
        try {
            Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'system-escape-valve' -Message ('System escape valve at Pass {0}: Reason={1} ({2}/{3}). Routing to OOBE handoff with INCOMPLETE state.' -f $iter, $sysEvProbe.Reason, $sysEvProbe.CurrentValue, $sysEvProbe.Threshold)
        } catch {}
        $opFutileCarry = 0
        try { $opFutileCarry = [int]$passStartProbe.FutileBootCount } catch {}
        Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff -Reason $sysEvProbe.Reason -FutileNow $opFutileCarry -Threshold $sysEvProbe.Threshold
        break
    }

    # 2026.05.13.1900-two-stage-sysprep-handoff: top-of-iteration
    # STAGE 2 short-circuit. If a prior boot ran STAGE 1 (latched
    # PendingSysprep=true and rebooted to let CBS settle), and we
    # are now on the post-reboot boot with CBS settled, route
    # directly to Invoke-FbOobeHandoff without burning another
    # scan iteration. The startup InstallsCompletedAtStartup gate
    # above would normally have caught this case for a state file
    # written by the new build, but on a stranded device whose
    # state file is from build 1700 (no PendingSysprep field) the
    # Load-State backward-compat synthesis kicks in and we want
    # the next scan iteration to short-circuit cleanly to STAGE 2
    # rather than burn time on a redundant scan.
    try {
        $iterStageDisk = Load-State
        if ([bool]$iterStageDisk.PendingSysprep) {
            $iterStageOsClean = $false
            try { $iterStageOsClean = (-not (Test-FbWuRebootRequired)) } catch { $iterStageOsClean = $false }
            if ($iterStageOsClean) {
                Write-FbLog ("Main-loop iteration {0}: STAGE 2 short-circuit (PendingSysprep=true AND Test-FbWuRebootRequired=false). Routing directly to Invoke-FbOobeHandoff." -f $iter) 'WARN'
                $script:UiSysprepStage = '2of2'
                # 2026.05.13.2213j-multipass-and-dedup-and-buildmarker Bug Z:
                # see top-guard rationale - we route to OOBE handoff
                # which itself may refuse via the STAGE 1 convergence
                # gate. Until the gate has voted, claim 'Verifying',
                # NOT 'Completed'.
                try {
                    Set-FbDashboardLatchDiag -Stage 'main-loop-stage2-shortcircuit' -Message ('STAGE 2 short-circuit at iter {0}: PendingSysprep=true and OS clean; verifying convergence before sysprep launch.' -f $iter) -Phase 'Verifying'
                } catch {
                    try { Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Verifying' -Message ('STAGE 2 short-circuit at iter {0}; verifying convergence before sysprep launch.' -f $iter) } catch {}
                }
                Invoke-FbOobeHandoff -Reason ('Main-loop top-of-iteration STAGE 2 short-circuit (iter={0}, PendingSysprep=true, OS clean).' -f $iter)
                # Invoke-FbOobeHandoff exits. Defensive break.
                break
            } else {
                $script:UiSysprepStage = '1of2'
                Write-FbLog ("Main-loop iteration {0}: PendingSysprep=true but Test-FbWuRebootRequired=true; CBS has not settled yet. Continuing scan iteration; STAGE 2 will be retried after the next reboot." -f $iter) 'WARN'
            }
        }
    } catch {
        Write-FbLog ("Main-loop iteration STAGE 2 short-circuit probe threw: {0}; continuing." -f $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
    }

    # 2026.05.13.0900-escape-valve: operator-override + budget probes
    # at the very top of every iteration. A tech can drop
    # FORCE-OOBE-HANDOFF.flag at any time and have the loop bail
    # out without waiting for the next scheduled reboot. The
    # EXTEND-BUDGET.flag is read by Get-FbFutileBootThreshold each
    # time the threshold is evaluated, so no explicit reload is
    # needed here - we only LOG its presence so the operator sees
    # confirmation on the dashboard / log that their override was
    # observed.
    if (Test-Path $ForceOobeHandoffFlag) {
        Write-FbLog ("OPERATOR OVERRIDE: FORCE-OOBE-HANDOFF.flag detected at top of iteration {0} ('{1}'). Routing to escape-valve handoff immediately." -f $iter, $ForceOobeHandoffFlag) 'ERROR'
        $opStateNow = $null
        try { $opStateNow = Load-State } catch {}
        $opFutile = 0
        try { $opFutile = [int]$opStateNow.FutileBootCount } catch {}
        $opThresholdInfo = Get-FbFutileBootThreshold
        Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff -Reason 'OPERATOR_OVERRIDE' -FutileNow $opFutile -Threshold ([int]$opThresholdInfo.Threshold)
        # Invoke-FbEscapeValveHandoff -> Invoke-FbOobeHandoff exits
        # the process. Defensive break so static analyzers see a
        # clean exit from the while loop even though we never get
        # here.
        break
    }
    if (Test-Path $ExtendBudgetFlag) {
        try {
            $extInfo = Get-FbFutileBootThreshold
            Write-FbLog ("OPERATOR OVERRIDE: EXTEND-BUDGET.flag - threshold extended to {0}." -f $extInfo.Label) 'WARN'
        } catch {}
    }

    # [DEBUG] 2249: Developer bypass — Ctrl+Shift+End → skip updates, force OOBE handoff
    # Test-FbDebugForceOobeHandoff checks C:\ProgramData\FirstBase\.debug-force-oobe-handoff,
    # removes the flag on detection, sets $script:FbDebugBypassActive=$true, and returns $true.
    # The STAGE 1 convergence gate in Invoke-FbOobeHandoff checks $script:FbDebugBypassActive
    # to skip the residual scan and accept convergence unconditionally.
    if (Test-FbDebugForceOobeHandoff) {
        Write-FbLog ('[DEBUG] 2249: Debug bypass active at iteration {0}; forcing OOBE handoff immediately.' -f $iter) 'WARN'
        Invoke-FbOobeHandoff -Reason '[DEBUG] 2249: .debug-force-oobe-handoff flag triggered by developer hotkey (Ctrl+Shift+End); bypassing update loop.'
        # Invoke-FbOobeHandoff exits the process. Defensive break for static analyzers.
        break
    }

    Wait-ForInternet -SleepSeconds 30
    Disable-FbRecallFeatureOnce

    if ($iter -eq 1 -and $scanRetry -eq 0) {
        Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Scanning' -Message 'Scanning Microsoft Update for available updates...'
        Reset-WindowsUpdateServices
        Start-Sleep -Seconds 5
        Invoke-UsoScanKick
        Start-Sleep -Seconds 8
    } else {
        Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Scanning' -Message ("Re-scanning Microsoft Update (iteration {0})..." -f $iter)
    }

    try {
        # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: every pass
        # MUST start with a fresh online scan (no filters; the
        # tolerant Invoke-FbWuScan classifier puts each result into
        # one of Critical|Driver|Cumulative|Optional). The result
        # count is persisted as $state.LastScanResultCount and the
        # WorkQueue is rebuilt from it - this is what powers the
        # convergence-confirm rescan: a clean pass followed by a
        # fresh scan returning 0 applicable updates declares full
        # convergence and routes to STAGE 1 sysprep handoff.
        Write-FbLog "[Pass $iter] Single unfiltered online scan (IsInstalled=0 and IsHidden=0)"
        $available = Invoke-FbWuScan -TimeoutSeconds $ScanTimeoutSec
        $count = ($available | Measure-Object).Count
        Write-FbLog "[Pass $iter] Scan returned $count update(s) total"
        $scanRetry = 0
        $script:WuScanCountThisBoot += $count

        # ===========================================================
        # 2026.05.18.2213l Bug FF Mechanism 1: SOFT auto-GivenUp.
        # 2026.05.19.2213m: refactored into Invoke-FbSoftGivenUpEvaluate
        # so the same evaluation also runs in the OOBE-handoff STAGE 1
        # gate (Invoke-FbOobeHandoff) and the inline retry pass
        # (Invoke-FbWuInlineRetryPass). See helper function header for
        # the regression background (dump 651, 5/19).
        # ===========================================================
        $null = Invoke-FbSoftGivenUpEvaluate -ScanResults @($available) -GivenUpSet $givenUpSet -CallContext ("main-loop-pass-{0}" -f $iter)

        # Persist LastScanResultCount immediately so the EndOfPass
        # decision (and any panic dump that happens between here
        # and the end of the iteration) can read it from disk.
        # 2026.05.18.2213l Bug FF: also persist SoftGivenUpCounters
        # / SuccessLedgerIds / GivenUpUpdateIds (the latter may
        # have grown via the post-scan promotion block above) so
        # the next boot's startup load sees the freshly bumped
        # counters and any newly promoted GivenUp entries.
        try {
            Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass ('pass-{0}-scan' -f $iter) `
                -StartTime $startTime -FailureCount $failureCount `
                -BootCount $bootCount `
                -LastScanResultCount $count `
                -GivenUpUpdateIds @($givenUpSet) `
                -SoftGivenUpCounters $script:FbSoftGivenUpCountersLoop `
                -SuccessLedgerIds @($script:FbSuccessLedgerSet)
        } catch {}

        # Reset the working WorkQueue array for this pass. We
        # rebuild it from scratch each pass (the scan is the
        # source of truth for "what's still owed") with one
        # caveat: rows from the prior pass that are still in
        # the same Reboot/Transient state are preserved in
        # Update-FbWuWorkQueueFromBucketResults via the
        # existingByUid lookup. So if a Critical update was
        # marked "Pending reboot - will reapply next pass" and
        # WUA still offers it, the row stays with the SAME
        # RetryCount and FirstSeenBoot.
        $passWorkQueue = @()
        if ($passStartProbe -and $passStartProbe.WorkQueue) {
            $passWorkQueue = @($passStartProbe.WorkQueue)
        }

        if ($count -eq 0) {
            # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: empty
            # scan - this is the convergence-confirm path. Run
            # the end-of-pass decision so PendingSysprep gets
            # latched and STAGE 1 fires. Do NOT break to the
            # legacy final-verification block.
            Write-FbLog ("[Pass {0}] Empty scan; routing to EndOfPassDecision for convergence handling." -f $iter) 'WARN'
            Invoke-FbWuEndOfPassDecision -PassIndex $iter -WorkQueue @() `
                -RealInstallsAtPassStart $realInstallsAtPassStart `
                -LastScanResultCount 0 -StartTime $startTime -BootCount $bootCount
            # Invoke-FbWuEndOfPassDecision routes to Invoke-FbReboot
            # (which either patient-shuts-down or fast-paths sysprep).
            # Defensive break in case it returns.
            break
        }

        $byBucket = @{}
        foreach ($pass in $passes) { $byBucket[$pass.Name] = @() }
        foreach ($u in @($available)) {
            $bucket = [string]$u.Bucket
            if (-not $byBucket.ContainsKey($bucket)) {
                Write-FbLog "[Pass $iter] Unknown bucket '$bucket' for update '$($u.Title)'; routing to Optional." 'WARN'
                $bucket = 'Optional'
            }
            $byBucket[$bucket] += $u
        }

        $summary = ($passes | ForEach-Object { ("{0}={1}" -f $_.Name, $byBucket[$_.Name].Count) }) -join ' '
        Write-FbLog "[Pass $iter] Bucket counts: $summary"
        Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Scanning' -Message ("Pass {0} scan complete: {1}" -f $iter, $summary)
        $script:UiPendingUpdates = $count

        $iterationRebootRequired = $false
        $iterationForcePendingRestart = $false
        $haltedBucket = $null
        # Track per-bucket outcomes so we can render a single greedy-install
        # summary line at the end of this iteration.
        $bucketSummary = [ordered]@{
            Critical   = @{ Targeted=0; Succeeded=0; Failed=0; Skipped=0 }
            Driver     = @{ Targeted=0; Succeeded=0; Failed=0; Skipped=0 }
            Cumulative = @{ Targeted=0; Succeeded=0; Failed=0; Skipped=0 }
            Optional   = @{ Targeted=0; Succeeded=0; Failed=0; Skipped=0 }
        }
        $rebootingForBuckets = @()

        foreach ($pass in $passes) {
            $bucketUpdates = @($byBucket[$pass.Name])
            $script:UiCurrentBucket = $pass.Name
            if ($bucketUpdates.Count -eq 0) {
                Write-FbLog "===== Pass $iter Bucket: $($pass.Name) (0 updates, skipping) ====="
                continue
            }

            Write-FbLog "===== Pass $iter Bucket: $($pass.Name) ($($bucketUpdates.Count) update(s)) ====="
            Set-UiPhase -Phase $pass.Name -Message ("Pass {0} | {1}: {2} update(s) queued" -f $iter, $pass.Name, $bucketUpdates.Count)
            $script:WuApplyAttempted = $true

            # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: HaltOnFailure
            # is now ALWAYS $false. The user's spec is explicit:
            # "No halt on Critical failure - Critical failures route
            # through the same per-update outcome routing as other
            # buckets and the pass continues to Driver/Cumulative/
            # Optional." Even if the bucket sets Halted=true (explicit
            # reboot signal), we now PROCESS all 4 buckets in the same
            # pass; the end-of-pass decision handles the reboot at a
            # single point.
            # 5.4.18: do not run DISM/CBS cleanup while Driver COM
            # Install() is on the stack (Recall/FoD enumerate during a
            # hung OEM driver is what pinned HP ~20 min).
            $isDriverBucket = ($pass.Name -eq 'Driver')
            if ($isDriverBucket) { $script:FbWuDriverApplyActive = $true }
            try {
            $bucketResult = Invoke-FbBucketApply `
                -BucketName $pass.Name `
                -BucketUpdates $bucketUpdates `
                -RetryMap $updateRetryMap `
                -GivenUpSet $givenUpSet `
                -StartTime $startTime `
                -FailureCount $failureCount `
                -HaltOnFailure $false `
                -BootCountByKey $bootCountByKey `
                -ForceHiddenSet $forceHiddenSet `
                -BootCount $bootCount `
                -RebootIterations $rebootIterations `
                -SysprepRetryCount $sysprepRetryCount `
                -UpdateRetryBudget $updateRetryBudgetMap
            } finally {
                if ($isDriverBucket) { $script:FbWuDriverApplyActive = $false }
            }

            if ($bucketSummary.Contains($pass.Name)) {
                $bucketSummary[$pass.Name].Targeted  = [int]$bucketResult.Targeted
                $bucketSummary[$pass.Name].Succeeded = [int]$bucketResult.Succeeded
                $bucketSummary[$pass.Name].Failed    = [int]$bucketResult.Failed
                $bucketSummary[$pass.Name].Skipped   = [int]$bucketResult.Skipped
            }

            if ($bucketResult.RebootRequired)      { $iterationRebootRequired = $true }
            if ($bucketResult.ForcePendingRestart) { $iterationForcePendingRestart = $true }
            if ($bucketResult.RebootRequired -or $bucketResult.ForcePendingRestart) {
                $rebootingForBuckets += $pass.Name
            }

            # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: refresh
            # the persistent WorkQueue from this bucket's per-update
            # results. Updates with Reboot/Transient/Unknown
            # outcomes stay on the queue for the next pass; updates
            # with Success/Terminal outcomes are removed. The queue
            # is the system-of-record the EndOfPass decision uses
            # to declare convergence.
            try {
                # 2026.05.13.2210-fullscope-multipass-progress-fixes
                # Critical Fix 3: pass [ref]$givenUpSet so any
                # Class=Terminal outcome adds the UpdateId +
                # retry-key to the same GivenUp hashset that the
                # Save-State sweep below writes via -GivenUpUpdateIds.
                Update-FbWuWorkQueueFromBucketResults `
                    -BucketName $pass.Name `
                    -BucketUpdates $bucketUpdates `
                    -BucketApplyResult $bucketResult `
                    -WorkQueueRef ([ref]$passWorkQueue) `
                    -BootCount $bootCount `
                    -RetryBudgetMap $updateRetryBudgetMap `
                    -GivenUpSetRef ([ref]$givenUpSet)
            } catch {
                Write-FbLog ("Update-FbWuWorkQueueFromBucketResults threw for bucket {0}: {1}. Queue not advanced this bucket." -f $pass.Name, $_.Exception.Message) 'WARN'
            }

            # Persist the running WorkQueue + LastScanResultCount
            # after each bucket so a mid-pass crash leaves the disk
            # state coherent for the next boot's pass-start probe.
            try {
                # 2026.05.13.2210-fullscope-multipass-progress-fixes
                # Critical Fix 3: persist GivenUpUpdateIds so any
                # Terminal outcomes added by Update-FbWuWorkQueue-
                # FromBucketResults survive a mid-pass crash and
                # are honored by the next boot's pass-start probe.
                Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass ('pass-{0}-bucket-{1}' -f $iter, $pass.Name) `
                    -StartTime $startTime -FailureCount $failureCount `
                    -BootCount $bootCount `
                    -WorkQueue $passWorkQueue `
                    -LastScanResultCount $count `
                    -GivenUpUpdateIds @($givenUpSet)
            } catch {}
            $script:UiPendingUpdates = ($passWorkQueue | Measure-Object).Count

            # 5.4.18: pending reboot wins over keep installing drivers.
            # After any bucket, if the OS wants a reboot AND this boot
            # actually installed something, reboot before a long Driver
            # (or later) serial pass. Bug R still skips the PRE-loop
            # reboot when RealInstallsTotal=0 (image Edge PFRO).
            $osPendingAfterBucket = $false
            try { $osPendingAfterBucket = [bool](Test-FbWuRebootRequired) } catch {
                $osPendingAfterBucket = [bool]$bucketResult.RebootRequired
            }
            $realThisBootNow = 0
            try { $realThisBootNow = [int]$script:RealInstallsThisBoot } catch { $realThisBootNow = 0 }
            if ($osPendingAfterBucket -and $realThisBootNow -gt 0) {
                $laterBucketsWithWork = @()
                $pastCurrent = $false
                foreach ($laterPass in $passes) {
                    if (-not $pastCurrent) {
                        if ($laterPass.Name -eq $pass.Name) { $pastCurrent = $true }
                        continue
                    }
                    $laterN = 0
                    try { $laterN = @($byBucket[$laterPass.Name]).Count } catch { $laterN = 0 }
                    if ($laterN -gt 0) { $laterBucketsWithWork += $laterPass.Name }
                }
                if ($laterBucketsWithWork.Count -gt 0) {
                    Write-FbLog ("Pending reboot after bucket {0} with RealInstallsThisBoot={1}; not starting remaining buckets ({2}). Invoking Invoke-FbReboot." -f $pass.Name, $realThisBootNow, ($laterBucketsWithWork -join ',')) 'WARN'
                    Invoke-FbRebootWithFreshStateCheck -Reason ("pending reboot after {0} real installs this boot; remaining buckets: {1}" -f $pass.Name, ($laterBucketsWithWork -join ','))
                }
            }

            # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: NO
            # break here on Halted unless 5.4.18 pending-reboot rail
            # above already invoked reboot. Remaining buckets still
            # run when OSPendingReboot is false or this boot installed
            # nothing (Bug R image baggage).
        }

        # Render the greedy-install summary so the user can see
        # (without log access) exactly what got installed in this
        # pass before the EndOfPass decision routes the reboot.
        $summaryParts = @()
        $totalApplied = 0
        foreach ($name in @('Critical','Driver','Cumulative','Optional')) {
            $row = $bucketSummary[$name]
            $applied = [int]$row.Succeeded + [int]$row.Skipped
            $totalApplied += $applied
            $summaryParts += ('{0}={1}' -f $name, $applied)
        }
        $passQueueCountForSummary = ($passWorkQueue | Measure-Object).Count
        $passSummaryMsg = ("Pass {0} complete: applied {1} update(s) ({2}); WorkQueue={3} pending; reboot signalled by buckets: {4}" -f $iter, $totalApplied, ($summaryParts -join ' '), $passQueueCountForSummary, ($rebootingForBuckets -join ',' | ForEach-Object { if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($_)) { '(none)' } else { $_ } }))
        Write-FbLog $passSummaryMsg 'INFO'
        Set-UiPhase -Phase 'PassSummary' -Message $passSummaryMsg

        # Per-key boot-count escape hatch retained from build 1830.
        # Force-hide any update that has caused $MaxKeyBootCount
        # reboots while remaining offered. Only runs when at least
        # one bucket signalled a reboot OR the WorkQueue is non-
        # empty; that's when we are about to burn another boot on
        # a perpetually-stuck KB.
        if ($iterationRebootRequired -or $iterationForcePendingRestart -or $passQueueCountForSummary -gt 0) {
            foreach ($u in @($available)) {
                $uid = [string]$u.UpdateId
                if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($uid)) { continue }
                $key = Get-FbRetryKey -Update $u
                $isGivenUp = $givenUpSet.Contains($uid) -or $givenUpSet.Contains($key)
                if (-not $isGivenUp) { continue }
                $current = 0
                if ($bootCountByKey.ContainsKey($key)) { $current = [int]$bootCountByKey[$key] }
                $bootCountByKey[$key] = $current + 1
                if ($bootCountByKey[$key] -ge $MaxKeyBootCount -and -not $forceHiddenSet.Contains($key)) {
                    Write-FbLog ("Force-hide ESCAPE HATCH: '{0}' (key={1}) has caused {2} reboots while remaining offered; hiding via IUpdate.IsHidden=true." -f $u.Title, $key, $bootCountByKey[$key]) 'WARN'
                    $ok = Hide-FbOfferedUpdate -UpdateId $uid
                    if ($ok) {
                        [void]$forceHiddenSet.Add($key)
                        [void]$forceHiddenSet.Add($uid)
                        $title = [string]$u.Title; if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($title)) { $title = $uid }
                        $detail = ("Force-hidden after {0} failed boots; skipped permanently. (Convergence escape hatch.)" -f $bootCountByKey[$key])
                        Set-UiUpdateStatus -UpdateId $uid -Title $title -Status 'Completed' -Retries 0 -Detail $detail
                    } else {
                        Write-FbLog ("Force-hide of '{0}' returned false; will retry on next iteration." -f $u.Title) 'WARN'
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: persist the
        # per-pass state including the carry-over RetryMap, GivenUp
        # set, BootCountByKey, ForceHidden set, and RetryBudget map.
        # The EndOfPass decision will make its own Save-State call
        # for the pass-end-specific fields (WorkQueue,
        # BootsSinceLastInstall, ConvergenceConfirmed,
        # PendingSysprep) but does NOT carry the per-bucket retry
        # state forward - we do that here.
        try {
            Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass ('pass-{0}-end' -f $iter) `
                -StartTime $startTime -FailureCount $failureCount `
                -UpdateRetry $updateRetryMap `
                -GivenUpUpdateIds @($givenUpSet) `
                -BootCountByKey $bootCountByKey `
                -BootCount $bootCount `
                -ForceHiddenKeys @($forceHiddenSet) `
                -RebootIterations $rebootIterations `
                -SysprepRetryCount $sysprepRetryCount `
                -UpdateRetryBudget $updateRetryBudgetMap `
                -WorkQueue $passWorkQueue `
                -LastScanResultCount $count
        } catch {}

        # Iteration-cap fallback: if we have already burned the
        # max reboot budget within this update cycle, don't issue
        # yet another reboot. Just persist and break to the final
        # verification block (which today routes to OOBE handoff).
        if ($rebootIterations -ge $MaxRebootIterations) {
            $remainingCount = ($passWorkQueue | Measure-Object).Count
            $capMsg = ("Max reboot iterations ({0}) reached at end of Pass {1}; deferring {2} remaining update(s) to first user logon." -f $MaxRebootIterations, $iter, $remainingCount)
            Write-FbLog $capMsg 'WARN'
            Set-UiPhase -Phase 'IterationCap' -Message $capMsg
            break
        }

        # 2026.05.13.2200-fullscope-multipass-progress: the strict
        # convergence end-of-pass decision. This routes:
        #   - Empty queue + no reboot + ConvergenceConfirmed/Last
        #     ScanResultCount=0  ->  STAGE 1 sysprep handoff
        #   - Empty queue + no reboot + first time  ->  provisional
        #     reboot for confirmation rescan
        #   - Empty queue + reboot owed  ->  patient shutdown,
        #     no sysprep latch
        #   - Non-empty queue  ->  patient shutdown, bump or reset
        #     BootsSinceLastInstall depending on RealInstallsThisPass
        # Each branch ends in Invoke-FbReboot which never returns.
        # Defensive break in case it does.
        Invoke-FbWuEndOfPassDecision -PassIndex $iter -WorkQueue $passWorkQueue `
            -RealInstallsAtPassStart $realInstallsAtPassStart `
            -LastScanResultCount $count -StartTime $startTime -BootCount $bootCount
        break

    } catch {
        if (-not (Test-InternetConnectivity)) {
            Write-FbLog "[Pass $iter] Internet lost during scan. Soft waiting." 'WARN'
            Wait-ForInternet -SleepSeconds 30
            $iter--
            continue
        }

        if ($_.Exception.Message -match 'timed out after') {
            $scanRetry++
            Write-FbLog "[Pass $iter] Scan timed out. Retry $scanRetry/2." 'WARN'
            Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Scanning' -Message ("Scan timed out. Recovery retry {0}/2..." -f $scanRetry)
            Reset-WindowsUpdateServices
            Invoke-UsoScanKick
            Start-Sleep -Seconds 20
            if ($scanRetry -le 2) {
                $iter--
                continue
            }
            Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass 'scan-timeout' -StartTime $startTime -FailureCount $failureCount -UpdateRetry $updateRetryMap -GivenUpUpdateIds @($givenUpSet) -BootCountByKey $bootCountByKey -BootCount $bootCount -ForceHiddenKeys @($forceHiddenSet) -RebootIterations $rebootIterations -SysprepRetryCount $sysprepRetryCount
            if ($rebootIterations -lt $MaxRebootIterations) {
                Invoke-FbRebootWithFreshStateCheck -Reason 'scan timeout exceeded retries'
            } else {
                Write-FbLog ("Scan timed out but already at MaxRebootIterations ({0}); proceeding to final verification." -f $MaxRebootIterations) 'WARN'
                break
            }
        }

        $runHadErrors = $true
        Write-FbLog "[Pass $iter] WU error: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN'
        break
    }
}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Final verification
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Set-UiPhase -Phase 'FinalCheck' -Message 'Running final update verification...'
Wait-ForInternet -SleepSeconds 30
Reset-WindowsUpdateServices
Start-Sleep -Seconds 5
Invoke-UsoScanKick
Start-Sleep -Seconds 8

$finalCheck = @()
$finalCheckSucceeded = $false
$finalRetry = 0
while (-not $finalCheckSucceeded) {
    Wait-ForInternet -SleepSeconds 30
    try {
        $finalCheck = Invoke-FbWuScan -TimeoutSeconds $RescanTimeoutSec
        $finalCheckSucceeded = $true
    } catch {
        if (-not (Test-InternetConnectivity)) {
            Write-FbLog "Final check failed because internet is unavailable. Soft waiting." 'WARN'
            continue
        }
        $finalRetry++
        Write-FbLog "Final check failed (retry $finalRetry/3): $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN'
        if ($finalRetry -gt 3) {
            $runHadErrors = $true
            break
        }
        Reset-WindowsUpdateServices
        Invoke-UsoScanKick
        Start-Sleep -Seconds 20
    }
}

$finalCount = ($finalCheck | Measure-Object).Count
Write-FbLog "End-of-cycle check: $finalCount remaining update(s)"
Set-UiPhase -Phase 'FinalCheck' -Message ("Final verification found {0} remaining update(s)." -f $finalCount)

if (-not $finalCheckSucceeded -or $runHadErrors) {
    $failureCount++
    Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass 'final-check-error' -StartTime $startTime -FailureCount $failureCount -UpdateRetry $updateRetryMap -GivenUpUpdateIds @($givenUpSet) -BootCountByKey $bootCountByKey -BootCount $bootCount -ForceHiddenKeys @($forceHiddenSet) -RebootIterations $rebootIterations -SysprepRetryCount $sysprepRetryCount
    if ($failureCount -ge $MaxFailureRetry) {
        Write-FbLog "Update cycle has failed $failureCount times; continuing strict retries." 'WARN'
        $failureCount = 0
    }
    if ($rebootIterations -ge $MaxRebootIterations) {
        $capMsg = ("Max reboot iterations ({0}) reached after final-check error; finalizing to OOBE." -f $MaxRebootIterations)
        Write-FbLog $capMsg 'WARN'
        Set-UiPhase -Phase 'IterationCap' -Message $capMsg
    } else {
        # 2026.05.13.2213h-real-convergence-and-ti-serialization Bug P:
        # attempt inline retry BEFORE rebooting. Pre-2213h this path
        # went straight to Invoke-FbRebootWithFreshStateCheck, which
        # rebooted and re-scanned, and the next boot hit the same TI
        # batch collision. With the per-update serialized engine (Bug
        # M) we can attempt the still-pending updates inline first;
        # only if that fails AND/OR the OS demands a reboot do we
        # consume one. The inline retry is gated on Test-FbWuReboot-
        # Required=false (already checked inside Invoke-FbWuInline-
        # RetryPass).
        $inlineP = Invoke-FbWuInlineRetryPass `
            -Reason "post-final-check error rail ($failureCount/$MaxFailureRetry)" `
            -StartTime $startTime `
            -RetryMap $updateRetryMap `
            -GivenUpSet $givenUpSet `
            -BootCountByKey $bootCountByKey `
            -ForceHiddenSet $forceHiddenSet `
            -BootCount $bootCount `
            -RebootIterations $rebootIterations `
            -SysprepRetryCount $sysprepRetryCount `
            -FailureCount $failureCount `
            -UpdateRetryBudget $updateRetryBudgetMap `
            -AttemptIndex 1
        Start-Sleep -Seconds 60
        Invoke-FbRebootWithFreshStateCheck -Reason "retrying after update cycle errors ($failureCount/$MaxFailureRetry) [inline-retry: attempted=$($inlineP.Attempted), installed=$($inlineP.Installed), stillFailed=$($inlineP.StillFailed)]"
    }
}

if ($finalCount -gt 0) {
    if ($rebootIterations -ge $MaxRebootIterations) {
        $capMsg = ("Max reboot iterations ({0}) reached; deferring {1} remaining update(s) to first user logon." -f $MaxRebootIterations, $finalCount)
        Write-FbLog $capMsg 'WARN'
        Set-UiPhase -Phase 'IterationCap' -Message $capMsg
        Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass 'final-check-cap-hit' -StartTime $startTime -FailureCount 0 -UpdateRetry $updateRetryMap -GivenUpUpdateIds @($givenUpSet) -BootCountByKey $bootCountByKey -BootCount $bootCount -ForceHiddenKeys @($forceHiddenSet) -RebootIterations $rebootIterations -SysprepRetryCount $sysprepRetryCount
    } else {
        Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass 'final-check-has-updates' -StartTime $startTime -FailureCount 0 -UpdateRetry $updateRetryMap -GivenUpUpdateIds @($givenUpSet) -BootCountByKey $bootCountByKey -BootCount $bootCount -ForceHiddenKeys @($forceHiddenSet) -RebootIterations $rebootIterations -SysprepRetryCount $sysprepRetryCount
        # 2026.05.13.2213h-real-convergence-and-ti-serialization Bug P:
        # attempt inline retry BEFORE rebooting. Same rationale as the
        # final-check-error path above; this path fires when the post-
        # cycle scan still finds applicable updates, which is exactly
        # the condition where the new per-update serialized engine
        # has a real shot at converging without another reboot.
        $inlineP2 = Invoke-FbWuInlineRetryPass `
            -Reason "post-final-check still has $finalCount update(s) on offer" `
            -StartTime $startTime `
            -RetryMap $updateRetryMap `
            -GivenUpSet $givenUpSet `
            -BootCountByKey $bootCountByKey `
            -ForceHiddenSet $forceHiddenSet `
            -BootCount $bootCount `
            -RebootIterations $rebootIterations `
            -SysprepRetryCount $sysprepRetryCount `
            -FailureCount 0 `
            -UpdateRetryBudget $updateRetryBudgetMap `
            -AttemptIndex 2
        # If the inline retry installed everything and the OS no longer
        # demands a reboot, we may now be converged - re-check via a
        # fresh scan in the post-reboot loop. Otherwise reboot as
        # before. Either way the reboot reason now carries the inline
        # retry outcome for post-mortem.
        Invoke-FbRebootWithFreshStateCheck -Reason "updates still available after final check [inline-retry: attempted=$($inlineP2.Attempted), installed=$($inlineP2.Installed), stillFailed=$($inlineP2.StillFailed)]"
    }
}

if (Test-FbWuRebootRequired) {
    if ($rebootIterations -ge $MaxRebootIterations) {
        $capMsg = ("Reboot pending after final check, but Max reboot iterations ({0}) reached; finalizing to OOBE." -f $MaxRebootIterations)
        Write-FbLog $capMsg 'WARN'
        Set-UiPhase -Phase 'IterationCap' -Message $capMsg
        Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass 'final-check-pending-reboot-cap' -StartTime $startTime -FailureCount 0 -UpdateRetry $updateRetryMap -GivenUpUpdateIds @($givenUpSet) -BootCountByKey $bootCountByKey -BootCount $bootCount -ForceHiddenKeys @($forceHiddenSet) -RebootIterations $rebootIterations -SysprepRetryCount $sysprepRetryCount
    } else {
        Save-State -EmptySweepCount 0 -LastPass 'final-check-pending-reboot' -StartTime $startTime -FailureCount 0 -UpdateRetry $updateRetryMap -GivenUpUpdateIds @($givenUpSet) -BootCountByKey $bootCountByKey -BootCount $bootCount -ForceHiddenKeys @($forceHiddenSet) -RebootIterations $rebootIterations -SysprepRetryCount $sysprepRetryCount
        Invoke-FbRebootWithFreshStateCheck -Reason 'reboot pending after final check'
    }
}

$emptySweepCount++
$failureCount = 0
Save-State -EmptySweepCount $emptySweepCount -LastPass 'final-check-clean' -StartTime $startTime -FailureCount $failureCount -UpdateRetry $updateRetryMap -GivenUpUpdateIds @($givenUpSet) -BootCountByKey $bootCountByKey -BootCount $bootCount -ForceHiddenKeys @($forceHiddenSet) -RebootIterations $rebootIterations -SysprepRetryCount $sysprepRetryCount
Write-FbLog "Empty sweep count: $emptySweepCount / $RequiredEmptySweeps"

# $RequiredEmptySweeps is now 1 (2026-05-11) so a single clean
# final-check immediately drops to Complete-And-Exit. The block below
# is retained for safety in case operators want to raise it again -
# it's a no-op while the constant is 1.
if ($emptySweepCount -lt $RequiredEmptySweeps) {
    if ($rebootIterations -ge $MaxRebootIterations) {
        Write-FbLog ("Confirmation-sweep reboot blocked: Max reboot iterations ({0}) reached; finalizing." -f $MaxRebootIterations) 'WARN'
    } else {
        Set-UiPhase -Phase 'FinalCheck' -Message 'Running confirmation reboot before completion.'
        Invoke-FbRebootWithFreshStateCheck -Reason 'confirmation reboot before declaring complete'
    }
}

Set-UiPhase -Phase 'Completed' -Message 'All updates complete. Finalizing...'
Complete-And-Exit -Reason 'All passes complete. Dropping markers and unregistering task.' -Success $true -ExitCode 0

# Completeness canary for destage / TechInstall copy gates. Do not remove.
# FIRSTBASE-LOOP-EOF-OK
